          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c3fc8df54eff6cb3f1585cca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a time in every properly made boy\u2019s life when he feels a fierce desire to go somewhere and dig for buried treasure. This feeling suddenly seized Tom one day. He went out to find Joe Harper, but could not find him. Then he looked for Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Soon he came across Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a private spot and told him his plan in confidence. Huck was willing. Huck was always ready to join any adventure that promised fun and needed no money, because he had far too much of the kind of time that is not money. \u201cWhere\u2019ll we dig?\u201d Huck said.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it buried all over?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, it surely isn\u2019t. It\u2019s buried in very special places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests under the end of a branch on an old dead tree, right where the shadow falls at midnight; but mostly under the floors of haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho buries it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, of course\u2014who did you think? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it was mine, I wouldn\u2019t bury it; I\u2019d spend it and enjoy myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t do things that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they mean to, but usually they forget the signs, or else they die. Anyway, it stays there a long time and gets rusty; then, after a while, somebody finds an old yellow paper that tells how to find the signs\u2014a paper you have to puzzle over for about a week, because it\u2019s mostly marks and hieroglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro\u2014what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics\u2014pictures and things, you know, that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the signs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any signs. They always bury it under a haunted house, or on an island, or under a dead tree with one branch sticking out. Well, we\u2019ve searched Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can search it again sometime; and there\u2019s the old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and lots of dead-branch trees\u2014loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under every one of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow you talk! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how will you know which one to dig under?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDig under all of them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, what of it? Suppose you found a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. What then?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes shone.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s splendid. Plenty splendid enough for me. Just give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
