          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9cb30530e4931c5882d6d816\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She had left everything behind, except the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nAnne had not entered Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need, and on the few occasions when it might have been possible for her to visit the Hall, she had managed to avoid it and get away. Her first return was to take up her place again in the modern, elegant rooms of the Lodge, and to delight its mistress.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s pleasure at seeing her again was touched with some anxiety. She knew who had been often at Uppercross. But fortunately, either Anne had grown fuller and better-looking, or Lady Russell imagined that she had; and, as Anne accepted her compliments, she amused herself by linking them to her cousin\u2019s quiet admiration, and hoping she might be granted a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nOnce they began to talk, Anne quickly became aware of a change in her own mind. The matters that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, which she had felt were neglected and had been forced to suppress among the Musgroves, had now become only secondary concerns. Lately she had scarcely thought even of her father, her sister, or Bath. Their affairs had been overshadowed by those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell returned to their old hopes and fears, expressing satisfaction with the house taken in Camden Place and regret that Mrs Clay was still with them, Anne would have been ashamed for anyone to know how much more she was thinking of Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her friends there. The Harvilles\u2019 home and friendship, and Captain Benwick, interested her far more than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs Clay. She was actually obliged to make an effort to meet Lady Russell with even the appearance of equal concern over subjects that naturally had the strongest claim upon her.\\n\\nAt first there was some awkwardness when they spoke of another matter. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had been there barely five minutes the previous day before a complete account of it all had poured out upon her; yet it still had to be discussed. She had to ask questions, regret the recklessness, mourn the outcome, and both of them had to mention Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne knew she did not manage it as well as Lady Russell. She could not say his name and look directly into Lady Russell\u2019s eyes until she found the means of briefly telling her what she thought of the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
