          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"41a37b7da289f3f20ae0a92f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had, indeed, been entirely unaware of his own influence. He had gone after her into the shrubbery with no thought of exerting it. In his concern to discover how she endured Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, he had come with no selfish purpose\u2014no purpose beyond trying, should she give him the opportunity, to comfort or advise her.\u2014All that followed had been the work of the instant, the direct consequence of what he heard upon his feelings. The charming conviction of her complete indifference to Frank Churchill, of her heart being wholly free from him, had produced the hope that, in time, he might win her affection himself;\u2014yet it was not an immediate hope\u2014he had merely, in a moment when eagerness overcame judgment, ventured to ask whether she would not forbid his endeavour to attach her.\u2014The higher hopes that unfolded by degrees were all the more delightful.\u2014The affection which he had asked leave to awaken, if possible, was already his!\u2014In less than half an hour, he had risen from a state of utter distress to something so nearly perfect happiness that it could be called by no other name.\\n\\n_Her_ alteration was as great.\u2014That single half-hour had bestowed on both the same dear certainty of being loved, and had removed from both an equal measure of ignorance, jealousy, and distrust.\u2014On his part, there had been a jealousy of long duration, dating from Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival, or even from the expectation of it.\u2014He had loved Emma and been jealous of Frank Churchill from nearly the same time, one feeling probably having revealed the other to him. It was jealousy of Frank Churchill that had driven him from the country.\u2014The party at Box Hill had determined his departure. He would spare himself the sight of such attentions, permitted and encouraged, ever again.\u2014He had gone away to become indifferent.\u2014But he had chosen the wrong place. There was too much family happiness in his brother\u2019s home; woman appeared there in too engaging a shape; Isabella resembled Emma too closely\u2014differing only in those conspicuous inferiorities which invariably set the other before him in greater brilliance\u2014for much progress to be made, even if his stay had been longer.\u2014Still, he had remained resolutely, day after day\u2014until the post of that very morning brought the account of Jane Fairfax.\u2014Then, along with the satisfaction which he must feel\u2014nay, which he did not hesitate to feel, having never considered Frank Churchill at all worthy of Emma\u2014there was such tender concern, such acute anxiety for her, that he could remain no longer. He had ridden home in the rain, and, directly after dinner, walked up to learn how this sweetest and best of all beings, faultless despite all her faults, endured the revelation.\"}"
