          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f97a7d94ab61c58c3ecaae2f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"What sort of life could be theirs while Fate still kept this dreadful destiny poised in the balance? The marriage had to be delayed, whatever the cost. On that point he was firmly determined. Deeply as he loved the girl, and though the slightest touch of her hand, as they sat side by side, sent every nerve in him trembling with delicious happiness, he saw no less plainly where his duty lay, and understood fully that he had no right to marry before he had carried out the murder. Once that was done, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton and place his life in her keeping without any fear of guilt. Once that was done, he could clasp her in his arms, certain that she would never need to blush for him or bow her head in disgrace. Yet it had to be done first; and the sooner, the better for them both.\\n\\nMany men in his circumstances would have chosen the primrose path of dalliance rather than the hard ascent of duty; but Lord Arthur was too scrupulous to put pleasure before principle. His love contained more than passion alone; Sybil represented to him everything good and noble. For an instant he felt a natural horror of the deed demanded of him, but it quickly vanished. His heart assured him that it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason told him that no other path remained. He must choose between living for himself and living for others, and however dreadful the burden imposed upon him, he knew he must not allow selfishness to prevail over love. Sooner or later, all of us are summoned to make that same choice\u2014the same question is put to every one of us. It came to Lord Arthur early\u2014before the calculating cynicism of middle age had spoiled his nature, or the shallow, fashionable egotism of the day had eaten into his heart\u2014and he did not hesitate to do his duty. Happily, too, he was no dreamer or idle dilettante. Had he been, he would have faltered like Hamlet and allowed indecision to ruin his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him life signified action rather than reflection. He possessed the rarest of all gifts: common sense.\\n\\nBy now the wild and troubled emotions of the night before had wholly subsided, and he looked back almost shamefacedly on his frantic roaming from street to street and his violent anguish of feeling. The very genuineness of his suffering now made it appear unreal. He marvelled that he could have been foolish enough to rant and rave against the inevitable.\"}"
