istinction of manner, accomplishment, or intelligence. Lady Dalrymple had gained the reputation of \u201ca charming woman\u201d because she smiled and gave a polite reply to everyone. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she would never have been tolerated in Camden Place except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted that she had expected something better; but still, \u201cit was an acquaintance worth having\u201d; and when Anne ventured to give Mr Elliot her opinion of them, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, yet continued to insist that, as family connections, as good society, as people who would gather good society around them, they had value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of intelligent, well-informed people who have a great deal to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
