          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c29d4ca82ea5759c58a60032\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologised. He said, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, and, as you may imagine, was considered a very desirable match. Her half-brother had plenty of money again, but debts and fresh madness soon wasted it most terribly. There were greater differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father, and it is believed that he held a deep and deadly grudge against her for having influenced their father\u2019s anger. Now I come to the cruel part of the story\u2014only pausing, my dear Handel, to point out that a dinner napkin will not fit into a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was trying to force mine into my tumbler, I cannot possibly explain. I only know that I found myself, with persistence better suited to a worthier cause, making every effort to squeeze it into that small space. Again I thanked him and apologised, and again he said most cheerfully, \u201cNot at all, I am sure!\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014at the races, public balls, or wherever you please\u2014a certain man who courted Miss Havisham. I never saw him, for this happened twenty-five years ago, before you and I existed, Handel, but I have heard my father say that he was a flashy man, exactly the sort for such a purpose. Still, my father strongly insisted that no one, without ignorance or prejudice, could mistake him for a gentleman. My father believes that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart has ever, since the world began, been a true gentleman in manner. He says that no varnish can conceal the grain of wood, and that the more varnish is applied, the more clearly the grain shows through. Well, this man pursued Miss Havisham closely and claimed to be devoted to her. I believe she had shown little feeling before then, but whatever capacity for feeling she had came out at that time, and she loved 