          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7aa2c8252da5313f38bfbd30\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had come when I put the Manuscript down. The rain had nearly stopped; the world was gray and mournful, and the spent storm was sighing and sobbing itself into repose. I went to the stranger\u2019s chamber and listened at his door, which stood a little open. I heard his voice, and knocked. No answer came, yet the voice continued. I looked in. The man was lying on his back in bed, speaking in broken phrases but with animation, emphasizing them with his arms, which he flung about restlessly, as delirious sick folk do. I entered quietly and bent over him. His mutterings and exclamations continued. I spoke\u2014only a word, to attract his notice. His glassy eyes and ashen face kindled at once with pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome:\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you have come at last\u2014how I have yearned for you! Sit beside me\u2014do not leave me\u2014never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand?\u2014give it to me, dear, let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now everything is well, all is peace, and I am happy once more\u2014we are happy once more, are we not, Sandy? You are so dim, so shadowy, only a mist, a cloud; but you are here, and that is blessedness enough; and I have your hand; do not take it away\u2014it is only for a little while, I shall not need it long.... Was that the child?... Hello-Central!... she does not answer. Sleeping, perhaps? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and bid her good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you are there. I lost myself for a moment, and thought you had gone.... Have I been ill long? It must be so; it seems like months to me. And such dreams! such weird and dreadful dreams, Sandy! Dreams as real as reality\u2014delirium, of course, but so real! Why, I thought the king was dead, I thought you were in Gaul and could not return, I thought there had been a revolution; in the wild madness of those dreams, I thought that Clarence and I, with a handful of cadets, fought and destroyed the whole chivalry of England! Yet even that was not the strangest thing. I seemed to be a being from a far-off age yet unborn, centuries ahead, and even that was as real as all the rest! Yes, I seemed to have flown back from that age into ours, then forward into it again, and to have been left, a stranger and desolate, in that strange England, with an abyss of thirteen centuries gaping between me and you! between me and my home and friends! between me and all that I hold dear, all that could make life worth living!\"}"
