          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e6cb85b6f7b9c321b6253214\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was animated by the keen sensations of returning health; he felt that, as general of the Athenians, he occupied a station worthy of his ambition; and, in his expectation of conquering Constantinople, he looked forward to an event that would stand as a landmark amid the waste of ages, a deed without equal in the records of mankind: when a city of mighty historical associations, whose beautiful situation was the wonder of the world, and which for many centuries had been the stronghold of the Moslems, should be delivered from slavery and barbarism, and restored to a people renowned for genius, civilization, and the spirit of freedom. Perdita reposed upon his recovered companionship, his love, his hopes, and his fame, as a Sybarite upon a sumptuous couch; every thought was delight, every feeling steeped, as it were, in a kindred and balmy atmosphere.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on the 7th of July. Throughout our journey the weather had been clear. Every day, before sunrise, we quitted our nightly encampment, and watched the shadows withdraw from hill and vale before the golden glory of the advancing sun. The soldiers who accompanied us drew, with national liveliness, eager pleasure from the spectacle of lovely nature. The rising of the star of day was greeted with triumphant music, while birdsong, caught in fragments, filled the pauses between the strains. At noon, we set our tents in some shaded valley, or in an embowered mountain wood, where a brook murmuring over pebbles invited grateful sleep. Our evening march, quieter, was still more pleasing than the restless spirit of the morning. When the band played, they unconsciously selected airs of tempered passion; the lover's farewell, or a lament for absence, was succeeded and concluded by some solemn hymn, harmonizing with the peaceful beauty of evening and raising the soul to lofty and religious meditation. Often every sound was hushed, that we might hear the nightingale, while fireflies moved in brilliant measure, and the gentle cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travellers. Did we cross a valley? Soft shadows enclosed us, with rocks coloured in lovely tints. If we climbed a mountain, Greece, like a living map, lay spread below, her famous peaks dividing the ether, her rivers tracing silver threads across the fertile country. Almost fearful to draw breath, we English travellers gazed in ecstasy upon this magnificent prospect, so unlike the sober colouring and melancholy charms of our native landscape. When we left Macedonia, the rich yet low plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties; nevertheless, our journey remained interesting. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people were soon astir to pay honour to Lord Raymond.\"}"
