          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6eda00045833584f684b40a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that all? That is not much! Many people are under the Republic\u2019s displeasure, and must look out through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. Englishman. Which one is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Naturally, since I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has answered all the earlier questions. It is Jarvis Lorry who has stepped down and stands with one hand on the coach door, answering a group of officials. They slowly walk around the carriage and slowly climb onto the box to inspect the small amount of luggage on its roof. The country people gathered nearby press closer to the coach doors and stare greedily inside. A little child in its mother\u2019s arms has its small arm held out so that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, properly countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay one leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave. Go on, my postilions! Have a good journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.\u2014And the first danger is past!\u201d\\n\\nThese are Jarvis Lorry\u2019s words again, as he clasps his hands and looks upward. Terror fills the carriage; there is weeping, and the heavy breathing of the unconscious traveller.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not moving too slowly? Can they not be made to go faster?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would look like flight, my darling. I must not press them too hard; it would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back! See whether we are being followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, no one is following us.\u201d\\n\\nHouses in groups of two and three pass us by: lonely farms, ruined buildings, dye works, tanneries, and the like; open country and rows of leafless trees. Hard, uneven paving lies beneath us, with soft, deep mud on either side. Sometimes we drive into the mud at the edge of the road to avoid the stones that rattle and shake us; sometimes we become stuck there in ruts and boggy places. Then our impatient agony is so great that, in our frantic fear and haste, we want to get out and run\u2014hide\u2014do anything rather than stop.\\n\\nOut of the open country, and back among ruined buildings, lonely farms, dye works, tanneries, and the like; cottages in twos and threes, rows of leafless trees. Have these men tricked us and brought us back by another road? Is this not the same place all over again? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back! See whether we are being followed! Hush! The posting-house.\\n\\nSlowly, our four horses are taken out. Slowly, the coach stands in the little street without horses, seeming unlikely ever to move again. Slowly, the new horses appear, one at a time. Slowly, the new postilions follow, sucking and braiding the lashes of their whips. Slowly, the old postilions count their money, add it wrongly, and reach unhappy totals.\"}"
