          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"55e349b98e6bdce6787eec68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have belonged to that very part of Mr Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cwhich has always awakened my especial curiosity. It must have been at the same period when he became acquainted with my father and sister. I never knew him myself; I merely heard of him; but there was something in his behaviour then, towards my father and sister, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, which I could never entirely reconcile with the present. It appeared to proclaim a different kind of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d exclaimed Mrs Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I knew him, but I heard him talk of them continually. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he would not go. Perhaps I can enlighten you on matters you would scarcely expect; and as for his marriage, I knew everything about it at the time. I was aware of all the arguments for and against it; I was the friend in whom he placed his hopes and schemes; and though I had not known his wife beforehand\u2014her lower station in society, indeed, made that impossible\u2014I knew her throughout all her later life, or at least until within the final two years of it, and can answer whatever question you may wish to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular question to put concerning her. I have always understood that they were not a happy pair. But I should like to learn why, at that point in his life, he should have slighted my father\u2019s acquaintance as he did. My father was certainly inclined to show him very kind and proper attention. Why did Mr Elliot withdraw?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr Elliot,\u201d answered Mrs Smith, \u201cat that time of his life, had a single aim: to make his fortune, and by a somewhat swifter means than the law. He was resolved to make it through marriage. He was resolved, at least, not to spoil it by an imprudent marriage; and I know that he believed\u2014whether rightly or wrongly, I cannot of course determine\u2014that your father and sister, in their attentions and invitations, intended a match between the heir and the young lady, and such a match could not possibly have suited his notions of wealth and independence. That, I assure you, was why he drew back. He told me the entire history. He kept nothing from me. It was curious that, having only just left you behind in Bath, my first and chief acquaintance after marrying should be your cousin; and that through him I should be constantly hearing of your father and sister.\"}"
