{"request_id":"cb860c8686c6143e123f9a2e","rewritten_text":"“I reasoned as follows—a posteriori. The murderers escaped through one of these windows. If that was so, they could not have refastened the sashes from inside, since they were found secured; this obvious point was what stopped the police from investigating this aspect further. Yet the sashes were secured. Therefore, they must be capable of fastening themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed casement, removed the nail with some difficulty, and tried to raise the sash. It resisted every effort, exactly as I expected. There must be a hidden spring; and this confirmation of my idea convinced me that my premises, at least, were correct, however mysterious the matter of the nails still remained. A careful search soon revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with the discovery, did not raise the sash.

“I replaced the nail and examined it closely. Someone leaving through this window could have shut it again, and the spring would have caught—but the nail could not have been put back. The conclusion was clear, and the scope of my inquiry narrowed again. The assassins must have escaped through the other window. If, then, the springs on both sashes were alike, as was likely, there had to be some difference between the nails, or at least in how they were fixed. Climbing onto the bedstead’s sacking, I examined the second casement closely over the headboard. Running my hand down behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring, which was, as I had supposed, identical to its neighbor. I then examined the nail. It was as stout as the other and seemed to be fitted in the same way—driven in almost to its head.

“You may say I was puzzled; but if you do, you have misunderstood the nature of my deductions. To use a sporting expression, I had not once been ‘at fault.’ I had never lost the scent for an instant. There was no break in any link of the chain. I had followed the secret to its final result—and that result was the nail. In every respect, I say, it looked like its counterpart in the other window; but that fact was utterly meaningless, however conclusive it appeared, beside the consideration that here, at this point, the clue ended. ‘There must be something wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head, along with about a quarter-inch of the shank, came away in my fingers."}

