{"request_id":"751457871e75043d70691e62","rewritten_text":"At last, we lightened the box by taking out two-thirds of its contents, and then, with some difficulty, managed to lift it from the hole. We placed the articles we had removed among the brambles and left the dog to guard them, under strict orders from Jupiter not, on any excuse, to leave the spot or make a sound until we returned. We then hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely at one in the morning, though only after exhausting labor. Worn out as we were, we could do nothing more at once. We rested until two and ate supper; immediately afterward, we set out again for the hills, carrying three sturdy sacks that, fortunately, were on the premises. A little before four, we reached the pit, divided what remained of the treasure as evenly as possible among ourselves, and, leaving the holes unfilled, started back to the hut. There, for the second time, we set down our golden burdens just as the first pale streaks of dawn appeared above the treetops in the east.

We were now completely exhausted, but the intense excitement of the moment would not let us rest. After an uneasy sleep of three or four hours, we rose, as though by prior agreement, to examine our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the brim, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night inspecting its contents. There had been no order or arrangement; everything had been thrown in together. Once we had carefully sorted it all, we discovered that we possessed even greater wealth than we had first imagined. In coins alone, there was a little more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, judging the pieces' value as accurately as we could from the tables of the period. There was not a trace of silver. Everything was gold, old and remarkably varied: French, Spanish, and German coins, along with a few English guineas and some counters unlike any we had ever seen. Several coins were very large and heavy, but so worn that we could make nothing of their inscriptions. There was no American money. We found the jewels harder to value. There were diamonds, some extraordinarily large and fine—one hundred and ten in all, not one of them small; eighteen rubies of exceptional brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, along with an opal. All these stones had been torn from their settings and tossed loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we picked out from among the other gold, seemed to have been hammered flat, as though to prevent their identification."}

