          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"31897f993e7c6d39b23d151c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If it had not been for me, he would never have gotten out. Morgan le Fay hated him with all her heart, and she would never have relented toward him. Yet his offense had been committed through carelessness rather than intentional wickedness. He had said that she had red hair. Well, she did; but that was not the proper way to put it. When red-haired people belong to a sufficiently high social class, their hair is called auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: among those forty-seven prisoners were five whose names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known. One woman and four men\u2014all stooped, wrinkled, mindless old patriarchs. They themselves had forgotten those particulars long before; or at least they had only vague notions of them, nothing certain, and nothing they told the same way twice. The line of priests whose duty it had been to pray daily with the prisoners, remind them that God had placed them there for some wise purpose, and teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were what He most loved in persons of lower rank, possessed traditions concerning these poor human wrecks, but nothing beyond that. Those traditions did not go far, for they dealt only with the duration of the imprisonment, not with the names of the crimes. Even with tradition's help, all that could be established was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer they had endured that deprivation could not be guessed. The king and queen knew nothing of these poor beings except that they were heirlooms, assets inherited with the throne from the previous owners. No account of their history had passed along with them, so their new owners considered them worthless and took no interest in them. I said to the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why on earth didn't you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question baffled her. She did not know why she had not; the idea had simply never entered her mind. Thus, without realizing it, she was foreshadowing the actual history of future prisoners in the Ch\u00e2teau d'If. It was clear to me now that, given her upbringing, these inherited prisoners were simply property\u2014nothing more and nothing less. When we inherit property, after all, it does not occur to us to throw it away, even if we place no value on it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats up into the open world and the blazing afternoon sunlight\u2014having first blindfolded them, out of mercy for eyes so long untroubled by light\u2014they were a sight to behold. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pitiful horrors, every one of them; the most legitimate possible children of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
