          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bda68430043722c3aec2f27f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet there was no cheerfulness in the small town on that same peaceful Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s household were being clothed in mourning, amid deep sorrow and abundant tears. A strange stillness lay over the village, though it was quiet enough at the best of times. The townspeople went about their business absent-mindedly and spoke but little; yet they sighed frequently. The Saturday holiday weighed upon the children. They felt no spirit for their games, and by degrees abandoned them.\\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher found herself wandering sadly about the empty schoolyard, feeling very forlorn. But she discovered nothing there that could console her. She said to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if only I had a brass andiron knob again! But I haven\u2019t anything now to remember him by.\u201d And she swallowed a small sob.\\n\\nAfter a while she paused and told herself:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for all the world. But he is gone now; I shall never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThe thought overcame her, and she drifted away with tears streaming down her face. Soon a little company of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014passed by and stood peering over the picket fence, speaking reverently of how Tom had done this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe had said some trifling thing (full of dreadful prophecy, as they could plainly perceive now!)\u2014and every speaker indicated the precise place where the missing boys had stood, then added something such as, \u201cand I was standing just so\u2014just as I am now, and if you was him, I was that close\u2014and he smiled this way\u2014and then something went all over me, like\u2014awful, you know\u2014and naturally I never guessed what it meant, but I can see it now!\u201d\\n\\nThen they argued over who had seen the dead boys last while alive, and many laid claim to that mournful honor, producing proofs more or less altered by the witness; and when it was finally settled who really had seen the departed last and spoken the final words with them, the fortunate ones assumed a kind of holy consequence, while all the others stared at them and envied them. One poor fellow, having no other distinction to present, remarked with fairly obvious pride at the recollection:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer licked me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that claim to glory did not succeed. Most of the boys could say the same, and that made the distinction far too common. The group slowly moved away, still recounting memories of the lost heroes in hushed, awestruck voices.\"}"
