          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6431a51b45df26862b13abe2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI now come quickly to the more touching part of my story. I will tell of events that filled me with feelings which, compared with what I once was, have made me what I am.\\n\\n\u201cSpring came on rapidly; the weather grew pleasant and the skies clear. I was amazed that what had once seemed barren and gloomy now blossomed with the loveliest flowers and green plants. My senses were pleased and renewed by a thousand delightful scents and a thousand beautiful sights.\\n\\n\u201cOn one of these days, when my cottagers took their usual rest from work\u2014the old man played his guitar while the children listened\u2014I noticed that Felix\u2019s face was sad beyond words. He sighed often, and once his father stopped playing. From his manner, I guessed that he asked why his son was unhappy. Felix answered in a cheerful voice, and the old man was about to begin playing again when someone knocked at the door.\\n\\n\u201cIt was a lady on horseback, with a countryman guiding her. She wore dark clothes and was covered by a heavy black veil. Agatha asked her a question, but the stranger answered only by saying Felix\u2019s name in a sweet accent. Her voice was musical, yet unlike the voices of either of my friends. At the sound of his name, Felix hurried to her. When she saw him, she lifted her veil, and I saw a face of angelic beauty and expression. Her hair was shining raven-black and carefully braided; her eyes were dark but gentle, though lively; her features were regular, and her skin wonderfully fair, with each cheek touched by a lovely pink.\\n\\n\u201cFelix seemed overcome with delight at seeing her. Every sign of sorrow disappeared from his face, which at once showed such joyful happiness that I could scarcely have believed it possible. His eyes shone and his cheeks flushed with pleasure; at that moment, I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. She seemed moved by different feelings. After wiping a few tears from her lovely eyes, she held out her hand to Felix. He kissed it passionately and called her, as well as I could make out, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him, but she smiled. He helped her down from her horse, sent away her guide, and led her into the cottage. He spoke for some time with his father, and the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet and tried to kiss his hand. But he lifted her up and embraced her warmly.\\n\\n\u201cI soon saw that although the stranger made clear sounds and seemed to have a language of her own, neither she nor the cottagers could understand one another.\"}"
