          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8611b64780f89d2b8d685a5d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was in all this a strangeness that drew and delighted me. He seemed, as it were, newly roused from his long and visionary slumber, and felt somewhat like one of the seven sleepers, or like Nourjahad in that charming imitation of an eastern story: Diana had vanished; his friends were altered or dead, and, on awakening, I was all that remained for him to love upon earth.\\n\\nHow precious to me became the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond, now that I possessed so dear a companion in my wanderings. With my father I explored every lovely place, whether upon the islands or beside the waterfalls shaded by trees; every dark path and every dingle tangled with fern and underwood. His conversation expanded my thoughts. I felt as though I had been made anew, surrounded by all the freshness and animation of a new existence: since his coming, I seemed transported from a confined portion of earth into a universe without bounds for imagination or understanding. Before, my life had resembled a pleasant country stream, never meant to quit its native meadows, but, when its office was done, quietly to sink away and leave no mark. Now it appeared a varied river, passing through a rich and beautiful landscape, always changing and always fair. Alas! I did not know the desert it was soon to enter; the rocks that would rend its waters, and the dreadful prospect that would be mirrored, more distorted still, in its waves. Life was then radiant; I was beginning to learn hope\u2014and what brings more bitter despair to the heart than hope overthrown?\\n\\nIs it not strange that sorrow should so swiftly succeed happiness so divine? I drank from an enchanted cup, yet gall lay beneath its prolonged sweetness. My heart overflowed with profound affection, but by reason of its very depth and fullness it was tranquil. I had never imagined that love could give rise to misery, and this lesson, which all must finally learn, was imparted to me in a manner few are compelled to endure. I mourn now, and must always mourn, those few brief months of paradisaical bliss; I broke no command, I tasted no apple, yet was pitilessly expelled from it. Alas! my companion did, and in his fall I too was cast down. But I stray from my narrative\u2014let grief arrive in its appointed hour; at this point in my tale I may yet speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months went by in this delightful communion, when my aunt became ill. I spent an entire month in her room tending her, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for a time. Death is so terrible to those who live; the bonds of habit are so powerful, even where affection does not unite them, that the heart must suffer agony when they are broken.\"}"
