          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ca684745b26f0c29209647b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe and the children could stay here perfectly well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, papa\u2014that is what you have never been able to manage, and I do not think you ever will. Isabella cannot bear to be apart from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThis was too true to deny. Though he disliked it, Mr. Woodhouse could only sigh in resignation; and Emma, seeing that the thought of his daughter\u2019s attachment to her husband had lowered his spirits, at once turned to a part of the subject that would cheer him.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must spend as much time with us as she can while my brother and sister are here. I am sure she will be delighted with the children. We are very proud of the children, are we not, papa? I wonder which she will think more handsome, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I wonder which she will. Poor little dears, how happy they will be to come. They are very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I am sure I do not know anyone who is not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John is very like his mama. Henry is the eldest; he was named after me, not after his father. John, the second, is named after his father. Some people are surprised, I believe, that the eldest was not named after his father, but Isabella wanted him called Henry, which I thought very pretty of her. And he is a very clever boy indeed. They are all remarkably clever, and have so many charming little ways. They come and stand beside my chair and say, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?\u2019 Once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were made only for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them very often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are so very gentle yourself. But if you could compare him with other fathers, you would not think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and strong, and if they misbehave, he may speak sharply to them now and then; but he is an affectionate father\u2014Mr. John Knightley is certainly an affectionate father. The children are all fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and tosses them up to the ceiling in a most frightening way!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they like it, papa; there is nothing they enjoy more. They enjoy it so much that, if their uncle did not insist that they take turns, whichever one began would never let the other have a turn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I cannot understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true of all of us, papa. One half of the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other.\u201d\"}"
