          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"863351616f7fd88732ad89ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well past noon when he woke. His valet had slipped into the room several times on tiptoe to see whether he was stirring, wondering why his young master was sleeping so late. At last the bell rang, and Victor entered quietly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters on a small tray of old S\u00e8vres china. He drew back the olive-satin curtains, lined with shimmering blue, that covered the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept well this morning,\u201d he said with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked sleepily.\\n\\n\u201cOne fifteen, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He sat up and, after sipping his tea, began sorting through his letters. One was from Lord Henry and had been delivered by hand that morning. He hesitated a moment, then set it aside. He opened the others without interest. They contained the usual assortment of cards, dinner invitations, tickets to private viewings, programmes for charity concerts, and similar things showered every morning during the season upon fashionable young men. There was a rather substantial bill for an engraved silver Louis-Quinze toilet set, which he had not yet dared send to his guardians. They were extremely old-fashioned people and did not understand that we live in an age when unnecessary things are our only necessities. There were also several very politely phrased letters from Jermyn Street moneylenders, offering to advance him any amount at a moment\u2019s notice and at the most reasonable rates of interest.\\n\\nAfter about ten minutes, he rose and, putting on an elaborate dressing-gown of silk-embroidered cashmere, went into the bathroom with its onyx floor. The cool water revived him after his long sleep. He seemed to have forgotten everything he had endured. Once or twice, a vague feeling came over him that he had played a part in some strange tragedy, but it had the unreality of a dream.\\n\\nAs soon as he was dressed, he went into the library and sat down to a light French breakfast laid out on a small round table beside the open window. It was a beautiful day. The warm air seemed heavy with spices. A bee flew in and buzzed around the blue-dragon bowl, filled with sulphur-yellow roses, that stood before him. He felt completely happy.\\n\\nThen his eyes fell on the screen he had placed before the portrait, and he started.\\n\\n\u201cIs Monsieur too cold?\u201d his valet asked, setting an omelette on the table. \u201cShall I close the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI am not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
