          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e8520ad95ba89eaa24e32497\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThe shock and bewilderment this extraordinary reversal of circumstances produced in my mind was perhaps, after all, the part of the adventure least open to explanation. For the change itself was not only natural and inevitable, but had long been anticipated as something bound to occur whenever I reached that precise point in my journey at which the planet\u2019s attraction would be overcome by that of the satellite\u2014or, more exactly, when the balloon\u2019s pull toward the earth became weaker than its pull toward the moon. Certainly, I woke from a deep sleep, my senses wholly confused, to confront a most startling phenomenon\u2014one that, though expected, had not been expected at that particular moment. The reversal itself must, of course, have happened gently and gradually, and it is by no means certain that, even had I been awake when it occurred, I would have noticed it through any inward sign of inversion\u2014that is, through any discomfort or disorder affecting either myself or my equipment.\\n\\n\u201cIt is scarcely necessary to say that, once I fully understood my situation and emerged from the terror that had consumed every faculty of my soul, my attention was first directed entirely toward the moon\u2019s general physical appearance. It lay beneath me like a map; and although I believed it was still at a considerable distance, the irregularities of its surface appeared with a striking and wholly inexplicable clarity. The complete absence of ocean or sea\u2014indeed, of any lake, river, or body of water whatever\u2014struck me at first sight as the most remarkable feature of its geological condition. Yet, strangely enough, I saw vast level tracts that were unmistakably alluvial, though by far the greater part of the visible hemisphere was covered with countless volcanic mountains, conical in form and more like artificial constructions than natural elevations. The tallest of them rises no more than three and three-quarter miles vertically; but a map of the volcanic regions of the Campi Phlegraei would give your Excellencies a better notion of their general character than any inadequate description I might attempt. Most were plainly erupting, and revealed their fury and power to me through the repeated thunder of the wrongly named meteoric stones, which now shot upward past the balloon with ever more terrifying frequency.\u201d\"}"
