          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f1eb121ee0cb679c9793397\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, then, the ring-master made a short speech, saying he hoped there wouldn\u2019t be any disturbance, and that if the man promised not to cause more trouble, he would let him ride, provided he believed he could keep on the horse. So everybody laughed and said that was all right, and the man climbed aboard. The instant he was up, the horse began to tear around, bucking and jumping and cutting up, with two circus fellows clinging to the bridle and trying to restrain him, while the drunk man held to his neck, his heels kicking into the air at every leap, and the whole crowd on its feet, yelling and laughing till the tears ran down. At last, in spite of all the circus men could do, the horse got free and tore away like fury, circling the ring, with that sot sprawled over him and gripping his neck, first one leg nearly dragging the ground on one side, then the other on the other side, and the people going wild. It wasn\u2019t amusing to me, though; I was shaking all over at the danger he was in. But before long he worked himself astride and caught the bridle, swaying this way and that; then, the next minute, he sprang upright, let go the bridle, and stood there!\u2014and the horse was going like a house afire, too. He stood there sailing round as easy and at home as if he had never been drunk a day in his life\u2014and then he started stripping off his clothes and flinging them away. He threw them off so fast they seemed to clog the air, and in all he shed seventeen suits. Then there he was, slender and handsome, dressed in the gaudiest, prettiest outfit you ever saw; and he went at that horse with his whip and made him fairly hum\u2014then at last he jumped down, bowed, and danced away to the dressing-room, while everybody howled with delight and amazement.\\n\\nThen the ring-master saw how he had been fooled, and he was the sickest ring-master I ever expect to see. Why, it was one of his own men! He had planned that whole joke himself and never breathed a word of it to anybody. Well, I felt foolish enough at being taken in that way, but I wouldn\u2019t have traded places with that ring-master for a thousand dollars. I don\u2019t know; maybe there are better circuses than that one, but I never came across them. Anyhow, it was more than good enough for me; and wherever I meet with it, it can have all my custom every time.\"}"
