          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb4d0ad9455cd2c2aca38b49\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, I took you wrongly. I thought you said \u2018anybody,\u2019 and therefore I imagined \u2018anybody\u2019 meant--well, anybody; that is to say, everybody.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt doth--anybody of exalted birth; and so much the better if he be royal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat, meseemeth, might very well be,\u201d said the abbot, perceiving his chance to make peace and prevent disaster, \u201cfor it would scarcely be likely that a gift so marvellous as this should be bestowed to reveal the affairs of beings less important than those born close to the heights of greatness. Our King Arthur--\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould you hear of him?\u201d the enchanter interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cMost gladly, indeed, and thankfully.\u201d\\n\\nAt once everybody was brimming with awe and curiosity again, the hopeless fools. They followed the incantations with rapt attention, and when the proclamation came, turned toward me with an air of, \u201cThere, then, what have you to say now?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe king is fatigued by the chase, and hath lain these two hours in his palace, asleep without dreams.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s blessing be upon him!\u201d said the abbot, crossing himself; \u201cmay that slumber refresh both his body and his soul.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd it might, if he were asleep,\u201d I said, \u201cbut the king is not asleep; the king is riding.\u201d\\n\\nThere was trouble again--a clash of authorities. No one knew which of us to credit; I still retained a little standing. The magician\u2019s contempt was aroused, and he said:\\n\\n\u201cLo, in all my days I have beheld many wondrous seers and prophets and magicians, yet never one before who could sit doing nothing and look into the heart of matters without a single incantation to aid him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have dwelt in the woods, and suffered much loss by it. I employ incantations myself, as this worthy brotherhood know--but only upon occasions of consequence.\u201d\\n\\nWhen it comes to sarcasm, I reckon I can hold my own. That thrust made the fellow writhe. The abbot asked concerning the queen and court, and received this answer:\\n\\n\u201cThey are all asleep, overcome with weariness, even as is the king.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cThat is simply another falsehood. Half of them are at their diversions; the queen and the other half are not sleeping, but riding. Now perhaps you can exert yourself a little, and tell us where the king and queen, and all those riding with them at this moment, are bound?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey sleep now, as I have said; but to-morrow they will ride, for they journey toward the sea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where shall they be at vespers the day after to-morrow?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFar north of Camelot, with half their journey accomplished.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is another lie, by a matter of a hundred and fifty miles.\u201d\"}"
