          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dd7b4afc84f271bbfb71113\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI examined the death\u2019s-head carefully. Its outer lines\u2014the lines nearest the edge of the vellum\u2014were much clearer than the rest. It was obvious that the heat had acted imperfectly or unevenly. I immediately lit a fire and held every part of the parchment to a strong heat. At first, the only result was that the faint lines of the skull grew darker; but as I continued the experiment, a figure appeared in the corner of the slip, diagonally opposite the place where the death\u2019s-head was drawn. At first I took it for a goat. On closer examination, however, I saw that it was meant to be a kid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d I said. \u201cOf course, I have no right to laugh at you\u2014a million and a half in money is too serious a matter for joking\u2014but surely you are not going to add a third link to your chain. You will not find any special connection between pirates and a goat. Pirates, you know, have nothing to do with goats; goats belong to farming.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I have just told you that the figure was not a goat.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, a kid, then\u2014much the same thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMuch the same, but not exactly,\u201d said Legrand. \u201cYou may have heard of a certain Captain Kidd. I immediately saw the animal\u2019s figure as a sort of punning or symbolic signature. I call it a signature because its place on the vellum suggested that idea. The death\u2019s-head in the diagonally opposite corner had, in the same way, the appearance of a stamp or seal. But I was greatly troubled by the absence of anything else\u2014of the body for my imagined document, of the text to go with its context.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you expected to find a letter between the stamp and the signature.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSomething like that. The truth is, I felt an irresistible sense that some great good fortune was about to come to me. I can hardly say why. Perhaps it was more a wish than a real belief; but do you know that Jupiter\u2019s foolish words about the bug being solid gold had a remarkable effect on my imagination? Then there was the chain of accidents and coincidences\u2014they were so extraordinary. Do you see how purely accidental it was that these events happened on the one day of the year when it had been, or might be, cool enough for a fire? Without the fire, or without the dog\u2019s intervention at the exact moment he appeared, I would never have discovered the death\u2019s-head, and therefore never become the owner of the treasure.\u201d\"}"
