          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aed0c618072b6d516d10eabf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don't spring upon him suddenly and run the chance of making a clumsy, half-done affair of it; no, I move well off to one side and edge up on him by degrees, till he never dreams I mean to strike him at all; then, presently, in a twinkling, there he is stretched on his back, unable for the life of him to make out how it came about. That was how I tackled brother Dowley. I began speaking in an easy, indolent fashion, as though I were merely talking to while away the time; and the oldest man alive could not have got the direction of my starting-point and guessed where I meant to land:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a great many odd things about law, and custom, and usage, and matters of that kind, when you examine them; yes, and about the course and advancement of human opinion and action, too. There are laws that are written\u2014they pass away; but there are unwritten laws also\u2014they are everlasting. Consider the unwritten law of wages: it declares that they must rise, little by little, down through the centuries. And observe its working. We know what wages are now, here, there, and yonder; we take an average and call that the wages of to-day. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and what they were two hundred years ago; that is as far back as we can reach, but it is enough to show us the law of progress, the amount and pace of the recurring increase; and thus, without a paper to aid us, we can come quite near finding what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Very well, thus far. Do we halt there? No. We cease looking behind us; we turn about and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people's wages will be on any future date you care to name, for hundreds upon hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Seven hundred years hence wages will have climbed to six times what they are now in this part of the country, and farm laborers will be permitted 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I might die now and live then!\u201d broke in Smug, the wheelwright, with a splendid greedy gleam in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that is not all; they will have their board besides\u2014such as it may be: it will not make them fat. Two hundred and fifty years afterward\u2014mark this now\u2014a mechanic's wages will be\u2014remember, this is law, not mere guessing; a mechanic's wages will then be twenty cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nA general breath of astonished awe went up, and Dickon the mason murmured, his eyes and hands uplifted:\"}"
