{"request_id":"e29d4e811a48b35801bff21d","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they discussed the ghost at considerable length. The United States Minister was naturally somewhat annoyed to discover that his gift had not been accepted. “I have no desire,” he said, “to cause the ghost any personal harm, and I must say that, considering how long he has lived in this house, it is not at all polite to throw pillows at him”—a very reasonable observation, at which, I regret to say, the twins burst into loud laughter. “On the other hand,” he continued, “if he truly refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall have to take away his chains. It would be quite impossible to sleep with that kind of noise outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, however, they were left undisturbed. The only thing that drew any notice was the continual reappearance of the bloodstain on the library floor. This was certainly very strange, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and kept the windows securely barred. The stain’s chameleon-like changes of colour also caused a great deal of comment. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it would become vermilion, then a deep purple; and once, when they came downstairs for family prayers according to the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it bright emerald green. These kaleidoscopic transformations naturally amused the family greatly, and every evening they freely placed bets on the matter. The only person who did not join in the joke was little Virginia, who, for some unexplained reason, was always deeply troubled by the sight of the bloodstain, and nearly cried on the morning it was emerald green.

The ghost appeared for the second time on Sunday night. Soon after they had gone to bed, they were suddenly startled by a terrible crash in the hall. Rushing downstairs, they found that a large suit of old armour had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. Seated in a high-backed chair was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense pain on his face. The twins, who had brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him, with the accuracy of aim that can be achieved only through long and careful practice on a writing-master. Meanwhile, the United States Minister covered him with his revolver and, in accordance with Californian etiquette, ordered him to put up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a wild shriek of rage and passed through them like mist, putting out Washington Otis’s candle as he went and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

