          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"04ff5bdd0fffb4a85fdec47b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She was right about the rats. Every so often, you could see one poke his nose out of a hole in the corner. She said she had to keep things close by to throw at them when she was alone, or they would give her no peace. She showed me a bar of lead twisted into a knot, and said she was usually a good shot with it, but she had strained her arm a day or two before and did not know if she could throw straight now. Still, she waited for a chance, and soon threw at a rat; but she missed him by a long way and said, \u201cOuch!\u201d because her arm hurt so badly. Then she told me to try at the next one. I wanted to get away before the old man came back, but of course I did not show it. I took the thing, and when the first rat showed his nose, I threw it hard; and if he had stayed where he was, he would have been a pretty sick rat. She said that was first-rate, and reckoned I would get the next one. She went and picked up the lump of lead and brought it back, along with a hank of yarn she wanted me to help her with. I held up both hands, and she put the yarn over them, talking all the while about her own affairs and her husband\u2019s. But then she stopped to say:\\n\\n\u201cKeep watching the rats. You\u2019d better keep the lead in your lap, handy.\u201d\\n\\nSo she dropped the lump into my lap right then, and I clapped my legs together over it while she went on talking. But only for about a minute. Then she took the yarn off and looked me straight in the face, very pleasant-like, and said:\\n\\n\u201cCome now, what is your real name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWh\u2014what, ma\u2019am?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is your real name? Is it Bill, or Tom, or Bob\u2014or what?\u201d\\n\\nI reckon I shook like a leaf, and hardly knew what to do. But I said:\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t make fun of a poor girl like me, ma\u2019am. If I\u2019m in your way here, I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, you won\u2019t. Sit down and stay where you are. I am not going to hurt you, and I am not going to tell on you either. Just tell me your secret and trust me. I\u2019ll keep it; and more than that, I\u2019ll help you. So will my old man, if you want him to. You see, you\u2019re a runaway apprentice, that\u2019s all. It is nothing. There is no harm in it. You were treated badly, and you decided to run away. Bless you, child, I would not tell on you.\"}"
