{"request_id":"f0a543d889700539b7fee794","rewritten_text":"The stars were yet shining clear when I awoke, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, told me that midnight had come. I started from troubled dreams. I thought that I had been bidden to Timon’s final banquet; I arrived ravenous, the dishes were uncovered, the hot water rose in its barren vapours, and I fled from the wrath of the host, who wore Raymond’s form. To my sick imagination, the vessels he flung after me were laden with noisome fumes, while my friend’s figure, changed by a thousand monstrous shapes, swelled into a vast phantom, marked upon its brow with pestilence. The enlarging shade rose ever higher, filling, then seeming to strive to break beyond, the adamantine vault which arched above, upholding and enclosing the world. The nightmare grew into torment; by a violent effort I cast off sleep, and restored reason to her accustomed office. My first thought was of Perdita; I must return to her, must sustain her, drawing from despair such nourishment as could best uphold her stricken heart; must call her back from grief’s wild extravagance through the stern laws of duty and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my sole direction. I turned away from the dreadful wreck of the Golden City, and, after much labour, managed to free myself from its bounds. Beyond the walls I encountered a band of soldiers; from one I borrowed a horse, and hurried to my sister. In that brief time the aspect of the plain had altered; the camp had been dispersed; remnants of the broken army gathered in little groups here and there; every countenance was overcast, and every motion expressed amazement and alarm.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, and paused, afraid to go forward, to speak, or even to look. Perdita was in the centre of the hall; seated on the marble floor, her head drooped upon her breast, her hair in disorder, her fingers ceaselessly interlaced. She was white as marble, and agony had drawn every feature. She saw me and raised her eyes in question; that faint look of hope was itself misery. The words expired before I could utter them; I felt a hideous smile crease my lips. She read my gesture; once more her head sank, and once more her fingers moved restlessly. At length I found speech, but my voice frightened her; the unhappy girl had understood my expression, and for all the world would not have her grievous misery given shape and certainty by harsh, irrevocable words. Indeed, she seemed anxious to turn my thoughts away from it: she rose from the pavement. “Hush!” she whispered; “after weeping long, Clara is asleep; we must not wake her.”"}

