          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0492b5901cc1f640b9e7e4a7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to think, and hardly knowing what she felt, Elizabeth finished the letter, immediately seized the other, and opened it with desperate impatience. It had been written a day after the first.\\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received my hurried letter. I hope this one may be clearer, but though I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to write sensibly. Dearest Lizzy, I scarcely know what to say, but I have bad news for you, and it cannot wait. However unwise a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now anxious to know that it has happened, for there is far too much reason to fear they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, only a few hours after the express. Lydia\u2019s short letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe they were going to Gretna Green, but Denny let fall something that showed he believed W. never meant to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel F., who was immediately alarmed and set out from B., intending to follow their route. He traced them easily as far as Clapham, but no farther. When they arrived there, they got into a hired coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known after that is that they were seen continuing along the London road. I do not know what to think. After making every possible inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. came into Hertfordshire, asking anxiously at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success\u2014no such people had been seen passing through. With the greatest kindness, he came to Longbourn and told us of his fears in a way that does great credit to his heart. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F., but no blame can be placed on them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father 