{"request_id":"17026c291803d18f887b5e76","rewritten_text":"It seemed to me that this odd lie was told in the simplest and prettiest way; but I had heard it only once, and that makes a difference. No doubt the others had enjoyed it when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first, and soon woke the rest with a practical joke of very poor quality. He tied metal mugs to a dog's tail and let him go. The dog raced wildly around the place in terror, while all the other dogs howled after him, knocking into and crashing against everything in their path, until there was complete confusion and a terrible racket. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces; some tumbled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in delight. They were exactly like children. Sir Dinadan was so pleased with his trick that he kept telling, over and over, until everyone was tired of it, how the wonderful idea had come to him. And, as humorists of his kind always do, he was still laughing after everyone else had stopped. He was so puffed up that he decided to make a speech—naturally, a humorous speech. I do not think I had ever heard so many worn-out old jokes tied together in my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It was especially sad to sit there, thirteen hundred years before I was born, listening again to poor, stale, worm-eaten jokes that had given me dry gripes when I was a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me that no new joke is possible. Everybody laughed at these ancient things—but they always do; I had noticed that in later centuries. Of course, the scoffer did not laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there was nothing he would not scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan's jokes were rotten, and the rest were petrified. I said “petrified” was good, because I myself believed that the only proper way to classify the grand ages of some of those jokes was by geological periods. But that clever thought struck the boy in an empty spot, since geology had not been invented yet. Still, I made a note of it and planned to educate the commonwealth up to it if I survived. There is no sense in wasting a good thing just because the market is not ready for it.

Now Sir Kay got up and began to turn his history-mill on me for fuel."}

