          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5ec8f72790ac0d4b6007f571\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Great God! If I had for one moment understood the terrible purpose of my monstrous enemy, I would rather have left my native country forever and wandered the earth as a friendless exile than agree to this wretched marriage. But the creature seemed to have bewitched me, blinding me to his true intentions; and while I believed I was preparing only for my own death, I was rushing toward the death of someone far more dear to me.\\n\\nAs the date set for our marriage came closer, whether from cowardice or some warning feeling, my heart began to fail me. Yet I hid my feelings beneath an appearance of cheerfulness, which brought smiles and happiness to my father\u2019s face but scarcely fooled Elizabeth\u2019s watchful, perceptive eye. She looked forward to our marriage with calm satisfaction, though mixed with a little fear. Past misfortunes had taught her that happiness, however certain and real it seemed, might quickly vanish like a dream, leaving behind only deep and lasting regret.\\n\\nPreparations were made, visitors came to offer congratulations, and everything appeared cheerful. As best I could, I locked away the anxiety that tormented me and took part, with apparent interest, in my father\u2019s plans, though they might serve only as decorations for my tragedy. Through my father\u2019s efforts, the Austrian government had restored part of Elizabeth\u2019s inheritance to her. She owned a small property on the shores of Lake Como. We agreed that immediately after our marriage we would go to Villa Lavenza and spend our first happy days beside the beautiful lake near it.\\n\\nMeanwhile, I took every precaution to protect myself if the fiend attacked me openly. I always carried pistols and a dagger, and remained constantly alert against any trick. These measures gave me some peace of mind. Indeed, as the time approached, the threat began to seem like a delusion, too insignificant to disturb my peace. At the same time, the happiness I expected from my marriage seemed more certain as the wedding day drew near, and I continually heard it discussed as an event that no accident could possibly prevent.\\n\\nElizabeth seemed happy, and my calm manner did much to ease her mind. But on the day meant to fulfill my hopes and decide my fate, she was sad, troubled by a sense that evil was near. Perhaps she also thought of the dreadful secret I had promised to tell her the next day.\"}"
