          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"059e7dff3c2f4bc4d6528c13\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I encountered the curious stranger I am about to describe. Three things drew me to him: his open simplicity, his remarkable knowledge of ancient armor, and the peacefulness of his company\u2014for he did all the talking. We found ourselves, as shy people often do, at the rear of the group being conducted through the castle, and he immediately began saying things that interested me. As he spoke, softly, pleasantly, and fluently, he seemed to slip imperceptibly out of this world and time into some distant age and long-forgotten land; gradually he cast such a spell over me that I seemed to walk among the ghosts and shadows, dust and decay, of a gray antiquity, conversing with one of its relics. Just as I might speak of my closest friends or enemies, or my most familiar neighbors, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table\u2014and as he continued, how very, very, indescribably old, faded, dry, musty, and ancient he came to seem! At last he turned to me and said, as casually as one might mention the weather or any ordinary matter\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know about the transmigration of souls; do you know about the transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI said I had never heard of it. He was so little concerned\u2014just as people are when discussing the weather\u2014that he did not notice whether I answered him or not. There was a brief silence, at once broken by the droning voice of the paid guide:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, dating from the sixth century, from the time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the round hole in the chain mail over the left breast; cannot be explained; believed to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy companion smiled\u2014not a modern smile, but one that must have vanished from common use many, many centuries ago\u2014and murmured, apparently to himself:\\n\\n\u201cKnow this well: I saw it happen.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added, \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBy the time I had recovered from the electric shock of that remark, he had disappeared.\\n\\nAll that evening I sat beside my fire at the Warwick Arms, absorbed in a dream of olden times, while rain struck the windows and the wind howled around the eaves and corners. From time to time I opened old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s enchanting book and feasted on its wealth of marvels and adventures, breathed in the fragrance of its obsolete names, and dreamed again.\"}"
