e, carrying torches; for I could not rest while I imagined that my sweet boy had wandered away and lay exposed to the dampness and dew of the night; Elizabeth too endured the keenest distress. At about five in the morning I found my beloved boy, whom only the evening before I had seen healthy, blooming, and full of activity, lying upon the grass, pale and still; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was upon his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was carried home, and the grief visible on my face revealed the secret to Elizabeth. She was most eager to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she insisted; and, entering the room where it lay, she quickly examined the victim\u2019s neck and, clasping her hands, cried, \u2018O God!\u2019\"}"
