content.text:
{"request_id":"ae4737e06a33dd371ca29691","rewritten_text":"Yet this reflection, which sustained me at the beginning of my career, now only casts me deeper into the dust. All my schemes and expectations are nothing, and, like the archangel who sought omnipotence, I am bound in an everlasting hell. My fancy was ardent, while my faculties of analysis and application were powerful; through the union of these gifts I imagined the design and accomplished the creation of a man. Even now I cannot recall without emotion the dreams I cherished while the labour remained unfinished. In thought I walked in heaven, at one moment rejoicing in my powers, at another inflamed by the notion of their consequences. From childhood I had been filled with great expectations and exalted ambition; yet how low am I fallen! Oh, my friend, had you known me as I formerly was, you would not know me in this degraded condition. Despair seldom entered my heart; a lofty fate appeared to carry me onward, until I fell, never—never more to rise.”

Must I, then, be deprived of this excellent being? I have desired a friend; I have looked for one who might feel with me and love me. See, upon these lonely seas I have discovered such a person, yet I fear I have won him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I would restore him to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your benevolent purposes toward so wretched a creature; but when you speak of new bonds and renewed affections, do you suppose that any can take the place of those who have departed? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even when the affections have not been deeply stirred by some extraordinary merit, the companions of childhood retain a certain influence over our minds which scarcely any friend of later years can acquire. They know our youthful dispositions, which, though they may afterward be altered, are never wholly effaced; and they can form surer judgments of our actions and the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never, unless such signs have appeared from the first, suspect the other of deceit or dishonour, while another friend, however warmly attached, may nevertheless be regarded with distrust. But I possessed friends, beloved not merely from custom and companionship, but for their own virtues; and wherever I may be, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and Clerval’s discourse will forever murmur in my ear. They are dead, and only one sentiment in such solitude could persuade me to preserve my life. Were I occupied in some noble enterprise or purpose, charged with broad usefulness to my fellow creatures, then I might live to accomplish it."}

