content.text:
{"request_id":"7c24107ba2147a1a15ee9dc9","rewritten_text":"I remembered what things had been like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day, measured in gold; in the South, he earned fifty—paid in Confederate shinplasters worth about a dollar a bushel. In the North, a pair of overalls cost three dollars—a day’s pay; in the South, it cost seventy-five—which was two days’ pay. Everything else was in the same proportion. So wages were really twice as high in the North as in the South, because one wage had that much more buying power than the other.

Yes, I made several acquaintances in the hamlet, and one thing that pleased me greatly was finding our new coins in circulation—plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, plenty of cents, quite a few nickels, and some silver; all of it among the artisans and common people generally; yes, and even some gold—but that was at the bank, meaning the goldsmith’s. I stopped in there while Marco, son of Marco, was bargaining with a shopkeeper over a quarter-pound of salt, and asked them to make change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They did—after they had bitten it, rung it on the counter, tested it with acid, and asked where I got it, who I was, where I came from, where I was going, when I expected to arrive, and perhaps two hundred more questions. When they ran out of questions, I simply went on and volunteered a great deal of information: I told them I owned a dog named Watch; that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, and her grandfather a Prohibitionist; that I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip, who died hoping for a glorious resurrection; and so on, and so on, and so on, until even that ravenous village interrogator began to look satisfied—and a little annoyed. Still, he had to respect a man of my financial standing, so he gave me no insolence; though I noticed that he took it out on his subordinates, which was perfectly natural.

Yes, they changed my twenty, but I could see it put some strain on the bank, as was only to be expected. It was like walking into a shabby village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly demanding that the proprietor change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might manage it, but he would naturally wonder how a small farmer came to be carrying so much money in his pocket. That was probably what this goldsmith was thinking too, for he followed me to the door and stood there watching me go with reverent admiration."}

