          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"453013133aaebca219923bbc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must go, not knowing what will happen; but I shall return here, or follow your party, as soon as I can. A word, a look, will be enough to decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening, or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter could not quickly be recovered from. Half an hour alone to think might have calmed her; but the ten minutes before she was interrupted, with all the limits of her situation, could do nothing to settle her. Every moment only brought new agitation. It was happiness too powerful to bear. And before she had passed through that first rush of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all came in.\\n\\nThe need to appear like herself at once caused a struggle; but after a time she could do no more. She began not to understand a word they said, and had to claim illness and excuse herself. They then saw how unwell she looked, were shocked and worried, and would not leave her for anything. This was dreadful. If only they would go away and leave her in the peaceful possession of that room, it would have cured her; but having them all standing or waiting around her was unbearable, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once, and take care of yourself, so that you may be well enough for this evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair would never do. Worse than anything! To lose the chance of speaking two words to Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town\u2014and she felt almost sure she would meet him\u2014was more than she could bear. She strongly objected to the chair, and Mrs Musgrove, who could think only of one kind of illness, anxiously made sure there had been no fall: that Anne had not recently slipped and struck her head; that she was entirely certain she had not fallen. Then she could let her go cheerfully, trusting that she would find her better that night.\\n\\nAnxious not to leave out any possible precaution, Anne made an effort and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid, ma\u2019am, that this is not fully understood. Please be so kind as to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your whole party this evening. I am afraid there has been some mistake; and I particularly wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear, it is quite understood, I give you my word.\u201d\"}"
