          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a8331efc2a61db6475b510c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I hastened along, and before long we reached my college. Then it occurred to me, and the thought sent a shudder through me, that the creature I had abandoned in my apartment might yet be there, living and moving about. I shrank from seeing this monster, but dreaded even more that Henry might behold him. I therefore begged him to wait a few moments at the foot of the stairs, while I rushed up to my room. My hand had nearly turned the lock before I came to myself. I stopped, seized by a chill tremor. I flung the door violently open, as children do when they fancy a spectre waits beyond it; yet nothing was there. I entered timidly: the room was vacant, and my bedchamber likewise delivered from its loathsome inhabitant. I could scarcely credit such extraordinary good fortune; but once convinced that my enemy had truly escaped, I struck my hands together in delight and hurried down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up to my room, and the servant soon served breakfast; yet I could not restrain myself. Joy alone did not govern me; every nerve seemed to quiver with excessive sensibility, and my pulse raced. I could not stay even an instant in one spot; I leapt over chairs, clapped my hands, and burst into loud laughter. At first Clerval supposed my strange animation arose from happiness at his arrival; but as he watched me more closely, he perceived an unaccountable wildness in my eyes, while my noisy, unchecked, joyless laughter alarmed and amazed him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he exclaimed, \u201cwhat, in God\u2019s name, is wrong? Do not laugh so. How very ill you are! What can be the cause of this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not question me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes with my hands, for I imagined the dreaded spectre slipping into the room; \u201che can tell you. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I fancied the monster had grasped me; I fought violently and fell in a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A reunion he had expected with such pleasure had been so strangely changed to misery. Yet I did not witness his sorrow, for I lay senseless and did not regain consciousness for a very, very long time.\\n\\nThis marked the beginning of a nervous fever that kept me confined for several months. Throughout that period Henry was my sole attendant. I later discovered that, aware of my father\u2019s great age and inability to undertake so lengthy a journey, and of the misery my illness would inflict upon Elizabeth, he spared them this anguish by hiding the seriousness of my condition.\"}"
