mber, and finally every object in the car. But all was useless. I continued to fall with dreadful swiftness, and was now less than half a mile from the ground. As a final expedient, having thrown away my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car itself loose from the balloon, it being of considerable weight; then, grasping the net-work with both hands, I had scarcely time to notice that the entire country, as far as sight extended, was densely dotted with tiny dwellings, before I plunged headlong into the very center of a fantastically appearing city, and among a vast multitude of ugly little people. Not one of them spoke a syllable or made the slightest effort to help me; instead, they stood like a company of fools, grinning absurdly and looking sidelong at me and my balloon, their arms planted akimbo.\u201d\"}"
