          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"34d347321fd4dccdadac987d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The little man's body was disproportionately broad, giving his whole figure a highly absurd roundness. His feet, naturally, could not be seen at all, though a hard substance of suspicious character occasionally stuck through a tear in the bottom of the car\u2014or, more properly speaking, the top of the hat. His hands were enormously large. His hair was very gray and gathered into a queue behind him. His nose was remarkably long, crooked, and inflamed; his eyes full, bright, and sharp; his chin and cheeks, though wrinkled by age, broad, puffy, and doubled. Yet no trace of ears, of any sort or description, could be found anywhere on his head. This strange little gentleman wore a loose sky-blue satin coat, with matching tight breeches fastened at the knees by silver buckles. His waistcoat was made of some vivid yellow fabric; a white taffeta cap sat jauntily to one side of his head; and, to complete his attire, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped his throat and fell gracefully over his chest in a fantastical bow of extraordinary size.\\n\\nHaving descended, as I have said, to within about a hundred feet of the earth's surface, the little old gentleman was suddenly overcome by a fit of alarm and seemed unwilling to come any closer to solid ground. He therefore threw out some sand from a canvas bag, which he raised with great difficulty, and at once became stationary. Then, hurried and agitated, he drew a large morocco pocketbook from a side pocket of his coat. He held it suspiciously in his hand, stared at it with an expression of extreme surprise, and was plainly astonished by its weight. At last he opened it and took out an enormous letter, sealed with red wax and carefully tied with red tape, which he dropped exactly at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk. His Excellency bent down to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still deeply disturbed and apparently having no further business in Rotterdam, began at that moment to make brisk preparations for departure. Since he needed to cast off some ballast in order to rise again, he threw out half a dozen bags, one after another, without troubling to empty them. Every one, most unfortunately, landed on the burgomaster's back and rolled him over and over no fewer than twenty-one times before every man in Rotterdam. It should not be imagined, however, that the great Underduk allowed such impertinence from the little old man to go unpunished.\"}"
