          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4d15406bc1740d7d269f7048\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had travelled so far over the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face at once took on the deepest sadness, and he replied, \u201cTo find someone who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you were pursuing travel in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we may have seen him, for the day before we found you, we saw dogs pulling a sledge with a man in it across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis immediately caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked many questions about the path the demon, as he called him, had taken. Soon afterward, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI have no doubt stirred your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you are too thoughtful to question me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; it would be both rude and cruel of me to trouble you with my curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you saved me from a strange and dangerous situation; you kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon after, he asked whether I thought the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I answered that I could not say with any certainty, because the ice had not broken until nearly midnight, and the traveller might have reached safety before then; but I could not know.\\n\\nFrom that time, new life seemed to fill the stranger\u2019s failing body. He was extremely eager to go on deck and watch for the sledge we had seen before; but I persuaded him to stay in the cabin, since he was much too weak to endure the bitter air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and tell him at once if anything new came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of this strange event up to the present day. The stranger has slowly regained his health, but he is very quiet and seems troubled whenever anyone but me enters his cabin. Still, his manner is so gentle and pleasing that all the sailors have become interested in him, though they have spoken with him very little. As for me, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his constant, profound sorrow fills me with sympathy and pity. He must have been a noble man in happier days, for even now, ruined as he is, he remains so appealing and kind.\\n\\nI said in one of my letters, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the wide ocean; yet I have found a man who, before misery broke his spirit, I would gladly have had as the brother of my heart.\"}"
