          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dbbd16fb09128d5339c0f86d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His manner was stubborn in the extreme, and \u201cdat deuced bug\u201d were the only words he spoke during the trip. As for me, I carried two dark lanterns, while Legrand was satisfied with the scarab, which he had tied to the end of a piece of whip-cord. As he walked, he swung it back and forth like a magician. When I saw this clear sign that my friend\u2019s mind was disturbed, I could hardly keep from crying. Still, I thought it best to go along with his strange idea for the moment, until I could take stronger steps with some hope of success. Meanwhile, I tried, without success, to learn the purpose of our expedition. Once he had persuaded me to come with him, he seemed unwilling to discuss anything less important, and to every question I asked he gave only one answer: \u201cwe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the head of the island in a skiff. Then we climbed the high ground on the mainland shore and traveled northwest through a stretch of country so wild and empty that there was no sign of any human footstep. Legrand led us confidently, stopping only now and then for a moment to consult what seemed to be landmarks he had made on an earlier visit.\\n\\nWe traveled this way for about two hours, and the sun was just setting when we entered a place far more bleak than any we had yet seen. It was a kind of flat highland near the top of an almost unreachable hill, thickly wooded from bottom to summit. Huge rocks lay scattered over the ground, seeming barely secure; in many cases, they were kept from falling into the valleys below only by the trees against which they leaned. Deep ravines running in different directions made the scene seem even more grim and solemn.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed onto was thick with brambles. We soon saw that we could never have pushed through them without the scythe, and Jupiter, following his master\u2019s orders, began cutting a path for us to the base of an enormously tall tulip-tree. It stood on the level ground among eight or ten oaks, surpassing them all\u2014and every other tree I had ever seen\u2014in the beauty of its leaves and shape, the broad spread of its branches, and the general majesty of its appearance.\"}"
