          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"391581aa0740e9a20b341048\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, has followed? What effect have I produced upon your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nA shudder passed over me as Dupin put the question. \u201cA lunatic,\u201d I answered, \u201chas committed this act\u2014some furious madman, who has fled from a neighboring _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn certain particulars,\u201d he returned, \u201cyour supposition is not beside the mark. Yet the voices of madmen, even amid their fiercest fits, are never known to correspond with that singular voice heard upon the stairs. Madmen belong to some country, and their speech, however confused in expression, always preserves the consistency of syllables. Moreover, a madman\u2019s hair is not like that which I now hold in my hand. I drew this small lock from the stiffly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Say what you think of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, utterly shaken; \u201cthis hair is exceedingly strange\u2014this is not _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not said that it is,\u201d he replied; \u201cbut before settling that question, I would have you examine the small outline I have made upon this sheet. It is a _fac-simile_ representation of what one part of the evidence calls \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails,\u2019 on the throat of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye, and what another, given by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne, terms a \u2018series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will observe,\u201d my friend went on, laying the paper open on the table before us, \u201cthat this sketch conveys the notion of a strong, unchanging grip. No _slipping_ is visible. Every finger retained\u2014perhaps until the victim\u2019s death\u2014the dreadful clutch in which it had first sunk itself. Now try to set all your fingers at once into the corresponding marks you see here.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but without success.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing the matter fairly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies upon a flat surface, whereas the human throat is cylindrical. Here is a piece of wood whose circumference is nearly that of a throat. Wind the drawing about it, and repeat the experiment.\u201d\\n\\nI did so; yet the difficulty became still plainer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I declared, \u201cis the impression of no human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cthis extract from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical and broadly descriptive account of the great fulvous Ourang-Outang of the East Indian Islands. The enormous size, extraordinary strength and agility, savage ferocity, and imitative habits of these mammalia are well enough known to everyone. At once I grasped the entire horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe account of the fingers,\u201d I said, when I had finished reading, \u201cagrees exactly with this sketch.\u201d\"}"
