rance. He was there, and so was the decree; he had been arrested in France, and his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cOff with his head!\u201d cried the crowd. \u201cAn enemy of the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence the cries and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived in England for many years.\\n\\nIt certainly was.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? What else did he call himself?\\n\\nHe hoped he was not an emigrant within the meaning and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President wished to know.\\n\\nBecause he had willingly given up a title he disliked, and a position he disliked, and had left his country\u2014before, he submitted, the word emigrant had acquired the meaning now given it by the Tribunal\u2014to earn his own living in England rather than live upon the labour of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
