          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f07fb527d3ec7da64fc99ccf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019s lavish hospitality was a powerful temptation. That, along with Tom\u2019s persuasion, soon won out. So they decided not to tell anyone about the night\u2019s plan. Then Tom thought that perhaps Huck might come that very night and give the signal. The idea drained much of the excitement from what he had been looking forward to. Still, he could not bear to miss the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019s. And why should he, he argued\u2014the signal had not come the night before, so why should it be any more likely tonight? The certain pleasure of the evening outweighed the uncertain treasure; and, like a boy, he resolved to follow the stronger desire and not let himself think again that day about the box of money.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded ravine and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and before long the distant woods and rocky heights rang far and wide with shouts and laughter. They tried every possible way of becoming hot and tired, and eventually the wanderers straggled back to camp with hearty appetites, and the attack on the good food began. After the feast came a pleasant time of resting and talking beneath the shade of spreading oaks. Then someone shouted:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. They gathered bundles of candles, and at once there was a general scramble up the hill. The cave\u2019s entrance was on the hillside\u2014an opening shaped like the letter A. Its heavy oak door stood unbarred. Inside was a small chamber, cold as an icehouse, its natural walls of solid limestone damp with a chilly sweat. It was romantic and mysterious to stand there in the deep darkness and look out at the green valley shining in the sun. But the solemn effect soon faded, and the rough play began again. The instant anyone lit a candle, everyone rushed at its owner; a struggle and a brave defense followed, but the candle was quickly knocked down or blown out, followed by delighted laughter and another chase. But everything must end. Before long, the procession filed down the steep slope of the main passage, the flickering line of lights faintly showing the towering rock walls almost to where they met sixty feet overhead. This main passage was no more than eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other high, still narrower cracks branched off on either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s cave was only a vast maze of twisting passages that crossed and recrossed one another and led nowhere.\"}"
