          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12ba3a2e88bc9e848e31c2f1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short in stature, yet wonderfully upright, and his voice was the most melodious I had ever listened to. He opened his lecture with a review of the history of chemistry and the several advances achieved by learned men, uttering with enthusiasm the names of its most eminent discoverers. He next glanced briefly at the science in its current condition and defined many of its basic expressions. Having performed a few preliminary experiments, he ended with an encomium on modern chemistry, whose language I can never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe old instructors in this science,\u201d he said, \u201cheld out impossibilities and accomplished nothing. Modern teachers promise little; they understand that metals cannot be changed into one another, and that the elixir of life is a fantasy; yet these philosophers, whose hands appear fit only to meddle with filth, and whose eyes to bend over microscope or crucible, have truly wrought miracles. They enter nature\u2019s secret retreats and reveal her labour in her concealed places. They rise to the heavens; they have found the circulation of the blood and the character of the air we breathe. They have gained powers new and nearly boundless; they can summon the thunders of heaven, imitate the earthquake, and even deride the unseen world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nThese were the professor\u2019s words\u2014or rather, let me call them the utterances of fate\u2014spoken for my ruin. While he continued, I felt my soul contending with a tangible foe; the separate keys composing the mechanism of my being were struck in turn; chord followed chord, until my mind held but one thought, one idea, one aim. So much has been achieved, cried the soul of Frankenstein\u2014more, vastly more, shall I accomplish; following the path already traced, I will open a new road, investigate powers unknown, and disclose to the world creation\u2019s profoundest mysteries.\\n\\nThat night I did not close my eyes. My inward being was in revolt and confusion; I sensed that order would emerge from it, though I lacked the strength to bring it forth. Gradually, after morning broke, sleep overtook me. I awoke, and the thoughts of the preceding night seemed like a dream. Nothing remained except a determination to resume my former studies and give myself to a science for which I thought I possessed a natural aptitude. That same day I visited M. Waldman. In private his manner was still gentler and more engaging than before an audience, since the dignity visible in his bearing during a lecture gave way, in his own home, to the utmost warmth and kindness.\"}"
