          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c09745319fd9232a96d37bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired escort went riding across Brockley meadows. While forcing her way through a hedge, she tore her riding habit so badly that, when she returned home, she decided to go upstairs by the back staircase to avoid being seen. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door happened to be open, she thought she saw someone inside. Assuming it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her sewing there, Virginia looked in to ask her to mend the habit. To her great surprise, however, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He sat by the window, watching the faded gold of the yellowing trees drift through the air and the red leaves whirl wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his entire posture expressed the deepest dejection. He looked so lonely and so dilapidated that little Virginia, whose first impulse had been to flee and lock herself in her room, felt sorry for him and resolved to comfort him. Her footsteps were so soft, and his sadness so profound, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u2018I am very sorry for you,\u2019 she said, \u2018but my brothers are going back to Eton tomorrow, and then, if you behave yourself, nobody will bother you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is ridiculous to ask me to behave myself,\u2019 he replied, turning in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to speak to him. \u2018Completely ridiculous. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night, if that is what you mean. It is my only reason for existing.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is no reason to exist at all, and you know you have been very wicked. Mrs. Umney told us, on the first day we came here, that you had killed your wife.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, I freely admit it,\u2019 said the Ghost irritably, \u2018but it was entirely a family matter and concerned no one else.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is very wrong to kill anyone,\u2019 said Virginia, who sometimes possessed a gentle Puritan seriousness inherited from some old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I detest the shallow harshness of abstract morality! My wife was very plain, never had my ruffs properly starched, and knew nothing about cooking. Why, there was a buck I shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket\u2014and do you know how she had it served at table? Still, it does not matter now; it is all over. And I do not think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u2019\"}"
        }
