          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9d4b18ea3cbe23d6ddc79b71\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the days of my youth, discontent had never entered my thoughts; and if at any time I was oppressed by _ennui_, the view of nature's beauty, or the contemplation of what was noble and sublime in the works of man, could invariably engage my heart and restore buoyancy to my spirits. But I am a stricken tree; the lightning has pierced my soul; and I then felt that I was destined to live on, displaying what I shall shortly cease to be\u2014a wretched image of ruined humanity, an object of pity to others and of horror to myself.\\n\\nWe remained for a long time at Oxford, wandering through the surrounding country and seeking out every place connected with the most stirring period of English history. Our small expeditions of discovery were frequently extended by the new objects continually appearing before us. We saw the grave of the celebrated Hampden and the field where that patriot was slain. For a moment my soul rose above its degrading and miserable apprehensions, to meditate upon the sacred principles of liberty and self-devotion for which these scenes stood as monuments and memorials. For an instant I ventured to cast aside my fetters and survey the world with a liberated and exalted spirit; but the iron had penetrated my flesh, and I fell back once more, trembling and despairing, into my unhappy self.\\n\\nWe quitted Oxford reluctantly and went on to Matlock, our next resting-place. The landscape around this village bore a stronger resemblance to that of Switzerland; yet all was upon a lesser scale, and the verdant hills lacked the diadem of far-off white Alps that ever accompanies the pine-clad mountains of my native land. We explored the marvellous cavern and the small natural-history cabinets, where the curiosities were arranged in the same fashion as in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. When Henry uttered the latter name, I trembled, and hastened away from Matlock, which had thus become linked with that dreadful scene.\\n\\nFrom Derby, continuing our northward course, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could now nearly imagine myself amid the Swiss mountains. The small remnants of snow still resting upon the northern mountain slopes, the lakes, and the rush of the rocky streams were all sights familiar and beloved to me. Here we likewise formed several acquaintances, who almost succeeded in beguiling me into happiness. Clerval's pleasure was proportionately greater than my own; his mind enlarged in the society of gifted men, and within himself he discovered capacities and resources greater than he could have supposed he possessed while he kept company with his inferiors.\"}"
