          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82fdfd08941c0a9f35a28ba4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my great joy, at about nine o\u2019clock, with the moon\u2019s surface terribly close and my fears at their height, the pump of my condenser finally showed clear signs that the atmosphere was changing. By ten, I had reason to think its density had increased greatly. By eleven, the apparatus required very little work; and at twelve, though somewhat hesitant, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding no ill effect from this, I at last opened the gum-elastic chamber and removed it from around the car. As might have been expected, spasms and a violent headache immediately followed an experiment so sudden and dangerous. But since these and the other difficulties of breathing were not severe enough to threaten my life, I resolved to bear them as well as I could, knowing that I would soon leave them behind as I approached the denser layers near the moon. This approach, however, was still extremely rapid; and it soon became alarmingly clear that, although I had probably been right in expecting an atmosphere denser in proportion to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had been wrong in believing that this density, even at the surface, would be enough to support the great weight in my balloon\u2019s car. Yet it should have done so, and to the same degree as on the earth\u2019s surface, if the actual gravity of bodies on either world were in proportion to the density of the atmosphere. My swift fall proved that this was not so; why it was not so can only be explained by those possible geological disturbances I mentioned before. In any case, I was now very near the planet and descending with terrible force. I lost no time in throwing overboard first my ballast, then my water-kegs, then my condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every object in the car. But it was useless. I still fell with dreadful speed and was now less than half a mile from the surface. As a final measure, after throwing away my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car itself loose from the balloon, for it was no small weight. Clinging with both hands to the netting, I had barely time to see that the whole land, as far as I could see, was thickly scattered with tiny dwellings before I plunged headfirst into the center of a strange-looking city, among a vast crowd of ugly little people. Not one spoke a word or made the slightest effort to help me; they stood like fools, grinning absurdly and looking sideways at me and my balloon, their arms planted on their hips.\u201d\"}"
