          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d73909c5693b52c3d0993e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now come to my own story. There is little to tell of the early part of my life, and I will be brief; but I must linger somewhat over my childhood, so that it may be understood how, when one hope failed, all life became empty; and how, when the only affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my existence died with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt was very unlike my father. I believe that, without the slightest trace of malice, she possessed the coldest heart ever held in a human breast: it was wholly incapable of affection. She took me into her care because she considered it her duty; but she had lived too long alone, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, to permit me to disturb her peace. She had never married, and for the previous five years had lived entirely alone on an estate inherited through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had wished her to live with me at his family mansion, set in beautiful countryside near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused this proposal; but as soon as she had settled the affairs that her brother\u2019s departure had placed in her hands, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nMy care as a baby, and later until I reached my eighth year, fell to one of my mother\u2019s servants, who had accompanied us into retirement for that purpose. I was kept in a distant part of the house and saw my aunt only at fixed times. These came twice each day: around noon she visited my nursery, and after dinner I was brought to her. She never embraced me, and throughout my stay in the room seemed afraid that some childish prank would irritate her. My good nurse always instructed me most carefully before she dared take me into the parlour\u2014and the awe inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold looks and few forced words was so great that I rarely disgraced those lessons or departed from the exemplary silence I had been taught to maintain during these brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse\u2019s care, I ran freely through our park and the neighbouring fields. Born of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the greatest sensitivity of feeling. I cannot express the passion with which I loved everything, even the lifeless objects around me.\"}"
