          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d120aa3c9fe1be14f0971d00\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he questioned why I desired it, and why I imagined I had any claim to it, I would tell him, though he cared little for such humble dreams, that I had loved Estella deeply and for a long time, and that, though I had lost her and must lead a desolate life, all that related to her remained closer and more precious to me than anything else in the world. And as Mr. Jaggers stood perfectly motionless and silent, seemingly quite unmoved by this appeal, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know that you are a man of a kind heart. I have seen your agreeable home, and your aged father, and all the harmless, merry, playful habits by which you relieve your business life. And I beg you to speak a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to show him that, considering every circumstance, he ought to deal more frankly with me!\u201d\\n\\nNever had I seen two men regard one another more strangely than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this address. At first I feared that Wemmick would be dismissed from his post on the spot; but the fear passed when I saw Mr. Jaggers soften into something resembling a smile, and Wemmick grow more confident.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is all this?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou with an old father, and you with agreeable and playful habits?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d replied Wemmick. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what difference does it make?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling freely, \u201cthis man must be the cleverest impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing of the sort,\u201d returned Wemmick, becoming bolder still. \u201cI think you are another.\u201d\\n\\nAgain they gave each other those same strange looks, each seeming still suspicious that the other was deceiving him.\\n\\n\u201c_You_ with an agreeable home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cSo long as it does not interfere with business,\u201d Wemmick replied, \u201clet it stand. Now that I look at you, sir, I should not be surprised if _you_ were planning and arranging to have an agreeable home of your own some day, when you are weary of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded reflectively two or three times, and actually sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe will not speak of \u2018poor dreams\u2019; you know more of such matters than I do, having far more recent experience of that kind. But now for this other business. I will state a case to you. Observe! I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe paused until I declared that I fully understood he had expressly said he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201csuppose this case. Suppose that a woman, in circumstances such as those you have described, kept her child hidden, and was compelled to reveal the fact to her legal adviser, because he represented that, with regard to the scope of his defence, he must know exactly how matters stood concerning that child.\u201d\"}"
