          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f7bdf6a2e5a11a6943a4da89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was somewhat surprised by the match, but spoke less of her astonishment than of her sincere wish for their happiness; nor could Elizabeth persuade her to think it unlikely. Kitty and Lydia were far from envying Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was only a clergyman; the news affected them only as something to repeat around Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not help feeling triumphant at being able to return Mrs. Bennet\u2019s consolation of having a daughter well married; and she called at Longbourn more often than usual to declare how happy she was, though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour looks and spiteful remarks might have been enough to banish happiness altogether.\\n\\nThere was a reserve between Elizabeth and Charlotte that kept them both silent on the subject; and Elizabeth was convinced that no true confidence could ever exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her look with greater affection on her sister, whose integrity and sensitivity she knew could never lose her esteem, and for whose happiness she became more anxious each day, since Bingley had now been gone a week and there was no word of his return.\\n\\nJane had sent Caroline an immediate reply to her letter and counted the days until she could reasonably expect to hear again. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks arrived on Tuesday, addressed to their father and written with all the solemn gratitude that a year\u2019s residence with the family might have inspired. Having satisfied his conscience on that point, he went on, in many ecstatic expressions, to announce his happiness at winning the affection of their agreeable neighbour, Miss Lucas. He then explained that it was solely for the pleasure of her company that he had so readily accepted their kind invitation to visit Longbourn again, where he hoped to return a fortnight from Monday. Lady Catherine, he added, approved so warmly of his marriage that she wished it to take place as soon as possible; and he trusted this would be an irresistible argument with his amiable Charlotte to set an early date for making him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer a source of pleasure to Mrs. Bennet. On the contrary, she was as inclined to complain of it as her husband. It was very strange that he should come to Longbourn rather than Lucas Lodge; it was also highly inconvenient and extremely troublesome. She hated having visitors in the house while her health was so poor, and lovers were the most disagreeable visitors of all.\"}"
