          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d7b51442db6afef0b66be078\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dear me, she was a prodigious talker. It would hardly do for me to hint that it was very late, and that I was weary and desperately sleepy. I wished I had retired when I had the opportunity. But now I had to endure it; there was no escape. So she chattered on and on, through the deep, spectral silence of the sleeping castle, until presently there rose, as though from far beneath our feet, a distant sound like a smothered cry--with such agony in it that my flesh crept. The queen paused, and her eyes kindled with delight; she cocked her lovely head as a bird does when listening. Once more the sound forced its way upward through the quiet.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is that?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a most stubborn soul, and endureth greatly. It hath been many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndureth what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come--ye shall behold a merry sight. If he yield not his secret now, ye shall see him rent apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a sleek, smooth fiend she was; so calm and tranquil, while every cord in my legs seemed to ache in fellow-feeling with that man's torment. Escorted by armored guards carrying blazing torches, we marched through resounding corridors and down wet, dripping stone stairs, reeking of mould and the ages of captive night--a cold, eerie journey, and a long one, made neither briefer nor more pleasant by the sorceress's discourse concerning this victim and his offense. An unnamed informer had charged him with killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous evidence is scarcely the proper thing, your Highness. It would be more just to bring the accuser face to face with the accused.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered that, it being of little moment. Yet if I wished it, I could not do it, for the accuser came masked in the night, told the forester, and immediately departed again; thus the forester knoweth him not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen this Unknown is the sole person who saw the stag slain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMarry, no man saw the deed, but this Unknown saw this sturdy wretch near the place where the stag lay, and with most loyal zeal went and betrayed him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag as well? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal--while masked--seems a trifle suspicious. But what does your highness hope to gain by racking the prisoner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise, and then his soul would be lost. By law his life is forfeit for his crime--and assuredly I shall see that he pays it!--but it would endanger my own soul to permit him to die unconfessed and unabsolved.\u201d\"}"
