          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca116aa6b6996f10f96d036c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There is a moment in every properly-made boy\u2019s life when he is seized with a fierce longing to go off somewhere and hunt for buried treasure. This longing struck Tom one day. He set out to look for Joe Harper, but could not find him. Then he tried Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Before long he came across Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him aside to a secluded spot and confided the plan to him. Huck agreed readily. Huck was forever ready to join any undertaking that promised amusement and called for no money, since he possessed an inconvenient excess of the kind of time that is not money. \u201cWhere are we going to dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, nearly anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it buried everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, it surely isn\u2019t. It\u2019s buried in very special places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in decayed chests beneath the end of a branch on an old dead tree, exactly where the shadow lies at midnight; but most often beneath the floors of ha\u2019nted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho buries it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, naturally\u2014who did you suppose? Sunday-school sup\u2019rintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it was mine, I wouldn\u2019t bury it; I\u2019d spend it and enjoy myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t act that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they mean to, but usually they forget the signs, or else they die. Anyhow, it stays there a long while and turns rusty; then, after a time, somebody finds an old yellow paper explaining how to locate the signs\u2014a paper you have to cipher over for about a week, because it is mostly marks and hy\u2019roglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro\u2014what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHy\u2019roglyphics\u2014pictures and such, you know, that don\u2019t appear to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the signs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any signs. They always bury it under a ha\u2019nted house, or on an island, or beneath a dead tree with one limb sticking out. Well, we\u2019ve searched Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can search it again sometime; and there\u2019s the old ha\u2019nted house up the Still-House branch, and plenty of dead-limb trees\u2014dead loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under every one of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow you do talk! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how will you know which one to try?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTry every one of them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that would take the whole summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if it does? Suppose you found a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest packed with di\u2019monds. What then?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes shone.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s bully. More than bully enough for me. You just give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want any di\u2019monds.\u201d\"}"
