          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4f405e82791a8c0615a77591\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAll right, now, let\u2019s hear all about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, you see, it was this way. Old mistress\u2014that\u2019s Miss Watson\u2014was always picking at me and treating me pretty rough, but she always said she wouldn\u2019t sell me down to Orleans. But I noticed there\u2019d been a slave trader around the place quite a lot lately, and I began to feel uneasy. Then one night I crept to the door pretty late, and it wasn\u2019t quite shut, and I heard old mistress tell the widow she was going to sell me down to Orleans. She said she didn\u2019t want to, but she could get eight hundred dollars for me, and it was such a big pile of money she couldn\u2019t resist. The widow tried to make her say she wouldn\u2019t do it, but I didn\u2019t wait to hear the rest. I took off mighty quick, I can tell you.\\n\\n\u201cI ran out and slipped down the hill, meaning to steal a skiff somewhere along the shore above town, but people were still stirring, so I hid in the old rundown cooper\u2019s shop on the bank and waited for everybody to leave. Well, I stayed there all night. There was somebody around all the time. Around six in the morning skiffs began going by, and by eight or nine every skiff that passed was talking about how your father had come into town and said you\u2019d been killed. Those last skiffs were full of ladies and gentlemen going over to see the place. Sometimes they\u2019d pull up at the shore and rest before starting across, so from their talk I learned all about the killing. I was terribly sorry you were killed, Huck, but I\u2019m not anymore.\\n\\n\u201cI lay there under the shavings all day. I was hungry, but I wasn\u2019t afraid, because I knew old mistress and the widow were going to the camp meeting right after breakfast and would be gone all day. They knew I went off with the cattle around daylight, so they wouldn\u2019t expect to see me around the place, and they wouldn\u2019t miss me until after dark that evening. The other servants wouldn\u2019t miss me either, because they\u2019d slip out and take a holiday as soon as the old folks were out of the way.\\n\\n\u201cWell, when dark came, I set out up the river road and went about two miles or more, where there weren\u2019t any houses. I\u2019d made up my mind what I was going to do. You see, if I kept trying to get away on foot, the dogs would track me; if I stole a skiff to cross over, they\u2019d miss that skiff, you see, and they\u2019d know about where I landed on the other side, and where to pick up my trail.\"}"
