          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"273276da3203a1d9a5a957c9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For many years, Dorian Gray was unable to escape the influence of this book. Or, perhaps, it would be truer to say that he had never wished to escape it. He ordered from Paris no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition, and caused them to be bound in various colours, that they might accord with his different moods and the shifting caprices of a nature over which he appeared, at times, to have almost wholly lost command. The hero, the extraordinary young Parisian in whom the romantic and scientific dispositions were so oddly united, became for him a sort of prophetic image of himself. Indeed, the entire book seemed to tell the history of his own life, composed before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he was luckier than the fantastic hero of the novel. He never experienced\u2014never, in truth, had reason to experience\u2014that rather grotesque terror of mirrors, polished metal, and quiet water which seized the young Parisian so early in life, and which had been caused by the sudden decline of a beau once, apparently, so remarkable. With an almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps cruelty has a part in nearly every joy, as it surely does in every pleasure\u2014he would read the later portion of the book, with its genuinely tragic, though somewhat exaggerated, tale of the grief and despair of one who had himself lost that which he had valued most dearly in others and in the world.\\n\\nFor the marvellous beauty that had so enthralled Basil Hallward, and many besides him, appeared never to desert him. Even those who had heard the worst things said of him\u2014and now and then strange reports of his manner of life spread through London and became club gossip\u2014could believe nothing dishonourable of him when they saw him. He always bore the appearance of one who had remained untainted by the world. Men speaking coarsely fell silent when Dorian Gray came into the room. There was something in the purity of his face that reproved them. His very presence seemed to bring back to them the memory of the innocence they had stained. They marvelled that one so graceful and charming could have avoided the corruption of an age both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, when he returned home from one of those long and mysterious absences that prompted such odd conjectures among those who were his friends, or believed themselves to be, he would himself steal upstairs to the locked room, unlock the door with the key that now never left his possession, and stand before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him, holding a mirror, gazing first at the wicked, aging face upon the canvas and then at the lovely young face that smiled back from the polished glass.\"}"
