          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d128b5c0b2800cd4a536cad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, his face white with terror and his eyes distraught with sorrow, Lord Arthur Savile hurried out of Bentinck House, forcing his way through the throng of fur-clad footmen gathered about the great striped awning, apparently blind and deaf to all around him. The night was piercingly cold, and the gas-lights about the square blazed and trembled in the sharp wind; yet his hands were fever-hot, and his brow seemed on fire. He walked on and on, with almost the unsteady step of a drunken man. A policeman stared after him curiously, and a beggar, slinking from an archway to solicit alms, recoiled in alarm at the sight of wretchedness deeper than his own. Once he paused beneath a lamp and examined his hands. He fancied that he could already see blood upon them, and a low cry escaped his shaking lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had read there. Murder! The night itself appeared to know it, and the lonely wind seemed to shriek it into his ear. It filled the black corners of the streets. It leered down at him from the house-tops.\\n\\nHe reached the Park first, and its gloomy trees seemed to draw him towards them. He rested heavily against the railings, pressing his forehead to the damp iron to cool it, and hearkening to the quivering stillness among the branches. \u2018Murder! murder!\u2019 he repeated continually, as if saying the word again and again might lessen its horror. The sound of his own voice made him tremble, though he nearly wished that Echo would catch it and rouse the sleeping city from its dreams. He longed madly to halt some indifferent passer-by and confess everything.\\n\\nAfterwards he strayed across Oxford Street into narrow, sordid lanes. Two women with painted faces jeered at him as he passed. From a shadowy yard came the noise of curses and blows, then piercing cries; and on a wet doorstep he saw the bent shapes of poverty and old age crouched together. An odd compassion seized him. Were these offspring of sin and misery fated to their destiny, as he was to his? Were they too, like himself, only puppets in some monstrous spectacle?\\n\\nYet it was not the mystery but the absurdity of suffering that impressed him: its utter futility, its grotesque lack of purpose. Everything seemed so confused! So devoid of harmony! He marvelled at the contrast between the facile optimism of the age and the actual conditions of life. He was still very young.\\n\\nAt last he found himself before Marylebone Church. The quiet road lay like a long ribbon of burnished silver, marked here and there by the dark arabesques of moving shadows.\"}"
