          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"afde50444d67e481a34ad76f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they passed deserted brickworks. The fog was thinner here, and he could make out the strange bottle-shaped kilns, their orange flames spreading like fans. A dog barked as they passed, and somewhere far off in the darkness a wandering seagull cried. The horse stumbled in a rut, then veered aside and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and clattered once more over roughly paved streets. Most windows were dark, but from time to time fantastic shadows appeared against a lit blind. He watched them with curiosity. They moved like monstrous puppets and gestured like living creatures. He hated them. A dull anger filled his heart. As they turned a corner, a woman shouted something after them from an open doorway, and two men chased the hansom for nearly a hundred yards. The driver struck at them with his whip.\\n\\nThey say passion makes a person think in circles. Certainly, with dreadful repetition, Dorian Gray\u2019s bitten lips formed and re-formed those subtle words concerning soul and sense, until he found in them, as it were, the complete expression of his mood, and justified through intellectual approval passions that, without such justification, would still have ruled his nature. From cell to cell in his brain crept a single thought; and the fierce desire to live, most terrible of all human appetites, stirred every trembling nerve and fibre into strength. Ugliness, once hateful to him because it made things real, became precious to him now for precisely that reason. Ugliness was the only reality. The vulgar brawl, the disgusting den, the raw violence of disordered life, the very degradation of thief and outcast, were more vivid in the intensity of their actual impression than all the graceful forms of art and dreamy shadows of song. They were what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the driver pulled up sharply at the head of a dark lane. Above the low roofs and jagged chimney stacks of the houses rose the black masts of ships. Coils of white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere around here, sir, isn\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked about him. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he replied; then, getting out quickly and giving the driver the extra fare he had promised, he walked swiftly toward the quay. Here and there, a lantern glimmered at the stern of some enormous merchant ship. Its light trembled and broke apart in the puddles. A red glow came from an outward-bound steamer taking on coal. The slimy pavement looked like wet rubberized cloth.\"}"
