          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e566e1d224713f69c68a6944\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\nNo reply.\\n\\n\u201cTOM!\u201d\\n\\nNo reply.\\n\\n\u201cWhere has that boy got to, I wonder? You TOM!\u201d\\n\\nNo reply.\\n\\nThe old woman drew her spectacles down and peered over them around the room; then she raised them and looked beneath them. She rarely, if ever, looked through them for anything as insignificant as a boy; they were her ceremonial pair, the pride of her heart, made for \u201cstyle,\u201d not use\u2014she could have seen just as well through a pair of stove-lids. She seemed puzzled for a moment, then said, not angrily, though loudly enough for the furniture to hear:\\n\\n\u201cWell, I declare, if I catch you I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe left it unfinished, for she was now stooping and thrusting the broom beneath the bed, and needed her breath to mark the thrusts. She brought forth nothing but the cat.\\n\\n\u201cI never saw the like of that boy!\u201d\\n\\nShe went to the open doorway, stood there, and searched among the tomato vines and \u201cjimpson\u201d weeds that made up the garden. No Tom. So she raised her voice to an angle meant to carry far and called:\\n\\n\u201cY-o-u-u TOM!\u201d\\n\\nA faint sound came from behind her, and she turned in time to catch a small boy by the loose back of his roundabout and stop his escape.\\n\\n\u201cThere! I might have known it was that closet. What have you been doing in there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing! Look at your hands. Look at your mouth. What is that stuff?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know, aunt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I do. It\u2019s jam\u2014that\u2019s what it is. Forty times I\u2019ve told you that if you didn\u2019t leave that jam alone, I\u2019d skin you. Bring me that switch.\u201d\\n\\nThe switch hung poised in the air\u2014the danger was extreme\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy! Look behind you, aunt!\u201d\\n\\nThe old lady spun around and jerked her skirts out of harm\u2019s way. The boy instantly ran, climbed the tall board fence, and vanished over the top.\\n\\nHis aunt Polly stood astonished for a moment, then gave a soft laugh.\\n\\n\u201cConfound the boy, can\u2019t I ever learn? Hasn\u2019t he fooled me often enough for me to be on guard by now? But old fools are the greatest fools there are. You can\u2019t teach an old dog new tricks, as they say. But goodness, he never does them the same way two days running, and how can anybody know what\u2019s next? He seems to know exactly how long he can plague me before my temper rises, and he knows that if he can put me off a minute or make me laugh, it all goes down again and I can\u2019t strike him a blow.\"}"
