{"request_id":"85a052e2f5913b9ba3dfe1b7","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was unspoken; still, I could not question that his love for her occasioned the uncertainty of purpose he displayed. And who was more deserving of affection than my high-souled sister? Who merited the hand of this self-raised king more than she whose look was fit for a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, though disappointment had subdued her passion, while ambition contended fiercely with his.

That evening we went together to the House. Raymond, though aware that his schemes and hopes were to be examined and settled in the anticipated debate, remained cheerful and unconcerned. As we entered the coffee-room, a murmur, like the sound of ten thousand hives of bees in swarm, deafened us. Groups of politicians stood about, their faces anxious, their voices either loud or low. The aristocratic party, the wealthiest and most powerful men in England, seemed less disturbed than the rest, since the matter was to be debated without their intervention. Ryland and his adherents stood near the fire. Ryland, of humble origin and vast fortune, had inherited his wealth from his father, a manufacturer. In his youth he had seen the king’s abdication and the union of the Houses of Lords and Commons; he had approved these popular encroachments, and had made it his life’s work to strengthen and enlarge them. Since that time the influence of the landed proprietors had increased; and at first Ryland had not regretted Lord Raymond’s intrigues, which withdrew many of his adversary’s followers. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the restoration of sovereignty as an event that would return to them their lost authority and privileges. The nearly extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in men’s minds; and they, willing slaves and self-created subjects, stood ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few upright and manly spirits yet survived, pillars of the state; but the word republic had become stale to vulgar ears, and many—whether a majority, the event would show—longed for the glitter and pageantry of royalty. Ryland was awakened to opposition; he declared that only his forbearance had allowed this party to grow, but that the hour for indulgence had passed, and that with a single sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs that obscured his countrymen’s sight.

When Raymond came into the coffee-room, his friends greeted his appearance almost with a cheer. They crowded about him, reckoned their numbers, and explained why they were now to gain the support of various members who had not yet announced themselves."}

