{"request_id":"84faa2d6e51cb3cf271ed76d","rewritten_text":"Few could claim such a pure heart, such genuine humility of spirit, combined with a steady trust in her own integrity and faith in that of others. From birth she had lived a secluded life. She lost her mother very young, but her father had devoted himself to her education. He held many unusual ideas that shaped the system he adopted for her. She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those English heroes who had lived several centuries before, while she was almost ignorant of current events. She had read few authors from the previous fifty years, but apart from this exception her reading was extensive. Thus, though she seemed less familiar with the mysteries of life and society than he was, her knowledge was deeper and rested on firmer foundations; and even if her beauty and sweetness had not captivated him, her intelligence would always have held him enthralled. He looked to her as his guide, and his adoration was such that he delighted in increasing within himself the sense of inferiority she sometimes inspired.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. On this occasion he left college and, for a time shaking off his old friends, withdrew to the neighbourhood of his Diana, receiving all his comfort from her sweet voice and still dearer caresses. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to assert his independence. He felt that, however much they might mock his intended marriage, they would not dare show their ridicule once it had taken place. Therefore, having sought the consent of his guardian, which he obtained with some difficulty, and that of his mistress's father, which was more readily granted, he told no one else of his purpose; and by the time he reached his twentieth birthday, he had become Diana's husband.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness held such charm for him that he could think of nothing but her. He invited several college friends to visit him, but their frivolity disgusted him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him in boyhood: he had become a man, and was astonished that he could ever have shared the empty phrases and ideas of his fellow students, or feared for a moment the criticism of people such as these. He abandoned his old friendships not from fickleness, but because they were truly unworthy of him."}

