          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"56bc235dbfc03e1555ff7178\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With great care, my friend drove a peg into the ground at the exact spot where the beetle had fallen, then took a tape measure from his pocket. He fastened one end to the point on the tree trunk nearest the peg, stretched it to the peg, and then continued in the direction established by the tree and peg for fifty feet, while Jupiter cleared the brambles with the scythe. At the point reached, a second peg was driven, and around it, as a center, we marked a rough circle about four feet across. Legrand then took a spade for himself, gave one to Jupiter and another to me, and asked us to begin digging as quickly as possible.\\n\\nTo tell the truth, I had no particular taste for such work at any time, and at that moment I would gladly have refused; night was approaching, and I was already exhausted by the exertion we had made. But I saw no way to escape, and feared that a refusal might upset my poor friend. Had I been able to rely on Jupiter\u2019s help, I would not have hesitated to try to force the madman home; but I knew the old negro\u2019s nature too well to expect that he would aid me, under any circumstances, in a physical struggle with his master. I had no doubt that Legrand had been affected by one of the countless Southern superstitions concerning buried treasure, and that his fancy had been strengthened by finding the scarab, or perhaps by Jupiter\u2019s stubborn insistence that it was \u201ca bug of real gold.\u201d A mind inclined toward madness could easily be carried away by such notions, especially when they agreed with cherished beliefs; and I remembered the poor man\u2019s remark that the beetle was \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d Altogether, I was deeply annoyed and bewildered, but at last resolved to make the best of necessity: to dig willingly, and so more quickly prove to the dreamer, by what we saw with our own eyes, the falseness of his beliefs.\\n\\nOnce the lanterns were lit, we all set to work with a zeal worthy of a more sensible cause. As their glare fell over us and our tools, I could not help thinking what a picturesque group we made, and how strange and suspicious our labor must have seemed to any passerby who happened upon us.\\n\\nWe dug steadily for two hours.\"}"
