{"request_id":"ce5c9b7d793c3d71d1a29413","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a light breeze soon blew it into a flame. I noticed this and made a fan from branches, which stirred the embers when they were almost out. When night came again, I was pleased to find that the fire gave both light and warmth. This discovery also helped me with food, for I found that some scraps the travellers had left behind had been roasted, and they tasted far better than the berries I picked from the trees. I therefore tried to prepare my food in the same way, placing it on the hot embers. The berries were ruined by this, but the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, however, grew scarce, and I often spent an entire day searching unsuccessfully for a few acorns to ease my hunger. When I realized this, I decided to leave the place where I had lived until then and seek another, where my few needs might be more easily met. In leaving, I deeply regretted losing the fire I had found by chance and did not know how to make again. I spent several hours seriously considering this problem, but at last I had to give up trying to replace it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I set off through the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days and finally reached open country. A heavy snow had fallen the night before, and the fields were covered in an unbroken white. The sight was bleak, and my feet were chilled by the cold, wet substance covering the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on rising ground, no doubt built for the use of some shepherd. It was a new sight to me, and I examined it with great curiosity. Finding the door open, I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, preparing his breakfast. He turned when he heard a sound, and when he saw me, he gave a loud cry. Leaving the hut, he ran across the fields with a speed his weak body hardly seemed capable of. His appearance, unlike any I had seen before, and his flight surprised me somewhat. But I was delighted by the hut. Here snow and rain could not enter; the ground was dry; and at that moment it seemed to me as perfect and heavenly a refuge as Pandæmonium had seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

