          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa25d2956467ecb8b2f18442\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, twelve o\u2019clock rang out from the tall tower at Westminster, and with every deep stroke of the bell the night seemed to shake. Then the railway lights went out, leaving one lonely lamp shining like a great ruby on a huge mast, and the noise of the city grew quieter.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and walked towards Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal, like some strange dream. The houses across the river seemed to be made of darkness. It was as if silver and shadow had remade the world. The great dome of St. Paul\u2019s rose like a bubble in the dim air.\\n\\nAs he came near Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man leaning over the parapet. When he drew closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight fell directly on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the palm-reader. No one could mistake that fat, loose face, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the weak, unhealthy smile, or the sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A sudden, brilliant idea came to him, and he crept quietly up behind the man. In an instant, he grabbed Mr. Podgers by the legs and threw him into the Thames. There was a rough curse, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur looked anxiously over the edge, but he could see nothing of the palm-reader except a tall hat spinning in a swirl of moonlit water. After a while, that too sank, and no sign of Mr. Podgers remained. Once he thought he saw the large, misshapen figure swimming towards the steps by the bridge, and a terrible sense of failure seized him. But it was only a reflection, which vanished when the moon came out from behind a cloud. At last, he felt he had carried out destiny\u2019s command. He gave a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u2018Have you dropped something, sir?\u2019 a voice suddenly asked behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and saw a policeman holding a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u2018Nothing important, sergeant,\u2019 he replied with a smile. Then, calling to a passing hansom, he jumped in and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days, he moved between hope and fear. Sometimes he almost expected Mr. Podgers to enter the room, yet at other times he felt that Fate could not be so unfair to him. Twice he went to the palm-reader\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but could not make himself ring the bell. He wanted certainty, but feared it too.\\n\\nAt last it came. He was sitting in the club smoking-room, drinking tea and listening rather tiredly to Surbiton describe the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
