content.text:
{"request_id":"62a49386db3611051a04a2c8","rewritten_text":"They were her very life; more beloved than any place hallowed by affection, more precious than all that earth possessed. The boys listened with childish delight to news of our departure: Clara inquired whether we were going to Athens. “It may be so,” I answered; and her face brightened with joy. There she would see her parents’ tomb, and the land crowded with memories of her father’s renown. Silently, yet continually, she had dwelt upon these scenes. Their remembrance had changed her childish mirth into gravity, and filled her with lofty, uneasy thoughts.

There were many cherished friends, however humble, whom we could not abandon. There was the high-spirited, tractable horse Lord Raymond had bestowed upon his daughter; there were Alfred’s dog and a pet eagle, its eyes weakened by old age. Yet in making this list of favourites to accompany us, we could not avoid grieving over our grievous losses, and sighing deeply for the multitude of things we were compelled to leave. Tears filled Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought first a favourite rosebush, then a finely sculptured marble vase, declaring that these must be carried, and lamenting that we could not take the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, treasured thing with us. “Dear foolish creatures,” I said, “we have lost treasures infinitely more valuable than these, and leave them behind to save possessions beside which they are as nothing. Let us never lose sight of our purpose and our hope; they will raise an irresistible barrier against the flood of regret we feel for such trifles.”

The children were readily diverted, and soon returned to their visions of future pleasure. Idris had vanished. She had gone to conceal her frailty; leaving the castle, she descended into the small park and sought solitude, that she might give way to tears. I found her clasping an ancient oak, touching its rugged trunk with her rosy lips, while tears streamed freely and sobs and interrupted cries escaped her despite herself; with overwhelming sorrow I saw this beloved being so absorbed in grief. I drew her to me; and when she felt my kisses upon her eyelids and my arms enfolding her, she returned to awareness of what was still hers. “You are very good not to blame me,” she said. “I weep, and a sharp agony of unbearable sorrow rends my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives their husbands, while you and my children remain to me.”"}

