          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a1a8059d4e874b03cc7fcaff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe appeared deliberately to misunderstand the word she had repeated, and hastened to say, \u201cYes, customers; in banking, we commonly term our connection our customers. He was a French gentleman; a gentleman of science; a man of high attainments\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot of Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman came from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was esteemed in Paris. I had the honour to know him there. Our intercourse was business intercourse, though confidential. I was then in our French House, and had been\u2014oh! twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask, at what time, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was among the trustees. His affairs, as were those of many French gentlemen and French families, were wholly in Tellson\u2019s hands. In much the same manner, I am, or have been, trustee of one sort or another for scores of our customers. These are simply business connections, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special concern, nothing resembling sentiment. Throughout my business life I have gone from one to another, as in a business day I go from one customer to the next; in brief, I have no feelings; I am merely a machine. To proceed\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is the story of my father, sir; and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014the strangely furrowed forehead was fixed intently on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was made an orphan, my mother having survived my father by only two years, it was you who took me to England. I am nearly certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry accepted the small, hesitant hand that was trustfully put forward to meet his own, and with some formality raised it to his lips. Then he led the young lady directly back to her chair; and, grasping its back with his left hand, while his right alternately rubbed his chin, tugged his wig beside the ears, or emphasized his words, he stood gazing down at her face as she looked up at his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will perceive how truthfully I described myself just now, when I said that I had no feelings, and that every relation I maintain with my fellow-creatures is merely a business relation, if you consider that I have never seen you since. No; ever since, you have been the ward of Tellson\u2019s House, and ever since I have been occupied with Tellson\u2019s House\u2019s other business. Feelings! I have neither time nor opportunity for them. My entire life, miss, is spent in turning an enormous pecuniary Mangle.\u201d\"}"
