          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e07e014f7c8b012bcf1770c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself a moment to reflect, and scarcely aware of her own feelings, Elizabeth, when she had finished this letter, immediately caught up the other; and, opening it in the greatest impatience, read the following. It had been written one day after the first was concluded.\\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received my hasty letter. I hope this may be clearer; but though I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to be coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I scarcely know what to say, but I have ill news for you, and it must not be postponed. However imprudent a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now eager to know that it has occurred, for there is far too much cause to fear that they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the preceding day, only a few hours after the express. Though Lydia\u2019s brief note to Mrs. F. led them to suppose they were bound for Gretna Green, Denny let fall something which showed his belief that W. had never meant to go there, or indeed to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel F., who, immediately alarmed, set out from B., intending to follow their course. He traced them without difficulty as far as Clapham, but no further; for when they entered that place, they got into a hackney-coach and sent away the chaise which had brought them from Epsom. All that has since been discovered is that they were seen proceeding along the London road. I do not know what to think. Having made every inquiry possible on that side of London, Colonel F. came into Hertfordshire, anxiously asking after them at every turnpike, and at the inns of Barnet and Hatfield, but with no success,--no such persons had been observed passing through. With the most considerate concern, he came to Longbourn and revealed his fears to us in a manner highly honourable to his heart. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F.; but no blame can be laid upon either of them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother expect the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make a private marriage in town more desirable to them than carrying out their original scheme; and even if _he_ could entertain such a design against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections, which is unlikely, can I believe her so utterly lost to every consideration? Impossible! I am grieved to find, however, that Colonel F. does not rely upon their being married: he shook his head when I spoke of my hopes, and said he feared W. was not a man to be depended on.\"}"
