r; but it was widely suspected that he was a Nihilist agent, and there was no doubt that the Russian Embassy viewed his presence in London with disapproval. Lord Arthur thought he was exactly the man he needed, and one morning drove to his rooms in Bloomsbury to seek his advice and help.\\n\\n\u2018So you are taking politics seriously?\u2019 said Count Rouvaloff, when Lord Arthur had explained the purpose of his visit. But Lord Arthur, who disliked any sort of swagger, felt obliged to admit that he had not the slightest interest in social questions, and wanted the explosive device only for a purely family affair, one that concerned no one but himself.\"}"
