{"request_id":"42966d905ba821681669b4d0","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when, turning suddenly, I found myself near the lake, beside a small cove where a little skiff was tied. It was not far from our house, and I saw my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, being used to such things, pushed it from the shore and rowed across with all my strength. As I came toward them, dressed in white and covered only by my tartan rachan, with my hair streaming over my shoulders, moving faster than anyone would have thought I could make the boat go, my father often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. I reached the shore; my father held the boat, I sprang lightly out, and in a moment I was in his arms.

Then I began to live. Everything around me changed from dull sameness into the brightest scene of joy and pleasure. The happiness I found in my father’s company went far beyond my hopeful expectations. We were always together, and we never ran out of things to discuss. He had spent the sixteen years of his absence among peoples almost unknown in Europe. He had travelled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, and had entered the homes of the native people with a freedom allowed to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his stories, and his descriptions of the landscapes filled many delightful hours, when we had grown tired of speaking about our own plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I listened with delight when he told me what he had felt for me during those long years when he had seemed to forget me. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl. But later, as my grief faded and hope returned, I could think only of her; and among cities and deserts, her little fairy form, as I imagined it, was always before me. The northern breeze, when it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and gentler because it appeared to carry some of your spirit with it. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island, where we would live in peace forever. As I came back, my eager hopes were crushed by so many fears; my impatience became almost unbearable. I dared not think that the sun might shine and the moon rise over your grave instead of your living form. But no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my comfort, and my hope.”"}

