          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6f17832e4b45fb38aa6bd93\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I returned, I found the following letter from my father:\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting anxiously for a letter that would set the date of your return to us. At first I was tempted to write only a few lines, simply telling you the day I expected you. But that would be a cruel mercy, and I cannot do it. How shocked would you be, my son, if you expected a joyful welcome and instead found tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I tell you of our misfortune? Your absence cannot have made you indifferent to our happiness and sorrow; how can I bring pain to my son, who has been away so long? I want to prepare you for this dreadful news, but I know it is impossible; even now your eyes must be racing over the page, searching for the words that will bring you these terrible tidings.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that sweet child, whose smiles brought warmth and joy to my heart, who was so gentle and yet so lively! Victor, he has been murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you, but will simply tell you what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday, May 7th, I, my niece, and your two brothers went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, and we walked farther than usual. It was already growing dark when we thought of returning, and then we realized that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were nowhere to be found. We sat down on a bench to wait for them. Soon Ernest came and asked whether we had seen his brother. He said they had been playing together, that William had run off to hide, and that he had searched for him without success. He had then waited a long time, but William had not returned.\\n\\n\u201cThis worried us, and we kept looking for him until night came. Then Elizabeth guessed that he might have gone back to the house. But he was not there. We went out again with torches, for I could not rest while I thought of my sweet boy lost and exposed to the cold dampness and dew of the night. Elizabeth too was in terrible distress. At about five in the morning, I found my lovely boy, whom I had seen the night before healthy, bright, and active, lying on the grass, pale and still; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was carried home, and the grief on my face revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She begged urgently to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she insisted. Entering the room where it lay, she quickly examined the victim\u2019s neck and, clasping her hands, cried, \u2018O God!\u2019\"}"
