{"request_id":"e7a1eee6f8ec89e55973b880","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which sustained me at the beginning of my career, now only drives me deeper into the dust. All my plans and hopes are nothing, and, like the archangel who sought unlimited power, I am bound in an eternal hell. My imagination was strong, and my ability to analyze and act was equally powerful; together, these qualities led me to imagine and create a man. Even now, I cannot remember my dreams while the work was unfinished without deep emotion. In my thoughts I walked in heaven, sometimes rejoicing in my power, sometimes burning with thoughts of what it might accomplish. From childhood I had been filled with great hopes and proud ambition; but how low I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this ruined state. Despair rarely entered my heart; a great destiny seemed to carry me forward, until I fell, never, never to rise again.”

Must I then lose this remarkable being? I had long wished for a friend; I had searched for someone who would understand and love me. Here, on these empty seas, I have found such a person, but I fear I have found him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I would persuade him to live, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind wishes toward so miserable a creature; but when you speak of new bonds and new affections, do you think any could replace those who are gone? Could any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even when our feelings are not deeply stirred by some special excellence, the companions of childhood always hold a power over our minds that few later friends can gain. They know our childhood natures, which, though later changed, are never completely erased; and they can judge our actions more surely by understanding the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never—unless signs of it appeared early—suspect the other of deceit or dishonesty, while another friend, however deeply attached, may still be viewed with suspicion despite himself. But I had friends dear to me not only through habit and shared memories, but because of their own worth; and wherever I am, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and Clerval’s conversation will always whisper in my ear. They are dead, and only one feeling in such loneliness could persuade me to keep living. If I were involved in some great task or purpose, one that would bring wide benefit to my fellow creatures, then I could live to complete it."}

