          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82715f3df770a8318464229d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With matters settled so pleasantly and satisfactorily, the agreement was formally confirmed over another teacupful of peppermint mixture, made all the more necessary by the lady\u2019s nervous agitation. As she drank it, she told Mr. Bumble of the old woman\u2019s death.\\n\\n\u201cVery good,\u201d said that gentleman, sipping his peppermint. \u201cI\u2019ll call at Sowerberry\u2019s on my way home, and tell him to send someone tomorrow morning. Was that what frightened you, love?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt was nothing particular, dear,\u201d said the lady, avoiding the question.\\n\\n\u201cIt must have been something, love,\u201d Mr. Bumble insisted. \u201cWon\u2019t you tell your own B.?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot now,\u201d replied the lady. \u201cSome other day. After we\u2019re married, dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAfter we\u2019re married!\u201d cried Mr. Bumble. \u201cIt wasn\u2019t any insolence from any of them male paupers as\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, love!\u201d the lady quickly interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cIf I thought it was,\u201d continued Mr. Bumble, \u201cif I thought any one of \u2019em had dared to raise his vulgar eyes to that lovely face\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey wouldn\u2019t have dared, love,\u201d replied the lady.\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019d better not!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, clenching his fist. \u201cLet me see any man, parish or outside the parish, who would presume to do it; and I can tell him he wouldn\u2019t do it a second time!\u201d\\n\\nWithout all his violent gestures, this might not have seemed a very great compliment to the lady\u2019s beauty. But Mr. Bumble made his threat with many warlike motions, and she was deeply moved by this proof of his devotion. With great admiration, she declared that he was truly a dove.\\n\\nThe dove then turned up his coat collar and put on his cocked hat. After exchanging a long, affectionate embrace with his future wife, he once more faced the cold night wind. He paused only a few minutes in the male paupers\u2019 ward, to scold them a little, so that he could assure himself he was capable of the necessary harshness for the post of workhouse-master. Satisfied with his qualifications, Mr. Bumble left the building light-hearted, with bright dreams of his coming promotion. These occupied his mind until he reached the undertaker\u2019s shop.\\n\\nMr. and Mrs. Sowerberry had gone out for tea and supper, and Noah Claypole was never willing to exert himself beyond what was needed for the convenient business of eating and drinking. So the shop had not been closed, though it was past the usual closing hour. Mr. Bumble tapped his cane on the counter several times, but got no response. Seeing a light through the glass window of the little parlour behind the shop, he boldly peeped in to see what was happening; and when he saw it, he was greatly surprised.\"}"
