          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e25b572c33b5442e3dabb53\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February; and Anne, after a month in Bath, had become increasingly anxious for tidings from Uppercross and Lyme. She wished to know far more than Mary had told her. Three weeks had passed since she had heard anything. All she knew was that Henrietta had returned home; and that Louisa, though thought to be mending quickly, remained at Lyme; and one evening, while she was dwelling earnestly on them all, a letter from Mary, thicker than usual, was brought to her; and, to heighten both pleasure and surprise, it came with Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts were in Bath! It was a circumstance to engage her interest. They were people towards whom her feelings naturally inclined.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d exclaimed Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts are come to Bath? The Crofts who have Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! such letters make very useful passports. They procure an introduction. I should have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know the attention owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could attend no longer; she could scarcely have said whether the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion had escaped notice; her letter wholly occupied her. It had been commenced some days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI offer no excuse for not writing, for I know how little letters are valued in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to concern yourself with Uppercross, which, as you know very well, supplies little worth writing of. We have had a most tiresome Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not given a single dinner party throughout the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. The holidays, at any rate, have ended at last: I do not believe children ever had such lengthy ones. I am certain I never did. The house was emptied yesterday, apart from the little Harvilles; but you will be astonished to learn that they have not gone home at all. Mrs Harville must be a strange mother to leave them away so long. I cannot comprehend it. They are by no means agreeable children, in my opinion; yet Mrs Musgrove appears to like them as much as, if not more than, her own grandchildren. What terrible weather we have had! Perhaps it is not noticed in Bath, with your fine pavements; but in the country it matters a good deal. No creature has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who has called much more frequently than was desirable. Between ourselves, I think it a great misfortune that Henrietta did not stay at Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her somewhat out of his path.\"}"
