          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2461d934aadefc67ba643e25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In justice to the young lady, it must be said that she did not flatly declare she would not, but only expressed a strong and earnest wish to be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did; a courteous and delicate way of declining the proposal, which proved her to possess that natural refinement which cannot endure to give a fellow-creature the pain of a plain and direct refusal.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s face sank. He turned from this young lady, who was cheerfully, if not splendidly, dressed in a red gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other woman.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d said the Jew gently, \u201cwhat do _you_ say?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it won\u2019t answer; so there\u2019s no use trying it on, Fagin,\u201d Nancy replied.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by that?\u201d asked Mr. Sikes, raising his head with a sullen look.\\n\\n\u201cWhat I say, Bill,\u201d returned the lady calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re the very person for it,\u201d argued Mr. Sikes; \u201cnobody round here knows anything about you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd as I don\u2019t want them to, either,\u201d answered Nancy, with the same composure, \u201cit\u2019s much more no than yes with me, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes was correct. Through a succession of threats, promises, and bribes, the lady concerned was at last persuaded to accept the errand. She was not, in truth, restrained by the same motives as her pleasant companion; for, having lately moved into the neighbourhood of Field Lane from the distant yet fashionable suburb of Ratcliffe, she had no equal fear of being recognised by any among her many acquaintances.\\n\\nAccordingly, with a clean white apron fastened over her gown, and her curl-papers tucked beneath a straw bonnet\u2014both items of attire supplied from the Jew\u2019s unfailing store\u2014Miss Nancy made ready to set out upon her mission.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment, my dear,\u201d said the Jew, bringing out a small covered basket. \u201cCarry that in one hand. It gives a more respectable appearance, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door-key in the other, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes; \u201cit looks real and genivine-like.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, my dear, it does indeed,\u201d said the Jew, placing a large street-door key upon the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere; very good! Very good indeed, my dear!\u201d cried the Jew, rubbing his hands.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d cried Nancy, breaking into tears and clutching the little basket and street-door key in an agony of grief. \u201cWhat has become of him? Where have they taken him? Oh, have pity, and tell me what they have done with the dear boy, gentlemen; do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
