          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42a7494ace844ab400f7fec5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Madame and Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye were not slain by spirits. Those who committed the act were corporeal, and made their escape in a corporeal way. How, then? Happily, there is only one method of reasoning on this matter, and that method must bring us to a fixed conclusion. Let us consider, one by one, every possible avenue of escape. It is evident that the murderers were in the chamber where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye was discovered, or at least in the adjoining room, when the party came up the stairs. Thus it is from these two rooms alone that we need seek exits. The police have stripped bare the floors, ceilings, and wall masonry in every direction. No secret passage could have eluded their scrutiny. Yet, not relying upon their eyes, I inspected them myself. There were, therefore, no hidden outlets. Both doors from the rooms into the passage were firmly locked, the keys remaining within. Let us look to the chimneys. Though of usual breadth for eight or ten feet above the hearths, they would not, for their whole length, admit the body of a large cat. Since escape by the means already named is thus absolutely impossible, we are driven to the windows. No one could have fled through those of the front room without being observed by the crowd below in the street. The assassins must, then, have gone through the back-room windows. Having reached this conclusion by reasoning so unmistakable, we must not, as reasoners, cast it aside because of seeming impossibilities. It remains only to show that these seeming \u2018impossibilities\u2019 are not, in truth, impossible.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two windows in the chamber. One is free of obstruction by furniture and lies fully exposed to view. The lower part of the other is concealed by the head of the cumbersome bedstead, pressed close against it. The first was found securely fastened on the inside. It withstood the greatest force of those who tried to lift it. A large gimlet-hole had been bored in the frame on the left, and a very stout nail was found set in it almost to its head. On examining the second window, a like nail was found similarly placed; and a strong effort to raise that sash likewise failed. The police were now wholly convinced that no escape had occurred in these directions. Therefore, they considered it needless to remove the nails and open the windows.\\n\\n\u201cMy own inspection was rather more exact, for the reason I have just stated\u2014because I knew that here all apparent impossibilities must be demonstrated to be no impossibilities in reality.\"}"
