          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6217c925001d51f5f59582d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to think, and scarcely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth, as soon as she had finished this letter, immediately took up the other. Opening it with the greatest impatience, she read as follows. It had been written a day after the first ended.\\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received my hurried letter. I hope this one may be clearer; but though I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to make sense. Dearest Lizzy, I scarcely know what to write, but I have bad news for you, and it cannot wait. However imprudent a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now anxious merely to know that it has happened, for there is far too much reason to fear they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, only a few hours after the express. Although Lydia\u2019s short letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe they were going to Gretna Green, Denny let fall something that showed he believed W. had never intended to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel F., who, immediately alarmed, left B. to trace their route. He followed them easily as far as Clapham, but no farther; for when they entered that place, they got into a hackney coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known after that is that they were seen continuing along the London road. I do not know what to think. After making every possible inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. came into Hertfordshire, anxiously asking about them at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but with no success\u2014no such people had been seen passing through. With the kindest concern, he came to Longbourn and told us of his fears in a way that does great credit to his heart. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F.; but no blame can be placed on them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make a private marriage in town more suitable for them than following their original plan; and even if he could form such a scheme against a young woman with Lydia\u2019s connections, which is unlikely, can I believe that she is so utterly lost to all sense? Impossible! I am grieved to find, however, that Colonel F. does not rely on their marriage. He shook his head when I expressed my hopes, and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\"}"
