{"request_id":"50e04dfc422bdd9642876f64","rewritten_text":"Still, even then I had not drained the bitter cup to its lees; I was not wholly convinced of my bereavement; I did not yet feel, in every throb, every nerve, every thought, that I alone survived of my kind,—that I was the LAST MAN.

The sky grew overcast, and at sunset a fine rain began to fall. The everlasting heavens themselves are weeping, I thought; is it shameful, then, that mortal man should waste himself in tears? I recalled the old fables, where men are said to have melted through weeping into fountains that flowed for ever. Ah! would that it were so; then my fate might in some degree resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Oh! grief is fanciful; it spins a web upon which it marks the tale of its misery from every shape and alteration around it; it blends itself with all animate nature; it draws nourishment from every object; like light, it pervades all things, and, like light, lends them its own hues.

In my wandering search I had gone some way from the place where I had been thrown ashore, and reached one of those watch-towers which stand at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I welcomed its shelter, and welcomed the sight of a thing made by human hands, after so long contemplating nature's bleak desolation; I therefore entered and climbed the rude spiral stair to the guard-room. Thus far fate had dealt gently with me: no dreadful trace of its former occupants remained. A few boards placed across two iron trestles, and covered with dry Indian-corn leaves, formed the bed offered me; while an open chest, holding several half-mouldered biscuits, stirred an appetite that perhaps had been present before, though I had not until then perceived it. Thirst too, fierce and burning, caused by the sea-water I had swallowed and by my body's exhaustion, afflicted me. Kindly nature had joined pleasurable feelings to the relief of such needs, so that I—even I!—felt restored and soothed as I ate this wretched food and drank a little sour wine from a flask half full, left in that deserted habitation. Then I lay down upon the bed, which no shipwrecked sufferer need despise. The earthy scent of the dry leaves was balm to me after the loathsome smell of seaweed. I forgot my solitude. I looked neither behind nor ahead; my senses sank into rest; I slept, and dreamed of beloved inland scenes—of haymakers, of a shepherd whistling to his dog when he called for help in driving the flock into fold; of the sights and sounds belonging to my mountain childhood, long since forgotten."}

