          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0df0063a8a2f3bbd48749bb4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself enough to visit the Coles. The next day, the visit gave her many pleasant memories; and whatever she might be thought to have lost in dignified privacy was surely more than repaid by the glory of being popular. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014good people, who deserved to be pleased!\u2014and left behind a name they would not soon forget.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is rare; and there were two things that made her slightly uneasy. She wondered whether she had failed in the duty one woman owes another by revealing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill. It had hardly been right; but the idea had been so strong that it slipped out, and his ready acceptance of everything she said was such a compliment to her insight that she found it hard to be entirely sure she ought to have kept silent.\\n\\nHer other regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and about this she had no doubt. She sincerely and plainly regretted that her own playing and singing were inferior. She deeply blamed the laziness of her childhood\u2014and sat down to practise hard for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet\u2019s arrival; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not put us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear\u2014I think you play better than either of you. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am sure I would much rather listen to you. Everybody last night said how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything about it must have felt the difference. The truth is, Harriet, my playing is only good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far beyond it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is any difference, nobody could ever discover it. Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill spoke a great deal about your taste, and said he valued taste much more than skill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I could see that she had skill, but I did not know she had any taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing.\u2014You cannot understand a word of it. Besides, if she does play so very well, you know, it is only what she must do, because she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
