          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d4ad4b441e56da72cd703e56\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If she had known that her sister meant to pull her away from hopes and realities like these, what would she have felt? Only her mother could have understood it, for she might have felt almost the same. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was all that comforted her for the sad certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they knew nothing of what had happened, and their excitement continued almost without pause until the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since returning home, she had often been in his company, and her agitation had mostly passed; the feelings of former preference had vanished entirely. She had even learned to see, in the gentleness that had first charmed her, an affected quality and a sameness that tired and disgusted her. His present behaviour toward her gave her fresh reason to dislike him. He soon showed an intention of renewing the attentions he had paid her early in their acquaintance, and after all that had happened, this could only provoke her. She lost all concern for him when she found herself chosen as the object of such empty, frivolous flirtation. Though she firmly discouraged it, she could not help feeling insulted by his belief that, however long or for whatever reason he had withdrawn his attentions, he could flatter her vanity and win back her preference whenever he chose simply by renewing them.\\n\\nOn the regiment\u2019s very last day in Meryton, he dined at Longbourn with several other officers. Elizabeth was so little inclined to part from him pleasantly that, when he asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both stayed three weeks at Rosings, and asked whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s thought, his smile returned, and he replied that he had often seen him before. After saying that he was a very gentlemanlike man, he asked how she had liked him. Her answer was warmly favourable. Soon afterward, with an indifferent air, he added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves as one gets to know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look Elizabeth did not miss. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d But stopping himself, he added in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in his manner of speaking that he improves?\u201d\"}"
