          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"865ffc6d64c1e994b88d0e84\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor bewildered village boy, escape that extraordinary inconsistency into which even the best and wisest men fall every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be perfectly true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, when I reached that point, I threw myself face down and seized the hair on either side of my head, pulling it hard. All the while, I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so foolish and so misplaced that I was quite aware my face would have deserved it if I had raised it by my hair and struck it against the pebbles, as punishment for belonging to such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she did not try to reason with me any further. She laid her hand\u2014comforting though roughened by work\u2014on each of mine in turn, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder to soothe me, while, with my face against my sleeve, I cried a little\u2014just as I had done in the brewery yard\u2014and felt dimly certain that someone, or everyone, had treated me very badly; I could not say which.\\n\\n\u201cI am glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cwhich is that you felt able to trust me, Pip. And I am glad of another: that, of course, you know you can rely on my keeping your trust and always deserving it as far as I can. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor one, and so much in need of teaching herself!\u2014were your teacher now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a difficult lesson to learn, and you have gone beyond her, so it is no use now.\u201d Then, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a newly cheerful, pleasant voice, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI will always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you become a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never will, so that means always. Not that I need tell you anything, because you know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d Biddy said, almost in a whisper, looking away toward the ships. Then, returning to her earlier pleasant tone, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did; and the summer afternoon softened into summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
