          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"07adea1b4e8b681e41743634\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox!\\n\\nI rushed to the king and spoke in his ear:\\n\\n\u201cOut the door at once, sire! The woman is dying of the disease that ravaged the outskirts of Camelot two years ago.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not move.\\n\\n\u201cTruly, I shall stay\u2014and help as well.\u201d\\n\\nI whispered again:\\n\\n\u201cKing, you must not. You have to go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mean well, and you speak wisely. But it would shame a king to show fear, and shame a knight to hold back his hand where people need help. Peace, I will not go. You must go. The Church\u2019s ban is not upon me, but it forbids you to be here, and it will punish you severely if it learns of your trespass.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a terrible place for him to be, and it might cost him his life, but there was no use arguing. If he thought his knightly honor was involved, that ended the matter; he would remain, and nothing could stop him. I knew that. So I let the subject drop. The woman spoke:\\n\\n\u201cKind sir, will you climb that ladder and bring me word of what you find? Do not fear to tell me, for there are times when even a mother\u2019s heart cannot break any further\u2014because it is broken already.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWait,\u201d said the king. \u201cGive the woman something to eat. I will go.\u201d And he set down the knapsack.\\n\\nI turned to leave, but the king had already gone ahead. He stopped and looked down at a man lying in the dim light, who had not noticed us or spoken so far.\\n\\n\u201cIs that your husband?\u201d the king asked.\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank God for that one mercy, yes\u2014these three hours. How can I ever repay my gratitude? My heart is bursting with thanks for the sleep he sleeps now.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cWe will be careful. We will not wake him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, no, you will not, for he is dead.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDead?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, what a triumph it is to know it! No one can hurt him now, no one can insult him again. He is in heaven now, and happy; or if not, he waits in hell and is content, for there he will find neither abbot nor bishop. We were boy and girl together; we were husband and wife for five and twenty years, never apart until this day. Think how long that is to love and suffer together. This morning he was out of his mind, and imagined we were boy and girl again, wandering through the happy fields. In that innocent, joyful talk he wandered farther and farther, still chatting lightly, until he entered those other fields we know nothing of, and was shut away from mortal sight.\"}"
