          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c6eb79401b1d1dbfc6239b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I could not stay away from the places where they were, but felt that they were more promising and less hopeless when I was near them. In this irrational uneasiness and distress of mind, I would walk the streets in the evening, passing those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even now, the tired western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their lines of severe, closed-up houses and their long rows of lamps, are melancholy to me because of that association.\\n\\nThe daily visits I was allowed to make to him were now shorter, and he was kept under stricter watch. Seeing, or imagining, that I was suspected of meaning to bring him poison, I asked to be searched before sitting beside his bed, and told the officer who was always present that I was willing to do anything that would convince him my intentions were honest. No one was cruel to him or to me. A duty had to be performed, and it was performed, but not harshly. The officer always assured me that he was worse; and some other sick prisoners in the room, along with other prisoners who tended them as nurses\u2014criminals, but not incapable of kindness, thank God!\u2014always gave the same report.\\n\\nAs the days passed, I noticed more and more that he would lie peacefully gazing at the white ceiling, with no light in his face until some word from me brightened it for a moment; then it would fade again. Sometimes he was nearly, or entirely, unable to speak. Then he answered me with faint pressures of my hand, and I came to understand his meaning very well.\\n\\nTen days had passed when I saw a greater change in him than I had yet seen. His eyes were turned toward the door, and brightened when I entered.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said as I sat beside his bed, \u201cI thought you were late. But I knew you couldn\u2019t be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is exactly the time,\u201d I said. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always wait at the gate, don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. So as not to lose a moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear boy, thank you. God bless you! You have never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand in silence, for I could not forget that I had once intended to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou have been more comfortable beside me while I was under a dark cloud than when the sun was shining.\u201d\"}"
