          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dfafa3092044e0688e79f694\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried to the skies on flowery beds of ease,\\n\\nWhile others fight to win the prize and sail through bloody seas?\\n\\nHe was considered a remarkable reader. At church socials, he was always asked to read poetry; and when he finished, the ladies would raise their hands and let them drop helplessly into their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn was sung, the Reverend Mr. Sprague became a bulletin board and read out notices of meetings, societies, and other matters until it seemed the list would continue until doomsday\u2014a strange custom still maintained in America, even in cities, here in this age of plentiful newspapers. Often, the less reason there is for a traditional custom, the harder it is to get rid of it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a good, generous prayer, full of particulars: it pleaded for the church and the church\u2019s little children; for the other churches in the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the State officials; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for government officers; for poor sailors tossed on stormy seas; for the oppressed millions groaning beneath the heel of European monarchies and Oriental despotisms; for those who had the light and the good news, yet had no eyes to see or ears to hear; for the heathen on far islands of the sea; and it ended by asking that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, and be like seed sown in fertile soil, producing in time a grateful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustle of dresses, and the standing congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if he even did that much. He was restless throughout; unconsciously, he kept track of its details, for he was not listening, but he knew the ground well and the clergyman\u2019s usual route across it. Whenever some small new matter was inserted, his ear caught it and his whole nature rebelled; he thought additions unfair and downright scoundrelly. In the middle of the prayer, a fly landed on the back of the pew before him and tormented his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, wrapping its arms around its head, and polishing it so vigorously that it seemed nearly to separate from its body, exposing the thin thread of a neck; by scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them against its body as though they were coattails; by performing its entire toilet as peacefully as if it knew itself perfectly safe.\"}"
