          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8902aac856d74635d6941eb3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou won\u2019t, will you? Oh, you dear innocent!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s wicked, isn\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d asked Charley Bates. \u201cHe\u2019ll end up being scragged, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know what that means,\u201d Oliver replied.\\n\\n\u201cLike this, old fellow,\u201d said Charley. As he spoke, Master Bates seized one end of his neckerchief and held it upright in the air; then he let his head fall onto his shoulder and made a strange choking sound through his teeth, vividly acting out that scragging and hanging meant the same thing.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s what it means,\u201d said Charley. \u201cLook how he stares, Jack! I never saw such splendid company as this boy; he\u2019ll be the death of me, I know he will.\u201d Having laughed heartily once more, Master Charley Bates returned to his pipe, tears in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve been badly brought up,\u201d said the Dodger, admiring his boots with great satisfaction after Oliver had polished them. \u201cStill, Fagin will make something of you, or you\u2019ll be the first he\u2019s ever had who didn\u2019t turn out profitable. You\u2019d better start right away, because you\u2019ll come to the trade sooner than you think; and you\u2019re only wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates supported this advice with several moral lessons of his own. When he had run out of them, he and his friend Mr. Dawkins launched into an enthusiastic account of the many pleasures of the life they lived, dropping various hints to Oliver that the best thing he could do was win Fagin\u2019s favour without further delay, by using the same means they had used to gain it.\\n\\n\u201cAnd remember this, Nolly,\u201d said the Dodger, as they heard the Jew unlocking the door upstairs, \u201cif you don\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking like that?\u201d interrupted Master Bates. \u201cHe doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take handkerchiefs and watches,\u201d said the Dodger, bringing his speech down to Oliver\u2019s level, \u201csome other fellow will. Then the people who lose them will be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off too, and nobody will be a halfpenny better off except the chaps who get them\u2014and you\u2019ve as much right to them as they have.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course!\u201d said the Jew, who had entered without Oliver noticing. \u201cThat puts it in a nutshell, my dear; in a nutshell. Take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He understands the catechism of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands together gleefully as he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s reasoning in these words, and chuckled with pleasure at his pupil\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe conversation went no further then, because the Jew had come home with Miss Betsy and a gentleman Oliver had never seen before. The Dodger greeted him as Tom Chitling; after lingering on the stairs to exchange a few flirtatious remarks with the lady, he now came in.\"}"
