{"request_id":"58074700fc3635843a9a9ac0","rewritten_text":"You see, I had put my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things; but it was the kind of helmet you cannot take off by yourself. That had not occurred to me when I put it there; in fact, I did not know it. I thought it would be especially convenient there. So now, knowing it was so near, so handy, and yet impossible to reach, made it all the worse and harder to bear. Yes, the thing you cannot get is usually the thing you want most; everybody has noticed that. Well, it took my mind off everything else; took it completely away and fixed it on my helmet. Mile after mile, it stayed there, thinking of the handkerchief, seeing the handkerchief in my mind; and it was bitter and maddening to have salty sweat trickling into my eyes when I could not reach it. It may sound like a small matter on paper, but it was not small at all; it was the purest misery. I would not say so if it were not true. I decided that next time I would carry a reticule, no matter how it looked or what people said. Of course those iron-clad fellows of the Round Table would think it disgraceful, and perhaps raise Sheol about it, but for my part, give me comfort first and style afterward. So we jogged on, and every now and then we crossed a dusty stretch, where the dust rose in clouds, got into my nose, and made me sneeze and cry; and naturally I said things I should not have said. I do not deny it. I am no better than other people.

We could not seem to meet anyone in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in, that was fortunate for the ogre—at least, for an ogre carrying a handkerchief. Most knights would have thought only of taking his armor; but if I got his bandanna, he could keep his hardware, as far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile, it was getting hotter and hotter inside that armor. The sun kept beating down and heating the iron more every minute. Well, when you are hot in that way, every little thing bothers you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that irritated me; besides, I could not stand the shield slapping and banging, first against my chest, then around my back. And if I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and squealed in the tiresome way a wheelbarrow does. Since we made no breeze at that pace, I was likely to be roasted in that stove; and besides, the slower you moved, the more heavily the iron settled on you, and the more tons you seemed to weigh with every minute."}

