{"request_id":"d074b29fd41528fdba2a9180","rewritten_text":"It then showed my altitude to be 132,000 feet, or five-and-twenty miles; and I was therefore at that moment surveying a portion of the earth’s surface equal to no less than the three hundred-and-twentieth part of its whole superficies. At nine o’clock I once more lost sight of land eastward, though not until I had perceived that the balloon was drifting swiftly toward the N. N. W. The curvature of the ocean below was exceedingly plain, although my prospect was frequently broken by masses of cloud floating hither and thither. I now remarked that even the faintest vapors never ascended more than ten miles above the sea-level.

“At half past nine I made the experiment of casting a handful of feathers through the valve. Instead of floating, as I had anticipated, they fell straight downward, like a bullet, all together and with the utmost swiftness—vanishing from sight in only a few seconds. At first I could not account for this singular occurrence, being unable to suppose that my ascending speed had suddenly acquired so enormous an acceleration. Soon, however, it struck me that the atmosphere had become much too rare to support even feathers; that they were truly falling, as they seemed to be, with extreme rapidity; and that I had been startled by the combined velocities of their descent and my own ascent.

“By ten o’clock I discovered that little required my immediate care. Everything proceeded smoothly, and I judged the balloon to be rising at a speed that increased from moment to moment, although I no longer possessed any means of determining the progress of that increase. I felt neither pain nor discomfort of any sort, and was in higher spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam, occupying myself alternately with inspecting the condition of my various apparatus and renewing the atmosphere in the chamber. I resolved to attend to this latter matter at stated intervals of forty minutes, rather for the preservation of my health than because such frequent renewal was strictly needed. Meanwhile, I could not refrain from anticipation. Fancy rioted amid the moon’s wild and dreamlike regions. Imagination, conscious that she was for once released from restraint, wandered freely among the shifting marvels of a dim and uncertain land. At one time there were ancient, venerable forests, rugged precipices, and cataracts plunging noisily into bottomless gulfs. Then I found myself abruptly amid quiet noonday solitudes, where no breath of heaven ever entered, and where broad fields of poppies and delicate, lily-like flowers stretched away for a weary distance, forever silent and unmoving."}

