          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4554363f1599dead189f9575\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t rush at him suddenly and risk making a clumsy, half-finished job of it; no, I move well off to one side and work my way toward him gradually, so he never suspects I mean to strike him at all. Then, before he knows it, he is flat on his back and cannot, for the life of him, tell how it happened. That was how I handled Brother Dowley. I began talking in an easy, idle way, as though I were merely passing the time; and the oldest man alive could not have figured out from where I began where I meant to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a great many curious things about law, custom, usage, and all that sort of thing, when you look into them; yes, and about the course and progress of human opinion and action, too. There are written laws\u2014they perish; but there are unwritten laws\u2014they are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says they must rise, little by little, through the centuries. Notice how it works. We know what wages are now, here, there, and everywhere; we take an average and call that the wages of today. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and two hundred years ago; that is as far back as we can go, but it is enough to give us the law of progress, the scale and rate of periodic increase. So, without a single document to help us, we can come quite close to deciding what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward, turn around, and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be on any future date you care to name, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years, wages will have risen to six times what they are now in your region, and farm laborers will be allowed 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf only I could die now and live then!\u201d interrupted Smug, the wheelwright, with a splendid greedy gleam in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that is not all; they will get their board besides\u2014such as it is: it will not make them fat. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014pay attention now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wages will be\u2014remember, this is law, not guesswork; a mechanic\u2019s wages will then be twenty cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a general gasp of astonished awe, and Dickon the mason murmured, raising his eyes and hands:\"}"
