          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fcef6b7fe714f8f645ca8826\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Country folk, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and vagabonds of every mean degree, were blended in one mass; the whistles of drovers, barking dogs, bellowing and plunging oxen, bleating sheep, grunting and squealing pigs, hawkers\u2019 cries, and shouts, oaths, and quarrels on every side; the clanging bells and roar of voices issuing from each public-house; the crowding, thrusting, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling; the dreadful discordant din that rose from every quarter of the market; and the unwashed, unshaven, ragged, filthy figures continually hurrying to and fro, bursting into and out of the throng; made it a deafening, bewildering spectacle, that utterly confused the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, pulling Oliver along behind him, forced his way through the densest part of the crowd, paying scant heed to the many sights and sounds that so amazed the boy. He nodded two or three times to a friend who passed; and, declining as many offers of a morning dram, pushed resolutely forward, until they had escaped the uproar and gone through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, glancing up at the clock of St. Andrew\u2019s Church, \u201cclose upon seven! you must move faster. Come, don\u2019t fall behind already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nMr. Sikes reinforced these words with a tug at his small companion\u2019s wrist; Oliver, increasing his pace to a sort of trot, midway between walking quickly and running, matched the house-breaker\u2019s long strides as best he might.\\n\\nThey continued at this pace until they had gone beyond Hyde Park corner and were travelling towards Kensington; then Sikes slackened until an empty cart, some distance behind them, drew level. Observing \u201cHounslow\u201d painted upon it, he asked the driver, with all the politeness he could command, whether he would carry them as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cGet in,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your lad?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; he\u2019s my lad,\u201d answered Sikes, fixing Oliver with a hard look and absently slipping his hand into the pocket that held the pistol.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a little too fast for you, eh, my man?\u201d asked the driver, noticing that Oliver was breathless.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes broke in. \u201cHe\u2019s accustomed to it. Here, hold my hand, Ned. Get in with you!\u201d\\n\\nSo speaking to Oliver, he lifted him into the cart; and the driver, indicating a pile of sacks, bade him lie down upon them and rest.\\n\\nAs they went by the several mile-stones, Oliver wondered increasingly where his companion intended to bring him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford all slipped behind them; yet they travelled onward as steadily as though their journey had only just commenced.\"}"
