{"request_id":"26b1bfddc30efaca92fc8f58","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me: I was never truly mad. Even when my wild thoughts seemed to force me toward insanity, I remained aware of my condition, and revealed them only to silence and solitude. Those around me saw none of it. They saw only a poor girl, crushed in spirit, who spoke softly and gently, and whose tears sometimes slipped from beneath her lowered eyelids despite her efforts to conceal them. One who loved to be alone and avoided notice; who never smiled—oh, no, I never smiled—and that was all.

At last I escaped. I left my guardian’s house and was never heard from again; the letters I left, together with other circumstances I arranged, led people to believe that I had taken my own life. I was therefore searched for less carefully than I might otherwise have been, and soon every trace and memory of me disappeared. I left London in a small vessel sailing to a port in northern England. Now that I had succeeded and was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently onward. I sat on deck beneath the open sky and felt myself transformed. I was no longer the wild, raving, utterly miserable Mathilda, but a young hermit, devoted to seclusion, who must strive to keep her heart free from all turmoil and unholy despair. The fanciful, nun-like dress I had adopted; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to myself; the solitude to which I was destined forever after—all these nourished gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze playing through my hair revived me, and with quiet eyes I watched the sunlight glittering on the waves and the birds chasing one another over the water, barely touching it with their feathers. I slept without dreams to disturb me, and woke refreshed to enjoy again my peaceful freedom.

Four days later we reached the harbour for which we had sailed. I would not stay on the coast, but went at once inland. I had already chosen the kind of place in which I would live: a solitary house on a broad plain, far from any other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander widely without being troubled by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the gentle course of my feelings depended upon being alone. I settled in a vast solitude: on a bleak heath scattered with stones, among which short grass grew, with here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

