          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1a2923d2f88180d161890e41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very fine-looking old gentleman, and a fine-looking younger one with his right arm in a sling. My souls, how the people shouted and laughed, and kept it up. But I couldn\u2019t see anything funny in it, and I figured it would be hard for the duke and king to see any joke either. I expected them to turn pale. But no, they didn\u2019t turn pale one bit. The duke never showed that he suspected what was happening; he just went around looking pleased and contented, like a jug bubbling out buttermilk. And the king only stared sadly at the newcomers, as if it gave his very heart a stomachache to think there could be such cheats and scoundrels in the world. Oh, he played it wonderfully. Many of the leading people gathered around the king, to show him they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely puzzled. Soon he began to speak, and I saw at once that he pronounced words like an Englishman\u2014not the king\u2019s way, though the king\u2019s was a pretty good imitation. I can\u2019t repeat the old gentleman\u2019s exact words, nor can I copy his accent; but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I wasn\u2019t expecting; and I\u2019ll admit, openly and honestly, that I\u2019m not very well prepared to face it or answer it. My brother and I have had bad luck: he has broken his arm, and our baggage was mistakenly put off at a town above here last night. I am Peter Wilks\u2019 brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can neither hear nor speak\u2014and now he can hardly even make signs, since he has only one hand to make them with. We are who we say we are, and in a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. But until then I won\u2019t say anything more. I\u2019ll go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nThen he and the new dummy started away. The king laughed and blurted out:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014very likely, ain\u2019t it? And very convenient, too, for a fraud who has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s mighty good\u2014and mighty clever\u2014under the circumstances!\u201d\\n\\nThen he laughed again, and so did everybody else, except three or four, maybe half a dozen. One was the doctor; another was a sharp-looking gentleman with an old-fashioned carpetbag made of carpet material. He had just come off the steamboat and was speaking quietly with the doctor, glancing toward the king now and then while they nodded their heads. It was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville. Another was a big, rough, powerful man who had come along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
