          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"51bbca2f89dced605b4103d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well after noon when he woke. His valet had slipped several times, on tiptoe, into the room to discover whether he was moving, and had marvelled at his young master\u2019s sleeping so late. At last his bell rang, and Victor entered quietly carrying a cup of tea and a heap of letters on a little tray of old S\u00e8vres china, then drew aside the olive-satin curtains, lined with gleaming blue, before the three high windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept well this morning,\u201d he said with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked sleepily.\\n\\n\u201cA quarter past one, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He raised himself and, after drinking a little tea, looked through his letters. One was from Lord Henry and had been delivered by hand that morning. He paused a moment, then laid it aside. The rest he opened without interest. They held the customary assortment of cards, dinner invitations, tickets for private views, programmes for charitable concerts, and such things as are poured each morning in the season upon fashionable young men. There was a fairly substantial account for a chased silver Louis-Quinze toilet-set, which he had not yet dared forward to his guardians. They were exceedingly old-fashioned people, unable to understand that we live in an age in which superfluous things alone are necessary. There were also several most politely phrased letters from Jermyn Street money-lenders, offering to provide any amount at a moment\u2019s notice and on the most reasonable terms of interest.\\n\\nAfter some ten minutes, he rose and, putting on an elaborate dressing-gown of silk-embroidered cashmere wool, went into the bathroom paved with onyx. The cold water revived him after his long sleep. He appeared to have forgotten everything he had endured. Once or twice a vague feeling that he had played a part in some odd tragedy came over him, but it possessed the unreality of a dream.\\n\\nWhen he was dressed, he went into the library and seated himself before a light French breakfast arranged on a small round table near the open window. It was a lovely day. The warm air seemed heavy with spices. A bee came flying in and hummed about the blue-dragon bowl, filled with sulphur-yellow roses, that stood before him. He felt entirely happy.\\n\\nAll at once his gaze rested on the screen he had set before the portrait, and he gave a start.\\n\\n\u201cIs Monsieur too cold?\u201d asked his valet, as he placed an omelette on the table. \u201cShall I close the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI am not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
