          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c36581d2f8da795e4cd0ce55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My heart beat fast, and, though I knew not why, I was afraid. I sent away the servant, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were its contents.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child\\n\\n\u201cI have violated your trust; I have sought to corrupt your mind, and have made your innocent heart familiar with the looks and speech of unlawful, monstrous passion. I must atone for these offences, and strive, in some measure, to make my punishment answer to my guilt. You are, I doubt not, prepared for what I am about to tell you; we must part and be separated for ever.\\n\\n\u201cI take from you your parent and sole friend. You are thrown, without shelter, upon the world: your hopes are ruined; the tranquillity and safety of your pure mind are destroyed; memory will present you with dreadful images of guilt, and the torment of innocent love deceived. Yet I, who bring all this wretchedness upon you; I, who drive you forth and pitilessly have stamped distrust and anguish upon the heart and brow of my own child, who with fiendish thoughtlessness have tried to rob her of her loveliness and put in its place the hideous deformity of sin; I, in the overflowing misery of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not seek your pity; you must, and do, loathe me: but forgive me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts pursue me in my exile with implacable anger. I must never again see you; never again hear your voice; yet the gentle murmurs of your forgiveness will reach me and soothe the burning of my disordered brain and heart; I am certain I should feel them even in my grave. And I dare support this plea by telling you how miserably I was ensnared in this web of fiery anguish, and of every struggle I made to free myself: indeed, were your soul less pure and radiant, I would not try to excuse myself before you; I should fear that, if I caused you to regard me with less horror, you might hate vice less. But in speaking to you, I feel as though I appealed to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must attempt to win it, or else despair. I implore you, therefore, to hear my words; and if guilt may in any degree be softened by keen agony and remorse that tears the brain like madness, perhaps you may believe\u2014though I dare not\u2014that I have some claim upon your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI beg you to remember our first happy life upon the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from a weary wandering of sixteen years, in which, though I had endured many perils and misfortunes, my affections had remained a complete blank.\"}"
