          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2856e720eee03684dd31adeb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must point out, Sir Walter,\u201d said Mr Shepherd one morning at Kellynch Hall, setting down the newspaper, \u201cthat the present moment is very much in our favour. This peace will bring all our wealthy naval officers ashore. They will all be looking for homes. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, to have a choice of tenants\u2014very reliable tenants. Many great fortunes have been made during the war. If a rich admiral should come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very fortunate man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter replied. \u201cThat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would indeed be a prize for him\u2014the greatest prize of all, even if he had captured ever so many before. Eh, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he was expected to do, at this joke, and then continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI venture to say, Sir Walter, that in business matters naval gentlemen are good people to deal with. I have had some small acquaintance with their way of conducting business, and I freely admit that they have very generous ideas and are as likely to make desirable tenants as any group of people one might encounter. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would suggest is this: if any rumours should spread about your intentions\u2014which must be considered possible, because we know how difficult it is to keep the actions and plans of one part of the world from the notice and curiosity of another; consequence has its price. I, John Shepherd, could hide any family affairs I chose, because no one would think me worth watching; but Sir Walter Elliot has eyes upon him that may be very hard to evade. So I will venture this much: it would not greatly surprise me if, despite all our caution, some rumour of the truth got abroad. And in that case, as I was about to say, since applications will certainly follow, I think any from our wealthy naval commanders would be especially worth considering; and I beg to add that I can be here within two hours at any time, to spare you the trouble of answering them.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter merely nodded. But shortly afterward, getting up and walking about the room, he remarked sarcastically\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are few among the gentlemen of the navy, I imagine, who would not be astonished to find themselves in a house of this kind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would certainly look about them and bless their good fortune,\u201d said Mrs Clay, who was present. Her father had driven her over, since nothing was so beneficial to Mrs Clay\u2019s health as a drive to Kellynch. \u201cBut I entirely agree with my father that a sailor might make a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
