          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"05e597da5b24f3da5bac6e0d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was something strange in all this that drew me in and enchanted me. It was as though he had awakened from a long, dreamlike sleep, and felt rather like one of the seven sleepers, or Nourjahad in that charming imitation of an eastern tale: Diana was gone; his friends had changed or died; and, when he awoke, I was all he had left to love on earth.\\n\\nHow dear the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond became to me, now that I had such a beloved companion on my walks. With my father, I visited every lovely place, whether on the islands or beside the waterfalls shaded by trees; every dark path and every little valley tangled with brushwood and ferns. His conversation broadened my thoughts. I felt remade, surrounded by the freshness and energy of a new life. Since his arrival, it seemed as if I had been carried from a small patch of earth into a universe without limits to imagination or understanding. Before, my life had been like a pleasant country stream, never meant to leave its native fields, but quietly to disappear when its work was done, leaving no mark behind. Now it seemed like a broad river flowing through a rich and beautiful landscape, always changing and always lovely. Alas! I did not know the desert it was soon to reach, the rocks that would tear its waters, or the dreadful scene that would be reflected, more horribly distorted, in its waves. Life was bright then; I was beginning to learn hope\u2014and what brings more bitter despair to the heart than hope destroyed?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should so quickly follow such heavenly happiness? I drank from an enchanted cup, but bitterness lay at the bottom of its long sweetness. My heart was filled with deep affection, yet it was calm because that feeling was so deep and full. I had never imagined that misery could come from love, and the lesson that everyone must eventually learn was taught to me in a way few are forced to endure. I mourn now, and always will mourn, those few short months of paradise. I broke no command, I ate no apple, yet I was cruelly driven from it. Alas! My companion did, and I was cast down with him in his fall. But I stray from my story\u2014let sorrow come at its proper time; at this point, I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship when my aunt became ill. I spent a whole month in her room nursing her, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for a time. Death is so terrible to those who remain alive; the bonds of habit are so strong, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must suffer when they are broken.\"}"
