          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c76cd658eccad8c60241edd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On Saturday nights in our village there was a pleasant feeling of tidying up and taking a peaceful halt before beginning life anew, and this encouraged Joe to venture on remaining out half an hour later on Saturdays than on other evenings. When the half-hour and the rum-and-water came to an end together, Joe rose to leave and took my hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait just a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the stranger. \u201cI believe I have a shining new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and, if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe picked it from a handful of loose coins, wrapped it in a bit of rumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cRemember! Your very own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, gazing at him much longer than good manners allowed, while clutching Joe tightly. He bade Joe good-night, and bade Mr. Wopsle good-night too, for Mr. Wopsle left with us; but to me he gave only a look from his aiming eye\u2014or rather, not a look, since he closed it, though an eye can work wonders by being hidden.\\n\\nOn our way home, had I felt inclined to speak, all the conversation would have had to come from me, because Mr. Wopsle left us at the Jolly Bargemen door, and Joe walked home with his mouth open as wide as possible, rinsing the rum away with air. But the reappearance of my former wrongdoing and old acquaintance had left me almost stunned, and I could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in an especially bad temper when we came into the kitchen, and that uncommon fact gave Joe courage to tell her of the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d said Mrs. Joe in triumph, \u201cor he\u2019d never have given it to the boy! Let us see it.\u201d\\n\\nI unwrapped it, and found it was a good shilling. \u201cBut what is this?\u201d cried Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were nothing less than two plump, sweaty one-pound notes, which appeared to have enjoyed the closest acquaintance with every cattle-market in the county. Joe seized his hat again and hurried with them to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was away, I sat on my usual stool, staring blankly at my sister, quite certain that the man would not be there.\\n\\nBefore long Joe returned, saying that the man had gone, though he, Joe, had left a message at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
