          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2b7d01ad7c94cd2b614a8ff4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last, we agreed to live together during my stay in the city. Since my finances were somewhat less troubled than his, I was allowed to pay for renting and furnishing a house suited to the strangely gloomy taste we shared: an old, grotesque mansion, long abandoned because of superstitions we did not investigate, and nearly falling down in a secluded, desolate part of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad the world known how we lived there, it would have considered us madmen\u2014though perhaps harmless ones. Our isolation was complete. We received no visitors. In fact, the location of our retreat had been carefully hidden even from my former acquaintances; and Dupin had long since ceased either to know anyone in Paris or to be known by them. We lived entirely within ourselves.\\n\\nMy friend had a whim\u2014what else can I call it?\u2014of loving the night for its own sake. I quietly joined him in this oddity, as I did in all his others, surrendering myself completely to his wild preferences. The dark goddess could not live with us always, but we could imitate her presence. At the first light of morning, we shut every grimy shutter in the old house and lit two heavily scented candles, which gave off only the weakest, most ghostly light. With their help, we occupied ourselves in dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the coming of real Darkness. Then we went out into the streets arm in arm, continuing the subjects of the day or wandering far and wide until late, seeking among the wild lights and shadows of the crowded city that endless mental excitement which quiet observation can provide.\\n\\nAt such times, I could not help noticing and admiring in Dupin a remarkable power of analysis, though his rich imagination had led me to expect it. He also seemed to take keen pleasure in using it\u2014if not precisely in showing it off\u2014and readily admitted the enjoyment it gave him. With a low, chuckling laugh, he would boast that most men wore windows in their chests when facing him, and he often supported such claims with direct, startling proofs of how intimately he knew my own thoughts. At these moments his manner was cold and distant; his eyes looked blank, while his voice, usually a rich tenor, rose to a high pitch that would have sounded peevish if not for the careful slowness and perfect clarity with which he spoke.\"}"
