          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ef3af659f304b7fd04776bf8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At least, he believed so. But who could know? ... No. There had been nothing else. Out of vanity, he had spared her. In hypocrisy, he had put on the mask of goodness. Out of curiosity, he had attempted self-denial. He saw that now.\\n\\nBut would this murder haunt him for the rest of his life? Must he always carry the weight of his past? Was he truly going to confess? Never. Only one piece of evidence remained against him. The picture itself\u2014that was the evidence. He would destroy it. Why had he kept it for so long? Once, he had taken pleasure in watching it change and grow old. Lately, it had given him no pleasure at all. It had kept him awake at night. Whenever he was away, he had been terrified that other eyes might see it. It had cast melancholy over his passions. The mere memory of it had spoiled many joyful moments. It had been like a conscience to him. Yes, it had been his conscience. He would destroy it.\\n\\nHe looked around and saw the knife that had stabbed Basil Hallward. He had cleaned it again and again until no trace of stain remained. It was bright and gleaming. Just as it had killed the painter, it would kill the painter\u2019s work, and everything it represented. It would kill the past, and once the past was dead, he would be free. It would kill this monstrous life of the soul, and without its hideous warnings, he would be at peace. He seized it and drove it into the picture.\\n\\nA cry rang out, followed by a crash. The cry was so terrible in its agony that the frightened servants woke and crept from their rooms. Two gentlemen passing through the square below stopped and looked up at the great house. They continued until they met a policeman, then brought him back. The man rang the bell several times, but no one answered. Apart from a light in one of the upper windows, the house was completely dark. After a while, he went away and stood beneath a neighboring portico, watching.\\n\\n\u201cWhose house is that, Constable?\u201d asked the older of the two gentlemen.\\n\\n\u201cMr. Dorian Gray\u2019s, sir,\u201d the policeman replied.\\n\\nAs they walked away, they exchanged looks and sneered. One of them was Sir Henry Ashton\u2019s uncle.\\n\\nInside, in the servants\u2019 quarters, the half-dressed domestics spoke to one another in hushed whispers. Old Mrs. Leaf was weeping and wringing her hands. Francis was pale as death.\\n\\nAfter about fifteen minutes, he fetched the coachman and one of the footmen, and they crept upstairs.\"}"
