          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"414757803000a9e28695aa24\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not everyone would have been so kind as she was, and refused to accept Jane\u2019s answer; but she firmly said she would not write any such refusal yesterday, as Jane wanted her to. She would wait\u2014and, sure enough, by yesterday evening it was all arranged for Jane to go. What a surprise to me! I had not the slightest idea! Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and told her immediately that, after considering the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s position, she had decided to accept it. I did not hear a word about it until everything was settled.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us; Mrs. Elton insisted that we come. It was arranged on the hill, while we were walking with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You must all spend the evening with us,\u2019 she said. \u2018I absolutely must have you all come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, then?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he declined from the beginning. Though I thought he would come, because Mrs. Elton declared she would not allow him to escape, he did not. But my mother, Jane, and I were all there, and we had a very pleasant evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse\u2014one must always find them agreeable, though everyone seemed rather tired after the morning party. Even pleasure, you know, can be tiring\u2014and I cannot say any of them appeared to have enjoyed it very much. Still, I shall always think it a very pleasant party, and feel extremely grateful to the kind friends who included me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you did not know it, had been making up her mind all day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time comes, it must be unwelcome to her and to all her friends\u2014but I hope her position will have every possible comfort\u2014I mean, in the character and manners of the family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, there is everything in the world to make her happy there. Except for the Sucklings and the Bragges, there is no other nursery establishment so generous and elegant in all Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge is a most delightful woman! Her style of living is almost equal to Maple Grove\u2014and as for the children, except for the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are no sweeter or more elegant children anywhere. Jane will be treated with such respect and kindness! It will be nothing but pleasure\u2014a life of pleasure. And her salary! I truly cannot bring myself to name her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse.\"}"
