          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12802252abc7bcadc0f174ba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, his face pale with terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile rushed out of Bentinck House. He forced his way through the crowd of fur-coated footmen gathered around the large striped awning, seeming neither to see nor hear anything. The night was bitterly cold, and the gas lamps around the square flared and flickered in the sharp wind; but his hands were feverishly hot, and his forehead burned like fire. He went on and on, walking almost like a drunken man. A policeman watched him curiously as he passed, and a beggar who came out from an archway to ask for money drew back in fear, seeing suffering greater than his own. Once, Lord Arthur stopped beneath a lamp and looked at his hands. He thought he could already see bloodstains on them, and a faint cry escaped his shaking lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had seen in his hand. Murder! The night itself seemed to know it, and the lonely wind seemed to howl it in his ear. It filled the dark corners of the streets. It grinned down at him from the rooftops.\\n\\nAt first he came to the Park, whose dark woods seemed to draw him in. He leaned wearily against the railings, pressing his brow to the cold wet metal and listening to the trembling silence of the trees. \u201cMurder! Murder!\u201d he kept saying, as if repeating the word might lessen its horror. The sound of his own voice made him shiver, yet he almost hoped Echo would hear him and wake the sleeping city from its dreams. He felt a mad urge to stop some passing stranger and tell him everything.\\n\\nThen he wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, wretched alleys. Two women with painted faces laughed at him as he passed. From a dark courtyard came the sounds of curses and blows, followed by sharp screams; and on a damp doorstep he saw the bent forms of poverty and old age huddled together. A strange pity came over him. Were these children of sin and misery destined for their fate, as he was for his? Were they, like him, only puppets in some monstrous show?\\n\\nYet it was not the mystery of suffering that struck him, but its absurdity: its complete uselessness, its grotesque lack of meaning. Everything seemed so confused! So without harmony! He was astonished by the gap between the shallow optimism of the day and the real facts of life. He was still very young.\\n\\nAfter a while, he found himself before Marylebone Church. The silent road looked like a long ribbon of polished silver, marked here and there by the dark, curling patterns of moving shadows.\"}"
