{"request_id":"48b7e54e720284e474173643","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sorrowful and despairing; she found no pleasure now in her usual pursuits; every enjoyment seemed to her a profanation against the dead; she believed that unending grief and tears were the proper offering she owed to innocence so cruelly ruined and destroyed. She was no longer the cheerful being who, in the days of our early youth, had roamed with me beside the lake and spoken rapturously of our hopes for the future. The first of those afflictions sent to detach us from the world had come upon her, and its shadow had extinguished her sweetest smiles.

“When I consider, my dear cousin,” she said, “the dreadful death of Justine Moritz, the world and all that is done in it no longer seem to me as they once did. Formerly, the stories of wickedness and injustice that I found in books or heard from others appeared like accounts from former ages or imagined misfortunes; at any rate, they were distant, and appealed more to reason than imagination. But now suffering has reached my own home, and men seem to me like monsters eager for one another’s blood. Yet I am surely unjust. Everyone believed that poor girl guilty; and had she been capable of the crime for which she died, she must indeed have been among the most depraved of human beings. For a handful of jewels, to murder the child of her benefactor and friend—a child she had cared for from birth, and seemed to love as though it were her own! I could never have agreed to the death of any person, yet I should certainly have considered such a being unfit to remain among mankind. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel that she was innocent; and you think so too, which strengthens my conviction. Alas, Victor! When falsehood may wear so perfect a likeness to truth, who can promise themselves secure happiness? I feel as though I stood upon the brink of a precipice, while thousands press around me, striving to force me down into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, while the murderer goes free; he moves through the world at liberty, perhaps even honoured. Yet though I myself were sentenced to die upon the scaffold for those same crimes, I would not exchange places with such a wretch.”

I heard her words in the deepest agony. Though not by my hand, I was in truth the murderer. Elizabeth saw my suffering in my face and, gently taking my hand, said, “My dearest friend, you must compose yourself. God knows how profoundly these events have affected me; but I am not as miserable as you are.”"}

