{"request_id":"a5cc1c83534a0919b99e858f","rewritten_text":"I then drilled a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted it with a soft wooden plug, cut into a tapering, cone-like shape. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out as needed until, after a few trials, I found the exact tightness at which the water leaking through the hole and falling into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This was, of course, quickly and easily determined by observing how much of the pitcher filled in a given time. Once this was arranged, the rest of the plan was clear. My bed was set up on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head rested directly beneath the mouth of the pitcher. At the end of an hour, when the pitcher became full, it would overflow at its mouth, which was slightly lower than its rim. And the water, falling from more than four feet above, could not help but strike my face, with the certain result of waking me instantly, even from the deepest sleep.

“It was nearly eleven when I finished these preparations, and I went straight to bed, fully confident that my invention would work. I was not disappointed. Exactly every sixty minutes, my faithful timepiece woke me. I emptied the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole, carried out the duties of the condenser, and returned to bed. These regular breaks in my sleep troubled me even less than I had expected; and when I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, with the sun already well above my horizon.

“April 3. I found the balloon at a truly immense height, and the earth’s visible curve had increased noticeably. In the ocean below lay a group of black specks, which were undoubtedly islands. Far to the north, I saw a thin, white, intensely bright line along the edge of the horizon, and I had no doubt that it was the southern edge of the ice of the Polar Sea. My curiosity was greatly stirred, for I hoped to travel much farther north and might perhaps, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole itself. I now regretted that my great height would, in that event, prevent me from making as accurate an observation as I wished.”"}

