{"request_id":"f7f452788b662e5fa6524651","rewritten_text":"When I had stopped weeping, reason and memory returned to me, and I began to consider more calmly what had happened and how I ought to act. Only a few hours had passed, yet a great revolution had taken place in my life—the natural work of years had been accomplished since morning. My father was as dead to me, and for a moment I felt as though he, with his white hair, lay in his coffin, while I—my youth gone, nearing age—wept over his timely death. But it was not so. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to others; but I, most miserable, must never see or speak to him again. I must flee from him more eagerly than from my greatest enemy: whether alone or among cities, I must never look upon him again. That thought left me breathless with anguish, and so possessed my imagination that for a time I could follow no further course of thought. From then on, I thought, I would live in the bleakest seclusion. I would withdraw to the Continent and become a nun—not for religion, since I was not Catholic, but so that I might be shut out from the world forever. There I would find solitude in which to weep, and the voices of life would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved and most wretched father? Would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Might he not, many years from now, when age had extinguished the burning feelings he now endured, become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow, and I felt—and wept to feel it—a half-melancholy smile draw my lips away from their expression of suffering. I dared to entertain better hopes for my future life. Years must pass, but they would fly lightly on the wings of hope; or, if they passed heavily, still they would pass, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering desolately. Let him once more speak his wild complaints to vast forests and terrible cataracts in another land. Let him again endure dreadful danger and hardships that crush the soul. Let the hot southern sun again burn his passion-worn cheeks, and cold night rains fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this life, miserable father, I devote you! Go! Let your days be spent among savages, and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs be worn down and your heart grow cold, and let all youth die within you!"}

