          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"465c075ada9676ba981ef595\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. We could all come on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and I\u2014with my caro sposo walking beside us. I really must speak to him about buying one. In the country, I think it is almost necessary; for however many ways a woman may have to occupy herself, she cannot always stay shut up at home. And long walks, you know\u2014in summer there is dust, and in winter there is mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will find neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and at present it is perfectly dry. Still, come on a donkey if you prefer. You may borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I want everything to suit you as much as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am sure you do. Indeed, I give you full credit for it, my good friend. Beneath that particular dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are a complete humourist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I fully appreciate your attention to me in this whole plan. You have thought of exactly what would please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that seeing any of them sit down outdoors to eat would certainly make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured, under the harmless appearance of a morning drive and an hour or two at Donwell, into making himself miserable.\\n\\nHe was invited sincerely. No hidden terrors were waiting to reproach him for trusting too easily. He agreed to come. He had not been to Donwell for two years. \u201cOn some very fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet could go very well; and he could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked about the gardens. He did not suppose the gardens could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He would like very much to see the old house again, and would be very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton and any other neighbours. He could see no objection at all to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there on some fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014much cleverer than dining out. He was not fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in receiving everyone\u2019s immediate agreement. The invitation was welcomed so warmly everywhere that, like Mrs. Elton, they all seemed to take the plan as a special compliment to themselves. Emma and Harriet declared that they expected great pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to bring Frank over to join them if possible\u2014a sign of approval and gratitude that might well have been spared. Mr. Knightley then had to say that he would be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston promised to waste no time in writing and to use every argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
