          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"87c89bba1f51d7ba7ef60295\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and horses in heavy armor could not travel very quickly. They would make the best speed they could, and now that the ground was firm, with no snow or mud, they would probably manage seven miles an hour. They would have to change horses a couple of times. They would arrive around six, or a little later. It would still be light enough. They would see the white cloth I would tie around my right arm, and I would take command. We would surround that prison and get the king out in no time. Considering everything, it would be showy and picturesque enough, though I would have preferred noon, because the whole thing would have looked more theatrical.\\n\\nThen, to give myself more options, I thought I would find some of those people I had recognized before and let them know who I was. That might get us out of our trouble without the knights. But I had to be careful, because it was risky. I needed to get into rich clothing, and I could not simply rush into it. No, I had to work my way up gradually, buying one suit after another in shops far apart, each one a little finer than the last, until I finally reached silk and velvet and was ready for my plan. So I set out.\\n\\nBut the plan collapsed at once. At the first corner I turned, I ran straight into one of our slaves, prowling around with a watchman. I happened to cough, and he gave me a sharp look that went straight through me. I guessed he thought he had heard that cough before. I immediately turned into a shop and moved along the counter, asking prices and watching from the corner of my eye. Those two had stopped, were talking together, and were looking in through the door. I decided to escape through the back, if there was a back way. I asked the shopwoman whether I could step out there and search for the escaped slave, who was believed to be hiding somewhere behind the shop. I told her I was an officer in disguise, and that my partner was at the door with one of the murderers in custody. Would she kindly go tell him not to wait, but to go at once to the far end of the back alley and be ready to cut him off when I drove him out?\\n\\nShe was burning with eagerness to see one of those already famous murderers, and she went on the errand immediately.\"}"
