          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a11875507c6691d14ad9551d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental powers called analytical are, in themselves, not easily analyzed. We recognize them only by their effects. Among other things, we know that, when someone possesses them in unusual strength, they give that person the keenest pleasure. Just as a strong man takes pride in his bodily power and enjoys exercises that put his muscles to work, the analyst delights in the mental activity that untangles things. He finds pleasure even in the smallest tasks that call his talent into use. He enjoys riddles, puzzles, and hieroglyphics, and in solving them shows a sharpness that seems almost supernatural to ordinary minds. His conclusions, reached through the very spirit and substance of method, truly have the appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe power of resolving problems may be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, especially by its highest branch, which has unfairly been called analysis simply because it works backward. Yet calculating is not, by itself, analyzing. A chess player, for instance, may do the former without making any effort at the latter. It follows that chess is greatly misunderstood in its effect on the mind. I am not writing a formal essay, but only introducing a rather unusual story with a few scattered remarks; so I will say that the higher powers of reflective thought are tested more clearly and more usefully by the quiet game of draughts than by all the elaborate triviality of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different and strange ways and have various changing values, mere complexity is mistaken, as often happens, for depth. Attention is strongly demanded. If it weakens for a moment, an error is made that brings loss or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but tangled, such errors become more likely; and nine times out of ten, the player with better concentration rather than greater sharpness wins. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are fixed and vary little, the chance of carelessness is reduced. Since simple attention is comparatively less needed, any advantage gained by either player comes from superior insight. To make this less abstract, imagine a game of draughts reduced to four kings, where, naturally, no oversight is expected. It is clear that victory, if the players are otherwise equal, can be decided only by some subtle move resulting from a strong effort of the intellect.\"}"
