{"request_id":"9e8af5b2f42ced0e9b76a014","rewritten_text":"As I passed my hand through the valve, carrying a cup of water for the old cat, the sleeves of my shirt caught in the loop that held up the basket, and in an instant detached it from the bottom. Had the entire thing dissolved into thin air, it could scarcely have disappeared from my view with greater suddenness. Indeed, not a tenth of a second could have elapsed between the basket’s release and its complete, utter vanishing, together with everything it held. My kind wishes went after it toward the earth, though naturally I could entertain no expectation that either the cat or her kittens would survive to recount their calamity.

“At six o’clock, I observed that a large part of the earth’s visible expanse to the east lay under a dense shadow, which moved onward with remarkable speed, until, five minutes before seven, every portion of the surface within sight was wrapped in the darkness of night. It was not until much later, however, that the beams of the declining sun stopped illuminating the balloon; and this fact, though certainly expected, afforded me an immense degree of pleasure. It was plain that in the morning I would see the rising orb at least several hours before the inhabitants of Rotterdam, despite their position so much farther east; and so, from day to day, according to the altitude I attained, I would enjoy the sunlight for an ever longer time. I then resolved to keep a journal of my voyage, counting the days as continuous periods of twenty-four hours, without regard to the intervals of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, becoming drowsy, I decided to lie down for the remainder of the night; but a difficulty then arose which, apparent as it may seem, had escaped my notice until the very instant of which I speak. If I slept as intended, how was the air in the chamber to be renewed meanwhile? To breathe it for more than an hour at most would be impossible; or, even if that period might be prolonged to an hour and a quarter, the most disastrous consequences could follow. Reflection upon this predicament caused me considerable uneasiness; and it will scarcely be credited that, after the perils I had endured, I regarded the matter so gravely as to abandon all hope of fulfilling my final purpose and at last resolve upon the necessity of descending. Yet this uncertainty lasted only a moment.”"}

