          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"16bda8bcaf9d1a861e011f8d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou must acquire a little of my philosophy. Think of the past only when its recollection affords you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot allow you any philosophy of that sort. _Your_ reflections must be so entirely free from self-reproach that the satisfaction they produce comes not from philosophy, but, what is far better, from ignorance. With _me_, however, it is otherwise. Painful memories will force themselves upon me, and cannot, and ought not, be driven away. I have been selfish all my life in conduct, though not in principle. As a boy, I was instructed in what was _right_, but not taught to govern my temper. I received sound principles, yet was left to pursue them with pride and conceit. Unhappily an only son\u2014for many years an only _child_\u2014I was indulged by my parents, who, though good themselves\u2014my father especially, everything benevolent and amiable\u2014permitted, encouraged, almost taught me to be selfish and domineering; to care for no one outside my own family circle; to think poorly of all the rest of the world; to _wish_, at least, to think poorly of their understanding and value in comparison with my own. So I was from eight to eight-and-twenty; and so I might yet have remained, if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! How much do I owe you! You gave me a lesson, severe indeed at first, but most beneficial. Through you, I was rightly humbled. I approached you without a doubt of my welcome. You showed me how inadequate were all my claims to please a woman worthy of being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you really convinced yourself that I should?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What will you say to my vanity? I imagined you wished for, and expected, my attentions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy behaviour must have been at fault, though never by design, I assure you. I never intended to mislead you, but my spirits may often have carried me too far. How you must have hated me after _that_ evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I was perhaps angry at first, but my anger soon began to find its proper object.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. You blamed me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed; I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have exceeded _mine_ at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no unusual civility, and I confess I did not expect to receive _more_ than my due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose _then_,\u201d replied Darcy, \u201cwas to show you, by every civility within my power, that I was not so petty as to resent the past; and I hoped to gain your forgiveness, and lessen your unfavourable opinion, by allowing you to see that your reproofs had been heeded.\u201d\"}"
