{"request_id":"ec564e544ba7020adb0c0396","rewritten_text":"“I reasoned as follows—after examining the facts. The murderers escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have fastened the sashes again from inside, since the windows were found fastened. This obvious point had stopped the police from investigating further. Yet the sashes were fastened. Therefore, they must have been able to fasten themselves. There was no avoiding that conclusion. I went to the clear window, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to raise the sash. As I expected, it would not move. There had to be a hidden spring; and this confirmation convinced me that my reasoning was correct, though the matter of the nails remained mysterious. A careful search soon revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it and, satisfied with my discovery, did not raise the sash.

“I replaced the nail and studied it closely. Someone leaving through this window could have shut it again, and the spring would have caught it—but he could not have put the nail back. The conclusion was clear, and my investigation narrowed further. The killers must have escaped through the other window. If the springs on both sashes were alike, as seemed likely, there had to be some difference in the nails, or at least in the way they were fixed. Climbing onto the bedstead’s sacking, I carefully examined the second window over the headboard. Running my hand down behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring. As I had expected, it was identical to the one beside it. I then examined the nail. It was as thick as the other and seemed to be fitted the same way—driven in almost to its head.

“You may say I was confused; but if you do, you have misunderstood my reasoning. To borrow a hunting phrase, I had never once lost the trail. The scent had not been lost for an instant. There was no weak link in the chain. I had followed the secret to its final point—and that point was the nail. In every way, I repeat, it looked like the nail in the other window; but that resemblance meant nothing, however convincing it seemed, beside the fact that the clue ended here. ‘There must be something wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head, along with about a quarter inch of the shaft, came off in my fingers."}

