          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"16f7f3eb8cc94ceeb1dd7794\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOn that subject, then, I shall always remain silent; and you may be sure that, once we are married, no unkind reproach will ever pass my lips.\u201d\\n\\nIt was now absolutely necessary to interrupt him.\\n\\n\u201cYou are too quick, sir,\u201d she cried. \u201cYou forget that I have given you no answer. Let me give it without wasting any more time. Please accept my thanks for the compliment you have paid me. I am very aware of the honour of your proposal, but it is impossible for me to do anything but refuse it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not so inexperienced,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, making a formal gesture with his hand, \u201cas not to know that young ladies often reject the attentions of the man they secretly intend to accept when he first asks for their favour; and that the refusal is sometimes repeated a second or even third time. I am therefore not at all discouraged by what you have just said, and I hope to lead you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cyour hope is quite extraordinary after what I have declared. I assure you, I am not one of those young ladies\u2014if such young ladies exist\u2014who are bold enough to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked a second time. My refusal is perfectly serious. You could not make me happy, and I am convinced that I am the last woman in the world who could make you happy. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am sure she would find me entirely unfit for such a position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins very seriously\u2014\u201cbut I cannot imagine that her Ladyship would disapprove of you at all. And you may be sure that, when I have the honour of seeing her again, I shall speak in the highest terms of your modesty, thrift, and other pleasing qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, any praise of me will be unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the honour of believing what I say. I wish you to be very happy and very rich, and by refusing your hand I do everything in my power to prevent you from being otherwise. By making me this offer, you must have satisfied your delicate feelings concerning my family, and may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it comes to you without any self-reproach. This matter may therefore be considered finally settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had Mr. Collins not addressed her again\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honour of speaking to you again on this subject, I hope to receive a more favourable answer than the one you have given me now. Still, I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it is the established custom of your sex to reject a man when he first applies, and perhaps even now you have said as much as is consistent with the true delicacy of a woman\u2019s character to encourage my suit.\u201d\"}"
