          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aec786d756c65a6425ebdeae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou have bred an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am, quite unsuitable to one in his station: as the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, being practical philosophers, will inform you. What business have paupers with soul or spirit? It is quite sufficient that we allow \u2019em living bodies. Had you kept the boy to gruel, ma\u2019am, this could never have occurred.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d cried Mrs. Sowerberry, devoutly lifting her eyes toward the kitchen ceiling: \u201cthis is what comes of generosity!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s generosity toward Oliver had taken the form of lavishly giving him every dirty scrap and remnant that no one else would consume; thus there was much meekness and self-sacrifice in her willingly submitting to Mr. Bumble\u2019s grave charge, of which, in fairness to her, she was entirely guiltless in thought, word, and action.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d observed Mr. Bumble, when the lady lowered her eyes to the world again; \u201cthe only course I know of now is to shut him in the cellar for a day or two, until he is somewhat starved down; then bring him out, and keep him on gruel throughout his apprenticeship. He is from a bad family. Excitable dispositions, Mrs. Sowerberry! The nurse and the doctor both said that his mother forced her way here, through hardships and suffering that would have killed any respectable woman, weeks earlier.\u201d\\n\\nHere, Oliver, having heard just enough to understand that his mother was being referred to, began kicking again with such fury that every other noise was drowned out. Sowerberry came back at that moment. When Oliver\u2019s offence had been described to him, with the exaggerations the ladies judged most likely to inflame his anger, he instantly unlocked the cellar door and hauled his defiant apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes were torn from the beating he had endured; his face was bruised and scratched, and his hair hung loose across his forehead. Yet the angry colour had not faded; and, when drawn from his prison, he glared boldly at Noah and appeared wholly undaunted.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a fine young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, shaking Oliver and striking him on the ear.\\n\\n\u201cHe called my mother names,\u201d Oliver answered.\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if he did, you little thankless wretch?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe deserved all he said, and more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe did not,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry broke into a torrent of tears.\\n\\nThat torrent left Mr. Sowerberry with no choice. Had he paused for even a moment before punishing Oliver with the utmost severity, every experienced reader must plainly see that, by all the precedents established in matrimonial quarrels, he would have been a brute, an unnatural husband, an offensive creature, a poor imitation of a man, and many other pleasing characters too numerous to recount within this chapter\u2019s bounds.\"}"
