          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6966bcc4deb4bf707486c5d9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the power of gravitation would continually lessen, according to the squares of the distances; and so, with a velocity enormously increasing, I should finally reach those remote regions in which the earth\u2019s attraction would yield to that of the moon. Guided by these considerations, I judged it unnecessary to burden myself with provisions beyond what would suffice for forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere remained, nevertheless, another difficulty which gave me some slight uneasiness. It has been remarked that, in balloon ascents to any great elevation, apart from the pain connected with breathing, considerable discomfort is felt in the head and body, frequently attended by bleeding from the nose and other symptoms of an alarming character, all becoming increasingly troublesome as the height increases. This consideration was somewhat startling. Might not such symptoms grow without limit, or at least continue until death itself put an end to them? At last I concluded they would not. Their source was to be sought in the gradual withdrawal of the usual atmospheric pressure from the body\u2019s surface, and the resulting expansion of the superficial blood-vessels\u2014not in any actual derangement of the animal system, as occurs in difficult breathing, when the density of the atmosphere is chemically inadequate for the proper renewal of blood in a ventricle of the heart. Except through failure of this renewal, I could perceive no reason why life might not be maintained even in a vacuum; for the expansion and contraction of the chest, ordinarily termed breathing, is an action wholly muscular, and is the cause rather than the consequence of respiration. In short, I believed that, as the body became accustomed to the absence of atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would slowly decline\u2014and, while they lasted, I trusted confidently in the iron endurance of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, if it please your Excellencies, I have set forth some, though certainly not all, of the reflections that induced me to devise the plan of a voyage to the moon. I shall now go on to present the result of an undertaking so seemingly bold in its design and, in any event, so wholly without precedent in the records of mankind.\\n\\n\u201cHaving reached the altitude already stated\u2014that is, three miles and three-quarters\u2014I cast a quantity of feathers from the car, and discovered that I was still rising with adequate speed; hence there was no need to throw out any ballast. This pleased me, since I desired to keep with me as much weight as I was able to bear, for reasons that will be explained hereafter.\"}"
