          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9194e5eec311e5d337e92fa2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But in that bitter tirade against Chantilly, published in yesterday\u2019s \u2018Mus\u00e9e,\u2019 the satirist, making some shameful references to the cobbler\u2019s change of name when he took up the buskin, quoted a Latin line we have often discussed. I mean the line\\n\\nPerdidit antiquum litera prima sonum.\\n\\n\u201cI had told you that it referred to Orion, formerly spelled Urion; and because of certain pointed associations connected with that explanation, I knew you could not have forgotten it. It was plain, then, that you would be sure to connect the two ideas of Orion and Chantilly. I saw that you had done so by the kind of smile that crossed your lips. You were thinking of the poor cobbler\u2019s sacrifice. Until then, you had been walking with a stoop; but now I saw you straighten yourself to your full height. I was certain then that you were reflecting on Chantilly\u2019s small stature. At that point I broke into your thoughts to observe that, since he was in fact such a very little fellow\u2014that Chantilly\u2014he would be better suited to the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\nNot long afterward, we were reading an evening edition of the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux\u201d when the following paragraphs caught our attention.\\n\\n\u201cExtraordinary Murders.\u2014This morning, at about three o\u2019clock, the residents of the Quartier St. Roch were awakened by a series of terrible screams, apparently coming from the fourth floor of a house in the Rue Morgue, known to be occupied solely by Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, Mademoiselle Camille L\u2019Espanaye. After some delay, caused by an unsuccessful attempt to gain entry in the ordinary way, the gate was forced with a crowbar, and eight or ten neighbors entered, accompanied by two gendarmes. By then the cries had stopped; but as the party hurried up the first flight of stairs, they heard two or more harsh voices engaged in angry dispute, apparently from the upper part of the house. When they reached the second landing, these sounds too had ceased, and all was completely silent. The party separated and rushed from room to room. On reaching a large rear chamber on the fourth floor (whose door, found locked with the key inside, was forced open), they encountered a sight that filled everyone present with as much astonishment as horror.\\n\\n\u201cThe room was in the utmost confusion\u2014the furniture smashed and scattered in every direction. There was only one bedstead, and its bed had been removed and thrown into the center of the floor. On a chair lay a razor, stained with blood.\"}"
