          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c3b36829d880c8c7ad71edc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I said, \u201cI think you could tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have one. All the nobility do.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just like him. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something to you, he wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t make any difference.\\n\\nHe had all that coat-of-arms business settled, so he began finishing the rest of that part of the plan: making up a mournful inscription. He said Jim had to have one, the way they all did. He invented a whole lot of them, wrote them on paper, and read them aloud:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart burst._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and friends, wore out his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook while he read them, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, because they were all so good. But finally he said he would let him scratch all of them on. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch so much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and besides, he didn\u2019t know how to make letters. But Tom said he would outline them for him, and then Jim would only have to follow the lines. Then, after a little while, he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, logs won\u2019t do. They don\u2019t have log walls in a dungeon. We\u2019ve got to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll bring in a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than logs. He said it would take him such a terribly long time to carve them into a rock that he would never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he looked to see how Jim and I were getting along with the pens. It was awfully tedious, hard, slow work, and it gave my hands no chance to heal from their sores. We hardly seemed to be making any progress, so Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to arrange it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with the same rock.\u201d\"}"
