          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"babe93ce00239f868c23456d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGoodbye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thank you for giving me the information I wanted. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve invited myself and Mr. Gray. He is her newest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to trouble me any more with her appeals for charity. I\u2019m tired of them. The good woman seems to think I have nothing to do except write cheques for her foolish causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, though it won\u2019t do any good. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity. It is their defining trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman grunted approvingly and rang for his servant. Lord Henry went up the low arcade into Burlington Street and made his way toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Though it had been told to him crudely, it stirred him with the suggestion of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a reckless passion. A few wild weeks of happiness ended by a horrible, treacherous crime. Months of silent agony, and then a child born in pain. The mother taken by death, the boy left to loneliness and the tyranny of an old man without love. Yes, it was an interesting background. It gave the lad a pose, made him more perfect, in a way. Behind every exquisite thing in existence there was something tragic. Worlds had to labour before even the humblest flower could bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the previous night, sitting opposite him at the club with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened delight, while the red candle shades deepened the awakening wonder in his face to a richer rose. Talking to him was like playing an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and tremor of the bow.... There was something terribly compelling in the exercise of influence. No other pursuit resembled it. To cast one\u2019s soul into some gracious form and let it remain there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own intellectual opinions echoed back with all the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another as if it were a subtle liquid or an unfamiliar perfume: there was a true pleasure in that\u2014perhaps the most satisfying pleasure left to us in an age as narrow and vulgar as our own, an age grossly sensual in its pleasures and grossly commonplace in its ambitions.... This lad was a marvellous type as well, whom he had met by such a curious chance in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or who could, at any rate, be shaped into a marvellous type.\"}"
