          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a37c3ee0f14f49859837165c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Accordingly, I determined to get down the instant we reached the town, and place myself beyond his hearing. This plan I carried out successfully. My small portmanteau lay in the boot beneath my feet; I had only to move a hinge to draw it forth; I cast it down before me, descended after it, and found myself left at the first lamp upon the first paving-stones of the town. The convicts, meanwhile, continued on with the coach, and I knew where they would be whisked away to the river. In imagination, I beheld the boat and its convict crew awaiting them at the slime-stained steps,\u2014once more heard the harsh \u201cGive way, you!\u201d as though addressed to dogs,\u2014once more saw the evil Noah\u2019s Ark afloat upon the dark water.\\n\\nI could not have told what I feared, for my fear was wholly vague and without definition; yet a great fear possessed me. Walking towards the hotel, I felt myself shaken by a dread far beyond the simple expectation of a painful or unpleasant recognition. I am sure it assumed no definite form, and that for a few minutes it was the return of childhood terror.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was vacant, and I had both ordered my dinner there and begun to eat it before the waiter recognised me. When he had apologised for his failure of memory, he asked whether he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201cmost certainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter (the same man who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was bound) seemed astonished, and seized the first chance to lay an old dirty local newspaper so plainly in my path that I picked it up and read this paragraph:\u2014\\n\\nOur readers will hear, with no little interest, in connexion with the recent romantic advancement in fortune of a young worker in iron from this neighbourhood (what a subject, incidentally, for the enchanted pen of our still insufficiently recognised fellow-townsman TOOBY, poet of these columns!) that the young man\u2019s first patron, associate, and friend was a much-respected person closely connected, though not exclusively, with the corn and seed business, whose notably handy and spacious premises stand within a hundred miles of the High Street. We do not record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus without some regard to our own feelings, for it is gratifying to know that our town gave rise to the founder of the latter\u2019s prosperity.\"}"
