          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90be559b2830ff025230d6d1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her, gathered her into my arms, covered her with kisses, and pleaded:\\n\\n\u201cSpeak, darling, speak! What is it?\u201d\\n\\nHer head dropped helplessly against my chest, and she whispered, scarcely audibly:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I called to Clarence. \u201cTelephone the king\u2019s homeopath and have him come!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, while Sandy sent servants racing in every direction throughout the palace. I understood the trouble almost at once\u2014membranous croup. I bent over and whispered:\\n\\n\u201cWake up, sweetheart. Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe slowly opened her gentle eyes and managed to say:\\n\\n\u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat was reassuring. She was still a long way from death. I ordered sulphur preparations and fetched the croup-kettle myself, because I do not sit waiting for doctors when Sandy or the child is ill. I knew how to care for them both, and I had experience. This little one had spent much of her short life in my arms, and often I could calm her distress and make her laugh through the tear-drops on her lashes when even her mother could not.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, wearing his finest armor, was striding through the great hall on his way to the stock exchange. He was its president and held the Siege Perilous, which he had bought from Sir Galahad; for the stock exchange was made up of the Knights of the Round Table, who now used the Round Table for business. A seat there was worth\u2014well, you would never believe the amount, so there is no point in naming it. Sir Launcelot was a bear, and had engineered a corner in one of the new lines; today he was preparing to squeeze the shorts. But what did that matter? He was still the same old Launcelot, and when he glanced through the doorway as he passed and learned that his little favorite was sick, that settled it. Bulls and 