          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0253364f4b170840ba3ac0d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It seemed to be a collection of back lanes, ditches, and small gardens, with the air of a rather dreary retreat. Wemmick\u2019s house was a little wooden cottage among garden plots, its roof cut out and painted to resemble a gun battery.\\n\\n\u201cMy own work,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cLooks nice, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nI praised it warmly. I think it was the smallest house I had ever seen, with the oddest Gothic windows\u2014most of them fake\u2014and a Gothic door almost too small to enter.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s a real flagstaff, you see,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand on Sundays I fly a real flag. Then look here. Once I\u2019ve crossed this bridge, I raise it\u2014like this\u2014and cut off communication.\u201d\\n\\nThe bridge was a plank crossing a gap about four feet wide and two feet deep. But it was very pleasant to see the pride with which he lifted and secured it, smiling with genuine enjoyment rather than doing it merely by habit.\\n\\n\u201cAt nine every night, Greenwich time,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cthe gun goes off. There it is, you see! And when you hear it fire, I think you\u2019ll call it a Stinger.\u201d\\n\\nThe cannon he meant stood in a separate fortress made of latticework. It was sheltered from the weather by a clever little tarpaulin arrangement, like an umbrella.\\n\\n\u201cThen, at the back,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cout of sight, so it doesn\u2019t spoil the idea of fortifications\u2014for it\u2019s a principle of mine that if you have an idea, you should carry it through and maintain it\u2014I don\u2019t know whether you agree\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI said I certainly did.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014At the back, there\u2019s a pig, and fowls and rabbits; then I put together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers. You\u2019ll judge at supper what kind of salad I can produce. So, sir,\u201d said Wemmick, smiling again, though seriously as well, while shaking his head, \u201cif you can imagine this little place under siege, it could hold out a devilishly long time for provisions.\u201d\\n\\nThen he led me to a bower about a dozen yards away, though it was reached by such ingenious turns in the path that it took quite a while to arrive there. In this retreat our glasses had already been laid out. Our punch was cooling in an ornamental lake, beside which the bower stood. This water\u2014with an island in the middle that might have served as the supper salad\u2014was circular, and he had built a fountain in it. When you started a little mill and pulled a cork from a pipe, it played with such force that it made the back of your hand quite wet.\"}"
