          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"59b8ceb466e505915c7d0bbe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, bought Canterville Chase, everyone told him he was doing something very foolish, since there was no doubt that the place was haunted. Lord Canterville himself, a man of the strictest honour, had felt obliged to mention the matter to Mr. Otis when they began discussing the terms.\\n\\n\u2018We have not cared to live there ourselves,\u2019 said Lord Canterville, \u2018ever since my great-aunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a fit from which she never truly recovered, when two skeleton hands were laid upon her shoulders as she dressed for dinner. And I feel bound to tell you, Mr. Otis, that the ghost has been seen by several living members of my family, as well as by the parish rector, the Reverend Augustus Dampier, a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the Duchess\u2019s unfortunate accident, none of our younger servants would remain with us, and Lady Canterville often slept very little at night because of the mysterious noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My Lord,\u2019 replied the Minister, \u2018I will take the furniture and the ghost at a fair valuation. I come from a modern country, where we have everything money can buy; and with all our lively young men painting the Old World red and carrying off your finest actresses and prima donnas, I reckon that if there were such a thing as a ghost in Europe, we would have one at home very soon, either in a public museum or travelling the roads as a show.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I am afraid the ghost does exist,\u2019 said Lord Canterville, smiling, \u2018though it may have resisted the offers of your enterprising impresarios. It has been well known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always appears before the death of a member of our family.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, so does the family doctor, for that matter, Lord Canterville. But there is no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I suppose the laws of Nature are not going to be suspended for the British aristocracy.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You are certainly very natural in America,\u2019 replied Lord Canterville, who did not entirely understand Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark, \u2018and if you do not mind having a ghost in the house, that is all right. Only remember that I warned you.\u2019\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was completed, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who as Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street had been a celebrated New York beauty, was now a very handsome middle-aged woman, with fine eyes and a superb profile.\"}"
