          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"53b951b19bb4ba4ca241dbc2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have not the nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really be comfortable, spending the whole evening away from the poor child?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see, his papa can, so why should I not? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us news every hour about how he is. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly alarmed yesterday, but things are very different to-day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it is too late to send word for yourself, suppose you go too, with your husband. Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cDear me, that is a very good idea\u2014very good indeed. Of course, I may as well go as stay, for I am no use at home, am I? It only worries me. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s feelings, are much more suited to it. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at a word. It will be much better than leaving him with Jemima alone. Oh, I shall certainly go. I am sure I ought to, if I can, quite as much as Charles, because they are so eager for me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. What an excellent idea, Anne. I will go and tell Charles, and get ready at once. You can send for us at a moment\u2019s notice, you know, if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to alarm you. You may be sure I would not go if I did not feel perfectly easy about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door. Anne followed her upstairs and arrived in time to hear the whole conversation, which began with Mary saying, in a tone of great triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I am no more use at home than you are. If I shut myself up forever with the child, I still could not persuade him to do anything he did not want to do. Anne will stay; Anne has agreed to stay at home and care for him. It was Anne\u2019s own suggestion, so I shall go with you. It will be much better, because I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be very glad to have you go, but it seems rather hard that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
