ide the London drawl. The supernatural was not mentioned at all, nor was any reference made in any fashion to Sir Simon de Canterville. At eleven the family went to bed, and by half-past eleven every light had been extinguished. Some while later, Mr. Otis woke to an odd sound in the corridor outside his room. It resembled the clanking of metal and appeared to draw nearer with each moment. He rose immediately, lit a match, and checked the time. It was precisely one o\u2019clock. He remained perfectly composed and felt his pulse, which was not feverish in the slightest. The peculiar sound went on, and he could now plainly hear footsteps with it. He slipped on his slippers, drew a small oblong phial from his dressing-case, and opened the door.\"}"
