{"request_id":"e16f9df17a80b302be28d7b2","rewritten_text":"In the course of his present brief visit, Emma had scarcely encountered him; yet sufficiently often to feel that the first meeting had passed, and to perceive that he was not improved by the blend of resentment and self-importance now visible in his manner. Indeed, she was beginning seriously to marvel that she could ever have found him agreeable; and the sight of him was so closely associated with several unpleasant sensations, that, save in a moral view—as a punishment, a warning, a means of useful humiliation to her own thoughts—she would gladly have been promised never to see him more. She wished him well enough; but he caused her pain, and his prosperity at twenty miles’ distance would afford her the greatest pleasure.

Yet the discomfort of his remaining at Highbury must surely be diminished by his marriage. It would avert many idle anxieties—remove many embarrassments. A _Mrs._ _Elton_ would justify any alteration in their intercourse; their former intimacy might fade away unnoticed. It would be nearly the commencement of a new life of mere civility between them.

Of the lady herself, Emma gave little thought. She was quite good enough for Mr. Elton, undoubtedly; sufficiently accomplished for Highbury—pretty enough—to seem plain, most likely, beside Harriet. As for family connexion, Emma felt entirely at ease; convinced that, after all his boastful pretensions and contempt for Harriet, he had achieved nothing. On that point, the truth appeared discoverable. _What_ she was must remain doubtful; but _who_ she was could be learned; and, apart from the 10,000 l., she did not seem in any respect Harriet’s superior. She brought neither name, blood, nor alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, naturally he must be termed; though, as the entire gains of his mercantile career appeared so very modest, it was not unjust to suppose that the rank of his trade had been modest too. She had been accustomed to pass part of each winter at Bath; but Bristol was her residence, in the very centre of Bristol; for although her father and mother had been dead for some years, she had an uncle remaining—in the law line—nothing more particularly respectable was ventured concerning him than that he was in the law line; and with him she had lived. Emma supposed him the hack of some attorney, and too dull ever to advance. All the splendour of the connexion appeared to rest upon the elder sister, who was _very_ _well_ _married_, to a gentleman in a _great_ _way_, near Bristol, who maintained two carriages! That was the conclusion of the tale; that was Miss Hawkins’s distinction."}

