          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c862d23a9047a0a9aac2fcff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She had put everything behind her, except the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nAnne had not been inside Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need for it, and on the few occasions when a visit to the Hall had been possible, she had managed to avoid and escape it. Her first return was to take her place again in the fashionable, handsome rooms of the Lodge, and to delight the eyes of its mistress.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s pleasure in seeing her was mingled with some uneasiness. She knew who had lately been much at Uppercross. Yet, fortunately, Anne was either fuller in figure and better in appearance, or Lady Russell imagined her to be so; and, as she accepted the compliments paid her, Anne was amused to connect them with her cousin\u2019s unspoken admiration, and to hope that she might be granted a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nOnce they began to talk, Anne quickly perceived a change in her own mind. The matters which had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, which she had felt neglected in, and had been obliged to suppress among the Musgroves, had now become only secondary concerns. Lately she had almost lost sight even of her father, her sister, and Bath. Their interests had been overwhelmed by those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell returned to their old hopes and apprehensions, expressing satisfaction in the house taken in Camden Place and regret that Mrs Clay was still with them, Anne would have been ashamed had anyone known how much more her thoughts were occupied by Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her friends there; how much more engaging to her were the Harvilles\u2019 home and friendship, and Captain Benwick, than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs Clay. She was truly obliged to make an effort to meet Lady Russell with anything resembling equal concern on subjects which naturally had the strongest claim upon her.\\n\\nAt first there was some slight embarrassment in speaking of another matter. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had been in the place scarcely five minutes the previous day before a complete account of it had been poured out to her; but it still had to be discussed: she must ask questions, condemn the imprudence, grieve over the consequence, and Captain Wentworth\u2019s name had to be spoken by them both. Anne knew she did not manage it as well as Lady Russell. She could not say his name and look directly into Lady Russell\u2019s eyes until she had resorted to briefly telling her what she believed of the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
