{"request_id":"a1c934b8313f8b6ab37927c8","rewritten_text":"During his brief visit, Emma had hardly seen him; but she had seen enough to feel that the first awkward meeting was past, and to notice that he had not improved. A mixture of resentment and self-importance now showed in his manner. Indeed, she was beginning to wonder how she could ever have found him agreeable. Seeing him was so closely tied to unpleasant feelings that, except as a moral punishment—a lesson and a useful humiliation—she would have been grateful to know she need never see him again. She wished him well, but he caused her pain, and she would have been far more satisfied with his welfare twenty miles away.

Still, the discomfort of his remaining in Highbury would certainly be reduced by his marriage. It would prevent many useless worries and smooth over many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would give an excuse for changing their relationship; their former closeness could fade away without notice. It would almost be like beginning again with mere politeness.

Emma thought very little of the lady herself. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, certainly; accomplished enough for Highbury; and pretty enough—though probably plain beside Harriet. As for family connections, Emma felt perfectly easy. She was convinced that, despite all his proud claims and his contempt for Harriet, he had done nothing remarkable. On that point, the truth seemed possible to discover. What Miss Hawkins was like might remain uncertain, but who she was could be found out; and, apart from her ten thousand pounds, she did not seem at all superior to Harriet. She brought no distinguished name, family, or connections. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, of course, he had to be called; but since the profits of his business seemed so modest, it was fair to suppose that the importance of his trade had been modest too. She had spent part of every winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home—the very centre of Bristol. Her father and mother had died some years before, but an uncle remained, in the law line. Nothing more honourable was claimed for him than that he was in the law line, and the daughter had lived with him. Emma guessed that he was some attorney's drudge, too dull to advance. All the grandeur of the connection seemed to depend on the elder sister, who was very well married to a gentleman in a great way near Bristol, who kept two carriages. That was the end of the story; that was Miss Hawkins's glory."}

