          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b4eb5cad534156a9cb10077\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made her understand that Frank admired her greatly\u2014he thought her very beautiful and charming; and with so much else in his favour, she felt she ought not to judge him too severely. As Mrs. Weston said, \u201call young people have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nThere was one person among Frank\u2019s new acquaintances in Surrey who was less forgiving. In general, throughout Donwell and Highbury, people judged him very kindly; they made generous excuses for the small faults of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so often and bowed so gracefully. But there was one person whose power of criticism could not be softened by smiles or bows: Mr. Knightley. The news was told to him at Hartfield. At first he said nothing; but Emma soon heard him mutter to himself over the newspaper in his hand, \u201cHum! Just the foolish, insignificant fellow I thought him.\u201d She was half inclined to take offence; but a moment\u2019s observation showed her that he had said it only to ease his own feelings, not to provoke her, and so she let it pass.\\n\\nThough Mr. and Mrs. Weston\u2019s visit that morning brought unwelcome news in one respect, it was especially timely in another. While they were at Hartfield, something happened that made Emma want their advice; and, even more fortunately, it was exactly the advice they gave.\\n\\nThis was what happened: The Coles had lived in Highbury for several years, and were very good people\u2014friendly, generous, and unpretentious. But they came from a low background, were in trade, and were only moderately genteel. When they first arrived in the neighbourhood, they had lived according to their income, quietly, seeing little company and entertaining that little cheaply. But during the last year or two, their means had increased considerably\u2014their business in town had made greater profits, and fortune had generally favoured them. As their wealth grew, so did their ambitions: they wanted a larger house and more company. They enlarged their house, increased their servants, and spent more in every way; and by now, in wealth and style of living, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their enjoyment of society, along with their new dining room, led everyone to expect that they would begin giving dinner parties; and they had already held a few, mostly for unmarried men. Emma could hardly imagine that they would dare invite the established and best families\u2014not Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. Nothing would persuade her to go if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal seem less significant than she wished.\"}"
