          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"74bf76c17aea5a8993cea314\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014what mysteries they were! There was animal nature in the soul, while the body knew moments of spirituality. The senses might become refined, and the intellect might sink into degradation. Who could tell where the impulse of the flesh ended and the impulse of the psyche began? How superficial were the arbitrary classifications of common psychologists! Yet how hard it was to choose among the assertions of the different schools! Was the soul a shadow dwelling in the house of sin? Or was the body truly contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The division of spirit from matter was a mystery, and so too was their union.\\n\\nHe found himself wondering if psychology could ever be made so exact a science that every small source of life would be laid bare. As matters stood, we continually misread ourselves and seldom understood anyone else. Experience possessed no moral worth. It was only the title men bestowed upon their errors. Moralists, generally, had treated it as a warning, had attributed to it a certain ethical influence in shaping character, and had commended it as that which taught us what to pursue and indicated what to shun. But experience contained no driving force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly proved was that our future would resemble our past, and that the sin we had committed once, with disgust, we would commit repeatedly, with delight.\\n\\nIt seemed plain to him that the experimental method alone could lead to any scientific analysis of the passions; and Dorian Gray was certainly a subject ready to his hand, one that appeared to offer abundant and valuable results. His abrupt, wild love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological occurrence of considerable interest. Curiosity had undoubtedly played a large part in it\u2014curiosity and the craving for fresh experiences\u2014yet it was not a simple passion, but an exceedingly complicated one. Whatever in it belonged to the merely sensuous instinct of boyhood had been altered through imagination, made into something that appeared to the young man himself far removed from the senses, and was therefore all the more perilous. The passions whose origins we concealed from ourselves ruled us most completely. Our feeblest motives were those whose character we recognized. Often, when we imagined we were experimenting upon others, we were in fact experimenting upon ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat absorbed in these reflections, there came a knock at the door, and his valet entered to remind him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
