          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"363e898bf525e739fd7b7652\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour told me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I remember. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter making a few sleepy attempts to fall asleep again, defeated by the man\u2019s skillful stirring of the fire for five straight minutes, he got up, threw on his hat, and went out. He turned into the Temple and, after refreshing himself by walking twice along the pavements of King\u2019s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, went to Stryver\u2019s chambers.\\n\\nStryver\u2019s clerk, who never took part in these meetings, had gone home, and Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing-gown, with his throat bare for comfort. Around his eyes was the somewhat wild, tired, worn look seen in hard-living men of his kind, from portraits of Jeffries onward, and visible, in the different styles of art, in portraits from every age of drinking.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a little late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual time; perhaps a quarter of an hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey went into a dark room lined with books and covered in papers, where a fire blazed. A kettle steamed on the hearth, and amid the scattered papers stood a shining table, well supplied with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou have had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I have been dining with the day\u2019s client\u2014or watching him dine; it is all the same!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was an excellent point, Sydney, the one you used about the identification. How did you get it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was a rather handsome fellow, and I thought I might have been much the same sort of fellow, if I had had any luck.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his early-growing belly shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nRather sulkily, the jackal loosened his clothes, went into the next room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and one or two towels. He soaked the towels in the water, partly wrung them out, folded them over his head in a dreadful-looking way, sat at the table, and said, \u201cNow I am ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much work to boil down tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, looking through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Go ahead!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled on his back on a sofa at one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own proper table, covered with papers, on the other side, with bottles and glasses close at hand.\"}"
