{"request_id":"0d9d41c86f7ea3662b386fcd","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the stairs, he regained his composure and decided to unleash his famous burst of demonic laughter. He had found it extremely useful on more than one occasion. It was said to have turned Lord Raker’s wig grey overnight, and it had certainly caused three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to give notice before the end of their first month. So he laughed his most dreadful laugh, until the old vaulted ceiling echoed again and again. But scarcely had the terrible reverberation faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis emerged in a light-blue dressing gown. ‘I’m afraid you are very unwell,’ she said, ‘and I have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it is indigestion, you will find it an excellent remedy.’ The ghost glared at her furiously and immediately began preparing to transform himself into a large black dog, a feat for which he was rightly famous and which the family doctor had always blamed for the permanent idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. But the sound of approaching footsteps made him pause in his sinister intention, so he merely became faintly phosphorescent and vanished with a deep graveyard groan, just as the twins came up to him.

When he reached his room, he collapsed completely and was seized by the most violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s crude materialism naturally annoyed him deeply, but what truly upset him was that he had been unable to wear the suit of armour. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be thrilled by the sight of a Spectre in Armour, if for no better reason than respect for their national poet, Longfellow, whose graceful and appealing poetry he himself had used to pass many a weary hour when the Cantervilles were in town. Besides, it was his own armour. He had worn it with great success at the tournament at Kenilworth, and had received high praise for it from no less a person than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet when he put it on, the enormous breastplate and steel helmet had completely overwhelmed him with their weight, and he had fallen heavily onto the stone floor, badly bruising both knees and the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward, he was extremely ill and scarcely left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. However, by taking great care of himself, he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

