          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d8ce30ac9d6fce87d0e5e755\",\"rewritten_text\":\"_PS._\u2014Do tell me about the bows. Jennings says they are fashionable.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so grave and unhappy over the letter that the Duchess burst into laughter.\\n\\n\u2018My dear Arthur,\u2019 she cried, \u2018I shall never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what am I to say about the clock? I think it is an excellent invention, and I should like one myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I don\u2019t think much of them,\u2019 said Lord Arthur, with a sad smile; and, after kissing his mother, he left the room.\\n\\nWhen he reached his room upstairs, he threw himself onto a sofa, his eyes full of tears. He had done his best to commit this murder, yet both times he had failed, through no fault of his own. He had tried to do his duty, but it seemed that Destiny herself had betrayed him. He felt crushed by the emptiness of good intentions, by the uselessness of trying to act nobly. Perhaps it would be better to end the engagement altogether. Sybil would suffer, certainly, but suffering could not truly damage a nature as noble as hers. And for himself, what did it matter? There was always some war in which a man could die, some cause for which he could give his life; and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny carry out his fate. He would do nothing to help her.\\n\\nAt half-past seven he dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and Lord Arthur had to dine with them. Their shallow talk and pointless jokes did not interest him, and as soon as coffee arrived, he left them, making up an engagement so that he could get away. As he was leaving the club, the hall porter gave him a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, asking him to come the following evening and inspect an explosive umbrella, which went off the moment it was opened. It was the newest invention and had just arrived from Geneva. He tore the letter into pieces. He had decided not to attempt any more experiments. Then he wandered down to the Thames Embankment and sat for hours beside the river. The moon looked through a mane of tawny clouds like a lion\u2019s eye, and countless stars glittered in the hollow sky like gold dust scattered over a purple dome. From time to time, a barge swung into the muddy current and drifted away with the tide, while the railway signals changed from green to scarlet as trains shrieked across the bridge.\"}"
