          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c162370e622190c2407a3c62\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It then showed that I had reached an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, and I could therefore survey no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth\u2019s entire surface. At nine o\u2019clock I again lost sight of land to the east, though not before I realized that the balloon was drifting rapidly north-northwest. The curvature of the ocean below me was plainly visible, although my view was often blocked by masses of cloud floating back and forth. I now observed that even the lightest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level.\\n\\n\u201cAt half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers out through the valve. They did not float as I had expected, but fell straight down, like a bullet, all together and with the greatest speed, vanishing from sight within a few seconds. At first I did not know what to make of this extraordinary occurrence, for I could not believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly accelerated so enormously. But it soon occurred to me that the atmosphere was now far too thin to support even the feathers; that they truly fell, as they seemed to, with great speed; and that I had been startled by the combined velocities of their descent and my own ascent.\\n\\n\u201cBy ten o\u2019clock I found I had very little requiring my immediate attention. Everything was proceeding smoothly, and I believed the balloon was rising at a speed that increased from moment to moment, though I no longer had any means of measuring that increase. I felt no pain or discomfort of any kind, and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself first by examining the condition of my various instruments, then by renewing the atmosphere within the chamber. I decided to attend to this latter matter at regular intervals of forty minutes, more for the sake of preserving my health than because such frequent renewal was absolutely necessary. Meanwhile, I could not help indulging in anticipation. Fancy delighted in the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. Imagination, feeling herself unbound at last, wandered freely among the ever-changing marvels of a shadowy, unstable land. At one moment there were ancient, time-worn forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls roaring into bottomless chasms. Then I suddenly entered still noonday solitudes, where no wind from heaven ever intruded, and where vast meadows of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers stretched away for a weary distance, all silent and forever motionless.\"}"
