          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"86e61ef8a40e1840bf389642\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, his face white with terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile rushed out of Bentinck House, forcing his way through the crowd of fur-coated footmen gathered around the large striped awning, seeming neither to see nor hear anything. The night was bitterly cold, and the gas lamps around the square flared and flickered in the sharp wind; but his hands were feverishly hot, and his forehead burned like fire. He went on and on, almost staggering like a drunken man. A policeman watched him curiously as he passed, and a beggar who slouched out from an archway to ask for alms drew back in fear, seeing misery greater than his own. Once he stopped beneath a lamp and looked at his hands. He thought he could already see bloodstains on them, and a faint cry escaped his trembling lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had seen there. Murder! The very night seemed to know it, and the lonely wind seemed to howl it in his ear. It filled the dark corners of the streets. It grinned down at him from the rooftops.\\n\\nFirst he came to the Park, whose gloomy woods seemed to draw him in. He leaned wearily against the railings, cooling his brow on the wet metal and listening to the trembling silence of the trees. \u201cMurder! Murder!\u201d he kept repeating, as if saying the word again and again could lessen its horror. The sound of his own voice made him shudder, yet he almost hoped Echo might hear him and wake the sleeping city from its dreams. He felt a mad urge to stop some passing stranger and tell him everything.\\n\\nThen he wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, sordid alleys. Two women with painted faces mocked him as he passed. From a dark courtyard came the sounds of curses and blows, followed by shrill screams; and, huddled on a damp doorstep, he saw the bent forms of poverty and old age. A strange pity came over him. Were these children of sin and misery destined for their end, as he was for his? Were they, like him, only puppets in some monstrous spectacle?\\n\\nYet it was not the mystery but the comedy of suffering that struck him: its complete uselessness, its grotesque lack of meaning. How incoherent everything seemed! How utterly without harmony! He was astonished by the contrast between the shallow optimism of the age and the real facts of existence. He was still very young.\\n\\nAfter a while, he found himself before Marylebone Church. The silent road looked like a long ribbon of polished silver, marked here and there by the dark arabesques of moving shadows.\"}"
