          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"22ef95c4353161f5bf1fc2c5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day was fixed for the meeting (lest our honour should catch cold through delay), and on that day Drummle came forward with a civil little declaration in Estella\u2019s handwriting, that she had done him the honour of dancing with him more than once. This left me nothing but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, generally, to reject as untenable the notion that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove practised indiscriminate contradiction, and at last the advancement of good feeling was pronounced to have proceeded at a wonderful pace.\\n\\nI relate this lightly, but it was far from light to me. For I cannot sufficiently convey the pain it caused me to imagine Estella showing favour to a contemptible, awkward, sullen booby, so greatly beneath the common level. Even now, I believe it sprang from some pure flame of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her, that I could not bear the thought of her lowering herself to that hound. Doubtless I should have been unhappy whoever she had favoured; but a more deserving object would have given me another sort and measure of suffering.\\n\\nIt was not difficult for me to discover, and I quickly did discover, that Drummle had begun to attend her closely, and that she permitted it. Before long he was continually pursuing her, and he and I encountered one another daily. He persisted in his heavy, obstinate fashion, and Estella kept him on; at one time encouraging, at another discouraging him, now nearly flattering him, now plainly scorning him, now knowing him intimately, now hardly recollecting who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had named him, was accustomed to watching in ambush, however, and possessed the patience of his kind. Besides this, he had a stupid confidence in his wealth and family consequence, which occasionally served him well,\u2014almost standing in for concentration and resolute purpose. Thus the Spider, stubbornly observing Estella, outwatched many livelier insects, and would often unroll himself and descend at the very proper moment.\\n\\nAt a certain Assembly Ball at Richmond (Assembly Balls were held in most places in those days), where Estella had eclipsed every other beauty, this clumsy Drummle lingered so constantly about her, and met with such tolerance from her, that I determined to speak with her about him. I seized the next occasion; it was when she was awaiting Mrs. Blandley to carry her home, and sat apart amid some flowers, prepared to leave.\"}"
