{"request_id":"b7438e32a3cdecbb05383fb8","rewritten_text":"The stars still shone brightly when I woke, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, showed that it was midnight. I woke from troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s last feast. I arrived hungry; the covers were lifted, and hot water gave off its unsatisfying steam. Then I fled from the host’s anger, for he had taken Raymond’s form. In my sick imagination, the dishes he threw after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s figure, twisted by a thousand distortions, grew into a giant phantom with the mark of plague upon its brow. The rising shadow grew and grew, filling the hard vault above us, which seemed to hold and enclose the world, and then trying to break beyond it. The nightmare became torment. With a great effort, I shook off sleep and restored my reason to its usual work. My first thought was of Perdita. I had to return to her; I had to support her, finding in despair whatever strength might sustain her wounded heart, calling her back from wild grief through the stern laws of duty and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my only guide. I turned away from the dreadful ruin of the Golden City and, after much effort, managed to free myself from its walls. Outside, I met a group of soldiers. I borrowed a horse from one and hurried to my sister. In this short time, the plain had changed. The camp had broken up; remnants of the scattered army stood in small groups here and there. Every face was darkened, and every movement showed shock and fear.

With a heavy heart, I entered the palace and stood afraid to go forward, speak, or look. Perdita was in the middle of the hall. She sat on the marble floor, her head bowed upon her breast, her hair loose, her fingers constantly twisting around each other. She was pale as marble, and agony tightened every feature. She saw me and looked up in question; that brief hopeful glance was misery. The words died before I could say them, and I felt a dreadful smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture. Her head fell again, and her fingers moved restlessly once more. At last I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my look, and she would have given worlds not to have her terrible sorrow put into firm, irreversible words. Indeed, she seemed to want to turn my thoughts away from it. She rose from the floor. “Hush!” she whispered. “After much weeping, Clara is asleep; we must not wake her.”"}

