{"request_id":"c1848643674a6f4320438922","rewritten_text":"At last, we made the box lighter by taking out two thirds of what it contained, and then, though not without difficulty, succeeded in lifting it from the pit. The things removed were laid among the brambles, and the dog was left to watch them, Jupiter having given him strict commands neither, on any account, to leave the place nor to utter a sound until we came back. We then hastened homeward with the chest, arriving safely at the hut, though only after severe exertion, at one in the morning. Exhausted as we were, it was impossible for us to do anything further at once. We rested until two, took supper, and immediately set off again for the hills, carrying three strong sacks which, fortunately, we found on the premises. A little before four we reached the pit, shared the remaining treasure among ourselves as evenly as possible, and, leaving the excavations open, once more started for the hut. There, for the second time, we set down our golden loads, just as the earliest pale lines of dawn showed above the tree-tops in the East.

By this time we were utterly spent; yet the excitement of the occasion would not permit us to rest. After a troubled sleep lasting three or four hours, we rose, as though by mutual agreement, to inspect our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the very top, and the entire day, with most of the following night, was consumed in examining what it held. There had been no order or method in its packing. Everything had been thrown together indiscriminately. When we had carefully sorted it all, we discovered that our riches were even greater than we had first imagined. In coin there were somewhat over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars—reckoning the pieces as closely as we could by the tables of the time. There was not the smallest trace of silver. All was old gold, of great variety—French, Spanish, and German coins, with a few English guineas, and certain counters unlike any specimens we had ever before encountered. Several coins were exceedingly large and weighty, but so worn that their inscriptions could not be deciphered. There was no American currency. We found the jewels harder to value. There were diamonds—some remarkably large and splendid—one hundred and ten altogether, with not a small one among them; eighteen rubies of extraordinary brightness; three hundred and ten emeralds, all exceedingly beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, together with an opal. All these stones had been torn from their settings and cast loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we gathered from amid the other gold, seemed to have been hammered flat, apparently to prevent their identification."}

