          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f1381dd9e899cf91dd50ee2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She regarded me intently for a short time, then said in a low voice,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you ask for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly,\u201d I replied, \u201cthat you will not mistake them for the others. They may share the same blood, but, trust me, they are not of the same disposition.\u201d\\n\\nStill studying me closely, Miss Havisham repeated,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you ask for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not clever enough, you see,\u201d I answered, aware that I coloured slightly, \u201cto conceal from you, even if I wished it, that I do ask something. Miss Havisham, if you would provide the money to render my friend Herbert a permanent service in life, though the nature of it requires that it be done without his knowing, I could tell you how.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy should it be done without his knowing?\u201d she asked, placing both hands on her stick so that she could observe me more closely.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d I said, \u201cI began the service myself, over two years ago, without his knowledge, and I do not wish to be discovered. Why I am unable to complete it, I cannot tell. That belongs to a secret which is another person\u2019s, not mine.\u201d\\n\\nLittle by little she turned her eyes away from me and fixed them on the fire. She watched it for what, in the silence and the dim light of the slowly burning candles, seemed a long while; then the fall of some red coals stirred her, and she looked back at me\u2014first blankly, then with attention that slowly gathered itself. Estella knitted throughout. When Miss Havisham had fully fixed her gaze upon me, she said, as though our conversation had never paused,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat more?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEstella,\u201d I said, turning to her and striving to steady my shaking voice, \u201cyou know that I love you. You know I have loved you long and deeply.\u201d\\n\\nAt my address she lifted her eyes to my face; her fingers continued their work, and she looked at me without any change of expression. I saw Miss Havisham glance from me to her, and then from her back to me.\\n\\n\u201cI would have said this earlier, except for my long error. It led me to hope that Miss Havisham intended us for each other. While I believed that you were unable, as it were, to help yourself, I held back from saying it. But I must say it now.\u201d\\n\\nWithout altering her expression, and while her fingers still moved, Estella shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said in reply to that gesture,\u2014\u201cI know. I have no hope that I shall ever call you mine, Estella.\u201d\"}"
