          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cdf513bb193e0b9117767134\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No condoling letter had gone to Ireland. The slight had fallen upon the sinner\u2019s own head; for when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no letter of sympathy arrived at Kellynch, and there was therefore only too much cause to fear that the Dalrymples regarded the connexion as ended. The question was how this troublesome affair might be repaired, and how they might once more be received as cousins; and it was a question which Lady Russell and Mr Elliot, in a more sensible spirit, did not think insignificant. \u201cFamily connexions were always desirable to retain, and good society always desirable to seek; Lady Dalrymple had taken a house for three months in Laura Place, where she would live in style. She had been at Bath the preceding year, and Lady Russell had heard her described as a charming woman. It was highly desirable that the connexion should be restored, if it might be done without any sacrifice of propriety by the Elliots.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would employ his own methods, and at length composed a very handsome letter, full of explanation, regret, and entreaty, to his right honourable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot could approve the letter; but it achieved all that was necessary, producing three scrawled lines from the Dowager Viscountess. \u201cShe was greatly honoured, and would be happy to know them.\u201d The labour of the affair was finished, and its pleasures commenced. They paid visits in Laura Place; they possessed the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, to place wherever they could be most conspicuous; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was mortified. Even had Lady Dalrymple and her daughter been exceedingly pleasant, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they occasioned; but they were nothing at all. They had no distinction of manner, talent, or intellect. Lady Dalrymple had gained the title of \u201ca charming woman\u201d merely because she smiled and returned a civil answer to all. Miss Carteret, who had even less to say, was so plain and so ungainly that she could never have been endured in Camden Place except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell owned that she had looked for something better; yet still, \u201cit was an acquaintance worth possessing;\u201d and when Anne ventured to give Mr Elliot her opinion of them, he allowed that they were nothing in themselves, but continued to insist that, as family connexions, as good company, and as persons likely to gather good company about them, they had their worth. Anne smiled, and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy notion of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of intelligent, well-informed people, who have much to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
        }
