          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68a8b1b11c7815977b01658f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had secured a very handsome house in Camden Place, in an elevated and imposing position, such as suited a man of importance; and he and Elizabeth were established there, greatly to their contentment.\\n\\nAnne went into it with a heavy heart, foreseeing a confinement of many months, and asking herself anxiously, \u201cOh! when shall I quit you again?\u201d Yet an unexpected warmth in the reception she met with did her some good. Her father and sister were pleased to see her, for the pleasure of displaying the house and its furnishings, and welcomed her kindly. Her presence as a fourth person at dinner was considered an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was very agreeable and full of smiles, though her civilities and smiles were more habitual. Anne had always supposed she would assume whatever was proper on her arrival, but the graciousness of the others had not been expected. They were plainly in high spirits, and Anne was soon to hear why. They had no wish to hear her. Having allowed for a few compliments on her being much missed in their former neighbourhood, which Anne could not offer, they made only a few languid inquiries before the conversation became entirely their own. Uppercross interested them not at all, Kellynch scarcely: Bath was everything.\\n\\nThey delighted in assuring her that Bath had surpassed their expectations in every particular. Their house was unquestionably the finest in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms possessed many clear advantages over every other they had seen or heard described, and their superiority was equally apparent in the manner of their arrangement and the elegance of the furniture. Their acquaintance was eagerly desired. Everyone wished to call upon them. They had declined many introductions, yet cards were continually being left by persons wholly unknown to them.\\n\\nHere was ample material for enjoyment. Could Anne be surprised that her 