          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cec0c44c4d7054d4cd886e44\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d I said, handing him back the slip, \u201cI am no less bewildered than before. Though every jewel of Golconda were promised me for solving this riddle, I am certain I could never win them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd still,\u201d said Legrand, \u201cthe answer is far from being as difficult as you may have been led to suppose by a first cursory glance at the symbols. These symbols, as anyone might easily infer, constitute a cipher\u2014that is, they bear a meaning; yet, from all that was known of Kidd, I could not imagine him able to devise any of the more intricate cryptographs. I decided immediately that it was of a simple sort\u2014one which, nevertheless, would seem to the sailor\u2019s uncultivated mind utterly insoluble without its key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually deciphered it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWithout difficulty; I have unraveled others ten thousand times more obscure. Circumstances, together with a certain turn of mind, have induced me to interest myself in such puzzles, and it may fairly be questioned whether human ingenuity can frame an enigma of this nature which human ingenuity cannot, through suitable application, solve. Indeed, once I had determined that the characters were connected and legible, I gave scarcely any consideration to the simple difficulty of discovering their meaning.\\n\\n\u201cIn this instance\u2014indeed, in every instance of secret writing\u2014the first inquiry concerns the language of the cipher; for the rules of solution, particularly in regard to simpler ciphers, depend upon and vary with the character of the particular idiom. Generally, one has no choice but to experiment, guided by probability, with every language known to the person attempting the solution, until the right one is found. With the cipher before us, however, the signature removed all uncertainty. The play upon the name \u2018Kidd\u2019 can be understood in English alone. Had it not been for that circumstance, I should have begun with Spanish and French, the languages in which a pirate of the Spanish Main would most naturally have written a secret of this sort. As matters stood, I assumed the cryptograph was English.\\n\\n\u201cYou notice that there are no separations between the words. If there had been, the work would have been comparatively easy. In that event, I should have begun by comparing and analyzing the shorter words; and if a one-letter word had appeared, as would be most probable\u2014an a or I, for instance\u2014I should have regarded the solution as certain. But since there were no divisions, my first task was to determine the most common letters, as well as those occurring least often.\u201d\"}"
