          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"97e02ce90068fc4dcbff5c92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was brought to you. You understand that I know the particulars?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch was the case, certainly,\u201d said Defarge. By a chance pressure of his wife\u2019s elbow, as she knitted and hummed, he had been told that he should answer, though always shortly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and from your protection his daughter removed him, attended by a trim brown monsieur; what is his name?\u2014in a small wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of the bank of Tellson and Company\u2014to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch was the case,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting recollections!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou hear little of them now?\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIndeed,\u201d madame interposed, raising her eyes from her work and her quiet song, \u201cwe hear nothing of them. We had news of their safe arrival, and perhaps one letter more, perhaps two; but after that they have gone their way through life\u2014we ours\u2014and there has been no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so, madame,\u201d returned the spy. \u201cShe is about to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout to?\u201d repeated madame. \u201cShe was handsome enough to have married years ago. You English seem cold to me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know that I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can tell it by your speech,\u201d replied madame; \u201cand whatever the speech is, I suppose the man must be.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not regard the description as flattering; yet he made the most of it, dismissing it with a laugh. When he had finished his cognac, he went on:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is to be married. Yet not to an Englishman, but to one who, like herself, was born French. And, speaking of Gaspard\u2014ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!\u2014it is curious that she is to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was raised to that height of so many feet; that is to say, the present Marquis. But in England he lives unknown; there he is no Marquis, but Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge continued knitting evenly, though the news visibly affected her husband. Whatever he attempted behind the little counter, in striking a light and lighting his pipe, he was disturbed, and his hand could not be relied upon. The spy would have been no spy had he not noticed it, and stored it away in his mind.\\n\\nHaving made this one stroke, at least, whatever value it might later have, and with no customers entering to assist him in making another, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and departed; taking the opportunity, before leaving, to say politely that he anticipated the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
