          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"41192010d85ff490416a3a4b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, have you reached? What picture has formed in your mind?\u201d\\n\\nA chill ran over me as Dupin asked the question. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said, \u201ccommitted this crime\u2014some raging lunatic who escaped from a nearby _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some ways,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour idea is not beside the point. But the voices of madmen, even during their wildest fits, never match that strange voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and their speech, however confused its words may be, still has the natural order of syllables. Besides, a madman\u2019s hair is not like the hair I hold in my hand. I pulled this small tuft from the tightly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you think of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I said, utterly shaken; \u201cthis hair is very strange\u2014this is not _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not said that it is,\u201d he answered. \u201cBut before we settle that question, I want you to look at the small sketch I have drawn on this paper. It is an exact copy of what one witness called \u2018dark bruises and deep marks from fingernails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what Messrs. Dumas and Etienne called \u2018a series of livid marks, clearly made by fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou can see,\u201d my friend continued, laying the paper on the table before us, \u201cthat this drawing suggests a strong, unchanging grip. There is no sign of _slipping_. Every finger remained\u2014perhaps until the victim died\u2014in the terrible hold in which it first sank. Now try to place all your fingers at once in the separate marks you see here.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but could not do it.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing this fairly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies flat, but a human throat is round. Here is a piece of wood about the same size around as a throat. Wrap the drawing around it, and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did so, but the problem was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis not the mark of a human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d Dupin replied, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical description of the large tawny Ourang-Outang of the East Indian Islands. The creature\u2019s enormous size, extraordinary strength and agility, savage fierceness, and tendency to imitate were well known. At once I understood the full horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the fingers,\u201d I said when I had finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
