          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0d6551224630f6870d2fcc3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On one occasion, however, the lieutenant asked him why he had ventured so far over the ice in so unusual a conveyance.\\n\\nHis face at once took on the deepest melancholy, and he answered, \u201cTo pursue one who escaped from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the person you followed travel in the same manner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I believe we may have seen him; for the day before we found you, we observed dogs drawing a sledge, with a man seated in it, over the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis awakened the stranger\u2019s interest, and he put countless questions about the course the d\u00e6mon, as he named him, had taken. Shortly afterward, when we were alone, he said, \u201cI have no doubt stirred your curiosity, as well as that of these worthy people; but you are too kind to question me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAssuredly; it would be both impertinent and inhuman for me to distress you with my own curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYet you saved me from a singular and dangerous condition; with benevolence, you restored me to life.\u201d\\n\\nNot long afterward he asked whether I believed the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I told him I could not reply with certainty, since the ice had not broken until close to midnight, and the traveller might have reached a secure place before then; but I was unable to determine it.\\n\\nFrom that moment, a fresh spirit of life seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed the utmost desire to go on deck and look for the sledge that had previously been seen; but I have convinced him to stay in the cabin, since he is much too feeble to endure the harshness of the air. I have promised that someone will keep watch on his behalf and immediately inform him should any new object come into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of all that concerns this strange event to the present time. The stranger has slowly regained his health, but remains very quiet and seems troubled whenever anyone but myself enters his cabin. Still, his manner is so winning and mild that all the sailors take an interest in him, though they have spoken with him very little. For myself, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his continual, profound sorrow moves me to sympathy and pity. In happier times he must have been a noble being, for even now, in ruin, he is so engaging and amiable.\\n\\nIn one of my letters, my dear Margaret, I said that I would find no friend upon the vast ocean; yet I have found a man whom, before misery had crushed his spirit, I would gladly have had as the brother of my heart.\"}"
