          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e5991cd83877166a359ea1f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He sprang to his feet and caught her harshly by the arm. \u201cShow him to me. Which one is he? Point him out. I must see him!\u201d he cried; but just then the Duke of Berwick\u2019s four-in-hand passed between them, and by the time it had gone by, the carriage had driven out of the park.\\n\\n\u201cHe has gone,\u201d Sibyl said sadly. \u201cI wish you had seen him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I had; for, as surely as there is a God in heaven, if he ever wrongs you, I will kill him.\u201d\\n\\nShe stared at him in horror. He said it again. The words cleft the air like a dagger. People nearby began to stare. A lady beside them gave a little laugh.\\n\\n\u201cCome away, Jim, come away,\u201d she whispered. He followed her stubbornly as she made her way through the crowd. He was pleased with what he had said.\\n\\nWhen they came to the Achilles Statue, she faced him. Her eyes held pity, though laughter touched her lips. She shook her head. \u201cYou are foolish, Jim, completely foolish; only a bad-tempered boy. How can you say such dreadful things? You have no idea what you mean. You are merely jealous and unkind. Ah! I wish you would fall in love. Love makes people good, and what you said was wicked.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am sixteen,\u201d he replied, \u201cand I know what I mean. Mother is no use to you. She does not know how to take care of you. I wish now I were not going to Australia at all. I am half-minded to give the whole thing up. I would, if my articles were not signed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, don\u2019t be so solemn, Jim. You are like one of the heroes in those foolish melodramas Mother used to love acting in. I will not quarrel with you. I have seen him, and oh! seeing him is perfect happiness. We shall not quarrel. I know you would never hurt anyone I love, would you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot while you love him, I suppose,\u201d he answered sulkily.\\n\\n\u201cI shall love him forever