          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"811c7bfad25a91492cdc3d3b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMen marry because they are tired; women because they are curious. Both end up disappointed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I am likely to marry, Harry. I am too much in love. That is one of your sayings. I am acting on it, as I do with everything you say.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho are you in love with?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a pause.\\n\\n\u201cWith an actress,\u201d said Dorian Gray, blushing.\\n\\nLord Henry shrugged. \u201cThat is a rather ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not say that if you saw her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is she?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHer name is Sibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever heard of her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has. But people will someday. She is a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are decorative. They never have anything to say, but they say it charmingly. Women show the triumph of matter over mind, just as men show the triumph of mind over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you say that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it is quite true. I am studying women at present, so I should know. The subject is not as difficult as I thought. In the end, I find there are only two kinds of women: plain and painted. Plain women are very useful. If you want a reputation for respectability, you need only take one of them down to supper. The other women are very charming. But they make one mistake: they paint themselves to look young. Our grandmothers painted themselves to seem witty. Rouge and cleverness used to go together. That is over now. As long as a woman can look ten years younger than her own daughter, she is perfectly satisfied. As for conversation, there are only five women in London worth speaking to, and two of them cannot be admitted into decent society. But tell me about your genius. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Harry, your opinions frighten me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you meet her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will tell you, Harry, but you must not be unsympathetic. After all, it would never have happened if I had not met you. You filled me with a wild desire to know everything about life. For days after I met you, something seemed to pulse in my veins. As I lounged in the park or walked down Piccadilly, I looked at everyone who passed and wondered, with mad curiosity, what kind of lives they lived. Some fascinated me. Others filled me with terror. There was an exquisite poison in the air. I had a passion for sensations....\"}"
