          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"54346ba18eb1d98b0b694137\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He got up and left the room with my nurse, and a moment later my father came in.\\n\\nNothing could have pleased me more at that moment than seeing my father. I held out my hand to him and cried,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe, then\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father reassured me that they were well, and tried to lift my low spirits by speaking of those dear subjects; but he soon saw that no one could be cheerful in a prison. \u201cWhat a place you live in, my son!\u201d he said sadly, looking at the barred windows and the miserable room. \u201cYou travelled in search of happiness, but some cruel fate seems to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe name of my unhappy murdered friend caused more pain than I could bear in my weak condition; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas, yes, my father,\u201d I replied. \u201cSome most terrible destiny hangs over me, and I must live to carry it out, or I surely would have died beside Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to speak for long, because my uncertain health made every precaution necessary to keep me calm. Mr. Kirwin came in and insisted that I must not wear out my strength with too much effort. Yet my father\u2019s presence was like that of a kind guardian angel, and I slowly regained my health.\\n\\nAs my illness left me, I was overcome by a dark, hopeless sadness that nothing could drive away. Clerval\u2019s image was always before me, pale and murdered. More than once, the distress these thoughts caused made my friends fear that I would suffer a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why did they keep alive a life so miserable and hateful? Surely it was so that I might fulfil my destiny, which was now nearing its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death would silence this beating heart and free me from the crushing weight of grief that pressed me into the dust; and, when justice gave its sentence, I too would sink into rest. At that time death still seemed far away, though I constantly wished for it; and I often sat for hours without moving or speaking, longing for some great upheaval that would bury both me and my destroyer beneath its ruins.\\n\\nThe time of the assizes drew near. I had already spent three months in prison, and though I was still weak and always in danger of falling ill again, I had to travel nearly a hundred miles to the county town where the court met. Mr. Kirwin took charge of gathering witnesses and arranging my defence.\"}"
