          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"08d80796b4177111ebc56c6f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed deliberately to misunderstand the word she repeated, and quickly added, \u201cYes, customers; in banking, we generally call the people connected with us our customers. He was a French gentleman; a man of science; a man of great learning\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot from Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our connection was business, but confidential. At that time I was in our French office, and had been for\u2014oh! twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask what time you mean, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many French gentlemen and French families, were entirely in Tellson\u2019s hands. In the same way, I am, or have been, a trustee of one sort or another for scores of our customers. These are only business connections, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special concern, nothing like feeling. During my business life, I have gone from one to another just as I go from one customer to another during a business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am only a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014her strangely furrowed forehead was fixed intently on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan, my mother surviving my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the small, hesitant hand that trustingly reached for his, and formally raised it to his lips. Then he led the young lady directly back to her chair. Holding its back with his left hand, and using his right in turn to rub his chin, tug his wig by the ears, or point as he spoke, he stood looking down at her face while she looked up at his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truthfully I spoke of myself just now, when I said I had no feelings and that every connection I have with my fellow human beings is merely business, if you consider that I have never seen you since. No; you have been a ward of Tellson\u2019s House ever since, and I have been occupied with Tellson\u2019s other business ever since. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an enormous financial mangle.\u201d\"}"
        }
