          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f0fd04aae61d04b0a417a6ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All this, and still more\u2014far more\u2014would I gladly set down in detail. But, to be concise, I must receive my recompense. I languish to return to my family and my home; and, as the condition of any further communication from me\u2014in return for the illumination which it is in my power to cast upon many highly important departments of physical and metaphysical science\u2014I must request, through the influence of your honorable body, a pardon for the offence of which I have been guilty in the deaths of the creditors at my departure from Rotterdam. Such, then, is the purpose of this present paper. Its bearer, a native of the moon, whom I have persuaded and duly instructed to serve as my messenger to the earth, will wait upon your Excellencies\u2019 pleasure, and return to me with the pardon referred to, should it be possible by any means to procure it.\\n\\n\u201cI have the honor to remain, etc., your Excellencies\u2019 most humble servant,\\n\\n\u201cHANS PFAALL.\u201d\\n\\nWhen the reading of this most singular document was concluded, Professor Rub-a-dub, it is reported, let his pipe fall to the ground in the excess of his amazement; while Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, removing his spectacles, cleaned them and placed them in his pocket, then so completely forgot himself and his dignity as to whirl three times upon his heel in the very essence of astonishment and admiration. There could be no question\u2014the pardon must be secured. At least Professor Rub-a-dub declared so with a hearty oath; and the celebrated Von Underduk at last came to the same conclusion, taking his fellow man of science by the arm and, without a word, setting off homeward to consider what measures ought to be taken. On arriving, however, at the burgomaster\u2019s door, the professor ventured to observe that, since the messenger had seen fit to vanish\u2014doubtless terrified to death by the fierce aspect of the Rotterdam burghers\u2014the pardon would be of scant service, for no one save a man of the moon would undertake a journey across so immense a distance. The burgomaster admitted the force of this remark, and thus the affair came to an end. The rumors and conjectures, however, did not. Once published, the letter produced a great variety of gossip and opinions. Certain excessively wise persons even rendered themselves absurd by pronouncing the entire affair nothing more than a hoax. Yet hoax, among people of that sort, is, I believe, a broad term for everything beyond their understanding. For myself, I cannot imagine upon what grounds they have based so grave a charge. Let us hear what they allege:\"}"
