          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1cf91fb0604cc5f7782ccf2f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was set for the meeting, lest our honour should grow cold through delay. The next day Drummle appeared with a polite little statement in Estella\u2019s handwriting, saying that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. This left me no choice but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and generally to deny, as impossible to maintain, that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat glaring and snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove contradicted everyone without distinction; and at last it was declared that good feeling had advanced at an astonishing rate.\\n\\nI tell it lightly, but it was no light matter to me. I cannot properly describe the pain it gave me to think that Estella should show any favour to such a contemptible, awkward, sulky fool, so far below even the ordinary level. Even now, I believe that some pure generosity and selflessness in my love for her made me unable to bear the thought of her lowering herself to that hound. Of course, I should have been miserable whoever she favoured; but someone more worthy would have caused me a different kind and degree of suffering.\\n\\nIt was easy to discover, and I soon did discover, that Drummle had begun to follow her closely, and that she allowed it. Before long, he was always pursuing her, and he and I met each other every day. He persisted in his dull way, and Estella kept him going: sometimes encouraging him, sometimes discouraging him, sometimes almost flattering him, sometimes openly despising him, sometimes seeming to know him very well, and sometimes scarcely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was accustomed to waiting in ambush, and had the patience of his kind. Besides that, he had a stupid confidence in his money and his family importance, which sometimes served him well, almost replacing focus and firm purpose. So the Spider, stubbornly watching Estella, outwaited many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at exactly the right moment.\\n\\nAt an Assembly Ball in Richmond\u2014there used to be Assembly Balls in most places then\u2014Estella outshone every other beauty. There this clumsy Drummle hovered around her so constantly, and she tolerated him so much, that I decided to speak to her about him. I took the next chance, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to leave.\"}"
