          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a406a75820ec8eafad1a1ade\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He got up and left the room with my nurse, and a moment later my father entered.\\n\\nNothing could have given me greater pleasure at that moment than my father\u2019s arrival. I reached out my hand to him and cried,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe, then\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father reassured me that they were well and tried, by speaking of those subjects so dear to my heart, to lift my despairing spirits; but he soon saw that a prison could not be a place of cheerfulness. \u201cWhat a place you are living in, my son!\u201d he said, gazing sadly at the barred windows and the miserable state of the room. \u201cYou travelled in search of happiness, but some fatality seems to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe name of my unfortunate murdered friend stirred emotions too violent for me to bear in my weakened condition; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas, yes, my father,\u201d I replied. \u201cSome destiny of the most terrible kind hangs over me, and I must live to carry it out, or I surely would have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to speak for long, because my uncertain health made every precaution necessary to preserve my calm. Mr. Kirwin came in and insisted that my strength must not be drained by too much effort. Yet my father\u2019s presence was like that of a guardian angel, and I gradually regained my health.\\n\\nAs my illness left me, I became consumed by a dark, hopeless melancholy that nothing could dispel. Clerval\u2019s image stood always before me, pale and murdered. More than once, the agitation caused by these thoughts made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why did they preserve a life so miserable and hateful? Surely it was so that I might fulfil my destiny, which is now nearing its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death will still these throbbings and free me from the immense burden of anguish that crushes me into the dust; and in carrying out justice\u2019s sentence, I too shall sink into rest. At that time death still seemed far away, though the desire for it was always in my mind; and I often sat for hours without moving or speaking, longing for some great upheaval that would bury both me and my destroyer beneath its ruins.\\n\\nThe time of the assizes drew near. I had already spent three months in prison, and though I remained weak and constantly at risk of relapse, I was required to travel nearly a hundred miles to the county town where the court was held. Mr. Kirwin took upon himself every task of gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
