          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ad7aaca07a80b9df56e8ddbb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any such business as that, I said to myself.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed up and the watchers were gone. Nobody was around except the family, Widow Bartley, and our crowd. I watched their faces to see whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t make it out.\\n\\nAround midday the undertaker came with his assistant. They put the coffin in the middle of the room on a pair of chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were full. I saw that the coffin lid was as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare look beneath it with people there.\\n\\nThen people began pouring in, and the boys and girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin. For half an hour, people passed slowly around in single file and looked down at the dead man\u2019s face for a moment. Some shed a tear, and everything was very quiet and solemn, except for the girls and boys holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and sobbing a little. There was no other sound but feet scraping on the floor and people blowing their noses\u2014because people always blow them more at funerals than anywhere else except church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker moved about in his black gloves, with his gentle, soothing manner, putting on the final touches and getting people and things arranged neatly and comfortably, without making more noise than a cat. He never spoke; he shifted people about, squeezed in latecomers, opened passageways, and did it all with nods and hand signals. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the smoothest, quietest, most stealthy man I ever saw, and he had no more smile in him than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeon\u2014a sickly one\u2014and when everything was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It sounded pretty squeaky and wheezy, and everybody joined in singing, and Peter was the only one who had anything good, in my opinion. Then Reverend Hobson began, slow and solemn, to speak; and right away the most outrageous racket broke out in the cellar that anybody ever heard. It was only one dog, but he made a tremendous noise and kept it up without stopping. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
