          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"04e1ad5fc6e98b4bfa6fd63d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s last reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was more crowded than ever. Six Cabinet Ministers had arrived from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e in their stars and ribbons; all the pretty women wore their finest gowns; and at the end of the picture gallery stood Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a large, Tartar-looking woman with tiny black eyes and magnificent emeralds, speaking bad French at the top of her voice and laughing extravagantly at everything said to her. It was certainly an extraordinary mixture of people. Splendid peeresses chatted pleasantly with fierce Radicals, popular preachers brushed coattails with distinguished sceptics, a whole flock of bishops followed a stout prima donna from room to room, several Royal Academicians stood on the staircase disguised as artists, and it was said that at one point the supper room was completely packed with geniuses. Indeed, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s finest evenings, and the Princess remained until nearly half past eleven.\\n\\nAs soon as she had left, Lady Windermere returned to the picture gallery, where a famous political economist was gravely explaining the scientific theory of music to an outraged Hungarian virtuoso, and began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. She looked remarkably beautiful, with her stately ivory throat, large blue eyes like forget-me-nots, and thick coils of golden hair. Or pure gold, they were\u2014not the pale straw shade that now steals the lovely name of gold, but the gold woven into sunbeams or concealed in strange amber; and they gave her face something of a saint\u2019s halo, along with no small part of a sinner\u2019s allure. She was an interesting psychological study. Early in life, she had discovered the important truth that nothing resembles innocence so closely as an indiscretion; and through a succession of reckless adventures, half of them entirely harmless, she had gained all the privileges of a personality. She had changed husbands more than once; indeed, Debrett lists three marriages for her; but since she had never changed her lover, society had long since stopped gossiping about her. She was now forty, childless, and possessed of that excessive passion for pleasure which is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she looked eagerly around the room and said in her clear contralto voice, \u2018Where is my cheiromantist?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Your what, Gladys?\u2019 cried the Duchess, starting despite herself.\\n\\n\u2018My cheiromantist, Duchess. I can\u2019t do without him at the moment.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Gladys! You are always so original,\u2019 murmured the Duchess, trying to recall what a cheiromantist actually was, and hoping it was not the same thing as a chiropodist.\\n\\n\u2018He comes to examine my hand twice a week, regularly,\u2019 Lady Windermere continued, \u2018and is most interesting about it.\u2019\"}"
