{"request_id":"db0ab3a0b9bc5d4f1e44b972","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have while Fate still held this dreadful destiny in the balance? The marriage had to be postponed, whatever the cost. On that point he was completely determined. Deeply as he loved the girl, and though the slightest touch of her fingers as they sat together sent every nerve in his body trembling with exquisite delight, he saw just as clearly where his duty lay. He knew perfectly well that he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once it was done, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton and place his life in her hands without fearing that he had done wrong. Once it was done, he could take her in his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him or bow her head in shame. But it had to be done first; and the sooner, the better for them both.

Many men in his circumstances would have chosen the easy path of flirtation rather than the hard ascent of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure before principle. His love contained more than passion alone; Sybil represented to him everything good and noble. For a moment he felt a natural horror at what he was being asked to do, but it quickly faded. His heart told him it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that no other course was open to him. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others, and though the task imposed on him was undeniably terrible, he knew he must not allow selfishness to overcome love. Sooner or later, all of us are called upon to make the same decision—the same question is put to us all. It came to Lord Arthur early in life, before his nature had been ruined by the calculating cynicism of middle age or his heart eaten away by the shallow, fashionable selfishness of the present day, and he did not hesitate to do his duty. Fortunately for him, he was no mere dreamer or idle dilettante. Had he been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and allowed indecision to spoil his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him, life meant action rather than thought. He possessed the rarest of all qualities: common sense.

By now, the wild and confused emotions of the previous night had entirely passed, and he looked back almost ashamed at his frantic wandering from street to street and his fierce emotional torment. The very genuineness of his suffering now made it seem unreal. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rage and despair over the inevitable."}

