          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e79cd0487dc1e854963bfba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her position was truly one of unusual trouble and difficulty. Though she felt a strong, urgent wish to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s past, she could not betray the trust the unhappy woman she had just spoken with had placed in her, a young and innocent girl. The woman\u2019s words and manner had moved Rose Maylie deeply; and along with her love for the young boy in her care, almost as strong and sincere, was her earnest desire to bring the outcast girl back to repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to stay in London for only three days before leaving for several weeks at a distant part of the coast. It was now midnight on the first day. What could she decide to do that might be carried out within forty-eight hours? And how could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was staying with them and would remain for the next two days; but Rose knew the excellent gentleman\u2019s quick temper too well. She could clearly foresee the anger with which, in his first burst of indignation, he would view the person responsible for Oliver\u2019s recapture. She could not trust him with the secret when no experienced person could support her plea for the girl. For the same reasons, she had to be extremely careful in telling Mrs. Maylie, whose first impulse would certainly be to discuss the matter with the worthy doctor. Asking a lawyer for help, even if she had known how, was hardly possible for the same reason. For a moment she thought of seeking Harry\u2019s assistance; but then she remembered their last farewell. It seemed unfair to call him back when\u2014the tears rose to her eyes as she followed this thought\u2014he might by now have learned to forget her and be happier away from her.\\n\\nTroubled by these thoughts, leaning first toward one plan and then another, only to draw back from them all as each new difficulty came to mind, Rose spent a sleepless, anxious night. After thinking further the next day, she came to the desperate decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it pains him,\u201d she thought, \u201cto return here, how much it will pain me! But perhaps he will not come. He may write, or he may come himself and carefully avoid meeting me\u2014he did so when he left. I scarcely expected that he would, but it was better for us both.\u201d Here Rose laid down her pen and turned away, as if even the paper that was to carry her message must not see her weep.\"}"
