{"request_id":"bd8b7a7666ddeeceae795ceb","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. Even when my frantic thoughts appeared to urge me towards insanity, I remained aware of my condition, and revealed them to nothing save silence and solitude. Those about me perceived none of it. They saw only a poor girl crushed in spirit, speaking with a soft and gentle voice, while tears at times escaped from beneath her lowered lids, though she endeavoured to conceal them. One who delighted in being alone and recoiled from notice; who never smiled; oh, no! I never smiled—and that was all.

At last, I escaped. I quitted my guardian’s house, and was never heard of more; from the letters I left behind, and other circumstances I arranged, it was supposed that I had taken my own life. I was therefore sought with less diligence than might otherwise have been used, and before long every trace and recollection of me had vanished. I departed London in a small vessel sailing for a harbour in the north of England. Having accomplished my design and found myself wholly alone, peace returned to me. The sea lay calm, and the vessel glided gently forward; I sat upon the deck beneath heaven’s open canopy, and thought myself transformed. I was no longer the wild, raving, most wretched Mathilda, but a young Hermitess devoted to seclusion, whose breast she must labour to preserve from every tumult and impious despair—The fanciful, nunlike attire I had assumed; the knowledge that my very being was a secret possessed by myself alone; the solitude to which I was henceforth for ever appointed, fostered mild thoughts within my wounded heart. The breeze stirring my hair restored me, and with tranquil eyes I watched the sunlight sparkling on the waves, and the birds pursuing one another above the water, scarcely touching it with their feathers. I slept also, untroubled by dreams, and woke refreshed, to enjoy once more my peaceful liberty.

After four days we reached the harbour for which we had sailed. I would not stay upon the coast, but went at once inland. I had already determined the place in which I should dwell. It was to be a lonely house upon a broad plain, near no other habitation: there I might see the entire horizon, and roam far without being disturbed by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not misanthropic, but felt that the mild course of my feelings depended upon solitude. I established myself in a vast wilderness: on a bleak heath strewn with stones, amid which short grass grew, with here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

