          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6147a79fd699e5fe779c2b16\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, midnight sounded from the great tower at Westminster, and with every stroke of the deep bell the night itself appeared to shudder. Then the railway lamps were extinguished, save for one lone light shining like a great ruby upon a colossal mast, and the city\u2019s din grew more distant.\\n\\nAt two he rose and wandered towards Blackfriars. How ghostly all things appeared! How much like some fantastic dream! The houses across the river seemed to have been made of darkness. It was as though silver and shadow had remade the world. The vast dome of St. Paul\u2019s hung like a bubble in the dim air.\\n\\nNear Cleopatra\u2019s Needle he noticed a man bent over the parapet, and, as he drew closer, the man raised his head, with the gaslight shining directly on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist! None could have failed to recognise that fat, loose face, the gold-rimmed glasses, the wan and feeble smile, the sensual lips.\\n\\nLord Arthur paused. A dazzling thought came to him, and he crept quietly behind the man. In an instant he caught Mr. Podgers by the legs and hurled him into the Thames. There came a rough oath, a weighty splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur peered over anxiously, but saw nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat spinning in an eddy of moonlit water. Presently that too went down, and every sign of Mr. Podgers had vanished. Once he fancied he saw the large, distorted figure swimming towards the steps beside the bridge, and a dreadful sense of defeat seized him; but it proved only a reflection, which disappeared when the moon emerged from a cloud. At last he felt that he had fulfilled destiny\u2019s decree. He drew a long sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u2018Have you lost anything, sir?\u2019 a voice suddenly asked behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and saw a policeman carrying a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u2018Nothing that matters, sergeant,\u2019 he replied with a smile; then, calling to a passing hansom, he sprang inside and directed the driver to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nDuring the following days he passed continually from hope to fear. At times he nearly expected Mr. Podgers to enter the room, while at others he believed Fate could not be so cruelly unjust to him. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s house in West Moon Street, but could not bring himself to ring. He desired certainty, yet dreaded it.\\n\\nAt last it arrived. He was seated in the club smoking-room, drinking tea and listening somewhat wearily to Surbiton\u2019s description of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered carrying the evening newspapers.\"}"
