          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"190b7527a91170d0f99439fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made no second attempt. The boys lay there counting the slow-moving minutes, until it seemed time itself must be ending and eternity turning gray; then they were relieved to see that the sun was finally setting.\\n\\nAt last one snore stopped. Injun Joe sat up, looked around, and smiled grimly at his companion, whose head hung on his knees. He nudged him awake with his foot and said:\\n\\n\u201cHere! You\u2019re supposed to be keeping watch, aren\u2019t you? Well, all right\u2014nothing happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy! Was I asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, partly, partly. It\u2019s nearly time for us to move, pard. What are we going to do with the little bit of swag we have left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here, as we always have, I suppose. No point carrying it off until we head south. Six hundred and fifty dollars in silver is something to haul around.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014all right\u2014it won\u2019t hurt to come here one more time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I\u2019d say come at night, like we used to. It\u2019s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, but listen; it may be quite a while before I get the right chance at that job. Something might happen; this isn\u2019t such a very good place. We\u2019ll bury it properly\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood idea,\u201d said his companion. He crossed the room, knelt down, lifted one of the back hearthstones, and took out a bag that jingled pleasantly. He took twenty or thirty dollars from it for himself, and the same amount for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to Joe, who was now kneeling in the corner and digging with his bowie knife.\\n\\nThe boys instantly forgot all their fear and misery. They watched every movement with greedy eyes. Luck! Its glory was beyond imagining! Six hundred dollars was enough to make half a dozen boys rich. Here was treasure hunting under the best possible conditions\u2014there would be no troublesome uncertainty about where to dig. They nudged each other every moment, eloquent nudges that were easy to understand, for they meant only: \u201cOh, aren\u2019t you glad now that we\u2019re here!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife struck something.\\n\\n\u201cHello!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d his companion asked.\\n\\n\u201cA half-rotted plank\u2014no, I think it\u2019s a box. Here\u2014give me a hand, and we\u2019ll see what it\u2019s doing here. Never mind, I\u2019ve broken a hole.\u201d\\n\\nHe reached his hand inside and pulled it out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men examined the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys above were as excited and delighted as the men were.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s companion said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll make short work of this. There\u2019s an old rusty pick among the weeds in the corner on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I saw it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
