{"request_id":"ddf82781fa7b812abf794176","rewritten_text":"My father had been, and his remembrance was the life within my life. I could feel gratitude towards another, but never again could I love or hope as I once had; all was suffering; even my pleasures were borne, not enjoyed. I was like a lonely place amid mountains, enclosed on every side by sheer black precipices, where no beam of warmth could enter, and whence there was no passage to more sunny fields. Thus, though the spirit of friendship comforted me awhile, it could not renew me. It arrived like some mild visitation; it departed, and I scarcely perceived its loss. The spirit of life was dead in me; do not wonder, then, that when there came, I did not welcome more joyfully, nor, when it left, mourn more deeply, the finest gift of heaven—a friend.

My friend's name was Woodville. I shall briefly tell his history, that you may understand how cold my heart must have been not to be kindled by his persuasive speech and gentle sympathy; and how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we were suited to offer each other mutual comfort, had I not been turned to stone by Misery's Medusa head. Woodville's sorrows were not rooted in the heart's core as mine were; his grief was natural, meant not to ruin but to refine the heart, and when its shadow had passed away, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the child of a poor clergyman and had been given a classical education. He was among those rare beings whom fortune smiles upon from birth; upon whom she lavishes every gift of mind and form with boundless generosity, and whom, beneath her special guardianship, no flaw however small, nor disappointment however brief, is permitted to touch. She appeared to have shaped his mind from an excellence no dross could stain, and endowed him with an understanding no error could corrupt. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a brilliant eastern star, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a Poet. That title has been so frequently debased that it cannot express all he was. He resembled a poet of ancient days, crowned by the muses in his cradle, whose lips had been fed by bees. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a celestial halo which set him apart from them and raised him above them. His extraordinary beauty, the radiant fire of his eyes, and words whose rich tones held the hearer in silent, ecstatic wonder, made him surpass all others, so that beside him they seemed created only to serve his higher excellence."}

