          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d168670321cb2489e03f347f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI regard it as among the earliest refinements of civilized society.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, sir; and it likewise has the merit of being fashionable among the world\u2019s less civilized societies: every savage knows how to dance.\u201d\\n\\nSir William merely smiled. \u201cYour friend dances charmingly,\u201d he went on, after a pause, as Bingley joined the party; \u201cand I have no doubt that you yourself are skilled in the art, Mr. Darcy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou saw me dance at Meryton, I think, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, certainly, and I derived no small pleasure from the spectacle. Do you dance often at St. James\u2019s?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you not consider it a suitable compliment to the place?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is a compliment I pay nowhere, when I can help it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have a house in town, I presume?\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy bowed.\\n\\n\u201cI once entertained the idea of settling in town myself, being fond of distinguished society; but I was not entirely persuaded that London air would suit Lady Lucas.\u201d\\n\\nHe stopped, expecting a reply; but his companion showed no inclination to offer one. Elizabeth then happened to approach them, and he was seized with the idea of performing a very gallant action, so he called to her,--\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must permit me to introduce this young lady to you as a most desirable partner. Surely you cannot decline a dance when such beauty stands before you.\u201d And, taking her hand, he would have placed it in Mr. Darcy\u2019s, who, though greatly astonished, was quite willing to accept it, when she immediately withdrew and said, somewhat flustered, to Sir William,--\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, sir, I have no intention whatever of dancing. I beg you not to imagine that I came this way to solicit a partner.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy, with solemn courtesy, asked leave to have the honour of her hand, but to no purpose. Elizabeth was resolute, nor did Sir William\u2019s efforts at persuasion alter her determination in the least.\\n\\n\u201cYou are so accomplished a dancer, Miss Eliza, that it is cruel to deprive me of the pleasure of seeing you; and though this gentleman generally dislikes the amusement, he can surely have no objection to oblige us for half an hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Darcy is nothing but politeness,\u201d Elizabeth said, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cHe truly is; but, given the inducement, my dear Miss Eliza, we need not be surprised at his readiness to oblige; for who could object to such a partner?\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth gave him an arch look and turned away. Her refusal had not diminished her in the gentleman\u2019s esteem, and he was considering her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley addressed him thus,--\"}"
