          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"295cba582a9b1450824355ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Bereft of his usual resources, the analyst casts himself into the mind of his adversary, becomes identified with it, and often perceives at once the only means\u2014sometimes, indeed, absurdly simple\u2014by which he can mislead him or drive him into error of calculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been remarked for its effect upon what is called the calculating faculty; and men of the loftiest intellect have been known to find in it a seemingly inexplicable pleasure, while dismissing chess as frivolous. Certainly nothing of the same kind so severely exercises the power of analysis. The finest chess-player in Christendom may be scarcely more than the finest player of chess; but skill at whist denotes an ability to succeed in all those graver contests in which mind is matched against mind. By skill, I mean that perfection in the game which embraces an understanding of all the sources from which lawful advantage can be drawn. These sources are not merely numerous, but various in kind, and often lie in recesses of thought wholly beyond the reach of ordinary comprehension. To observe closely is to remember clearly; and thus far the concentrated chess-player will acquit himself well at whist, while the rules of Hoyle\u2014being founded upon the mere mechanism of the game\u2014are sufficiently easy to grasp in general. Hence a retentive memory and adherence to \u201cthe book\u201d are commonly considered the whole of good play. Yet it is in concerns lying outside the bounds of bare rule that the analyst's ability reveals itself. Silently, he forms a multitude of observations and deductions. His companions may do likewise; but the difference in the amount of information gained lies less in the soundness of the inference than in the character of the observation. The essential knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player limits himself in no way; nor, because the game is his object, does he refuse conclusions drawn from matters outside it. He studies his partner's face, carefully comparing it with those of each opponent. He watches the manner in which the cards are arranged in every hand, often reckoning trump after trump and honor after honor from the glances their possessors cast upon them. He marks every change of countenance as play advances, collecting material for reflection from expressions of confidence, surprise, triumph, or vexation. From the way a trick is gathered up, he decides whether its winner can take another in that suit. He detects what is played as a feint by the fashion in which it is laid upon the table. A chance or careless word; the accidental fall or exposure of a card, together with the anxiety or indifference shown in concealing it; the counting of tricks and the order in which they are placed; confusion, hesitation, eagerness, or alarm\u2014all furnish his seemingly instinctive perception with signs of the true condition of affairs.\"}"
