{"request_id":"479f6ee6c5fda5e62463001e","rewritten_text":"I am a native of a sea-girt corner, a land shadowed by clouds, which, when the globe’s surface, with its boundless ocean and pathless continents, rises before my mind, seems but an insignificant point in the vast entirety; yet, weighed in the balance of intellectual power, it far surpassed realms of wider compass and more crowded people. Thus truly was the mind of man alone the maker of all that was noble or beneficial to man, while Nature herself served merely as his first minister. England, lying far north amid the troubled sea, now comes to me in dreams under the image of a great, well-equipped ship, mastering the winds and proudly breasting the waves. In my childhood she was my universe. When I stood upon my native hills and beheld plain and mountain extending to the furthest verge of sight, dotted with the homes of my countrymen and rendered fruitful by their toil, the very centre of the earth was, for me, established there; all the remainder of her sphere was a fable, whose loss from memory would have demanded no effort from either imagination or understanding.

My history has, from its outset, illustrated the influence which changefulness may exercise over the shifting course of human life. In my own case, it seemed almost hereditary. My father belonged to that class of men upon whom nature had lavishly conferred the coveted endowments of wit and fancy, then abandoned the vessel of their lives to the force of those winds, without supplying reason for its helm or judgment for its pilot. His birth was humble; yet circumstances soon drew him into public observation, and his slight inherited estate was quickly squandered amid the brilliant world of fashion and luxury in which he played a part. Through the brief period of his heedless youth, he was idolized by the fashionable idlers of the time, and especially by the young sovereign, who fled the party intrigues and laborious concerns of royalty to seek unfailing diversion and liveliness of spirit in his company. My father’s impulses, never subject to his own control, continually carried him into embarrassments from which only his ingenuity could release him; and the growing burden of debts, both of honour and commerce, which would have crushed another man, he bore with airy temper and untamed gaiety. Meanwhile, his presence was so indispensable at the dinners and gatherings of the wealthy that his failings were judged pardonable, and he himself was welcomed with heady flattery.

Such popularity, like all others, is fleeting; and the troubles of every description against which he struggled grew in a dreadful proportion to his scanty means of escaping them."}

