          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9719c61fd3654a9903e19043\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were overjoyed to see their beloved friend once more, declared it had been an age since they last met, and continually inquired how she had occupied herself during their absence from one another. Of the rest of the family they took scarcely any notice; keeping clear of Mrs. Bennet whenever they could, speaking little to Elizabeth, and not at all to the others. They shortly departed, springing from their chairs with an alacrity that astonished their brother, and making haste away as though anxious to flee Mrs. Bennet\u2019s attentions.\\n\\nThe expectation of the Netherfield ball was highly pleasing to every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet resolved to regard it as a compliment intended for her eldest daughter, and felt especially gratified that the invitation had come from Mr. Bingley himself rather than by means of a formal card. Jane imagined a delightful evening among her two friends and under the notice of their brother; while Elizabeth looked forward with pleasure to dancing often with Mr. Wickham, and to finding every suspicion confirmed by Mr. Darcy\u2019s appearance and conduct. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s expected enjoyment rested less upon one occurrence or one individual; for although each of them, like Elizabeth, intended to dance half the night with Mr. Wickham, he was far from being the sole partner capable of pleasing them, and a ball, in any case, was a ball. Even Mary was able to assure the family that she felt no aversion to attending it.\\n\\n\u201cSo long as I may keep my mornings to myself,\u201d she said, \u201cI am satisfied. I do not consider it any sacrifice to take part now and then in evening engagements. Society makes demands upon us all; and I count myself among those who think periods of recreation and amusement desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth was in such high spirits over the occasion that, though she seldom addressed Mr. Collins without necessity, she could not refrain from asking whether he meant to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation and, if so, whether he would consider it proper to share in the evening\u2019s entertainments. She was somewhat surprised to discover that he had not the slightest scruple on the matter, and was nowhere near so fearful of a reprimand from either the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh as to avoid dancing.\\n\\n\u201cI am not at all persuaded, I assure you,\u201d he said, \u201cthat a ball of this nature, offered by a young gentleman of character to respectable company, can possess any harmful tendency; and so far am I from objecting to dancing myself that I shall hope to be honoured, during the evening, by the hands of all my fair cousins. I take this occasion particularly to request yours, Miss Elizabeth, for the first two dances; a preference which I trust my cousin Jane will ascribe to its proper cause, and not to any want of respect toward her.\u201d\"}"
