 conical in form, and bearing more the semblance of artificial than natural elevations. The loftiest of these does not surpass three and three-quarter miles in perpendicular height; but a chart of the volcanic regions of the Campi Phlegraei would convey to your Excellencies a better notion of their general surface than any inadequate description I might venture to offer. Most of them were plainly in eruption, and revealed to me, in dreadful fashion, their rage and power through the repeated thunders of the wrongly named meteoric stones, which now sped upward past the balloon with a frequency ever more appalling.\"}"
