          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d18221ab3fc13f9d85c0ade9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At noon on Saturday I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke powders, clawing at the air, and muttering nonsense as fiercely as ever, but he looked fairly discouraged, since naturally he had not raised even a drop of sweat from that well yet. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow is it looking by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cObserve, I am at this very moment engaged in testing the most powerful enchantment known to the masters of occult arts in the Eastern lands; if it fails me, nothing can help. Be silent until I am finished.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that darkened the whole area, and it must have made things unpleasant for the hermits, since the wind was blowing toward them and carried it down over their caves in a thick, rolling fog. He poured out a matching flood of words, twisted his body, and slashed the air with his hands in a most remarkable fashion. At the end of twenty minutes he collapsed, panting and nearly spent. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns, followed by a crowd of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the tremendous smoke and all in a state of great excitement. The abbot anxiously asked about the results. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any exertion of mortal power could break the spell binding these waters, what I have just attempted would have done so. It has failed; therefore I now know that what I feared is established truth: the sign of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name no one may speak and live, has cast his spell upon this well. No mortal lives, or ever will live, who can discover the secret of that spell, and without that secret no one can break it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done all a man can do. Allow me to leave.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally, this threw the abbot into considerable alarm. He turned to me, his face showing it plainly, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYou heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all of it, then\u2014not all! Which part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has put his spell on the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen you also mean that when he says no one can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he is saying something that is not necessarily true. Under certain conditions, an attempt to break it may have some chance\u2014some slight, trivial chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
