{"request_id":"9fc748178549a0c7bf1058e7","rewritten_text":"It is but four o’clock; yet it is winter, and the sun has already gone down: no clouds drift across the clear, frosty heaven to catch its oblique rays, but the very air is faintly dyed with a rosy hue, which is reflected again upon the snow spread over the earth. I dwell in a single cottage upon a broad and lonely heath: no sound of living thing comes to me. Before me lies the desolate expanse, white-covered, except for a few dark spots which the midday sun has formed upon the summits of those steep, pointed mounds, where the snow, slipping as it descended, has rested less thickly than upon the level ground: a few birds pick at the hard ice sealing the pools—for the frost has endured long.

I am in an unusual mood. I am alone—utterly alone—in the world—the curse of calamity has swept over and blighted me; I know I am soon to die, and I am glad—exultant.—I feel my pulse; it throbs rapidly: I lay my wasted hand upon my cheek; it is hot: a faint, swift spirit stirs within me, now casting out its final sparks. Never shall I behold the snows of another winter—I truly believe I shall never more feel the life-giving heat of another summer sun; and in this conviction I commence the writing of my mournful history. Perhaps such a history as mine ought to perish with me, yet some undefinable feeling urges me onward, and I am too feeble in body and spirit to withstand the least impulse. While life was vigorous in me, I believed indeed that my tale possessed a sacred dread which made it unfit to be spoken; and now, near death, I profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the grove of the Eumenides, into which none but the dying may pass; and Oedipus is near to die.

What am I setting down?—I must gather my thoughts. I cannot tell whether any person will read these pages save you, my friend, who will receive them when I am dead. I do not write to you alone, because it would please me to linger over our friendship in a manner needless if you were the sole reader of what I am to write. I shall therefore tell my story as though I addressed strangers. You have often questioned me concerning the reason for my secluded existence; my tears; and, above all, my impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I did not dare; in death I disclose the secret. Others will turn these pages carelessly over: but to you, Woodville, gentle and loving friend, they will be dear—the valued remembrances of a broken-hearted girl who, in dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you: your tears will fall upon the words that recount my sorrows; I know they will—and while I yet live, I thank you for your compassion."}

