          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5420f85cd57fc86eb77d5d57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I remembered what things were like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day in gold value; in the South, he earned fifty\u2014paid in Confederate shinplasters, which were worth about a dollar a bushel. In the North, a suit of overalls cost three dollars\u2014a day's pay; in the South, it cost seventy-five\u2014which was two days' pay. Other prices followed the same pattern. So wages were really twice as high in the North as in the South, because Northern pay had twice the buying power.\\n\\nYes, I made several acquaintances in the hamlet, and one thing pleased me greatly: our new coins were in circulation\u2014plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, plenty of cents, a fair number of nickels, and some silver. The artisans and common people used them generally; there was even some gold, though that was at the bank\u2014that is, the goldsmith's. I stopped in while Marco, son of Marco, was bargaining with a shopkeeper over a quarter-pound of salt, and asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They gave it to me\u2014but only after they had bitten the coin, rung it on the counter, tested it with acid, and asked where I got it, who I was, where I came from, where I was going, when I expected to arrive, and perhaps two hundred more questions. When they ran out of questions, I kept going and volunteered a great deal of information. I told them I owned a dog named Watch; that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist; that her grandfather was a Prohibitionist; and that I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip, who died hoping for a glorious resurrection; and so on, and so on, and so on. At last even that question-hungry village man looked satisfied, though a little annoyed. Still, he had to respect a man with my financial power, so he did not talk back; but I noticed that he took it out on his underlings, which was perfectly natural. Yes, they changed my twenty, but I could see it put some strain on the bank. That was only to be expected. It was like walking into a small nineteenth-century village store and suddenly asking the owner to change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might manage it, but he would wonder how a small farmer came to carry so much money in his pocket. That was probably what this goldsmith thought too, for he followed me to the door and stood there watching me go with respectful admiration.\"}"
