          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"77a3a71fdb00888d28488279\",\"rewritten_text\":\"That was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must know that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been rented by a young man of considerable fortune from the north of England. He came down on Monday in a carriage and four to look at the place, and was so delighted with it that he made an agreement with Mr. Morris at once. He is to move in before Michaelmas, and some of his servants will be in the house by the end of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, my dear, of course! A single man with a large fortune\u2014four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful thing for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? How can it concern them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife replied, \u201chow can you be so tiresome? You must know I am thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that why he has chosen to settle here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy? Nonsense, how can you speak so! But it is very likely that he may fall in love with one of them, and so you must call on him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see no reason for it. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be better still; since you are as pretty as any of them, Mr. Bingley may prefer you to the rest of the party.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you flatter me. I certainly have had my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be anything remarkable now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she ought to stop thinking about her own looks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn such circumstances, a woman does not often have much beauty left to think about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you truly must go and see Mr. Bingley when he comes into the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is more than I promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Just imagine what an establishment it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are determined to go for that reason alone, since they generally do not visit newcomers. You really must go, because it will be impossible for us to visit him if you do not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are being overly particular, surely. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be very pleased to see you, and I will send a few lines with you to assure him of my wholehearted consent to his marrying whichever of the girls he chooses\u2014though I must put in a good word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI insist that you do no such thing. Lizzy is not at all better than the others, and I am sure she is not half as pretty as Jane, nor half as good-tempered as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
