          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7532a2d565515dc3304f475f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity that always brings misery must have been broken, and the cruel fate that rules it would not allow such a breach in her eternal laws. But why should I complain? Misery was my natural element, and nothing but misery could come near me; if Woodville had been happy, I should never have known him. And can I, who for so many years was fed by tears and raised beneath the dew of grief, hesitate to tell a story of sorrow and death?\\n\\nWoodville was forced to travel into the country and was kept there, day after day, painfully separated from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her saying that she was slightly ill, but begging him to hurry to her, for she would find health in his eyes and his presence would be her surest medicine. He was delayed three more days, then hurried to her. His heart, though he did not know why, foretold disaster; he had heard nothing further from her. He feared she had grown worse, and this fear made him impatient and restless to see her again, standing before him in health and beauty. For a dark voice seemed always to whisper, \u201cYou will never see her again as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her home, all was silent. He passed through several rooms; in one he saw a servant weeping bitterly. Weak with fear, he could barely ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d He listened only for the dreadful reply: \u201cNot yet.\u201d These shocking words seemed less terrible than what he had expected; to learn that she still lived, and that he might still hope, brought him some relief. He remembered her letter and gave himself up to the wild thought that his kisses, warm with love and life, would give her new strength; that she could not die while he was near her; that his presence was the charm that preserved her life.\\n\\nHe hurried to her sickroom. She lay with her cheeks burning from fever, her eyes closed, apparently unconscious. He gathered her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses on her burning lips; he called to her, in a voice restrained by anguish, using the tenderest names: \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am with you\u2014your life, your love. Return, dearest one; you promised me this gift, that I should bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? Never to see you again? To be parted from one who is part of myself, without whom I have no memory and no future?\u201d\"}"
