          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"affb4b6eb64ba69b6587b89f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI now pass quickly to the more affecting portion of my tale. I will recount occurrences that filled me with emotions which, from what I once was, have fashioned me into what I am.\\n\\n\u201cSpring came on swiftly; the weather grew pleasant and the heavens clear. I marvelled that what had formerly been barren and dismal should now blossom with the loveliest flowers and greenery. My senses were pleased and renewed by a thousand delightful perfumes and a thousand beautiful prospects.\\n\\n\u201cIt was upon one of these days, when my cottagers took their customary rest from work\u2014the old man played his guitar, while the children attended to him\u2014that I noticed Felix\u2019s countenance was melancholy beyond all expression; he often sighed, and once his father stopped his music, and from his manner I supposed he asked the reason for his son\u2019s grief. Felix answered in a cheerful tone, and the old man was beginning his music again when someone knocked at the door.\\n\\n\u201cIt was a lady mounted on horseback, attended by a countryman acting as guide. She wore a dark dress and was concealed beneath a heavy black veil. Agatha put a question, to which the stranger replied only by speaking Felix\u2019s name in a sweet accent. Her voice was melodious, yet unlike that of either of my friends. At this word Felix hurried to the lady, who, on seeing him, lifted her veil, and I saw a face of angelic loveliness and expression. Her hair was glossy raven-black and intricately braided; her eyes dark yet mild, though lively; her features regularly formed, and her complexion wonderfully fair, with each cheek touched by a charming rose.\\n\\n\u201cFelix appeared transported with joy at the sight of her; every mark of sorrow disappeared from his face, which at once showed an ecstasy I could scarcely have thought it able to express. His eyes gleamed and his cheeks glowed with pleasure; at that instant I considered him as beautiful as the stranger. She seemed moved by other feelings; brushing several tears from her lovely eyes, she extended her hand to Felix, who kissed it with rapture and called her, so far as I could make out, his sweet Arabian. She seemed not to comprehend him, but smiled. He helped her from her horse and, sending away her guide, led her into the cottage. Some discourse passed between him and his father, and the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet and would have kissed his hand, but he lifted her up and embraced her tenderly.\\n\\n\u201cI soon discovered that, though the stranger made articulate sounds and seemed to possess a language of her own, neither she nor the cottagers could understand one another.\u201d\"}"
