{"request_id":"950fd59c1cea54c3526163e2","rewritten_text":"After this, several more loops were loosened from the rim, another section of cloth was pushed in, and the freed loops were fastened again to their proper buttons. By repeating this process, I was able to place the entire upper part of the bag between the netting and the hoop. The hoop would then hang down inside the car, while the full weight of the car and everything in it would be supported only by the buttons. At first this might seem unsafe, but it was not; the buttons were very strong, and set so close together that each one bore only a small share of the weight. Even if the car and its contents had been three times as heavy, I should not have felt uneasy. I then raised the hoop again inside the gum-elastic covering and held it nearly at its former height with three light poles made for that purpose. This was done to keep the top of the bag stretched out and the lower part of the netting in its proper place. Nothing remained but to close the mouth of the enclosure. This was easily done by gathering the folds of material together and twisting them very tightly on the inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, as arranged around the car, were three round panes of thick, clear glass. Through them I could see easily in every horizontal direction. In the cloth forming the bottom there was also a fourth window of the same kind, matching a small opening in the floor of the car. This allowed me to look straight down. But I had found it impossible to make a similar opening overhead, because of the unusual way the opening there had to be closed, and the wrinkles this made in the cloth. I therefore could not expect to see anything directly above me. This was of little importance, however, since even if I had been able to put a window at the top, the balloon itself would have prevented me from using it.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a round opening, eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded for a screw. Into this rim the large tube of the condenser was screwed, while the body of the machine remained, of course, inside the gum-elastic chamber."}

