          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"164777eff7a10412b71012bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was greatly upset by the second delay of the marriage, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her wedding dress, did everything she could to persuade Sybil to end the engagement. But although Sybil loved her mother dearly, she had placed her whole life in Lord Arthur\u2019s hands, and nothing Lady Julia said could shake her faith in him. Lord Arthur himself took several days to recover from his terrible disappointment, and for a while his nerves were completely shattered. Yet his excellent common sense soon returned, and his practical, sensible mind did not leave him uncertain for long about what he should do. Since poison had been a complete failure, dynamite, or some other kind of explosive, was clearly the proper thing to try.\\n\\nHe went over his list of friends and relatives again and, after careful thought, decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of great learning and culture, was extremely fond of clocks. He owned a remarkable collection of timepieces, from the fifteenth century to the present day, and Lord Arthur thought this interest of the good Dean\u2019s gave him an excellent chance to carry out his plan. Finding an explosive device, of course, was quite another problem. The London Directory told him nothing, and he felt there was little point in asking Scotland Yard, since they never seemed to know anything about the dynamite group until after an explosion had happened\u2014and not much even then.\\n\\nThen he suddenly remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with strongly revolutionary views, whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s during the winter. Count Rouvaloff was said to be writing a life of Peter the Great, and to have come to England to study documents connected with that Tsar\u2019s time in the country as a ship carpenter. But people generally suspected that he was a Nihilist agent, and there was no doubt t