          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f991065df73dca4ab174788\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the middle of this opening I saw a white spot, but at first I could not tell what it was. I adjusted the telescope\u2019s focus and looked again. Then I saw that it was a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cOnce I made this discovery, I was confident that I had solved the mystery. The words \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could only describe the skull\u2019s place in the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 could have only one meaning in a search for buried treasure. I understood that the plan was to drop a bullet through the skull\u2019s left eye. Then a bee-line\u2014that is, a straight line\u2014drawn from the nearest point of the trunk through \u2018the shot,\u2019 or the place where the bullet landed, and extended fifty feet farther, would mark a certain spot. Beneath that spot, I thought, it was at least possible that something valuable had been buried.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this,\u201d I said, \u201cis very clear, and though clever, still simple and direct. After you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAfter carefully noting the tree\u2019s position, I started home. But the moment I left \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 the round opening disappeared, and no matter how I turned, I could not see it again. What seems to me the greatest cleverness in the whole matter is that, as repeated experiments have convinced me, this circular opening can be seen from no other reachable viewpoint except the narrow ledge on the face of the rock.\\n\\n\u201cJupiter had gone with me on this trip to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel.\u2019 He had no doubt noticed, during the past few weeks, how distracted I had been, and he took special care not to leave me alone. But the next day I rose very early, managed to slip away from him, and went into the hills to look for the tree. After much hard work, I found it. When I returned home that night, my valet offered to give me a whipping. I believe you know the rest of the adventure as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose,\u201d I said, \u201cthat on your first attempt at digging, you missed the spot because of Jupiter\u2019s stupidity in letting the bug fall through the skull\u2019s right eye instead of its left.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. That mistake changed \u2018the shot\u2019\u2014the position of the peg nearest the tree\u2014by about two and a half inches. If the treasure had been directly beneath \u2018the shot,\u2019 the error would have mattered little. But \u2018the shot\u2019 and the nearest point of the tree were only two points used to establish a line of direction. Naturally, though the error was small at the beginning, it grew as we followed the line, and by the time we had gone fifty feet, it had led us completely away from the right place.\u201d\"}"
