{"request_id":"ed666bce98d1b6dfcd3967c3","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove seized the first chance of being alone with Anne, and said, “Oh! Miss Anne, I cannot help wishing Mrs Charles possessed a little of your way with those children. They are quite altered creatures when with you! But certainly, in general, they are so spoiled! It is a pity you cannot teach your sister how to manage them. They are as handsome, healthy children as ever were seen, poor little things! without any partiality; but Mrs Charles knows no more how they ought to be dealt with—! Bless me! how troublesome they can be sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wishing to have them at our house as often as I otherwise might. I believe Mrs Charles is not altogether pleased that I do not ask them more frequently; but you know it is very unpleasant to have children with one whom one must be correcting every instant; ‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that;’ or whom one can only keep in decent order by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

She received, besides, this account from Mary. “Mrs Musgrove thinks every one of her servants so perfectly steady, that it would be downright treason to question it; but I am certain, without the least exaggeration, that her upper housemaid and laundry-maid, instead of attending to their work, are wandering about the village all day. I meet them wherever I go; and I declare, I never enter my nursery twice without seeing one of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to ruin her; for she tells me they are continually persuading her to go walking with them.” And from Mrs Musgrove came, “I make it a rule never to meddle with any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because I know it would never answer; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, because perhaps you may be able to put matters right, that I have no very favourable opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear odd tales of her; she is always gadding about; and, from what I know myself, I can say she is such a dressy lady that she is enough to spoil every servant she comes near. Mrs Charles quite swears by her, I know; but I merely give you this warning, that you may keep watch; for if you observe anything wrong, you need not hesitate to mention it.”

Again, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was very liable not to grant her the precedence properly due to her, when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she could see no reason why she should be thought so much at home there as to forfeit her place."}

