          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dfdfbfd4a7fe3505579153e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Why, we might build up a very exciting piece of superstition from this suggestion. I suppose you will name the insect _scarab\u00e6us caput hominis_, or something similar\u2014there are plenty of such names in the Natural Histories. But where are the _antenn\u00e6_ of which you spoke?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe _antenn\u00e6!_\u201d cried Legrand, seeming to grow strangely heated over the matter; \u201csurely you can see the _antenn\u00e6_. I drew them as plainly as they appear upon the actual insect, and I should think that enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said I, \u201cperhaps you did\u2014yet I cannot perceive them;\u201d and, unwilling to disturb his temper, I returned the paper without further observation. Still, I was greatly astonished by the course things had taken; his irritability perplexed me\u2014and, as to the beetle sketch, there were absolutely _no antenn\u00e6_ to be seen, while the entire figure bore a remarkably close likeness to the common representations of a death\u2019s-head.\\n\\nHe took the paper with much peevishness, and seemed on the point of crushing it and tossing it into the fire, when an accidental look at the drawing appeared suddenly to fix his gaze. In a moment his countenance became deeply red\u2014in the next, equally pale. For several minutes he examined the design closely from where he sat. At last he rose, lifted a candle from the table, and went to sit upon a sea-chest in the remotest corner of the room. There he again inspected the paper with anxiety, rotating it in every direction. He said nothing, however, and his behavior astonished me greatly; yet I considered it wise not to aggravate the increasing sullenness of his mood by remarking upon it. Soon he drew a wallet from his coat pocket, carefully put the paper inside, and placed both in a writing-desk, which he locked. His manner now became calmer; but every trace of his former enthusiasm had vanished. He appeared less sullen than absorbed. As the evening passed, he sank ever deeper into reverie, from which none of my attempts at conversation could rouse him. I had meant to spend the night in the hut, as I had often done previously, but, seeing my host in such a state, I thought it best to bid him farewell. He did not urge me to stay, though, when I left, he shook my hand with more than his customary warmth.\\n\\nAbout a month afterward (during which time I had seen nothing of Legrand), I was visited in Charleston by his servant, Jupiter. Never before had I seen the worthy old negro so dejected, and I feared some grave misfortune had overtaken my friend.\"}"
