{"request_id":"18d07dffdeb17f59b3e751ae","rewritten_text":"The line of flickering gas lamps curved far into the distance, and outside a small walled house stood a lone hansom cab, its driver asleep inside. He walked quickly towards Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as if afraid someone was following him. At the corner of Rich Street, two men were reading a small notice posted on a hoarding. Curious, he crossed the road. As he drew near, the word ‘Murder,’ printed in black letters, caught his eye. He started, and a deep flush rose in his cheeks. The notice offered a reward for information leading to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, wearing a billy-cock hat, a black coat, and checked trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the miserable man would be caught and how he had received the scar. Perhaps one day his own name would be posted on the walls of London. Perhaps a price would be put on his head as well.

The thought made him feel sick with horror. He turned sharply and hurried into the night.

He scarcely knew where he went. He vaguely remembered wandering through a maze of shabby houses, lost in a vast web of dark streets, and it was bright dawn when he finally found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he walked home towards Belgrave Square, he met the great wagons heading for Covent Garden. The carters, in white smocks, with cheerful sunburned faces and rough curly hair, marched steadily along, cracking their whips and occasionally calling to one another. On the back of a huge grey horse, leading a clattering team, sat a plump boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat. He held tightly to the mane with his small hands and laughed. The great heaps of vegetables looked like jade against the morning sky, like green jade against the pink petals of some wonderful rose. Lord Arthur was strangely moved, though he could not say why. There was something deeply sad in the delicate beauty of dawn, and he thought of all the days that begin in beauty and end in storm. These country people, too, with their rough, good-natured voices and easy ways—what a strange London they saw: a London free from the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale, ghostly city, a deserted town of tombs. He wondered what they thought of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendour and shame, its fierce, flame-coloured pleasures and terrible hunger, of all it creates and destroys from morning until evening."}

