o owls. But such creatures stubbornly almost never say what people write down for them.\\n\\nFor three long hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly into the night. Deep darkness covered the whole landscape, adding its own silence to the quiet dust on every road. The burial ground had reached the point where its small patches of poor grass could not be told apart; the figure on the Cross might have come down, for all that could be seen of it. In the village, tax collectors and taxpayers slept deeply. Perhaps they dreamed of feasts, as starving people usually do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and yoked ox may. Its thin inhabitants slept soundly, and in their dreams were fed and free.\"}"
