{"request_id":"49ed0a7e88f81b39310969d8","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange occurrences, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was drawn by eight black horses, each bearing upon its head a large tuft of waving ostrich-plumes, while the leaden coffin was draped in a splendid purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville arms. Servants carrying lighted torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, and the entire procession was exceedingly impressive. Lord Canterville, who had come specially from Wales for the funeral, was chief mourner, and rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney occupied the last carriage. It was commonly thought that, having been terrified by the ghost for over fifty years, she was entitled to witness his end. A deep grave had been prepared in a corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew-tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. When it was finished, the servants, in accordance with an ancient Canterville custom, put out their torches; and as the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia came forward and placed upon it a great cross of white and pink almond-blossoms. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud and poured its quiet silver light over the little churchyard, while a nightingale began singing in a distant copse. Virginia remembered the ghost’s account of the Garden of Death; tears dimmed her eyes, and she scarcely uttered a word on the journey home.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville departed for town, Mr. Otis spoke with him concerning the jewels the ghost had bestowed upon Virginia. They were extraordinarily beautiful, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a truly magnificent example of sixteenth-century workmanship; and their worth was so considerable that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about permitting his daughter to keep them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country mortmain is considered to extend to ornaments no less than to land, and it seems perfectly plain to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms of your family. I must therefore ask you to carry them to London, and to treat them merely as part of your property, restored to you in certain unusual circumstances. My daughter, after all, is only a child, and has, I am happy to say, as yet little concern for such accessories of vain luxury.’"}

