          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e02bc8ccc57ea622a525208\",\"rewritten_text\":\"WHEN the marriage was celebrated, about three weeks later, St. Peter\u2019s was packed with an absolute throng of fashionable people. The Dean of Chichester performed the service with the greatest impressiveness, and everyone declared they had never beheld a more beautiful bride and bridegroom. Yet they were more than beautiful\u2014they were happy. Not once did Lord Arthur repent of anything he had endured for Sybil\u2019s sake, while she, for her part, bestowed on him the finest gifts a woman can offer a man\u2014adoration, gentleness, and love. With them, reality had not destroyed romance. They always remained young in spirit.\\n\\nSeveral years later, after two lovely children had been born to them, Lady Windermere paid a visit to Alton Priory, a charming old house which the Duke had given his son as a wedding gift; and one afternoon, while she sat beneath a lime-tree in the garden with Lady Arthur, watching the little boy and girl run and play along the rose-walk like flickering shafts of sunlight, she suddenly clasped her hostess\u2019s hand and said, \u2018Are you happy, Sybil?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Lady Windermere, naturally I am happy. Are you not?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I have no leisure for happiness, Sybil. I invariably like the latest person presented to me; but generally, once I know people, I grow weary of them.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Do not your lions content you, Lady Windermere?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, certainly not! lions last for only a season. Once their manes have been clipped, they become the most tedious creatures imaginable. And besides, if one is genuinely kind to them, they behave abominably. Do you remember that dreadful Mr. Podgers? He was a terrible fraud. Naturally, I did not mind that in the least, and I even forgave him when he wished to borrow money, but I could not endure his making love to me. He has positively made me detest cheiromancy. I practise telepathy now. It is far more entertaining.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You must not speak against cheiromancy here, Lady Windermere; it is the one subject about which Arthur dislikes being teased. I assure you, he takes it quite seriously.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Surely you do not mean that he believes in it, Sybil?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ask him, Lady Windermere\u2014there he is\u2019; and Lord Arthur came up through the garden, carrying a great bunch of yellow roses, with his two children dancing about him.\\n\\n\u2018Lord Arthur?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, Lady Windermere.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You cannot mean that you believe in cheiromancy?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Of course I do,\u2019 the young man replied, smiling.\\n\\n\u2018But why?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Because I owe all the happiness of my life to it,\u2019 he murmured, sinking into a wicker chair.\"}"
