{"request_id":"995e6dd2ab8bf5f638d106ae","rewritten_text":"He had to consider Sybil’s father and mother as well, for they were somewhat old-fashioned people and might conceivably oppose the marriage if there were anything resembling a scandal; though he was convinced that, if he laid the entire circumstances before them, they would be the first to understand the motives by which he had been guided. He had every reason, therefore, to choose poison. It was secure, certain, and silent, and spared him the need for distressing scenes, for which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep-rooted dislike.

Of the science of poisons, however, he knew nothing whatever; and since the waiter appeared unable to discover in the library anything beyond _Ruff’s Guide_ and _Bailey’s Magazine_, he searched the shelves himself, eventually finding a finely bound edition of the _Pharmacopoeia_, together with a copy of Erskine’s _Toxicology_, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members, having been elected there by mistake in place of someone else; a _contretemps_ that so infuriated the Committee that, when the actual man presented himself, they black-balled him without exception. Lord Arthur was considerably bewildered by the technical language of both volumes, and had begun to wish he had given greater attention to his classics at Oxford, when, in Erskine’s second volume, he came upon a highly interesting and thorough account of aconitine’s properties, expressed in reasonably plain English. It struck him as precisely the poison he required. Its action was rapid—indeed, nearly instantaneous—entirely painless, and, when administered in a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew advised, by no means unpleasant to take. He therefore noted on his shirt-cuff the quantity required for a fatal dose, restored the books to their places, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the celebrated chemists. Mr. Pestle, who invariably served the aristocracy in person, was rather astonished by the request, and murmured, with great deference, something about the necessity of a medical certificate. Yet when Lord Arthur told him it was intended for a large Norwegian mastiff of which he was compelled to dispose, since it displayed symptoms of incipient rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf, he declared himself wholly satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of Toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a charming little silver _bonbonnière_ he noticed in a Bond Street shop window, discarded Pestle and Humbey’s unsightly pill-box, and immediately drove to Lady Clementina’s."}

