          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"593c3810078f1a78bcce847f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"According to recent reports from Rotterdam, that city appears to be in a state of intense philosophical excitement. Indeed, events have occurred there so wholly unexpected\u2014so entirely new\u2014so completely opposed to all previous ideas\u2014that I have no doubt that, by now, all Europe is in turmoil, physics is in upheaval, and reason and astronomy are at one another\u2019s throats.\\n\\nIt seems that on the \u2014\u2014 day of \u2014\u2014 (I cannot be certain of the date), an immense crowd had gathered, for reasons not clearly stated, in the great Exchange square of the prosperous city of Rotterdam. The day was warm\u2014unusually warm for the season\u2014with scarcely a breath of wind; and the crowd was not displeased to be occasionally sprinkled by brief, friendly showers falling from great white cloud masses that drifted irregularly across the blue vault of the sky. Yet around noon, a slight but striking disturbance became visible among the people: ten thousand tongues began clattering, and an instant later ten thousand faces turned upward, ten thousand pipes dropped at once from the corners of ten thousand mouths, and a cry\u2014comparable to nothing but Niagara\u2019s roar\u2014rang long, loud, and furious through every part of Rotterdam.\\n\\nThe cause of this commotion soon became clear enough. From behind the enormous bulk of one of those sharply outlined cloud masses already described, there slowly emerged into a clear expanse of blue sky a strange, mixed-looking, apparently solid object. It was so oddly formed, so whimsically assembled, that the sturdy burghers standing below with open mouths could neither understand it in the least nor admire it enough. What could it be? In the name of all the vrows and devils in Rotterdam, what could it possibly mean? No one knew; no one could guess. No one\u2014not even the burgomaster, Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk\u2014possessed the slightest clue to solve the mystery. Since nothing more sensible could be done, every man carefully returned his pipe to the corner of his mouth and, raising his right eye toward the phenomenon, puffed, paused, waddled about, and grunted meaningfully\u2014then waddled back, grunted, paused, and at last puffed again.\\n\\nMeanwhile, the object of such curiosity, and the source of so much smoke, descended lower and lower toward the worthy city. Within a few minutes it came near enough to be clearly seen. It appeared to be\u2014yes! it was unquestionably a kind of balloon; but surely Rotterdam had never seen a balloon like it before.\"}"
