          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b3717e88316ee2ec484c7617\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou must learn a little of my philosophy. Think of the past only when remembering it gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot believe you have any philosophy of that sort. Your reflections on the past must be so entirely free from regret that the satisfaction they give you comes not from philosophy, but from something much better\u2014ignorance. But it is not so with me. Painful memories force themselves upon me, and cannot, and should not, be driven away. All my life I have been selfish in practice, though not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was right, but not taught to control my temper. I was given good principles, but left to follow them with pride and conceit. Unhappily, I was an only son\u2014and for many years an only child\u2014and my parents spoiled me. Though they were good themselves\u2014my father especially, everything kind and generous\u2014they allowed, encouraged, almost taught me to be selfish and domineering; to care for no one outside my own family; to think poorly of all the rest of the world; at least to wish to think poorly of their judgment and worth compared with my own. That was what I was from eight to twenty-eight, and I might still have been so if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! How much I owe you! You taught me a lesson, difficult at first indeed, but most beneficial. You humbled me properly. I came to you with no doubt of how I would be received. You showed me how inadequate all my claims were to please a woman worthy of being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you really convinced yourself that I would be?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What will you think of my vanity? I believed you wished for, and expected, my proposal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy behavior must have been at fault, though never deliberately, I assure you. I never intended to mislead you, but my high spirits may often have led me astray. How you must have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon began to turn in the proper direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. You blamed me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, truly. I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have been greater than mine when you noticed me. My conscience told me I deserved no unusual politeness, and I admit I did not expect to receive more than I deserved.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, through every courtesy I could offer, that I was not petty enough to resent the past. I hoped to gain your forgiveness and lessen your poor opinion of me by showing you that I had taken your reproaches to heart.\u201d\"}"
