          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b774b5188ad18d613209ae16\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said that, while travelling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke coming from the inn chimney. Thinking he might find fellow travellers gathered there, he knocked and was let in. No one was in the house except Lucy and her mother. Her mother had lost the use of her limbs after an attack of rheumatism, and so, one after another, all the remaining people in the district had set out, leaving the two women alone. Lucy begged the man to stay with them; in a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could leave. But if they were abandoned in their helpless state, they must die. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and that, in his view, he could not possibly remain. As a last hope, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, asking him to deliver it wherever he met us. At least he carried out this task, and Idris received the following letter with deep feeling:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am certain you will remember me and feel pity for me, and I dare to hope that you will help me; what other hope do I have? Forgive the way I write; I am so confused. A month ago, my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and in another month, I am sure, she would be able to travel, in the way you were kind enough to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As they left, each person said that perhaps my mother would recover before we were completely deserted. But three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his newborn child, had stayed until the end. Since they were such a large family, I thought I could persuade them to wait a little longer for us; but I found their house empty. I had not seen another soul until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have kept it from her.\\n\