          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ebd2979b00b78a4f279bb88d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction, and though I had fully expected it, it gave me the most unqualified pleasure. Having attained, in its previous course, about the twentieth parallel of south latitude, it suddenly diverged at an acute angle toward the east, and continued thus during the entire day, preserving nearly, if not quite, the precise plane of the lunar ellipse. It was notable that this alteration of course produced a distinctly perceptible swaying of the car\u2014a swaying which endured, in a greater or lesser measure, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again greatly alarmed by a recurrence of the loud crackling sound which had terrified me on the tenth. I reflected long upon it, yet could arrive at no satisfactory explanation. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter had greatly diminished, and now subtended from the balloon an angle of scarcely more than twenty-five degrees. The moon was wholly invisible, being almost at my zenith. I still kept within the plane of the ellipse, but advanced little eastward.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter diminished with extreme rapidity. To-day I was powerfully impressed by the belief that the balloon was actually ascending the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014that is, pursuing the direct route which would carry it immediately to the moon at that portion of its orbit nearest the earth. The moon itself stood directly overhead, and was therefore concealed from my sight. Great and protracted exertion was required to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. Even the forms of continents and seas could no longer be discerned upon the earth with anything like clearness. At about twelve o\u2019clock I heard, for the third time, that dreadful sound which had previously so amazed me. This time, however, it lasted several moments, increasing in force as it went on. At last, while I stood benumbed and overcome with terror, awaiting I knew not what horrible ruin, the car shook with extraordinary violence, and an immense flaming mass of some substance I could not identify swept past the balloon, roaring and booming with the voice of a thousand thunders. When my terror and astonishment had somewhat abated, I found little difficulty in judging it to be some vast volcanic fragment hurled forth from that world toward which I was speeding so rapidly, and, most likely, one of that peculiar class of materials sometimes found upon the earth and called meteoric stones, for lack of a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. To-day, gazing upward as best I could through each side window in turn, I saw, to my great joy, a very small part of the moon\u2019s disk appearing, as though on every side, beyond the enormous circumference of the balloon.\"}"
