          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc155d4ce418a08b5c104e4d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in such moods, I frequently reflected upon the ancient doctrine of the Bi-Part Soul, and entertained myself with the notion of a twofold Dupin\u2014the inventive and the analytic.\\n\\nLet no one infer, from what I have said, that I am recounting a mystery or composing a romance. What I have related of the Frenchman was simply the effect of an excited, or possibly disordered, intellect. Yet an instance of the nature of his observations at such times will best make the matter clear.\\n\\nOne night we were walking along a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. As we were both seemingly absorbed in thought, neither had uttered a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin spoke these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small fellow, certainly, and would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere is no question of it,\u201d I answered unconsciously, not at first noticing\u2014so deeply was I lost in reflection\u2014the remarkable way in which he had joined my own meditations. A moment later I recovered myself, and my surprise was extreme.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said solemnly, \u201cthis surpasses my understanding. I do not scruple to say that I am astonished, and can hardly trust my senses. How could you possibly know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d Here I stopped, to determine beyond all doubt whether he truly knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were saying to yourself that his slight stature made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly the subject of my thoughts. Chantilly had once been a cobbler in the Rue St. Denis and, becoming stage-struck, had attempted the _r\u00f4le_ of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name, for which effort he had been notoriously Pasquinaded.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, in Heaven\u2019s name,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe means\u2014if there be any means\u2014by which you have managed to penetrate my soul in this affair.\u201d Indeed, I was more alarmed than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of soles lacked the height for Xerxes _et id genus omne_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer! You amaze me\u2014I know no fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who collided with you as we entered this street\u2014perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI then recalled that a fruiterer, bearing a large basket of apples on his head, had almost knocked me down by accident as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the thoroughfare in which we stood; but I could not conceive what this had to do with Chantilly.\"}"
