          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"034a1d2f0cda45c10c1c56e0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We were alike in our affection for Adrian: though she, not yet wholly emerged from childhood, could not value, as I did, the full measure of his excellence, nor share my sympathy with his pursuits and opinions. I was continually beside him. There was such sensibility and gentleness in his nature, that our discourse assumed a tender, almost unearthly character. Yet he was gay as a lark singing from its airy tower, lofty in thought as an eagle, and innocent as the soft-eyed dove. He could chase away Perdita's gravity, and blunt the edge of my own tortured activity. I recalled my restless wishes and painful contests with my fellow-creatures as one remembers a disturbed dream, and believed myself as greatly altered as though I had passed into another shape, whose new sensorium and nervous frame changed the image of the visible world reflected in the mind's mirror. Yet this was not so; I remained the same in strength, in eager need of sympathy, in my longing for active endeavour. My manly qualities had not forsaken me; for the enchantress Urania spared Samson's locks while he rested at her feet: but everything was gentled and made more human. Nor did Adrian teach me merely the frigid truths of history and philosophy. While, through these, he instructed me to restrain my own rash and untutored spirit, he also unfolded before me the living page of his heart, enabling me to feel and comprehend its marvellous nature.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had, even in his infancy, sought to plant bold and aspiring purposes in her son's mind. She perceived that he possessed genius and extraordinary ability; she nurtured these, intending later to employ them in advancing her own designs. She fostered his thirst for learning and his vehement courage; she even endured his ungovernable love of liberty, hoping that it would, as so often happens, ripen into a desire for dominion. She tried to rear him with resentment toward, and a wish to avenge himself on, those who had helped bring about his father's abdication. In this she failed. Yet the narratives given him, however perverted, of a great and wise people claiming the right to govern themselves, awakened his admiration: from his earliest years he was a republican by conviction. His mother nevertheless did not abandon hope. To a love of power and the lofty pride of birth, she joined fixed ambition, patience, and command of herself. She gave herself to studying her son's character. By praise, rebuke, and persuasion, she sought to discover and touch the proper strings; and although the music produced beneath her hand sounded like discord to her, she founded her hopes upon his abilities, confident that in the end she would prevail over him.\"}"
