          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ab4cde2ca8519a6a6c3c345\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat is it?\u201d Oliver demanded.\\n\\n\u201cQuiet!\u201d the Dodger replied. \u201cSee that old fellow at the bookstall?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old gentleman across the street?\u201d said Oliver. \u201cYes, I see him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019ll do,\u201d said the Dodger.\\n\\n\u201cA perfect mark,\u201d remarked Master Charley Bates.\\n\\nOliver looked from one to the other in complete surprise, but he was given no chance to ask questions. The two boys slipped stealthily across the road and crept close behind the old gentleman to whom they had pointed. Oliver followed them by a few steps and, uncertain whether to go forward or turn back, stood watching in silent astonishment.\\n\\nThe old gentleman looked highly respectable, with powdered hair and gold spectacles. He wore a bottle-green coat with a black velvet collar, white trousers, and carried a neat bamboo cane beneath his arm. He had picked up a book from the stall and stood reading it as intently as though he were seated in an armchair in his own study. Indeed, he may have imagined himself there, for he was so absorbed that he saw neither the bookstall nor the street nor the boys\u2014nothing at all except the book. He read straight through it, turning the page when he reached the bottom, beginning at the first line of the next, and continuing steadily with the deepest interest and eagerness.\\n\\nOliver stood a few paces away, his eyes opened as wide as they could go, and felt horror and alarm when he saw the Dodger slip his hand into the old gentleman\u2019s pocket and pull out a handkerchief. He saw him pass it to Charley Bates, and then watched both boys race away around the corner at full speed.\\n\\nAt once, the whole mystery of the handkerchiefs, the watches, the jewels, and the Jew came rushing into the boy\u2019s mind.\\n\\nFor a moment he stood still, the blood racing through his veins with such terror that he