          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"441f3436858d9501a1c0f441\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo raising by hand, then. Not in the least!\u201d\\n\\nJoe passed me more gravy, which I was too frightened to accept.\\n\\n\u201cHe was a heap of trouble to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble, sympathising with my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d repeated my sister; \u201ctrouble?\u201d and then launched into a dreadful list of every illness I had ever been responsible for, every night of wakefulness I had caused, every high spot I had fallen from, every low spot I had fallen into, every hurt I had inflicted on myself, and every occasion on which she had wished me in my grave and I had stubbornly declined to go.\\n\\nI suppose the Romans must have irritated each other terribly with their noses. Perhaps that was why they grew into such a restless people. At any rate, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose irritated me so much, while my offences were being recounted, that I longed to tug it until he cried out. Yet all I had suffered until then was nothing beside the dreadful sensations that overcame me when the silence followed my sister\u2019s account, and during that silence everyone looked at me, as I was painfully aware, with anger and disgust.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently steering the company back to the subject from which they had wandered, \u201cPork\u2014when biled\u2014is rich too, ain\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTake a little brandy, uncle,\u201d said my sister.\\n\\nO Heaven, it had arrived at last! He would discover it was weak, he would pronounce it weak, and I was undone! I gripped the table-leg beneath the cloth with both hands, and waited for my doom.\\n\\nMy sister fetched the stone bottle, returned with it, and poured out his brandy; nobody else had any. The miserable man played with his glass,\u2014lifted it, examined it against the light, set it down,\u2014and stretched out my torment. Meanwhile Mrs. Joe and Joe were busily clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI could not take my eyes from him. Still clutching the table-leg with hands and feet, I watched the wretched creature toy with his glass, raise it, smile, toss back his head, and swallow the brandy. The next instant the company were struck with indescribable alarm, for he sprang up, spun round several times in a frightful spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and dashed through the door; then he appeared at the window, plunging and spitting violently, pulling the most horrible faces, and seeming quite mad.\\n\\nI held fast while Mrs. Joe and Joe hurried to him.\"}"
