          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b4c01df1b6f0458628d2867d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI agree. It\u2019s the best thing. Will you just hold hands and swear that we\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no, that wouldn\u2019t be enough for this. That\u2019s good enough for little worthless everyday things\u2014especially with girls, because they go back on you anyway, and blab if they get mad\u2014but there ought to be writing for a big thing like this. And blood.\u201d\\n\\nTom\u2019s whole soul approved of the idea. It was deep, dark, and terrible; the hour, the circumstances, and the surroundings all suited it. He picked up a clean pine shingle lying in the moonlight, took a small piece of red chalk from his pocket, arranged his work in the moonlight, and laboriously scrawled these lines, stressing every slow downward stroke by clamping his tongue between his teeth and easing the pressure on the upward strokes.\\n\\n\u201cHuck Finn and Tom Sawyer swears they will keep mum about This and They wish They may Drop down dead in Their Tracks if They ever Tell and Rot.\u201d\\n\\nHuckleberry was full of admiration for Tom\u2019s skill at writing and the grandeur of his language. He immediately took a pin from his lapel and was about to prick himself, but Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cWait! Don\u2019t do that. A pin\u2019s brass. It might have verdigris on it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s verdigris?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s poison. That\u2019s what it is. Just swallow some once\u2014you\u2019ll find out.\u201d\\n\\nSo Tom unwound the thread from one of his needles, and each boy pricked the ball of his thumb and squeezed out a drop of blood. At last, after many squeezes, Tom managed to sign his initials, using the ball of his little finger as a pen. Then he showed Huckleberry how to make an H and an F, and the oath was complete. They buried the shingle near the wall, with some gloomy ceremonies and incantations, and considered the chains binding their tongues locked, with the key thrown away.\\n\\nA figure now crept stealthily through a gap at the other end of the ruined building, but they did not notice it.\\n\\n\u201cTom,\u201d Huckleberry whispered, \u201cdoes this stop us from ever telling\u2014always?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course it does. It doesn\u2019t matter what happens, we have to keep quiet. We\u2019d drop dead\u2014don\u2019t you know that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I suppose that\u2019s true.\u201d\\n\\nThey went on whispering for a while. Then a dog began a long, mournful howl just outside\u2014within ten feet of them. The boys suddenly clutched each other in an agony of fear.\\n\\n\u201cWhich one of us does he mean?\u201d Huckleberry gasped.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014look through the crack. Quick!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, you, Tom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t\u2014I can\u2019t do it, Huck!\u201d\"}"
