{"request_id":"75dc42c76ae145c13e4a5912","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, that is nothing! It gives me pleasure. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art.’

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after managing to decline an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and went to the Park.

For the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at twelve he drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon the impassive hall-porter kept putting up telegrams from different parts of the country reporting horse-race results, verdicts in divorce cases, the weather, and similar matters, while the tape clicked out tedious details of an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to the great indignation of Colonel Goodchild, who wished to read the reports of a speech he had made that morning at the Mansion House on South African Missions and the desirability of having black Bishops in every province, and who, for some reason, had a strong dislike of the _Evening News_. Yet none of the papers contained the faintest reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that the attempt had failed. It was a dreadful blow, and for a while he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, was full of elaborate apologies and offered to provide another clock free of charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost price. But Lord Arthur had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything was so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite was scarcely obtainable in a pure state. The little German, however, though conceding that something must have gone wrong with the mechanism, still hoped the clock might yet explode. He cited the case of a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa which, though set to explode after ten days, had failed to do so for nearly three months. It was true that, when it finally went off, it only blew a housemaid to pieces, since the Governor had left town six weeks earlier; but it at least proved that dynamite, as a destructive force, was a powerful, if somewhat unpunctual, instrument when directed by machinery."}

