          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb1b59b5f746ccd52bda501f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered and went back inside, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He did not even look at the murdered man. He felt that the secret was not to understand what had happened. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait, and caused all his misery, had passed out of his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was an unusual Moorish lamp, made of dull silver, decorated with patterns of polished steel, and set with rough turquoises. Perhaps his servant would notice it was gone and ask questions. He hesitated for a moment, then went back and took it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the dead body. How still it was! How terribly white the long hands looked! It was like a horrible wax figure.\\n\\nAfter locking the door behind him, he crept quietly downstairs. The woodwork creaked and seemed to cry out in pain. Several times he stopped and listened. No: everything was silent. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nWhen he reached the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden somewhere. He unlocked a secret cupboard in the wall paneling, where he kept his own strange disguises, and put them inside. He could burn them later. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and began to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were hanged in England for what he had done. There seemed to be a madness for murder in the air. Some red star had come too near the earth.... Yet what evidence was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him return. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.... Paris! Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, on the midnight train, as he had planned. With his strange, private habits, it would be months before anyone became suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nA sudden idea came to him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went out into the hall. There he stopped, hearing the slow, heavy steps of the policeman on the pavement outside and seeing the flash of his lantern reflected in the window. He waited, holding his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments, he pulled back the latch and slipped outside, closing the door very gently behind him. Then he began to ring the bell.\"}"
