{"request_id":"60e6957ae3aac35ac77517f4","rewritten_text":"Many American women, on leaving their own country, take on an air of permanent ill health, believing it to be a mark of European sophistication; but Mrs. Otis had never made this mistake. She had a splendid constitution and an astonishing supply of high spirits. Indeed, in many ways she was quite English, and offered an excellent example of the fact that America and England now have everything in common except, naturally, the language. Her eldest son, named Washington by his parents in a patriotic moment he never stopped regretting, was a fair-haired, fairly handsome young man. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three seasons in succession, and was known even in London as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weaknesses. In every other respect, he was extremely sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, graceful and lovely as a fawn, with a fine openness in her large blue eyes. She was a remarkable horsewoman, and had once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park, beating him by a length and a half just before the statue of Achilles, to the enormous delight of the young Duke of Cheshire. He proposed to her on the spot and was sent back to Eton that very night by his guardians, in floods of tears. After Virginia came the twins, usually called “The Stars and Stripes,” because they were always being caned. They were delightful boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the only true republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase lay seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a wagonette to meet them, and they set off in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was faintly scented by the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood pigeon crooning over its own sweet voice, or glimpsed, deep among the rustling ferns, the gleaming breast of a pheasant. Little squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and over the mossy rises, their white tails raised in the air. But as they entered the avenue of Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds, an odd stillness seemed to settle over the air, a great flock of rooks flew silently above them, and before they reached the house, several large drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to receive them, neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

