          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dbdf0f4bbbb97781769cead7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and earnestly that we caught the interested attention of that liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic crowd. They gathered around us and took on a very resolute air. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf you truly are freemen, you have nothing to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround you as shield and shelter! (Applause.) You shall soon see. Produce your proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proof?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that you are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered! I came to my senses and said nothing. But the king burst out:\\n\\n\u201cYou are mad, man. It would be better, and more reasonable, for this thief and scoundrel to prove that we are not freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he knew his own laws as people so often know laws: by their wording, not their consequences. They acquire a meaning, and become very vivid, when they are applied to yourself.\\n\\nEveryone shook their heads and looked disappointed; some turned away, no longer interested. The orator said\u2014and this time in the practical tones of business, not sentiment:\\n\\n\u201cIf you do not know your country\u2019s laws, it is time you learned them. You are strangers to us; you will not deny that. You may be freemen\u2014we do not deny it\u2014but you may also be slaves. The law is plain: it does not require the claimant to prove that you are slaves; it requires you to prove that you are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cDear sir, give us only time to send to Astolat; or only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and you cannot expect them to be granted. They would take much time and would improperly inconvenience your master\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaster, idiot!\u201d the king thundered. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d\\n\\nI got the words out in time to stop the king. We were in enough trouble already; it could not help us to give these people the idea that we were lunatics.\\n\\nThere is no point in dragging out the details. The earl put us up and sold us at auction. This same infernal law had existed in our own South in my own time, more than thirteen hundred years later, and under it hundreds of freemen unable to prove they were freemen had been sold into lifelong slavery, without the fact making any particular impression on me. But the moment the law and the auction block entered my own experience, something that had merely seemed improper suddenly became hellish. Well, that is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like swine. In a large town with an active market, we would have brought a good price; but this place was utterly stagnant, and so we sold for an amount that makes me ashamed whenever I think of it.\"}"
