          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"53d3018df227ec6afbc68494\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Country people, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, loafers, and vagabonds of every low sort were packed together in one mass. The drovers whistled; dogs barked; oxen bellowed and plunged; sheep bleated; pigs grunted and squealed; hawkers cried out; and on every side came shouts, curses, and quarrels. Bells rang and voices roared from every public house; there was crowding, pushing, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling. The ugly, discordant din rose from every corner of the market, while unwashed, unshaven, ragged, filthy figures ran constantly back and forth, forcing their way into and out of the throng. It was a deafening, bewildering scene that completely overwhelmed the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, dragging Oliver behind him, forced his way through the densest part of the crowd, paying little attention to the many sights and sounds that amazed the boy. He nodded two or three times to friends passing by and, refusing just as many invitations to take a morning drink, pushed steadily on until they had escaped the uproar and gone through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, glancing up at the clock of St. Andrew\u2019s Church. \u201cNearly seven! You must move faster. Come, don\u2019t start lagging already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nMr. Sikes emphasized this with a tug on his small companion\u2019s wrist. Oliver quickened his pace into a sort of trot, halfway between walking fast and running, and kept up with the housebreaker\u2019s long strides as best he could.\\n\\nThey continued at this pace until they had passed Hyde Park Corner and were heading toward Kensington. Then Sikes slowed down until an empty cart, some distance behind them, caught up. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d written on it, he asked the driver, with as much politeness as he could manage, whether he would give them a ride as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump in,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, he\u2019s my boy,\u201d Sikes replied, staring hard at Oliver and absently putting his hand into the pocket where the pistol lay.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a little too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, my lad?\u201d the driver asked, seeing that Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, take my hand, Ned. Get in!\u201d\\n\\nWith these words to Oliver, he helped him into the cart. The driver pointed to a pile of sacks and told him to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed the various milestones, Oliver wondered more and more where his companion intended to take him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford all went by, yet they travelled on as steadily as though they had only just set out.\"}"
