{"request_id":"f8da7e26cabd395ff00c9d6c","rewritten_text":"We were raised together, with less than a year between our ages. Needless to say, we knew nothing of discord or quarrelling. Harmony was the spirit of our companionship, and the differences and contrasts in our characters only drew us closer. Elizabeth had a calmer, more focused nature; yet, despite all my passion, I could apply myself more intensely and was more deeply seized by a thirst for knowledge. She occupied herself with the airy creations of poets; and in the grand and marvellous scenes around our Swiss home—the sublime forms of the mountains, the changing seasons, storm and calm, winter's silence, and the life and tumult of our Alpine summers—she found abundant cause for wonder and delight. While my companion regarded the magnificent appearances of things with a serious and contented mind, I took pleasure in seeking out their causes. The world was a mystery I longed to solve. Curiosity, earnest inquiry into nature's hidden laws, and a joy almost like rapture when they revealed themselves to me are among my earliest remembered feelings.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents abandoned their wandering life altogether and settled in their native country. We had a house in Geneva and a campagne at Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, a little more than a league from the city. We lived chiefly at the latter, and my parents spent their lives in considerable seclusion. By nature, I avoided crowds and formed ardent attachments to a few people. I was therefore indifferent to most of my schoolmates, but I bound myself in the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant. He was a boy of unusual talent and imagination. He loved enterprise, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He had read deeply in tales of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began many stories of enchantment and knightly adventure. He tried to persuade us to stage plays and take part in masquerades, with characters drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur's Round Table, and the chivalrous company who shed their blood to rescue the holy sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents were filled with kindness and indulgence. We felt they were not tyrants who ruled our fate according to their whims, but the agents and creators of all the many pleasures we enjoyed."}

