          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"286ffd4b0f554411d3ae6bd8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed, and winter was well underway. I had gone to school nearly all the time and could spell, read, and write a little, and could recite the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t suppose I could ever get beyond that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t put much faith in mathematics anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got so I could bear it. Whenever I got especially tired of it, I played hooky, and the whipping I got the next day did me good and cheered me up. So the longer I attended school, the easier it became. I was getting somewhat used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they were not so hard on me. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed usually weighed on me heavily, but before the cold weather came I would slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that gave me a rest. I liked the old ways best, but I was beginning to like the new ones too, a little. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely, and doing very satisfactorily. She said she was not ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast, I happened to knock over the saltcellar. I reached for some salt as quickly as I could, to throw it over my left shoulder and ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson got there before me and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you are always making!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but that was not going to keep away the bad luck; I knew that well enough. After breakfast I set out feeling anxious and unsteady, wondering where it would strike me and what it would be. There are ways to prevent certain kinds of bad luck, but this was not one of those kinds. So I did not try to do anything; I just wandered along, low-spirited and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I saw someone\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry and stood around the stile for a while, then gone on around the garden fence. It was strange that they had not come in after standing there so long. I could not understand it. Somehow, it was very curious. I was about to follow the tracks around, but first I bent down to examine them.\"}"
