          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dc1727a418032f87c583b5b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I cannot understand at all why she wished to become close to me; but if the same circumstances happened again, I am sure I would be deceived again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received neither note nor line from her. When she did come, it was very clear that she took no pleasure in the visit. She offered a slight, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing about wishing to see me again, and was so changed in every way that, when she left, I was completely determined not to continue the acquaintance.\\n\\nI pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I can safely say that every step toward intimacy came from her. Yet I pity her, because she must know she has behaved wrongly, and because I am certain that anxiety for her brother is the cause. I need not explain further; and though we know this anxiety is entirely unnecessary, if she feels it, it easily accounts for her behaviour toward me. Since he is so deservedly dear to his sister, any concern she feels for him is natural and affectionate.\\n\\nStill, I cannot help wondering that she should have such fears now, because if he had cared for me at all, we must have met long, long ago. He knows I am in town, I am certain, from something she said herself; yet from the way she speaks, it seems as though she is trying to persuade herself that he is truly attached to Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging too harshly, I would almost be tempted to say that there is a strong appearance of deceit in all this.\\n\\nI will try to put away every painful thought and think only of what can make me happy: your affection, and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley said something about his never returning to Netherfield, about giving up the house, though she did not speak with 