in!\u201d cried Uncle Pumblechook. \u201cVery well said! Nicely made! Excellent indeed! Now, Joseph, you understand the matter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d said my sister, still reproachfully, while Joe apologetically rubbed the back of his hand repeatedly across his nose, \u201cyou do not yet\u2014though you may think you do\u2014understand the matter. You may suppose you do, but you do not, Joseph. Because you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing that, for all we know, this boy\u2019s fortune may be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, keep him overnight, and personally take him to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lord preserve us!\u201d cried my sister, flinging off her bonnet in sudden despair, \u201chere I am talking to complete fools, while Uncle Pumblechook is waiting, the mare is catching cold at the door, and the boy is filthy with soot and dirt from the top of his head to the soles of his feet!\u201d\"}"
