          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ab33d593f1de512b1c5983df\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft and he counted it up, he found he had gathered eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. Besides that, he had carried off a three-gallon jug of whisky he found under a wagon as he was heading home through the woods. The king said that, all things considered, it beat any day\u2019s work he had ever done in the missionary business. He said there was no use denying it: heathens were nothing compared with pirates when it came to working a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke had thought he was doing pretty well until the king showed up, but afterward he did not think so highly of it. In that printing office, he had set and printed two small jobs for farmers\u2014horse notices\u2014and collected four dollars. He had also taken in ten dollars\u2019 worth of advertisements for the paper, saying he would print them for four dollars if they paid in advance, so they did. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he got three subscriptions at fifty cents each, provided they paid beforehand. They had meant to pay in firewood and onions, as usual, but he said he had only just bought the business, had cut the price as low as he could afford, and intended to run it for cash. He set up a short poem he had composed out of his own head\u2014three verses, rather sweet and sad\u2014called \u201cYes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart,\u201d and left it all set, ready to print in the paper, without charging anything for it. Altogether, he had taken in nine dollars and a half, and said he had earned it by a pretty honest day\u2019s work.\\n\\nThen he showed us another small job he had printed without charging for it, because it was for us. It had a picture of a runaway Black man carrying a bundle on a stick over his shoulder, with \u201c$200 reward\u201d beneath it. The wording was all about Jim and described him exactly. It said he had escaped from St. Jacques\u2019 plantation, forty miles below New Orleans, the previous winter, and had probably gone north; whoever caught him and sent him back would receive the reward and expenses.\\n\\n\u201cNow,\u201d said the duke, \u201cafter tonight we can travel by day if we want to. Whenever we see anyone coming, we can tie Jim hand and foot with a rope, lay him in the wigwam, show them this handbill, and say we captured him upriver. Then we can say we are too poor to travel by steamboat, so our friends let us have this little raft on credit, and we are going down to collect the reward.\u201d\"}"
