          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7ca330bd2242f6b8660fbebf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always pretended Peter wrote those things to him; but that was a lie. He got every single one from that young fool we paddled up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane brought the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud and cried over it. It gave the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls; and it gave the tannery, which was doing good business, along with some other houses and land worth about seven thousand dollars, and three thousand dollars in gold, to Harvey and William. It also said where the six thousand dollars in cash was hidden in the cellar. So the two frauds said they would go get it and make everything fair and open, and they told me to bring a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag, they poured it out on the floor. It was a beautiful sight, all those gold coins. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said:\\n\\n\u201cOh, this ain\u2019t wonderful or anything! Oh, no, I reckon not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, don\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke said it did. They handled the gold coins, ran them through their fingers, and let them jingle onto the floor; and the king said:\\n\\n\u201cThere\u2019s no use talking; being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs that have been left money is the line for you and me, Bilge. This here comes from trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there ain\u2019t no better way.\u201d\\n\\nMost anybody would have been satisfied with the pile and taken it on trust; but no, they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king said:\\n\\n\u201cDarn him, I wonder what he did with that four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d\\n\\nThey fussed over it awhile and searched everywhere for it. Then the duke said:\\n\\