          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b4102cc3dac8a8c14f093cdd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At that moment, his eyes\u2014and mine as well\u2014fell upon the scrap of parchment, which I then took for paper. It lay half buried in the sand, with one corner showing. Near where we found it, I noticed the remains of the hull of what seemed to have been a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck had apparently been there a very long time, for it was barely possible to recognize the shape of boat timbers.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Soon afterward we started home, and on the way met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he begged permission to take it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately put it into his waistcoat pocket, leaving behind the parchment in which it had been wrapped, which I had kept in my hand while he examined it. Perhaps he feared I might change my mind and thought it best to secure the prize at once\u2014you know how eager he is about everything connected with Natural History. At the same time, without realizing it, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou remember that when I went to the table to sketch the beetle, I found no paper where it was usually kept. I looked in the drawer and found none there either. I searched my pockets, hoping to find an old letter, and my hand came upon the parchment. I give these exact details of how it came into my possession because the circumstances struck me very strongly.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you will think me fanciful\u2014but I had already formed a kind of connection. I had joined two links in a great chain. There was a boat on the seacoast, and near the boat was a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn upon it. You will naturally ask, \u2018What is the connection?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the familiar symbol of the pirate. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in every battle.\\n\\n\u201cI have said that the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment is lasting\u2014almost impossible to destroy. Things of little importance are seldom written or drawn on parchment, since for ordinary writing or drawing it is far less suitable than paper. This thought suggested that the death\u2019s-head had some meaning, some relevance. I also noticed the shape of the parchment. Though one corner had been destroyed by some accident, its original form could be seen to have been oblong.\"}"
