          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"955e55d9d425f03b08cc9ba2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShould I call on them? Or on Miss Bates? She is even closer.\u2014And I do not know whether Miss Bates might not understand the wishes of the others as well as anyone. I think we need a larger council. Suppose I go and ask Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, somewhat hesitantly, \u201cif you think she can be of any use.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing. She will not even hear your questions. I see no benefit in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining, so very entertaining! I am very fond of hearing Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment Mr. Weston joined them and, when he heard the proposal, gave it his firm approval.\\n\\n\u201cYes, do, Frank.\u2014Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle the matter at once. She will enjoy the plan, I am sure; and I do not know anyone better suited to show us how to overcome difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming a little too particular. She is a constant lesson in how to be happy. But bring them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady?\u201d...\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great fool, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not remember at once. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And off he ran.\\n\\nLong before he returned, escorting the short, neat, quick-moving aunt and her elegant niece,\u2014Mrs. Weston, like a sweet-natured woman and a good wife, had considered the matter again and found its disadvantages far less serious than she had first imagined\u2014indeed, very slight; and there the difficulty of deciding ended. Everything else, at least in theory, was perfectly easy. All the smaller arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, arranged themselves; or were left as mere details to be settled at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.\u2014Everyone invited was certain to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to ask to remain a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused. And it was to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she agreed most warmly that it must be. She was not needed as an adviser; but as one who approved, (a much safer role,) she was truly welcome. Her approval, at once broad and detailed, warm and unceasing, could not fail to please; and for another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the different rooms, some making suggestions, some listening, and all happily enjoying the prospect before them.\"}"
