{"request_id":"8fab78fed6f09c42085b84c0","rewritten_text":"Ye spirits of tempest, take me! ye ministers of ruin, spread wide your arms, and hold me for ever! unless some gentler power has appointed me another fate, that, after much suffering, I may gather my recompense, and once more feel my heart beat beside the heart of one akin to me.

The Tiber, that highway laid out by nature herself through her continent, lay before me, and many boats were moored along its banks. With a few books, some provisions, and my dog, I would enter one of them and drift with the river’s current to the sea; then, keeping close to shore, I would follow the lovely coasts and sunlit headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples, skirt Calabria, and venture through the double dangers of Scylla and Charybdis; afterwards, with resolute purpose—for what had I left to lose?—glide over the ocean towards Malta and the remoter Cyclades. I would shun Constantinople, whose familiar towers and bays belonged to a different condition of life than that which was now mine; I would sail along Asia Minor and Syria, and, beyond the seven-mouthed Nile, turn northward once more, until, leaving forgotten Carthage and desolate Lybia behind, I reached the pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever it might be—the slimy caverns and noiseless abysses of the sea might become my home before I completed this protracted voyage; or disease might strike my heart while I floated alone upon the heaving Mediterranean; or, at some place where I landed, I might discover what I sought—a companion. Or, should that be denied me—through endless years, feeble and grey-haired, my youth already buried with those whom I loved—the solitary wanderer would yet spread his sail and grasp the tiller; and, ever yielding to heaven’s winds, would pass for ever round one headland after another, cast anchor in bay after bay, and still cleave the barren sea, leaving the green land of native Europe behind, descending the tawny coast of Africa, and, having endured the violent waters of the Cape, might fasten my weathered skiff in some inlet shadowed by fragrant groves upon the scented islands of the distant Indian Ocean.

These are extravagant visions. Yet, since they first came upon me a week ago, as I stood upon the summit of St. Peter’s, they have governed my fancy. I have picked out my boat and stored it with my meagre supplies. I have chosen a few books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare—yet all the libraries of the world stand open to me, and at any harbour I may replenish my store. I expect no change for the better; but the sameness of the present has become unbearable to me."}

