          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"084f4bdf909e5261d6a78540\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was finally settled that we should reside together during my sojourn in the city; and, as my pecuniary affairs were somewhat less straitened than his, I was allowed to bear the cost of leasing and furnishing, in a manner suited to the somewhat fantastic melancholy of our shared disposition, an ancient and grotesque house, long abandoned owing to superstitions which we did not investigate, and crumbling toward ruin in a secluded and desolate quarter of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad the manner of our existence in this place become known to the public, we should have been thought madmen\u2014though perhaps madmen of an inoffensive kind. Our retirement was absolute. We received no visitors. Indeed, the situation of our retreat had been scrupulously concealed even from my former acquaintances; and many years had passed since Dupin had either known, or been known by, anyone in Paris. We lived wholly within ourselves.\\n\\nIt was a whim of my friend's fancy (for what else can I term it?) to love the night for her own sake; and into this _bizarrerie_, as into every other of his, I readily entered, surrendering myself to his wild caprices with complete _abandon_. The sable goddess could not herself abide with us continually; yet we could imitate her presence. At the first light of morning we shut every grimy shutter of our old dwelling, and kindled two tapers which, heavily perfumed, gave forth only the faintest and most ghastly beams. With their assistance we occupied our spirits in dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the coming of real Darkness. Then we went out into the streets arm in arm, pursuing the subjects of the day, or wandering far and wide until a late hour, in search, amid the strange lights and shadows of the crowded city, of that boundless mental excitement which tranquil observation may provide.\\n\\nAt such seasons I could not refrain from noticing and admiring (though his abundant ideality had led me to anticipate it) a singular power of analysis in Dupin. He appeared also to take keen pleasure in exercising it\u2014if not precisely in displaying it\u2014and freely acknowledged the enjoyment he drew from it. With a subdued, chuckling laugh, he assured me that most men wore windows in their breasts in relation to himself, and he commonly supported such claims with immediate and astonishing evidence of his close knowledge of my own thoughts. At these moments his bearing was cold and abstract; his eyes had a vacant look; while his voice, ordinarily a full tenor, rose to a treble that might have seemed petulant, had it not been for the measured and perfectly distinct character of his utterance.\"}"
