          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"51ee4a84138726d95d5771b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At noon on Saturday, I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke powders, waving his hands through the air, and muttering nonsense as fiercely as ever, but he looked rather discouraged, since he had not caused even a drop of sweat to appear in that well. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow is it looking by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSee, I am at this very moment trying the strongest enchantment known to the masters of the secret arts in the lands of the East; if it fails me, nothing can help. Peace, until I am finished.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he made a smoke that darkened the whole area, and it must have made things unpleasant for the hermits, because the wind was blowing toward them and carried the thick, rolling fog down over their caves. He poured out a matching flood of words, twisted his body, and slashed the air with his hands in a most remarkable fashion. After twenty minutes, he collapsed, panting and nearly worn out. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns, followed by a crowd of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all attracted by the enormous smoke and all in a state of great excitement. The abbot anxiously asked about the result. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any work within mortal power could break the spell that holds back these waters, the one I have just attempted would have done it. It has failed; therefore I now know that what I feared is proven true: the sign of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name no one may speak and live, has cast his spell upon this well. No mortal lives, or ever will live, who can discover the secret of that spell, and without that secret no one can break it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done all a man can do. Let me go.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally, this threw the abbot into great alarm. He turned to me, his face showing it plainly, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all of it, then\u2014not all! What part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has put his spell on the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen you also mean that when he says no one can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he says something that is not necessarily true. Under certain conditions, an attempt to break it may have some chance\u2014some small, slight chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
