yond the building.\\n\\nAt last the sun climbed so high that it cast a gentle beam, like one of hope or shelter, straight upon the old prisoner\u2019s head. The mercy was more than could be endured; in an instant the screen of dust and chaff, which had held out surprisingly long, scattered to the winds, and Saint Antoine had seized him!\\n\\nThe news was known at once to the farthest edges of the crowd. Defarge had only leapt over a railing and a table, and clasped the wretched creature in a fatal embrace\u2014Madame Defarge had only come after him and twisted her hand in one of the ropes binding him\u2014The Vengeance and Jacques Three had not yet reached them, and the men at the windows had not yet swooped into the Hall like birds of prey from their lofty perches\u2014when the cry appeared to rise throughout the whole city, \u201cBring him out!\u201d\"}"
