          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90f1a30892b8bba2e8e26e63\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is remarkable! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from his seat and paced through the garden. After a while, he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is simply an artistic inspiration to me. You might see nothing in him. I see everything in him. He is never more present in my work than when there is no image of him in it. As I have said, he suggests a new style. I find him in the curves of certain lines, in the beauty and delicacy of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why won\u2019t you exhibit his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I have put into it some sign of this strange artistic worship, which, of course, I have never wished to discuss with him. He knows nothing about it. He never shall know anything about it. But the world might guess, and I will not expose my soul to its shallow, curious eyes. My heart shall never be placed under their microscope. There is too much of myself in that painting, Harry\u2014far too much of myself!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are not as careful as you are. They know how useful passion can be in publishing. These days, a broken heart can go through many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hate them for it,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist should make beautiful things, but should put nothing from his own life into them. We live in an age when people treat art as though it were a kind of autobiography. We have lost the pure sense of beauty. One day I will show the world what that is; and for that reason, the world shall never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you are mistaken, Basil, but I will not argue with you. Only people who are intellectually lost ever argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter thought for a few moments. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he answered after a pause. \u201cI know he likes me. Of course, I flatter him terribly. I take a strange pleasure in saying things to him that I know I will regret saying. Usually, he is charming to me, and we sit in the studio talking about a thousand things. But now and then he is terribly thoughtless, and seems to take real pleasure in hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I have given my whole soul to someone who treats it like a flower to wear in his coat, a decoration for his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
