          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"28853c48d90c8f79260e14d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, have you reached? What impression have I made on your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nA chill ran over me as Dupin asked the question. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said, \u201ccommitted this crime\u2014some raging lunatic who escaped from a nearby asylum.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn certain respects,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour notion is not beside the point. But the voices of madmen, even in their fiercest fits, never correspond to that peculiar voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and their speech, however incoherent its words may be, always retains the coherence of syllables. Besides, a madman\u2019s hair is not like that which I now hold in my hand. I pulled this small tuft from the tightly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I said, utterly shaken; \u201cthis hair is extraordinary\u2014this is not human hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not said that it is,\u201d he answered; \u201cbut before we settle that question, I want you to look at the small sketch I have drawn on this paper. It is a facsimile of what one part of the testimony calls \u2018dark bruises and deep indentations of fingernails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what another, by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne, calls \u2018a series of livid marks, plainly the impressions of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will see,\u201d my friend continued, laying the paper open on the table before us, \u201cthat this drawing suggests a firm, unchanging grip. There is no sign of slipping. Every finger remained\u2014perhaps until the victim died\u2014in the dreadful hold with which it first sank into her flesh. Now try to place all your fingers at once in the separate impressions you see here.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but could not do it.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing the matter fairly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies flat, but the human throat is cylindrical. Here is a piece of wood with roughly the same circumference as a throat. Wrap the drawing around it, and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did so, but the difficulty was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis the mark of no human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d Dupin replied, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical and general description of the great tawny orangutan of the East Indian Islands. The enormous size, tremendous strength and agility, savage ferocity, and imitative tendencies of these mammals are well known to everyone. At once I understood the full horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the fingers,\u201d I said when I had finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
