{"request_id":"25d42bc5190bd08b61519656","rewritten_text":"I then bored a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and inserted a plug of soft wood, cut into a tapering, conical shape. I pushed the plug in or drew it out as needed until, after a few trials, it reached precisely that degree of tightness at which the water seeping through the hole and falling into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This, of course, was quickly and easily determined by observing what proportion of the pitcher filled in a given time. Once I had arranged this, the rest of the plan was obvious. My bed was set up on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head rested directly beneath the mouth of the pitcher. Clearly, at the end of an hour, when the pitcher became full, it would overflow, and overflow at the mouth, which stood somewhat lower than its rim. It was equally clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet could not fail to strike my face, and that the certain result would be to wake me instantly, even from the deepest sleep in the world.

“It was nearly eleven when I had finished these preparations, and I went to bed at once, fully confident in the effectiveness of my invention. Nor was I disappointed. Every sixty minutes, exactly, my faithful chronometer woke me; after emptying the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole and performing the duties of the condenser, I returned to bed. These regular interruptions of my sleep caused me even less inconvenience than I had expected; and when I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun stood many degrees above my horizon.

“April 3. I found the balloon at a truly immense height, and the apparent curvature of the earth had increased considerably. In the ocean below lay a cluster of black specks, undoubtedly islands. Far to the north I saw a thin, white, exceedingly brilliant line or streak along the edge of the horizon, and I did not hesitate to suppose it was the southern edge of the ice of the Polar Sea. My curiosity was greatly stirred, for I hoped to travel much farther north and might perhaps, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole itself. I now regretted that my great elevation would, in that event, prevent me from making as accurate a survey as I wished.”"}

