          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"810c8a2493f4871c4a963f85\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, I misunderstood you. I thought you said \u2018anybody,\u2019 and so I assumed \u2018anybody\u2019 meant\u2014well, anybody; that is, everybody.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt does\u2014anybody of high birth; and all the better if he is royal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat seems quite reasonable,\u201d said the abbot, seeing his chance to calm matters and prevent disaster, \u201cfor it is hardly likely that so marvelous a gift as this would be granted to reveal the affairs of lesser persons rather than those born near the heights of greatness. Our King Arthur\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould you know of him?\u201d the enchanter interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cMost gladly, yes, and gratefully.\u201d\\n\\nAt once everyone was filled with awe and interest again, the hopeless fools. They watched the incantations with fascination, and when the announcement came, looked at me as if to say, \u201cThere now, what can you say to that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe king is weary from the hunt, and has lain in his palace these two hours, sleeping without dreams.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s blessing upon him!\u201d said the abbot, crossing himself. \u201cMay that sleep refresh both his body and his soul.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd it might, if he were asleep,\u201d I said, \u201cbut the king is not asleep. The king is riding.\u201d\\n\\nThere was trouble again\u2014a clash of authorities. No one knew which of us to believe; I still had some reputation left. The magician\u2019s contempt was aroused, and he said:\\n\\n\u201cLook, I have seen many marvelous seers and prophets and magicians in my lifetime, but never one before who could sit idle and see into the heart of things without a single incantation to assist him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have lived in the woods, and suffered for it. I use incantations myself, as this good brotherhood knows\u2014but only on occasions of importance.\u201d\\n\\nWhen it comes to sarcasm, I suppose I know how to hold my own. That thrust made the fellow writhe. The abbot asked after the queen and the court, and received this answer:\\n\\n\u201cThey are all asleep, overcome by fatigue, as the king is.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cThat is simply another lie. Half of them are enjoying themselves; the queen and the other half are not asleep\u2014they are riding. Now perhaps you can show us what you can do, and tell us where the king and queen, and all those riding with them at this moment, are going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey sleep now, as I said; but tomorrow they will ride, for they are traveling toward the sea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where will they be at vespers the day after tomorrow?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFar north of Camelot, with half their journey completed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is another lie, by a hundred and fifty miles.\u201d\"}"
