          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"00ca59cdd7c162ed8519ed53\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is very strange that she can always hear Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so clearly! Still, she will not find her grandmother any deafer than she was two years ago; and that is saying a great deal at my mother\u2019s age. It really has been a full two years, you know, since she was here. We have never gone so long without seeing her before, and, as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we shall hardly know how to make enough of her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you expecting Miss Fairfax here soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! That must be a great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Everybody is so surprised, and everybody says the same kind things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury as they will be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell will need the carriage himself on one of those days. It is so good of them to send her all the way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is why she has written out of turn, as we call it; because ordinarily we should not have heard from her until next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that was what I thought. I was afraid there was little chance of my hearing anything about Miss Fairfax today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo kind of you! No, we should not have heard, if it had not been for this special reason\u2014her coming here so soon. My mother is so delighted! For she is to stay with us for at least three months. Three months, she says so positively, as I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you. You see, the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her at once. They had not meant to go until summer, but she is so impatient to see them again\u2014for before she married last October, she had never been away from them for more than a week. It must feel very strange to be in different kingdoms, I was going to say, though of course different countries. So she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I truly do not know which, but we shall see presently in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014writing in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, urging them to come over at once. They would meet them in Dublin and take them back to their country home, Bally-craig, which I imagine is a beautiful place.\"}"
