          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9dbc065cfc41e0bb853095af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath on her own account; the whole labour of maintaining a languid and unsatisfactory correspondence with Elizabeth devolved upon Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had been over only a short time when Captain and Mrs Harville, with Captain Benwick, joined them; they had arranged to take their final walk about Lyme together. They were to leave for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and until then meant to remain together and outdoors for as long as possible.\\n\\nAnne perceived Captain Benwick drawing close to her as soon as they were properly out in the street. Their talk of the previous evening had not made him less inclined to seek her company again; and for some time they walked side by side, discussing, as before, Mr Scott and Lord Byron, still differing as much as ever\u2014and as any two readers might\u2014over the merits of either, until some circumstance caused an almost universal rearrangement of the party, and Captain Harville replaced Captain Benwick at her side.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said, in a rather subdued voice, \u201cyou have done a kind thing in getting that poor fellow to speak so much. I wish he had such society more often. It is bad for him, I know, to be confined as he is; but what are we to do? We cannot leave him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can readily believe that impossible; but with time, perhaps\u2014we know what time can do in every sort of sorrow, and you must recollect, Captain Harville, that your friend is still a young mourner\u2014only since last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, indeed,\u201d he answered, with a deep sigh, \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not learn of it so immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week in August, when he returned from the Cape, having just been made into the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, afraid to hear of him; he sent letters in, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news had to follow him there, but who could tell him? Not I. I would as soon have been hoisted to the yard-arm. No one could do it except that good fellow\u201d\u2014pointing to Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had reached Plymouth the week before, with no likelihood of being sent out again. He took his chance for the rest; applied for leave, but without waiting for an answer, travelled day and night until he reached Portsmouth, rowed straight out to the Grappler, and did not leave the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You may judge, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty, and replied as fully as her own feelings allowed, or as his appeared able to endure; for he was too deeply moved to return to the subject, and when he spoke again, it was of something altogether different.\"}"
