          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5d7a4b2a1771b3a1ce6e880\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while you\u2019d wake up and look to see what had caused it, and maybe spot a steamboat puffing upstream, so far away toward the other bank that you couldn\u2019t tell anything about her except whether she had a stern wheel or side wheels. Then, for maybe an hour, there wouldn\u2019t be anything to hear or see\u2014just complete loneliness. Next you might see a raft drifting past, far off yonder, with maybe some fellow chopping wood on it, because they were almost always doing that on rafts. You\u2019d see the axe flash and fall, but hear nothing. You\u2019d see it rise again, and by the time it was above the man\u2019s head, you\u2019d hear the k\u2019chunk! It had taken all that time for the sound to cross the water. That was how we spent the day, loafing around and listening to the quiet. Once there was a heavy fog, and the rafts and other boats going by were beating tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run them over. A scow or raft passed so close that we could hear them talking, cussing, and laughing\u2014hear them clear as anything\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see a sign of them. It gave you a creepy feeling, like spirits carrying on in the air. Jim said he thought they were spirits, but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern this dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as night came, we pushed off. When we got the raft out about the middle of the river, we left her alone and let her float wherever the current wanted to take her. Then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all kinds of things. We were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it. The new clothes Buck\u2019s family had made for me were too good to be comfortable, and besides, I never cared much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we had that whole river to ourselves for a long time. There were the banks and islands across the water, and maybe a spark\u2014which was a candle in a cabin window. Sometimes you could see a spark or two on the water, on a raft or scow, you know; and maybe hear a fiddle or a song drifting over from one of those boats. It was lovely living on a raft. Above us was the sky, all speckled with stars, and we used to lie on our backs and look at them, arguing about whether they had been made or had simply happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they happened. I figured it would have taken too long to make so many.\"}"
