          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c55988c414ae9fe69917dd33\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day went by nearly as the preceding one had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley had passed several morning hours with the invalid, who still, though gradually, improved; and in the evening Elizabeth joined the party in the drawing-room. The loo table, however, was not brought out. Mr. Darcy was engaged in writing, while Miss Bingley, seated beside him, watched his letter grow, and continually diverted his attention with messages for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley played piquet, with Mrs. Hurst looking on.\\n\\nElizabeth took up her needlework, and found enough amusement in observing what occurred between Darcy and his companion. The lady's unceasing praises\u2014of his handwriting, the regularity of his lines, or the extent of his letter\u2014together with the complete indifference with which he accepted them, made a singular dialogue, entirely agreeing with her judgment of them both.\\n\\n\u201cHow pleased Miss Darcy will be to get such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe gave no reply.\\n\\n\u201cYou write extraordinarily quickly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mistake me. I write somewhat slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a number of letters you must need to write over a year! Business letters too! How disagreeable I should find them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is fortunate, then, that they are my concern rather than yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo tell your sister that I am eager to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have told her that already once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI fear you dislike your pen. Allow me to mend it for you. I am remarkably good at mending pens.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so uniformly?\u201d\\n\\nHe remained silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am charmed to hear of her progress on the harp, and pray inform her that I am in perfect raptures over her lovely little design for a table, which I consider infinitely better than Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you permit me to postpone your raptures until I write next? I have no room at present to give them proper justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it matters nothing. I shall see her in January. But do you invariably write such delightfully long letters to her, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are commonly long; but whether they are always delightful is not for me to say.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is my rule that anyone who can readily write a long letter cannot write badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat will not serve as a compliment to Darcy, Caroline,\u201d cried her brother, \u201cfor he does _not_ write readily. He labours too much over words of four syllables. Do you not, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manner of writing differs greatly from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d cried Miss Bingley, \u201cCharles writes in the most heedless manner imaginable.\u201d\"}"
