          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bb81ecb46d18f407aa4b12e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse, beautiful, intelligent, and wealthy, blessed with a pleasant home and cheerful temper, appeared to possess many of life\u2019s finest advantages; and she had passed almost twenty-one years in the world with scarcely anything to trouble or annoy her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of the two daughters of a very loving and indulgent father; and, owing to her sister\u2019s marriage, had been the mistress of his household from a very early age. Her mother had died so long before that she retained only a faint memory of her tenderness; and her place had been filled by an admirable governess, whose affection had been little less than maternal.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had spent sixteen years in Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family, acting less as governess than as friend, warmly attached to both daughters, though especially to Emma. Their connection was more like that of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor had relinquished the formal position of governess, her gentle disposition had scarcely permitted her to exercise restraint; and, with even the appearance of authority long gone, they had lived together as two devoted friends, Emma doing precisely as she pleased; valuing Miss Taylor\u2019s opinion greatly, yet guided principally by her own.\\n\\nThe true drawbacks of Emma\u2019s condition were, in fact, her power to have rather too much her own way, and her tendency to think somewhat too highly of herself; these were the faults likely to cast a shade over her many pleasures. Yet the risk was then so little noticed that she did not in the least regard it as misfortune.\\n\\nSorrow arrived\u2014a mild sorrow\u2014but in no form of painful self-reproach.\u2014Miss Taylor was married. It was the loss of Miss Taylor that first gave Emma grief. On the wedding-day of this cherished friend, Emma first sat in melancholy reflection upon anything lasting. Once the wedding was finished and the bridal party departed, she and her father remained to dine alone, with no third person in prospect to brighten a long evening. Her father, as usual, settled himself to sleep after dinner, and she had nothing to do but sit and consider what she had lost.\\n\\nThe occasion promised every happiness to her friend. Mr. Weston was a gentleman of irreproachable character, comfortable means, fitting age, and agreeable manners; and Emma found some comfort in remembering the self-denying, generous friendship with which she had always desired and encouraged the union; but for her it was a dark morning\u2019s business. Miss Taylor\u2019s absence would be felt in every hour of every day. She remembered all her former kindness\u2014the kindness and affection of sixteen years\u2014how she had instructed and played with her since she was five years old\u2014how she had devoted every faculty to winning and entertaining her in health\u2014and how she had nursed her through the several illnesses of childhood.\"}"
