          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8c998ed7ab4b90431488c3a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning, she was found dead in her bed, apparently without having suffered any pain. Sir Mathew Reid was sent for immediately, but naturally nothing could be done. She was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death, she had made her will. She left Lord Arthur her small house in Curzon Street, along with all her furniture, personal belongings, and pictures, except for her collection of miniatures, which was to go to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which was for Sybil Merton. The estate was not worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was very anxious for Lord Arthur to return as soon as possible, since many bills had to be paid and Lady Clementina had never kept proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s kind thought of him, and felt that Mr. Podgers had much to answer for. Yet his love for Sybil was stronger than every other feeling, and knowing that he had done his duty gave him peace and comfort. When he arrived at Charing Cross, he felt completely happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil made him promise never again to let anything come between them, and their wedding was set for the 7th of June. Life seemed bright and beautiful again, and all his old happiness returned.\\n\\nOne day, while he was going through the house in Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil, burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers full of useless odds and ends, the young girl suddenly gave a small cry of delight.\\n\\n\u2018What have you found, Sybil?\u2019 Lord Arthur asked, looking up from his work with a smile.\\n\\n\u2018This lovely little silver _bonbonni\u00e8re_, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it charming and Dutch? Please give it to me! I know amethysts will not suit me until I am over eighty.\u2019\\n\\nIt was the box that had contained the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a slight blush rose in his face. He had almost forgotten what he had done, and it seemed a strange coincidence that Sybil, for whose sake he had endured all that terrible anxiety, should be the first to remind him of it.\\n\\n\u2018Of course you may have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, thank you, Arthur; and may I have the _bonbon_ too? I had no idea Lady Clementina liked sweets. I thought she was much too intellectual.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur turned deathly pale, and a dreadful thought entered his mind.\"}"
