          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"213faa51b3a9190b1454ad9b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Rising early in the morning and once more managing to evade the watchfulness of my creditors, I hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall and spent the little cash I had on several volumes about Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Having brought them safely home, I gave every spare moment to studying them, and soon advanced far enough in such subjects, as I believed, to carry out my plan. During this time, I made every effort to win over the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble. At last I succeeded\u2014partly by selling enough of my household furniture to pay half their claim, and partly by promising to settle the remainder when I had completed a small project I told them I was planning, and for which I asked their assistance. By these means\u2014for they were ignorant men\u2014I had little trouble persuading them to serve my purpose.\\n\\n\u201cOnce these matters were settled, I arranged, with my wife\u2019s help and under the strictest secrecy and caution, to sell what property remained to me and to borrow, in small amounts and under various excuses, a considerable sum of ready money, without giving the slightest thought to how I might repay it in the future. With the funds thus obtained, I began, from time to time, to acquire very fine cambric muslin in pieces of twelve yards each; twine; a quantity of caoutchouc varnish; a large, deep wicker basket made to order; and several other articles needed for the construction and fitting-out of a balloon of extraordinary size. I instructed my wife to sew it together as quickly as possible, giving her all the necessary directions concerning the precise method. Meanwhile, I fashioned the twine into a net of sufficient size, fitted it with a hoop and the required cords, bought a quadrant, a compass, a spyglass, an ordinary barometer with certain important alterations, and two astronomical instruments less commonly known. Then, taking advantage of the night, I transported to a secluded place east of Rotterdam five iron-bound casks, each holding about fifty gallons, along with a larger one; six properly shaped tubes of tinned ware, three inches in diameter and ten feet long; a quantity of a certain metallic substance, or semi-metal, which I shall not name; and a dozen demijohns of a very common acid. The gas produced from these latter materials is one never before generated by anyone but myself\u2014or, at least, never used for any comparable purpose. I would have no hesitation in revealing the secret, except that it properly belongs to a citizen of Nantz, France, who communicated it to me on certain conditions.\"}"
