          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e06c876921755bf57f194c8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She and the children may very well remain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, papa\u2014that is what you have never been able to bring about, and I do not believe you ever shall. Isabella cannot endure being separated from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThis was too true to be disputed. However unwelcome it might be, Mr. Woodhouse could do no more than sigh submissively; and Emma, observing that the thought of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband had depressed his spirits, at once turned to a part of the subject that must enliven them.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must give us as much of her company as possible while my brother and sister are here. I am sure she will be delighted with the children. We are very proud of the children, are we not, papa? I wonder which she will consider the handsomest\u2014Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, I wonder which she will. Poor little creatures, how happy they will be to come. They are very fond of Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I am sure I cannot imagine who would not be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John greatly resembles his mama. Henry, being the eldest, was named for me, not for his father. John, the second, bears his father\u2019s name. Some people, I believe, are surprised that the eldest was not so called; but Isabella wished him to be named Henry, and I thought it very pretty in her. And he is indeed a very clever boy. They are all remarkably clever, and have so many pretty little ways. They come and stand beside my chair, saying, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a piece of string?\u2019 Once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him that knives were made only for grandpapas. I think their father is much too rough with them, very often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are yourself so exceedingly gentle; but if you could compare him with other papas, you would not find him rough. He wants his boys to be active and hardy, and when they are naughty he can speak sharply to them now and then; but he is an affectionate father\u2014Mr. John Knightley is certainly an affectionate father. The children are all fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and throws them up to the ceiling in a most frightful manner!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they enjoy it, papa; there is nothing they enjoy more. It gives them such pleasure that, unless their uncle made a rule that they must take turns, whichever one began would never yield to the other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I cannot comprehend it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true of all of us, papa. Half the world cannot comprehend the pleasures of the other half.\u201d\"}"
