          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f189e09b365148660efd6983\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He got up and left the room with my nurse, and almost immediately my father came in.\\n\\nAt that instant, nothing could have afforded me more joy than my father\u2019s arrival. I held out my hand to him and exclaimed,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe, then\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father reassured me of their well-being and tried, by speaking of matters so dear to my heart, to lift my dejected spirits; yet he quickly perceived that a prison could never be a dwelling for cheerfulness. \u201cWhat a place is this in which you live, my son!\u201d he said, gazing sadly at the barred windows and the miserable aspect of the chamber. \u201cYou went abroad in search of happiness, but some fatal influence appears to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe mention of my unhappy, murdered friend stirred feelings too violent for my feeble condition; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas! yes, my father,\u201d I answered; \u201ca destiny of the most dreadful sort hangs over me, and I must survive to accomplish it, or I surely would have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to speak together long, since the uncertain state of my health made every measure needed to secure quiet indispensable. Mr. Kirwin entered and declared that my strength must not be spent through excessive exertion. Yet my father\u2019s presence was to me as that of a benevolent angel, and by degrees I regained my health.\\n\\nWhen my illness left me, I became possessed by a dark and dismal melancholy which nothing could drive away. Clerval\u2019s image stood continually before me, pale and murdered. More than once the disturbance caused by such thoughts made my friends fear a perilous return of my sickness. Alas! Why had they saved a life so wretched and hateful? Surely it was that I might carry out my destiny, now nearing its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death will still these pulsations and free me from the enormous burden of grief that presses me into the dust; and, while fulfilling justice\u2019s sentence, I too shall fall into repose. At that time death still seemed far away, though the desire for it never left my mind; and I would often remain for hours silent and unmoving, longing for some vast upheaval that would entomb both me and my destroyer beneath its wreckage.\\n\\nThe time of the assizes drew near. I had already spent three months in prison, and though I remained feeble and constantly liable to relapse, I was compelled to journey almost a hundred miles to the county town where the court sat. Mr. Kirwin undertook every responsibility for gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
