          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8de1283687810d1e6ce9686f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is only fair to the young lady to say that she did not flatly declare she would not go. She merely said she hoped she might be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did; a polite and delicate way of refusing the request, showing that she had the natural good manners which cannot bear to cause another person the pain of a direct refusal.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s face fell. He turned from this young lady, who was brightly\u2014indeed, splendidly\u2014dressed in a red gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other woman.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d said the Jew soothingly, \u201cwhat do you say?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it won\u2019t do; so there\u2019s no use trying it on, Fagin,\u201d Nancy replied.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by that?\u201d said Mr. Sikes, looking up sourly.\\n\\n\u201cWhat I say, Bill,\u201d the lady answered calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re exactly the person for it,\u201d argued Mr. Sikes. \u201cNobody round here knows anything about you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd since I don\u2019t want them to,\u201d Nancy replied just as calmly, \u201cmy answer is more no than yes, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes was right. Through a mixture of threats, promises, and bribes, the lady was finally persuaded to take on the errand. She was not held back by the same concerns as her pleasant friend, for she had only recently moved into the Field Lane neighborhood from the distant but respectable suburb of Ratcliffe. She therefore had less reason to fear being recognized by any of her many acquaintances.\\n\\nSo, with a clean white apron tied over her gown and her curl-papers tucked beneath a straw bonnet\u2014both supplied from the Jew\u2019s endless store\u2014Miss Nancy prepared to set out on her errand.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment,