          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a38ec2f5a3285464bfb73f2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The little man's body was unusually broad for his height, giving his whole figure a highly ridiculous roundness. His feet, naturally, could not be seen at all, though a hard substance of doubtful kind sometimes stuck through a tear in the bottom of the car\u2014or, more properly speaking, the top of the hat. His hands were enormously large. His hair was very gray and gathered into a queue behind him. His nose was remarkably long, crooked, and red; his eyes were large, bright, and sharp; his chin and cheeks, though wrinkled with age, were broad, swollen, and doubled. Yet no trace of ears, of any sort, could be found anywhere on his head. This strange little gentleman wore a loose sky-blue satin coat, with matching tight breeches fastened at the knees by silver buckles. His vest was made of some bright yellow cloth; a white taffeta cap sat jauntily to one side of his head; and to finish his dress, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped his throat and fell neatly over his chest in a fantastically large bow.\\n\\nHaving descended, as I have said, to within about a hundred feet of the ground, the little old gentleman was suddenly overcome with fear and seemed unwilling to come any closer to solid earth. He therefore threw out some sand from a canvas bag, which he lifted with great difficulty, and at once became still. Then, hurried and agitated, he pulled a large morocco-leather pocketbook from a side pocket of his coat. He held it suspiciously in his hand, stared at it with great surprise, and seemed astonished by its weight. At last he opened it and took out a huge letter, sealed with red wax and carefully tied with red tape. He dropped it exactly at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk. His Excellency bent down to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still badly disturbed and apparently having no further business in Rotterdam, now began busily preparing to leave. Since he had to throw off some ballast in order to rise again, he tossed out half a dozen bags, one after another, without bothering to empty them. Every one of them most unfortunately landed on the burgomaster's back, rolling him over and over no fewer than twenty-one times before all Rotterdam. It should not be supposed, however, that the great Underduk allowed such an insult from the little old man to go unpunished.\"}"
