          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b1e9c84f3c5b73ab01b6c0d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To see a star clearly, one should glance at it\u2014to look at it from the side, using the outer parts of the retina, which are more sensitive to faint light than the center. In this way the star appears distinct, and its brightness is best appreciated. That brightness fades in direct proportion as we look straight at it. More rays do enter the eye when we stare directly, but a sideways glance gives a finer power of perception. Thought, too, can be confused and weakened by too much depth; even Venus herself may disappear from the sky under scrutiny that is too long, too fixed, or too direct.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let us make some inquiries ourselves before forming an opinion about them. The investigation will amuse us,\u201d [I thought this a strange word to use, but said nothing] \u201cand besides, Le Bon once did me a service for which I am not ungrateful. We shall visit the premises with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and will have no trouble getting the necessary permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was obtained, and we went at once to the Rue Morgue. It was one of those wretched streets lying between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We arrived late in the afternoon, since this district was far from the one where we lived. We found the house easily, for many people still stood across the street, staring with idle curiosity at its closed shutters. It was an ordinary Paris house, with a gateway and, on one side, a glass watch-box with a sliding window panel, showing it to be a concierge\u2019s lodge. Before entering, we walked up the street, turned down an alley, then turned again and passed behind the building. All the while, Dupin examined the neighborhood and the house itself with a careful attention whose purpose I could not imagine.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps to the front of the house, rang, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the officers in charge. We went upstairs to the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, and where both dead women still lay. As usual, the room had been left in its disordered state. I saw nothing beyond what had been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin examined everything closely, including the victims\u2019 bodies. Then we went through the other rooms and into the yard, accompanied throughout by a gendarme.\"}"
