          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"26963de4a1b9f7f5562d7984\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The sole question that appeared to perplex him was whom he should put out of the way; for he was not unaware that murder, like the religions of the pagan world, demands a victim no less than a priest. Being no genius, he had no enemies, and he felt, moreover, that this was scarcely the moment to indulge any private resentment or aversion, since the task upon which he had entered was one of deep and serious solemnity. He therefore drew up, on a sheet of note-paper, a list of his friends and relations, and, after much reflection, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady living in Curzon Street, who was his second cousin on his mother\u2019s side. He had always been greatly attached to Lady Clem, as everybody called her, and, being himself very rich, having inherited all Lord Rugby\u2019s estate on coming of age, he could gain no vulgar pecuniary benefit from her death. Indeed, the longer he considered it, the more perfectly suited she seemed to the purpose; and, thinking that delay would be unjust to Sybil, he resolved to make his preparations immediately.\\n\\nThe first matter, naturally, was to pay the cheiromantist; accordingly he seated himself at a little Sheraton writing-table by the window, wrote a cheque for \u00a3105 to the order of Mr. Septimus Podgers, and, placing it in an envelope, instructed his valet to carry it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables for his hansom, and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton\u2019s photograph, and vowed that, whatever happened, he would never allow her to learn what he was doing for her, but would conceal the secret of his self-sacrifice for ever in his heart.\\n\\nOn his way to the Buckingham, he paused at a florist\u2019s and sent Sybil a handsome basket of narcissus, with their lovely white petals and staring pheasants\u2019 eyes. Reaching the club, he went directly to the library, rang for the waiter, and ordered a lemon-and-soda and a book upon Toxicology. He had quite determined that poison was the best method for this awkward affair. Any form of personal violence was intensely repugnant to him; and besides, he was most anxious not to kill Lady Clementina in a manner likely to draw public notice, for he detested the prospect of being lionised at Lady Windermere\u2019s, or of seeing his name appear in the paragraphs of vulgar society newspapers.\"}"
