ied Uncle Pumblechook. \u201cWell said! Neatly put! Very good indeed! Now, Joseph, you understand the case.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d said my sister, maintaining her reproachful manner, while Joe apologetically passed the back of his hand repeatedly across his nose, \u201cyou do not yet\u2014though you may suppose you do\u2014understand the case. You may imagine that you do, but you do not, Joseph. For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing that, for anything we can say, this boy\u2019s fortune may be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to carry him into town to-night in his own chaise-cart, to keep him there to-night, and to bring him with his own hands to Miss Havisham\u2019s to-morrow morning. And Lor-a-mussy me!\u201d cried my sister, flinging off her bonnet in sudden despair, \u201chere am I talking to mere Mooncalfs, while Uncle Pumblechook waits, and the mare takes cold at the door, and the boy is begrimed with crock and dirt from the crown of his head to the sole of his foot!\u201d\"}"
