          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f837bc55171bada18ad320e0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I summon you, spirits of the departed, and you, roving agents of vengeance, to assist and guide me in my undertaking. Let the accursed, infernal monster drain the cup of suffering; let him know the despair that now afflicts me.\u201d\\n\\nI had commenced my invocation with a solemnity and dread that nearly persuaded me the ghosts of my murdered friends listened to and sanctioned my devotion; but as I ended, the furies took hold of me, and fury strangled my voice.\\n\\nThe silence of the night returned me a loud, demoniacal laugh. It sounded in my ears, prolonged and oppressive; the mountains repeated it, and I seemed encompassed by all hell, jeering and laughing at me. In that instant I surely would have yielded to madness and ended my wretched life, had not my oath been heard and I been preserved for revenge. The laughter faded, and then a familiar, detested voice, seeming to speak beside my ear, said in an audible whisper, \u201cI am content, unhappy wretch! You have resolved to live, and I am content.\u201d\\n\\nI sprang toward the place whence the sound had come, but the fiend slipped beyond my reach. At once the wide orb of the moon rose and cast its full light upon his hideous, misshapen form as he fled with speed beyond that of man.\\n\\nI chased him, and for many months this has been my occupation. Led by a faint trace, I followed the course of the Rhone, though without success. The blue Mediterranean came into view, and, by a singular accident, I saw the fiend enter at night and conceal himself aboard a ship sailing for the Black Sea. I secured passage on that same vessel, but he escaped me, by what means I cannot tell.\\n\\nThrough the wastes of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to avoid me, I have always pursued his trail. At times peasants, terrified by that dreadful apparition, told me which way he had gone; at others he himself, fearing that, if I lost every sign of him, I would despair and perish, left some token to direct me. Snow fell upon my head, and I beheld the impression of his enormous foot upon the white expanse. How can you, just entering life, to whom anxiety is unfamiliar and anguish unknown, comprehend what I have endured and yet endure? Cold, hunger, and weariness were the smallest sufferings appointed to me; some devil had cursed me, and I bore my everlasting hell within me. Yet a benevolent spirit still attended and led my steps, and, when I complained most bitterly, would suddenly free me from difficulties that appeared impossible to overcome. Sometimes, when nature, subdued by hunger, gave way beneath exhaustion, a meal was set before me in the wilderness, restoring my strength and courage.\"}"
