          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5bcf6923ee0e1d7a53c896bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His face, badly sunburned, was more than half concealed by whiskers and a mustache. He carried a huge oak club, but seemed otherwise unarmed. He bowed awkwardly and wished us \u201cgood evening\u201d in French accents which, though somewhat Neufch\u00e2tel-like, still clearly suggested a Parisian origin.\\n\\n\u201cSit down, my friend,\u201d said Dupin. \u201cI assume you have come about the orangutan. Upon my word, I almost envy you for owning him; he is a remarkably fine animal, and no doubt a very valuable one. How old do you think he is?\u201d\\n\\nThe sailor took a long breath, like a man released from some unbearable burden, then answered in a confident voice:\\n\\n\u201cI have no way of knowing\u2014but he cannot be more than four or five years old. Do you have him here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no, we have no place to keep him here. He is at a livery stable on the Rue Dubourg, nearby. You can collect him in the morning. Naturally, you are prepared to identify the property?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I am, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall be sorry to give him up,\u201d said Dupin.\\n\\n\u201cI do not mean for you to have gone to all this trouble for nothing, sir,\u201d said the man. \u201cI would not expect that. I am quite willing to pay a reward for finding the animal\u2014that is, anything reasonable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cthat is perfectly fair. Let me think\u2014what shall I ask? Ah, I will tell you. My reward will be this: you will give me every bit of information you possess about the murders in the Rue Morgue.\u201d\\n\\nDupin spoke those last words very softly and calmly. Just as calmly, he walked to the door, locked it, and put the key in his pocket. Then he drew a pistol from his coat and, without the slightest agitation, laid it on the table.\\n\\nThe sailor\u2019s face reddened as though he were fighting for breath. He sprang to his feet and seized his club, but the next instant sank back into his chair, trembling violently, with the face of a dead man. He said nothing. I pitied him from the depths of my heart.\\n\\n\u201cMy friend,\u201d said Dupin kindly, \u201cyou are frightening yourself without cause\u2014you truly are. We mean you no harm at all. I give you my word, as a gentleman and a Frenchman, that we intend no injury to you. I know perfectly well that you are innocent of the horrors in the Rue Morgue. Still, it is useless to deny that you are involved in them to some extent.\u201d\"}"
