          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f88f369bb22a9ab2d0fc558c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at a star indirectly\u2014to see it from the corner of the eye, by turning toward it the outer parts of the retina (which are more sensitive to faint impressions of light than the center)\u2014is to see the star clearly, to appreciate its brilliance most fully. That brilliance fades in exact proportion as we direct our gaze fully upon it. In the latter case, more rays actually strike the eye; but in the former, the capacity for perception is finer. Excessive depth confuses and weakens thought; and it is possible to make Venus herself disappear from the sky by examining her too steadily, too intently, or too directly.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let us make some inquiries ourselves before forming an opinion about them. An investigation will amuse us,\u201d [I thought this a strange word to use in such a case, but said nothing] \u201cand besides, Le Bon once did me a service for which I am not ungrateful. We will go and inspect the premises with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and will have no trouble obtaining the necessary permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was secured, and we went at once to the Rue Morgue. It is one of those wretched streets lying between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. It was late afternoon when we arrived, for this district was far from the one in which we lived. We found the house easily, since many people still stood across the street, staring up at the shuttered windows with aimless curiosity. It was an ordinary Parisian house, with a gateway; beside it stood a glass-fronted watch-box, its window fitted with a sliding panel, marking it as a concierge\u2019s lodge. Before entering, we walked up the street, turned into an alley, then turned again and passed behind the building\u2014while Dupin examined the entire neighborhood, as well as the house itself, with a minuteness of attention whose purpose I could not imagine.\\n\\nRetracing our route, we returned to the front of the house, rang, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the officers in charge. We went upstairs into the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, and where both dead women still lay. As usual, the disorder of the room had been left untouched. I saw nothing beyond what had been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin examined everything closely, including the bodies of the victims. We then entered the other rooms and the yard, accompanied throughout by a gendarme.\"}"
