          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d87e5e3e18b01be7d0872c5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her position was indeed one of unusual trial and difficulty. Though she felt an eager, burning desire to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s history, she could not betray the confidence the miserable woman she had just spoken with had placed in her, a young and innocent girl. The woman\u2019s words and manner had touched Rose Maylie\u2019s heart; and, joined with her love for her young charge, and scarcely less strong in its sincerity and warmth, was her earnest wish to bring the outcast back to repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to remain in London for only three days before leaving for several weeks at a distant part of the coast. It was now midnight on the first day. What action could she decide upon that might be carried out within forty-eight hours? Or how could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was staying with them and would remain for the next two days; but Rose knew the excellent gentleman\u2019s impulsive nature too well, and saw too clearly the anger with which, in his first burst of indignation, he would view the person responsible for Oliver\u2019s recapture, to entrust him with the secret when no experienced person could support her pleas on the girl\u2019s behalf. These were all reasons for the utmost caution and discretion in telling Mrs. Maylie, whose first impulse would certainly be to consult the worthy doctor about it. As for seeking legal advice, even if she had known how, that was scarcely possible for the same reason. At one point she thought of asking Harry for help; but this brought back the memory of their last farewell, and it seemed unworthy to summon him back when\u2014the tears rose to her eyes as she followed the thought\u2014he might by now have learned to forget her and be happier apart.\\n\\nTroubled by these conflicting thoughts, leaning first toward one course and then another, then shrinking from them all as each new consideration came to mind, Rose spent a sleepless, anxious night. After further reflection the next day, she came to the desperate decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it is painful for him,\u201d she thought, \u201cto return here, how painful it will be for me! But perhaps he will not come; he may write, or he may come himself and carefully avoid meeting me\u2014he did so when he left. I scarcely expected that he would, but it was better for us both.\u201d Here Rose laid down her pen and turned away, as if even the paper that was to carry her message must not see her weep.\"}"
