          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cfdc59ba4e5ed094fcc5f92a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, known as Darnay,\u201d was finally brought to trial.\\n\\nHis judges sat on the Bench wearing feathered hats, though rough red caps and tricoloured cockades were the usual dress elsewhere. Looking at the Jury and the wild crowd, he might have believed that the natural order had been turned upside down, and that criminals were judging honest men. The lowest, most cruel, and worst part of the city\u2019s population\u2014though every city has some such people\u2014controlled the scene. They shouted comments, approval, and disapproval, guessed at the verdict, and pushed it forward without restraint. Most of the men carried weapons of one kind or another. Some women wore knives or daggers; some ate and drank while they watched; many knitted. Among the knitters was a woman working with an extra piece of knitting tucked beneath her arm. She sat in the front row beside a man Darnay had not seen since arriving at the Barrier, but whom he immediately recognized as Defarge. Once or twice she whispered in his ear, and she appeared to be his wife. Yet what Darnay noticed most was that, though they sat as near to him as possible, neither of them ever looked at him. They seemed to be waiting stubbornly for something, watching the Jury and nothing else. Below the President sat Doctor Manette, dressed as quietly as usual. As far as the prisoner could tell, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there unconnected with the Tribunal who still wore their ordinary clothes instead of the rough Carmagnole dress.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was charged by the public prosecutor as an emigrant whose life was owed to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants under penalty of death. It did not matter that the decree had been dated after his return to France. He was there, and so was the decree; he had been arrested in France, and the crowd demanded his head.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d cried the audience. \u201cAn enemy of the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence them and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived in England for many years.\\n\\nIt certainly was true.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? What else could he call himself?\\n\\nHe hoped he was not an emigrant in the meaning and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President asked.\\n\\nBecause he had willingly given up a title he disliked and a position he disliked, and had left his country\u2014before, he submitted, the word emigrant had acquired the meaning now used by the Tribunal\u2014to earn his own living in England rather than live on the labour of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
