          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b88481a98b0bcaf52ca34500\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My heart was pounding, and I felt afraid without knowing why. I sent the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were its words.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child,\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your trust. I have tried to corrupt your mind and have made your innocent heart aware of the looks and words of forbidden, monstrous passion. I must pay for these crimes and try, in some measure, to make my punishment fit my guilt. You are, I do not doubt, prepared for what I am about to tell you: we must part and be separated forever.\\n\\n\u201cI take from you your parent and only friend. You are thrown out into the world without shelter; your hopes are ruined; the peace and safety of your pure mind are destroyed. Memory will bring you terrible images of guilt and the pain of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who bring all this misery upon you; I, who cast you out and cruelly set the mark of distrust and agony upon the heart and face of my own child; I, who with devilish carelessness tried to steal away her beauty and replace it with the ugly deformity of sin\u2014I, in the overflowing anguish of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must, and do, hate me. But forgive me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts follow me into exile with unending anger. I must never see you again, never hear your voice again; but the gentle whisper of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my troubled mind and heart. I am sure I would feel it even in my grave. And I dare support this request by telling you how miserably I was drawn into this trap of fiery anguish, and of all my struggles to escape it. Indeed, if your soul were less pure and bright, I would not try to excuse myself to you. I would fear that, if I led you to regard me with less horror, you might hate vice less. But when I speak to you, I feel as though I appeal to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must try to win it, or I must despair. I beg you, therefore, to listen to my words; and if guilt can in any degree be softened by severe suffering and remorse that tears the mind like madness, perhaps you may think\u2014though I do not dare\u2014that I have some claim upon your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI beg you to remember our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from sixteen years of weary wandering, during which, though I had faced many dangers and misfortunes, my affections had remained entirely blank.\"}"
