          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eefec0e65818bcf211b4b5df\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father may let you stay, of course, if your mother can spare you. Daughters are never as important to a father. And if you stay another full month, I could take one of you as far as London, since I am going there for a week early in June. Dawson does not mind riding on the barouche-box, so there would be plenty of room for one of you. Indeed, if the weather is cool, I should not mind taking both of you, since neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind, madam, but I think we must keep to our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared to accept this. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always say exactly what I think, and I cannot bear the thought of two young women travelling by post alone. It is highly improper. You must find someone to send. I dislike that sort of thing more than anything. Young women should always be properly protected and attended, according to their position in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted that two male servants go with her. Miss Darcy, daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not properly have travelled in any other way. I pay great attention to such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad I thought to mention it, for it would truly reflect badly on you if you let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is sending a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant, does he? I am very glad that someone thinks of such things. Where will you change horses? Oh, at Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, they will take good care of you.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions about their journey; and because she did not answer all of them herself, Elizabeth had to pay attention. Elizabeth thought this fortunate, for with her mind so occupied, she might otherwise have forgotten where she was. She had to save reflection for the hours when she was alone. Whenever she was by herself, she gave herself up to it as her greatest comfort; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, when she could enjoy all the pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nShe was soon on the way to knowing Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She examined every sentence, and her feelings toward its writer often changed greatly. When she remembered the manner in which he had spoken to her, she was still filled with anger; but when she considered how unfairly she had judged and reproached him, her anger turned against herself, and his disappointed feelings became an object of compassion.\"}"
