          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"663c2e66ed5c2e66ed5cba38d17dc0bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by their respective parties to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter; they had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and a desolated land, cherishing meanwhile a fierce hatred of one another; and now they urged their separate claims with unabated party zeal.\\n\\nBy questioning the deputies separately, and after long inquiry, we discovered the real condition of affairs at Paris. Ever since parliament had named him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had yielded obedience to Adrian. He was our captain, to conduct us from our native land to regions unknown; our legislator and our guardian. When our plan of emigration was first arranged, no lasting division among our members had been intended, and the authority over the entire body, rising by degrees, culminated in the Earl of Windsor. Yet unforeseen events altered our designs, and caused the larger portion of our people to be separated from their supreme leader for nearly two months. They had crossed in two distinct companies; and, upon reaching Paris, discord sprang up between them.\\n\\nThey found Paris deserted. When the plague first showed itself, returning travellers and merchants, together with letters, kept us regularly informed of the havoc wrought by disease upon the continent. But as mortality increased, this intercourse diminished and at last ceased. Even within England, communication between one part of the island and another became tardy and infrequent. No ship crossed the waters dividing Calais from Dover; or, if some sorrowful voyager, eager to learn whether his kindred lived or died, set out from the French coast to return to us, the ravenous sea often engulfed his frail vessel, or, after a day or two, he caught the disease and died before he could relate the tale of France\u2019s desolation. Thus we remained, in great measure, ignorant of the continent\u2019s condition, and retained some indistinct hope of finding many companions across its broad expanse. But the same causes which had so dreadfully reduced the English nation had found still wider room for destruction in the sister country. France was a void; along the long road from Calais to Paris, not a single human being appeared. In Paris there were a few\u2014perhaps a hundred\u2014who, resigned to their approaching doom, wandered through the capital\u2019s streets and gathered to speak of former days, with that liveliness, even gaiety, which rarely abandons the people of that nation.\\n\\nThe English entered into undisputed possession of Paris. Its lofty houses and confined streets were without life. A few wan figures could be seen at the familiar gathering-place in the Tuileries; they marvelled why the islanders had come near their doomed city\u2014for in the extremity of misery, sufferers always suppose their own share of calamity the most bitter, just as, under acute pain, we would trade the particular torment beneath which we writhe for any other that might afflict another part of the body.\"}"
