          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"429d2707eb1beaf5405efd55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities spoken of as analytical are, in themselves, scarcely open to analysis. We estimate them only through their effects. We know, among other things, that, when possessed in excess, they are ever to their owner a source of the keenest pleasure. As the powerful man rejoices in his bodily strength, taking delight in exercises that summon his muscles to action, so does the analyst glory in that moral activity which _unravels._ He finds enjoyment even in the most insignificant pursuits that bring his faculty into exercise. He delights in riddles, conundrums, and hieroglyphics; displaying in his answers to each a degree of _acumen_ that seems to ordinary understanding pr\u00e6ternatural. His conclusions, produced by the very spirit and essence of method, possess, indeed, all the appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe power of re-solution may perhaps be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, and particularly by that loftiest division of it which, unjustly, and solely because of its backward operations, has been termed, as though _par excellence_, analysis. But calculation is not, of itself, analysis. A chess-player, for instance, performs the former without striving after the latter. Hence the influence of chess upon mental character is seriously misconceived. I am not composing a treatise, but merely introducing a somewhat singular narrative with observations thrown out at random; I shall therefore venture to maintain that the higher faculties of the reflective mind are more distinctly and more profitably exercised by the unobtrusive game of draughts than by all the elaborate trifling of chess. In the latter game, where the pieces move in differing and _bizarre_ ways, and bear diverse and shifting values, mere complexity is mistaken\u2014an error by no means uncommon\u2014for profundity. The _attention_ is there strongly summoned into action. Should it falter for a moment, an oversight occurs, bringing loss or defeat. Since the possible moves are not merely numerous but involute, the opportunities for such errors are increased; and in nine instances out of ten, it is the player of greater concentration, rather than greater acuteness, who prevails. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are _unique_ and admit little variation, the likelihood of inadvertence is reduced; and, attention being comparatively unoccupied, whatever advantages either player gains are gained through superior _acumen_. To make this less abstract, suppose a game of draughts in which the pieces have been reduced to four kings, and in which, naturally, no oversight need be anticipated. It is plain that victory here can be determined, the players being at all equal, only by some _recherch\u00e9_ move, born of a vigorous exertion of intellect.\"}"
