          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"16ac821909c312240645788b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, known as Darnay,\u201d was finally brought to trial.\\n\\nHis judges occupied the Bench in plumed hats; but the coarse red cap and tricoloured cockade were otherwise the prevailing head-dress. As he surveyed the Jury and the stormy crowd, he might have imagined the common order of affairs turned upside down, and that criminals were judging honest men. The meanest, most savage, and vilest rabble of a city\u2014never lacking its share of the mean, savage, and vile\u2014were the ruling powers in the spectacle: loudly remarking, cheering, condemning, foretelling, and hurrying on the issue, without restraint. Most of the men bore weapons of one kind or another; among the women, some had knives, some daggers, some ate and drank while they watched, and many knitted. Of the latter was one woman, working with an extra piece of knitting tucked beneath her arm. She sat in the foremost row beside a man whom he had not seen since reaching the Barrier, but whom he at once recognized as Defarge. He observed that she whispered to him once or twice, and appeared to be his wife; yet what struck him most in the pair was that, though stationed as near him as possible, they never turned their eyes upon him. They appeared to await something with stubborn resolve, looking at the Jury and nowhere else. Beneath the President sat Doctor Manette, in his customary quiet attire. So far as the prisoner could discern, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men present, apart from the Tribunal, who retained their ordinary dress and had not put on the rough costume of the Carmagnole.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was charged by the public prosecutor as an emigrant, whose life had been forfeited to the Republic under the decree banishing all emigrants under penalty of Death. It mattered nothing that the decree was dated after his return to France. He stood there, and there stood the decree; he had been seized in France, and his head was required.\\n\\n\u201cOff with his head!\u201d shouted the audience. \u201cA foe of the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to quiet these cries, and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had resided for many years in England.\\n\\nIt was unquestionably true.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? By what name did he describe himself?\\n\\nHe trusted that he was not an emigrant in the meaning and intention of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? demanded the President.\\n\\nBecause he had freely surrendered a title offensive to him, and a rank equally offensive, and had quitted his country\u2014he submitted, before the word emigrant had acquired the present meaning assigned to it by the Tribunal\u2014to earn his living by his own labour in England, rather than by the labour of the overburdened people of France.\"}"
