{"request_id":"8e75237af8a989b06dcb404d","rewritten_text":"You see, I had put my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things; but it was one of those helmets that a man cannot remove without help. That had never entered my mind when I stowed it there; indeed, I did not know it. I had thought it would be especially handy in that place. And now the knowledge that it was there, so near and convenient, yet beyond reach, made the matter worse and harder to endure. Yes, the thing you cannot get is chiefly the thing you want; everybody has observed that. Well, it drove everything else out of my thoughts; drove it entirely out, and fixed them on my helmet; and mile after mile they remained there, thinking of the handkerchief, seeing the handkerchief in fancy; and it was cruel and exasperating to have the salty sweat continually running into my eyes while I could not reach it. It sounds like a small matter when written down, but it was no small matter whatever; it was misery of the most substantial sort. I would not say so if it were not true. I resolved that next time I would take along a reticule, however it looked and whatever people might say. Naturally, these iron dudes of the Round Table would call it scandalous, and perhaps raise Sheol over it, but for my part, give me comfort first and fashion afterward. So we plodded on, and from time to time came upon a patch of dust that rose in clouds and got into my nose, making me sneeze and weep; and naturally I said things I should not have said—I do not deny it. I am no better than other people.

We appeared unable to encounter anybody in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the temper I was then in, that was fortunate for the ogre—an ogre, that is, who had a handkerchief. Most knights would have cared only about taking his armor; but if I got his bandanna, he might keep his hardware, so far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile it was growing hotter and hotter inside there. The sun, you see, kept beating down and heating the iron more and more. Well, when you are hot in that fashion, every trifling thing vexes you. When I trotted, I clattered like a crate of crockery, and that irritated me; besides, I could not endure that shield flapping and thumping, now against my chest, now about my back; and if I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and squealed in the tiresome manner of a wheelbarrow. Since we made no breeze at that pace, I was likely to be roasted in that stove; and, furthermore, the more quietly you moved, the more heavily the iron seemed to settle upon you, and the more tons you appeared to weigh with every passing minute."}

