          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"144c89cd202e3dfc9e3be40e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at him, one might have thought Nemesis had taken Pallas\u2019s shield and held before him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He appeared petrified, his countenance marble-like in its melancholy. He had known the refined and sumptuous existence of a young man of rank and wealth, an existence made exquisite by its freedom from vulgar anxieties and its graceful boyish carelessness; and now, for the first time, he understood the dreadful mystery of Destiny, the fearful significance of Doom.\\n\\nHow insane and hideous it all appeared! Could it be that upon his hand, traced in signs he himself could not interpret but another might read, there stood some terrible secret of sin, some crimson mark of guilt? Was there no possible way of escape? Were we merely chess-pieces, shifted by an invisible hand, clay vessels shaped by the potter\u2019s whim, for glory or disgrace? His reason rebelled against the thought, yet he felt some tragedy poised above him, and that he had been summoned without warning to carry an unbearable load. Actors are indeed fortunate. They may decide whether to play in tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or be merry, to laugh or weep. In actual life it is otherwise. Most men and women must enact roles for which they are wholly unfit. Our Guildensterns play Hamlet, while our Hamlets must make jests like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, but the casting is poor.\\n\\nAt that instant Mr. Podgers came into the room. At sight of Lord Arthur he recoiled, and his gross, fleshy face assumed a curious greenish-yellow hue. Their eyes encountered one another, and for a moment neither spoke.\\n\\n\u2018The Duchess has forgotten one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and has sent me to take it to her,\u2019 said Mr. Podgers at last. \u2018Ah, there it is upon the sofa! Good evening.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Mr. Podgers, I must require you to give me a plain answer to a question I intend to ask.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018At another time, Lord Arthur; the Duchess is waiting. I fear I must leave.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You will not leave. The Duchess is not in such haste.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ladies ought not to be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u2019 Mr. Podgers replied, with his unhealthy smile. \u2018The fair sex is inclined to impatience.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s delicately chiselled lips curved with impatient contempt. The poor Duchess seemed of scant consequence to him then. He crossed the room to Mr. Podgers, where he stood, and extended his hand.\\n\\n\u2018Tell me what you saw in it,\u2019 he said. \u2018Tell me the truth. I must know. I am no child.\u2019\"}"
