          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d448404cb542fd7fd88e243a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNever!\u201d answered the girl.\\n\\n\u201cTell me the reason?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor one reason,\u201d the girl replied resolutely, \u201cone reason which the lady knows, and in which she will support me\u2014I am sure she will, for she has promised me: and for another reason too, that though he has led a wicked life, I have led a wicked life as well; many of us have gone on in the same paths together, and I will not betray them, when any one of them might have betrayed me, but never did, bad as they may be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d said the gentleman hastily, as though this were the very point he had sought to reach, \u201cplace Monks in my hands, and let me deal with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat if he betrays the rest?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI give you my word that, should that happen, if the truth is wrung from him, the matter shall end there; there must be things in Oliver\u2019s early history which it would be cruel to expose to the public, and once the truth is brought out, they shall escape free.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if it is not?\u201d the girl asked.\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d continued the gentleman, \u201cthis Fagin shall not be delivered up to justice without your consent. In that event, I think I could give you reasons that would persuade you to grant it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo I have the lady\u2019s promise of that?\u201d asked the girl.\\n\\n\u201cYou do,\u201d said Rose. \u201cMy honest and faithful word.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMonks would never find out how you came to know what you know?\u201d said the girl, after a brief pause.\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d answered the gentleman. \u201cThe knowledge should be used against him in such a way that he could not even suspect it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have been a liar, and have lived among liars since I was a small child,\u201d said the girl, after another silence, \u201cbut I will trust your words.\u201d\\n\\nWhen both had assured her that she could safely trust them, she went on, in a voice so quiet that the listener often found it hard even to catch the meaning of her speech, to describe by name and position the public-house from which she had been followed that evening. From her occasional pauses, it seemed that the gentleman was taking hurried notes of the details she gave. Having fully explained the situation of the place, the best spot from which it might be watched without attracting notice, and the night and hour when Monks most commonly visited it, she paused for several moments, apparently trying to call his face and figure more clearly to mind.\\n\\n\u201cHe is tall,\u201d said the girl, \u201cand powerfully built, though not heavy; he walks in a stealthy way, and while walking is always looking back over his shoulder, first to one side and then to the other.\u201d\"}"
