{"request_id":"1db80e6e7616486186606628","rewritten_text":"The next day he did not leave the house and, in fact, spent most of his time in his own room, sick with a frantic fear of death, yet indifferent to life itself. The awareness of being hunted, trapped, and tracked down had begun to rule him. If the tapestry so much as stirred in the wind, he trembled. The dead leaves blown against the leaded windows seemed like his own wasted resolutions and desperate regrets. When he shut his eyes, he saw again the sailor’s face looking through the mist-stained glass, and horror seemed once more to place its hand upon his heart.

But perhaps it had only been his imagination that had summoned vengeance from the night and set those hideous forms of punishment before him. Real life was chaos, but imagination possessed a dreadful logic. It was imagination that made remorse follow in sin’s footsteps. It was imagination that made every crime produce its deformed offspring. In the ordinary world of fact, the wicked were not punished, nor were the good rewarded. Success went to the strong; failure was forced upon the weak. That was all. Besides, if any stranger had been lurking around the house, the servants or gamekeepers would have seen him. If footprints had been found in the flower-beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes, it had been nothing but imagination. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to kill him. He had sailed away in his ship to sink in some winter sea. From him, at least, he was safe. Why, the man did not know who he was; he could not know who he was. The mask of youth had protected him.

And yet, if it had only been an illusion, how terrible it was to think that conscience could summon such dreadful phantoms, give them visible shape, and make them move before one’s eyes! What kind of life would his be if, day and night, the shadows of his crime stared at him from silent corners, mocked him from hidden places, whispered in his ear as he sat at a feast, and woke him with icy fingers while he slept? As the thought worked its way through his mind, he turned pale with terror, and the air seemed suddenly colder. Oh, in what a mad hour had he killed his friend! How ghastly even the memory of that scene was! He saw it all again. Every horrible detail returned with greater horror. From time’s black cave, terrible and wrapped in scarlet, rose the image of his sin."}

