          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abc470233dfe3182bb9a094d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, met Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay. The four knights charged fiercely and shattered their spears in their hands. Then Sir Pertolope rode out from the castle and met Sir Lionel; there Sir Pertolope, the Green Knight, struck down Sir Lionel, brother to Sir Launcelot. Noble heralds watched all this and judged him the best, recording their names. Then Sir Bleobaris broke his spear upon Sir Gareth, but from that blow Sir Bleobaris fell to the ground. When Sir Galihodin saw it, he told Sir Gareth to guard himself, and Sir Gareth struck him to the earth. Then Sir Galihud took a spear to avenge his brother, and Sir Gareth treated him in the same manner; likewise Sir Dinadan and his brother La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore le Disirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage. He bore all these down with a single spear.\\n\\nWhen King Aswisance of Ireland saw how Sir Gareth rode, he marvelled who he could be, for at one time he seemed green, and at another, when he returned, he seemed blue. Thus with every course he rode back and forth, he changed his color, so that neither king nor knight could recognize him at once. Then King Agwisance of Ireland met Sir Gareth, and Sir Gareth struck him from his horse, saddle and all. Next came King Carados of Scotland, and Sir Gareth struck down both horse and man. He served King Uriens of the land of Gore in the same way. Then Sir Bagdemagus entered, and Sir Gareth struck horse and man down to the earth. Bagdemagus's son Meliganus broke a spear upon Sir Gareth with great force and skill.\\n\\nThen Sir Galahault, the noble prince, cried aloud, \u201cKnight of many colors, you have jousted well. Now prepare yourself, so that I may joust with you.\u201d Sir Gareth heard him, took up a great spear, and they charged together. The prince broke his spear, but Sir Gareth struck him on the left side of his helm, so that he reeled this way and that; he would have fallen had his men not caught him. \u201cTruly,\u201d said King Arthur, \u201cthat knight of many colors is a good knight.\u201d Therefore the king called Sir Launcelot to him and asked him to meet that knight.\\n\\n\u201cSir,\u201d said Launcelot, \u201cI would rather spare him at this time, for he has labored enough today. When a good knight has done so well on any day, it is not another good knight's part to deprive him of his honor, especially when he sees that knight has performed such great deeds. Perhaps,\u201d said Sir Launcelot, \u201chis cause is here today, and perhaps he is best loved by this lady of all who are here. For I can see plainly that he strains and exerts himself to accomplish great deeds. Therefore, as for me, he shall have the honor today; even if it were in my power to take it from him, I would not.\u201d\"}"
