{"request_id":"bcb80b39c89c064334eaa002","rewritten_text":"The following morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they spoke at considerable length about the ghost. The United States Minister was, naturally, somewhat vexed to discover that his gift had been refused. ‘I have no desire,’ he said, ‘to inflict any personal harm upon the ghost, and I must observe that, considering how long he has lived in the house, it is hardly courteous to throw pillows at him’—a most reasonable remark, at which, I regret to say, the twins broke into loud laughter. ‘On the other hand,’ he went on, ‘if he positively refuses to employ the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall be obliged to remove his chains. It would be utterly impossible to sleep while such a racket is being made outside the bedrooms.’

For the remainder of the week, however, they were left in peace, the sole matter that attracted notice being the constant reappearance of the blood-stain upon the library floor. This was certainly most peculiar, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and kept the windows securely fastened. The stain’s chameleon-like changes of colour also provoked much discussion. On some mornings it was a dark, almost Indian red; then it became vermilion; then a deep purple; and once, when they came downstairs for family prayers according to the plain observances of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they discovered it to be a vivid emerald green. These kaleidoscopic alterations naturally entertained the family greatly, and wagers about them were freely laid each evening. The only one who took no part in the amusement was little Virginia, who, for some unaccountable reason, was always deeply troubled by the sight of the blood-stain, and nearly wept on the morning when it appeared emerald green.

The ghost made his second appearance on Sunday night. Not long after the family had retired, they were abruptly startled by a dreadful crash in the hall. Hurrying downstairs, they saw that a great suit of ancient armour had slipped from its stand and fallen upon the stone floor, while the Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense suffering. The twins, who had brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him, with that precision of aim obtainable only through long and diligent practice upon a writing-master; meanwhile the United States Minister covered him with his revolver and, in accordance with Californian etiquette, ordered him to hold up his hands. With a fierce shriek of anger, the ghost sprang up and passed through them like mist, putting out Washington Otis’s candle as he went, and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

