          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f9a33e4c5ddfb57d78a88701\",\"rewritten_text\":\"WHEN Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, purchased Canterville Chase, everyone assured him that he was acting very foolishly, for there could be no question that the house was haunted. Lord Canterville himself, indeed, a man of the strictest honour, had considered it his duty to tell Mr. Otis so when they began to settle the terms.\\n\\n\u2018We have not chosen to live there ourselves,\u2019 said Lord Canterville, \u2018ever since my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a fit, from which she never wholly recovered, when two skeleton hands were laid upon her shoulders as she dressed for dinner; and I feel obliged to inform you, Mr. Otis, that the ghost has been seen by several living members of my family, and also by the rector of the parish, the Rev. Augustus Dampier, a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the Duchess\u2019s unhappy accident, none of our younger servants would remain with us, and Lady Canterville frequently had scarcely any sleep at night because of the strange sounds that issued from the corridor and the library.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My Lord,\u2019 replied the Minister, \u2018I shall take the furniture and the ghost at a valuation. I come from a modern country, where we possess everything money can purchase; and with all our lively young fellows painting the Old World red, and carrying away your finest actresses and prima-donnas, I reckon that, if such a thing as a ghost existed in Europe, we should have one at home very soon, either in one of our public museums or travelling the roads as a show.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I am afraid the ghost does exist,\u2019 said Lord Canterville, smiling, \u2018although it may have resisted the offers of your enterprising impresarios. It has been known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and invariably appears before the death of any member of our family.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, the family doctor does that too, Lord Canterville. But there is no such thing, sir, as a ghost, and I suppose the laws of Nature are not likely to be suspended for the British aristocracy.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You are certainly very natural in America,\u2019 returned Lord Canterville, who did not entirely grasp Mr. Otis\u2019s final remark, \u2018and if a ghost in the house does not trouble you, it is all right. Only remember that I warned you.\u2019\\n\\nA few weeks later the sale was completed, and when the season ended the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who as Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street had been a celebrated New York belle, was now a very handsome woman of middle age, with fine eyes and a superb profile.\"}"
