{"request_id":"2bf231d931ecf0be7c7c934d","rewritten_text":"It then showed that I was at an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, and so at that moment I could survey no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth’s whole surface. At nine o’clock I again lost sight of land to the east, though not before I noticed that the balloon was drifting rapidly toward the north-northwest. The curve of the ocean beneath me was very clear, although my view was often blocked by masses of cloud floating back and forth. I now observed that even the lightest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers out through the valve. Instead of floating as I expected, they fell straight down like a bullet, all together and with tremendous speed, vanishing from sight within a few seconds. At first I could make nothing of this strange event, for I could not believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly increased so enormously. But I soon realized that the air was now too thin to support even the feathers; that they truly were falling as swiftly as they seemed; and that I had been startled by the combined speed of their fall and my own rise.

“By ten o’clock I found I had little that needed my immediate attention. Everything was going smoothly, and I believed the balloon was rising at a speed that increased from moment to moment, though I no longer had any way to measure that increase. I felt no pain or discomfort of any kind, and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself sometimes by examining my various instruments, and sometimes by renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to do this every forty minutes, more to protect my health than because such frequent renewal was truly necessary. Meanwhile, I could not help looking ahead in my thoughts. My fancy delighted in the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. My imagination, free for once from all restraint, wandered where it pleased among the shifting wonders of a dim and uncertain land. At one moment there were ancient forests, rocky cliffs, and waterfalls crashing into bottomless chasms. Then I suddenly entered quiet noon-day solitudes, where no wind from heaven ever came, and where vast fields of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers stretched away for a weary distance, all silent and forever still."}

