          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f460c822786f49e3e9e4d6d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut in fact, once one has risen to any given height, the weight of air passed through in any further ascent is not at all proportional to the additional height climbed, as may plainly be seen from what I have already stated. Instead, it decreases continually. It is therefore clear that, however high we may rise, we cannot, strictly speaking, reach a boundary beyond which there is no atmosphere. It must exist, I argued, though it may exist in a state of infinite thinness.\\n\\n\u201cOn the other hand, I knew that arguments had been made to prove that the atmosphere has a real and definite boundary, beyond which there is absolutely no air. Yet those who argued for such a boundary seemed to have overlooked one circumstance. Though it did not positively disprove their belief, it seemed to me a matter deserving serious investigation. When the intervals between Encke\u2019s comet\u2019s successive returns to perihelion are compared, after allowing as exactly as possible for every disturbance caused by the planets\u2019 attraction, the periods appear gradually to shorten. That is, the major axis of the comet\u2019s ellipse is becoming shorter in a slow but perfectly regular way. This is exactly what should happen if the comet met resistance from an extremely thin ethereal medium spread through the regions of its orbit. Such a medium, by slowing the comet\u2019s motion, must increase its centripetal force by weakening its centrifugal force. In other words, the sun\u2019s attraction would steadily gain power, and the comet would be drawn nearer with every revolution. Indeed, there is no other way to explain this variation. Again, the actual diameter of the same comet\u2019s nebulosity is seen to shrink rapidly as it approaches the sun, and to expand equally rapidly as it travels toward its aphelion. Was I not justified, with M. Valz, in supposing that this apparent shrinking in volume came from the compression of the same ethereal medium I have mentioned, which becomes denser only in proportion to its nearness to the sun? The lens-shaped phenomenon called the zodiacal light also deserved attention. This glow, so visible in the tropics and not to be mistaken for any meteoric light, stretches diagonally upward from the horizon and generally follows the direction of the sun\u2019s equator. It seemed clearly to me to be a thin atmosphere extending outward from the sun, at least beyond Venus\u2019s orbit, and, I believed, indefinitely farther. Indeed, I could not suppose this medium was confined either to the comet\u2019s elliptical path or to the sun\u2019s immediate neighborhood.\"}"
