          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cef1be7bf8ae4b6764c81a7f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must point out, Sir Walter,\u201d said Mr Shepherd one morning at Kellynch Hall, putting down the newspaper, \u201cthat the present moment is very much in our favour. This peace will bring all our wealthy naval officers ashore. They will all be looking for homes. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, to have a choice of tenants\u2014very reliable tenants. Many great fortunes have been made during the war. If a rich admiral should come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very fortunate man, Shepherd,\u201d replied Sir Walter. \u201cThat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would indeed be a prize for him\u2014the greatest prize of all, even if he had captured ever so many before. Eh, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he was expected to do, and then continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI may say, Sir Walter, that in business matters, naval gentlemen are good people to deal with. I have had some knowledge of how they conduct business, and I freely admit that they have very generous ideas, and are as likely to make desirable tenants as any people one might meet. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would suggest is this: if rumours should spread about your intentions\u2014which must be considered possible, because we know how hard it is to keep the actions and plans of one part of the world from the notice and curiosity of another\u2014importance has its price. I, John Shepherd, could hide any family matter I wished, because no one would think me worth watching; but Sir Walter Elliot has eyes upon him that may be difficult to escape. So I venture to say that, despite all our caution, it would not greatly surprise me if some rumour of the truth got out. And, as I was about to say, since applications will certainly follow, I think any from wealthy naval commanders would be especially worth considering. I also beg leave to add that I can come over within two hours at any time, to save you the trouble of answering them.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter merely nodded. Soon afterward, however, he rose and walked about the room, saying sarcastically\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are few among the gentlemen of the navy, I imagine, who would not be surprised to find themselves in a house of this kind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would certainly look around them and bless their good fortune,\u201d said Mrs Clay, who was present. Her father had driven her over, since nothing was so beneficial to Mrs Clay\u2019s health as a drive to Kellynch. \u201cBut I quite agree with my father that a sailor might be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
