{"request_id":"1850540b6f6fb5bd5047ec1b","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s father and mother, who were rather old-fashioned people and might object to the marriage if there were anything resembling a scandal. Still, he felt sure that if he told them all the facts, they would be the first to understand the motives that had driven him to act. He had every reason, then, to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and discreet, and spared him the need for painful scenes, to which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep-rooted aversion.

He knew absolutely nothing, however, about the science of poisons. Since the waiter seemed unable to find anything in the library except Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine, he searched the shelves himself. At last he found a handsomely bound edition of the Pharmacopoeia and a copy of Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the oldest members of the Buckingham, having been elected by mistake for someone else; an error that so infuriated the Committee that, when the actual man presented himself, they blackballed him unanimously. Lord Arthur was considerably confused by the technical language in both books, and had begun to regret not paying more attention to his classics at Oxford when, in the second volume of Erskine, he discovered a very interesting and thorough account of aconitine’s properties, written in reasonably plain English. It seemed exactly the poison he required. Its effect was swift—almost instantaneous, in fact—entirely painless, and, when taken in a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, by no means unpleasant to swallow. He therefore noted the quantity needed for a fatal dose on his shirt cuff, returned the books to their places, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the great chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always personally served the aristocracy, was rather surprised by the request and, with great deference, murmured something about a medical certificate being required. But as soon as Lord Arthur explained that it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to dispose of, since it showed signs of early rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf, he declared himself completely satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a pretty little silver bonbonnière he saw in a Bond Street shop window, threw away Pestle and Humbey’s ugly pill box, and immediately drove to Lady Clementina’s."}

