          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0a05cab4665fe968db048b76\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed to keep in her old position at Lady Canterville\u2019s urgent request. She gave each of them a deep curtsey as they got out, and said in a quaint old-fashioned way, \u2018I welcome you to Canterville Chase.\u2019 They followed her through the grand Tudor hall into the library, a long, low room with black oak panels and a large stained-glass window at one end. Tea had been set out there, and after removing their wraps, they sat down and looked about the room while Mrs. Umney served them.\\n\\nSuddenly Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain on the floor near the fireplace and, not knowing what it meant, said to Mrs. Umney, \u2018I am afraid something has been spilled there.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, madam,\u2019 the old housekeeper replied quietly, \u2018blood was spilled on that spot.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018How dreadful,\u2019 cried Mrs. Otis. \u2018I do not care at all for bloodstains in a sitting-room. It must be removed immediately.\u2019\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and answered in the same quiet, mysterious voice, \u2018It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, who was murdered on that very spot by her own husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon lived for nine years after her, then vanished suddenly in very mysterious circumstances. His body has never been found, but his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. The bloodstain has been greatly admired by tourists and others, and cannot be removed.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018That is complete nonsense,\u2019 cried Washington Otis. \u2018Pinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will clean it up in no time.\u2019 Before the frightened housekeeper could stop him, he dropped to his knees and quickly scrubbed the floor with a small stick of something that looked like black cosmetic. Within moments, there was no sign of the bloodstain.\\n\\n\u2018I knew Pinkerton