{"request_id":"59f2397c68b59bc0d190771d","rewritten_text":"I was born in a small island nook, a land shadowed by clouds, which, when I picture the globe with its boundless ocean and uncharted continents, seems no more than an insignificant speck in that vast whole. Yet, weighed by the measure of intellectual power, it far surpassed countries of greater size and more populous inhabitants. It is true that the human mind alone created all that was good or great for humanity, while Nature herself was merely its first servant. England, set far north in the troubled sea, now comes to me in dreams as a great, well-crewed ship, mastering the winds and riding proudly over the waves. In my childhood, she was the whole universe. When I stood upon my native hills and saw plain and mountain extending to the furthest reach of sight, dotted with my countrymen’s homes and made fertile by their labour, the very centre of the earth was fixed for me there; all the rest of the globe was a fable, and forgetting it would have cost neither my imagination nor my understanding any effort.

From the first, my fortunes have illustrated the power that change may exert over the shifting course of a human life. In my own case, this seemed almost inherited. My father was one of those men upon whom nature had lavishly bestowed the coveted gifts of wit and imagination, then left the vessel of his life to be driven by those winds without supplying reason as its rudder or judgment as its pilot. His origins were obscure, but circumstances brought him early to public attention, and his modest inherited estate was soon squandered amid the brilliant world of fashion and luxury in which he played a part. During the brief years of his careless youth, he was adored by the fashionable idlers of the day, not least by the young sovereign, who fled the intrigues of party and the demanding duties of kingship to find unfailing amusement and high spirits in his company. My father’s impulses, never under his own control, constantly led him into troubles from which only his ingenuity could free him; and the mounting burden of debts of honour and trade, which would have crushed any other man, he bore with a light heart and untamed cheerfulness. His presence was so necessary at the dinners and gatherings of the wealthy that his failings were judged forgivable, and he himself was welcomed with intoxicating flattery.

Such popularity, like all popularity, fades; and the difficulties of every kind against which he had to struggle increased at a terrifying rate, compared with his limited means of escaping them."}

