          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a055bcd540cc0f2f0882e069\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I will begin my story. It is my last duty, and I hope I have enough strength to complete it. I confess to no crimes; my errors may easily be forgiven, for they came not from evil intentions but from poor judgment. I believe few people could claim that, through different actions and greater wisdom, they would have escaped the misfortunes of which I am the victim. My fate has been ruled by necessity\u2014a dreadful necessity. It demanded hands stronger than mine, stronger, I believe, than any human power, to break the heavy, unyielding chain that has bound me. Once I breathed only joy, filled always with warm love and delight in goodness; now I have been brought to misery, which can end only, and is now about to end, in death. But I forget myself; my story has not yet been told. I will pause for a few moments, wipe my dim eyes, and try to lose the dark, heavy unhappiness of the present in the sharper feelings of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank. He lost his own father early and was raised by a weak mother, who gave him every indulgence she believed was owed to a wealthy nobleman. He was sent to Eton and then to college, and from childhood was allowed the free use of large sums of money. Thus, from his earliest youth, he enjoyed the independence that a boy with such advantages always gains at a public school.\\n\\nUnder these conditions, his passions found rich ground in which to take root and grow, as flowers or weeds according to their nature. Because he was always allowed to act for himself, his character formed strongly and early, showing many sides in which a sharp-eyed observer might see the beginnings of both virtues and misfortunes. His careless extravagance made him waste enormous sums on passing whims, which, because they seemed intense, he honored with the name of passions. Yet this extravagance often appeared as limitless generosity. While he eagerly concerned himself with the needs of others, his own wishes were satisfied to the fullest degree. He gave money, but sacrificed none of his own desires in giving it. He gave time, which he did not value, and affection, which he was glad to have called into action in any way.\\n\\nI do not say that, if his own wishes had been set against those of others, he would have shown excessive selfishness; but he was never put to that test. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its benefits; everyone loved him and wanted to please him.\"}"
