{"request_id":"bb98effa09f0ca1e6b197ebc","rewritten_text":"“It was only after some interval that I recovered myself enough to give heed to the usual concerns of the balloon. I then examined it carefully, and discovered, to my great comfort, that it was unhurt. All my instruments were secure, and, happily, I had lost neither ballast nor stores. Indeed, I had fastened them so firmly in their proper places that such a mishap was wholly impossible. On consulting my watch, I found the hour to be six o’clock. I was yet ascending with great rapidity, and my barometer indicated a present elevation of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me upon the ocean lay a little black object, somewhat oblong in form, apparently of the size, and in all respects strongly resembling, one of those children’s playthings called a domino. Applying my telescope to it, I distinctly perceived that it was a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled, and pitching violently upon the sea, her head toward the W.S.W. Apart from this vessel, I beheld nothing save ocean, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now quite time that I should acquaint your Excellencies with the purpose of my hazardous expedition. Your Excellencies will remember that my embarrassed circumstances at Rotterdam had finally compelled me to resolve upon suicide. It was not, however, that I felt any settled loathing for life itself, but that I was tormented beyond endurance by the accidental miseries connected with my condition. In this frame of mind, desirous of living, yet fatigued with existence, the treatise at the bookseller’s stall suggested a means to my fancy. I then came to a final determination. I resolved to depart, and still live—to quit the world, and nevertheless remain in being—in brief, to abandon riddles, I determined, whatever might follow, to make my way, if possible, to the moon. Now, that I may not be judged a greater madman than I truly am, I shall set forth, as clearly as I can, the reflections which induced me to think that such an undertaking, though unquestionably difficult and undeniably fraught with peril, was not altogether beyond the limits of possibility for a daring spirit.

“The moon’s true distance from the earth was the first matter requiring attention. The mean, or average, space between the centres of the two planets is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii, or merely about 237,000 miles. I speak of the mean or average space; yet it must be remembered that, since the moon’s orbit is an ellipse, with an eccentricity amounting to no less than 0.05484 of the major semi-axis of that ellipse, and since the earth’s centre lies at its focus, if I could by any means arrange to encounter the moon, as it were, at its perigee, the distance just mentioned would be considerably reduced."}

