          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"050cd2fdd26d40e4194faf45\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They know what a clever boy he is; he\u2019ll get life. They\u2019ll give the Artful nothing less than life.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by lagging and getting life?\u201d demanded Mr. Bolter. \u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking like that to me? Why don\u2019t you speak so I can understand you?\u201d\\n\\nFagin was about to translate these mysterious terms into plain language; and Mr. Bolter would then have learned that they meant \u201ctransportation for life,\u201d when the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of Master Bates, his hands in his trouser pockets and his face arranged in an expression of half-comic misery.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s all over, Fagin,\u201d said Charley, once he and his new companion had been introduced.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ve found the gentleman who owns the box; two or three more are coming to identify him; and the Artful\u2019s booked for a passage out,\u201d replied Master Bates. \u201cI must have a complete suit of mourning, Fagin, and a hatband, to visit him in before he sets off on his travels. To think of Jack Dawkins\u2014lummy Jack\u2014the Dodger\u2014the Artful Dodger\u2014being sent abroad over a common twopenny-halfpenny snuffbox! I never thought he\u2019d have done it for less than a gold watch, chain, and seals. Oh, why didn\u2019t he rob some rich old gentleman of all his valuables, and go out like a gentleman, instead of like a common thief, with no honour or glory!\u201d\\n\\nHaving expressed his feelings for his unfortunate friend, Master Bates sat down in the nearest chair, looking deeply disappointed and despondent.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean, saying he has neither honour nor glory?\u201d cried Fagin, shooting an angry glance at his pupil. \u201cWasn\u2019t he always the best among all of you? Is there one of you who could touch him, or come close to him on any job? Eh?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot one,\u201d replied Master Bates, his voice hoarse with regret. \u201cNot one.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen what are you talking about?\u201d Fagin answered angrily. \u201cWhat are you crying for?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBecause it won\u2019t be on the record, will it?\u201d said Charley, driven into open defiance of his venerable friend by the force of his grief. \u201cBecause it can\u2019t appear in the indictment; because nobody will ever know half of what he was. How will he look in the Newgate Calendar? Perhaps he won\u2019t be in it at all. Oh, my eye, my eye, what a blow!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d cried Fagin, holding out his right hand and turning to Mr. Bolter in a fit of chuckling that shook him as if he had palsy. \u201cSee what pride they take in their profession, my dear.\u201d\"}"
