{"request_id":"9f0c8d3586abe1bbb522c1c1","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the stairs, he pulled himself together and decided to give his famous burst of demonic laughter. He had found it very useful on more than one occasion. People said it had turned Lord Raker’s wig grey overnight, and it had certainly made three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses resign before the end of their first month. So he gave his most terrible laugh, until the old vaulted ceiling echoed again and again. But before the dreadful sound had faded, a door opened and Mrs. Otis came out in a light-blue dressing gown. ‘I am afraid you are not at all well,’ she said, ‘and I have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it is indigestion, you will find it an excellent remedy.’ The ghost glared at her furiously and at once began preparing to turn himself into a large black dog, a skill for which he was rightly famous. The family doctor had always blamed this transformation for the lasting stupidity of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. But the sound of footsteps coming nearer made him pause in his evil plan. Instead, he merely became faintly glowing and vanished with a deep graveyard groan, just as the twins came up to him.

When he reached his room, he completely collapsed and was overcome with violent distress. The twins’ rudeness and Mrs. Otis’s crude practicality naturally annoyed him greatly, but what troubled him most was that he had not been able to wear the suit of armour. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be thrilled by the sight of a Spectre in Armour, if not for any better reason, then at least out of respect for their national poet, Longfellow. The ghost himself had passed many weary hours reading Longfellow’s graceful and appealing poetry whenever the Cantervilles were staying in town. Besides, it was his own armour. He had worn it very successfully at the tournament at Kenilworth, and had been highly praised for it by none other than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet when he put it on, the enormous breastplate and steel helmet had completely overwhelmed him with their weight. He had fallen heavily onto the stone floor, badly hurting both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward, he was very ill and hardly left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain in good condition. However, by taking great care of himself, he recovered and decided to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

