          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d876a1d516f69d20d119bc05\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction, and though I had fully expected it, it gave me the greatest joy. After reaching about the twentieth parallel of south latitude on its former course, it suddenly turned sharply eastward. It continued in that direction all day, remaining nearly, if not exactly, in the plane of the moon\u2019s elliptical orbit. One notable result of this change was a clearly felt swaying of the car, which continued, more or less, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again greatly alarmed by a repetition of the loud crackling sound that had terrified me on the tenth. I thought long about it, but could reach no satisfactory explanation. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter had greatly decreased; from the balloon it now covered an angle of little more than twenty-five degrees. The moon was entirely invisible, being almost directly above me. I still traveled in the plane of the ellipse, but made little progress eastward.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter decreased with extreme speed. Today I became strongly convinced that the balloon was actually moving along the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014in other words, following the direct path that would bring it straight to the moon at the point in its orbit nearest the earth. The moon itself was directly overhead and therefore hidden from sight. Great and prolonged effort was needed to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. Even the outlines of continents and seas could no longer be seen on the earth with anything like clearness. At about twelve o\u2019clock, I heard for the third time that dreadful sound which had astonished me before. This time, however, it lasted several moments and grew louder as it continued. At last, while I stood stunned and terrified, expecting I knew not what horrible destruction, the car shook with terrible violence, and a huge flaming mass of some substance I could not identify rushed past the balloon, roaring and booming with the sound of a thousand thunders. When my fear and amazement had somewhat passed, I had little trouble deciding that it was some enormous volcanic fragment thrown out from the world I was approaching so rapidly\u2014probably one of that strange kind of objects sometimes found on earth and called meteoric stones, for lack of a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. Today, looking upward as best I could through each side window in turn, I saw, to my great delight, a very small part of the moon\u2019s disk appearing on every side beyond the balloon\u2019s vast circumference.\"}"
