          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ad39e936d19203dd6df9dcf6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After supper the whole gang set out to search the bar for turtle eggs. They thrust sticks into the sand, and whenever they struck a soft spot they dropped to their knees and scooped with their hands. At times they took fifty or sixty eggs from a single nest. They were round, white little things, somewhat smaller than an English walnut. That night they enjoyed a splendid feast of fried eggs, and had another on Friday morning.\\n\\nAfter breakfast they rushed out upon the bar, shouting and capering, chasing one another in circles and flinging off their clothes as they ran, until they were naked. Then they carried their sport far up into the shallow water of the bar, battling the strong current, which now and then swept their feet from beneath them and made the game all the merrier. Every so often they would bend together in a cluster and splash water into each other\u2019s faces with their hands, edging nearer while turning their faces aside from the choking spray, until at last they seized and wrestled, and the stronger fellow ducked his companion. Then all of them went down together in a confusion of white arms and legs, rising again blowing, coughing, laughing, and gasping all at once.\\n\\nOnce they were thoroughly tired, they ran ashore and stretched themselves on the dry, burning sand, covering their bodies with it; presently they would dash back into the water and repeat the whole performance. At last it occurred to them that their bare skins made a tolerably good imitation of flesh-colored \u201ctights\u201d; so they marked out a ring in the sand and put on a circus\u2014with three clowns, since no one would surrender that most honored role to another.\\n\\nThen they brought out their marbles and played \u201cknucks,\u201d \u201cringtaw,\u201d and \u201ckeeps\u201d until even that diversion lost its charm. Joe and Huck went swimming again, but Tom would not risk it, for when he had kicked off his trousers he had also knocked the string of rattlesnake rattles from his ankle. He wondered how he had avoided cramp for so long without that mysterious safeguard. He would not venture back until he had recovered it, and by then the other boys were weary and ready to rest. Little by little they drifted apart, fell into the \u201cdumps,\u201d and gazed wistfully across the broad river toward the village, sleeping in the sunshine. Tom caught himself tracing \u201cBECKY\u201d in the sand with his big toe; he rubbed it out and was vexed at his own weakness. Yet he wrote it again, unable to prevent himself. He erased it once more, then removed himself from temptation by herding the other boys together and going with them.\"}"
