          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bbc710ef9429e7432ed59c97\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any such business as that, I said to myself.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed up and the watchers were gone. Nobody was around except the family, Widow Bartley, and our crowd. I watched their faces to see whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t make it out.\\n\\nAround the middle of the day, the undertaker came with his helper. They put the coffin in the middle of the room on two chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were full. I saw that the coffin lid was just as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare look underneath it with people nearby.\\n\\nThen people began to come in. The boys and girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin, and for half an hour the people slowly passed around in a single line. Each looked down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute, and some shed a tear. Everything was very quiet and solemn, except for the girls and boys holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and sobbing a little. There was no other sound except feet scraping on the floor and people blowing their noses\u2014because people always blow their noses more at funerals than anywhere else except church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker moved about in his black gloves, with his gentle, soothing manner, making the final arrangements and getting people and things neat and comfortable, without making more noise than a cat. He never spoke. He shifted people around, squeezed in the late arrivals, opened passageways, and did it all with nods and hand signs. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the smoothest, quietest, sneakiest man I ever saw, and he had no more smile in him than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeon, and it was a poor one. When everything was ready, a 