          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6947f52c2fa47731c78dda9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019s generous hospitality was hard to resist. That, along with Tom\u2019s urging, soon won them over. So they decided not to tell anyone about the plan for that night. Then Tom thought that Huck might come that very night and give the signal. The idea took much of the excitement out of what he had been looking forward to. Still, he could not bear to miss the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019s. And why should he, he reasoned? The signal had not come the night before, so why should it be more likely tonight? The certain pleasure of the evening was worth more than the uncertain treasure; and, like a boy, he chose the stronger desire and refused to think again that day about the box of money.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the distant woods and rocky heights rang with shouts and laughter. They tried every possible way of getting hot and tired, and before long the wanderers straggled back to camp with hearty appetites. Then they began destroying the good food. After the feast, they rested and talked pleasantly in the shade of broad oak trees. Soon someone shouted:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. They gathered bundles of candles, and at once there was a general rush up the hill. The cave entrance was on the hillside\u2014an opening shaped like the letter A. Its heavy oak door stood unbarred. Inside was a small room, cold as an icehouse, with walls of solid limestone, damp with cold moisture. It was strange and mysterious to stand in that deep darkness and look out at the green valley shining in the sun. But the feeling soon faded, and the rough play began again. The instant someone lit a candle, everyone rushed at its owner; there was a struggle and a brave defense, but the candle was quickly knocked down or blown out. Then came happy bursts of laughter and another chase. But everything ends. Before long, the group filed down the steep slope of the main passage. Their flickering lights dimly showed the high rock walls, nearly meeting sixty feet above them. This main passage was only eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other high, even narrower cracks branched off on both sides\u2014for McDougal\u2019s cave was a huge maze of twisting passages that crossed and turned away again, leading nowhere.\"}"
