content.text:
{"request_id":"4847a794f965477305151dc2","rewritten_text":"In truth, he had been completely unaware of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery with no thought of using it. Anxious to see how she was bearing Frank Churchill’s engagement, he had come with no selfish purpose, no purpose at all except, if she gave him the chance, to comfort or advise her. The rest had happened in the moment, as an immediate result of what he heard and felt. The wonderful certainty that she was entirely indifferent to Frank Churchill, that her heart was wholly free of him, had raised the hope that he might someday win her affection himself; but it was not a hope for the present. In a brief victory of eagerness over judgment, he had only wished to hear that she did not forbid him from trying to win her. The greater hopes that slowly revealed themselves were even more delightful. The affection he had been asking permission to create, if he could, was already his. In less than half an hour, he had gone from deep distress to something so close to perfect happiness that it could be called nothing else.

Her change was just as great. This single half-hour had given them both the same precious certainty of being loved, and had freed them both from the same ignorance, jealousy, and distrust. On his side, there had been a long jealousy, as old as Frank Churchill’s arrival, or even the expectation of it. He had loved Emma and been jealous of Frank Churchill from nearly the same time; one feeling had probably made him aware of the other. His jealousy of Frank Churchill had driven him from the country. The party at Box Hill had decided him to leave. He would spare himself from again seeing attentions that were allowed and encouraged. He had gone away to learn indifference.

But he had gone to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother’s house; woman appeared there in too lovable a form; Isabella was too much like Emma—different only in those obvious lesser qualities that always made Emma seem more brilliant to him—for much to be achieved, even if he had stayed longer. Still, he had remained firmly, day after day, until that morning’s post brought the story of Jane Fairfax. Then, along with the gladness he must feel—indeed, which he did not hesitate to feel, since he had never thought Frank Churchill at all worthy of Emma—came such tender concern and sharp anxiety for her that he could remain no longer. He had ridden home through the rain and, directly after dinner, walked up to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless despite all her faults, was bearing the discovery."}

