content.text:
{"request_id":"d167b627cf3e9e872a3e5be9","rewritten_text":"At last, we made the box lighter by taking out two-thirds of what it held. Then, with some difficulty, we managed to lift it from the hole. We placed the things we had removed among the brambles and left the dog to guard them. Jupiter gave him strict orders not to leave the spot for any reason, or make a sound, until we returned. We then hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely at one in the morning, though only after exhausting labor. We were too worn out to do anything more at once. We rested until two, ate supper, and immediately set out again for the hills, carrying three strong sacks that luckily happened to be on the premises. A little before four, we reached the pit, divided the rest of the treasure among ourselves as evenly as possible, and, leaving the holes unfilled, started once more for the hut. There, for the second time, we set down our golden loads just as the first pale lines of dawn appeared above the eastern treetops.

By then we were completely exhausted, but the excitement of the moment would not let us rest. After a troubled sleep of three or four hours, we rose, as though by agreement, to examine our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the top, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night examining what it contained. There had been no order or arrangement; everything had been thrown in together. After carefully sorting it all, we discovered that we possessed even greater wealth than we had first imagined. In coins alone there was a little more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, judging the value of the pieces as accurately as we could from the tables of the time. There was not a trace of silver. It was all old gold, of many kinds: French, Spanish, and German coins, with a few English guineas and some coins unlike any we had ever seen. Several were very large and heavy, but so worn that we could not read their inscriptions. There was no American money. The jewels were harder to value. There were diamonds, some extremely large and fine—one hundred and ten in all, with none of them small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brightness; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; twenty-one sapphires; and an opal. All these stones had been torn from their settings and tossed loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we found among the other gold, seemed to have been hammered flat, as if to prevent anyone from identifying them."}

