content.text:
{"request_id":"8aaa1570b318d95cc7213612","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was not spoken; yet I could not doubt that his love for her caused the uncertainty of purpose he displayed. And who was more worthy of love than my noble-hearted sister? Who deserved the hand of this self-proclaimed king more than she whose look was fit for a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, though disappointment had subdued her passion, while ambition struggled fiercely with his.

We went together to the House that evening. Raymond, though he knew that the coming debate would discuss and decide his plans and prospects, was cheerful and unconcerned. A murmur, like that of ten thousand hives of swarming bees, deafened us as we entered the coffee-room. Groups of politicians stood together, their faces anxious, their voices loud or low. The aristocratic party, the wealthiest and most influential men in England, seemed less disturbed than the rest, since the question was to be debated without their involvement. Ryland and his supporters stood near the fire. Ryland was a man of humble birth and enormous wealth, inherited from his father, a manufacturer. As a young man, he had seen the king’s abdication and the union of the Houses of Lords and Commons; he had sympathized with these popular advances, and had made it his life’s work to strengthen and extend them. Since then, the influence of the landed proprietors had increased; and at first Ryland had not been displeased to see Lord Raymond’s schemes, which drew away many of his opponents’ followers. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty as an event that would restore their lost power and rights. The nearly extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in men’s minds; and they, willing slaves and self-made subjects, were ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. Some upright and manly spirits still remained, pillars of the state; but the word republic had become stale to common ears, and many—whether a majority, events would show—longed for the glitter and display of royalty. Ryland was stirred to resistance; he declared that only his tolerance had allowed this party to grow, but the time for indulgence had passed, and with one sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him almost with a shout. They crowded around him, counted their numbers, and explained why they were now to gain the support of certain members who had not yet declared themselves."}

