          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2e9819cbd9811c127427b24b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"That was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must know that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been rented by a young man of great fortune from the north of England. He came down on Monday in a carriage with four horses to see the place, and liked it so much that he made an agreement with Mr. Morris at once. He is to move in before Michaelmas, and some of his servants will be in the house by the end of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, of course, my dear! A single man with a large fortune\u2014four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful thing for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? How can it affect them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife replied, \u201chow can you be so tiresome? You must know I am thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that why he has settled here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy? Nonsense, how can you speak so! But it is very likely that he may fall in love with one of them, and so you must visit him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see no need for that. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be even better. Since you are as pretty as any of them, Mr. Bingley may like you best of the group.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you flatter me. I certainly have had my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be anything remarkable now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she ought to stop thinking about her own beauty.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn such cases, a woman does not often have much beauty to think about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you really must go and see Mr. Bingley when he comes into the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is more than I promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Just imagine what a fine match it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are determined to go for that reason alone, because, as you know, they generally do not visit newcomers. You really must go, because it will be impossible for us to visit him if you do not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are being too particular, surely. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be very pleased to see you, and I will send a few lines with you to assure him that I warmly approve of his marrying whichever of the girls he chooses\u2014though I must put in a good word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI beg you not to do any such thing. Lizzy is not at all better than the others, and I am sure she is not half as pretty as Jane, nor half as good-tempered as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
