          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"126c64cb54579e527219abf6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so completely satisfied with this conversation that he never again troubled himself, or provoked his dear sister Elizabeth, by bringing up the subject; and she was glad to find that she had said enough to keep him silent.\\n\\nThe day of his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived; and Mrs. Bennet had to submit to a separation which, since her husband would not at all agree to her plan for them all to go to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen shall we see each other again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Perhaps not for two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time to write. My sisters may write to me. They will have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s farewells were far more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many charming things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, as soon as they had left the house, \u201cas I ever saw. He simpers and smirks and flirts with us all. I am immensely proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas himself to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter left Mrs. Bennet very low-spirited for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthat nothing is so dreadful as parting from one\u2019s friends. One feels so lonely without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the result, you see, madam, of marrying off a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt must make you more content that your other four are unmarried.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is nothing of the kind. Lydia is not leaving me because she is married, but only because her husband\u2019s regiment happens to be so far away. If it had been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nBut the lifeless state into which this event had thrown her was soon relieved, and her mind was once more stirred by hopeful excitement, by a piece of news that began to circulate. The housekeeper at Netherfield had received orders to prepare for her master\u2019s arrival: he was coming down in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was quite agitated. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head in turn.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d she said\u2014for Mrs. Philips had brought her the news first. \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, of course. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never wish to see him again. Still, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield, if he chooses.\u201d\"}"
