          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e443ca7bc3fa15907505a8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! There was something animal in the soul, and the body sometimes seemed spiritual. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could sink low. Who could tell where physical desire ended and mental desire began? How limited the neat definitions of ordinary psychologists were! Yet how hard it was to choose among the claims of the different schools! Was the soul a shadow living in the house of sin? Or was the body really contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The division of spirit from matter was a mystery, and so was their union.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether psychology could ever become such an exact science that every small source of life would be revealed. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood anyone else. Experience had no moral value. It was only the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists generally treated it as a warning, claiming that it helped form character, praising it for teaching us what to pursue and what to avoid. But experience had no power to move us. It was no more an active force than conscience itself. All it truly proved was that our future would repeat our past, and that the sin we had committed once with disgust we would commit many times with pleasure.\\n\\nIt seemed clear to him that experiment was the only way to reach any scientific understanding of the passions; and Dorian Gray was certainly a subject perfectly suited to him, one that promised rich and useful results. His sudden, wild love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological event of considerable interest. Curiosity had clearly played a large part in it, along with the desire for new experiences, but it was not a simple passion. It was a very complicated one. Whatever purely sensual instinct of boyhood it contained had been altered by imagination, turned into something that seemed to the young man himself far removed from the senses, and was therefore all the more dangerous. The passions whose origins we lied to ourselves about ruled us most strongly. Our weakest motives were those whose nature we understood. Often, when we believed we were experimenting on others, we were really experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat thinking over these matters, there was a knock at the door. His valet came in and reminded him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
