          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f3b1a2d0abafbdb56c21cd2b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I watched him in these moods, I often reflected on the old idea of the divided soul, and amused myself by imagining a double Dupin\u2014one creative, the other analytical.\\n\\nDo not suppose, from what I have said, that I am describing some mystery or writing a romance. What I saw in the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited, perhaps even unhealthy, mind. But an example of his remarks at such times will best explain what I mean.\\n\\nOne night we were walking along a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Both of us seemed lost in thought, and neither had spoken a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin said:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small fellow, it is true, and would be better suited to the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I replied without thinking. At first, so deeply absorbed had I been in my own thoughts, I did not notice the extraordinary way in which he had joined them. A moment later I realized it, and my astonishment was immense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said seriously, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I do not hesitate to say that I am amazed, and can hardly trust my senses. How could you possibly know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d Here I stopped, to make certain that he truly knew whom I meant.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were saying to yourself that his small size made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been thinking about. Chantilly had once been a cobbler in the Rue St. Denis. Becoming obsessed with the stage, he had tried to play Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name, and had been famously mocked for it.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cwhat method\u2014if there is one\u2014has allowed you to read my mind in this matter?\u201d In truth, I was even more alarmed than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of shoes was not tall enough for Xerxes and all that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer! You astonish me\u2014I do not know any fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you as we entered the street\u2014perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI then remembered that a fruiterer, carrying a large basket of apples on his head, had nearly knocked me down by accident as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the street where we now stood. But I could not possibly understand what this had to do with Chantilly.\"}"
