          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"329051eb08a012bba39dde73\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a time in every properly made boy\u2019s life when he feels a fierce urge to go somewhere and dig for buried treasure. This urge seized Tom one day. He set out to find Joe Harper, but had no luck. Then he looked for Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Before long he ran into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a secluded spot and brought up the matter in confidence. Huck was willing. Huck was always ready to join any venture that promised amusement and required no money, since he had an inconvenient abundance of the kind of time that is not money.\\n\\n\u201cWhere shall we dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it buried everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, it surely isn\u2019t. It\u2019s buried in very particular places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests beneath the end of a branch on an old dead tree, exactly where its shadow falls at midnight; but mostly beneath the floors of haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho buries it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRobbers, of course\u2014who did you think? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it were mine, I wouldn\u2019t bury it; I\u2019d spend it and enjoy myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t do things that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they mean to, but usually they forget the signs, or else they die. Anyway, it lies there a long time and gets rusty; then, after a while, somebody finds an old yellow paper that explains how to find the signs\u2014a paper you have to puzzle over for about a week, because it\u2019s mostly symbols and hieroglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics\u2014what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics\u2014pictures and things, you know, that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the signs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any signs. They always bury it under a haunted house, or on an island, or beneath a dead tree with one branch sticking out. We\u2019ve searched Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can try it again sometime; and there\u2019s the old haunted house up by Still-House Branch, and plenty of dead-branch trees\u2014loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under every one of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a thing to say! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how will you know which one to dig under?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDig under all of them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so what? Suppose you found a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. What then?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes shone.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019d be splendid. More than splendid enough for me. Just give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
