          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"44bf2149a856787661967708\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wrapped it in paper and stuffed a piece of it in his mouth\u2014that was the way.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you believe, then, that your master was truly bitten by the beetle, and that the bite made him ill?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t just believe it\u2014I know it. What else make him dream about gold so much, if he wasn\u2019t bitten by the gold-bug? I heard about those gold-bugs before this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut how do you know he dreams about gold?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do I know? Why, because he talks about it in his sleep\u2014that\u2019s how I know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jup, perhaps you are right; but what fortunate reason brings you to visit me today?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s wrong, massa?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you bring any message from Mr. Legrand?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, massa, I brought this here letter;\u201d and Jupiter handed me a note that read as follows:\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR \u2014\u2014Why have I not seen you for such a long time? I hope you have not been foolish enough to take offence at any small rudeness of mine; but no, that seems unlikely. Since I last saw you, I have had much cause for worry. I have something to tell you, though I hardly know how to say it, or even whether I should say it at all.\\n\\n\u201cI have not been well for several days, and poor old Jup has been troubling me almost beyond endurance with his well-meant care. Would you believe it? The other day he prepared a large stick to punish me for slipping away from him and spending the day alone among the hills on the mainland. I truly believe that only my sickly appearance saved me from a beating.\\n\\n\u201cI have added nothing to my collection since we met.\\n\\n\u201cIf you can possibly manage it, come over with Jupiter. Do come. I wish to see you to-night on a matter of importance. I assure you it is of the highest importance.\\n\\n\u201cEver yours, \u201cWILLIAM LEGRAND\u201d.\\n\\nSomething in the tone of this letter made me deeply uneasy. Its entire style was very different from Legrand\u2019s usual manner. What could he be thinking about? What new obsession had taken hold of his excitable mind? What possible \u201cbusiness of the highest importance\u201d could he have? Jupiter\u2019s account of him gave no comfort. I feared that the constant weight of misfortune had finally disturbed my friend\u2019s reason. Without a moment\u2019s delay, I prepared to go with the negro.\\n\\nWhen we reached the wharf, I saw a scythe and three spades, all apparently new, lying in the bottom of the boat we were to take.\\n\\n\u201cWhat does all this mean, Jup?\u201d\"}"
