          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"72e3abbc8eaec76fc4f71bb2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes\u2019s crime, began to consider whether an honest life might not, in truth, be the best. Having decided that it unquestionably was, he turned away from the scenes of his former days, determined to reform in some fresh field of endeavour. For a while he struggled stoutly, and endured much; but, being of a cheerful temper and fixed purpose, he prospered at last; and, after serving as a farmer\u2019s drudge and a carrier\u2019s boy, is now the merriest young grazier in all Northamptonshire.\\n\\nAnd now the hand which writes these words trembles as it draws near the end of its labour, and would prolong, for a brief while yet, the thread of these adventures.\\n\\nI would gladly remain a little longer among some of those with whom I have moved so long, and partake of their happiness by trying to portray it. I would show Rose Maylie in the full bloom and loveliness of young womanhood, casting upon her quiet course through life a mild and tender radiance, which rested on all who walked beside her and shone within their hearts. I would depict her as the spirit and delight of the fireside circle and the cheerful summer party; I would attend her through the hot fields at midday, and hear the gentle music of her voice upon the moonlit evening walk; I would observe her in all her kindness and charity abroad, and in the smiling, unwearied fulfilment of her household duties at home; I would paint her and her dead sister\u2019s child, happy in their affection for each other, spending long hours in recalling the friends they had so sorrowfully lost; I would call before me once more those bright little faces gathered about her knee, and hear their merry chatter; I would bring back the sound of that clear laugh, and evoke the compassionate tear that sparkled in the soft blue eye. All these, with a thousand looks and smiles, turns of thought and turns of speech\u2014I would gladly remember every one.\\n\\nHow Mr. Brownlow proceeded day by day, storing the mind of his adopted child with knowledge, and growing more and more attached to him as his character unfolded and revealed the flourishing seeds of all he hoped he might become\u2014how he discovered in him fresh traces of his early friend, stirring in his own breast memories that were melancholy, yet sweet and consoling\u2014how the two orphans, schooled by adversity, retained its lessons of mercy toward others, mutual affection, and earnest gratitude to Him who had guarded and preserved them\u2014these are matters that need not be related.\"}"
