          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52aff62d39e9b827c20b1554\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His story was, that, travelling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was startled to observe smoke rising from the chimney of the inn; believing that he would find fellow-travellers gathered there, he knocked and was let in. No one was in the house except Lucy and her mother; the latter had lost the use of her limbs through an attack of rheumatism, and thus, one after another, all the remaining people of the country had departed, leaving the two alone. Lucy begged the man to remain with her; within a week or two her mother would improve, and then they could set out; but if abandoned in this helpless and desolate state, they must die. The man replied that his wife and children were already with the emigrants, and that, in his judgment, it was therefore impossible for him to stay. As a final expedient, Lucy entrusted him with a letter for Idris, to be given to her wherever he might encounter us. This charge, at least, he performed, and Idris received, deeply moved, the following letter:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am certain you will remember me and feel pity for me, and I venture to hope that you will help me; what other hope remains? Forgive the way I write; I am so confused. A month ago my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and in another month, I am sure, would be able to travel, in the manner you were kind enough to say you would arrange for us. But now every one has gone\u2014every one\u2014as they departed, each said that perhaps my mother would recover before we were entirely forsaken. Yet three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his new-born child, had stayed until the last; and as they were a large family, I thought I might persuade them to wait a little longer for us; but I found their house empty. Since then I have not seen a living soul, until this good man arrived. \u2014What is to become of us? My mother does not know our condition; she is so ill that I have concealed it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we must perish wretchedly as we are. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die upon the road; and if, once she is better, I were able\u2014I cannot imagine how\u2014to discover the roads and make our way so many, many miles to the sea, you would all be in France, with the vast ocean between us, so dreadful even to sailors.\"}"
