          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aadacac6859de88e211d645d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You don\u2019t know anything about me unless you\u2019ve read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer; but that doesn\u2019t matter. Mr. Mark Twain wrote that book, and he told the truth, mostly. There were things he exaggerated, but mostly he told the truth. That\u2019s nothing unusual. I never saw anybody who didn\u2019t lie sometime or other, except Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, that is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all in that book, which is mostly true, with a few exaggerations, as I said before.\\n\\nThis is how the book ends: Tom and I found the money the robbers had hidden in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars each\u2014all in gold. It was an awful lot of money when it was piled up. Judge Thatcher took it and put it out at interest, and it brought us a dollar a day each, all year long\u2014more than anybody could know what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me in as her son and said she would civilize me; but living in that house all the time was hard, considering how drearily proper and respectable the widow was in every way. So when I couldn\u2019t stand it any longer, I ran off. I got back into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead, and I was free and content. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he was going to start a band of robbers, and I could join if I went back to the widow and acted respectable. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, along with a lot of other names, but she didn\u2019t mean any harm by it. She put me into those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and sweat and feel cramped up. Then the old routine started again. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to show up on time. When you got to the table, you couldn\u2019t start eating right away. You had to wait for the widow to bow her head and mumble a little over the food, though there wasn\u2019t really anything wrong with it\u2014except that everything was cooked separately. In a barrel of odds and ends, it\u2019s different; things get mixed together, the juices sort of trade places, and everything tastes better.\\n\\nAfter supper, she took out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was eager to hear all about him. But after a while she let slip that Moses had been dead a very long time. Then I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t put any stock in dead people.\"}"
