          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"030466ab3106aeda5c453367\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost completed their course. It was now the end of May; the woods were dressed in their freshest green, and the sweet scent of newly cut grass filled the fields. I hoped that the soft air and the beautiful face of nature might help awaken gentle feelings in him, and prepare him with peace and love for the confidence I meant to gain from him.\\n\\nI chose the evening of one such day for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me and led him to a nearby wood of beech trees, where the light shade protected us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After we had walked silently for some time, I sat down with him on a mossy mound. Strange as it is, I can still see the place: many of the slender, smooth trunks were wrapped in ivy, whose dark, shining leaves stood out against the white bark and the pale leaves of young beech shoots growing from their parent trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss and partly covered with dead leaves from the previous autumn, which the wind had gathered here and there into little heaps. A few moss-covered stumps stood about. The leaves stirred gently in the breeze, and through their green canopy the bright blue sky could be seen. As evening drew on, the distant trunks were reddened by the sun, and the wind died away completely, while a few birds flew past us toward their nightly rest.\\n\\nThere we sat together; and when you hear all that happened\u2014all that terribly tore our souls apart\u2014even in this peaceful place, which without strange passions might have been a paradise to us, you will not wonder that I remember it. I looked at it, hoping its calm would make me calm and give me not only courage but persuasive words. I saw all these things and noted them vaguely in my mind while I tried to arrange my thoughts for what I meant to say. My heart beat quickly as