          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c99c9fffd84640c27156943\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short but remarkably upright, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his lecture by reviewing the history of chemistry and the advances made by different scholars, speaking with passion the names of its greatest discoverers. He then briefly described the present state of the science and explained many of its basic terms. After a few preliminary experiments, he ended with a praise of modern chemistry whose words I shall never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised impossibilities and achieved nothing. Modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be changed into other metals and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem made only for handling dirt and whose eyes are fixed on microscopes and crucibles, have truly worked miracles. They enter nature\u2019s hidden places and show us how she works there. They rise into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and almost limitless powers; they can command the thunder of heaven, imitate earthquakes, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nThese were the professor\u2019s words\u2014or rather, the words of fate, spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as though my soul were struggling with a real enemy; one after another, the keys that made up my being were touched. Chord after chord sounded, until my mind held only one thought, one idea, one purpose. So much has been done, cried Frankenstein\u2019s soul\u2014more, far more, will I accomplish. Following the path already marked, I will open a new road, explore unknown powers, and reveal to the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\\n\\nI did not close my eyes that night. My inner self was in rebellion and confusion; I felt that order would come from it, but I