          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3763a79f18eb6282ec3edde7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they passed lonely brickfields. The fog was thinner here, and he could see the strange bottle-shaped kilns, their orange fires spreading out like fans. A dog barked as they passed, and somewhere far off in the darkness a wandering seagull cried. The horse stumbled in a rut, then swerved and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled once more over roughly paved streets. Most windows were dark, but now and then strange shadows showed against a lit blind. He watched them with curiosity. They moved like huge puppets and gestured like living creatures. He hated them. A dull anger filled his heart. As they turned a corner, a woman shouted something from an open doorway, and two men ran after the hansom for about a hundred yards. The driver struck at them with his whip.\\n\\nIt is said that passion makes a person think in circles. Certainly, again and again, Dorian Gray's bitten lips formed and reformed those subtle words about soul and sense, until he found in them the fullest expression of his mood. They gave intellectual approval to passions that, even without such approval, would still have ruled his nature. One thought crept through every cell of his brain; and the wild desire to live, the most terrible of all human hungers, stirred every trembling nerve and fibre into strength. Ugliness, once hateful to him because it made things seem real, became precious to him for that very reason. Ugliness was the only reality. The rough quarrel, the filthy den, the crude violence of disordered life, even the vileness of thieves and outcasts, seemed more vivid in their intense reality than all the graceful forms of art and the dreamy shadows of song. They were what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the driver pulled up sharply at the top of a dark lane. Above the low roofs and jagged chimney-stacks rose the black masts of ships. Strips of white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere around here, sir, ain't it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked around. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he answered. He got out quickly, gave the driver the extra fare he had promised, and walked swiftly toward the quay. Here and there, a lantern shone at the stern of a huge merchant ship. Its light trembled and broke apart in the puddles. A red glow came from an outward-bound steamer taking on coal. The slimy pavement looked like a wet raincoat.\"}"
