          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"76fb38647160e7b90d94eb9d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I says, \u201cyou might tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have one. All noblemen do.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just like him. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something, he wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t make any difference.\\n\\nHe had all that coat-of-arms business settled, so he began finishing that part of the plan. Jim had to have a sad inscription, he said, like they all did. He made up several, wrote them on paper, and read them aloud:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart burst._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and his friends, wore out his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a tired spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, died a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook while he read them, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, because they were all so good. At last he said Jim could scratch all of them on. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch so much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and besides, he didn\u2019t know how to make letters. But Tom said he would draw them out for him, and then Jim would only have to follow the lines. Then, after a little while, he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, logs won\u2019t do. Dungeons don\u2019t have log walls. We\u2019ve got to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll bring in a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than logs. He said it would take him such a terribly long time to carve them into a rock that he would never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he looked to see how Jim and I were getting along with the pens. It was terribly slow, hard work, and it gave my hands no chance to heal from their sores. We hardly seemed to be getting anywhere, so Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the sad inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with one rock.\u201d\"}"
