          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a72800161bacc9452c5fdb0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I may shortly have the pleasure of presenting my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, quite ready to imagine that this hope conveyed a special compliment to herself, smiled with great graciousness.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard, I suppose, of a certain Frank Churchill,\u201d he went on\u2014\u201cand know that he is my son, though he does not carry my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! yes, and I shall be delighted to make his acquaintance. I am certain Mr. Elton will not delay in calling upon him; and we shall both be very glad to see him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind.\u2014Frank will be exceedingly pleased, I am sure.\u2014He is to come to town next week, if not before. We received word of it in a letter to-day. I met the post this morning, and, recognizing my son\u2019s hand, took the liberty of opening it\u2014though it was not addressed to me\u2014it was for Mrs. Weston. She is his chief correspondent, I assure you. I scarcely ever receive a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually opened a letter addressed to her! Oh! Mr. Weston\u2014(laughing with affectation) I must object to that.\u2014A very dangerous precedent indeed!\u2014I beg that you will not encourage your neighbours to imitate you.\u2014Upon my word, if this is what I am to look for, we married women must begin to assert ourselves!\u2014Oh! Mr. Weston, I could never have expected such a thing from you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, we men are miserable fellows. You must be on your guard, Mrs. Elton.\u2014This letter tells us\u2014it is a brief one\u2014hastily written, only to inform us\u2014it says they are all coming up to town at once, on Mrs. Churchill\u2019s account\u2014she has been unwell all winter, and finds Enscombe too cold for her\u2014so they are all moving south without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014from Yorkshire, I believe. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; they are nearly one hundred and ninety miles from London, quite a substantial journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, upon my word, a very substantial one. Sixty-five miles farther than Maple Grove is from London. But what does distance signify, Mr. Weston, to people with great fortunes?\u2014You would be astonished to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, sometimes rushes about. You will scarcely credit me\u2014but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge travelled to London and back with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand it, has been unable to leave her sofa for an entire week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter, he said she complained of being too weak to reach her conservatory without the support of both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d\"}"
