{"request_id":"c393ac0bf0b493cbe72fd5da","rewritten_text":"Summer moved on, and, armed with the sun’s fierce rays, the plague sent its deadly shafts across the earth. Nations under its power bowed their heads and died. The corn that had grown in abundance lay rotting on the ground in autumn, while the miserable man who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay rigid and plague-stricken in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches proudly, while the dying lay beneath their shade, answering their solemn music with discordant cries. Bright birds darted through the shadows; careless deer rested unharmed among the ferns; oxen and horses wandered from their unguarded stables and grazed in the wheat, for death struck man alone.

As summer and death increased, so did our fears. My poor love and I looked at one another, and at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we shall tell them of these fears, which will have passed away with their cause. Even if they alone remain on earth, they shall live; their cheeks shall not grow pale, nor their sweet voices fade.” Our eldest partly understood what was happening around him, and sometimes, with a grave expression, asked me why such terrible destruction had come. But he was only ten, and the cheerfulness of youth soon drove needless worry from his face. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful child with no thought of pain or sorrow, would toss his light curls back from his eyes and fill the halls with his laughter, drawing our attention to his games in a thousand innocent ways. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, our joy. She made it her duty to tend the sick, console the grieving, help the old, and share the games and awaken the spirits of the young. She moved through the rooms like a kind spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with an otherworldly light. Gratitude and praise followed where she had walked. Yet when she stood before us in quiet simplicity, playing with our children or eagerly doing small kindnesses for Idris, one wondered in what beautiful feature of her pure face, in what gentle note of her moving voice, so much courage, wisdom, and active goodness could dwell.

The summer passed slowly, for we trusted that winter would at least restrain the disease. To hope that it would disappear entirely was too precious, too deeply felt, to be spoken aloud. When someone carelessly voiced such a thought, the listeners answered with a rush of tears and passionate sobs, showing how deep their fears were and how little hope they possessed."}

