{"request_id":"5f6bc3f3c73891b76ddaf89c","rewritten_text":"All the young people were eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going there again himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and though it was November, the weather was not at all bad. In short, Louisa, the keenest of them all, had decided to go; and, beyond the pleasure of having her own way, she was now supported by the notion that there was merit in standing firm. She overruled every wish of her father and mother to postpone it until summer; and to Lyme they were to go—Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first careless plan had been to leave in the morning and return that night; but Mr. Musgrove would not agree to it, for the sake of his horses. Once they considered the matter sensibly, they saw that a day in mid-November would allow little time to see a new place after taking away the seven hours required, given the nature of the roads, for the journey there and back. They were therefore to stay the night and were not expected home until dinner the following day. This was considered a considerable improvement; and although they all gathered at the Great House for breakfast rather early, and left with great punctuality, it was well past noon before the two carriages—Mr. Musgrove’s coach carrying the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—came down the long hill into Lyme and entered the town’s even steeper main street. It was plain that they would have barely enough time to look around before the day’s light and warmth had disappeared.

Having secured rooms and ordered dinner at one of the inns, the next thing was unquestionably to walk straight down to the sea. They had come too late in the year for any of the entertainment or variety Lyme might offer as a resort. The assembly rooms were closed, nearly all the visitors had left, and scarcely any families remained except the residents. Since there was nothing remarkable in the buildings themselves, a stranger’s eye would naturally seek the town’s striking setting: its main street seeming almost to rush into the water; the walk to the Cobb, curving around the pleasant little bay, lively in season with bathing machines and visitors; the Cobb itself, with its old marvels and recent improvements; and the beautiful line of cliffs extending eastward from the town. And a very unusual stranger indeed would be one who found no charm in Lyme’s immediate surroundings, enough to make him wish to know it better."}

