          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e5cdf96275aa827d9f398a0e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His story was that, while travelling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke rising from the inn chimney. Thinking he might find fellow travellers gathered there, he knocked and was let in. No one was in the house except Lucy and her mother. The mother had lost the use of her limbs after an attack of rheumatism, and so, one after another, all the remaining people in the district had set out, leaving them alone. Lucy begged the man to stay with them; in a week or two her mother would improve, and then they could leave. But if they were abandoned so helpless and desolate, they must die. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and that, in his view, it was therefore impossible for him to remain. As a final measure, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to deliver wherever he met us. At least he carried out this task, and Idris received the following letter with deep emotion:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am certain you will remember me and feel pity for me, and I dare to hope you will help me; what other hope do I have? Forgive the way I write; I am so confused. A month ago, my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and in another month, I am sure, she would be able to travel, in the manner you were kind enough to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As they left, each person said that perhaps my mother would recover before we were completely deserted. But three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his newborn child, had stayed until the end. Since they were such a large family, I thought I could persuade them to wait a little longer for us; but I found the house empty. I had not seen a soul since, until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have concealed it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone for us? I am sure we must perish miserably as we are. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road; and if, once she is better, I were somehow able to discover the roads and travel so many, many miles to the sea, you would all be in France, with the vast ocean between us\u2014an ocean so dreadful even to sailors.\"}"
