          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"85f8f0ce92f79beb78d198a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief conversation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma great pleasure. The next morning, as she walked about the lawn, it was one of the pleasant memories of the ball that she enjoyed. She was very glad they had reached such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so alike. His praise of Harriet, and his willingness to admit her merits, pleased her especially. The Eltons\u2019 rudeness, which for a few minutes had threatened to spoil the rest of her evening, had led to some of its greatest pleasures; and she looked forward to another happy result\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s foolish attachment. From the way Harriet had spoken of the matter before they left the ballroom, Emma had strong hopes. It seemed as though her eyes had suddenly been opened, and she could now see that Mr. Elton was not the superior being she had imagined. The fever was over, and Emma had little fear that her pulse would be stirred again by harmful politeness. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 unkind feelings to provide all the sharp neglect that might still be needed. Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley not inclined to quarrel with her\u2014what a happy summer lay before her!\\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not permit himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he had to be home by midday. She did not regret it.\\n\\nHaving settled all these matters in her mind, examined them, and put them in order, she was just turning toward the house, her spirits refreshed for the demands of the two little boys and their grandfather, when the great iron gate swung open and two people entered whom she could not have less expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! A moment was enough to show Emma that something extraordinary had happened. Harriet was pale and frightened, while he was trying to comfort her. The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart; all three were soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be revived; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such events are very interesting, but the suspense cannot last long. Within a few minutes, Emma knew the whole story.\\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone walking together and taken the Richmond road. Though it seemed public enough to be safe, it had led them into danger. About half a mile beyond Highbury, it made a sudden turn and, with elm trees thickly shading both sides, became quite secluded for a considerable distance. When the young ladies had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead on a wider patch of grass beside the road, a group of gipsies.\"}"
