          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"59282b1fe508704fc55f426e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On Saturday nights in our village, there was a pleasant feeling of tidying up and pausing quietly before beginning life again, which encouraged Joe to risk staying out half an hour later on Saturdays than he did at other times. When the half-hour and the rum-and-water ran out together, Joe rose to leave and took my hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait just a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I may have a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe picked it from a handful of loose coins, wrapped it in a crumpled piece of paper, and gave it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cRemember! Your very own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him far beyond what good manners allowed, and gripping Joe tightly. He said good-night to Joe, and good-night to Mr. Wopsle, who left with us; but to me he gave only a look from his aiming eye\u2014or not a look, exactly, because he closed it. Still, much can be done with an eye by concealing it.\\n\\nOn the way home, had I felt inclined to speak, all the conversation would have had to come from me. Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe walked all the way home with his mouth wide open, trying to wash the rum away with as much air as possible. But I was almost stunned by the reappearance of my old wrongdoing and my old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in an especially bad mood when we appeared in the kitchen, and that unusual fact gave Joe courage to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d Mrs. Joe said triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be a good one. \u201cBut what is this?\u201d Mrs. Joe said, tossing down the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were nothing less than two thick, sweaty one-pound notes, which seemed to have enjoyed the closest acquaintance with every cattle market in the county. Joe seized his hat again and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was away, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, quite certain that the man would not be there.\\n\\nSoon Joe returned, saying that the man had gone, but that he had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
