          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c875c9f28dc4a237935fa3c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning set for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to view Kellynch Hall, Anne thought it perfectly natural to take her nearly daily walk to Lady Russell\u2019s and stay out of the way until it was all over; afterward, she thought it equally natural to regret missing the chance to see them.\\n\\nThe meeting between the two parties proved extremely satisfactory and settled the entire matter at once. Each lady had already been inclined to agree, and therefore saw nothing but good manners in the other; and as for the gentlemen, the Admiral showed such warm good humour, such frank, trusting generosity, that he could not fail to influence Sir Walter, who had also been flattered into his finest and most polished manner by Mr. Shepherd\u2019s assurances that the Admiral knew him by reputation as a model of good breeding.\\n\\nThe house, grounds, and furniture were approved; the Crofts were approved; the terms, the timing, everything and everyone was satisfactory; and Mr. Shepherd\u2019s clerks were put to work, without a single preliminary disagreement requiring alteration in all that \u201cThis indenture declares.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter readily pronounced the Admiral the best-looking sailor he had ever encountered, and went so far as to say that, if his own valet had been allowed to arrange the Admiral\u2019s hair, he would not be ashamed to be seen with him anywhere. And the Admiral, with equally cordial feeling, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI thought we would soon strike a bargain, my dear, despite what they told us at Taunton. The Baronet will never set the Thames on fire, but he seems harmless enough.\u201d These were reciprocal compliments, and would have been judged much the same in value.\\n\\nThe Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas; and since Sir Walter intended to move to Bath during the preceding month, there was no time to waste in making every necessary arrangement.\\n\\nLady Russell, certain that Anne would not be permitted to be of any use or importance in choosing the house they were about to take, was very reluctant to see her hurried away so soon. She wished it could be arranged for Anne to remain behind until she herself could take her to Bath after Christmas; but her own commitments, which would take her away from Kellynch for several weeks, prevented her from offering the full invitation she desired. Anne, though she dreaded the possible September heat amid Bath\u2019s white glare, and grieved to lose the influence, both sweet and sad, of the autumn months in the country, did not think, all things considered, that she wished to stay.\"}"
