          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"96f90dc72f4fbafb127976c8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut that is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI may as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, with such an obvious intention to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, your judgment, and even your character? Was that not some excuse for rudeness, if I was rude? But I have other reasons to be offended. You know I do. If my own feelings had not already decided against you\u2014if they had been indifferent, or even favourable\u2014do you think any consideration could persuade me to accept the man who has helped to destroy, perhaps forever, the happiness of a sister I love dearly?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the feeling passed quickly, and he listened without trying to interrupt as she went on\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason in the world to think badly of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous role you played there. You dare not\u2014you cannot\u2014deny that you were the chief, if not the only, cause of separating them, exposing one to the world\u2019s criticism for fickleness and instability, the other to its mockery for disappointed hopes, and bringing them both the sharpest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and saw, with considerable indignation, that he listened in a manner that showed him entirely untouched by remorse. He even looked at her with a smile of affected disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nWith assumed calm, he replied, \u201cI have no desire to deny that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. I have been kinder to him than I have been to myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth scorned to appear to notice this polite remark, though its meaning did not escape her, nor was it likely to soften her feelings.\\n\\n\u201cBut this is not the only matter on which my dislike is based,\u201d