          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fbbe2b41c675224cbfd3a495\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would be such a charming companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed with everything, but added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith is another such pretty, kind young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could have no better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very happy to hear it\u2014but everyone knows Jane Fairfax is so accomplished and superior!\u2014and exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThey discussed this subject very pleasantly, and moved on to others of equal importance, all with the same harmony; but the evening did not end without a small return of anxiety. The gruel arrived and gave them much to talk about\u2014much praise and many remarks\u2014firm opinions about how healthy it was for every constitution, and rather harsh speeches about the many houses where it was never made properly. But unfortunately, among the failures the daughter could mention, the most recent, and therefore most noticeable, was her own cook at South End: a young woman hired for the stay, who had never understood what was meant by a bowl of good smooth gruel, thin but not too thin. However often she had wanted and ordered it, she had never received anything tolerable. This was a dangerous subject.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. The sound, to Emma\u2019s ear, meant, \u201cAh! there is no end to the unhappy results of your going to South End. It cannot be spoken of.\u201d For a while she hoped he would not speak of it, and that silent thought might be enough to bring him back to enjoying his own smooth gruel. After several minutes, however, he began,\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the seaside this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir? I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you had to go to the sea, it would have been better not to go to South End. South End is unhealthy. Perry was surprised to hear that you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think so, but it is really quite mistaken, sir. We were all perfectly healthy there, and never felt the least trouble from the mud. Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to suppose the place unhealthy; and I am sure he can be trusted, because he understands the nature of the air thoroughly, and his own brother and family have been there many times.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry spent a week at Cromer once, and considers it the best of all places for sea bathing.\u201d\"}"
