          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"54684ab0e7ed8fba8ecd8096\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThe shock and confusion caused in my mind by this extraordinary reversal of circumstances was perhaps the part of the adventure hardest to explain. For the change itself was not only natural and unavoidable, but had long been expected whenever I reached that point in my voyage where the attraction of the planet would be overcome by that of the satellite\u2014or, more exactly, where the balloon\u2019s pull toward the earth became weaker than its pull toward the moon. Certainly, I woke from a deep sleep, my senses all confused, to face a most startling sight\u2014one that, though expected, was not expected at that moment. The reversal itself must naturally have happened slowly and gently, and it is far from certain that, even had I been awake when it occurred, I would have noticed any inward sign of being turned upside down\u2014that is, any discomfort or disorder in my person or my equipment.\\n\\n\u201cIt is hardly necessary to say that, once I understood my situation and recovered from the terror that had taken hold of every power of my soul, I first gave all my attention to the moon\u2019s general physical appearance. It lay beneath me like a map; and though I believed it was still a considerable distance away, the markings of its surface appeared with a striking and wholly unexplainable clearness. The complete lack of ocean or sea\u2014indeed, of any lake, river, or body of water at all\u2014seemed at first glance the most remarkable feature of its geology. Yet, strangely, I saw vast flat regions that were clearly alluvial, although by far the larger part of the visible hemisphere was covered with countless volcanic mountains. They were cone-shaped and looked more artificial than natural. The tallest of them rose no more than three and three-quarter miles straight upward; but a map of the volcanic districts of the Campi Phlegraei would give your Excellencies a better idea of their general appearance than any poor description I could offer. Most were plainly erupting, and showed me their fury and power through the repeated thunder of the wrongly named meteoric stones, which now rushed upward past the balloon with ever more frightening frequency.\"}"
