          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c37d7d8f131869a5d19ea09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we possessed a donkey. The proper thing would be for us all to arrive on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and myself\u2014and my caro sposo to walk beside us. I really must speak to him of buying one. In the country, I consider it almost a necessity; for, however many occupations a woman may have, she cannot always remain confined at home;\u2014and long walks, you know\u2014in summer there is dust, and in winter mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will meet with neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and at present it is entirely dry. Still, come on a donkey if you like. You may borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I should wish every thing to suit your convenience as nearly as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I am persuaded you would. Indeed, my good friend, I give you full credit. Beneath that particular dry and blunt manner, I know you possess the kindest heart. As I say to Mr. E., you are a complete humourist.\u2014Yes, depend upon it, Knightley, I am quite aware of your attention to me throughout this whole arrangement. You have devised the very thing to gratify me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had a further motive for avoiding a table beneath the trees. He wanted to induce Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to make one of the party; and he knew that the sight of any of them seated out of doors for a meal would certainly make him unwell. Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured, under the plausible guise of a morning drive and one or two hours at Donwell, into his own misery.\\n\\nThe invitation was sincerely given. No concealed terrors were waiting to reproach him for his trusting readiness. He consented. He had not visited Donwell for two years. \u201cOn some particularly fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet might go very comfortably; and he might sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls wandered through the gardens. He did not imagine they could be damp now, at the middle of the day. He should exceedingly like to see the old house once more, and would be very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, with any other neighbours.\u2014He could perceive no objection whatever to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there on some fine morning. He thought Mr. Knightley had done very well to invite them\u2014very kindly and sensibly\u2014far more clever than dining out.\u2014He was not fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in receiving every body\u2019s immediate agreement. The invitation was so favourably welcomed on all sides that, like Mrs. Elton, each seemed to regard the plan as a personal compliment.\u2014Emma and Harriet declared the highest expectations of enjoyment; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, undertook to bring Frank over to join them if he could; a mark of approval and gratitude that might well have been spared.\u2014Mr. Knightley was therefore obliged to say he should be pleased to see him; and Mr. Weston promised to waste no time in writing, nor spare any persuasion to make him come.\"}"
