          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"201e5d5977de4895b83b3a47\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped furtively from the wainscoting, an evil smile upon his cruel, wrinkled lips, while the moon veiled her face behind a cloud as he crept past the great oriel window, where his own arms and those of his murdered wife shone in azure and gold. On and on he moved, like a malignant shadow, and the very darkness appeared to shrink from him as he went by. Once he fancied he heard a cry, and halted; but it was merely a dog baying at the Red Farm, so he continued, muttering odd sixteenth-century curses and now and then waving the rusty dagger through the midnight air. At last he came to the turn in the passage leading to unfortunate Washington\u2019s room. He paused there a moment, the wind tossing his long grey hair about his head and folding the unnameable horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud into grotesque, fantastic shapes. Then the clock chimed the quarter, and he knew his hour had arrived. He gave a chuckle and turned the corner; but scarcely had he done so when, uttering a piteous cry of terror, he recoiled and covered his pallid face with his long, bony hands. Directly before him stood a dreadful spectre, still as a carved statue and as monstrous as a madman\u2019s vision! Its head was bald and gleaming; its face was round, fat, and white; and horrible laughter seemed to have twisted its features into a perpetual grin. Scarlet beams poured from its eyes, its mouth was a broad pit of fire, and a hideous robe, resembling his own, wrapped the Titan-like form in silent snows. Upon its breast hung a placard inscribed in strange antique letters\u2014some scroll of disgrace, it seemed, some chronicle of reckless sins, some fearful register of crime\u2014and in its right hand it held high a flashing steel falchion.\\n\\nAs he had never before encountered a ghost, he was naturally seized with terror, and, after one more hurried look at the dreadful apparition, fled back to his room. He stumbled in his long winding-sheet as he rushed along the corridor, and at last dropped the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jack-boots, where the butler discovered it the next morning. Safely within his own chamber, he threw himself upon a little pallet-bed and buried his face beneath the bedclothes. After a while, however, the gallant old Canterville spirit revived within him, and he resolved to seek out and address the other ghost as soon as daylight came. Thus, when dawn was just silvering the hills, he made his way back toward the place where he had first beheld the grisly phantom, thinking that two ghosts were, after all, preferable to one, and that with his new companion\u2019s help he might safely contend with the twins.\"}"
