          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"06121304f5cb6501c3177ec4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wrapped it in paper and stuffed a piece of it in his mouth\u2014that was the way.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you believe, then, that your master was actually bitten by the beetle, and that the bite made him ill?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t believe nothin\u2019 about it\u2014I know it. What else make him dream about gold so much, if it ain\u2019t because he was bitten by the gold-bug? I\u2019d heard about them gold-bugs before this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut how do you know he dreams about gold?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do I know? Why, because he talks about it in his sleep\u2014that\u2019s how I know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jup, perhaps you are right; but to what fortunate circumstance do I owe the honor of your visit today?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter, massa?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you bring any message from Mr. Legrand?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, massa, I brought this here letter;\u201d and Jupiter handed me a note, which read as follows:\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR \u2014\u2014Why have I not seen you for such a long time? I hope you have not been foolish enough to take offense at any little brusqueness of mine; but no, that seems unlikely. Since I saw you, I have had great cause for anxiety. I have something to tell you, yet scarcely know how to tell it, or even whether I ought to tell it at all.\\n\\n\u201cI have not been quite well for several days, and poor old Jup vexes me almost beyond endurance with his well-meant attentions. Would you believe it? The other day he prepared a huge stick with which to punish me for slipping away from him and spending the day alone among the hills on the mainland. I truly believe that only my sickly appearance saved me from a beating.\\n\\n\u201cI have added nothing to my collection since we met.\\n\\n\u201cIf you can possibly manage it, come over with Jupiter. Do come. I wish to see you tonight on a matter of importance. I assure you, it is of the highest importance.\\n\\n\u201cEver yours, \u201cWILLIAM LEGRAND\u201d.\\n\\nThere was something in the tone of this note that made me deeply uneasy. Its entire style differed greatly from Legrand\u2019s usual manner. What could he be thinking of? What new obsession had seized his excitable mind? What \u201cmatter of the highest importance\u201d could he possibly have to conduct? Jupiter\u2019s account of him gave no hopeful sign. I feared that the continued weight of misfortune had at last completely disturbed my friend\u2019s reason. Without a moment\u2019s hesitation, therefore, I prepared to go with the servant.\\n\\nWhen we reached the wharf, I noticed a scythe and three spades, all apparently new, lying in the bottom of the boat in which we were to set out.\\n\\n\u201cWhat does all this mean, Jup?\u201d\"}"
