          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"30fe6d5dcc12b70eff2877b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have said that he was a poet: at the age of twenty-three, he first published a poem, and the entire nation greeted it with enthusiasm and delight. Fortune seemed always to favor him; never had a reputation been made so swiftly, or become so universal. The crowd praised the very poems that astonished the scholar in his study: not a single voice dissented.\\n\\nIt was then, at the height of his fame, that he met Elinor. She was a young heiress of extraordinary beauty, living under her guardian\u2019s care; from the first time they were seen together, they seemed made for one another. Elinor did not possess Woodville\u2019s genius, but she was generous and noble, and, raised by her youth and by the love she inspired everywhere, knew nothing except virtue and excellence. She was lovely; her manner was open and simple; her deep blue eyes shone with a radiance that could come only from feeling joined with wisdom.\\n\\nThey were made for each other, and soon they loved. For the first time, Woodville knew the happiness of love; and Elinor was transported by the thought that she possessed the heart of one so beautiful and illustrious among his fellow men. What could flow from such a union but pure joy?\\n\\nWoodville was a poet\u2014every circle sought him out, and whenever he appeared, every eye turned to him alone; but he was the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor was a wealthy heiress. Her guardian did not object to their mutual affection: Woodville\u2019s merit was too great for anyone to criticize his lack of wealth. Yet her father\u2019s dying will forbade her to marry before she came of age, and her fortune depended on her obedience to this command. She had only just begun her twentieth year, and she and her lover had to accept the delay. Still, they were always together, and their happiness seemed like Paradise: they studied together, made plans for their future occupations, and, drinking love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and words, scarcely regretted the postponement of their complete union. Woodville continued to rise in glory, and Elinor grew lovelier and wiser under the guidance of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be twenty-one: everything was ready for their union. How can I tell of the catastrophe that came upon such joy? Yet the earth would not be the earth it is, covered in blight and sorrow, if a pair of angelic beings like these had been allowed to exist for one another. Search the world, and you will not find the perfect happiness their marriage would have given them; the established order of things among us miserable dwellers on earth would have needed to be overturned to permit such complete joy.\"}"
