          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"50322392b44987549111beb0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI consider it one of the first refinements of civilized society.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, sir; and it also has the advantage of being fashionable among the less civilized societies of the world: every savage can dance.\u201d\\n\\nSir William merely smiled. \u201cYour friend dances wonderfully,\u201d he continued after a pause, seeing Bingley join the group; \u201cand I have no doubt that you are skilled in the art yourself, Mr. Darcy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou saw me dance at Meryton, I believe, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, indeed, and I received no small pleasure from the sight. Do you often dance at St. James\u2019s?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you not think it would be a suitable compliment to the place?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is a compliment I never pay to any place if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have a house in town, I suppose?\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy bowed.\\n\\n\u201cI once considered settling in town myself, as I am fond of distinguished society; but I was not quite sure that London air would suit Lady Lucas.\u201d\\n\\nHe paused, hoping for an answer, but his companion had no wish to give one. At that moment Elizabeth moved toward them, and he was inspired to do something very gallant. He called to her,--\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must let me introduce this young lady to you as a most desirable partner. Surely you cannot refuse to dance when so much beauty stands before you.\u201d Taking her hand, he would have placed it in Mr. Darcy\u2019s. Darcy, though greatly surprised, was not unwilling to take it, when she immediately pulled back and said, somewhat flustered, to Sir William,--\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, sir, I have no intention at all of dancing. Please do not suppose I came this way to ask for a partner.\u201d\\n\\nWith serious courtesy, Mr. Darcy asked for the honour of her hand, but without success. Elizabeth was determined, and Sir William\u2019s efforts to persuade her did nothing to change her mind.\\n\\n\u201cYou dance so well, Miss Eliza, that it is cruel to deny me the pleasure of seeing you; and though this gentleman generally dislikes the amusement, I am sure he can have no objection to obliging us for half an hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Darcy is all politeness,\u201d said Elizabeth, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cHe is indeed; but considering the attraction, my dear Miss Eliza, we cannot be surprised by his willingness, for who could object to such a partner?\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth gave him a sly look and turned away. Her refusal had not made her less pleasing to the gentleman, and he was thinking of her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley addressed him,--\"}"
