          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"72a2440796ba12b3cd1daba7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other occasions for forming her observations were sure to arise. Anne had before long been often enough in the company of all four together to form an opinion, though she was too prudent to own it at home, where she knew it would have pleased neither husband nor wife; for though she thought Louisa rather the favourite, she could not help believing, so far as memory and experience allowed her to decide, that Captain Wentworth was in love with neither. They were more in love with him; yet even there it was not love. It was a slight fever of admiration; but it might, and likely must, end in love with one of them. Charles Hayter appeared conscious of being neglected, while Henrietta sometimes seemed divided between the two. Anne wished she had the power to show them all what they were doing, and to point out some of the dangers to which they exposed themselves. She suspected no one of design. It was her greatest comfort to think Captain Wentworth wholly unaware of the pain he caused. There was no triumph, no ungenerous triumph, in his manner. He had probably never heard of, nor considered, any claims on Henrietta from Charles Hayter. He was wrong only in receiving\u2014for receiving was the proper word\u2014the attentions of two young women at the same time.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter appeared to withdraw from the contest. Three days had gone by without his once visiting Uppercross; a very marked alteration. He had even declined a customary dinner invitation; and Mr Musgrove having found him on that occasion with several large books before him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were convinced that something must be amiss, and spoke gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had given him a direct dismissal, while her husband continually expected to see him the next day. Anne could only think Charles Hayter wise.\\n\\nOne morning, around this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth having gone shooting together, the sisters at the Cottage were sitting quietly at their work when the sisters from the Mansion-house appeared at the window.\\n\\nIt was a particularly fine November day, and the Miss Musgroves crossed the little grounds, stopping only to say that they meant to take a _long_ walk, and therefore supposed Mary would not wish to accompany them; and when Mary at once answered, somewhat jealous of not being thought a good walker, \u201cOh, yes, I should like to join you very much, I am very fond of a long walk;\u201d Anne was convinced, from the looks exchanged by the two girls, that this was exactly what they had not wanted, and again admired the kind of necessity produced by family habits, by which everything had to be communicated and everything done together, however unwelcome or inconvenient.\"}"
