          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a3c964c96713d12c6165315\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is only four o\u2019clock, but it is winter, and the sun has already gone down. No clouds cross the clear, frosty sky to catch its slanting rays; yet the air itself is faintly rose-coloured, and that colour is reflected on the snow covering the ground. I live in an isolated cottage on a broad, lonely heath: no sound of life reaches me. Before me lies the desolate plain, white except for a few black patches made by the midday sun on the tops of those sharp hillocks, where the snow, slipping as it fell, settled less deeply than on the level ground. A few birds peck at the hard ice over the pools\u2014for the frost has lasted long.\\n\\nI am in a strange state of mind. I am alone\u2014entirely alone\u2014in the world. Misfortune has passed over me like a blight and left me withered. I know I am about to die, and I feel happy\u2014joyful. I feel my pulse; it beats quickly. I lay my thin hand against my cheek; it burns. A slight, swift spirit remains within me, now giving off its final sparks. I will never see the snows of another winter. I truly believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of a summer sun; and in that belief I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a history like mine should die with me, but some feeling I cannot name urges me onward, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist even the smallest impulse. While life was strong in me, I believed there was a sacred horror in my story that made it unfit to be spoken; now, near death, I profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the grove of the Eumenides: none but the dying may enter it; and Oedipus is about to die.\\n\\nWhat am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know whether anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them when I die. I do not address them to you alone, because I would take pleasure in lingering over our friendship in a manner that would be unnecessary if you were the only reader of what I write. Therefore I will tell my story as though I were writing for strangers. You have often asked why I live so alone; why I weep; and, above all, why my silence is so impenetrable and unkind. In life I did not dare; in death I reveal the mystery. Others may turn these pages over carelessly. But to you, Woodville\u2014kind, loving friend\u2014they will be dear: precious remains of a heartbroken girl who, even as she dies, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall upon the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will. And while I still live, I thank you for your sympathy.\"}"
