          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9b96e0e90491f2a892041159\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well past noon when he woke. His valet had quietly entered the room several times on tiptoe to see whether he was moving, and had wondered why his young master was sleeping so late. At last the bell rang, and Victor came in softly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters on a small tray of old S\u00e8vres china. He drew back the olive-satin curtains, lined with shining blue silk, that covered the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept well this morning,\u201d he said with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked sleepily.\\n\\n\u201cA quarter past one, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He sat up, sipped some tea, and looked through his letters. One was from Lord Henry and had been delivered by hand that morning. He paused for a moment, then set it aside. He opened the others without much interest. They contained the usual cards, dinner invitations, tickets to private exhibitions, charity-concert programmes, and similar things that fashionable young men received every morning during the season. There was a fairly large bill for an engraved silver Louis-Quinze toilet set, which he had not yet dared send to his guardians. They were very old-fashioned people and did not understand that, in this age, useless things are our only necessities. There were also several politely written letters from Jermyn Street moneylenders, offering to lend him any amount at once and at the most reasonable interest rates.\\n\\nAfter about ten minutes, he rose and put on an elaborate dressing-gown of cashmere wool embroidered with silk. Then he went into the bathroom, whose floor was paved with onyx. The cool water refreshed him after his long sleep. He seemed to have forgotten everything he had been through. Once or twice, a faint feeling came over him that he had played a part in some strange tragedy, but it had the unreal quality of a dream.\\n\\nAs soon as he was dressed, he went into the library and sat down to a light French breakfast laid out on a small round table near the open window. It was a beautiful day. The warm air seemed full of spice. A bee flew in and buzzed around the blue dragon bowl, filled with sulphur-yellow roses, that stood before him. He felt completely happy.\\n\\nThen his eyes fell on the screen he had placed before the portrait, and he started.\\n\\n\u201cIs Monsieur too cold?\u201d his valet asked, setting an omelette on the table. \u201cShall I close the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI am not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
