          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b747a6cd7059e6dabec69199\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were taking place in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the very one from which the girl had taken Oliver\u2014brooding beside a dim, smoky fire. A pair of bellows lay across his knee, and he had apparently been trying to stir the fire into a brighter blaze; but he had sunk into deep thought. With his arms folded over them and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared absently at the rusty grate.\\n\\nAt a table behind him sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist. The Artful played dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. That gentleman\u2019s face, always unusually sharp and intelligent, gained fresh interest from the close attention he gave the game and the careful study he made of Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand. From time to time, whenever opportunity allowed, he cast it a number of earnest looks, wisely adjusting his own play according to what he observed in his neighbour\u2019s cards. Since it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat, as he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he thought it necessary to refresh himself from the quart pot on the table, filled and ready with gin and water for the company\u2019s use.\\n\\nMaster Bates, too, watched the game closely; but being more excitable than his accomplished friend, he turned more often to the gin and water, and also gave way to many jokes and irrelevant comments, all quite unsuitable to a serious rubber. Indeed, the Artful, relying on their close friendship, more than once solemnly lectured his companion on these improprieties. Master Bates took every rebuke in excellent humour, merely asking his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly phrased joke of the same sort, whose aptness greatly impressed Mr. Chitling. It was remarkable that Mr. Chitling and his partner always lost; and that, instead of irritating Master Bates, this seemed to amuse him enormously. At the end of every deal he laughed uproariously and declared he had never seen such a jolly game in all his life.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s two doubles and the rub,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, looking very miserable as he pulled a half-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never saw such a fellow as you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
