{"request_id":"9aafc1928d6ee40a4cbf5062","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Their killers were physical beings, and they escaped by physical means. But how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason about this question, and it must bring us to a clear answer. Let us consider, one by one, every possible way out. Clearly, when the group came up the stairs, the murderers were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the adjoining room. Therefore, we need look for exits only from these two rooms. The police had stripped open the floors, ceilings, and wall masonry in every direction. No hidden passage could have escaped their attention. Still, I did not rely on their eyes alone, and examined them myself. There were no hidden exits. Both doors from the rooms into the passage were firmly locked, with the keys on the inside. Let us consider the chimneys. Though they were of normal width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, they would not allow the body of even a large cat to pass through their full length. Since escape by the means already mentioned is absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have escaped through those in the front room without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must therefore have gone through the windows of the back room. Having reached this conclusion so clearly, we must not reject it merely because it seems impossible. We need only prove that these seeming impossibilities are not truly impossible.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One is not blocked by furniture and can be seen completely. The lower part of the other is hidden by the headboard of the heavy bed, which has been pushed close against it. The first was found securely fastened from within. It resisted every effort to raise it. A large hole had been bored into its frame on the left, and a very strong nail had been driven into it almost to the head. On examining the other window, a similar nail was found in the same way; and a forceful attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were then fully convinced that no escape had occurred through these windows. Therefore, they considered it unnecessary to remove the nails and open them.

“My own examination was more careful, for the reason I have just stated: I knew that here all apparent impossibilities had to be shown not to be impossible in fact."}

