          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9cc9bca7e57d80d6125fb455\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up after a while, and look around to find what caused it, and maybe spot a steamboat puffing up-stream, so far over toward the other shore you couldn\u2019t make out anything about her except whether she was stern-wheel or side-wheel; then for maybe an hour there wouldn\u2019t be a sound nor a sight\u2014nothing but downright lonesomeness. By-and-by you\u2019d see a raft drifting past, way off yonder, and perhaps some galoot aboard chopping wood, because they\u2019re nearly always at it on a raft; you\u2019d watch the axe gleam and fall\u2014you don\u2019t hear a thing; you see that axe rise again, and when it gets above the man\u2019s head you hear the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014it had taken all that while to travel across the water. That was how we spent the day, loafing about and hearkening to the quiet. Once there came a heavy fog, and the rafts and such things passing by were banging tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run them down. A scow or raft passed so near that we could hear them talking, swearing, and laughing\u2014hear them clear enough; but we couldn\u2019t see the least sign of them; it gave you a creepy feeling; it was like spirits making merry in the air. Jim said he reckoned they were spirits; but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern this dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as night came on, we shoved off; once we had her about midstream, we left her be and let her drift wherever the current chose to carry her; then we lighted our pipes, hung our legs in the water, and talked of every sort of thing\u2014we went naked all the time, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it\u2014the new clothes Buck\u2019s people had made me were too fine to feel easy in, and anyhow I never cared much for clothes.\\n\\nSometimes we had that whole river to ourselves for a mighty long spell. There were the banks and islands yonder, across the water; and maybe a spark\u2014which was a candle shining in a cabin window; and sometimes you could see one or two sparks out on the water\u2014on a raft or scow, you know; and perhaps hear a fiddle or a song floating over from one of those crafts. It is lovely living on a raft. Above us was the sky, all sprinkled with stars, and we used to lie on our backs looking at them, and argue whether they had been made or had merely happened. Jim allowed they were made, but I allowed they happened; I reckoned it would have taken too long to _make_ so many.\"}"
