          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ccbfe27f6dbaa804a558b3fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I call upon you, spirits of the dead, and you, wandering agents of vengeance, to help and guide me in my task. Let the cursed monster from hell drink deeply of suffering; let him know the despair that now tortures me.\u201d\\n\\nI began this appeal with such solemnity and awe that I almost believed the spirits of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion. But as I finished, fury took hold of me, and rage choked off my words.\\n\\nThe silence of the night was answered by a loud, devilish laugh. It sounded in my ears, long and heavy; the mountains echoed it, and I felt as though all hell had surrounded me with scorn and laughter. Surely, at that moment, I would have gone mad and destroyed my wretched life, if my vow had not been heard and I had not been kept alive for vengeance. The laughter faded, and then a familiar, hated voice, seeming to speak close beside my ear, whispered clearly, \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have chosen to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward the place where the sound had come from, but the devil escaped my grasp. Suddenly, the broad moon rose and shone fully on his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with speed beyond that of any human being.\\n\\nI chased him, and for many months this has been my work. Following a faint clue, I traced the winding course of the Rhone, but without success. Then the blue Mediterranean came into view, and by strange chance I saw the fiend enter a ship at night and hide aboard it; it was bound for the Black Sea. I booked passage on the same vessel, but he escaped, though I do not know how.\\n\\nThrough the wild regions of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to avoid me, I have always followed his trail. Sometimes peasants, frightened by this horrible figure, told me which way he had gone. At other times he himself, fearing that I would lose every trace of him, despair, and die, left some sign to lead me onward. Snow fell upon my head, and I saw the marks of his enormous footsteps across the white plain. You, just beginning life, for whom worry is new and suffering unknown\u2014how can you understand what I have felt, and still feel? Cold, hunger, and exhaustion were the smallest pains I was meant to endure. Some devil had cursed me, and I carried my own eternal hell within me. Yet a good spirit still followed and guided my steps, and when I complained most bitterly, it would suddenly free me from difficulties that seemed impossible to overcome. Sometimes, when hunger had overcome nature and I collapsed from exhaustion, a meal was prepared for me in the wilderness, restoring my strength and courage.\"}"
