          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2755a18c5a3e83af277f1f38\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond paced angrily up and down the room. I stood prepared to hear and answer his accusations. Adrian leaned against the wall. \u201cThis is utterly absurd,\u201d he cried. \u201cIf you were schoolboys, you could not behave more foolishly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou do not understand,\u201d said Raymond. \u201cThis is only one part of a system\u2014a plan of tyranny to which I will never submit. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the only slave in its empire? Is my privacy to be invaded, my actions criticized, my friends insulted? But I will cast off the whole thing at once.\u2014Be my witnesses.\u201d He took the star, the badge of his office, from his breast and threw it onto the table. \u201cI give up my office; I surrender my power\u2014let whoever wishes take it!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet him take it,\u201d exclaimed Adrian, \u201cwho can call himself, or whom the world can call, your superior. No man in England has such arrogance. Know yourself, Raymond, and your anger will fade; your confidence will return. A few months ago, whenever we prayed for the prosperity of our country or ourselves, we also prayed for the life and welfare of the Protector, knowing it was inseparably joined to our own. Your time was devoted to our good; your ambition was to win our praise. You adorned our towns with buildings, gave us useful institutions, and made the land richly fertile. The powerful and unjust trembled before your court, while the poor and oppressed rose like flowers awakened by morning beneath the sunshine of your protection.\\n\\n\u201cCan you wonder that we are shocked and grieve when this seems to have changed? But come, this bitter mood has already passed. Resume your duties; your supporters will welcome you, your enemies will be silenced, and our love, honour, and duty will again be shown to you. Master yourself, Raymond, and the world is subject to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this would be very sensible if you said it to someone else,\u201d Raymond replied gloomily. \u201cLearn the lesson yourself, and you, the first peer of the land, may become its ruler. You\u2014the good, wise, and just\u2014may govern every heart. But I see, too soon for my own happiness and too late for England\u2019s good, that I took on a task beyond my strength. I cannot govern myself. My passions rule me; my smallest impulse is my tyrant. Do you think I gave up the Protectorate\u2014and I have given it up\u2014in a moment of anger? By the living God, I swear I will never take up that trinket again; never again burden myself with the weight of care and misery of which it is the visible sign.\u201d\"}"
