          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"058a3b6a7d54cd97a30be9b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day, the ghost was very weak and exhausted. The terrible strain of the past four weeks was beginning to tell on him. His nerves were completely shattered, and he jumped at the slightest sound. For five days he stayed in his room, and finally decided to abandon the matter of the bloodstain on the library floor. If the Otis family did not want it, they plainly did not deserve it. They were clearly people on a low, material level of existence, quite unable to appreciate the symbolic value of sensory phenomena. The matter of ghostly appearances and the development of astral bodies was, of course, entirely different, and not really under his control. It was his solemn duty to appear in the corridor once a week, and to gibber from the large oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of every month, and he could not see how he could honorably avoid these obligations. It was true that he had led a very wicked life, but on the other hand, he was extremely conscientious in everything connected with the supernatural. For the next three Saturdays, therefore, he walked the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every possible care not to be heard or seen. He took off his boots, stepped as lightly as he could over the old worm-eaten boards, wore a large black velvet cloak, and made sure to use the Rising Sun Lubricator to oil his chains. I must admit that he found it rather difficult to bring himself to adopt this final precaution. Still, one night, while the family was at dinner, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and took the bottle. At first he felt somewhat humiliated, but afterward he was sensible enough to recognize that the invention had much to recommend it and, to some extent, served his purpose. Yet despite everything, he was not left alone. Strings were constantly stretched across the corridor, and he tripped over them in the dark; and on one occasion, dressed as \u2018Black Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u2019 he fell badly after stepping on a butter-slide the twins had built from the entrance of the Tapestry Chamber to the top of the oak staircase. This final insult enraged him so much that he resolved to make one last effort to assert his dignity and social standing. The following night, he decided to visit the insolent young Etonians in his famous role of \u2018Reckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u2019\\n\\nHe had not appeared in that costume for more than seventy years\u2014indeed, not since he had frightened pretty Lady Barbara Modish so badly with it that she abruptly ended her engagement to the present Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather and ran away to Gretna Green with handsome Jack Castleton. She declared that nothing in the world could persuade her to marry into a family that allowed such a dreadful phantom to pace up and down the terrace at twilight.\"}"
