          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"525fec561f093b0468d084df\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed by, and it was deep winter now. I had gone to school nearly all the time and could spell, read, and write a little, and could recite the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five, and I don\u2019t reckon I could ever get beyond that if I lived forever. I don\u2019t put much faith in mathematics, anyhow.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got where I could bear it. Whenever I got especially tired of it I played hookey, and the whipping I got the next day did me good and brightened me up. So the longer I attended school, the easier it became. I was getting somewhat accustomed to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they weren\u2019t so hard on me. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed cramped me pretty badly most of the time, but before cold weather came I used to slip out and sleep in the woods now and then, and that rested me. I liked the old ways best, but I was beginning to like the new ones too, a little. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely, and doing very satisfactory. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast I happened to upset the salt-cellar. I grabbed for some salt as fast as I could, to toss over my left shoulder and ward off the bad luck, but Miss Watson got there before me and stopped me. She says, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you are forever making!\u201d The widow spoke up kindly for me, but that wasn\u2019t going to turn away the bad luck; I knew that well enough. After breakfast I set out feeling troubled and unsteady, wondering where it would strike me and what it would be. There are ways to prevent certain kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those kinds; so I didn\u2019t try anything, but merely went along low in spirits and keeping watch.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you pass through the tall board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I saw somebody\u2019s footprints. They had come up from the quarry and lingered by the stile awhile, then gone on around the garden fence. It was strange they hadn\u2019t come in after standing there so long. I couldn\u2019t understand it. Somehow it was very curious. I was about to follow them around, but first I bent down to examine the tracks.\"}"
