          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5d29a287fbcdc18d1775fea5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIf I can\u2019t live on that, I won\u2019t ask anyone to help me. Is that satisfactory?\u201d\\n\\nA tremendous roar of applause rose up, and from the middle of it the king\u2019s voice called out:\\n\\n\u201cTake off his chains and set him free! Honor him, high and low, rich and poor, for he has become the king\u2019s right hand. He is clothed with power and authority, and his place is on the highest step of the throne! Now drive away this crawling night and bring back light and cheer, so that all the world may bless you.\u201d\\n\\nBut I said:\\n\\n\u201cFor an ordinary man to be shamed before the world is nothing; but it would dishonor the king if anyone who saw his minister naked did not also see him freed from his shame. If I might ask for my clothes to be brought back\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are not suitable,\u201d the king interrupted. \u201cBring clothes of another kind; dress him like a prince!\u201d\\n\\nMy plan worked. I wanted to keep things as they were until the eclipse was total; otherwise they would start trying again to make me send away the darkness, and of course I could not do that. Sending for the clothes bought some time, but not enough. So I had to find another excuse. I said it was only natural that the king might change his mind and partly regret what he had done in his excitement. Therefore, I would let the darkness continue for a while, and if, after a reasonable time, the king still felt the same, the darkness would be dismissed. Neither the king nor anyone else liked that arrangement, but I had to hold my ground.\\n\\nIt grew darker and darker, blacker and blacker, while I struggled with those awkward sixth-century clothes. At last it became completely dark, and the crowd groaned in horror as cold, strange night breezes moved through the place and the stars appeared and glittered in the sky. At last the eclipse was total, and I was very glad, though everyone else was miserable; naturally enough. I said:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, by his silence, still agrees to the terms.\u201d Then I raised my hands, held them that way for a moment, and said with the deepest solemnity: \u201cLet the enchantment dissolve and pass harmlessly away!\u201d\\n\\nFor a moment there was no answer in that deep darkness and graveyard silence. But when the silver edge of the sun appeared a moment or two later, the crowd burst into a mighty shout and rushed down like a flood to overwhelm me with blessings and thanks; and Clarence was certainly not the last among them.\"}"
