          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f367938a01c9d67b2fa318f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no labour to occupy him, he placed the knuckles of his right hand in the palm of his left, then those of his left in the palm of his right, then drew one hand over his bearded chin, and continued thus in regular alternation, without an instant\u2019s pause. To call him back from the wandering into which he invariably fell after speaking was like summoning a very feeble person from a faint, or striving, in expectation of some revelation, to detain the spirit of one who was swiftly dying.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that everything?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he stooped again to his work, until the silence was once more interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by profession?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gazing steadily at him.\\n\\nHis worn eyes turned toward Defarge, as though he would have handed the question over to him; but receiving no assistance from that direction, they sought the floor and then returned to the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by profession? No, I was not a shoemaker by profession. I-I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to--\u201d\\n\\nHe drifted away again, for several minutes, while all the while his hands kept up their measured changes. At length his eyes slowly returned to the face from which they had strayed; resting there, he started and continued, like a sleeper suddenly awakened and taking up a matter from the night before.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time I obtained it with great difficulty, and I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he stretched out his hand for the shoe that had been removed from him, Mr. Lorry said, still watching his face intently:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing about me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the floor, and he sat staring fixedly at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d; Mr. Lorry put his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm; \u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time, come back into your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nAs the prisoner of so many years sat gazing fixedly, now at Mr. Lorry and now at Defarge, certain long-erased traces of an alert and earnest intelligence in the centre of his forehead gradually pressed through the dark cloud that had settled over him. They were clouded over once more, they grew dimmer, they vanished; yet they had appeared.\"}"
