          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46d954673c3c5f5f45fea44c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you would have continued, if you had been left alone. I wonder when you would have spoken if I had not asked you! My decision to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had a great effect. Too great, I am afraid; for where is the moral, if our happiness comes from breaking a promise? I ought not to have mentioned it. This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou need not trouble yourself. The moral is perfectly sound. Lady Catherine\u2019s unreasonable attempts to separate us removed all my doubts. I do not owe my present happiness to your wish to express your gratitude. I was not in the mood to wait for an opportunity from you. What my aunt told me gave me hope, and I was determined to learn everything at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been of endless use, which ought to please her, since she loves being useful. But tell me, why did you come down to Netherfield? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and be embarrassed? Or did you intend something more serious?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true purpose was to see you, and to decide, if I could, whether I might ever hope to make you love me. My stated purpose\u2014or what I told myself it was\u2014was to see whether your sister still cared for Bingley; and, if she did, to make the confession to him that I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the courage to tell Lady Catherine what is going to happen to her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more likely to need time than courage, Elizabeth. But it ought to be done; and if you will give me a sheet of paper, it shall be done at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I did not have a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire the neatness of your handwriting, as another young lady once did. But I too have an aunt, who must not be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she had been unwilling to admit how much her closeness with Mr. Darcy had been exaggerated, Elizabeth had not yet answered Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter. But now, having news to share that she knew would delight her, she was almost ashamed to realize that her uncle and aunt had already lost three days of happiness. She immediately wrote as follows:--\\n\\n\u201cI should have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I ought, for your long, kind, satisfying account of everything; but, to tell the truth, I was too upset to write. You imagined more than truly existed. But now imagine as much as you please; let your fancy run free, let your imagination take every flight the subject allows, and unless you believe me actually married, you cannot be very far wrong.\u201d\"}"
