ee? He had never spoken of their marriage except as something far in the future.\\n\\nWhen they first raised the subject with him, he was so unhappy that they nearly lost hope. A second mention caused him less pain. He began to think it would happen, and that he could not stop it\u2014a very hopeful step toward accepting it. Still, he was not happy. In fact, he seemed so unhappy that his daughter's courage failed. She could not bear to see him suffer or know that he imagined himself neglected; and though her judgment almost accepted the assurance of both Mr. Knightleys that, once it was over, his distress would soon pass as well, she hesitated\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
