          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2e598df87413689dbc4d730f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made no further effort. The boys lay still, counting the slow-dragging minutes until it seemed that time itself must be exhausted and eternity turning gray; then they were thankful to see that the sun was finally going down.\\n\\nPresently one snore stopped. Injun Joe raised himself, looked about\u2014grinned grimly at his companion, whose head hung upon his knees\u2014roused him with his foot, and said:\\n\\n\u201cHere! _You\u2019re_ the watchman, ain\u2019t you! Well, all right\u2014nothing\u2019s happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy! have I been asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, a little, a little. It\u2019s nearly time we were moving, pard. What are we going to do with the little swag we\u2019ve got left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here, as we always have, I reckon. No point in carrying it off till we head south. Six hundred and fifty in silver is something to lug.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014all right\u2014it won\u2019t hurt to come here again.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I\u2019d say come at night, as we used to\u2014it\u2019s safer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but see here; it may be quite a while before I get the proper chance at that job; accidents could happen; it ain\u2019t in such a very good spot; we\u2019ll bury it regular\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood notion,\u201d said the companion, who crossed the room, knelt, lifted one of the back hearth-stones, and drew out a bag that jingled agreeably. He took twenty or thirty dollars from it for himself, and the same amount for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to the latter, who was now kneeling in the corner and digging with his bowie-knife.\\n\\nIn a moment the boys forgot every fear and misery. They watched each motion with greedy eyes. Luck!\u2014its magnificence surpassed imagination! Six hundred dollars was enough to make half a dozen boys wealthy! Here was treasure-hunting under the most fortunate conditions\u2014there would be no troublesome doubt about where to dig. They nudged one another constantly\u2014expressive nudges, readily understood, for they meant only\u2014\u201cOh, but ain\u2019t you glad _now_ we\u2019re here!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife struck something.\\n\\n\u201cHello!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d asked his comrade.\\n\\n\u201cA half-rotted plank\u2014no, I reckon it\u2019s a box. Here\u2014lend a hand and we\u2019ll find what it\u2019s doing here. Never mind, I\u2019ve knocked a hole in it.\u201d\\n\\nHe thrust in his hand and pulled it out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men inspected the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys overhead were as excited as the men, and as pleased.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s companion said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll make short work of this. There\u2019s an old rusty pick among the weeds in the corner on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I noticed it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
