          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4fdb42aaecf1590f4e7a69a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMark my words: I have learned the language of despair. I know it all by heart, for I am Despair itself\u2014a strange creature, joyful and triumphant Despair. Yet those words are false, for the wave may be dark, but it is not bitter. We lie down, close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. Come, then\u2014delay no longer, you slow one! See the pleasant drink! Look: it is a spirit of good, not a human girl, who invites you and, in persuasive tones\u2014oh, that they might persuade you\u2014says, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I watched his face. His perfect beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, and his gentle but earnest expression of protest and wonder, even before he spoke, changed my strained feelings. All the harshness of despair left me, and I felt only the deepest sorrow. I saw that his eyes too were wet as he took both my hands in his. Sitting beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad act to which you would lead me, dearest friend. Your grief must indeed be great to have filled you with such unhappy thoughts. You long for death, yet fear it, and wish me to go with you. But I have less courage than you; even with your company, I do not dare to die. Listen to me, then consider whether you should try to draw me into your plan, even if, with despair\u2019s overpowering eloquence, you could make black death seem so inviting that bright heaven appeared dark. Listen, I beg you, to one who has himself fed desperate thoughts and longed impatiently for death, but who at last has crushed that phantom beneath his feet and broken its sting. As you have played Despair with me, I will play Una with you and lead you safely from his dark cave. Listen to me, and let yourself be moved by words untouched by selfish passion.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know the meaning of this vast world, with its strange mixture of good and evil. But we have been placed here and told to live and hope. I do not know what we are meant to hope for, but there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that is our task on earth. If misfortune comes against us, we must struggle with her; we must cast her aside and continue on, seeking what our nature desires. Whether this hope of future good prepares us for another life, I do not know; or whether we are simply, like workers in God\u2019s vineyard, meant to help smooth the path for those who come after us.\"}"
