          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dc85a3afeab624c2826ccde4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would make such a delightful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed with everything, but added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith, though, is another such pretty, agreeable young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could have no better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very happy to hear it\u2014but everyone knows Jane Fairfax is so accomplished and superior!\u2014and exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis subject was discussed very pleasantly, and others of equal importance followed, passing with the same harmony; but the evening did not end without a small return of uneasiness. The gruel arrived and gave them a great deal to say\u2014much praise and many observations\u2014firm conviction that it was wholesome for every constitution, and rather severe speeches against the many houses where it was never made even tolerably well. But unfortunately, among the failures the daughter had to mention, the most recent, and therefore most striking, was that of her own cook at South End, a young woman hired for the occasion, who had never been able to understand what she meant by a bowl of nice, smooth gruel, thin but not too thin. However often she had wanted and ordered it, she had never managed to get anything tolerable. This was a dangerous opening.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. The exclamation, as Emma understood it, said, \u201cAh! There is no end to the unhappy consequences of your going to South End. It cannot be spoken of.\u201d For a little while, she hoped he would not speak of it, and that silent reflection might be enough to restore his enjoyment of his own smooth gruel. After several minutes, however, he began:\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the seaside this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir? I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you had to go to the sea, it would have been better not to go to South End. South End is unhealthy. Perry was surprised to hear that you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think so, but it is quite mistaken, sir. We were all perfectly well there, and never felt the slightest inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to suppose the place unhealthy. I am sure he can be relied upon, because he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have gone there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry spent a week at Cromer once, and considers it the best of all the sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
