          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"048f28e3c8af93a52e679dc0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She felt a strong urge to go to the outer door and see whether it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of having another reason? Captain Wentworth had to be out of sight. She rose from her seat and meant to go; one half of her ought not always to be wiser than the other, or always to suspect the other of worse motives than it truly had. She would see if it rained. But she was sent back at once by the arrival of Captain Wentworth himself, with a group of gentlemen and ladies who were clearly his friends, and whom he must have met a little way down Milsom Street. He was more plainly startled and confused by seeing her than she had ever seen him before; he turned quite red. For the first time since they had become acquainted again, she felt that she showed less feeling than he did. She had the advantage of the last few minutes to prepare herself. The first overwhelming, blinding, confusing effects of sudden surprise had passed for her. Yet she still had enough to feel. It was agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014something between happiness and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, then turned away. His manner was embarrassed. She could not have described it as cold or friendly, or anything so clearly as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short time, however, he came toward her and spoke again. They exchanged questions about ordinary matters; probably neither learned much from the other's answers, while Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than before. By being together so often, they had learned to speak with a fair appearance of calm indifference; but he could not manage it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. There was some kind of awareness between them. He looked very well, not like a man whose health or spirits had suffered, and he spoke of Uppercross, the Musgroves, even Louisa. As he named her, he gave for a moment his old knowing look; yet this was Captain Wentworth uncomfortable, uneasy, unable to pretend otherwise.\\n\\nIt did not surprise Anne, but it hurt her, to see that Elizabeth would not acknowledge him. She saw that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, and that each recognized the other inwardly. She was sure he was ready, and expected, to be greeted as an acquaintance; and it pained her to watch her sister turn away with fixed coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple's carriage, for which Miss Elliot had been growing impatient, now arrived, and the servant came in to announce it.\"}"
