{"request_id":"a5a9701047e45feec97013b1","rewritten_text":"They were her life, dearer than any place made sacred by love, dearer than everything else on earth. The boys heard of our departure with childish delight. Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I replied, and her face shone with pleasure. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. Silently, yet constantly, she had dwelt on these scenes. The memory of them had changed her childish gaiety into seriousness and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

There were many beloved friends, humble though they were, whom we could not leave behind. There was the lively, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; Alfred’s dog; and a pet eagle whose eyes had grown dim with age. Yet as we listed these favourites to take with us, we could not help grieving over our great losses and sighing for the many things we must leave behind. Tears filled Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought first a favourite rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting that these must go. They cried out at the pity of not being able to take the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, cherished thing with us. “Dear foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost forever treasures far more precious than these, and we leave them to save treasures beside which they are nothing. Let us not forget, even for a moment, our purpose and our hope; they will stand like a strong barrier against the flood of regret we feel for such trifles.”

The children were easily diverted and soon returned to thoughts of future pleasures. Idris had vanished. She had gone to conceal her weakness. Leaving the castle, she descended into the little park and sought solitude, so that she might give herself to tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing its rough trunk with her rosy lips, while tears poured down and she could not suppress her sobs and broken cries. With overwhelming grief, I saw this beloved creature so lost in sorrow. I drew her to me, and as she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms around her, she returned to the awareness of what still remained to her. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter, unbearable sorrow tears at my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children remain to me.”"}

