          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92fc6d3fd896e0628c0b9618\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I call upon you, spirits of the dead, and you, wandering agents of vengeance, to help and guide me in my task. Let the accursed, hellish monster drink deeply of suffering; let him know the despair that now tortures me.\u201d\\n\\nI began my invocation with a solemnity and awe that almost convinced me the spirits of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion; but as I finished, fury took hold of me, and rage choked my voice.\\n\\nThe stillness of the night was answered by a loud, fiendish laugh. It struck my ears, long and harsh; the mountains echoed it, and I felt as though all hell had surrounded me with scorn and laughter. Surely, at that moment, I would have been driven mad and destroyed my wretched life, had my vow not been heard and had I not been preserved for vengeance. The laughter faded, and then a familiar, hated voice, seeming to speak close beside my ear, said in an audible whisper, \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have chosen to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward the place from which the sound came, but the devil slipped from my grasp. Suddenly, the broad moon rose and shone fully upon his ghastly, misshapen form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI pursued him, and for many months this has been my purpose. Following a faint clue, I traced the winding course of the Rhone, but without success. The blue Mediterranean came into view, and by strange chance I saw the fiend enter a ship at night and conceal himself aboard a vessel bound for the Black Sea. I booked passage on the same ship, but he escaped, though I do not know how.\\n\\nThrough the wild regions of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to evade me, I have always followed his trail. At times peasants, terrified by that dreadful apparition, told me which way he had gone; at other times he himself, fearing that if I lost every trace of him I would despair and die