          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"721c3a3caf7b0b9f01943487\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If it had depended on me, he would never have escaped. Morgan le Fay detested him with all her heart, and she would never have relented toward him. Yet his offense sprang from carelessness rather than intentional wickedness. He had remarked that she possessed red hair. Why, she did; but it was not proper to describe it so. When red-haired persons rise above a certain station, their hair becomes auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: of these forty-seven prisoners, five had names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment that were no longer remembered! There was one woman and four men--all stooped, shriveled, and mind-darkened patriarchs. They themselves had forgotten such particulars ages ago; or, at least, they held only hazy notions of them, nothing certain, and nothing they told twice alike. The line of priests whose duty had been to pray each day with the prisoners, remind them that God had placed them there for some wise end, and instruct them that patience, meekness, and submission to oppression were what He delighted to find in persons of inferior degree, possessed traditions concerning these poor wrecks of humanity, but no more than that. Those traditions reached only a little distance, since they dealt with the duration of imprisonment, not with the names of the offenses. Even tradition could establish only that none of the five had beheld daylight for thirty-five years: how much longer they had endured that deprivation could not be guessed. The king and queen knew nothing of these wretched beings, save that they were heirlooms, property inherited with the throne from the previous concern. No account of their past had accompanied their persons, and thus their new proprietors had judged them worthless and taken no interest in them. I said to the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why on earth didn't you free them?\u201d\\n\\nIt was a bewildering question. She did not know why she had not; the matter had simply never entered her thoughts. Thus she was, without realizing it, foreshadowing the actual history of the future prisoners of the Castle d'If. It was clear to me now that, under her upbringing, these inherited captives were simply property--no more and no less. Well, when property comes to us by inheritance, we do not think of casting it aside, even if we place no value upon it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats into the upper world and the blazing afternoon sunlight--having first blindfolded them, out of mercy for eyes so long unassailed by light--they made a sight indeed. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pitiable horrors, every one; the most legitimate possible offspring of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
