          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da3be91a0630828402cea3b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Indeed, he was dressed as \u2018Jonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u2019 one of his most impressive roles. The Cantervilles had good reason to remember it, since it had been the true cause of their quarrel with their neighbour, Lord Rufford. It was about a quarter past two in the morning, and, as far as he could tell, no one was awake. But as he walked toward the library to see whether any sign of the bloodstain remained, two figures suddenly sprang out at him from a dark corner, waving their arms wildly over their heads and shrieking \u2018BOO!\u2019 into his ear.\\n\\nNaturally terrified, he fled toward the staircase, only to find Washington Otis waiting there with the large garden syringe. Surrounded by enemies on every side and nearly trapped, he disappeared into the great iron stove, which luckily was not lit. He had to find his way home through the flues and chimneys, reaching his room in a dreadful state of dirt, confusion, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was never seen on another midnight adventure. The twins waited for him several times and scattered nutshells through the passages every night, greatly annoying their parents and the servants, but it did no good. Clearly, his feelings had been so badly hurt that he would not show himself. Mr. Otis therefore returned to his great history of the Democratic Party, which he had been working on for years; Mrs. Otis arranged a remarkable clam-bake that amazed the whole county; the boys began playing lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games; and Virginia rode through the lanes on her pony with the young Duke of Cheshire, who had come to spend the last week of his holidays at Canterville Chase. Everyone assumed the ghost had gone away. Mr. Otis even wrote to Lord Canterville to say so, and Lord Canterville replied that he was delighted by the news and sent his warm congratulations to the 