          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b1c40c0b19cbf2b7b474286\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters at the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are rarely very harsh, and in autumn it is unusual indeed for anyone to think a fire necessary. Yet around the middle of October, 18\u2014, there came a remarkably cold day. Just before sunset, I made my way through the evergreens to the hut of my friend, whom I had not seen for several weeks. At that time I lived in Charleston, nine miles from the island, and travel back and forth was much less convenient than it is today. When I reached the hut, I knocked, as I usually did. Receiving no answer, I looked for the key where I knew it was hidden, unlocked the door, and entered. A fine fire blazed on the hearth. It was an unusual sight, and a welcome one. I took off my overcoat, settled into an armchair beside the crackling logs, and patiently waited for my hosts to return.\\n\\nSoon after nightfall they arrived and welcomed me warmly. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried about preparing marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his spells\u2014what else could I call them?\u2014of excitement. He had discovered an unknown bivalve, one that formed a new genus; and beyond that, with Jupiter\u2019s help, he had tracked down and captured a scarab beetle that he believed was entirely new. He wanted my opinion of it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, warming my hands over the fire and wishing the entire race of scarabs at the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I had known you were here!\u201d said Legrand. \u201cBut it has been so long since I saw you, and how could I have guessed that you would visit on this particular night? On my way home, I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and foolishly lent him the bug. So you cannot see it until morning. Stay here tonight, and I\u2019ll send Jup for it at sunrise. It is the loveliest thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNonsense! No\u2014the bug. It is a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back and another, somewhat longer one, at the other. Its antennae are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere ain\u2019t no tin in him, Massa Will; I keep tellin\u2019 you,\u201d Jupiter interrupted. \u201cDat bug is a gold bug, solid gold, every bit of him, inside and all, except his wings. Never felt a bug half so heavy in my life.\u201d\"}"
