          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14b581e7b8373453895439ba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings had prevailed over her self-interest, and, for the young man\u2019s sake, she had given up the chance of continuing her designs upon Sir Walter. Yet she possesses talents as well as feelings; and it remains uncertain whether his artifice or hers will ultimately succeed; whether, having kept her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he may not himself be coaxed and fondled at last into making her Sir William\u2019s.\\n\\nThere can be no question that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were astonished and humiliated by the departure of their companion, and by finding how she had deceived them. They had, certainly, their distinguished cousins to turn to for consolation; but they must for a long while perceive that flattering and attending on others, without receiving flattery and attendance in return, affords only half the pleasure.\\n\\nAnne, having been convinced very early that Lady Russell intended to love Captain Wentworth as she should, knew no drawback to the happiness before her except the awareness that she had no relations to offer him whom a sensible man could esteem. In this she felt her own deficiency most acutely. The inequality of their fortunes was nothing, and never caused her a moment\u2019s sorrow; but to possess no family capable of welcoming and valuing him as he deserved, nothing respectable, united, or kindly disposed to return the merit and eager reception he found among his brothers and sisters, gave her as much pain as her mind could feel amid such otherwise abundant happiness. She had only two friends in the world to add to his number\u2014Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. To both, however, he was quite willing to become attached. Lady Russell, despite all her former errors, he could now esteem sincerely. Though he was not required to declare that she had been right in separating them at first, he was prepared to say nearly everything else in her praise; and Mrs Smith had claims of several sorts to win his prompt and lasting regard.\\n\\nHer late kindnesses to Anne were sufficient of themselves; and their marriage, rather than taking one friend from her, gave her two. She was their first visitor after they were settled; and Captain Wentworth, by enabling her to recover her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies, by writing and acting on her behalf, and by guiding her through every small difficulty of the affair with the energy and exertion of a fearless man and steadfast friend, completely repaid the services she had done, or had ever intended to do, for his wife.\"}"
