          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3af0ab32474f6f7d0c11b66b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last, the narrow streets and alleys opened into a large square, with animal pens scattered around it and other signs of a cattle market. Sikes slowed his pace when they reached it, for the girl could no longer keep up with the speed at which they had been walking. Turning to Oliver, he roughly ordered him to take Nancy\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cDo you hear me?\u201d Sikes growled, as Oliver hesitated and looked around.\\n\\nThey stood in a dark corner, well away from any passers-by.\\n\\nOliver saw only too clearly that resistance would do no good. He held out his hand, and Nancy gripped it tightly in hers.\\n\\n\u201cGive me the other,\u201d said Sikes, taking Oliver\u2019s free hand. \u201cHere, Bull\u2019s-Eye!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog looked up and growled.\\n\\n\u201cLook here, boy!\u201d said Sikes, putting his other hand around Oliver\u2019s throat. \u201cIf he says even the quietest word, hold him! Understand?\u201d\\n\\nThe dog growled again and, licking his lips, watched Oliver as though eager to fasten himself on his throat without delay.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s as willing as any Christian, strike me blind if he isn\u2019t!\u201d said Sikes, looking at the animal with grim, savage approval. \u201cNow you know what to expect, master, so shout as soon as you please; the dog will soon put a stop to that. Move on, young \u2019un!\u201d\\n\\nBull\u2019s-Eye wagged his tail in response to this unusually affectionate manner of address and, giving another warning growl for Oliver\u2019s benefit, led the way onward.\\n\\nThey were crossing Smithfield, though it might as well have been Grosvenor Square, for all Oliver knew. The night was dark and foggy. The shop lights could scarcely force their way through the thick mist, which grew denser every moment and wrapped the streets and houses in gloom, making the unfamiliar place seem stranger still to Oliver and his uncertainty more miserable and oppressive.\\n\\nThey had hurried only a few steps when a deep church bell struck the hour. At its first stroke, his two companions stopped and turned their heads toward the sound.\\n\\n\u201cEight o\u2019clock, Bill,\u201d said Nancy when the bell stopped.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of telling me that? I can hear it, can\u2019t I?\u201d Sikes replied.\\n\\n\u201cI wonder whether they can hear it,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cOf course they can,\u201d Sikes answered. \u201cIt was Bartholomew time when I was taken; and there wasn\u2019t a penny trumpet at the fair whose squealing I couldn\u2019t hear. After they locked me up for the night, the noise and racket outside made that thundering old jail so quiet that I could almost have smashed my brains against the iron plates on the door.\u201d\"}"
