          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d4550e51b17454d1b6e4da79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading over his letter once more while answering it had so tendering an effect, that it became especially needful to strengthen herself with a few firm expressions; and she was so deeply troubled by the thought of making him unhappy, so occupied with what his mother and sisters might think and say, and so desirous that they should not consider her ungrateful, that Emma believed, had the young man crossed her path at that instant, he would have been accepted in the end.\\n\\nThe letter, however, was written, sealed, and dispatched. The matter was over, and Harriet was secure. She was somewhat depressed throughout the evening, but Emma could make allowance for her affectionate regrets, and would sometimes soothe them by speaking of her own attachment, sometimes by introducing the subject of Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be asked to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she said in a rather mournful voice.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were, could I possibly endure to lose you, my Harriet. You are far too essential at Hartfield to be spared for Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am certain I should never wish to go there; for I am happy nowhere except at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nA little later, she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be greatly surprised if she knew what had occurred. I am sure Miss Nash would\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, though it is only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne would be sorry to find more pride or delicacy in the mistress of a school, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such a chance as this of marriage. Even this triumph would seem important in her eyes. As for anything better suited to you, I suppose she knows nothing at all. The attentions of a certain person can scarcely yet be part of Highbury gossip. So far, I imagine you and I are the only people to whom his looks and behaviour have made their meaning clear.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet coloured and smiled, saying something of her wonder that people should be so fond of her. The thought of Mr. Elton was certainly encouraging; yet, after a while, she grew soft-hearted again toward the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow that he has received my letter,\u201d she said gently, \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not take it so very much to heart.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think instead of those among our absent friends who are engaged more happily,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this very moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, declaring how much lovelier the original is, and, after being asked for it five or six times, permitting them to hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
