{"request_id":"d29e87db8798dfc3b736dca7","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had in fact reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It therefore appeared clear to me that my speed of ascent was not merely increasing, but that its advance would have been perceptible, in some small measure, even if I had not cast off the ballast that I did. The pains in my head and ears came back at intervals with severity, and I still bled now and then from the nose; yet, on the whole, I endured far less than might have been anticipated. I breathed, nevertheless, with greater and greater difficulty every moment, and each breath was accompanied by an annoying spasmodic movement of the chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and prepared it for instant use.

“The prospect of the earth, at this stage of my ascent, was truly beautiful. Westward, northward, and southward, as far as my sight could reach, stretched an immeasurable expanse of seemingly untroubled ocean, which with every instant took on a richer and richer blue, and had already begun to show a faint convexity. At an immense distance eastward, though still perfectly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic shores of France and Spain, and a small part of the northern region of the African continent. Not the slightest sign of separate buildings could be seen, and the proudest cities of mankind had completely vanished from the earth’s face. From the rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a shadowy point, the dark Mediterranean, sprinkled with bright islands as heaven is sprinkled with stars, unfolded eastward as far as my vision went, until at last its entire body of water seemed to plunge headlong over the horizon’s abyss; and I caught myself listening, on tiptoe, for the reverberations of that mighty cataract. Above me the sky was jet black, and the stars shone with brilliant distinctness.

“The pigeons, about this time, appeared to be suffering greatly, and I resolved to set them free. I first loosened one of them, a handsome gray-mottled pigeon, and set him on the edge of the wicker-work. He seemed exceedingly distressed, glancing anxiously about, beating his wings, and uttering a loud coo; but he could not be induced to venture away from the car. At last I picked him up and flung him some half-dozen yards from the balloon. Yet he made no effort to descend, as I had expected, but strove with violent energy to return, while giving at the same time shrill, piercing cries. At length he managed to regain his old place upon the rim; but scarcely had he done so when his head sank upon his breast, and he dropped dead inside the car."}

