          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a98fc407c837baace9b77311\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I encountered the strange person of whom I am about to speak. Three qualities drew me to him: his open simplicity, his astonishing knowledge of ancient armor, and the repose of his companionship\u2014for he did all the talking. We found ourselves, as unassuming people commonly do, at the rear of the party being conducted through, and he immediately began saying things that caught my interest. As he spoke on, gently, agreeably, and with an easy flow, he appeared to slip imperceptibly out of this age and world into some distant period and long-forgotten land; and little by little he cast such an enchantment over me that I seemed to walk amid the ghosts and shades and dust and mildew of a hoary past, conversing with one of its remnants! As naturally as I should mention my closest friends or foes, or my most familiar neighbors, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great figures of the Round Table\u2014and, as he continued, how very old, unspeakably old, faded, dry, musty, and antique he himself came to seem! At last he turned to me and said, as casually as one might mention the weather or any ordinary subject\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know of transmigration of souls; do you know of transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI replied that I had never heard of it. He was so indifferent\u2014just as people are when discussing the weather\u2014that he did not observe whether I answered him or not. There was a brief silence, at once broken by the droning voice of the paid cicerone:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, dating from the sixth century, the age of King Arthur and the Round Table; reputed to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; note the round opening in the chain-mail over the left breast; cannot be explained; believed to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014possibly in malice by Cromwell's soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy companion smiled\u2014not with a modern smile, but with one that must have passed out of common use many, many centuries before\u2014and murmured, seemingly to himself:\\n\\n\u201cWit ye well, _I saw it done_.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBefore I had recovered from the electric shock of that statement, he had disappeared.\\n\\nAll that evening I sat beside my fire at the Warwick Arms, absorbed in a dream of ancient days, while rain lashed the windows and the wind bellowed around the eaves and corners. Now and then I turned to old Sir Thomas Malory's delightful book, feasting on its abundance of marvels and adventures, inhaling the perfume of its obsolete names, and dreaming once more.\"}"
