          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fb9dae6c9ecacbe54db5860d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He could not prevent it. And it enraged him to observe, or think he observed, that Becky Thatcher never for a moment imagined he was still among the living. Yet she did observe it, all the same; and she knew she was gaining her battle, too, and took pleasure in seeing him suffer as she herself had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter grew unbearable. Tom suggested that there were matters he must look after; things that had to be done; and that time was slipping away. But it was useless\u2014the girl twittered on. Tom thought, \u201cOh, hang her, am I never going to be rid of her?\u201d At last he declared he must go and attend to those matters\u2014and she innocently said she would be \u201caround\u201d when school was dismissed. He hurried off, hating her for saying it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town except that Saint Louis dandy who thinks he dresses so grand and belongs to the aristocracy! Oh, very well, I licked you the first day you ever set eyes on this town, mister, and I\u2019ll lick you again! Just wait till I catch you out! I\u2019ll just take and\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThen he acted out a beating upon an imaginary boy\u2014striking the air, kicking, and gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You holler \u2019nough, do you? Well then, let that teach you!\u201d And thus the imaginary whipping ended to his complete satisfaction.\\n\\nTom ran home at noon. His conscience could stand no more of Amy\u2019s thankful delight, and his jealousy could endure no more of the other torment. Becky went back to examining pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes crept by and Tom did not come to suffer, her triumph began to dim and she lost all interest; seriousness and abstraction came next, then sadness; two or three times she listened eagerly at a footstep, but each time it was a vain hope; Tom did not come. At last she became thoroughly wretched and wished she had not pushed matters so far. Poor Alfred, noticing that he was somehow losing her, kept crying: \u201cOh, here\u2019s a splendid one! look at this!\u201d But she finally lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care about them!\u201d Then she burst into tears, rose, and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred followed beside her and was about to try to console her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nSo the boy stopped, wondering what he could possibly have done\u2014for she had said she would look at pictures throughout the noon recess\u2014and she went on, weeping. Alfred then wandered thoughtfully into the empty schoolhouse. He was mortified and angry. He readily guessed the truth\u2014the girl had merely used him as a means of venting her spite against Tom Sawyer.\"}"
