          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5f4e7d37b147be43068d02f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to relieve you a great deal. But what gave you such a shock? What did you expect us to find?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was trapped\u2014the questioning eye was fixed on him. He would have given anything for something that sounded like a reasonable answer, but nothing came to him. The eye seemed to bore deeper and deeper. A foolish reply came to him; there was no time to consider it, so he said it weakly, on impulse:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too upset to smile, but the old man laughed loudly and happily. His whole body shook from head to foot, and when he finished he said a laugh like that was money in a man\u2019s pocket, because it reduced the doctor\u2019s bills wonderfully. Then he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old fellow, you\u2019re pale and worn out\u2014you\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re a little confused and unsteady. But you\u2019ll get over it. Rest and sleep will set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed to think he had been such a fool and shown such suspicious excitement. He had stopped believing that the package brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the talk at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only thought it was not the treasure, though\u2014he had not known for certain\u2014and the mention of a captured bundle was too much for his self-control. Still, he was glad the little incident had happened, because now he knew without doubt that the bundle was not the bundle, and his mind was peaceful and very comfortable. Everything seemed to be moving in exactly the right direction now. The treasure had to be still in No. 2; the men would be caught and put in jail that day; and he and Tom could take the gold that night without trouble or fear of being interrupted.\\n\\nJust as breakfast ended, someone knocked at the door. Huck rushed to find a hiding place, because he did not want to be connected, even slightly, with what had happened the night before. The Welshman let in several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas, and saw groups of townspeople climbing the hill to stare at the stile. The news had spread. The Welshman had to tell the visitors the story of the night. The widow openly expressed her gratitude for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t say anything about it, madam. There\u2019s someone else you may owe more to than me and my boys, but he won\u2019t let me tell his name. We wouldn\u2019t have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
