{"request_id":"d89005c9683d0f609206db09","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that no misfortune has attended the beginning of the venture you viewed with such dark fears. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to assure my dear sister that I am well and growing more confident that my undertaking will succeed.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburg, I feel a cold northern wind on my cheeks. It strengthens my spirits and fills me with delight. Do you understand this feeling? This breeze has travelled from the regions toward which I am heading, and gives me a first taste of those icy lands. Encouraged by this promising wind, my dreams grow warmer and clearer. I try in vain to convince myself that the pole is a place of frost and emptiness; in my imagination, it always appears as a land of beauty and pleasure. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible, its broad circle only touching the horizon and spreading an endless brightness. There—for, with your permission, sister, I will place some faith in earlier navigators—snow and frost are driven away; and, sailing across a calm sea, we may be carried to a land greater in wonder and beauty than any region yet discovered on the inhabited earth. Its natural products and scenery may be unlike anything known, just as the appearances of the heavenly bodies surely are in those unexplored solitudes. What might we not expect in a country of everlasting light? I may discover there the strange force that draws the compass needle, and settle a thousand observations of the heavens that need only this voyage to make their apparent irregularities understandable forever. I shall satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a part of the world never visited before, and perhaps set foot on land never marked by human feet. These hopes are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and make me begin this difficult voyage with the joy a child feels when he sets out in a small boat with his holiday companions to explore his native river. But even if all these guesses prove false, you cannot deny the priceless benefit I will give all mankind, to the final generation, by finding a passage near the pole to countries that now require so many months to reach; or by discovering the secret of magnetism, which, if it can be discovered at all, can only be done through an undertaking like mine.

These thoughts have driven away the uneasiness with which I began this letter, and I feel my heart burn with an enthusiasm that lifts me toward heaven. Nothing calms the mind so much as a firm purpose—a point on which the soul can fix its inward eye."}

