          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4dea87afef7fff91dbe9ac2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNever!\u201d the girl replied.\\n\\n\u201cTell me why.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor one reason,\u201d the girl answered firmly, \u201ca reason the lady knows and will support me in\u2014I know she will, because she has promised me. And for another reason: though he has lived a bad life, so have I. Many of us have followed the same path together, and I will not betray them. Any one of them might have betrayed me, but they did not, bad as they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d said the gentleman quickly, as though this were the point he had been trying to reach, \u201cput Monks into my hands, and leave me to deal with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat if he betrays the others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI promise you that, if the truth is forced from him, it will end there. There must be things in Oliver\u2019s early history that would be painful to bring before the public; and once the truth is known, they shall go free.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if it is not?\u201d the girl asked.\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d the gentleman continued, \u201cFagin shall not be brought to justice without your consent. In that case, I think I could show you reasons that would persuade you to give it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo I have the lady\u2019s promise of that?\u201d asked the girl.\\n\\n\u201cYou do,\u201d Rose replied. \u201cMy true and faithful word.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMonks would never find out how you learned what you know?\u201d said the girl, after a brief pause.\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d replied the gentleman. \u201cThe information would be used against him in a way he could never even suspect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have been a liar, and lived among liars, since I was a little child,\u201d said the girl after another silence, \u201cbut I will trust your words.\u201d\\n\\nAfter both assured her that she could safely do so, she began, in a voice so low that it was often hard for the listener to understand even what she meant, to describe by name and location the public house from which she had been followed that night. From the way she sometimes stopped, it seemed that the gentleman was making quick notes of the information she gave him. When she had fully explained the area, the best place to watch the house without being noticed, and the night and hour when Monks most often visited it, she paused for a few moments, trying to recall his face and appearance more clearly.\\n\\n\u201cHe is tall,\u201d said the girl, \u201cand strongly built, but not fat. He walks in a sneaking way, and as he walks he is always looking over his shoulder, first to one side and then to the other.\u201d\"}"
