{"request_id":"450c0f6db7b64ddec9dedcd5","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me: I was never truly mad. Even when my wild thoughts seemed to force me toward insanity, I was always aware of my condition, and I revealed them only to silence and solitude. Those around me saw none of it. They saw only a poor girl crushed in spirit, speaking softly and gently, with tears sometimes slipping from beneath her lowered eyes, though she tried to hide them. They saw one who loved to be alone and avoided notice; one who never smiled—oh, no, I never smiled—and that was all.

At last I escaped. I left my guardian’s house, and no one ever heard of me again. From the letters I left behind, and from other arrangements I had made, people believed that I had taken my own life. They therefore searched for me less carefully than they otherwise might have done, and soon every trace and memory of me disappeared. I left London in a small vessel sailing to a port in the north of England. Now that I had succeeded and was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently onward. I sat on deck beneath the open sky and felt myself changed. I was no longer the wild, raving, miserable Mathilda, but a young hermit, devoted to solitude, who must try to keep her heart free from all turmoil and sinful despair. The fanciful, nun-like dress I had chosen; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to myself; the solitude to which I was destined forever—all these encouraged gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze moving through my hair revived me. With quiet eyes I watched the sunlight glitter on the waves, and the birds chase one another over the water, barely touching it with their feathers. I slept without troubling dreams, and woke refreshed to enjoy again my peaceful freedom.

After four days, we reached the harbour for which we were bound. I would not stay on the coast, but went at once inland. I had already decided where I would live. It would be a lonely house on a broad plain, near no other dwelling, where I could see the whole horizon and wander far without being disturbed by the sight of other people. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the calm course of my feelings depended on being alone. I settled in a vast solitude: on a bleak heath scattered with stones, where short grass grew among them, and here and there a few rushes stood beside a small pool."}

