          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"778e2c7554e9f68cebd7f719\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes\u2019s crime, began to wonder whether an honest life might, after all, be the best one. Deciding that it certainly was, he turned away from his former life, determined to improve himself in some new line of work. For a time he struggled hard and suffered greatly; but, being naturally cheerful and guided by a good purpose, he succeeded at last. After working as a farmer\u2019s servant and a carrier\u2019s boy, he is now the happiest young grazier in all Northamptonshire.\\n\\nAnd now the hand that writes these words hesitates as it comes near the end of its task, and would gladly spin out the thread of these adventures a little longer.\\n\\nI would gladly remain a while with some of those among whom I have lived so long, and share their happiness by trying to describe it. I would show Rose Maylie in the full bloom and grace of young womanhood, casting a soft, gentle light over her quiet path in life\u2014a light that touched all who walked beside her and shone into their hearts. I would show her as the life and joy of the fireside circle and the cheerful summer gathering; I would follow her through the hot fields at noon, and hear the soft tones of her sweet voice on a moonlit evening walk. I would watch her in her goodness and charity beyond her home, and in the smiling, tireless care of her household within it. I would show her and her dead sister\u2019s child happy in their love for each other, spending whole hours imagining the friends they had so sadly lost. I would call before me once more those happy little faces gathered around her knee, and listen to their merry chatter. I would remember the sound of that clear laugh, and picture the sympathetic tear shining in her soft blue eye. These things, and a thousand looks and smiles, thoughts and turns of speech\u2014I would gladly recall every one.\\n\\nHow Mr. Brownlow continued, day by day, to fill the mind of his adopted child with knowledge, and grew more attached to him as his character unfolded and revealed the promising beginnings of all he hoped he would become; how he saw in him new traces of his early friend, stirring old memories in his own heart, sad yet sweet and comforting; how the two orphans, tested by hardship, remembered its lessons of mercy toward others, mutual love, and heartfelt thanks to Him who had protected and preserved them\u2014these are matters that need not be told.\"}"
