          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"69772b79fcb910895458fe57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She felt a strong desire to go to the outer door; she wished to discover whether it was raining. Why should she accuse herself of any other purpose? Captain Wentworth must be gone from view. She rose from her chair; she would go. One half of herself ought not always to be so much wiser than the other, nor forever suspect the other of worse intentions than it possessed. She would see whether it rained. Yet she was driven back at once by Captain Wentworth\u2019s own entrance, in company with several gentlemen and ladies, plainly known to him, whom he must have met a little way below Milsom Street. At sight of her he was more visibly startled and discomposed than she had ever seen him; he coloured deeply. For the first time since they had renewed their acquaintance, she felt herself the less affected of the two. The preceding few minutes had given her the advantage of preparation. The first overwhelming, blinding, confusing effects of a great surprise had already passed away with her. Yet she had still enough to feel! It was agitation, suffering, pleasure\u2014something poised between happiness and wretchedness.\\n\\nHe addressed her, then turned aside. Embarrassment was the quality of his manner. She could not have described it as cold, or friendly, or anything with such certainty as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a brief pause, however, he approached and spoke again. They exchanged enquiries on ordinary matters; neither, perhaps, gaining much knowledge from what was said, while Anne remained fully aware that he was less composed than he had once been. By being so frequently together, they had acquired the habit of speaking with a fair appearance of indifference and tranquillity; but he could not manage it now. Either time had altered him, or Louisa had altered him. There was some consciousness, of one kind or another. He looked remarkably well, not like a man whose health or spirits had suffered, and he spoke of Uppercross, the Musgroves, even of Louisa; and as he named her, there was for an instant his own arch expression of meaning. Yet he was Captain Wentworth uneasy, uncomfortable, unable to pretend that he was otherwise.\\n\\nIt did not astonish Anne, though it pained her, to see that Elizabeth would not recognise him. She perceived that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, that each perfectly knew the other inwardly; she was certain he stood ready, and expected, to be acknowledged as an acquaintance, and she had the pain of watching her sister turn away in unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot had become very impatient, now stopped at the door; the servant entered to announce it.\"}"
