          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f560c68fe3eb868474f6765a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To observe a star by sidelong glances\u2014to regard it obliquely, by directing toward it the outer portions of the _retina_ (which are more receptive of faint impressions of light than the inner), is to see the star clearly\u2014is to gain the truest sense of its brilliance\u2014a brilliance that fades precisely as we fix our sight _directly_ upon it. In the latter instance more rays do indeed reach the eye, yet in the former there is a finer power of apprehension. Excessive depth confuses and weakens thought; and even Venus herself may be made to disappear from the heavens by scrutiny too prolonged, too intent, or too straightforward.\\n\\n\u201cAs to these murders, let us make some investigations of our own before forming an opinion concerning them. The inquiry will amuse us,\u201d [I considered this a strange expression in such a connection, but made no remark] \u201cand, moreover, Le Bon once did me a kindness for which I am not ungrateful. We shall visit the premises and inspect them ourselves. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and shall find no trouble in securing the requisite permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was secured, and we immediately set out for the Rue Morgue. It is one of those wretched streets lying between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We arrived late in the afternoon, this district being far removed from the one in which we lived. We found the house without difficulty; for many people still stood on the other side of the street, staring at its shut shutters with purposeless curiosity. It was a common Parisian dwelling, with a gateway, beside which stood a glass-fronted watch-box, its window fitted with a sliding panel, denoting a _loge de concierge._ Before entering, we went farther along the street, turned into an alley, and then, turning once more, passed behind the house\u2014Dupin meanwhile inspecting the entire vicinity, as well as the building itself, with a minuteness of attention whose possible purpose I could not perceive.\\n\\nHaving retraced our way, we returned to the front of the house, rang, and, after displaying our credentials, were admitted by the agents in charge. We ascended the stairs\u2014to the chamber in which Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been discovered, and where both dead women still remained. The confusion of the room had, as was customary, been left undisturbed. I observed nothing beyond what had appeared in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin examined everything\u2014not even excluding the victims\u2019 bodies. We afterward entered the other rooms and the yard; a _gendarme_ attending us throughout.\"}"
