          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ed851eaf2ab2ab8347a7659\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want to see you securely established, and for that reason it would be best to have as few unsuitable acquaintances as possible. Therefore, I say that if you are still in this part of the country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your friendship with the sisters will not lead you into becoming acquainted with his wife, who will probably be merely some farmer\u2019s daughter, without education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly. Yes. Though I do not think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who had not received some education\u2014and been very well brought up. Still, I do not mean to put my opinion against yours\u2014and I am sure I shall not want to know his wife. I shall always feel great affection for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and should be very sorry to give them up, because they are quite as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then certainly I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her through the changes in this speech and saw no troubling sign of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but she trusted he had no further hold on her, and that there would be no serious obstacle, on Harriet\u2019s part, to any friendly plan of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day as they walked along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking at Emma with great respect, he looked at her companion with the most genuine pleasure. Emma was not sorry to have such a chance to inspect him; walking a few yards ahead while they spoke, her quick eye soon became sufficiently familiar with Mr. Robert Martin. He was very neatly dressed and looked like a sensible young man, but his appearance had no other advantage; and when compared with gentlemen, she thought he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s affections. Harriet was not indifferent to manners; she had herself remarked on her father\u2019s gentleness with both admiration and surprise. Mr. Martin looked as though he did not know what manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and Harriet then ran to her with a smiling face and such excited spirits that Miss Woodhouse hoped to calm them very soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust think of our meeting him!\u2014How very strange! It was entirely by chance, he said, that he had not gone around by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked this road. He thought we walked toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
