          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d49c289df7f1295c8e6fcad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General had to tell the jury that the prisoner before them, though young in age, was well practised in the treasonous acts that demanded the sacrifice of his life. This correspondence with the public enemy was not of today, yesterday, last year, or even the year before. It was certain that, for longer than that, the prisoner had been accustomed to travel back and forth between France and England on secret business for which he could give no honest explanation. If treasonous dealings could prosper\u2014which, happily, they never could\u2014the true wickedness and guilt of his business might have remained undiscovered. Providence, however, had moved a person beyond fear and beyond reproach to uncover the nature of the prisoner\u2019s schemes and, horrified by them, reveal them to His Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. This patriot would be brought before them. His position and conduct were, on the whole, sublime. He had been the prisoner\u2019s friend, but, detecting his infamy at once in an hour both fortunate and terrible, had resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer hold in his heart upon the sacred altar of his country. If Britain awarded statues to public benefactors, as ancient Greece and Rome had done, this brilliant citizen would certainly have received one. Since no such statues were awarded, he probably would not. Virtue, as the poets had observed\u2014in many passages which he knew the jury had, word for word, on the tips of their tongues, at which the jury\u2019s faces showed a guilty awareness that they knew nothing of the passages\u2014was in some measure contagious; especially the radiant virtue called patriotism, or love of country. The noble example of this spotless and unquestionable witness for the Crown, whom it was an honour even to mention, however unworthily, had spread to the prisoner\u2019s serva