{"request_id":"134d1a7ce931916c01c0161e","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when, turning suddenly, I found myself near the lake, beside a cove where a small skiff was tied up. It was not far from our house, and I saw my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I sprang into the boat and, well used to such things, pushed it from the shore and rowed across with all my strength. As I came toward them, dressed in white and covered only by my tartan rachan, my hair streaming over my shoulders, speeding across faster than anyone would have thought I could drive the boat, my father often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. I reached the shore; my father held the boat, I leapt lightly out, and in an instant was in his arms.

Then I began to live. Everything around me changed from dull sameness into the brightest scene of joy and delight. The happiness I found in my father’s company far surpassed my hopeful expectations. We were always together, and our subjects of conversation never ran out. He had spent the sixteen years of his absence among peoples almost unknown to Europe; he had travelled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, and had entered the homes of the natives with a freedom granted to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his anecdotes, and his descriptions of scenery filled delicious hours, when we had tired of speaking about our own plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I listened with delight when he told me what he had felt for me during those long years of seeming forgetfulness. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl. But later, as grief faded and hope returned to me, I could think only of her; and among cities and deserts, her little fairy form, as I imagined it, was always flitting before me. The northern breeze, when it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and gentler because it appeared to carry some of your spirit with it. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island, where we would live in peace forever. As I came back, my fervent hopes were shattered by so many fears; my impatience became unbearably painful. I dared not think that the sun might shine and the moon rise, not upon your living form, but upon your grave. But no—it is not so. I have my Mathilda, my consolation and my hope.”"}

