{"request_id":"a15aac04a9336b252951d233","rewritten_text":"Even now, I had not drained the bitter cup to its last drop. I was not yet convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel, in every heartbeat, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of my race—that I was the LAST MAN.

The day grew overcast, and a fine rain began at sunset. Even the everlasting heavens weep, I thought; is it shameful, then, for mortal man to waste himself in tears? I remembered old tales in which people, through weeping, melt away into fountains that flow forever. Ah, if only it were so; then my fate would in some way resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Grief is strange: it spins a web on which it traces the story of its sorrow from every shape and change around it. It joins itself to all living nature; it feeds on every object. Like light, it fills everything, and, like light, gives all things its own colours.

In my search I had wandered some distance from the place where I had been thrown ashore, and came to one of the watchtowers that stand at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I was glad of shelter, glad to find something made by human hands after looking so long on nature’s bleak emptiness. I went in and climbed the rough, winding stairs to the guard-room. Fate had been kind enough that no painful sign of its former occupants remained. A few planks laid across two iron supports, covered with dried Indian-corn leaves, made a bed for me. An open chest held some partly mouldered biscuits, and stirred an appetite that may have been there before, though I had not noticed it until then. I was also tortured by a fierce, burning thirst, caused by the seawater I had drunk and by my body’s exhaustion. Kind nature had made the satisfying of such needs pleasurable, so that I—even I—felt refreshed and calmed as I ate this poor food and drank a little sour wine from a flask half full in that deserted room. Then I lay down on the bed, good enough for one who had survived a shipwreck. The earthy scent of the dry leaves soothed me after the hateful smell of seaweed. I forgot that I was alone. I looked neither behind nor ahead; my senses sank into rest. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, a shepherd whistling to his dog when he needed help driving the flock into the fold, and the sights and sounds of my mountain childhood, long forgotten."}

