          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"708f252388893196cd2fcca6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His face, deeply browned by the sun, was concealed more than halfway by beard and mustachio. He carried an enormous oak cudgel, though he seemed otherwise without weapons. He made an awkward bow and wished us \u201cgood evening,\u201d with French intonations which, though somewhat Neufchatelish, still plainly betrayed a Parisian birth.\\n\\n\u201cBe seated, my friend,\u201d said Dupin. \u201cI presume you have come concerning the Ourang-Outang. Upon my word, I nearly envy you his ownership; he is an unusually fine animal, and doubtless a very valuable one. What age do you take him to be?\u201d\\n\\nThe sailor inhaled deeply, like a man freed from some unbearable weight, and answered then in a confident voice:\\n\\n\u201cI cannot say\u2014but he cannot be above four or five years old. Have you got him here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no; we have no means of keeping him here. He is in a livery stable close by, in the Rue Dubourg. You may fetch him in the morning. Naturally, you are ready to identify the property?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I am, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall regret giving him up,\u201d said Dupin.\\n\\n\u201cI do not intend that you should take all this trouble for nothing, sir,\u201d said the man. \u201cI could not expect it. I am quite willing to offer a reward for finding the animal\u2014that is, anything reasonable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d returned my friend, \u201cthat is perfectly fair, certainly. Let me consider!\u2014what shall I ask? Ah! I have it. This shall be my reward. You will give me every bit of information you possess concerning the murders in the Rue Morgue.\u201d\\n\\nDupin uttered these final words very softly and with great calm. With equal calm, he went to the door, locked it, and slipped the key into his pocket. Then he took a pistol from his breast and, without the slightest agitation, laid it on the table.\\n\\nThe sailor\u2019s face reddened as though he were fighting for breath. He sprang up and seized his cudgel; but in the next instant sank back into his chair, shaking violently, his face bearing the very look of death. He said nothing. I pitied him with all my heart.\\n\\n\u201cMy friend,\u201d said Dupin kindly, \u201cyou are frightening yourself without cause\u2014you truly are. We intend you no harm at all. I give you the honor of a gentleman, and of a Frenchman, that we mean you no injury. I know perfectly well that you are innocent of the horrors in the Rue Morgue. Yet it will not serve to deny that you are implicated in them to some extent.\"}"
