{"request_id":"5daf400da97d8bbbf47ddd1c","rewritten_text":"Each compares it—not with the voice of a person of any nation whose language he knows—but precisely the reverse. The Frenchman takes it for the voice of a Spaniard, and ‘might have made out certain words _if he had understood Spanish._’ The Dutchman insists that it was a Frenchman’s voice; yet we are told that ‘_this witness, not knowing French, was questioned through an interpreter._’ The Englishman judges it to be German, and ‘_does not understand German._’ The Spaniard ‘is certain’ it was an Englishman’s voice, but decides wholly from the intonation, ‘_having no acquaintance with English._’ The Italian imagines it the voice of a Russian, but ‘_has never spoken with a native of Russia._’ A second Frenchman, besides, disagrees with the first, and confidently declares the voice Italian; yet, _being ignorant of that language_, he too, like the Spaniard, is ‘persuaded by the intonation.’ How remarkably singular, then, must that voice itself have been, concerning which testimony of this kind _could_ be obtained!—a voice in whose very _tones_ the inhabitants of Europe’s five great divisions could detect nothing known! You may suggest that it was the voice of an Asiatic—an African. Asiatics and Africans are not plentiful in Paris; but, without disputing the conclusion, I shall merely direct your notice to three particulars. One witness calls the voice ‘harsh rather than shrill.’ Two others describe it as ‘rapid and _uneven._’ No witness mentioned any words—any sounds like words—as having been distinguishable.

“I cannot tell,” Dupin continued, “what effect I may thus far have produced upon your own mind; but I do not scruple to affirm that sound deductions, even from this part of the evidence—the part concerning the rough and shrill voices—are alone enough to create a suspicion that ought to guide every subsequent step in investigating the mystery. I said ‘sound deductions;’ yet that does not entirely convey my meaning. I meant to suggest that these deductions are the _only_ proper ones, and that the suspicion follows _inevitably_ from them as their sole result. What that suspicion is, however, I shall not reveal just now. I wish only that you remember that, in my own case, it was strong enough to lend a fixed shape—a particular direction—to my inquiries within the chamber.

“Let us now carry ourselves, in imagination, into this chamber. What should we first look for there? The means of escape used by the murderers. It is no exaggeration to say that neither of us believes in supernatural occurrences."}

