 the title of passions because of their seeming force\u2014frequently appeared as limitless generosity. Yet, while he busied himself earnestly with the needs of others, his own wishes were indulged to the utmost. He bestowed his money, but sacrificed none of his desires to his gifts; he gave his time, which he held cheap, and his affections, which he was pleased to have summoned into activity in whatever way.\\n\\nI do not assert that, had his own wishes been set against those of another, he would have shown excessive selfishness; but he was never subjected to such a test. He was reared amid prosperity and surrounded by all its benefits; everyone loved him and desired to please him.\"}"
