          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f926ba23fa1ea32187a3cc71\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You don\u2019t know anything about me unless you have read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer; but that don\u2019t matter. Mr. Mark Twain made that book, and he told the truth, mostly. There were some things he stretched, but mostly he told it true. That ain\u2019t anything. I never saw anybody that didn\u2019t lie sometime or other, except Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, she is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all mentioned in that book, which is chiefly a true book, with a few stretchers, as I said before.\\n\\nNow this is how the book ends: Tom and me found the money the robbers had hid in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars each\u2014all in gold. It was a powerful sight of money when it lay piled up. Well, Judge Thatcher took it and put it out on interest, and it brought us a dollar a day apiece the whole year through\u2014more than anybody could know what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me for her son, and said she would sivilize me; but it was hard living in the house all the time, seeing how mournful regular and decent the widow was in every one of her ways; so when I couldn\u2019t bear it any longer, I lit out. I got back into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead, and was free and contented. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he was going to begin a band of robbers, and I could join if I would return to the widow and be respectable. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me, and called me a poor lost lamb, and called me a heap of other names besides, but she didn\u2019t mean any harm by it. She put me into those new clothes again, and I couldn\u2019t do anything but sweat and sweat, and feel cramped all over. Well, then the old business started again. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to come on time. When you reached the table, you couldn\u2019t begin eating right away, but had to wait for the widow to bow her head and mumble a little over the victuals, though there wasn\u2019t really anything wrong with them\u2014that is, nothing except that everything was cooked separate. In a barrel of odds and ends it is different; the things get mixed together, and the juice sort of trades around, and everything tastes better.\\n\\nAfter supper she brought out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was all in a sweat to learn everything about him; but after a while she let on that Moses had been dead a good long time; so then I didn\u2019t care any more about him, because I don\u2019t take no stock in dead people.\"}"
        }
