l of a life for me. As to their being heirlooms, nothing can be called an heirloom unless it is named as such in a will or other legal paper, and these jewels have been entirely unknown. I assure you that I have no greater right to them than your butler; and when Miss Virginia is grown, I dare say she will be glad to possess pretty things to wear. Besides, Mr. Otis, you forget that you purchased the furniture and the ghost at a valuation, and everything belonging to the ghost immediately became your property, since, whatever liveliness Sir Simon may have displayed in the corridor by night, he was, in the eye of the law, quite dead, and you obtained his possessions by purchase.\u2019\"}"
