          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c432a32e9a0ccedd089298e6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"From the stairs where we had put him aboard, and ever afterward, I had watched anxiously for any sign that we were suspected. I had observed none. Certainly we had not been, and certainly at that moment were not, watched or pursued by any boat. Had any boat been lying in wait for us, I should have put in to shore, and forced her either to pass on or reveal her intention. But we kept our course, with no appearance of interference.\\n\\nHe wore his boat-cloak, and appeared, as I have said, a natural feature of the scene. It was singular (though perhaps the miserable life he had lived explained it) that he was less uneasy than any of us. He was not unconcerned, for he told me he hoped to live to see his gentleman become one of the best of gentlemen in a foreign land; neither was he inclined to be passive or resigned, as I took it; but he had no thought of going halfway to meet danger. When it came to him, he faced it, but until then he gave himself no trouble.\\n\\n\u201cIf you knowed, dear boy,\u201d he said to me, \u201cwhat it is to sit here alongside my dear boy and have my smoke, arter being shut up day after day betwixt four walls, you\u2019d envy me. But you don\u2019t know what it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I know the pleasures of freedom,\u201d I replied.\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d said he, gravely shaking his head. \u201cBut you don\u2019t know it equal to me. You must have been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it equal to me,\u2014but I ain\u2019t a-going to be low.\u201d\\n\\nIt struck me as inconsistent that, for any ruling purpose, he should have risked his freedom, and even his life. Yet I reflected that freedom without danger might be too unlike every habit of his existence to mean to him what it would mean to another man. I was not far mistaken, for after smoking awhile he said:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou see, dear boy, when I was over yonder, t\u2019other side the world, I