          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2938cd2d75c6b9f82552e5a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Furthermore, this is a knot which scarcely any but sailors can make, and it is distinctive of the Maltese. I found the ribbon at the base of the lightning-rod. It could not have been the property of either dead woman. Now, even if I am mistaken in inferring from this ribbon that the Frenchman was a sailor attached to a Maltese vessel, no injury can result from what I stated in the advertisement. If I am wrong, he will simply imagine that some circumstance has deceived me, and will not care to investigate it. But if I am correct, an important advantage is secured. Though aware of the murder and yet innocent, the Frenchman will naturally pause before answering the advertisement\u2014before claiming the Ourang-Outang. He will argue in this fashion:\u2014\u2018I am innocent; I am poor; my Ourang-Outang is highly valuable\u2014indeed, to a man in my position, a fortune in itself\u2014why should I surrender it through vain fears of peril? It is here, almost in my hands. It was discovered in the Bois de Boulogne\u2014far away from the place of that slaughter. How could anyone suspect that a mere beast committed the crime? The police are bewildered\u2014they have obtained not the faintest clew. Even if they should track the animal, they could never prove that I knew of the murder, or charge me with guilt because of such knowledge. Above all, _I am known._ The advertiser identifies me as the owner of the beast. I cannot tell how far his knowledge reaches. If I refrain from claiming property so valuable, property which is known to be mine, I shall at least make the animal an object of suspicion. It is not wise for me to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I will reply to the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it concealed until this affair has passed away.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that instant, we heard footsteps on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe prepared,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols; but do not employ or display them until I give the signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe outer door had been left unfastened, and the visitor had come in without ringing, proceeding several steps up the staircase. He now appeared to pause. Soon we heard him going down again. Dupin was hastening toward the door when we heard him once more ascending. This time he did not retreat, but mounted resolutely and knocked upon the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, with a cordial, cheerful voice.\\n\\nA man came in. He was plainly a sailor,\u2014a tall, powerfully built, muscular fellow, whose face bore a somewhat reckless expression, though not an altogether disagreeable one.\"}"
