          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"07ceadd25784746264ea0d65\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr Elliot looking up with such respect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuite charming!\u201d cried Mrs Clay, though she did not dare turn her eyes toward Anne. \u201cExactly like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not say father and son?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I place no restriction on anyone\u2019s words. If you choose to have such ideas! But honestly, I am hardly aware that his attentions exceed those of other men.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d exclaimed Mrs Clay, raising her hands and eyes, and concealing the rest of her astonishment in a convenient silence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear Penelope, you need not be so alarmed about him. I did invite him, you know. I sent him away smiling. When I found that he truly meant to spend the whole of tomorrow with his friends at Thornberry Park, I took pity on him.\u201d\\n\\nAnne admired her friend\u2019s fine performance, in being able to show as much pleasure as she did, both at the prospect and at the actual arrival of the very person whose presence must truly interfere with her chief aim. Mrs Clay could not possibly help hating the sight of Mr Elliot; yet she could wear a most obliging, calm expression, and seem perfectly content with having only half as much freedom to devote herself to Sir Walter as she otherwise would have had.\\n\\nFor Anne herself, seeing Mr Elliot enter the room was deeply distressing, and having him come near and speak to her was positively painful. She had previously been accustomed to feel that he could not always be entirely sincere, but now she saw insincerity in everything. His attentive respect toward her father, compared with his former language, was hateful; and when she thought of his cruel behavior toward Mrs Smith, she could scarcely endure the sight of his present smiles and gentleness, or the sound of his manufactured good principles.\\n\\nShe intended to avoid any change in her manner that might invite a protest from him. It was very important to her to avoid all inquiry or public notice; but she meant to be as distinctly cool toward him as their relationship allowed, and quietly retrace the few steps of needless intimacy into which she had gradually been drawn. Accordingly, she was more reserved and cooler than she had been the previous evening.\\n\\nHe wished to revive her curiosity about how and where he might formerly have heard her praised; he very much wanted the satisfaction of being urged further. But the spell was broken: he discovered that the warmth and excitement of a public room were needed to stir his modest cousin\u2019s vanity. At least, he discovered that it could not be done now by any of the attempts he could risk amid the overly commanding claims of the others.\"}"
