          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"edf7090f0d798c26fe5188a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She felt a strong urge to go to the outer door; she wanted to see whether it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of another reason? Captain Wentworth had to be out of sight. She rose from her chair; she would go. One half of her ought not always to be so much wiser than the other, nor always accuse the other of worse motives than it had. She would see whether it rained. But she was sent back at once by Captain Wentworth\u2019s entrance, with a group of gentlemen and ladies who were clearly his acquaintances, and whom he must have joined a little way below Milsom Street. He was more visibly startled and confused by seeing her than she had ever known him to be; he turned quite red. For the first time since they had met again, she felt that she was showing less feeling than he was. She had the advantage of the last few minutes\u2019 preparation. The overwhelming, blinding, bewildering first effects of a great surprise had passed for her. Yet she still had enough to feel: agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014something between happiness and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, then turned away. His manner was embarrassed. She could not have described it as cold or friendly, or anything so certainly as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a brief interval, however, he came toward her and spoke again. They exchanged enquiries about ordinary matters, neither of them, probably, much wiser for what was said; and Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than before. By being so often together, they had learned to speak with a considerable appearance of indifference and calm; but he could not manage it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. There was some kind of awareness between them. He looked very well, not like a man whose health or spirits had suffered, and he spoke of Uppercross, the Musgroves\u2014indeed, even of Louisa. As he named her, there was for a moment a look of his old teasing significance; yet this was Captain Wentworth uncomfortable, uneasy, unable to pretend otherwise.\\n\\nIt did not surprise Anne, but it hurt her, to see that Elizabeth would not acknowledge him. She saw that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, and that each recognized the other inwardly. She was convinced that he was prepared to be acknowledged as an acquaintance, and expected it; and it pained her to see her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot had been growing very impatient, now drew up, and the servant came in to announce it.\"}"
