{"request_id":"de0dbf067566cd16b4e9f60d","rewritten_text":"Among the public buildings in a certain town—which, for many reasons, it would be wise not to name, and to which I shall give no invented name—there stood one institution once common to towns both large and small: namely, a workhouse. In that workhouse, on a day and date I need not repeat, since at this point it can be of no possible importance to the reader, was born the mortal whose name appears at the head of this chapter.

For some time after the parish surgeon brought him into this world of grief and hardship, it remained very doubtful whether the child would live long enough to receive any name at all. Had he not, these memoirs would most likely never have been written; or, if they had been, they would have filled only a page or two, and possessed the priceless distinction of being the briefest and most accurate example of biography in the literature of any age or nation.

Though I do not claim that being born in a workhouse is, in itself, the happiest or most desirable event that can happen to anyone, I do say that in this particular case it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth was that there was considerable difficulty in persuading Oliver to undertake the business of breathing—a troublesome habit, though custom has made it necessary to our comfortable existence. For some time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, unevenly balanced between this world and the next, with the advantage clearly on the side of the latter. Had Oliver, during that short interval, been surrounded by attentive grandmothers, worried aunts, skilled nurses, and doctors of great wisdom, he would certainly and without question have been killed at once. But there was no one present except an old pauper woman, made somewhat hazy by an unusual allowance of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such matters by contract. Oliver and Nature therefore settled the question between them. After a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been placed upon the parish, by raising as loud a cry as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who had possessed that useful appendage, a voice, for no longer than three minutes and a quarter."}

