          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0cd62aef1a2a969158e94af4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Lacking ordinary means, the analyst enters into the spirit of his opponent, identifies himself with him, and often sees at once the only methods\u2014sometimes absurdly simple ones\u2014by which he can mislead him or drive him into a mistake.\\n\\nWhist has long been known for strengthening what is called the power of calculation; and men of the highest intellect have been known to take an apparently inexplicable pleasure in it while dismissing chess as frivolous. Without doubt, no similar game places such demands on the faculty of analysis. The best chess player in Christendom may be little more than the best player of chess; but skill at whist suggests the ability to succeed in all the more important contests where one mind struggles against another. By skill, I mean perfection in the game, including an understanding of every source from which a fair advantage may be gained. These sources are not merely numerous but varied, and often lie in corners of thought entirely beyond the reach of ordinary understanding. To observe closely is to remember clearly; and in this respect the focused chess player will do well enough at whist, while Hoyle's rules, based only on the mechanics of the game, are generally easy to understand. Thus, a strong memory and playing by \u201cthe book\u201d are commonly thought to make up the whole of good play. But the analyst shows his skill in matters beyond mere rules. Silently, he makes countless observations and deductions. His companions may do the same; yet the difference in the amount of information gained lies less in whether their deductions are sound than in the quality of what they observe. The essential knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player limits himself in no way; nor, simply because the game is his object, does he reject conclusions drawn from things outside the game. He studies his partner's face, carefully comparing it with those 