          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42469c55511daf2c8da154b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo raising him by hand, then. Not at all!\u201d\\n\\nJoe offered me more gravy, but I was too frightened to take it.\\n\\n\u201cHe was a great deal of trouble to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble, sympathizing with my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d repeated my sister. \u201cTrouble?\u201d Then she began a dreadful list of every illness I had ever caused, every night I had kept people awake, every high place I had fallen from, every low place I had fallen into, every injury I had done myself, and every time she had wished me in my grave and I had stubbornly refused to go there.\\n\\nI think the Romans must have annoyed one another terribly with their noses. Perhaps that was why they became such restless people. At any rate, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose irritated me so much during this account of my offences that I wanted to pull it until he howled. But everything I had suffered so far was nothing beside the dreadful feelings that came over me when there was a pause after my sister had finished, and everyone looked at me\u2014as I painfully knew\u2014with anger and disgust.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently bringing the company back to the subject they had wandered from, \u201cpork, when it is boiled, is rich too, isn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave a little brandy, uncle,\u201d said my sister.\\n\\nOh, Heaven, it had come at last! He would discover it was weak, he would say it was weak, and I was ruined. I gripped the table leg beneath the cloth with both hands and waited for my fate.\\n\\nMy sister fetched the stone bottle, returned with it, and poured out his brandy; nobody else had any. The miserable man played with his glass\u2014lifting it, holding it to the light, and setting it down again\u2014stretching out my misery. Meanwhile, Mrs. Joe and Joe were busily clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI could not take my eyes from him. Still gripping the table leg with my hands and feet, I watched the wretched creature toy with his glass, lift it, smile, throw back his head, and swallow the brandy. The next instant, everyone was struck with unspeakable alarm. He sprang to his feet, spun around several times in a terrible, spasmodic, whooping-cough dance, and rushed out through the door. Then we saw him through the window, plunging about and spitting violently, making horrible faces, and seeming completely out of his mind.\\n\\nI held on tightly while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him.\"}"
