          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d778edcf98f7f38cec2f9931\",\"rewritten_text\":\"About ten minutes later, the bell rang for tea. When Virginia did not come downstairs, Mrs. Otis sent one of the footmen up to tell her. After a while he returned, saying he could not find Miss Virginia anywhere. Since she often went into the garden each evening to pick flowers for the dinner table, Mrs. Otis was not worried at first. But when six o\u2019clock came and Virginia still had not appeared, she became truly anxious. She sent the boys out to search for her, while she and Mr. Otis looked through every room in the house. At half-past six, the boys returned and said they could find no sign of their sister. Everyone was now terribly excited and did not know what to do, when Mr. Otis suddenly remembered that a few days earlier he had allowed a group of gypsies to camp in the park. He immediately set out for Blackfell Hollow, where he knew they were staying, taking his eldest son and two farm servants with him. The little Duke of Cheshire, wild with worry, begged to go too, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing there might be a fight. When they reached the place, however, they found that the gypsies had left. Their departure had clearly been hurried, for the fire was still burning and several plates lay on the grass. After sending Washington and the two men to search the surrounding area, he hurried home and sent telegrams to every police inspector in the county, asking them to watch for a little girl kidnapped by tramps or gypsies. Then he ordered his horse brought round and, after making his wife and three boys sit down to dinner, rode off along the Ascot Road with a groom. He had gone barely two miles when he heard someone galloping behind him. Looking back, he saw the little Duke riding up on his pony, his face flushed and his head bare. \u2018I\u2019m terribly sorry, Mr. Otis,\u2019 the boy gasped, \u2018but I can\u2019t eat dinner while Virginia is missing. Please don\u2019t be angry with me. If you had let us become engaged last year, none of this trouble would have happened. You won\u2019t send me back, will you? I can\u2019t go! I won\u2019t go!\u2019\\n\\nThe Minister could not help smiling at the handsome young rascal, and he was deeply moved by his devotion to Virginia. Leaning down from his horse, he patted the boy kindly on the shoulder and said, \u2018Well, Cecil, if you refuse to go back, I suppose you must come with me. But I must get you a hat at Ascot.\u2019\"}"
