          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f4c47c8687043f17fa34eb4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fairness to the young lady, it must be said that she did not flatly declare she would not go; she merely expressed an emphatic and heartfelt wish to be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did. This was a polite and delicate way of evading the request, showing that she possessed the natural good manners which cannot bear to cause another person the pain of a direct and definite refusal.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s face fell. He turned from this young lady, who was cheerfully\u2014indeed, almost splendidly\u2014dressed in a red gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other woman.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d said the Jew soothingly, \u201cwhat do you say?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it won\u2019t do, so there\u2019s no use trying it on, Fagin,\u201d Nancy replied.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by that?\u201d said Mr. Sikes, looking up sourly.\\n\\n\u201cExactly what I said, Bill,\u201d the young woman answered calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re the very person for it,\u201d Sikes argued. \u201cNobody around here knows anything about you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd since I don\u2019t want them to,\u201d Nancy replied with the same composure, \u201cmy answer is much more no than yes, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes was right. Through alternating threats, promises, and bribes, the lady in question was eventually persuaded to undertake the errand. She was not restrained by the same concerns as her agreeable friend; having lately moved into the Field Lane neighborhood from the distant but respectable suburb of Ratcliffe, she had less reason to fear being recognized by any of her many acquaintances.\\n\\nSo, with a clean white apron tied over her gown and her curl-papers tucked beneath a straw bonnet\u2014both items supplied from the Jew\u2019s inexhaustible store\u2014Miss Nancy prepared to set out on her errand.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment, my dear,\u201d said the Jew, producing a small covered basket. \u201cCarry that in one hand. It makes you look more respectable, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door-key for the other hand, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes. \u201cIt looks real and genuine-like.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, my dear, it does,\u201d said the Jew, hanging a large front-door key over the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere; very good! Very good indeed, my dear!\u201d he said, rubbing his hands.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d cried Nancy, bursting into tears and clutching the little basket and street-door key in an agony of distress. \u201cWhat has happened to him? Where have they taken him? Oh, have pity and tell me what has been done with the dear boy, gentlemen; do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
