          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"49ca4a8bb7deffd9619abcf7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings had overcome her self-interest, and for the young man\u2019s sake she had given up the chance of continuing her schemes for Sir Walter. Yet she had talents as well as feelings; and it remained uncertain whether his cleverness or hers would ultimately prevail\u2014whether, having stopped her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he might not eventually be coaxed and charmed into making her Sir William\u2019s wife.\\n\\nThere could be no doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were shocked and humiliated by losing their companion and discovering how she had deceived them. Certainly, they had their distinguished cousins to turn to for consolation; but they must long continue to feel that flattering and following others, without being flattered and followed in return, was only half a pleasure.\\n\\nAnne, having been satisfied very early that Lady Russell meant to love Captain Wentworth as she should, had no shadow over the happiness before her except the awareness that she had no relations to offer him whom a sensible man could value. In this, she felt her own inferiority sharply. The difference in their fortunes was nothing and gave her not a moment\u2019s regret; but having no family to welcome and appreciate him properly, no respectability, harmony, or goodwill to offer in return for all the merit and ready affection she found among his brothers and sisters, caused her as much pain as her mind could feel amid such otherwise deep happiness. She had only two friends in the world to add to his list: Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. To both, however, he was very willing to become attached. Despite all Lady Russell\u2019s former errors, he could now value her sincerely. Though he was not required to say that he believed she had been right to separate them in the beginning, he was prepared to say almost everything else in her favour; and Mrs Smith had claims of several kinds that quickly and permanently recommended her to him.\\n\\nHer recent kindness to Anne would have been enough by itself, and their marriage, rather than taking one friend from her, gave her two. She was their first visitor after they were settled; and Captain Wentworth, by helping her recover her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014writing for her, acting for her, and guiding her through every small difficulty in the matter with the energy of a fearless man and steadfast friend\u2014fully repaid the services she had given, or had ever intended to give, his wife.\"}"
