          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c3a90bd483a4bf31a041816f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped quietly out of the wall panelling, an evil smile on his cruel, wrinkled mouth. The moon hid behind a cloud as he passed the great bay window, where his own coat of arms and that of his murdered wife shone in blue and gold. He moved on like a wicked shadow, and even the darkness seemed to hate him as he went by. Once he thought he heard someone call and stopped, but it was only a dog barking at the Red Farm. He continued, muttering strange curses from the sixteenth century and now and then waving his rusty dagger through the midnight air. At last he reached the corner of the passage leading to unfortunate Washington\u2019s room. He paused for a moment, while the wind blew his long grey hair around his head and twisted the dead man\u2019s shroud into grotesque, fantastic shapes. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he knew the moment had come. He chuckled and turned the corner. But the instant he did, he gave a pitiful cry of terror, stumbled backward, and covered his pale face with his long, bony hands. Directly before him stood a dreadful ghost, still as a carved statue and monstrous as something from a madman\u2019s dream. Its head was bald and shining; its face round, fat, and white; and horrible laughter seemed to have twisted its features into a permanent grin. Scarlet beams streamed from its eyes, its mouth was a wide pit of fire, and a terrible garment, like his own, wrapped the giant form in silent white folds. On its chest was a sign bearing strange old writing. It seemed to be some record of shame, wild sins, or terrible crimes. In its right hand, it held high a gleaming steel sword.\\n\\nAs he had never seen a ghost before, he was naturally terrified. After one more quick look at the awful figure, he fled back to his room, tripping over his long winding-sheet as he hurried down the corridor. At last he dropped the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jack-boots, where the butler found it the next morning. Safe in his own room, he threw himself onto a small bed and hid his face beneath the covers. After a while, however, the brave old Canterville spirit returned, and he decided that as soon as daylight came, he would go and speak to the other ghost. So, just as dawn touched the hills with silver, he went back toward the place where he had first seen the grim phantom. After all, he thought, two ghosts were better than one, and with this new friend\u2019s help, he might safely take on the twins.\"}"
