          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5f762726309d7dab1e7787e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February, and Anne, after a month in Bath, was becoming very anxious for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted to know far more than Mary had told her. Three weeks had passed since she had heard anything. All she knew was that Henrietta was home again, and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still at Lyme. One evening, while she was thinking deeply about them all, a letter from Mary arrived, thicker than usual; and, adding to her pleasure and surprise, it came with Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was enough to interest her. They were people toward whom her feelings naturally turned.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have come to Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Such letters are useful passports. They provide an introduction. I would have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could not listen any longer; she could not even have said whether the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion had escaped notice. Her letter absorbed her. It had been started several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI make no excuse for not writing, because I know how little anyone thinks about letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to care about Uppercross, which, as you know well, gives one very little to write about. We have had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not given a single dinner party during the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. At last the holidays are over: I do not believe children ever had such long ones. I am sure I never did. The house was emptied yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear that they have still not gone home. Mrs Harville must be a strange mother to let them stay away so long. I cannot understand it. They are not at all pleasant children, in my opinion; but Mrs Musgrove seems to like them as much as, if not more than, her own grandchildren. What terrible weather we have had! Perhaps it is not noticed in Bath, with your fine pavements; but in the country it matters a great deal. No one has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who has been calling much more often than I wished. Between ourselves, I think it a great pity Henrietta did not stay at Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little out of his way.\"}"
