          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1feaf04b82b538e0016b0eac\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their coming had kindled a sort of fire in the breast of Saint Antoine, spreading swiftly as they passed, and stirring into flickering flame the faces at nearly every door and window. Still, nobody followed them, and not a man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, although every eye within was fixed upon them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nPerhaps this was the sign that released the general tongue. A chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d replied.\\n\\n\u201cIt is foul weather, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, with a shake of his head.\\n\\nAt that, each man glanced at his neighbour; then all lowered their eyes and sat in silence. Save for one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d Defarge said aloud, speaking to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have travelled some leagues with this worthy mender of roads, named Jacques. I met him\u2014by chance\u2014a day and a half\u2019s journey from Paris. He is a good fellow, this mender of roads, named Jacques. Give him drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender called Jacques, who removed his blue cap to the company and drank. He had some rough dark bread in the breast of his blouse; from time to time he ate it, sitting and chewing and drinking beside Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge revived himself with a draught of wine\u2014but drank less than the stranger had been given, wine being no uncommon thing to him\u2014and waited until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He looked at no one there, and no one looked at him now; not even Madame Defarge, who had resumed her knitting and was working at it.\\n\\n\u201cHave you done with your meal, friend?\u201d he asked, at the proper time.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome, then! You shall look at the room I told you that you might have. It will suit you wonderfully.\u201d\\n\\nFrom the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep stairway, from the stairway into a garret\u2014once the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, bent forward and busily making shoes.\\n\\nThe white-haired man was no longer there; but the three men who had left the wine-shop one by one were there. And between them and that white-haired man far away lay the single small connection: they had once peered in at him through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge carefully shut the door and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness met by arrangement, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
