          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9bf5082b7071cf33fd76e51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr Elliot looking up with such profound respect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMost charming!\u201d cried Mrs Clay, though she did not venture to direct her eyes towards Anne. \u201cJust like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not call them father and son?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I place no restraint on anybody\u2019s expressions. If you choose to entertain such notions! But, upon my word, I am hardly aware that his attentions exceed those of other gentlemen.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d exclaimed Mrs Clay, raising her hands and eyes, and consigning all the remainder of her surprise to a discreet silence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear Penelope, there is no need for you to be so frightened of him. I did ask him, you know. I dismissed him with smiles. When I discovered that he was truly to spend the whole of to-morrow with his friends at Thornberry Park, I took pity on him.\u201d\\n\\nAnne admired her friend\u2019s fine performance, in being able to display so much pleasure, both in anticipating and in actually receiving the very man whose presence must in truth obstruct her chief purpose. Mrs Clay could not but detest the sight of Mr Elliot; yet she could wear a most civil, serene countenance, and seem entirely content with having her liberty to devote herself to Sir Walter reduced by one half.\\n\\nFor Anne herself, Mr Elliot\u2019s entrance into the room was exceedingly distressing; and it was positively painful when he came near and addressed her. She had formerly been accustomed to think him not always perfectly sincere, but she now perceived insincerity in all he did. His respectful attention to her father, in contrast with his earlier language, was hateful; and, remembering his cruel behaviour to Mrs Smith, she could scarcely endure the sight of his current smiles and gentleness, or hear his manufactured sentiments of goodness.\\n\\nShe resolved to avoid any change in her manner that might invite remonstrance from him. To escape all inquiry or notice was of great importance to her; but she intended to be as unmistakably cool towards him as their connection allowed, and quietly to retrace the few steps of needless intimacy into which she had been gradually drawn. Accordingly, she was more reserved and colder than she had been the preceding evening.\\n\\nHe wished to revive her curiosity about how and where he might formerly have heard her commended; he greatly wished to be rewarded with further entreaty; but the spell was gone. He discovered that the warmth and liveliness of a public room were needed to awaken his modest cousin\u2019s vanity; he discovered, at least, that it could not now be accomplished by any of those efforts he could venture upon amid the overly imperious demands of the others.\"}"
