          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0bbb272a710e01d16e93abfa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the force of gravity would steadily lessen in proportion to the squares of the distances, and so, with a velocity increasing enormously, I would eventually reach those remote regions where the earth\u2019s attraction would be replaced by that of the moon. Guided by these ideas, I did not consider it worthwhile to burden myself with provisions sufficient for more than forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was, however, another difficulty that caused me some concern. It has been observed that in balloon ascents to any considerable height, apart from the pain involved in breathing, one experiences great discomfort in the head and body, often accompanied by nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms, which grow increasingly troublesome as the altitude rises. This thought was somewhat unsettling. Was it not likely that such symptoms would increase without limit, or at least until death itself ended them? In the end, I decided they would not. Their cause was to be found in the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure from the body\u2019s surface, and the resulting swelling of the superficial blood vessels\u2014not in any actual disorder of the animal system, as occurs in difficulty of breathing, when the atmosphere is chemically too thin to properly renew the blood in a chamber of the heart. Except through failure of this renewal, I could see no reason why life could not be maintained even in a vacuum; for the expansion and contraction of the chest, commonly called breathing, is a purely muscular action, and the cause rather than the effect of respiration. In short, I believed that as the body grew accustomed to the absence of atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would gradually fade\u2014and while they lasted, I relied confidently on the iron toughness of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, if it please Your Excellencies, I have described some, though by no means all, of the considerations that led me to conceive the plan of a voyage to the moon. I shall now proceed to present the result of an attempt so apparently daring in conception and, in any case, so completely without precedent in the history of mankind.\\n\\n\u201cHaving reached the altitude already mentioned\u2014that is, three and three-quarter miles\u2014I threw a quantity of feathers from the car and found that I was still rising rapidly enough; there was therefore no need to release any ballast. I was pleased by this, since I wished to keep with me as much weight as I could carry, for reasons that will be explained later.\u201d\"}"
