          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"01aa46f8b3f62f66bba0d62c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man\u2014a very pleasant, kindly-looking old man when I roused him. I told him my brother and I had been over on Spanish Island hunting yesterday afternoon, and had camped on a bit of raft we found; and about midnight he must have kicked his gun in his sleep, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted him to come over and mend it, and not say anything about it or let anybody know, because we meant to get home this evening and surprise the folks.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he says.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. Then, after a minute, he says:\\n\\n\u201cHow did you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe dreamed,\u201d I says, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAn uncommon dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nThen he lit his lantern, took up his saddle-bags, and we set out. But when he saw the canoe he didn\u2019t fancy the looks of her\u2014said she was large enough for one, but not very safe-looking for two. I says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you needn\u2019t be scared, sir; she carried all three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns; that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nBut he put his foot on the gunnel and rocked her, shook his head, and said he reckoned he would look about for a larger one. But every one was locked and chained; so he took my canoe, and told me to wait until he came back, or hunt around some more, or perhaps I had better go down home and get things ready for the surprise, if I wanted. But I said I didn\u2019t; so I told him exactly how to find the raft, and off he went.\\n\\nPretty soon an idea came to me. I says to myself, s\u2019pose he can\u2019t mend that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, as the saying goes? S\u2019pose it takes three or four days? What are we going to do\u2014hang around there until he lets the cat out of the bag? No, sir; I know what I\u2019ll do. I\u2019ll wait, and when he comes back, if he says he has to go again, I\u2019ll get down there too, if I have to swim; and we\u2019ll tie him up, keep him, and push off down the river. And when Tom is through with him, we\u2019ll give him what it\u2019s worth, or everything we have, and then let him go ashore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a lumber-pile to sleep; and the next time I woke, the sun was high over my head! I jumped out and hurried to the doctor\u2019s house, but they said he had gone away sometime in the night, and hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
