          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4e0534657ac6881f2302def8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse\u2014beautiful, intelligent, and wealthy, with a comfortable home and a cheerful nature\u2014seemed to possess many of life\u2019s greatest advantages. She had lived almost twenty-one years with very little to trouble or annoy her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters of a deeply affectionate, indulgent father, and, after her sister married, had become the mistress of his household at a very early age. Her mother had died too long before for Emma to remember more than vaguely her affection; her place had been filled by an excellent governess, whose love had come very close to that of a mother.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had been with Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family for sixteen years, more as a friend than a governess. She was very fond of both daughters, but especially of Emma. Their relationship was more like that of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor gave up the formal position of governess, her gentle nature had scarcely permitted her to exercise restraint; and now that even the appearance of authority had long disappeared, they lived together as devoted friends. Emma did exactly as she pleased, valuing Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment greatly, but guided chiefly by her own.\\n\\nThe true drawbacks of Emma\u2019s position were that she had rather too much her own way and was inclined to think a little too highly of herself. These were the faults that threatened to spoil her many pleasures. Yet the danger was so unnoticed at present that she did not consider them misfortunes at all.\\n\\nSorrow came\u2014a mild sorrow, but not in the form of any unpleasant self-awareness. Miss Taylor married. It was the loss of Miss Taylor that first brought Emma grief. On the wedding day of this beloved friend, Emma first sat in lasting sadness. Once the wedding was over and the bridal party had gone, she and her father were left to dine alone, with no third person to brighten the long evening ahead. As usual, her father settled himself to sleep after dinner, and she had nothing to do but sit and think about what she had lost.\\n\\nThe marriage promised every happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of flawless character, comfortable means, suitable age, and agreeable manners. Emma found some comfort in remembering the selfless, generous friendship with which she had always wished for and encouraged the match; but for her it was a dark day\u2019s work. She would feel Miss Taylor\u2019s absence every hour of every day. She remembered all her past kindness\u2014sixteen years of kindness and affection\u2014how Miss Taylor had taught her and played with her since she was five years old, devoted all her abilities to winning and entertaining her when she was well, and nursed her through the many illnesses of childhood.\"}"
