          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1ea9757cbe38f01c2ee72d1e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, it is\u2014and I could\u2019ve had him if I\u2019d been big enough; I saw him first. Who caught him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt was an old man\u2014a stranger\u2014and he sold his claim on him for forty dollars, because he has to go upriver and can\u2019t wait. Just think of that! I\u2019d wait, even if it took seven years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s how I am, every time,\u201d I said. \u201cBut maybe his claim isn\u2019t worth any more than that, if he\u2019ll sell it so cheap. Maybe something\u2019s not right about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it is, though\u2014straight as a string. I saw the handbill myself. It tells all about him exactly\u2014describes him like a picture, and names the plantation he came from, below New Orleans. No sir, there\u2019s no trouble with that deal, you can bet on it. Say, give me a chew of tobacco, will you?\u201d\\n\\nI didn\u2019t have any, so he left. I went to the raft and sat down in the wigwam to think. But I couldn\u2019t come up with anything. I thought until my head ached, but I couldn\u2019t see any way out of the trouble. After that long journey, and after all we\u2019d done for those scoundrels, it had all come to nothing\u2014everything smashed up and ruined\u2014because they could bring themselves to play such a trick on Jim, making him a slave again for the rest of his life, among strangers too, for forty filthy dollars.\\n\\nAt one point I told myself it would be a thousand times better for Jim to be a slave at home, where his family was, if he had to be a slave at all; so I ought to write Tom Sawyer and tell him to tell Miss Watson where Jim was. But I soon gave up that idea for two reasons: she\u2019d be angry and disgusted at his wickedness and ingratitude in running away from her, and she\u2019d sell him straight down the river again; and if she didn\u2019t, everybody naturally despises an ungrateful Black man, and they\u2019d make Jim feel it all the time, so he\u2019d feel miserable and disgraced. And then there was me! Word would get around that Huck Finn had helped a Black man get his freedom; and if I ever saw anybody from that town again, I\u2019d be ready to kneel down and lick his boots from shame. That\u2019s how it is: a person does something low-down, then doesn\u2019t want to face the consequences. He thinks that as long as he can hide it, it isn\u2019t disgraceful. That was exactly my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience ground away at me, and the more wicked, low-down, and miserable I felt.\"}"
