          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9767e48a4506a9666550b7b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now turn to my own history. Of the first years of my life there is little to tell, and I shall be short; yet I must be permitted to linger somewhat over my childhood, that it may be seen how, when one hope was lost, all life became a void; and how, when the sole affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my being perished with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt differed greatly from my father. I think that, without the least stain of an evil heart, she possessed the coldest heart that ever inhabited a human breast: it was wholly incapable of affection. She received me into her care because she deemed it her duty; but she had lived too long alone, untroubled by the clamour and chatter of children, to permit me to interrupt her repose. She had never married; and during the preceding five years had lived entirely alone upon an estate which had come to her through her mother, on the banks of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters my father had wished that she would live with me at his family seat, situated in a lovely district near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused this proposal; but, as soon as she had settled the matters which her brother\u2019s departure had left in her charge, she left England and carried me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe charge of me in infancy, and afterwards until I had completed my eighth year, fell upon one of my mother\u2019s servants, who had accompanied us into retirement for that end. I was lodged in a distant part of the house, and saw my aunt only at appointed times. These came twice each day: about noon she visited my nursery, and after dinner I was brought to her. She never fondled me, and throughout my stay in the room appeared afraid that some childish prank might vex her. My kind nurse always instructed me most carefully before she dared enter the parlour\u2014and the dread inspired by my aunt\u2019s chilly looks and few forced words was so strong that I rarely dishonoured her teaching, or departed from the model quietness I had been taught to maintain during these brief calls.\\n\\nBeneath my good nurse\u2019s care I roamed freely through our park and the adjoining fields. The child of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the greatest tenderness of feeling. I cannot describe the ardour with which I loved everything, even the lifeless things around me.\"}"
