          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"716e1a14e49be9b3de989d8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMark my words; I have mastered the language of despair: I know it all by heart, for I am Despair; and a strange creature am I, glad, victorious Despair. Yet those words are untrue, for though the wave may be dark, it is not bitter. We lie down and shut our eyes with a quiet good night, and when we awaken, we are free. Come then, delay no longer, thou lingering one! See the welcome draught! Behold, I am a spirit of good, not a human maiden who invites thee, and in persuasive tones, (oh, that they might persuade thee!) cries, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nWhile I spoke, I kept my gaze upon his face; and his perfect beauty, the divine compassion shining from his eyes, his mild but earnest expression of remonstrance and astonishment, even before he answered, changed my overwrought feelings, stripping away all the harshness of despair and leaving me only the gentlest sorrow. I saw that his eyes too were moist as he took both my hands in his; then, seating himself beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a mournful act to which you would draw me, dearest friend, and your misery must truly be profound to have filled you with such unhappy thoughts. You desire death, yet fear it, and would have me share it with you. But I possess less courage than you, and even with such companionship I do not dare to die. Hear me, and then consider whether you should seek to win me to your design, even if, by the commanding eloquence of despair, you could render black death so alluring that bright heaven itself would seem dark. Listen, I beseech you, to one who has himself cherished desperate thoughts and yearned impatiently for death, but who at last has trodden the phantom beneath his feet and broken its sting. Come; as you have acted Despair toward me, I will perform Una\u2019s part toward you, and lead you unharmed from his gloomy cave. Listen to me, and let yourself be moved by words in which no selfish passion remains.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know the meaning of this vast world, with its strange mingling of good and evil. Yet we have been set here and commanded to live and hope. I do not know for what we are to hope; but some good lies beyond us which we must pursue, and that is our task on earth. If misfortune assails us, we must struggle against her; we must thrust her away, and still proceed to discover that which our nature bids us desire. Whether this promise of future good prepares us for another life, I cannot tell; or whether it is only that we, as labourers in God\u2019s vineyard, must help to make the path smoother for those who come after us.\"}"
