          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a6c7f3404f483fd48c21a95\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the force of gravity would steadily weaken according to the square of the distance. My speed would therefore increase enormously, until at last I reached those far-off regions where the earth\u2019s attraction would give way to that of the moon. With this in mind, I saw no reason to burden myself with provisions for more than forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was, however, another difficulty that caused me some concern. It has been observed that when balloons rise to great heights, people suffer not only pain in breathing, but severe discomfort in the head and body, often with nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms. These grow worse as the altitude increases. This thought was somewhat frightening. Was it not likely that such symptoms would continue to increase, perhaps ending only in death? In the end, I decided they would not. They arose from the gradual loss of the usual atmospheric pressure on the body\u2019s surface, which caused the small blood vessels to swell\u2014not from any actual breakdown of the body, as happens when breathing becomes difficult because the air is too thin to properly renew the blood in a chamber of the heart. Except for the lack of this renewal, I could see no reason why life could not continue even in a vacuum. The expansion and contraction of the chest, commonly called breathing, is purely a muscular action; it is the cause, not the effect, of respiration. In short, I believed that as the body grew accustomed to the absence of atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would gradually lessen. While they lasted, I trusted confidently in the iron strength of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, if it please your Excellencies, I have described some, though far from all, of the thoughts that led me to plan a voyage to the moon. I shall now lay before you the result of an attempt so bold in conception and, in any case, so completely without precedent i