          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"01d65a712f33670a26c6b086\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I shall be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thank you for giving me the information I wanted. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I have invited myself and Mr. Gray. He is her newest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to trouble me any more with her appeals for charity. I am tired of them. The good woman seems to think I have nothing to do except write cheques for her foolish causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, though it will do no good. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity. That is what sets them apart.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman grunted in approval and rang for his servant. Lord Henry went through the low arcade into Burlington Street and headed toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parents. Though it had been told to him bluntly, it stirred him with the idea of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a reckless passion. A few wild weeks of happiness ended by a horrible, treacherous crime. Months of silent suffering, and then a child born in pain. The mother taken by death, the boy left alone under the rule of an old man without love. Yes, it was an interesting history. It gave the lad a pose, made him seem more perfect. Behind every beautiful thing, there was something tragic. Worlds had to suffer in labour before even the smallest flower could bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the night before, sitting opposite him at the club with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened delight, while the red candle shades deepened the awakening wonder in his face to a richer rose. Talking with him was like playing a fine violin. He responded to every touch and movement of the bow.... There was something deeply captivating in the use of influence. No other activity was like it. To send one\u2019s soul into some graceful form and let it remain there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own ideas returned with the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another person as if it were a subtle liquid or strange perfume: there was real pleasure in that\u2014perhaps the greatest pleasure left to us in an age as narrow and vulgar as ours, an age crude in its pleasures and crude in its ambitions.... This lad was a marvellous type, too, whom he had met by such a curious chance in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or who could at least be shaped into one.\"}"
