          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"126ab252b921608576e40e7d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the sight of it, the Ourang-Outang immediately rushed through the chamber door, down the stairs, and then through a window that had unfortunately been left open, into the street.\\n\\nThe Frenchman followed in despair. The ape, still holding the razor, would sometimes stop to look back and make gestures at its pursuer until he nearly caught up. Then it would run off again. The chase continued this way for a long time. The streets were completely silent, since it was nearly three in the morning. As it ran down an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the fugitive noticed a light shining from the open window of Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s fourth-floor chamber. It rushed to the house, saw the lightning rod, and climbed it with unbelievable agility. Grasping the shutter, which stood fully open against the wall, it used it to swing itself directly onto the bed\u2019s headboard. The entire feat took less than a minute. As the Ourang-Outang entered the room, it kicked the shutter open again.\\n\\nMeanwhile, the sailor was both relieved and confused. He strongly hoped he could now recapture the beast, since it could hardly escape the trap it had entered except by the rod, where he might catch it as it climbed down. But he also had great reason to fear what it might do inside the house. That fear drove him to continue after it. A sailor can climb a lightning rod without much trouble; yet when he reached the height of the window, far to his left, he could go no farther. All he could do was stretch out far enough to glimpse the room inside. At that sight, horror nearly made him lose his grip. Then the terrible screams rose into the night, waking the residents of the Rue Morgue. Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, dressed in their nightclothes, seemed to have been arranging papers in the iron chest already mentioned, which had been rolled into the center of the room. It stood open, with its contents scattered on the floor beside it. The victims must have been seated with their backs to the window; and, judging by the time between the beast\u2019s entrance and their screams, they probably did not notice it at once. They would naturally have thought the shutter\u2019s closing was caused by the wind.\\n\\nAs the sailor looked in, the huge animal had seized Madame L\u2019Espanaye by her hair, loose because she had been combing it, and was waving the razor around her face, copying the movements of a barber.\"}"
