          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"340f0de2d16f849f8ec0c9fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father may of course spare you, if your mother can. Daughters are never of such importance to a father. And if you stay another full month, I shall be able to take one of you as far as London, since I am going there early in June for a week. Dawson does not object to the barouche-box, so there will be plenty of room for one of you\u2014and indeed, if the weather is cool, I should not object to taking both of you, as neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind, madam, but I believe we must keep to our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared resigned. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always say exactly what I think, and I cannot bear the thought of two young women travelling by post alone. It is highly improper. You must manage to send someone. I have the greatest dislike for that sort of thing. Young women should always be properly protected and attended, according to their position in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted that two male servants accompany her. Miss Darcy, daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not properly have appeared in any other way. I am extremely attentive to such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad it occurred to me to mention it, for it would truly reflect badly on you to let them travel alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is sending a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant, does he? I am very glad you have someone who thinks of such things. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, you will be well attended.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions about their journey; and because she did not answer all of them herself, attention was required\u2014which Elizabeth thought fortunate for her. Otherwise, with her mind so occupied, she might have forgotten where she was. Reflection had to be saved for hours of solitude: whenever she was alone, she gave herself up to it as her greatest relief; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, during which she could indulge in the full pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nShe was well on her way to knowing Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She examined every sentence, and her feelings toward its writer varied greatly at different times. When she recalled the manner of his proposal, she was still filled with indignation; but when she considered how unjustly she had judged and reproached him, her anger turned upon herself, and his disappointed feelings became an object of compassion.\"}"
