          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"add0f652f8f94abfab56c69b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You suggested it yourself, two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember\u2014the time the Church first tested its strength against us, then decided it would be wiser to wait for a more promising season. Well, how have you arranged the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI run twelve enormously strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014from a large dynamo in the cave\u2014a dynamo with no brushes except a positive and a negative one\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a circular stretch of level ground a hundred yards across; they form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014that is, twelve concentric circles\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight; continue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fences are attached to heavy oak posts only three feet apart, and the posts are sunk five feet into the ground.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is good and solid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. The wires have no ground connection outside the cave. They run from the dynamo\u2019s positive brush; there is a ground connection through the negative brush; the other ends return to the cave, and each is grounded separately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s too costly\u2014it wastes power for nothing. You need no ground connection except the one through the negative brush. The other end of every wire must be brought back into the cave and fastened separately, and without any ground connection. Now observe the economy. A cavalry charge rushes against the fence; you are using no power, spending no money, because there is only one ground connection until those horses strike the wire. The instant they touch it, they make a connection with the negative brush through the ground, and fall dead. Don\u2019t you see? You use no energy until it is required; your lightning is there and ready, like a charge in a gun, but it costs you nothing until you fire it. Oh, yes\u2014the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I don\u2019t know how I missed that. It is not only cheaper, but more effective than the other arrangement, because if wires break or become tangled, no damage is done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, especially if we have an indicator in the cave and disconnect the broken wire. Well, go on. The Gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014that is arranged. In the center of the innermost circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I have placed a battery of thirteen Gatling guns and supplied plenty of ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it. They command every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights arrive, there will be music. The edge of the precipice above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have a wire fence there, and a Gatling.\u201d\"}"
