          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7c8b3198915e15a1fad57dc5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; the entire burden of maintaining a slow, unsatisfying correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had only recently ended when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them, as arranged, for their final walk around Lyme. They were meant to leave for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and until then they were to remain together and outdoors for as long as possible.\\n\\nAs soon as they were properly in the street, Anne found Captain Benwick drawing near her. Their conversation the evening before had not discouraged him from seeking her out again; and for some time they walked together, speaking as before of Mr. Scott and Lord Byron. They remained as unable as ever\u2014and as unable as any two readers\u2014to agree exactly on either writer\u2019s merits, until something caused an almost general rearrangement of the party, and Captain Harville replaced Captain Benwick at Anne\u2019s side.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said in a low voice, \u201cyou have done a kind thing by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he had company like yours more often. I know it is bad for him to be shut up as he is, but what can we do? We cannot leave him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d Anne said, \u201cI can readily believe that would be impossible. But in time, perhaps\u2014we know what time can do in every kind of grief; and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend is still a young mourner\u2014only since last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that is true enough,\u201d he said with a deep sigh. \u201cOnly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not learn of it so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, having just been made captain of the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading news of him. He sent letters in, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news had to follow him there\u2014but who was to tell him? Not I. I would sooner have been hauled up to the yardarm. No one could do it except that good fellow\u201d\u2014pointing to Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had arrived at Plymouth the week before, with no danger of being sent to sea again. He took his chance for the rest; applied for leave, but without waiting for an answer travelled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler at once, and did not leave the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You can imagine, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty, and replied as fully as her own feelings allowed, or as his seemed able to endure. He was too moved to return to the subject, and when he spoke again, it was of something entirely different.\"}"
