{"request_id":"dc0f5d3cc26934b9f9a059cf","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was uselessly consuming his time where he was, while letters from the friends he had made in London urged his return to conclude the arrangements they had begun for his Indian undertaking. He could postpone his departure no longer; yet, since his journey to London might be followed, sooner even than he supposed, by his more distant voyage, he begged me to grant him as much of my company as I could afford. He therefore implored me to leave my lonely island and join him at Perth, so that we might travel southward together. This letter in some measure restored me to life, and I resolved to leave my island at the end of two days.

But before I went, a duty remained which I shuddered even to contemplate: I had to pack my chemical apparatus, and for that I must enter the room that had witnessed my hateful labour and touch the instruments whose very sight disgusted me. Next morning, at dawn, I gathered enough courage to unlock my laboratory door. The fragments of the unfinished creature I had destroyed were strewn across the floor, and I nearly imagined that I had torn apart the living body of a man. I stopped to compose myself, then went into the chamber. With a shaking hand I carried the instruments from the room; but I considered that I ought not to leave the remnants of my work to awaken the peasants' horror and suspicion. I therefore placed them in a basket with many heavy stones and, having secured it, resolved to cast it into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the shore, cleaning and putting my chemical apparatus in order.

Nothing could have been more entire than the change in my feelings since the night when the dæmon appeared. Before, I had viewed my promise with dark despair, as something which, whatever its results, had to be kept; but now it seemed that a veil had been lifted from my eyes, and that I saw plainly for the first time. The thought of resuming my labours never once entered my mind. The threat I had heard pressed upon my thoughts, yet I did not consider that any willing action of mine might prevent it. I had determined that creating another being like the fiend I had first fashioned would be an act of the lowest and most dreadful selfishness, and I drove away every thought that might have brought me to another judgment."}

