          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b453e0c134c181a2cf58680f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These delegates had been sent to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter. They had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and a desolate countryside, nursing fierce hatred for one another all the way; and now they argued their respective cases with undiminished party zeal.\\n\\nBy questioning the delegates separately, and after lengthy inquiry, we learned the true condition of Paris. Ever since parliament had appointed him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had submitted to Adrian. He was our captain, to lead us from our native land to unknown shores; our lawgiver and our protector. When our plan of emigration was first arranged, no lasting division among our people had been intended, and the authority over the entire body, rising by degrees, culminated in the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events altered our plans, and caused the greater number of us to be separated from the supreme leader for nearly two months. They had crossed in two separate groups, and dissension arose between them upon reaching Paris.\\n\\nThey found Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, returning travellers and merchants, along with letters, kept us regularly informed of the disease\u2019s ravages on the continent. But as deaths increased, this contact diminished and then stopped. Even within England, communication between one part of the island and another became slow and infrequent. No ship crossed the waters between Calais and Dover; or if some sorrowful traveller, anxious to learn whether his relatives lived or died, set out from the French coast to return to us, the hungry sea often swallowed his small vessel, or within a day or two he caught the disease and died before he could tell of France\u2019s desolation. We were therefore largely ignorant of conditions on the continent, and still held some vague hope of finding many companions across its broad expanse. But the same causes that had so terribly reduced the English nation had found even wider scope for destruction in the sister country. France was a blank: along the long road from Calais to Paris, not a single human being was encountered. In Paris there were a few\u2014perhaps a hundred\u2014who, resigned to their approaching fate, drifted through the capital\u2019s streets and gathered to speak of former times, with that liveliness, even cheerfulness, that rarely abandons the people of that nation.\\n\\nThe English took possession of Paris without opposition. Its tall houses and narrow streets were without life. A few pale figures could be seen at the familiar gathering place in the Tuileries; they wondered why the islanders had come to their doomed city\u2014for in the depths of misery, sufferers always imagine that their own share of calamity is the most bitter, just as, under severe pain, we would trade the particular torment that makes us writhe for any other that might afflict a different part of the body.\"}"
