{"request_id":"15df7b9dc305e1f9a26393b0","rewritten_text":"Few could lay claim to a heart so pure, or to such genuine humility of spirit, united with a steadfast trust in her own rectitude and faith in that of others. From her birth she had led a secluded life. Her mother had died while she was still very young, but her father had given himself up to the direction of her education—He entertained many singular notions which had shaped the plan he pursued concerning her—She knew well the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those English heroes who had flourished some centuries before, while she remained almost uninformed of the events then passing around her: she had read scarcely any writers of the preceding fifty years, though, excepting this, her reading was exceedingly wide. Thus, though she seemed less versed in the mysteries of life and society than he, her knowledge was of a more profound nature and rested on surer grounds; and even had her beauty and gentleness failed to enchant him, her intellect would always have kept his in subjection. He regarded her as his guide, and so deep was his worship that he took pleasure in increasing within himself the sense of inferiority which she at times inspired.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. On this occasion he quitted college, and, withdrawing for a time from his former friends, settled near his Diana, receiving all his comfort from her soft voice and still dearer embraces. This brief absence from his companions gave him the resolution to claim his independence. He felt that, however much they might ridicule his proposed marriage in speech, they would not venture to show it after it was accomplished; and so, having sought the approval of his guardian, which he gained with some trouble, and that of his mistress's father, which was granted more readily, he informed no one else of his design, and before his twentieth birthday had become Diana's husband.

He loved her passionately, and her affection possessed a charm which would not allow him to think of anything but her. He asked several of his college friends to visit him, but their levity revolted him. Diana had lifted the veil that had hitherto confined him to boyhood: he had become a man, and marvelled that he could ever have shared the cant phrases and notions of his fellow collegians, or that he had for an instant dreaded the judgment of people such as these. He cast aside his old friendships, not through inconstancy, but because they were truly beneath him."}

