{"request_id":"01a0849135d631bdd9bd8e17","rewritten_text":"“I reasoned in this manner—_a posteriori_. The murderers had made their escape by one of these windows. If so, they could not have secured the sashes again from within, since the sashes were discovered fastened;—a point whose very plainness had checked the police inquiry in this direction. Yet the sashes _were_ secured. They _must_, therefore, possess the means of fastening themselves. There was no avoiding that inference. I went to the unobstructed casement, drew out the nail with some trouble, and tried to lift the sash. It withstood every effort, as I had expected. Some hidden spring, I now knew, must be present; and this confirmation of my notion assured me that my premises, at least, were sound, however mysterious the circumstances connected with the nails still seemed. A close examination quickly revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it, and, content with the discovery, did not raise the sash.

“I then restored the nail and examined it closely. A person leaving by this window might have shut it again, and the spring would have caught it—but the nail could not have been put back. The inference was evident, and once more restricted the scope of my investigation. The assassins _must_ have fled through the other window. If, then, the springs of both sashes were alike, as was likely, some difference _must_ appear in the nails, or at least in the manner in which they were fixed. Climbing upon the sacking of the bedstead, I minutely inspected the second casement above the head-board. Running my hand down behind the board, I easily found and pressed the spring, which, as I had presumed, was identical in nature with its neighbor. I next examined the nail. It was as strong as the first, and seemed to be fitted in precisely the same way—driven almost to its head.

“You will say I was perplexed; but if you suppose that, you have mistaken the character of the inductions. To borrow a sporting expression, I had never been ‘at fault.’ The trail had not, for a moment, been lost. No link in the chain contained a defect. I had followed the secret to its final issue,—and that issue was _the nail._ In every particular, I repeat, it resembled its counterpart in the other window; yet this resemblance was utterly nothing (however conclusive it might appear) beside the fact that here, at this very point, the clew came to an end. ‘There _must_ be some defect,’ I said, ‘in the nail.’ I touched it; and the head, together with about a quarter-inch of the shank, came away in my fingers."}

