{"request_id":"679bbbef6271b2a80c99b3c9","rewritten_text":"I then made a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher, and inserted a plug of soft wood, shaped tapering, or conical. I thrust this plug farther in or drew it out, as occasion required, until, after several trials, it reached precisely that degree of tightness at which the water, trickling through the hole and dropping into the pitcher beneath, would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This, naturally, was quickly and readily determined by observing what proportion of the pitcher was filled within any stated time. All this being settled, the remainder of the scheme was plain. My bed was arranged on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head rested directly beneath the pitcher’s mouth. It was clear that, at the end of an hour, the full pitcher would overflow, and would overflow at its mouth, which stood somewhat lower than its edge. It was equally clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet must strike my face, and that the certain result would be to awaken me at once, even from the deepest sleep imaginable.

“It was quite eleven when I had finished these preparations, and I went immediately to bed, entirely confident in the success of my contrivance. In this I was not disappointed. Exactly every sixty minutes my faithful chronometer roused me; after emptying the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole and discharging the office of condenser, I returned to bed. These stated breaks in my sleep gave me even less inconvenience than I had expected; and when I at last rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, with the sun already many degrees above my horizon.

“April 3d. I found the balloon at a truly immense elevation, and the apparent convexity of the earth had increased considerably. In the ocean below lay a group of black dots, undoubtedly islands. At a great distance northward I saw a narrow white line, or streak, of extraordinary brilliance along the horizon’s edge, and I did not hesitate to suppose it the southern border of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity was strongly stirred, for I hoped to proceed much farther north, and might perhaps at some time find myself directly over the Pole itself. I now regretted that my great altitude would, in such an event, prevent me from making as exact a survey as I desired.”"}

