          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f71a04bd59abfb7913f65e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s final reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was more thronged than ever. Six Cabinet Ministers had arrived straight from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e, wearing their stars and ribands; every pretty woman was in her finest gown; and at the far end of the picture-gallery stood the Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a stout, Tartar-faced lady with little black eyes and magnificent emeralds, speaking atrocious French at the height of her voice and laughing extravagantly at all that was said to her. It was, indeed, a remarkable mixture of people. Splendid peeresses conversed pleasantly with furious Radicals, fashionable preachers grazed coat-tails with distinguished sceptics, a whole flock of bishops pursued a plump prima-donna from room to room, several Royal Academicians stood upon the staircase in the guise of artists, and rumour had it that the supper-room had at one point been completely packed with geniuses. Altogether, it was among Lady Windermere\u2019s most successful evenings, and the Princess did not leave until nearly half-past eleven.\\n\\nThe moment she had departed, Lady Windermere went back to the picture-gallery, where a famous political economist was gravely expounding the scientific theory of music to an offended Hungarian virtuoso, and began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. She was extraordinarily lovely, with her noble ivory throat, great blue eyes like forget-me-nots, and thick coils of golden hair. _Or pur_ they were\u2014not that washed-out straw shade which now arrogates to itself the charming title of gold, but gold such as is spun through sunbeams or concealed in curious amber; and they lent her face something of a saint\u2019s setting, together with no small share of a sinner\u2019s allure. She made a curious psychological subject. In early life she had learned the significant truth that nothing resembles innocence so closely as indiscretion; and through a succession of wild adventures, half of them entirely harmless, she had won every privilege of a personality. She had changed her husband more than once; Debrett, indeed, assigns her three marriages; yet, since she had never changed her lover, society had ceased long before to gossip scandalously about her. She was now forty, without children, and possessed of that excessive appetite for pleasure which is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nAll at once she glanced eagerly about the room and said, in her clear contralto, \u2018Where is my cheiromantist?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Your what, Gladys?\u2019 cried the Duchess, starting despite herself.\\n\\n\u2018My cheiromantist, Duchess; at present I cannot exist without him.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Gladys! You are invariably so original,\u2019 murmured the Duchess, endeavouring to recall precisely what a cheiromantist was, while hoping it was not identical with a cheiropodist.\\n\\n\u2018He visits my hand regularly twice a week,\u2019 Lady Windermere continued, \u2018and is most fascinating about it.\u2019\"}"
