          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f635cbc26dc8447a622206f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man, a very pleasant, kind-looking old man when I woke him up. I told him my brother and I had been hunting on Spanish Island yesterday afternoon, and had camped on part of a raft we found. Around midnight, he must have kicked his gun while dreaming, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted the doctor to go over and fix it, without saying anything or letting anybody know, because we meant to come home that evening and surprise the folks.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he says.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. Then, after a minute, he says:\\n\\n\u201cHow did you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe was dreaming,\u201d I says, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAn unusual dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nSo he lit his lantern, got his saddlebags, and we started. But when he saw the canoe, he didn\u2019t like the looks of it. He said it was big enough for one person, but didn\u2019t seem safe for two. I says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you don\u2019t need to be afraid, sir. It carried all three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns; that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nBut he put his foot on the gunwale and rocked it, then shook his head and said he reckoned he\u2019d look for a bigger one. But they were all locked and chained. So he took my canoe and told me to wait until he came back. Or I could look around some more, or maybe I\u2019d better go home and get things ready for the surprise, if I wanted to. But I said I didn\u2019t. So I told him exactly how to find the raft, and off he went.\\n\\nPretty soon I got an idea. I says to myself, suppose he can\u2019t fix that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, as the saying goes? Suppose it takes three or four days? What are we going to do\u2014stay around there until he lets the cat out of the bag? No, sir; I know what I\u2019ll do. I\u2019ll wait, and when he comes back, if he says he has to go again, I\u2019ll get down there too, even if I have to swim. Then we\u2019ll tie him up, keep him, and push off down the river. When Tom is done with him, we\u2019ll give him whatever it\u2019s worth, or everything we have, and then let him go ashore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a pile of lumber to get some sleep. The next time I woke up, the sun was high over my head! I jumped out and hurried to the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he had gone away sometime in the night and hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
