          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"18902a2012519302adc6b463\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning she was discovered dead in her bed, apparently having suffered no pain. Sir Mathew Reid was summoned immediately, but naturally nothing could be done, and she was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death she had made her will, leaving Lord Arthur her small house in Curzon Street, together with all her furniture, personal belongings, and pictures, except for her collection of miniatures, which was to pass to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which was intended for Sybil Merton. The estate was not worth much; yet Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was most anxious that Lord Arthur should return at once, if he could, since there were many bills requiring payment, and Lady Clementina had never kept proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s kindly thought of him, and felt that Mr. Podgers had much to answer for. Still, his love for Sybil outweighed every other feeling, and the knowledge that he had fulfilled his duty brought him peace and consolation. On reaching Charing Cross, he felt entirely happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him with great kindness. Sybil made him promise never again to let anything stand between them, and their marriage was arranged for the 7th of June. Life appeared bright and lovely to him once more, and all his former happiness returned.\\n\\nOne day, however, while he was going through the Curzon Street house with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil, burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers full of miscellaneous rubbish, the young girl gave a sudden little cry of pleasure.\\n\\n\u2018What have you found, Sybil?\u2019 asked Lord Arthur, raising his eyes from his task with a smile.\\n\\n\u2018This charming little silver _bonbonni\u00e8re_, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it quaint and Dutch? Do give it to me! I know amethysts will not suit me until I am more than eighty.\u2019\\n\\nIt was the box that had contained the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a slight flush rose in his face. He had almost wholly forgotten what he had done, and it struck him as a strange coincidence that Sybil, for whose sake he had endured all that dreadful anxiety, should be the first to recall it to him.\\n\\n\u2018Certainly you may have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, thank you, Arthur; and may I have the _bonbon_ as well? I never imagined Lady Clementina cared for sweets. I thought her much too intellectual.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur turned deathly pale, and a dreadful thought passed through his mind.\"}"
