          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c515ff0a175710b655befa23\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After sharing in the general distress over the dreadful outcome of this event, which Elizabeth thought almost certain and Miss Bennet could not say was entirely impossible, Elizabeth continued, \u201cBut tell me everything I have not already heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they suspected anything before the elopement? They must have seen them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some attachment, especially on Lydia\u2019s part, but nothing that gave him cause for alarm. I am so sorry for him. He behaved with the greatest attention and kindness. He was on his way to us to assure us of his concern before he had any notion that they had not gone to Scotland; when that fear first arose, it hurried his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny convinced that Wickham would not marry her? Did he know they meant to run away? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Colonel Forster questioned him, Denny denied knowing anything of their plan and would not give his true opinion of it. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and from that I am inclined to hope he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came himself, none of you doubted, I suppose, that they were truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea have entered our minds? I felt a little uneasy\u2014a little afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness in marrying him, because I knew his conduct had not always been entirely proper. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only felt what an imprudent match it must be. Kitty then admitted, with the very natural satisfaction of knowing more than the rest of us, that Lydia\u2019s last letter had prepared her for such a step. She had apparently known for many weeks that they were in love.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I do not think so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think poorly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit that he did not speak as well of Wickham as he once had. He believed him imprudent and extravagant; and since this sad affair occurred, it has been said that he left Meryton deeply in debt. But I hope that is untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secretive\u2014if we had told what we knew of him\u2014this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut exposing anyone\u2019s past faults, without knowing what their present feelings might be, seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
