          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5adcda744e3b3af285641f1f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of sympathy had been sent to Ireland. The slight had come back upon the offender; for when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no letter of condolence arrived at Kellynch. There was therefore only too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples considered the family connection ended. The question was how to put this troubling matter right and be accepted as cousins again. In a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot thought it unimportant. \u201cFamily connections were always worth keeping, and good company was always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple had taken a house in Laura Place for three months, where she would live in style. She had been in Bath the year before, and Lady Russell had heard her described as a charming woman. It was very desirable to renew the connection, if it could be done without any loss of propriety on the Elliots\u2019 side.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, chose his own method, and at last wrote a very elegant letter, full of explanation, regret, and pleading, to his right honourable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot could admire the letter, but it achieved what was needed: three scrawled lines from the Dowager Viscountess. \u201cShe was very much honoured, and would be happy to know them.\u201d The effort was over, and the pleasures began. They visited Laura Place; they had cards from the Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, to display wherever they would be most noticed; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been very pleasant, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they caused; but they were nothing at all. They had no special grace of manner, talent, or intelligence. Lady Dalrymple had gained the name of \u201ca charming woman\u201d because she smiled and gave a polite answer to everyone. Miss Carteret, who had even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she would never have been accepted in Camden Place except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted that she had expected something better, but still said, \u201cIt was an acquaintance worth having.\u201d When Anne ventured to give Mr Elliot her opinion of them, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves. Yet he continued to insist that, as family connections, as good company, and as people who would gather good company around them, they had value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is clever, well-informed people who have a great deal to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
