          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"09379d279fa9d653fb8e729b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If it had been up to me, he would never have gotten out. Morgan le Fay hated him completely, and she would never have forgiven him. Yet his offense came more from carelessness than real wickedness. He had said that she had red hair. Well, she did; but that was not the proper way to put it. When red-haired people belong to a certain social class, their hair is called auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: among those forty-seven prisoners were five whose names, crimes, and imprisonment dates were no longer known. One woman and four men\u2014all bent, wrinkled, and with their minds gone, like ancient patriarchs. They themselves had forgotten these facts long before; or at least they had only vague ideas about them, nothing certain, and nothing they told the same way twice. The line of priests whose duty had been to pray daily with the prisoners, remind them that God had placed them there for some wise reason, and teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were what He liked to see in people of lower rank, had traditions about these poor human wrecks, but nothing more. Those traditions did not go far, because they concerned only how long the prisoners had been confined, not what their crimes were. Even with tradition's help, all that could be proved was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years. How much longer they had been denied it, no one could guess. The king and queen knew nothing about these poor beings except that they were inherited property, passed down with the throne from the previous owners. No history had come with them, so their new owners considered them worthless and took no interest in them. I said to the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why in the world didn't you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question puzzled her. She did not know why she had not; the idea had simply never entered her mind. So, without knowing it, she was predicting the exact future history of the prisoners in the Castle d'If. It was clear to me now that, because of her upbringing, these inherited prisoners were simply property\u2014nothing more and nothing less. When we inherit property, after all, it does not occur to us to throw it away, even if we do not value it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats up into the open world and the blazing afternoon sun\u2014after first blindfolding them, out of kindness to eyes so long untroubled by light\u2014they were a sight to see. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pitiful horrors, every one of them; the truest possible children of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
