believed\u2014indeed, scarcely worth notice; and thus the difficulty of deciding was over. Everything else, at least in imagination, went on with perfect ease. The lesser concerns of tables and chairs, lamps and music, tea and supper, arranged themselves; or were dismissed as small matters to be settled whenever convenient between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.\u2014Every person invited was sure to attend; Frank had already written to Enscombe, asking leave to remain a few days beyond his fortnight, and this could not possibly be denied. It was to be a most delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she agreed most heartily that it must be so. They did not need her as an adviser; but as an approver, (a far safer office,) she was most welcome. Her approval, at once broad and particular, eager and unceasing, could not fail to gratify; and for another half-hour they all paced about between the various rooms, some proposing, some listening, and every one happily enjoying the prospect before them.\"}"
