          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c86e3ffc35013ff6af1df077\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was then that his eyes, as well as my own, rested on the bit of parchment, which I at first took for paper. It lay partly covered by the sand, with one corner protruding. Close to the place where we discovered it, I noticed the remains of the hull of what seemed to have been a ship\u2019s long-boat. The wreck appeared to have lain there for an immense time; for its likeness to boat timbers was barely discernible.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, folded the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Shortly afterward we set out for home, and on the road encountered Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he asked me to permit him to carry it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately slipped it into his waistcoat pocket, leaving out the parchment in which it had been wrapped, and which I had kept in my hand while he examined it. Possibly he feared I might alter my resolution, and judged it wisest to secure the prize without delay\u2014you know his zeal in every matter relating to Natural History. Meanwhile, unconsciously, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou recollect that, when I approached the table to make a drawing of the beetle, I could find no paper in its customary place. I searched the drawer, but there was none there either. I felt through my pockets, in hopes of finding an old letter, and my hand came upon the parchment. I give you this exact account of how it reached my possession, because the circumstances struck me with unusual force.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you will call me fanciful\u2014but I had already formed a sort of connexion. I had joined two links in a great chain. A boat lay on a sea-shore, and near that boat was a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014bearing the figure of a skull. You will naturally inquire, \u2018what connexion is there?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the familiar emblem of the pirate. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in every engagement.\\n\\n\u201cI have said that the fragment was parchment, not paper. Parchment is lasting\u2014almost indestructible. Things of slight consequence are seldom entrusted to parchment; for, in the common business of drawing or writing, it is far less suitable than paper. This thought suggested some significance\u2014some pertinence\u2014in the death\u2019s-head. Nor did I overlook the shape of the parchment. Though one corner had been destroyed by some accident, its original form could plainly be seen to have been oblong.\"}"
