          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3546e5a9aa0569987b207505\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked why I wanted it, and why I believed I had any right to it, I would tell him\u2014though he cared little for such poor dreams\u2014that I had loved Estella deeply and for a long time. Though I had lost her and must live a life of grief, anything connected with her was still closer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. But Mr. Jaggers stood perfectly still and silent, seeming completely unmoved by my appeal. So I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you are a man with a kind heart. I have seen your cheerful home, your old father, and all the innocent, happy, playful habits that brighten your business life. I beg you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and make him see that, considering everything, he ought to be more frank with me!\u201d\\n\\nI had never seen two men look at each other as strangely as Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this speech. At first I feared Wemmick would be dismissed from his job at once; but that fear faded when I saw Mr. Jaggers soften into something like a smile, while Wemmick grew more confident.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is all this?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou, with an old father, and you, with pleasant and playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what harm does it do?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis man must be the cleverest fraud in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Wemmick replied, becoming bolder still. \u201cI think you are another.\u201d\\n\\nAgain they gave each other those strange looks, each seeming still unsure whether the other was deceiving him.\\n\\n\u201cYou, with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cAs long as it does not interfere with business,\u201d Wemmick replied, \u201clet it be so. And now, looking at you, sir, I should not be surprised if you were planning to have a pleasant home of your own someday, when you are tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded two or three times, as if thinking back, and actually sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe will not speak of \u2018poor dreams.\u2019 You know more about such things than I do, because your experience of them is much more recent. But now, about this other matter. I will put a case to you. Remember: I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited for me to say that I understood he had clearly stated that he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201csuppose this case. Suppose that a woman, in circumstances like those you have described, kept her child hidden and had to tell her legal adviser about it, because he explained that, in order to know how far his defence could go, he had to know the truth about that child.\u201d\"}"
