          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1956cf3b17b721a2d4f77b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water to finish their meal. After a while Becky suggested that they go on again. Tom was quiet for a moment. Then he said:\\n\\n\u201cBecky, can you stand it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face turned pale, but she thought she could.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky, we have to stay here, where there is water to drink. That little bit is our last candle!\u201d\\n\\nBecky burst into tears and cries. Tom did what he could to comfort her, but it helped little. At last Becky said:\\n\\n\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and come looking for us!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will! Of course they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe they\u2019re looking for us now, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, maybe they are. I hope they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen will they miss us, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen they get back to the boat, I suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it may be dark by then\u2014will they notice we haven\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. But your mother will miss you as soon as they get home.\u201d\\n\\nThe frightened look on Becky\u2019s face brought Tom to his senses, and he saw he had made a mistake. Becky was not supposed to go home that night! The children fell silent and thoughtful. A moment later, Becky\u2019s fresh burst of grief showed Tom that she had thought of it too\u2014that Sunday morning might be half over before Mrs. Thatcher discovered Becky was not at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children stared at their small piece of candle and watched it slowly, cruelly melt away. At last only half an inch of wick remained. They saw the weak flame rise and fall, climb the thin column of smoke, pause at its top for a moment, and then\u2014the horror of complete darkness took over!\\n\\nNeither could say how long afterward Becky slowly realized she was crying in Tom\u2019s arms. All they knew was that, after what seemed like a very long time, both woke from a heavy, numb sleep and returned to their misery. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014perhaps Monday. He tried to make Becky talk, but her grief was too great; all her hope was gone. Tom said they must have been missed long ago, and surely people were searching for them. He would shout, and perhaps someone would come. He tried, but the far-off echoes sounded so terrible in the darkness that he did not try again.\\n\\nThe hours dragged by, and hunger began to torture the prisoners again. Part of Tom\u2019s half of the cake remained; they divided it and ate it. But they felt hungrier than before.\"}"
