          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4486521b2b1ccab2be1083a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading his letter over again while writing her reply made Harriet feel so tender toward him that Emma found it especially necessary to strengthen her resolve with a few firm phrases. Harriet was so troubled by the thought of making him unhappy, so concerned about what his mother and sisters would think and say, and so eager that they should not consider her ungrateful, that Emma believed she would have accepted the young man after all if he had appeared before her at that moment.\\n\\nStill, the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was over, and Harriet was safe. She was rather sad all evening, but Emma could understand her kind regrets, and sometimes eased them by speaking of her own affection, and sometimes by bringing up Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d Harriet said rather sadly.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were invited, could I bear to part with you, my Harriet. You are far too necessary at Hartfield to be spared for Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure I should never wish to go there, for I am never happy except at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nAfter some time, she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what had happened. I am sure Miss Nash would be\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and she only married a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne would be sorry to find more pride or finer feelings in a schoolteacher, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such a chance of marriage. Even this success would seem valuable in her eyes. As for anything better for you, I suppose she knows nothing. The attentions of a certain person can hardly be part of Highbury gossip yet. So far, I imagine you and I are the only people whose looks and manners have made his feelings clear.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about wondering why people liked her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton certainly cheered her; but after a while, she again felt tenderly toward the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has received my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not take it so very much to heart.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think of absent friends who may be more happily occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful the original is, and, after they have asked five or six times, letting them hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
