          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"839b0fb5867bffd5fb519e82\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity that always brings misery must have been broken, and the malignant fate that rules it would not allow such a violation of her eternal laws. But why should I complain? Misery was my natural element, and nothing except what was miserable could come near me; if Woodville had been happy, I would never have known him. And can I, who for so many years had been fed on tears and sustained by the dew of grief, hesitate to tell a story of sorrow and death?\\n\\nWoodville was forced to travel into the country and was delayed day after day, painfully separated from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her saying that she was slightly ill, yet urging him to hurry to her: she would find health in his eyes, and his company would be her surest medicine. He was held back three more days, then rushed to her. His heart, though he did not know why, foretold disaster; he had heard nothing further from her. He feared she had grown worse, and that fear made him impatient and restless until he could once again see her standing before him in health and beauty. For a dark voice seemed always to whisper, \u201cYou will never see her again as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her home, everything within was silent. He passed through several rooms; in one, he saw a servant weeping bitterly. Weak with fear, he could barely ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d He only heard the dreadful reply: \u201cNot yet.\u201d These shocking words struck him as less terrible than those he had expected; to learn that she still lived, and that he might still hope, brought him some relief. He remembered her letter and gave himself up to the wild thought that his kisses, warm with love and life, would breathe new spirit into her; that she could not die while he was beside her; that his presence was the talisman preserving her life.\\n\\nHe hurried to her sickroom. She lay there, her cheeks burning with fever, her eyes closed, apparently unconscious. He gathered her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses upon her burning lips; in a voice restrained by anguish, he called her by the most tender names. \u201cCome back, Elinor; I am with you\u2014your life, your love. Come back, dearest one. You promised me this gift: that I would bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? Never to see you again? To be parted from one who is part of myself, without whom I have neither memory nor future?\u201d\"}"
