          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0836abdd4f3347827c9fae8b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am very pleased to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cAnd please tell her from me that she cannot expect to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he replied, \u201cshe has no need of such advice. She practises very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practise too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist that she neglect it on no account. I often tell young ladies that no one can become excellent at music without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play truly well unless she practises more; and although Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is very welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would be in no one\u2019s way there, you know, in that part of the house.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked slightly ashamed of his aunt\u2019s bad manners and said nothing.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him, and she immediately sat down at the instrument. He pulled a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then resumed talking, as before, with her other nephew. At last he moved away from her and, with his usual deliberate manner, came toward the pianoforte and placed himself where he could see the performer\u2019s face clearly. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing, and at the first suitable pause she turned to him with a teasing smile and said,\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in such grandeur to hear me. But I will not be alarmed, even though your sister does play so well. There is a stubbornness in me that cannot bear to be frightened by other people\u2019s wishes. My courage always grows whenever anyone tries to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not say you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly believe that I meant to alarm you. And I have known you long enough to understand that you take great pleasure in sometimes claiming opinions that are not, in fact, your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this description of herself and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very charming idea of me, and teach you not to believe a word I say. I am especially unlucky to meet someone so able to reveal my true character in a part of the world where I had hoped to make a respectable impression.\u201d\"}"
