          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1f10ad824da8c12ead04dbb6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having seen our friend properly settled in his new office, we turned our attention to Windsor. Its closeness to London made our departure from Raymond and Perdita seem less like a painful separation. We took leave of them at the Protectoral Palace. It was charming to see my sister, as if entering into the spirit of a play, trying to assume her position with suitable dignity. Her inward pride and her humble manner were now more at odds than ever. Her shyness was not affected, but sprang from a fear that she would not be properly valued, and from the slight regard she gave to the world's neglect\u2014qualities that also marked Raymond. Yet Perdita thought more steadily of others than he did; part of her reserve came from a desire not to make those around her feel inferior, a thought that never entered her own mind. By birth and education, Idris would have been better suited to the forms of ceremony; but the ease with which habit enabled her to perform them made them tiresome to her. Perdita, despite every disadvantage, plainly enjoyed her new position. She was too occupied by fresh ideas to feel much sorrow at our departure. She bade us farewell affectionately and promised to visit soon; but she did not regret the circumstances that separated us. Raymond's spirits were boundless. He scarcely knew what to do with his newly acquired power; his mind was crowded with plans, though he had settled on none. Yet he promised himself, his friends, and the world that the era of his Protectorship would be marked by some deed of extraordinary glory. So we spoke of them and reflected upon them as, reduced in number, we returned to Windsor Castle. We were deeply pleased to have escaped political turmoil, and sought our solitude with renewed eagerness. We lacked no occupation; but my restless nature now turned solely toward intellectual effort, and I found serious study an excellent remedy for the feverish spirit that idleness would surely have stirred in me. Perdita had allowed us to bring Clara back to Windsor, and she, together with my two lovely children, provided constant interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe only thing that troubled our peace was Adrian's health. It visibly declined, though without any symptom that might reveal his illness, unless his brighter eyes, lively expression, and flushed cheeks gave us reason to fear consumption. But he felt neither pain nor alarm. He devoted himself eagerly to books, and rested from study in the company he loved best: that of his sister and myself.\"}"
