          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63c594ad7a27c3c95d51d787\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut that is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI may as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, when you so clearly meant to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, your judgment, and even your character? If I was uncivil, was that not some excuse? But I have other reasons to be offended. You know I do. Even if my own feelings had not already decided against you\u2014if they had been indifferent, or even favourable\u2014do you think anything could persuade me to accept the man who has ruined, perhaps forever, the happiness of a sister I dearly love?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the feeling passed quickly. He listened without trying to interrupt as she went on:\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason to think badly of you. Nothing can excuse the unfair and ungenerous part you played in that matter. You dare not, and cannot, deny that you were the chief, if not the only, cause of separating them, exposing one to the world\u2019s blame for being fickle and unreliable, the other to ridicule for disappointed hopes, and bringing them both the deepest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused and saw, with considerable anger, that he listened in a way that showed no remorse at all. He even looked at her with a smile of forced disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nWith pretended calm, he replied, \u201cI do not deny that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased I succeeded. I have been kinder to him than I have been to myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth ignored the appearance of this polite remark, though she understood its meaning, and it was not likely to soften her feelings toward him.\\n\\n\u201cBut this is not the only matter on which my dislike of you rests,\u201d she continued. \u201cLong before it happened, I had formed my opinion of you. Your character was revealed in the account Mr. Wickham gave me many months ago. What can you say about that? By what imagined act of friendship can you defend yourself here? Or by what false account can you deceive others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a strong interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, less calmly now, his face growing redder.\\n\\n\u201cWho could know of his misfortunes and not take an interest in him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated scornfully. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have indeed been great.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd caused by you,\u201d Elizabeth cried passionately. \u201cYou have brought him to his present poverty\u2014relative poverty.\u201d\"}"
