{"request_id":"5953d756afaa20b2b1fe42e0","rewritten_text":"I cannot understand why she ever wished to be close to me; but if the same circumstances happened again, I am sure I would be deceived again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received neither note nor line from her. When she did come, it was plain that she took no pleasure in the visit. She made a brief, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing about wishing to see me again, and was so changed in every way that, when she left, I was fully resolved to end our acquaintance. I pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I can safely say that every step toward friendship began with her. Yet I pity her, because she must know she has acted wrongly, and because I am certain that concern for her brother is the cause of it. I need say no more; and though we know this concern is entirely unnecessary, if she feels it, it easily explains her behaviour toward me. Since he is so deservedly dear to his sister, any anxiety she feels for him is natural and affectionate. Still, I cannot help wondering that she has such fears now, because if he had cared for me at all, we must have met long ago. I am certain he knows I am in town, from something she herself said; yet from the way she speaks, it seems she wishes to persuade herself that he truly prefers Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging too severely, I might almost say there is a strong appearance of deceit in all this. I will try to put away every painful thought and think only of what can make me happy: your affection and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley said something about his never returning to Netherfield, and about giving up the house, though she did not speak with certainty. We had better not mention it. I am very glad you have such pleasant news of our friends at Hunsford. Please visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits recovered when she reflected that Jane would no longer be deceived, at least by the sister. All hope concerning the brother was now completely gone."}

