          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e7056b017c26e1f577fb4e3e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to make her observations were sure to arise. Anne had soon spent enough time with all four together to form an opinion, though she was too sensible to admit it at home, where she knew it would please neither husband nor wife. Though she thought Louisa was rather the favourite, she could not help believing, as far as memory and experience allowed her to judge, that Captain Wentworth was in love with neither sister. They were more in love with him; yet even that was not truly love. It was a small fever of admiration, but it might, and probably must, become love for one of them. Charles Hayter seemed aware that he was being neglected, though Henrietta sometimes appeared divided between the two men. Anne wished she could show them all what they were doing, and point out some of the troubles they were bringing upon themselves. She suspected no one of deceit. It gave her great comfort to believe that Captain Wentworth was entirely unaware of the pain he caused. There was no triumph, no cruel triumph, in his manner. He had probably never heard of, or considered, any claim Charles Hayter might have. His only fault was in accepting\u2014for accepting was the right word\u2014the attentions of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to withdraw. Three days passed without his coming even once to Uppercross, a very clear change. He had even declined one of his usual dinner invitations; and when Mr Musgrove found him on that occasion sitting before some large books, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were sure that something was wrong, and spoke gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had given him a definite refusal, while her husband constantly expected to see him the next day. Anne could only feel that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth had gone shooting together, while the sisters at the Cottage sat quietly at their work. The sisters from the Mansion-house appeared at the window.\\n\\nIt was a beautiful November day, and the Miss Musgroves came through the small grounds. They stopped only to say that they were going for a long walk and had therefore assumed Mary would not wish to join them. When Mary at once replied, somewhat offended at not being thought a good walker, \u201cOh yes, I should like to join you very much. I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne was convinced by the looks of the two girls that this was exactly what they had not wanted. Once again, she admired the kind of necessity created by the family\u2019s habits: everything had to be shared, and everything had to be done together, however unwanted or inconvenient.\"}"
