          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5d427bd46f7b3aebd0523dce\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Still, in spite of all this, the boy became increasingly melancholy, pale, and downcast. She prescribed hot baths, sitz baths, shower baths, and plunges. The boy stayed as gloomy as a hearse. She began to reinforce the water with a scant oatmeal diet and blister-plasters. She reckoned his capacity as if he were a jug, and every day filled him with patent remedies.\\n\\nBy this time Tom had grown indifferent to persecution. This condition filled the old lady\u2019s heart with alarm. That indifference had to be shattered at any price. Then she heard of Pain-killer for the first time. She immediately ordered a quantity. She sampled it and was overcome with thankfulness. It was nothing but liquid fire. She abandoned the water cure and all the rest, and placed her faith in Pain-killer. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and waited in the keenest anxiety for the effect. At once her worries vanished and her soul was peaceful again; for the \u201cindifference\u201d had been broken up. The boy could scarcely have displayed a more frantic, vigorous interest had she kindled a fire beneath him.\\n\\nTom decided it was time to rouse himself; this manner of life might be romantic enough in his afflicted state, but it was coming to contain too little sentiment and too much bewildering variety. So he considered several schemes of escape, and at last thought of pretending to like Pain-killer. He requested it so frequently that he became a pest, and finally his aunt told him to take it himself and stop troubling her. Had it been Sid, no doubt would have spoiled her satisfaction; but because it was Tom, she secretly kept watch on the bottle. She saw that the medicine truly was decreasing, though it never entered her mind that the boy was improving the health of a crack in the sitting-room floor with it.\\n\\nOne day Tom was dosing the crack when his aunt\u2019s yellow cat appeared, purring, gazing greedily at the teaspoon, and pleading for a taste. Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask for it unless you want it, Peter.\u201d\\n\\nBut Peter indicated that he did want it.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019d better be certain.\u201d\\n\\nPeter was certain.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019ve asked for it, and I\u2019ll give it to you, because there isn\u2019t anything mean about me; but if you discover you don\u2019t like it, you mustn\u2019t blame anyone except yourself.\u201d\\n\\nPeter was willing. So Tom forced his mouth open and poured in the Pain-killer. Peter leaped a couple of yards into the air, then gave a war-whoop and raced round and round the room, crashing into furniture, overturning flower-pots, and creating general havoc.\"}"
