          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da53ad4fbdbb9461c4f2e796\",\"rewritten_text\":\"So I passed two years. Day after day, hundreds of them, went by; they brought no outward change, but a few slowly changed my mind as I drifted toward death. I began to study more, to feel more deeply the thoughts of others as they were expressed in books, to read history, and to lose my own identity among the crowds who had lived before me. Perhaps, as the feeling of immediate suffering faded, I became more human. Solitude too lost some of its appeal: I began again to long for sympathy. I was never tempted to seek a crowd, but I wished for one friend who would love me. Perhaps you will say that I was gradually becoming fit to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I wanted had to be so pure, so free from the influence of outward circumstances, that in the world I could not help being disappointed by the coarse elements that mix even with its best feelings. Believe me, I was then less fit for any connection with my fellow creatures than before. When I left them, they had tormented me, but in the way pain and illness torment: as something outside the mind that irritated it and that I wished to throw off. But now I would have wanted sympathy; I would have wished to join my soul to another's, and so prepared for myself deep disappointment and suffering. For I was as delicate as the sensitive plant, all nerves. I did not want sympathy and help in ambition or wisdom, but sweet, mutual affection: smiles to cheer me and gentle words of comfort. I wished for one heart into which I could pour my complaints without restraint, where, through the heavenly nature of the soil, blessed fruit might grow from such bad seed. Yet how could I find it? The love that is the soul of friendship is a gentle spirit, seldom found except when two kind creatures have been joined from early youth, or are bound by shared suffering and pursuits. It comes to some chosen people without being sought or expected; it falls like soft dew on favored places which, however barren before, become fertile under its kind influence with every sweet plant. But when it is desired, it flees; it mocks the prayers of those who worship it; it may give itself, but cannot be pursued.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go looking for sympathy; yet there, on my lonely heath, beneath my humble roof, with desert all around me, it came to me like a winter sunbeam, beautiful as it helps melt the piled snow. Alas, the sun shone on ruined fruit; I did not revive in its light, for I was too completely destroyed to feel its healing power.\"}"
