{"request_id":"41673d3918a54be5d003a438","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals. Few mourners attended them, and the onlookers saw them as signs of the worst disaster. Some watched the processions with frantic interest; others ran away in fear; some wept openly.

After giving immediate help to the sick, Adrian’s main effort had been to hide the signs and spread of the plague from London’s people. He knew that fear and gloomy expectations greatly aided disease, and that despair and anxious brooding made people especially open to infection. So no shocking scenes were visible: most shops remained open, and the streets still held some passing crowds. Yet, though the look of an infected city had been concealed, London seemed greatly changed to me, who had not seen it since the plague began. There were no carriages, and grass had grown high in the streets; the houses looked deserted, most shutters were closed, and the people I met wore a pale, frightened expression, unlike the usual busy manner of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading there looked even sadder and more empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded; it was the hour when he received visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and watched the petitioners enter and leave. They were people of the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had failed as trade stopped and the active pursuit of money, so common in our country, came to an end. The newcomers looked anxious, sometimes terrified, in sharp contrast to the calm, even satisfied faces of those who had seen him. I could see my friend’s influence in their quicker steps and brighter expressions. At two o’clock, no more were admitted. Those turned away left angrily or sadly, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was struck by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He was no longer bent toward the ground like a spring flower too carefully tended, which, growing beyond its strength, is weighed down by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were bright, his face calm, and an air of focused energy filled his whole person, very unlike his former weakness. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who arranged petitions or recorded the notes made during that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners still waited. I admired his fairness and patience. Those able to live outside London, he urged to leave at once, giving them the means to do so."}

