          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32c678508f2cfacf1d9939d5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at last, \u201cthere is no disputing that, since this new feeding system was introduced, the coffins have become somewhat narrower and shallower than formerly; but we must make a profit, Mr. Bumble. Properly seasoned wood is a costly commodity, sir; and the iron handles all arrive by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d observed Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery business has its inconveniences. A reasonable profit is, naturally, permitted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, certainly,\u201d returned the undertaker; \u201cand if I fail to make a profit on one particular article or another, why, I recover it in the long run, you understand\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPrecisely so,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cStill, I must say,\u201d pursued the undertaker, taking up the thread of remarks the beadle had broken off: \u201cstill, I must say, Mr. Bumble, that I labour under one very serious disadvantage: namely, that all the stout people die the soonest. Those who have lived more comfortably, and paid rates for a great many years, are the first to fail when they enter the house; and I assure you, Mr. Bumble, that three or four inches beyond one\u2019s estimate cuts a great gap in one\u2019s profits: particularly when a man has a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nAs Mr. Sowerberry spoke thus, with the proper indignation of a man unfairly treated; and as Mr. Bumble felt that it seemed to cast some reflection upon the parish\u2019s honour; that gentleman judged it prudent to alter the subject. Oliver Twist being foremost in his thoughts, he made him the topic.\\n\\n\u201cBy the way,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cyou don\u2019t happen to know anyone in want of a boy, do you? A parochial apprentice, who is at present a dead weight; a millstone, as I may call him, about the parochial neck? Generous terms, Mr. Sowerberry, generous terms?\u201d While Mr. Bumble spoke, he lifted his cane toward the bill over him, and struck three clear taps upon the words \u201cfive pounds,\u201d printed there in enormous Roman capitals.\\n\\n\u201cGadso!\u201d cried the undertaker, catching Mr. Bumble by the gold-edged lapel of his official coat; \u201cthat is the very matter I wished to mention to you. You know\u2014dear me, what an exceedingly handsome button that is, Mr. Bumble! I had never remarked it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I consider it rather handsome,\u201d said the beadle, looking proudly down at the great brass buttons adorning his coat. \u201cThe die is identical with the parochial seal\u2014the Good Samaritan tending the sick and wounded man. The board gave it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I recollect putting it on for the first time when I attended the inquest upon that impoverished tradesman, who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
