          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a8066a7abab67fed3fb1427e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were occurring in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the very room from which the girl had taken Oliver\u2014pondering beside a dim, smoky fire. A pair of bellows lay across his knee, and he had evidently been trying to stir the fire into a livelier blaze; but he had sunk into profound reflection, and, folding his arms over them with his chin upon his thumbs, stared absently at the rusted grate.\\n\\nAt a table behind him were seated the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist; the Artful playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The face of the first-mentioned gentleman, always remarkably shrewd, gained a fresh degree of interest from the care with which he watched the game and examined Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand; upon which he now and then, whenever opportunity allowed, cast a number of earnest looks, prudently directing his own play by what he discovered of his neighbour\u2019s cards. As the night was cold, the Dodger kept his hat on, as was indeed frequently his habit indoors. He likewise held a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only for a moment whenever he thought fit to seek refreshment from a quart pot on the table, standing ready charged with gin-and-water for the company\u2019s use.\\n\\nMaster Bates, too, attended to the game; yet, being of a livelier temperament than his accomplished companion, he was seen to resort more often to the gin-and-water, and to indulge besides in a great many jokes and stray observations, all most unsuitable to a scientific rubber. The Artful, relying on their intimate friendship, more than once solemnly argued with his companion concerning these offences; and Master Bates accepted every such remonstrance with excellent humour, merely asking his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head into a sack, or answering with some other smartly fashioned jest of the same description, whose aptness awakened considerable admiration in Mr. Chitling\u2019s mind. It was noteworthy that this gentleman and his partner lost without exception; and that, instead of provoking Master Bates, the fact seemed to give him the keenest delight, for at the close of each deal he laughed with the greatest uproar and declared he had never witnessed such a merry game in all his born days.\\n\\n\u201cThat makes two doubles and the rub,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, wearing a very lengthy face as he took half-a-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never saw such a chap as you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
