          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"828b177c935fb5b6bcd24ce2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my immense joy, at about nine o\u2019clock, with the moon\u2019s surface terrifyingly close and my fears at their height, the pump of my condenser finally showed clear signs of a change in the atmosphere. By ten, I had reason to think its density had increased considerably. By eleven, the apparatus required very little effort; and at twelve, though somewhat hesitant, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding that this caused no ill effects, I at last opened the gum-elastic chamber and removed it from around the car. As might have been expected, spasms and a violent headache immediately followed an experiment so rash and dangerous. But since these and the other difficulties of breathing were not severe enough to endanger my life, I resolved to bear them as best I could, knowing that I would leave them behind moment by moment as I approached the denser layers near the moon. This approach, however, remained exceedingly swift; and it soon became alarmingly clear that, although I had probably been right in expecting an atmosphere whose density corresponded to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had been mistaken in believing that this density, even at the surface, would be sufficient to support the great weight in my balloon\u2019s car. Yet it ought to have done so, and to the same degree as at the earth\u2019s surface, the actual gravity of bodies on either planet being assumed to be in proportion to the condensation of the atmosphere. My rapid descent was proof enough that this was not so; why it was not so can only be explained by reference to those possible geological disturbances I mentioned before. In any event, I was now very near the planet and descending with dreadful force. I lost no time in throwing overboard first my ballast, then my water-kegs, then my condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every object in the car. But it was useless. I continued to fall with horrible speed, and was now less than half a mile from the surface. As a final measure, after discarding my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car itself loose from the balloon, for it was far from light. Then, clinging to the netting with both hands, I had barely time to notice that the entire country, as far as I could see, was thickly dotted with tiny dwellings before I plunged headfirst into the center of a fantastical-looking city, and into the midst of a vast crowd of ugly little people. Not one spoke a word or made the slightest effort to help me; they merely stood there like a pack of fools, grinning absurdly and staring sideways at me and my balloon, their arms planted on their hips.\u201d\"}"
