          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6aa71f55eece7ea7b8856d8b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI now come quickly to the more affecting part of my story. I will recount events that filled me with feelings which, from what I once was, have made me what I am.\\n\\n\u201cSpring came on rapidly; the weather grew fine and the skies clear. I was astonished that what had once been barren and gloomy should now blossom with the loveliest flowers and greenery. My senses were pleased and renewed by a thousand delightful scents and a thousand beautiful sights.\\n\\n\u201cIt was on one of these days, when my cottagers took their customary rest from work\u2014the old man played his guitar, while the children listened\u2014that I noticed Felix\u2019s face was sad beyond description. He sighed often, and once his father stopped playing; from his manner, I guessed that he asked the cause of his son\u2019s grief. Felix answered in a cheerful voice, and the old man was beginning his music again when someone knocked at the door.\\n\\n\u201cIt was a lady on horseback, accompanied by a countryman as her guide. She wore a dark outfit and was covered by a heavy black veil. Agatha asked a question, to which the stranger replied only by speaking Felix\u2019s name in a sweet accent. Her voice was musical, yet unlike that of either of my friends. At the sound of that name, Felix hurried to the lady, who, upon seeing him, lifted her veil, and I saw a face of angelic beauty and expression. Her hair was shining raven-black and intricately braided; her eyes were dark but gentle, though lively; her features were perfectly proportioned, and her complexion wonderfully fair, with each cheek touched by a lovely pink.\\n\\n\u201cFelix seemed overcome with delight at the sight of her. Every sign of sorrow disappeared from his face, which at once showed such ecstatic joy that I could scarcely have believed it capable of it. His eyes shone and his cheeks flushed with pleasure; at that moment, I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. She seemed moved by different emotions; after wiping a few tears from her lovely eyes, she held out her hand to Felix. He kissed it passionately and called her, as far as I could make out, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him, but she smiled. He helped her dismount and, after dismissing her guide, led her into the cottage. Some conversation passed between him and his father, and the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet and would have kissed his hand, but he lifted her up and embraced her warmly.\\n\\n\u201cI soon realized that although the stranger made distinct sounds and seemed to possess a language of her own, neither she nor the cottagers could understand one another.\u201d\"}"
