          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68c294d1ff2da15e341fcb59\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Anyone looking at him might have thought Nemesis had taken Pallas\u2019s shield and shown him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He seemed frozen into stone, and his face had the sadness of marble. He had lived the refined, luxurious life of a young man with rank and wealth, a life wonderfully free from common worries, with the graceful carelessness of youth; and now, for the first time, he understood the dreadful mystery of Fate, the terrible meaning of Doom.\\n\\nHow insane and horrible it all seemed! Could it be that his hand bore, in signs he could not read but another person could, some dreadful secret of sin, some blood-red mark of crime? Was there no way to escape? Were people no more than chess pieces, moved by an unseen hand, like vessels shaped by a potter as he pleased, for honour or disgrace? His mind rebelled against the thought, yet he felt that some tragedy hung over him, and that he had suddenly been made to carry an unbearable burden. Actors are fortunate. They can choose whether to appear in tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or be cheerful, to laugh or weep. Real life is different. Most men and women must play parts for which they are not suited. Our Guildensterns play Hamlet, while our Hamlets must joke like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, but the roles are badly assigned.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers came into the room. At the sight of Lord Arthur, he recoiled, and his coarse, fat face turned a greenish yellow. The two men looked into each other\u2019s eyes, and for a moment neither spoke.\\n\\n\u2018The Duchess has left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and asked me to bring it to her,\u2019 Mr. Podgers said at last. \u2018Ah, I see it on the sofa! Good evening.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Mr. Podgers, I must insist that you give me a direct answer to a question I am about to ask.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Another time, Lord Arthur. The Duchess is waiting, and I am afraid I must go.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You will not go. The Duchess is in no great hurry.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ladies should not be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u2019 said Mr. Podgers with his unhealthy smile. \u2018The fair sex can be impatient.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s finely shaped lips curled with impatient contempt. At that moment, the poor Duchess seemed of very little importance to him. He crossed the room to where Mr. Podgers stood and held out his hand.\\n\\n\u2018Tell me what you saw there,\u2019 he said. \u2018Tell me the truth. I must know. I am not a child.\u2019\"}"
