          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bc6792057221a6dfa49c4247\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Week after week passed, and I became ever more deeply absorbed in you. Then a fresh thing happened. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armour, and as Adonis in a hunter\u2019s cloak, carrying a bright boar-spear. With great lotus-flowers crowning you, you had sat upon the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking out over the green, muddy Nile. You had bent above the quiet pool in some Greek forest and beheld, in the water\u2019s mute silver, the wonder of your own face. And all this had been what art ought to be\u2014unconscious, ideal, and distant. One day\u2014a disastrous day, I sometimes believe\u2014I resolved to paint a splendid portrait of you as you truly were, not in the dress of vanished centuries, but in your own clothes and your own age. Whether it was the realism of the treatment, or simply the marvel of your personality, so directly before me without cloud or covering, I cannot say. But I know that, while I worked, every touch and layer of colour appeared to disclose my secret. I became frightened that others might discover my worship of you. I felt, Dorian, that I had revealed too much, that I had placed too much of myself within it. It was then that I decided never to let the picture be exhibited. You were somewhat vexed; but you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, when I spoke to him of it, laughed at me. Yet I did not care. When the portrait was complete, and I sat alone before it, I knew that I had been right.... Well, a few days later it left my studio, and once I was free of the unbearable fascination of its presence, I thought I had been foolish to imagine I saw anything there beyond the fact that you were remarkably handsome and that I knew how to paint. Even now I cannot help thinking it mistaken to suppose that the passion felt in creation is ever truly visible in the work created. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and colour speak only of form and colour\u2014that is all. It often seems to me that art hides the artist much more completely than it reveals him. So, when this offer came from Paris, I resolved that your portrait should be the chief work in my exhibition. It never entered my mind that you would object. I see now that you were right. The picture must not be displayed. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have said.\"}"
