{"request_id":"d04fa8401edc210d3afbcfa3","rewritten_text":"Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, met Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay; and these four knights encountered fiercely, breaking their spears in their hands. Then Sir Pertolope came forth from the castle and encountered Sir Lionel, and there Sir Pertolope, the green knight, struck down Sir Lionel, brother to Sir Launcelot. All this was observed by noble heralds, who recorded who bore himself best, and their names. Then Sir Bleobaris broke his spear upon Sir Gareth, yet from that stroke Sir Bleobaris fell to the ground. When Sir Galihodin beheld this, he bade Sir Gareth guard himself, and Sir Gareth smote him down to earth. Then Sir Galihud took a spear to avenge his brother, and Sir Gareth dealt with him in the same manner; likewise Sir Dinadan and his brother La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore le Disirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage: all these he overthrew with one spear. When King Aswisance of Ireland saw how Sir Gareth fared, he marvelled who he could be, for at one time he seemed green, and at another, when he returned, he appeared blue. Thus at every course he rode back and forth he changed his colour, so that neither king nor knight could readily know him.

Then Sir Agwisance, King of Ireland, encountered Sir Gareth, and Sir Gareth smote him from his horse, saddle and all. Then came King Carados of Scotland, and Sir Gareth struck down both horse and man. In the same fashion he served King Uriens of the land of Gore. Then Sir Bagdemagus entered, and Sir Gareth smote horse and man down to the earth. And Meliganus, Bagdemagus's son, broke a spear upon Sir Gareth with great might and knightly skill. Then Sir Galahault, the noble prince, cried aloud, “Knight of the many colours, thou hast jousted well; now make thee ready, that I may joust with thee.” Sir Gareth heard him and took a great spear, and they encountered together. There the prince broke his spear, but Sir Gareth struck him upon the left side of the helm, so that he reeled this way and that, and would have fallen had not his men caught him.

“Truly,” said King Arthur, “that knight of the many colours is a good knight.” Therefore the king called Sir Launcelot to him and prayed him to encounter that knight. “Sir,” said Launcelot, “I can find it in my heart to spare him at this time, for he has had labour enough today. When a good knight has done so well upon a day, it is not a good knight's part to hinder his worship, especially when he sees that a knight has performed such great labour. For perhaps,” said Sir Launcelot, “his quarrel is here today, and perhaps he is best beloved by this lady above all who are here; for I see plainly that he pains and strains himself to do great deeds. Therefore,” said Sir Launcelot, “as for me, he shall have the honour this day; though it were in my power to take it from him, I would not.”"}

