          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66285e4781c07438d6ab4cf5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When this had been accomplished, several more loops were loosened from the rim, an additional section of the cloth was passed in, and the released loops were again secured to their respective buttons. Thus the entire upper portion of the bag could be introduced between the netting and the hoop. It will be seen that the hoop would then sink down inside the car, while the entire weight of the car, together with all it contained, would be sustained solely by the buttons' strength. At first glance this might appear a feeble reliance; yet it was nothing of the sort, since the buttons were not only exceedingly strong, but placed so near one another that each bore only a trifling share of the total burden. Indeed, had the car and its contents been thrice as heavy, I should have felt no uneasiness whatever. I then lifted the hoop once more within the gum-elastic covering, and supported it at nearly its previous elevation with three light poles made ready for that purpose. This was naturally intended to keep the bag expanded at its summit, and to retain the lower portion of the netting in its proper place. Nothing remained except to close the mouth of the enclosure; and this was easily effected by drawing the folds of material together and twisting them very tightly on the inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.\\n\\n\u201cIn the sides of the covering, as arranged about the car, three round panes of thick, transparent glass had been inserted, through which I could readily see about me in every horizontal direction. In the part of the cloth that formed the bottom there was also a fourth window of the same description, corresponding to a small opening in the floor of the car itself. By this means I could look directly downward; but, having found it impossible to arrange any similar device overhead, owing to the peculiar fashion in which the opening there was closed, and the resulting wrinkles in the cloth, I could hope to see no objects lying immediately at my zenith. This was, of course, of slight importance; for even if I had been able to put a window at the top, the balloon itself would have prevented me from deriving any benefit from it.\\n\\n\u201cAbout a foot beneath one of the side windows was a circular aperture, eight inches across, furnished with a brass rim whose inner edge was fitted to receive the turns of a screw. Into this rim the large tube of the condenser was screwed, the main body of the machine being, naturally, inside the chamber of gum-elastic.\"}"
