{"request_id":"89af7d009a065557a468d36f","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to concern him was whom he should kill; for he was not unaware that murder, like the religions of the pagan world, needs a victim as well as a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies, and he felt, besides, that this was no time to indulge any personal resentment or dislike, since the mission he had undertaken was one of immense and solemn gravity. He therefore drew up a list of his friends and relations on a sheet of writing paper and, after careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady living in Curzon Street, who was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and since he was himself very rich, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property upon coming of age, there was no chance that he could gain any vulgar financial benefit from her death. Indeed, the more he considered it, the more she appeared to be exactly the right person; and, believing that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his arrangements immediately.

The first matter, naturally, was to pay the cheiromantist. He sat at a small Sheraton writing desk near the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, enclosed it in an envelope, and instructed his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never allow her to know what he was doing for her, but would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice forever hidden in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with exquisite white petals and staring pheasant’s eyes. When he reached the club, he went directly to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on toxicology. He had firmly decided that poison was the best method for this troublesome affair. Any form of personal violence was deeply unpleasant to him; moreover, he was anxious not to kill Lady Clementina in a manner likely to draw public notice, for he hated the thought of being lionised at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name appear in the columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

