          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d43b48b37eb7d966da3c071f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"That was encouragement enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must have heard that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been let to a young gentleman of considerable fortune from the north of England; that he arrived on Monday in a chaise and four to inspect the place, and was so pleased with it that he came to terms with Mr. Morris at once; that he is to enter into possession before Michaelmas, and that several of his servants will be in the house by the close of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married, or unmarried?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, unmarried, my dear, certainly! A single man with a large fortune; four or five thousand a year. What an excellent thing for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn what way? How can it concern them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d said his wife, \u201chow can you be so provoking? You must understand that I am thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that why he has settled here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy? Nonsense, what can you mean! Yet it is quite possible that he _may_ fall in love with one of them, and so you must call upon him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can see no need for it. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be better still; for since you are as pretty as any of them, Mr. Bingley may prefer you to the whole party.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you are flattering me. I certainly _have_ possessed my portion of beauty, but I make no claim to anything remarkable now. Once a woman has five grown daughters, she should cease to think about her own looks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn those circumstances, a woman seldom has much beauty left to consider.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you truly must go to see Mr. Bingley when he comes into the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is more than I promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Only imagine what an establishment it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas mean to go, solely for that reason; for, generally speaking, you know, they never visit newcomers. Indeed, you must go, because it will be impossible for _us_ to visit him unless you do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are surely too scrupulous. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be delighted to receive you; and I shall send a few lines with you, assuring him of my sincere consent to his marrying whichever of the girls he pleases\u2014though I must put in a favourable word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI beg you will do nothing of the kind. Lizzy is not at all superior to the others; and I am certain she is not half as handsome as Jane, nor half as good-tempered as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
