{"request_id":"0db42667d868cdda8b2f1ea2","rewritten_text":"On the following day he did not go out, and in fact passed the greater part of it in his own room, ill with a frantic dread of death, yet careless of life itself. The sense that he was being pursued, trapped, and run to earth had begun to rule him. If the tapestry so much as stirred in the wind, he started. The dead leaves blown against the leaded windows appeared to him like his own squandered purposes and desperate regrets. When he shut his eyes, he saw once more the sailor’s face looking through the mist-dimmed glass, and horror seemed again to clutch his heart.

Yet perhaps it was only his imagination that had summoned vengeance from the darkness and placed those dreadful forms of retribution before him. Real life was disorder, but imagination possessed a terrible logic. It was imagination that made remorse follow in the footsteps of sin. It was imagination that caused every crime to bring forth its distorted offspring. In the ordinary world of fact, the wicked went unpunished and the good unrewarded. Success belonged to the strong; failure was imposed upon the weak. That was all. Moreover, if a stranger had been lurking about the house, the servants or the keepers must have noticed him. If footprints had appeared in the flower-beds, the gardeners would have told him. Yes, it had been nothing but fancy. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to murder him. He had gone away in his ship, to sink in some wintry sea. From him, at least, he was secure. Why, the man did not know who he was, and could not possibly know. The disguise of youth had preserved him.

Still, if it had been only an illusion, how dreadful to think that conscience could conjure up such frightful phantoms, lend them visible shape, and set them moving before one! What kind of existence would be his if, by day and night, the shadows of his crime were to watch him from quiet corners, deride him from hidden places, murmur in his ear while he sat at a feast, and rouse him with frozen fingers as he slept! As the thought stole through his mind, he turned pale with fear, and the air seemed suddenly to grow colder. Oh! in what a mad, wild moment he had slain his friend! How horrible was the bare recollection of it! He saw the whole scene again. Every loathsome detail returned with fresh terror. From time’s black cavern, dreadful and wrapped in scarlet, rose the image of his sin."}

