          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc9d949990699d1a5b5f586f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at last, \u201cthere is no denying that, since the new feeding system began, the coffins have become somewhat narrower and shallower than they once were; but we must make some profit, Mr. Bumble. Properly seasoned wood is expensive, sir; and all the iron handles are brought by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery business has its disadvantages. A reasonable profit is, of course, permitted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course,\u201d replied the undertaker; \u201cand if I do not make a profit on one particular item or another, why, I recover it in the end, you see\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cThough I must say,\u201d continued the undertaker, returning to the remarks the beadle had interrupted, \u201cthough I must say, Mr. Bumble, that I have one very serious difficulty to deal with: all the large people die the soonest. Those who were once better off, and paid rates for many years, are the first to fail when they enter the house; and let me tell you, Mr. Bumble, that three or four inches beyond one\u2019s estimate makes a great difference to one\u2019s profits, especially when one has a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nAs Mr. Sowerberry spoke with the proper indignation of a badly treated man, and as Mr. Bumble felt that his words seemed to cast doubt on the parish\u2019s honour, the latter thought it wise to change the subject. Oliver Twist was foremost in his thoughts, so he began to speak of him.\\n\\n\u201cBy the way,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cyou do not know anyone who wants a boy, do you? A parish apprentice, who is at present a burden; a millstone, as I might say, around the parish neck? Generous terms, Mr. Sowerberry, generous terms?\u201d As Mr. Bumble spoke, he lifted his cane to the notice above him and struck the words \u201cfive pounds\u201d three times. They were printed there in enormous Roman capitals.\\n\\n\u201cGadso!\u201d said the undertaker, taking Mr. Bumble by the gold-trimmed lapel of his official coat. \u201cThat is exactly what I wanted to speak to you about. You know\u2014dear me, what an elegant button this is, Mr. Bumble! I had never noticed it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I think it is rather handsome,\u201d said the beadle, looking proudly down at the large brass buttons decorating his coat. \u201cThe design is the same as the parish seal\u2014the Good Samaritan tending the sick and injured man. The board gave it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I remember putting it on for the first time when I attended the inquest on that ruined tradesman who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
