ogether, devised plans for their future employments, and, drinking love and joy from one another's looks and words, scarcely murmured at the delay of their complete union. Woodville continued ever to rise in glory; and Elinor grew lovelier and wiser beneath the instruction of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be one and twenty: all was ready for their union. How can I tell the catastrophe that befell such happiness? Yet the earth would not be the earth, covered as it is with blight and sorrow, had a pair of angelic beings like these been permitted to exist for one another: seek the whole world through, and you will not discover the perfect felicity their marriage would have bestowed; the established order of things among us wretched dwellers on earth must have been overturned to allow such consummate joy.\"}"
