in approving the arrangement. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how could Mr. Woodhouse be persuaded to consent?\u2014he who had never spoken of their marriage except as something far distant.\\n\\nWhen the matter was first mentioned to him, he was so wretched that they nearly despaired.\u2014A second reference, however, caused him less suffering.\u2014He began to believe it must happen, and that he could not stop it\u2014a most encouraging advance of mind toward resignation. Yet he was still unhappy. Indeed, he seemed so decidedly otherwise that his daughter's resolution gave way. She could not endure to watch him suffer, or to know that he imagined himself neglected; and though her reason nearly yielded to the assurances of both Mr. Knightleys that, once the event had passed, his distress would soon pass as well, she paused\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
