{"request_id":"0a380de780650d2c54f93d60","rewritten_text":"I cannot describe the ecstatic joy with which I turned from political quarrels at home and the bodily sufferings of foreign lands to my own beloved house—the chosen dwelling of goodness and love, of peace and the sharing of every sacred feeling. Had I never left Windsor, these emotions would not have been so powerful; but in Greece I had been the victim of fear and dreadful upheaval. In Greece, after a time of anxiety and grief, I had watched two depart whose very names stood for greatness and virtue. Yet such sorrows could never enter the family circle that remained to me, while, secluded in our cherished forest, we lived in tranquillity. The passing years did bring some slight changes even there; and time, as always, marked our pleasures and hopes with signs of mortality. Idris, the most loving wife, sister, and friend, was a gentle and devoted mother. Motherhood was not, as it is for many, a diversion to her; it was a passion. We had had three children; one, the second eldest, died while I was in Greece. This had mingled the triumphant ecstasy of motherhood with grief and fear. Before that loss, the little creatures born of her, the young inheritors of her brief life, had seemed assured of life; now she feared that the merciless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings, as it had seized their brother. The slightest illness brought agonies of terror; she was unhappy whenever she was away from them at all. She had stored her whole wealth of happiness in their fragile lives, and remained constantly watchful lest the stealthy thief should again steal these precious jewels. Fortunately, she had little reason to fear. Alfred, now nine, was an honest, manly little boy, with a bright brow, soft eyes, and a gentle yet independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheek was touched with the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed the age which, because of its silent helplessness, had caused Idris such fear. Clara was dear to her—to us all. In her, intelligence was joined to innocence, sensitivity to patience, and seriousness to perfect good humour; such surpassing beauty was united with such winning simplicity that she seemed like a pearl set in the shrine of all we possessed, a treasure of marvel and excellence.

At the beginning of winter, Alfred, now nine years old, first went to school at Eton. He regarded this as his first step toward manhood, and was pleased accordingly. The shared life of study and play brought out the finest qualities of his character: his steady perseverance, generosity, and disciplined firmness."}

