          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ec5c605bbf50703c77ca2caf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you about?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. The sun was already up, and I had been sleeping hard. Pap stood over me, looking sour and sick besides. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t know anything about what he had been doing, so I said:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me up?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t. I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day. Get out and see whether there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll come along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I hurried out up the riverbank. I noticed bits of branches and things floating downstream, with a scattering of bark, so I knew the river had started to rise. I reckoned I\u2019d have fine times now if I were over in town. The June rise had always been lucky for me, because as soon as it began, cordwood came floating down, along with pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs tied together. All you had to do was catch them and sell them to the woodyards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI went up the bank, keeping one eye out for Pap and the other for whatever the rising water might bring. Then, all at once, here came a canoe\u2014a real beauty, too, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I dove headfirst off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam for the canoe. I expected somebody might be lying down in it, because people often did that to fool others; when a fellow had pulled a skiff almost up to it, they would sit up and laugh at him. But not this time. It was truly a drift canoe, and I climbed in and paddled it ashore. I thought the old man would be glad when he saw it\u2014it was worth ten dollars. But when I reached shore, Pap was nowhere in sight, and as I ran it into a little creek, like a gully, overhung with vines and willows, I had another idea. I decided to hide it well, and then, instead of taking to the woods when I ran away, I would go fifty miles downriver and camp in one place for good, without having such a hard time tramping on foot.\\n\\nIt was pretty close to the shanty, and I thought I heard the old man coming the whole time; but I got it hidden. Then I stepped out and looked around a clump of willows, and there was the old man a little way down the path, just taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
