{"request_id":"7aba9034140b2ff0eb78b729","rewritten_text":"When he reached the head of the stairs, he regained his composure and resolved to utter his famous burst of fiendish laughter. He had found this exceedingly effective on more than one occasion. It was reported to have made Lord Raker’s wig turn grey overnight, and it had certainly caused three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to give notice before the month had ended. Accordingly, he gave his most dreadful laugh, until the ancient vaulted ceiling echoed again and again; but scarcely had the terrible reverberation faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing-gown. ‘I fear you are not at all well,’ she said, ‘and I have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it is indigestion, you will find it an excellent remedy.’ The ghost fixed her with an angry glare, and immediately began preparing to transform himself into a great black dog, a feat for which he was deservedly famous, and which the family physician had always blamed for the lasting idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. Yet the noise of footsteps drawing near made him pause in his dreadful design; so he merely became dimly phosphorescent and disappeared with a profound graveyard groan, at the very moment the twins came up to him.

Once in his room, he collapsed completely and was seized by the most violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s coarse materialism were, naturally, intensely irritating; but what troubled him most was that he had failed to wear the armour. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be stirred by the appearance of a Spectre In Armour, if not for any sounder reason, then at least from respect for their national poet, Longfellow, whose elegant and pleasing verses he himself had used to pass many a tedious hour while the Cantervilles were in town. Moreover, it was his own armour. He had worn it with immense success at the tournament at Kenilworth, and had received high praise for it from no less a person than the Virgin Queen herself. But when he put it on, the weight of the enormous breastplate and steel helmet had utterly overcome him; he fell heavily upon the stone floor, badly grazing both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was very ill, and scarcely left his room at all, save to maintain the blood-stain properly. Still, by taking great care of himself, he recovered and determined to make a third effort to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

