          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a5bda58562bc48166ebd9965\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke in the morning, he wondered where he was. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and looked around. Then he understood. It was the cool gray dawn, and the deep calm and silence of the woods gave him a wonderful feeling of rest and peace. Not a leaf moved; no sound disturbed Nature\u2019s quiet thoughts. Dewdrops shone on the leaves and grass. A white layer of ashes covered the fire, and a thin blue thread of smoke rose straight into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep.\\n\\nFar off in the woods, a bird called, and another answered. Soon a woodpecker could be heard tapping. Little by little, the cool, dim gray morning grew white, and more sounds appeared as life began to stir. The boy watched thoughtfully as Nature woke from sleep and went to work. A little green worm crawled over a wet leaf, raising two-thirds of its body into the air now and then and \u201csniffing around\u201d before moving on again. It was measuring, Tom said. When the worm came toward him on its own, he sat as still as a stone. His hopes rose and fell as the creature kept coming closer or seemed ready to go another way. At last it paused for a painful moment, its curved body raised in the air, then came down firmly on Tom\u2019s leg and began to travel over him. Tom\u2019s heart filled with joy, for this meant he was going to have a new suit of clothes\u2014without a doubt, a bright pirate uniform. Then a line of ants appeared from nowhere in particular and went about their work. One bravely struggled along carrying a dead spider five times bigger than itself, hauling it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug climbed the dizzying height of a blade of grass. Tom bent close and said, \u201cLadybug, ladybug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone,\u201d and she spread her wings and flew off to check\u2014which did not surprise him. He had long known that ladybugs believed such stories about fires, and he had fooled them more than once. Next came a tumblebug, pushing hard at its ball. Tom touched it to watch it pull in its legs and pretend to be dead. By now the birds were making a great noise. A catbird, the northern mocker, landed in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and happily sang imitations of its neighbors. Then a sharp-voiced jay swooped down like a flash of blue fire and landed on a twig almost within the boy\u2019s reach. It tilted its head and stared at the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large fox squirrel came hurrying along, stopping now and then to sit up, inspect the boys, and chatter at them. The wild creatures had probably never seen human beings before and hardly knew whether they should be afraid.\"}"
