          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a72f8824efbe0101d95a4095\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! There was animal nature in the soul, and the body had moments of spirituality. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could sink into degradation. Who could tell where physical impulse ended and psychological impulse began? How superficial the arbitrary definitions of ordinary psychologists were! Yet how hard it was to choose among the claims of the different schools! Was the soul a shadow living in the house of sin? Or was the body actually contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The division of spirit from matter was a mystery, and so was their union.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether psychology could ever become such an exact science that every small source of life would be revealed to us. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and seldom understood anyone else. Experience had no moral value. It was merely the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists had generally treated it as a warning, claiming that it had some ethical power in shaping character, praising it as something that taught us what to pursue and showed us what to avoid. But experience contained no driving force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly proved was that our future would resemble our past, and that the sin we had committed once with disgust we would commit many times with pleasure.\\n\\nIt seemed clear to him that the experimental method was the only way to reach any scientific analysis of the passions; and Dorian Gray was certainly a subject perfectly suited to his purposes, one that promised abundant and valuable results. His sudden, reckless love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon of considerable interest. Curiosity had undoubtedly played a large part in it, along with the desire for new experiences, but it was not a simple passion; it was a highly complex one. Whatever in it came from the purely sensual instinct of boyhood had been transformed by imagination, altered into something that seemed to the young man himself far removed from the senses, and was therefore all the more dangerous. The passions whose origins we deceived ourselves about ruled us most completely. Our weakest motives were those whose nature we understood. Often, when we believed we were experimenting on others, we were really experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat reflecting on these matters, there was a knock at the door, and his valet came in to remind him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
