          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ef694b5b600eeb99d0e3125\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water with which to end the feast. After a while Becky proposed that they go on again. Tom was quiet for a moment. Then he said:\\n\\n\u201cBecky, can you stand it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face turned pale, but she believed she could.\\n\\n\u201cWell then, Becky, we have to remain here, where there is water to drink. That little bit is our last candle!\u201d\\n\\nBecky broke into tears and cries. Tom did his best to soothe her, but it helped little. At last Becky said:\\n\\n\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and come looking for us!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will! Of course they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps they\u2019re looking for us now, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, I suppose perhaps they are. I hope so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen will they miss us, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen they return to the boat, I suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it may be dark by then\u2014will they see that we haven\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. But your mother will miss you as soon as they get home, anyway.\u201d\\n\\nThe frightened expression on Becky\u2019s face brought Tom to himself, and he realized he had blundered. Becky was not supposed to go home that night! The children fell silent and thoughtful. Presently a fresh outburst of grief from Becky showed Tom that the thought in his own mind had reached hers too\u2014that Sunday morning might be half gone before Mrs. Thatcher found out Becky was not at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children fixed their eyes on their scrap of candle and watched it slowly, mercilessly melt away; watched the half-inch wick finally stand by itself; watched the weak flame flutter and sink, rise along the slender thread of smoke, pause at its summit for an instant, and then\u2014the terror of complete darkness took possession!\\n\\nHow long afterward Becky gradually became aware that she was weeping in Tom\u2019s arms, neither of them could say. All they knew was that, after what seemed an immense length of time, they both roused from a deathlike stupor of sleep and took up their misery again. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014perhaps Monday. He tried to make Becky talk, but her grief was too heavy; every hope had vanished. Tom said they must have been missed long since, and surely the search was under way. He would shout, and perhaps somebody would hear. He tried; but the far-off echoes sounded so dreadful in the darkness that he did not try again.\\n\\nThe hours slipped away, and hunger returned to plague the prisoners. Part of Tom\u2019s half of the cake remained; they shared it and ate it. Yet they seemed hungrier than ever.\"}"
