{"request_id":"e4a05b98e7131793c150238c","rewritten_text":"We spent several miserable hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. My father and the rest of the family were required to attend as witnesses, and I went with them to the court. Throughout this dreadful mockery of justice, I endured living torment. It was to be decided whether the consequences of my curiosity and unlawful experiments would bring about the deaths of two fellow creatures: one, a smiling child full of innocence and happiness; the other, murdered even more horribly, under every disgraceful circumstance that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine, too, was a worthy girl, with qualities that promised her a happy life; now everything was to be buried in a shameful grave, and I was the cause! A thousand times I would rather have confessed myself guilty of the crime charged against Justine, but I had been absent when it was committed, and such a confession would have been dismissed as the madness of a lunatic and would not have cleared her, who suffered because of me.

Justine appeared calm. She wore mourning, and her face, always appealing, had been made exquisitely beautiful by the gravity of her feelings. Yet she seemed assured of her innocence and did not tremble, though thousands stared at her and cursed her; for whatever kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired had been erased from the spectators’ minds by their image of the terrible crime she was believed to have committed. She was composed, though her composure was plainly forced; and because her earlier confusion had been offered as evidence of guilt, she summoned her strength to appear courageous. When she entered the courtroom, she looked around and quickly found where we sat. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she saw us, but she recovered herself at once, and an expression of sorrowful affection seemed to declare her complete innocence.

The trial began, and after the prosecutor had presented the charge, several witnesses were called. A number of strange facts weighed against her, enough to shake anyone who did not possess the proof of her innocence that I did. She had been out all night on the night of the murder, and near morning a market woman had seen her not far from the place where the murdered child’s body was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strange and gave only a confused, unintelligible reply. She returned to the house at about eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently demanded whether anything had been heard of him."}

