          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d59992aa6e1e4d7ac8055af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, few people besides sailors can tie this knot, and it is especially associated with the Maltese. I found the ribbon at the base of the lightning rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead women. Now, even if I am mistaken in concluding from this ribbon that the Frenchman was a sailor on a Maltese vessel, no harm can come from what I said in the advertisement. If I am wrong, he will simply think I was led astray by some detail he will not bother to investigate. But if I am right, we gain an important advantage. Though innocent of the murder, the Frenchman knows about it, and will naturally hesitate before answering the advertisement or claiming the Ourang-Outang. He will think like this:\u2014\u2018I am innocent; I am poor; my Ourang-Outang is very valuable\u2014indeed, to a man in my position, it is a fortune. Why should I lose it because of foolish fears? It is here, almost in my hands. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne, far from the scene of that slaughter. How could anyone suspect that a wild animal committed the crime? The police are confused; they have not found the slightest clue. Even if they trace the animal, they could never prove that I knew of the murder, or make me guilty because I knew of it. Above all, I am known. The advertiser identifies me as the animal\u2019s owner. I do not know how far his knowledge goes. If I fail to claim property so valuable, property known to be mine, I will make the animal itself seem suspicious. It is not wise to draw attention to either myself or the beast. I will answer the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it hidden until this affair has passed.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment, we heard footsteps on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe prepared,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols. But do not use or show them until I give the signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe front door had been left open, and the visitor had entered without ringing. He had climbed several steps of the staircase. Now, however, he seemed to hesitate. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin was moving quickly toward the door when we heard him coming up again. This time he did not turn back, but climbed the stairs firmly and knocked on the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, in a warm, cheerful voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was clearly a sailor: tall, broad, and muscular, with a somewhat reckless look on his face, though not an unpleasant one.\"}"
