          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35f9b2ccb6fb4edea3314edb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes\u2019s crime, began to wonder whether an honest life might not, after all, be the best one. Deciding that it certainly was, he turned his back on the scenes of his past, determined to reform himself in some new line of work. For a time he struggled hard and suffered greatly; but, being naturally cheerful and guided by a good purpose, he succeeded at last. After serving as a farmer\u2019s drudge and a carrier\u2019s boy, he is now the merriest young grazier in all Northamptonshire.\\n\\nAnd now the hand that writes these words falters as it nears the end of its task, and would spin out the thread of these adventures a little longer.\\n\\nI would gladly remain a while with some of those among whom I have moved for so long, and share their happiness by trying to portray it. I would show Rose Maylie in the full bloom and grace of young womanhood, casting upon her quiet path through life a soft, gentle light that touched all who walked beside her and shone into their hearts. I would paint her as the life and joy of the fireside circle and the cheerful summer gathering; I would follow her through the hot fields at noon, and hear the soft notes of her sweet voice on a moonlit evening walk. I would watch her in all her goodness and charity beyond her home, and in the smiling, tireless performance of her household duties within it. I would show her and her dead sister\u2019s child happy in their love for each other, spending whole hours imagining the friends they had so sorrowfully lost. I would call before me once more those happy little faces gathered around her knee, and listen to their merry chatter; I would recall the sound of that clear laugh, and summon the sympathetic tear that shone in her soft blue eye. These, and a thousand looks and smiles, turns of thought and ways of speaking\u2014I would gladly recall every one.\\n\\nHow Mr. Brownlow continued, day by day, to fill his adopted child\u2019s mind with knowledge, and grew more attached to him as his character unfolded and revealed the flourishing seeds of all he hoped he would become\u2014how he discovered in him fresh traits of his early friend, awakening old memories in his own heart, sad yet sweet and comforting\u2014how the two orphans, tested by adversity, remembered its lessons of mercy toward others, mutual love, and heartfelt thanks to Him who had guarded and preserved them\u2014these are matters that need not be told.\"}"
