          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b48fa9c84781153e73f53b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew glanced at him, fruitlessly, as he went on toward the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI anticipate the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Conduct Monsieur my nephew to his room there!--And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you please,\u201d he added to himself, before ringing his small bell once more and calling his valet to his own chamber.\\n\\nWhen the valet had come and departed, Monsieur the Marquis paced up and down in his loose dressing-gown, gently preparing himself for sleep on that hot, motionless night. Stirring softly through the room, his slippered feet soundless upon the floor, he moved like a polished tiger:--he resembled some bewitched marquis of the persistently wicked kind in a tale, whose regular transformation into a tiger was either just ending or just beginning.\\n\\nHe went from one end of his luxurious bedroom to the other, again reviewing the fragments of the day\u2019s journey that rose uninvited in his thoughts: the laborious climb up the hill at sunset, the sinking sun, the downward road, the mill, the prison on the rock, the small village in the hollow, the peasants by the fountain, and the road-mender in his blue cap indicating the chain beneath the carriage. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain, the little bundle upon the step, the women stooping over it, and the tall man, arms raised, shouting, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am calm now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis, \u201cand can go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nAccordingly, leaving a single light alight on the broad hearth, he dropped his thin gauze curtains about him, and heard the night interrupt its silence with a long sigh while he settled himself to sleep.\\n\\nThe stone faces on the outside walls gazed sightlessly into the black night for three oppressive hours; for three oppressive hours, the horses in the stables clattered at their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl uttered a sound bearing scant resemblance to that conventionally attributed to the owl by poetical men. Yet it is the stubborn habit of such creatures scarcely ever to speak as they are recorded to speak.\\n\\nFor three oppressive hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human alike, stared blindly into the night. Dead darkness covered the whole countryside; dead darkness added its own stillness to the settling dust upon every road. The burial-ground had reached the point where its small mounds of poor grass could not be told apart; the figure on the Cross might have descended, for all that was visible of it. In the village, tax-gatherers and taxed slept deeply. Perhaps dreaming of feasts, as the starving commonly do, and of comfort and repose, as the driven slave and harnessed ox may, its gaunt inhabitants slept soundly, and were nourished and set free.\"}"
