          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f90c74f9ec37f4cf8e23a79b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dear me, she was a tireless talker. It would not have been proper for me to hint that it was quite late, and that I was tired and very sleepy. I wished I had gone to bed when I had the opportunity. Now I had to endure it; there was no other choice. So she chattered on and on, in the otherwise deep, ghostly silence of the sleeping castle, until at last there came, as though from far beneath us, a distant sound like a muffled scream\u2014with such agony in it that my flesh crawled. The queen stopped, her eyes brightening with pleasure; she cocked her graceful head as a bird does when listening. The sound forced its way up through the silence again.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is that?\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a truly stubborn soul, and it holds out long. It has been many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHolds out against what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come\u2014you shall see a merry sight. If he does not yield his secret now, you shall see him torn apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a smooth, silky fiend she was; so calm and serene, while every nerve in my legs ached in sympathy with that man\u2019s pain. Escorted by armored guards carrying blazing torches, we marched through echoing corridors and down damp, dripping stone stairways, reeking of mold and ages of imprisoned night\u2014a cold, uncanny journey, and a long one, made neither shorter nor more pleasant by the sorceress\u2019s talk about this sufferer and his crime. He had been accused by an anonymous informer of killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony is hardly the proper thing, Your Highness. It would be fairer to confront the accused with his accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered that, since it is of little consequence. But even if I wished it, I could not, for the accuser came masked at night, told the forester, and immediately departed again; thus the forester does not know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen this Unknown is the only person who saw the stag killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, no man saw the killing, but this Unknown saw this hardened wretch near the place where the stag lay, and with most loyal zeal reported him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag too? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal\u2014in a mask\u2014seems just a little suspicious. But what does Your Highness hope to gain by racking the prisoner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise, and then his soul would be lost. By law, his life is forfeit for his crime\u2014and certainly I shall see that he pays it!\u2014but it would endanger my own soul to let him die unconfessed and without absolution.\u201d\"}"
