          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b9b48ddb00be6ad72fffb943\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had come when I put the Manuscript aside. The rain had nearly stopped; the world was gray and sorrowful, and the worn-out storm seemed to sigh and sob itself to sleep. I went to the stranger\u2019s room and listened at his door, which stood slightly open. I could hear his voice, so I knocked. There was no reply, though the voice continued. I looked in. The man lay on his back in bed, speaking in broken phrases but with energy, throwing his arms about restlessly, as sick people do when delirious. I slipped quietly inside and bent over him. His muttering and sudden cries went on. I spoke\u2014only a word, to draw his notice. At once, pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome lit his glassy eyes and ashen face:\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you have come at last\u2014how I have longed for you! Sit beside me\u2014do not leave me\u2014never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand? Give it to me, dear; let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now everything is well, everything is peaceful, and I am happy again\u2014we are happy again, are we not, Sandy? You are so dim, so uncertain, only a mist, a cloud; but you are here, and that is blessing enough. And I have your hand; do not take it away\u2014it will only be for a little while, I shall not need it long.... Was that the child?... Hello, Central!... she does not answer. Perhaps she is asleep? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and tell her good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you are there. I lost myself for a moment, and thought you had gone.... Have I been ill long? It must be so; it feels like months. And such dreams! Such strange, terrible dreams, Sandy! Dreams as real as reality\u2014delirium, of course, but so real! Why, I thought the king was dead; I thought you were in Gaul and could not get home; I thought there had been a revolution. In the wild madness of those dreams, I thought Clarence and I, with a handful of cadets, fought and destroyed all the chivalry of England! But even that was not the strangest thing. I seemed to be a creature from a distant age not yet born, centuries ahead, and even that felt as real as the rest! Yes, I seemed to have flown back from that age into ours, then forward to it again, and been left there, a stranger and alone in that strange England, with an abyss of thirteen centuries opening between me and you! Between me and my home and friends! Between me and everything dear to me, everything that could make life worth living!\"}"
