          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9d2bff773cbfa0fa56979380\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, once more resting his hand gently on the Doctor\u2019s arm, after a brief silence between them, \u201cto what do you attribute this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think,\u201d replied Doctor Manette, \u201cthere was a powerful and unusual revival of the thoughts and memories which first caused the illness. Some painful associations, of the most distressing kind, were brought back with great vividness, I believe. It is likely that a fear had long lain hidden in his mind, that these associations might return\u2014under certain conditions, perhaps\u2014on some particular occasion. He strove in vain to prepare himself; possibly that very effort made him less capable of enduring it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he recollect what happened during the relapse?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked, with understandable reluctance.\\n\\nThe Doctor cast a desolate look about the room, shook his head, and replied in a subdued voice, \u201cNot in the least.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd now, regarding the future,\u201d suggested Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cRegarding the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining his steadiness, \u201cI should entertain strong hopes. Since Heaven, in its mercy, was pleased to restore him so quickly, I should entertain strong hopes. Having yielded beneath the weight of some complicated thing, long feared, dimly anticipated, and resisted, and having recovered when the cloud had broken and gone by, I should hope the worst had passed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is comforting news. I am grateful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am grateful!\u201d echoed the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two further matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cupon which I wish to be advised. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou could render your friend no better service.\u201d The Doctor held out his hand.\\n\\n\u201cTo the first, then. He is studious by nature, and remarkably active; he devotes himself with much zeal to gaining professional knowledge, carrying out experiments, and many other pursuits. Does he, then, do too much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI do not think so. It may be the nature of his mind always to require occupation in an unusual degree. That may arise partly from its natural character, and partly from suffering. The less it was engaged with wholesome matters, the greater would be its danger of turning toward unhealthy ones. He may have watched himself and reached that conclusion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certain he is not subjected to too severe a strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe I am entirely certain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked at present\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I question whether that would be easy. There has been a violent strain in one direction, and it requires a counterbalance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPardon me, as an obstinate man of business. Suppose, for a moment, that he was overworked; would it reveal itself in some return of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
