          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e8652bba7774285fa3091fa5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief explanation with Mr. Knightley afforded Emma great pleasure. It was among the pleasant remembrances of the ball which she paced about the lawn the following morning to savour.\u2014She was exceedingly happy that they had reached so satisfactory an understanding concerning the Eltons, and that their views of husband and wife were so nearly the same; while his commendation of Harriet, his yielding in her favour, was especially delightful. The impertinence of the Eltons, which had for a few moments seemed likely to spoil all that remained of her evening, had produced some of its greatest pleasures; and she anticipated one further fortunate consequence\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s infatuation.\u2014From Harriet\u2019s way of speaking of the affair before they left the ballroom, she had every hope. It appeared as though her eyes had been opened at once, and she could now perceive that Mr. Elton was not the superior being she had imagined. The fever had passed, and Emma had little apprehension that the pulse would be stirred again by harmful civility. She trusted to the Eltons\u2019 unkind feelings to provide every lesson of marked neglect that might still be needed.\u2014Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not excessively in love, and Mr. Knightley with no wish to quarrel with her\u2014what a happy summer lay before her!\\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not permit himself the pleasure of calling at Hartfield, since he was required to be home by midday. She did not regret it.\\n\\nHaving settled all these concerns, considered them, and set them properly in order, she was just turning towards the house, her spirits renewed for the claims of the two little boys and their grandpapa, when the large iron sweep-gate opened, and two people entered whom she could scarcely have expected less to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet resting on his arm\u2014Harriet herself!\u2014One instant was enough to assure her that something unusual had occurred. Harriet was pale and alarmed, while he was endeavouring to comfort her.\u2014The iron gates and the front door stood less than twenty yards apart;\u2014all three were quickly in the hall, and Harriet, sinking at once into a chair, fainted away.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be restored; inquiries must be met, and astonishments accounted for. Such occurrences are highly interesting, but their suspense cannot endure long. Within a few minutes Emma knew the entire story.\\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour-boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had likewise attended the ball, had gone out walking together and chosen the Richmond road, which, though seemingly public enough to be safe, had brought them into danger.\u2014About half a mile beyond Highbury, it made a sharp turn and, being thickly shaded by elms on either side, became for a considerable distance very secluded; and when the young ladies had proceeded some way along it, they suddenly saw, a little ahead of them, on a wider piece of greensward beside the road, a group of gipsies.\"}"
