          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3cd5c7697d1abc1a58354e8b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was occupied with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note in the post. The sight of the envelope alone threw me into great agitation; for although I had never seen the handwriting in which it was addressed, I guessed whose hand had written it. It had no formal opening\u2014no Dear Mr. Pip, Dear Pip, Dear Sir, or Dear Anything\u2014but read as follows:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after tomorrow by the midday coach. I believe it was arranged that you should meet me? In any case, Miss Havisham has that understanding, and I write in accordance with it. She sends you her regards.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nHad there been time, I would probably have ordered several new suits of clothes for the occasion; but there was not, and I had to be satisfied with what I possessed. My appetite disappeared at once, and I knew neither peace nor rest until the day came. Its arrival brought neither, however; for then I was worse than before, and began hovering about the coach office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. Though I knew this perfectly well, I still felt it was unsafe to let the coach office out of my sight for more than five minutes at a time; and while in this unreasonable state, having completed the first half-hour of a watch that was to last four or five hours, Wemmick came upon me.\\n\\n\u201cHello, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said. \u201cHow are you? I would hardly have thought this was your usual ground.\u201d\\n\\nI explained that I was waiting to meet someone arriving by coach, and asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth thriving, thank you,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand especially the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful spirits. He\u2019ll be eighty-two on his next birthday. I have an idea of firing eighty-two shots, if the neighbourhood does not object, and if my cannon proves able to bear the strain. But this is not London conversation. Where do you suppose I am going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, since he was heading that way.\\n\\n\u201cThe next best thing,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI am going to Newgate. We are involved in a banker\u2019s-parcel case at present, and I have been out of town taking a look at the scene of the action; so now I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d Wemmick answered, very dryly. \u201cBut he is accused of it. You or I might be accused of it as well. Either of us might be, you know.\u201d\"}"
