          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d618c0ec6aeb2e48ed30f179\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is remarkable! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from his chair and paced the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is, to me, merely a motive for art. You might find nothing in him. I find everything in him. He is never more fully in my work than when there is no likeness of him in it. As I have said, he suggests a new style. I discover him in the sweep of certain lines, in the beauty and delicacy of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why do you refuse to exhibit his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, though I did not mean to, I have put into it some sign of this strange artistic worship, which naturally I have never wished to mention to him. He knows nothing of it. He never shall know anything of it. But the world might suspect it, and I will not expose my soul to their superficial, inquisitive eyes. My heart shall never lie beneath their microscope. There is too much of myself in that picture, Harry\u2014far too much of myself!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are less scrupulous than you. They understand how profitable passion can be in print. These days, a broken heart can go through many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI despise them for it,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist ought to make beautiful things, yet put none of his own life into them. We live in an age when people regard art as though it were a kind of autobiography. We have lost the abstract feeling for beauty. One day I shall show the world what that is; and for that very reason the world shall never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe you are mistaken, Basil, though I shall not dispute it with you. Only those who are intellectually astray ever argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very attached to you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter reflected for a few moments. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he replied after a 