{"request_id":"f9fdf0bec495e1a82f2d3da8","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, that is nothing! It gives me pleasure. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art.’

Lord Arthur put £4, 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after managing to refuse an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and went to the Park.

For the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at twelve o’clock he drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon the impassive hall-porter kept putting up telegrams from different parts of the country, reporting horse-race results, divorce verdicts, the weather, and similar matters, while the tape machine clicked out tiresome details of an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, greatly angering Colonel Goodchild. The Colonel wanted to read reports of a speech he had made that morning at the Mansion House about South African Missions and whether every province should have black Bishops; for some reason, he strongly disliked the _Evening News_. Yet none of the papers contained the slightest mention of Chichester, and Lord Arthur decided that the attempt had failed. It was a terrible shock, and for a while he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, offered many elaborate apologies and proposed to give him another clock free of charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost price. But Lord Arthur had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything was so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite was difficult to obtain in a pure state. Still, while agreeing that something must have gone wrong with the mechanism, the little German hoped the clock might yet explode. He gave the example of a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor of Odessa. Though it had been set to explode in ten days, it had not done so for nearly three months. True, when it finally exploded, it only blew a housemaid to pieces, since the Governor had left town six weeks earlier; but it at least proved that dynamite, as a destructive force, was powerful when controlled by machinery, though somewhat late in its work."}

