          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4a0174d3a36d426741c36627\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThey ought to concern you, Dorian. Every gentleman cares about his reputation. You cannot wish people to speak of you as vile and fallen. Naturally, you have your rank, your fortune, and all that sort of thing. But rank and fortune are not everything. Understand, I do not believe these rumours in the least. At any rate, I cannot believe them while I look at you. Sin is something that inscribes itself upon a man\u2019s face. It cannot be hidden. People sometimes speak of secret vices. There is no such thing. If an unhappy man has a vice, it reveals itself in the curves of his mouth, the heaviness of his eyelids, even the shaping of his hands. Someone\u2014I shall not name him, though you know him\u2014came to me last year to have me paint his portrait. I had never seen him before, and knew nothing of him then, though I have heard much since. He named an outrageous sum. I declined. There was something in the form of his fingers that repelled me. I know now that my impression of him was entirely right. His life is terrible. But you, Dorian, with that clear, radiant, innocent face of yours, and your wonderful, untroubled youth\u2014I cannot believe any evil of you. Yet I see you so rarely, and you no longer come to the studio, and when I am apart from you and hear these dreadful things people whisper about you, I do not know what to think. Why, Dorian, does a man like the Duke of Berwick leave a club room when you come in? Why will so many gentlemen in London neither visit your house nor ask you to theirs? You were once a friend of Lord Staveley. I met him at dinner last week. Your name came up by chance, in connection with the miniatures you lent to the exhibition at the Dudley. Staveley sneered and said that you might possess the finest artistic tastes, but that you were a man no pure-minded girl ought to be permitted to know, and no chaste woman ought to sit in the same room with. I reminded him that I was your friend and asked what he meant. He told me. He told me plainly, before everyone. It was dreadful! Why is your friendship so ruinous to young men? There was that miserable boy in the Guards who took his own life. You were his closest friend. There was Sir Henry Ashton, who was forced to leave England with his name disgraced.\"}"
