content.text:
{"request_id":"a5bf3607268bafdc4d8201b5","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, once they leave their own country, assume an air of perpetual ill-health, imagining it to be a mark of European elegance; but Mrs. Otis had never made this mistake. She possessed a splendid constitution and an astonishing supply of animal spirits. In fact, in many ways she was thoroughly English, and offered an admirable proof that America and ourselves now have everything in common, save, naturally, the language. Her eldest son, named Washington by his parents in a patriotic impulse he never stopped regretting, was a fair-haired, fairly handsome young man. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three seasons in succession, and was known even in London as a very fine dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his sole weaknesses. In every other respect he was exceedingly sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, slim and beautiful as a fawn, with a noble frankness in her large blue eyes. She was a remarkable amazon, and had once ridden her pony in a race against old Lord Bilton twice around the park, beating him by a length and a half directly before the statue of Achilles, to the immense delight of the young Duke of Cheshire. He proposed to her immediately, and his guardians sent him back to Eton that same night in a flood of tears. After Virginia came the twins, generally known as ‘The Stars and Stripes,’ because they were forever being swished. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the only genuine republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase lay seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them, and they set out on their drive in excellent spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was faintly perfumed by the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood-pigeon crooning over its own sweet voice, or glimpsed, far down among the whispering ferns, the gleaming breast of a pheasant. Small squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and across the mossy rises, their white tails held aloft. Yet as they turned into the avenue of Canterville Chase, the sky abruptly darkened with clouds; an odd stillness appeared to settle over the air, a large flight of rooks went silently above them, and before they arrived at the house, several heavy raindrops had begun to fall.

An old woman stood on the steps awaiting them, tidily dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

