{"request_id":"4875c20d0d20bee1e1b108b0","rewritten_text":"My father was gone, and his memory was the life of my life. I could feel gratitude toward another, but I could never again love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering. Even my pleasures were endured rather than enjoyed. I was like a solitary place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black cliffs, where no ray of warmth could enter and from which there was no path to sunnier fields. So, although the spirit of friendship comforted me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitation; it left, and I scarcely felt its loss. The spirit of life was dead within me; do not be surprised, then, that when heaven's greatest gift—a friend—came, I did not welcome it more gladly, nor mourn it more bitterly when it departed.

My friend's name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his history, so that you may judge how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy; and how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we were well suited to comfort one another, had I not been turned to stone by the Medusa's head of Misery. Woodville's misfortunes did not strike at the heart's core as mine did; his grief was natural, meant not to destroy but to purify the heart, and when its shadow had passed over him, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the very few whom fortune favors from birth, upon whom she lavishes every gift of mind and person without limit, and whom, under her special protection, no flaw however slight or disappointment however brief is permitted to touch. She seemed to have shaped his mind from an excellence no dross could stain, and given him an understanding no error could corrupt. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a bright star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet. That name has been so often degraded that it cannot convey all that he was. He was like a poet of old, crowned by the muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly halo that set him apart from them and raised him above them. His surpassing beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and words whose rich tones held the listener in silent, ecstatic wonder made him exceed all others, so that beside him they seemed formed only to serve his superior excellence."}

