          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a832ec12c1b2ccbb35fe284c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Directly before him, in the pale moonlight, he beheld an old man of dreadful appearance. His eyes glowed red like burning coals; long grey hair hung in tangled masses over his shoulders; his clothes, fashioned in an ancient style, were filthy and torn, while heavy manacles and rusted fetters dangled from his wrists and ankles.\\n\\n\u2018My dear sir,\u2019 said Mr. Otis, \u2018I must really ask you to oil those chains, and I have brought you a small bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator for that very purpose. It is reputed to be entirely effective after a single application, and the wrapper bears several testimonials to that effect from some of our most distinguished native divines. I will leave it for you here beside the bedroom candles, and shall be pleased to provide more if you need it.\u2019 Saying this, the United States Minister set the bottle upon a marble table and, shutting his door, went back to bed.\\n\\nFor an instant the Canterville ghost remained perfectly still in righteous indignation; then, hurling the bottle furiously onto the gleaming floor, he rushed along the corridor, giving hollow groans and casting out a horrible green light. But just as he arrived at the head of the great oak staircase, a door flew open, two small figures in white robes appeared, and a large pillow shot past his head! Plainly there was not a moment to spare, and so, quickly resorting to the Fourth Dimension of Space as his escape, he disappeared through the wainscoting, and the house fell entirely silent.\\n\\nWhen he reached a little secret room in the left wing, he braced himself against a moonbeam to regain his breath and began to comprehend his situation. Never, throughout a splendid and unbroken career of three hundred years, had he suffered such a gross affront. He recalled the Dowager Duchess, whom he had terrified into a fit while she stood before the mirror in lace and diamonds; the four housemaids, who had fallen into hysterics when he had merely grinned at them through the curtains of a spare bedroom; the parish rector, whose candle he had extinguished as the man returned late one night from the library, and who had since remained under Sir William Gull\u2019s care, a complete martyr to nervous ailments; and old Madame de Tremouillac, who, waking early one morning, had seen a skeleton sitting in an armchair by the fire and reading her diary, had been kept to her bed for six weeks with brain fever and, after recovering, had reconciled herself to the Church and ended her association with that notorious sceptic, Monsieur de Voltaire.\"}"
