          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b49d65e225b3ac0fb5fb956\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get tangled up in any such business as that, I says to myself.\\n\\nWhen I come down stairs in the morning, the parlor was closed up and the watchers had gone. There warn\u2019t anybody about except the family, widow Bartley, and our tribe. I studied their faces to find out whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t make it out.\\n\\nAlong about the middle of the day the undertaker arrived with his man, and they put the coffin in the center of the room on two chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows, borrowing others from the neighbors till the hall, parlor, and dining-room were full. I noticed the coffin lid was fixed just as it had been before, but I dasn\u2019t look under it with people there.\\n\\nThen folks begun coming in by crowds, and the beats and the girls sat in the front row at the coffin\u2019s head. For half an hour the people passed slowly around, one at a time, and looked at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute; some let fall a tear, and everything was mighty quiet and solemn. Only the girls and the beats held handkerchiefs to their eyes, with their heads bowed, sobbing a little. There warn\u2019t any sound except feet scraping on the floor and noses blowing\u2014because people always blow their noses more at funerals than anywhere else, except church.\\n\\nWhen the rooms were crammed full, the undertaker slid about in his black gloves, with his gentle, soothing manners, giving the last touches and putting people and things all ship-shape and easy, without making more noise than a cat. He didn\u2019t speak; he shifted people about, squeezed in the late arrivals, opened passages, and did it all with nods and hand signs. Then he stood over against the wall. He was the softest, glidingest, sneakiest man I ever seen; and he had no more smile in him than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeum\u2014a poorly one; and when all was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It was pretty skreeky and colicky, and everybody joined in singing, and Peter was the only one who had a good thing, to my way of thinking. Then Reverend Hobson started in, slow and solemn, and begun speaking; but right away the most dreadful racket a body ever heard broke loose in the cellar. It was only one dog, but he raised a powerful row and kept it going steady; the parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
