{"request_id":"ed4d0b6036516b5c3722c967","rewritten_text":"Spirits of storm, receive me! powers of destruction, open your arms wide and hold me forever! Unless some kinder power has decreed another end, so that, after long suffering, I may gain my reward and once more feel my heart beat beside the heart of another like myself.

The Tiber, the road laid out by nature’s own hand through her continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were moored along its banks. With a few books, provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and drift down the river into the sea; then, keeping close to shore, I would follow the beautiful coasts and sunlit headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and Calabria, and dare the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. Then, with steady purpose—for what had I to lose?—I would skim across the ocean toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belonged to another existence than my present one. I would sail along Asia Minor and Syria and, passing the seven-mouthed Nile, turn north again, until, leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I reached the Pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever it might be—the slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean might become my home before I completed this long voyage; or disease might strike my heart as I floated alone on the heaving Mediterranean. Or, at some place where I landed, I might find what I sought—a companion. Or, if that could not be, then through endless years, feeble and grey-haired—my youth already buried with those I love—the solitary wanderer would still spread his sail and grasp the tiller. Still obeying heaven’s breezes, forever passing one headland after another, anchoring in one bay after another, still cutting through the barren sea, leaving green native Europe behind, following Africa’s tawny coast, having endured the fierce seas around the Cape, I might moor my worn skiff in a creek shaded by the spice groves of the fragrant islands in the distant Indian Ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet ever since they came upon me a week ago, as I stood on the height of St. Peter’s, they have governed my imagination. I have chosen my boat and stored my meagre supplies. I have selected a few books; Homer and Shakespeare are the chief among them—but the libraries of the world lie open to me, and in any port I can replenish my stock. I expect no change for the better; but the monotony of the present is unbearable to me."}

