          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"047b82d3e54fd9d1f96df304\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then I recited to myself that beautiful passage describing Dante\u2019s entrance into the terrestrial Paradise; and I imagined how delightful it would be, when I roamed along those lovely shores, to behold the car of light descend, bearing my long-lost parent back to me. As I waited in anticipation of that hour, I considered how I would weave a garland from the fair flowers growing there, and crown myself in my happiness: I would sing _sul margine d\u2019un rio_, my father\u2019s favourite air, so that my voice, floating through the still air, might tell him, in whatever bower he awaited our meeting, that his daughter had arrived. Then the stamp of wretchedness would vanish from my brow, and I would lift my eyes without fear to encounter his, which had ever shone with the mild radiance of guiltless love. At the thought of the enchanted expression of those deep eyes, I wept, though softly, lest my sobbing should break the fairy vision.\\n\\nSo wholly was I absorbed in this reverie that I went onward without observing my footsteps, until I bent to pluck a flower for my garland upon that barren plain, where no flower was to be found; then I started from my waking dream and discovered that I knew not where I was.\\n\\nThe sun had gone down, and the rosy colour the clouds had received from him at his setting had almost faded. A wind passed over the plain; I looked about and saw nothing that could inform me of my situation: I was lost, and tried in vain to recover my way. I continued wandering, while the advancing darkness rendered indistinct every sign by which I might have been directed. At last all was hidden beneath the profound gloom of the blackest night; I grew tired, and, knowing that my servant was to lodge that night in the neighbouring village, so that none would be alarmed by my absence, and that in this desolate place I was secure from all intruders, I determined to remain where I was until morning. Indeed, I was too exhausted to proceed: the air was cold, but I disregarded physical discomfort, believing myself well hardened against the weather by my two years of solitude, during which no alteration of the seasons had interrupted my continual wanderings.\\n\\nI stretched myself upon the grass, encompassed by a darkness through which not the faintest ray of light could enter\u2014there was no sound, for the deep night had put the insects to rest, the sole creatures inhabiting that lonely place, where neither tree nor bush could give refuge to anything else\u2014there was a strange stillness in the air which soothed my senses while it animated my spirit; my thoughts rushed from image to image and appeared to embrace eternity.\"}"
