          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"58bd91759f90f3d072b60cc0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is quite extraordinary that she should invariably hear Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so plainly! Still, she will not find her grandmama at all deafer than she was two years since; which is saying a great deal at my mother\u2019s age\u2014and it is really a full two years, you know, since she was last here. We were never so long without seeing her before; and, as I was saying to Mrs. Cole, we shall scarcely know how to make enough of her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you expect Miss Fairfax here shortly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014that must be a very great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Every body is so surprized, and every body says the same obliging things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury as they can be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot tell which, because Colonel Campbell will want the carriage himself on one of those days. So very kind of them to send her all the way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes of. That is why she writes out of rule, as we call it; for in the ordinary course, we should not have heard from her until next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that was what I supposed. I feared there could be little prospect of my hearing any thing of Miss Fairfax to-day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo obliging of you! No, we should not have heard, but for this particular circumstance of her coming here so soon. My mother is so delighted!\u2014for she is to remain with us at least three months. Three months, she says so positively, as I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you. The matter is, you see, that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and visit her directly. They had not meant to go till the summer, but she is so impatient to see them again\u2014for, until her marriage last October, she had never been away from them for more than a week\u2014which must make it very strange to be in separate kingdoms, I was going to say, though different countries, however; and so she wrote a most urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I declare I cannot tell which it was, though we shall see presently in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014writing in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, to urge their coming over directly; and they would meet them in Dublin, and take them back to their country seat, Baly-craig, a beautiful place, I fancy.\"}"
