          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"de7a3ee0b04bc0c9ded03266\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI examined the death\u2019s-head carefully. Its outer lines\u2014the lines of the drawing closest to the edge of the vellum\u2014were much clearer than the rest. It was evident that the heat had acted imperfectly or unevenly. I immediately lit a fire and exposed every part of the parchment to intense heat. At first, the only result was that the faint lines of the skull grew stronger; but as I continued the experiment, a figure appeared in the corner of the slip, diagonally opposite the place where the death\u2019s-head was drawn. At first I took it for a goat. Closer examination, however, convinced me that it was meant to be a kid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d I said. \u201cOf course, I have no right to laugh at you\u2014a million and a half in money is too serious a matter for amusement\u2014but surely you are not going to forge a third link in your chain. You will not discover any special connection between your pirates and a goat. Pirates, as you know, have nothing to do with goats; goats belong to farming.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I have just told you that the figure was not a goat.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, then, a kid\u2014much the same thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMuch the same, but not entirely,\u201d said Legrand. \u201cYou may have heard of a certain Captain Kidd. I immediately regarded the animal\u2019s figure as a sort of punning or hieroglyphic signature. I call it a signature because its position on the vellum suggested that idea. The death\u2019s-head in the diagonally opposite corner likewise had the appearance of a stamp or seal. But I was greatly troubled by the absence of anything else\u2014the body to my imagined instrument, the text to my context.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you expected to find a letter between the stamp and the signature.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSomething of that sort. The truth is, I felt irresistibly possessed by a sense that some great good fortune was about to come to me. I can hardly explain why. Perhaps it was more a wish than a genuine belief\u2014but do you know that Jupiter\u2019s foolish remarks about the bug\u2019s being solid gold had a remarkable effect on my imagination? Then there was that chain of accidents and coincidences; they were so extraordinary. Do you see how purely accidental it was that these events occurred on the one day of the entire year when it was, or might be, cool enough for a fire? Without the fire, or without the dog\u2019s intervention at the exact moment he appeared, I would never have noticed the death\u2019s-head, and thus would never have become the owner of the treasure.\u201d\"}"
