          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fa331be8d62c8f391af4a56b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was brought to you. You see, I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is certainly true,\u201d said Defarge. His wife, knitting and humming, had signaled him with a casual touch of her elbow that he should answer, but always briefly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and it was from your care that she took him away, accompanied by a tidy brown monsieur\u2014what is his name?\u2014wearing a little wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company bank\u2014to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting memories!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou do not hear much of them now?\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIndeed,\u201d Madame interrupted, looking up from her work and her quiet song, \u201cwe never hear of them. We received word that they had arrived safely, and perhaps another letter, perhaps two; but since then, they have gone their way through life, and we have gone ours, and we have had no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so, madame,\u201d replied the spy. \u201cShe is going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing to be married?\u201d Madame echoed. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have married long ago. You English are cold, it seems to me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know that I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can tell by your tongue,\u201d Madame replied; \u201cand I suppose a man is what his tongue is.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not take this identification as a compliment, but made the best of it and laughed it away. Having finished his cognac, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is going to marry. But not an Englishman; someone who, like her, was born French. And speaking of Gaspard\u2014ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!\u2014it is curious that she is to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was raised to such a height, so many feet above the ground; in other words, the present Marquis. But he lives anonymously in England; he is no Marquis there. He is Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge continued knitting steadily, but the news visibly affected her husband. Whatever he did behind the little counter\u2014striking a light, lighting his pipe\u2014he was disturbed, and his hand was unsteady. The spy would not have been much of a spy if he had failed to notice it and store it away in his mind.\\n\\nHaving scored at least this one point, whatever it might later prove worth, and with no customers entering to offer him another, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and left. Before departing, he took the opportunity to say politely that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
