          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a1f5aa923e8acb1f2887bba5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; the whole effort of maintaining a slow, unsatisfying correspondence with Elizabeth was left to Anne.\\n\\nNot long after breakfast, Captain and Mrs Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had arranged to take one last walk around Lyme together. They were meant to leave for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and until then they planned to stay together and outdoors as long as possible.\\n\\nAs soon as they were properly in the street, Anne found Captain Benwick moving close to her. Their conversation the evening before had not discouraged him from seeking her out again; and for some time they walked together, talking as before about Mr Scott and Lord Byron. They still differed, as much as any two readers might, over the merits of either writer, until something caused an almost general rearrangement of the party, and Captain Harville was beside her instead of Captain Benwick.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said quietly, \u201cyou have done a kind thing by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he had such company more often. I know it is bad for him to shut himself away as he does; but what can we do? We cannot leave him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can easily believe that would be impossible; but in time, perhaps\u2014we know what time can do in every kind of sorrow. And you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend is still a young mourner\u2014only since last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that is true enough,\u201d he said with a deep sigh. \u201cOnly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not learn of it until some time later.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, having just been made captain of the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading the news of him. He sent letters in, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news had to follow him there, but who was to tell him? Not I. I would sooner have been hauled up to the yard-arm. No one could do it except that good fellow\u201d\u2014pointing to Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had come into Plymouth the week before, with no chance of being sent to sea again. He took his chance for the rest; applied for leave, but without waiting for an answer, travelled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler at once, and never left the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You can imagine, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty, and replied as warmly as her own feelings allowed, or as his seemed able to bear. He was too moved to continue the subject, and when he spoke again, it was of something entirely different.\"}"
