          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f1deb587b6aac4dc17ffdfc0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd think, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, as tears rose in his eyes despite every effort to hold them back. \u201cOh, think how young and good she is, and how much happiness and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014certain\u2014completely certain\u2014that for your sake, because you are so good yourself; and for her own sake; and for the sake of all those she makes so happy, she will not die. Heaven will never allow her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou reason like a child, poor boy. Yet you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, though I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old and have seen enough illness and death to understand the agony of being separated from those we love. I have seen enough, too, to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them. But that should comfort us in our grief, for Heaven is just; and such things teach us, with great force, that there is a brighter world than this, and that the journey to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke these words, she stopped her grieving as if by a single effort; and, straightening herself as she spoke, became calm and resolute. He was even more astonished to find that this strength endured, and that throughout all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie was always prepared and composed, carrying out every duty that had fallen to her steadily and, outwardly at least, even cheerfully. But he was young, and did not know what strong minds can endure in times of trial. How could he, when those who possess them so rarely know it themselves?\\n\\nA troubled night followed. When morning came, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears were only too fully confirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a severe and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not surrender to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, putting a finger to her lips as she looked steadily into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne as quickly as possible. It must be taken to the market town, which is no more than four miles away by the footpath across the fields, and from there sent by a mounted express directly to Chertsey. The people at the inn will arrange it, and I know I can trust you to make sure it is done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his face showed how eager he was to leave at once.\"}"
