          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"74f148dacc5f97787ed56e5c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNothing but a tick.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere\u2019d you find him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOut in the woods.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat will you take for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. I don\u2019t want to sell him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right. It\u2019s a very small tick, anyway.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, anybody can chase down a tick that doesn\u2019t belong to them. I\u2019m happy with it. It\u2019s a good enough tick for me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSho, there are plenty of ticks. I could get a thousand if I wanted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, why don\u2019t you? Because you know perfectly well you can\u2019t. This is an early tick, I reckon. It\u2019s the first one I\u2019ve seen this year.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSay, Huck\u2014I\u2019ll give you my tooth for it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nTom took out a small piece of paper and carefully unwrapped it. Huckleberry looked at it longingly. The temptation was powerful. At last he said:\\n\\n\u201cIs it real?\u201d\\n\\nTom raised his lip and showed the gap.\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right,\u201d said Huckleberry. \u201cIt\u2019s a trade.\u201d\\n\\nTom put the tick into the percussion-cap box that had recently been the pinchbug\u2019s prison, and the boys went their separate ways, each feeling richer than before.\\n\\nWhen Tom reached the small, lonely frame schoolhouse, he marched in quickly, looking like someone who had hurried there as fast as he honestly could. He hung his hat on a peg and dropped into his seat with brisk efficiency. The master, sitting high in his large splint-bottomed armchair, was dozing in the sleepy hum of studying. The interruption woke him.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer!\u201d\\n\\nTom knew that hearing his full name meant trouble.\\n\\n\u201cSir!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome here. Now, sir, why are you late again, as usual?\u201d\\n\\nTom was about to hide behind a lie when he saw two long yellow braids hanging down a back he recognized through love\u2019s instant feeling; and beside that figure was the only empty seat on the girls\u2019 side of the schoolroom. At once he said:\\n\\n\u201c_I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn!_\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s pulse seemed to stop, and he stared helplessly. The hum of study died away. The pupils wondered whether this reckless boy had lost his mind. The master said:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2014you did what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn.\u201d\\n\\nThere was no misunderstanding the words.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer, this is the most astonishing confession I have ever heard. A simple ferule will not do for this offense. Take off your jacket.\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s arm kept working until it was tired and his supply of switches had noticeably shrunk. Then came the order:\\n\\n\u201cNow, sir, go sit with the girls! Let this be a warning to you.\u201d\\n\\nThe laughter that ran through the room seemed to embarrass the boy, but really he was more overwhelmed by his admiring awe of his unknown idol and the fearful pleasure of his great good fortune.\"}"
