          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6b8ce36e812e737ab854fd1b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would live among people who loved her and had more sense than she did; secluded enough to be safe, and busy enough to be cheerful. She would never be led into temptation, nor left for temptation to seek her out. She would be respectable and happy; and Emma acknowledged her to be the luckiest creature in the world, for having inspired such steady, enduring affection in such a man\u2014or, if not quite the luckiest, second only to herself.\\n\\nHarriet, naturally occupied by her obligations to the Martins, came to Hartfield less and less; and this was not to be regretted. The closeness between her and Emma had to fade; their friendship had to become a quieter kind of goodwill; and fortunately, what ought to happen, and had to happen, already seemed to be beginning in the most gradual and natural way.\\n\\nBefore the end of September, Emma accompanied Harriet to church and saw her hand given to Robert Martin with such complete satisfaction that no memories, even those connected with Mr. Elton standing before them, could lessen it. Perhaps, indeed, she scarcely saw Mr. Elton then except as the clergyman whose blessing at the altar might soon be bestowed on herself. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, the last of the three couples to become engaged, were the first to marry.\\n\\nJane Fairfax had already left Highbury and returned to the comforts of her beloved home with the Campbells. Mr. and Mrs. Churchill were also in town, and they were only waiting for November.\\n\\nThe month in between was the one chosen, as far as they dared, by Emma and Mr. Knightley. They had decided that their marriage should take place while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, so that they could allow themselves a fortnight away on a trip to the seaside, which was their plan. John and Isabella, along with every other friend, agreed in approving it. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how could Mr. Woodhouse be persuaded to consent? He had never yet referred to their marriage except as something far in the future.\\n\\nWhen the subject was first raised with him, he was so unhappy that they nearly lost hope. A second mention, however, caused him less pain. He began to think it would happen, and that he could not stop it\u2014a very encouraging step toward resignation. Yet he was still not happy. Indeed, he seemed so far from it that his daughter's courage failed. She could not bear to see him suffer, or know that he imagined himself neglected; and though her judgment nearly accepted the assurances of both Mr. Knightleys that, once it was over, his distress would soon be over too, she hesitated\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
