          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5ce81221950b242429c5058\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThese were the circumstances that weighed upon Felix\u2019s heart and made him, when I first beheld him, the unhappiest member of his family. Poverty he might have borne, and, since this suffering had been the reward of his virtue, he had even taken pride in it; but the Turk\u2019s ingratitude and the loss of his adored Safie were calamities more cruel and beyond repair. The coming of the Arabian now breathed fresh life into his spirit.\\n\\n\u201cWhen word came to Leghorn that Felix had lost his fortune and station, the merchant ordered his daughter to dismiss all thought of her lover and make ready to return to her own country. Safie\u2019s noble disposition revolted at this command; she tried to reason with her father, but he angrily left her, repeating his despotic order.\\n\\n\u201cA few days later, the Turk came into his daughter\u2019s room and hurriedly informed her that he had cause to think his dwelling at Leghorn had been discovered, and that he would soon be surrendered to the French government. He had therefore engaged a vessel to carry him to Constantinople, and was to sail for that city within a few hours. His purpose was to leave his daughter in the charge of a trusted servant, so that she might follow at her convenience with the larger portion of his property, which had not yet reached Leghorn.\\n\\n\u201cLeft by herself, Safie considered what course she ought to take in this crisis. To live in Turkey was hateful to her; both her faith and her feelings opposed it. From certain papers belonging to her father that came into her possession, she learned of her lover\u2019s banishment and discovered the name of the place where he was then living. For a while she wavered, but finally fixed upon her resolve. Carrying some jewels of her own and a quantity of money, she left Italy with an attendant, a native of Leghorn who understood the usual language of Turkey, and set out for Germany.\\n\\n\u201cShe safely reached a town some twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage, when her attendant became dangerously sick. Safie tended her with the tenderest devotion, but the poor girl died, leaving the Arabian alone, ignorant of the country\u2019s language and wholly unfamiliar with the customs of the world. Yet she found herself among kind people. The Italian had named the place to which they were travelling, and after her death the mistress of the house where they had stayed ensured that Safie arrived safely at her lover\u2019s cottage.\u201d\"}"
