          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f5b3561be19baeee5cbc2dbd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and earnestly that we drew the interested notice of that liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic company; they clustered around us and took on a highly resolute air. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf truly ye are freemen, ye have nothing to dread--Britain's God-bestowed liberties stand about ye as shield and refuge! (Applause.) Ye shall presently see. Produce your proofs.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proofs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProofs that ye are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh--I recalled it! I came back to myself, and said nothing. But the king burst forth:\\n\\n\u201cThou art mad, man. It would be better, and more reasonable, for this thief and rascal here to prove that we are _not_ freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he understood his own laws as people so commonly understand laws: in words, not in consequences. They acquire a _meaning_, and become exceedingly vivid, when they are applied to oneself.\\n\\nEverybody shook their heads and appeared disappointed; some lost interest and moved away. The orator said--now in a businesslike tone rather than one of sentiment:\\n\\n\u201cAn ye know not the laws of your country, it is time ye learned them. Ye are strangers among us; that ye will not deny. Ye may be freemen, and we deny it not; yet ye may likewise be slaves. The law is plain: it requireth not the claimant to prove ye slaves, but requireth you to prove ye are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cGood sir, grant us but time to send to Astolat; or but time to send to the Valley of Holiness--\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man; these are unusual requests, and ye cannot expect them to be allowed. They would consume much time, and would improperly inconvenience your master--\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_Master_, fool!\u201d the king thundered. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m--\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God's sake!\u201d\\n\\nI spoke soon enough to check the king. We were already in sufficient difficulty; it could do us no good to give these people the impression that we were madmen.\\n\\nThere is no point in drawing out the particulars. The earl put us on the block and sold us by auction. This very same accursed law had existed in our own South in my own day, more than thirteen hundred years afterward; under it, hundreds of freemen unable to establish that they were freemen had been sold into slavery for life, without the fact making any special impression on me. But the instant the law and auction block entered my own experience, what had formerly seemed merely improper became abruptly hellish. Well, that is how we are fashioned.\\n\\nYes, we were auctioned off like hogs. In a large town with a lively market, we would have fetched a fair price; but this place was wholly dead, and we sold for a sum that shames me whenever I remember it.\"}"
