          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0db9ec0b8da5467211d27255\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters at the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are rarely very harsh, and in autumn it is indeed unusual for a fire to be thought necessary. Around the middle of October, 18\u2014, however, there came a day of remarkable cold. Just before sunset, I made my way through the evergreens to the hut of my friend, whom I had not visited for several weeks. At that time I lived in Charleston, nine miles from the island, and the means of traveling back and forth were far inferior to those available today. Reaching the hut, I knocked, as was my habit; receiving no answer, I looked for the key where I knew it was hidden, unlocked the door, and entered. A fine fire blazed on the hearth. It was an unusual sight, and a welcome one. I removed my overcoat, settled into an armchair beside the crackling logs, and patiently waited for my hosts to return.\\n\\nSoon after nightfall they arrived and welcomed me warmly. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried about preparing marsh hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his spells\u2014what else could I call them?\u2014of excitement. He had discovered an unknown bivalve that formed a new genus; and, beyond that, with Jupiter\u2019s help he had tracked down and captured a scarab beetle he believed entirely new, though he wanted my opinion of it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, warming my hands over the fire and wishing the entire race of scarabs to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I had known you were here!\u201d said Legrand. \u201cBut it has been so long since I saw you, and how could I have expected you to visit on this particular night? On my way home, I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort and, very foolishly, lent him the bug. So you cannot see it until morning. Stay here tonight, and I\u2019ll send Jup for it at sunrise. It is the loveliest thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat? Sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNonsense\u2014no, the bug. It is a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back and another, somewhat longer one, at the other. Its antennae are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere ain\u2019t no tin in him, Massa Will; I keeps tellin\u2019 you,\u201d Jupiter interrupted. \u201cDat bug is a gold bug, solid gold, every bit of him, inside and all, except his wings. Never felt a bug half so heavy in my life.\u201d\"}"
