          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"573faab2bb30fc7d241d6683\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would make such an agreeable companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse assented to every thing, but observed,\\n\\n\u201cOur young friend Harriet Smith, though, is another such pretty, amiable young creature. You will be fond of Harriet. Emma could have no better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear it\u2014but Jane Fairfax alone is known to be so highly accomplished and superior!\u2014and she is precisely Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis subject was pursued with great satisfaction, and others of equal importance followed, passing with the same good humour; yet the evening was not to end without some renewal of uneasiness. The gruel arrived, and gave occasion for much conversation\u2014abundant commendation and remark\u2014positive conviction of its fitness for every constitution, and rather sharp Philippics against the many households where it was never to be had tolerably;\u2014but unhappily, among the deficiencies which the daughter had to mention, the latest, and consequently the most conspicuous, was that of her own cook at South End, a young woman engaged for the occasion, who had never been able to comprehend what was intended by a basin of good smooth gruel, thin, yet not excessively thin. However often she had desired and directed it, nothing tolerable had ever been produced. This was a perilous opening.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with affectionate anxiety.\u2014The exclamation, as Emma understood it, said, \u201cAh! there is no end to the unhappy effects of your visit to South End. It will not bear speaking of.\u201d For a short time she hoped he would say no more, and that silent reflection might be enough to bring him back to the enjoyment of his own smooth gruel. After several minutes\u2019 pause, however, he began,\\n\\n\u201cI shall always regret that you went to the sea this autumn, rather than coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you regret it, sir?\u2014I assure you, it was of great benefit to the children.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you were obliged to go to the sea, it ought not to have been South End. South End is an unhealthy spot. Perry was surprised to hear that you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think so, but it is truly quite mistaken, sir.\u2014We all enjoyed perfectly good health there, and never experienced the smallest inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is wholly an error to consider the place unhealthy; and I am certain he may be trusted, for he understands the nature of the air thoroughly, and his own brother and family have gone there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou ought to have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere.\u2014Perry spent a week at Cromer once, and considers it the best of all sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
