          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ef92797851308b3f7e9b4a81\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJupiter,\u201d he said when we reached the foot of the tree, \u201ccome here! Was the skull nailed to the branch with its face outward, or facing the branch?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDe face was out, massa, so de crows could get at de eyes easy, widout no trouble.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, was it this eye or that one that you dropped the beetle through?\u201d Here Legrand touched each of Jupiter\u2019s eyes.\\n\\n\u201c\u2019Twas dis eye, massa\u2014the lef eye\u2014jis like you told me,\u201d he said, indicating his right eye.\\n\\n\u201cThat will do. We must try again.\u201d\\n\\nMy friend, in whose madness I now saw\u2014or imagined I saw\u2014certain signs of method, moved the peg marking the place where the beetle had fallen about three inches west of its former position. Then, measuring from the nearest point of the trunk to the peg as before, and extending the line straight out for fifty feet, he marked a spot several yards away from where we had been digging.\\n\\nHe drew a circle around the new location, somewhat larger than the first, and we set to work again with the spades. I was terribly tired, but, without quite understanding what had changed my mind, I no longer felt much reluctance toward the labor forced upon me. I had become strangely interested\u2014indeed, even excited. Perhaps there was something in all Legrand\u2019s wild behavior\u2014some air of planning or deliberation\u2014that affected me. I dug eagerly, and from time to time caught myself looking with something very like expectation for the imagined treasure, the vision of which had driven my unfortunate companion mad. At a moment when such fancies had fully taken hold of me, and when we had been working perhaps an hour and a half, we were interrupted again by the dog\u2019s violent howling. His earlier restlessness had plainly been only playfulness or whim, but now it had become harsh and serious. When Jupiter again tried to muzzle him, he resisted furiously, leaped into the hole, and frantically tore up the earth with his claws. Within seconds he uncovered a mass of human bones: two complete skeletons, mixed with several metal buttons and what seemed to be the dust of rotted wool. One or two strokes of a spade turned up the blade of a large Spanish knife, and as we dug farther, three or four loose gold and silver coins came to light.\"}"
