          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6ce27b53eb8ec3d2337c2f3f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGit up! What you \u2019bout?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked about, trying to tell where I was. The sun was already up, and I had been sleeping sound. Pap stood over me, looking cross and sick besides. He says:\\n\\n\u201cWhat you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI reckoned he didn\u2019t know anything about what he\u2019d been doing, so I says:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me up?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t; I couldn\u2019t stir you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there jawing all day; get out and see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll come along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I got out up the river-bank. I saw bits of limbs and things floating downstream, with a scattering of bark; so I knew the river had started rising. I figured I\u2019d have fine times now if I were over at the town. The June rise had always brought me luck; for as soon as it began, down came floating cordwood and pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs tied together; and all you had to do was catch them and sell them to the wood-yards and sawmill.\\n\\nI went up the bank, keeping one eye out for pap and the other for whatever the rise might bring. Then, all at once, along came a canoe; a real beauty, too, thirteen or fourteen feet long, floating high like a duck. I dived head-first off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam for the canoe. I expected somebody might be lying down in it, because folks often did that to fool people, and when a fellow had pulled a skiff nearly up to it, they\u2019d sit up and laugh at him. But it wasn\u2019t that way this time. It was a genuine drift-canoe, and I climbed in and paddled her ashore. I thought the old man would be pleased when he saw it\u2014she was worth ten dollars. But when I reached shore, pap was not in sight yet; and while I was running her into a little creek, like a gully, all overhung with vines and willows, another notion struck me: I decided to hide her well, and then, instead of taking to the woods when I ran away, I\u2019d go downriver about fifty miles and camp in one spot for good, without having such a hard time tramping on foot.\\n\\nIt was quite near the shanty, and I kept thinking I heard the old man coming; but I got her hidden. Then I stepped out and peered around a clump of willows, and there was the old man a little way down the path, just taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
