          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"457fe51c6a51a32f44f133ba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I would pretend to die; my satisfied heirs would take my fortune, and I would buy my freedom. But my plan had to be carefully arranged; I would not be left penniless, and I had to secure some money. Alas! What hateful measures I was forced to consider! Yet otherwise a whole life of deceit lay before me; and whenever remorse at planning any trick made me draw back, I was inevitably driven to it again and confirmed in my purpose by a visit from some aunt or cousin. They would tell me that death comes to all men. Then they would say that my father had certainly lost his reason since my mother\u2019s death; that he was mad, and that I was fortunate, because in one of his attacks he might have killed me instead of ending his own deranged life. And all this, of course, was said delicately\u2014not openly, lest my feelings be wounded\u2014but\\n\\nWhispered this and that\\nIn dark, soft, quiet hints\\n\\nwith lowered eyes and sympathetic smiles or little sobs; and I listened with a calm face while every nerve shook. I dared not say yes or no to all this blasphemy. Oh, what a delightful life, so free from deceit! I, with the face of a dove and the heart of a fox: for I felt only the shame of lying, not any holy sense of innocence that could make it bearable. I, who had once dressed myself in the bright clothing of sincerity, now had to borrow garments of many colours. They might fit awkwardly at first, but habit would teach me to arrange them in graceful folds, to lie elegantly. Yes, I might stain my soul with falsehood until its natural colour was completely hidden. Oh, beloved father! Receive the pure heart of your unhappy daughter; let me join you unstained as I was, or you will not know my changed appearance. As grief might change Constance, so deceit would change me, until in heaven you would say, \u201cThis is not my child\u201d\u2014My father, if I am to be happy now and when we meet