          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"41686734b8b7617979f50138\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHow long I continued in this condition it is impossible to determine. It must, nevertheless, have been no trifling period, since, when I partly regained the consciousness of existence, I perceived the dawn breaking, the balloon at an immense elevation above a waste of ocean, and not the slightest sign of land anywhere to be seen within the bounds of the broad horizon. My feelings, however, on thus returning to myself, were by no means so full of anguish as might have been expected. Indeed, there was something of beginning madness in the composed inspection I commenced of my circumstances. I raised each hand in turn before my eyes, and marvelled what accident could have caused the veins to swell and the fingernails to assume so dreadful a blackness. I next examined my head with care, shaking it again and again, and passing my hands over it with the closest attention, until I had convinced myself that it was not, as I had more than half imagined, larger than the balloon itself. Then, with an air of sagacity, I searched both pockets of my breeches, and, finding a set of tablets and a toothpick case absent, tried to explain their loss; and, failing in this, was seized with inexpressible vexation. It then struck me that I was suffering considerable pain in the joint of my left ankle, and a faint perception of my true situation began to dawn upon my mind. Yet, strange to relate! I was neither amazed nor terrified. If I experienced any feeling whatever, it was a species of inward chuckling satisfaction at the ingenuity I was about to show in freeing myself from this predicament; nor did I, for an instant, regard my final preservation as open to doubt. For some minutes I sat absorbed in the deepest reflection. I distinctly remember often pressing my lips together, laying my forefinger against the side of my nose, and employing other gestures and contortions usual with men who, comfortably seated in arm-chairs, ponder questions of difficulty or consequence. Having, as I believed, sufficiently arranged my thoughts, I then, with much care and deliberation, carried my hands behind my back and loosened the great iron buckle attached to the waistband of my inexpressibles. This buckle possessed three teeth, which, being rather rusted, revolved upon their axis only with great difficulty. After some exertion, however, I brought them to right angles with the buckle's body, and was pleased to discover that they held firmly in that posture. Keeping the instrument thus procured between my teeth, I proceeded to loosen the knot of my cravat. I was obliged to pause several times before I could complete this operation, but at last it was done.\"}"
