{"request_id":"c171eeca46dd371044548fc4","rewritten_text":"I cannot convey the ecstatic pleasure with which I turned from political strife at home, and the bodily calamities of foreign lands, to my own beloved dwelling, that chosen seat of goodness and affection; to peace, and the communion of every holy sympathy. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings could not have been so vivid; but in Greece I had been the victim of terror and grievous alteration; in Greece, after a season of care and grief, I had watched two depart whose very names signified greatness and virtue. Yet such sorrows could never enter the domestic circle that remained to me, while, withdrawn within our cherished forest, we spent our days in quiet. The passage of years did indeed bring some slight alteration even there; and time, as ever, impressed the marks of mortality upon our joys and hopes. Idris, the fondest of wives, sisters, and friends, was a gentle and devoted mother. With her this feeling was no mere amusement, as with many; it was a passion. We had had three children; one, the second-born, died during my stay in Greece. This had mingled the proud and blissful emotions of motherhood with sorrow and alarm. Before that loss, the little creatures born of herself, the young inheritors of her fleeting life, had seemed assured of life; now she feared that the merciless destroyer might seize her surviving darlings, as he had taken their brother. The slightest sickness brought agonies of dread; she was unhappy whenever she was separated from them at all; she had stored her wealth of happiness in their frail existence, and remained always vigilant lest the stealthy robber should again carry off these precious jewels. Happily, she had little reason for apprehension. Alfred, now nine, was a frank, manly little boy, with a shining brow, mild eyes, and a gentle yet self-reliant temper. Our youngest was still an infant; but roses of health were scattered over his soft cheek, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with harmless laughter.

Clara had outgrown the age which, through its silent ignorance, had been the cause of Idris's fears. Clara was beloved by her, by us all. So much intelligence was joined to innocence, tenderness to patience, and gravity to the most perfect cheerfulness; such surpassing beauty was united with so winning a simplicity, that she seemed like a pearl suspended in the shrine of our possessions, a treasure of marvel and worth.

At the opening of winter, Alfred, now nine years old, went for the first time to school at Eton. He regarded this as the first step toward manhood, and was pleased in proportion. The shared pursuits of study and play drew forth the finest qualities of his nature: his constant perseverance, generosity, and disciplined firmness."}

