          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a94056c4411508804b490112\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now come to my own story. There is little to tell of the early part of my life, and I will be brief; but I must linger somewhat over my childhood, so that it may be understood how, when one hope failed, all life became empty; and how, when the only affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my own existence seemed to die with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt was very unlike my father. I believe that, without the least trace of wickedness, she possessed the coldest heart ever held in a human breast: it was wholly incapable of affection. She took me into her care because she thought it her duty; but she had lived too long alone, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, to permit me to disturb her peace. She had never married, and for the previous five years had lived entirely alone on an estate inherited through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had wished her to live with me at his family home, in beautiful countryside near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused, but as soon as she had settled the affairs that her brother's departure had left in her charge, she left England and took me to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe care of me as a baby, and later until I reached my eighth year, fell to one of my mother's servants, who had come with us into retirement for that purpose. I was kept in a distant part of the house and saw my aunt only at fixed times. This happened twice each day: around noon she came to my nursery, and after dinner I was brought to her. She never embraced me, and throughout my stay in the room seemed afraid that some childish act would trouble her. My good nurse always trained me most carefully before she dared take me into the parlour\u2014and the fear inspired by my aunt's cold looks and few forced words was so great that I rarely failed my nurse's lessons or broke the perfect stillness I had been taught to keep during these brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse's care, I ran freely through our park and the nearby fields. Born from the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years a very sensitive nature. I cannot describe how passionately I loved everything, even the lifeless objects around me.\"}"
