          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"151f55777b9b7ab3d77e4c25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having joined in the general sorrow over the terrible consequences of this affair, which Elizabeth thought nearly certain, though Miss Bennet could not declare entirely impossible, Elizabeth pursued the matter, saying, \u201cBut tell me every thing about it that I have not yet heard. Give me more particulars. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no suspicion of any thing before the elopement occurred? They must have seen them constantly together.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had frequently suspected some attachment, particularly on Lydia\u2019s part, but nothing that could alarm him. I am exceedingly sorry for him. His conduct was as attentive and kind as possible. He _was_ on his way to us, to assure us of his concern, before he had any notion that they might not have gone to Scotland: when that suspicion first arose, it hurried his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny persuaded that Wickham would not marry her? Did he know they meant to run away? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when _he_ questioned him, Denny denied all knowledge of their scheme, and would not state his true opinion of it. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and from _that_ I am led to hope he may have been misunderstood earlier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster arrived himself, none of you, I suppose, doubted that they were truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such a thought have entered our minds? I was somewhat uneasy--somewhat afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness in marrying him, because I knew his behaviour had not always been entirely proper. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they felt only how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then confessed, with the very natural satisfaction of knowing more than the rest of us, that Lydia\u2019s last letter had prepared her for such a step. She had known, it appeared, that they had been in love for many weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I do not think so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think poorly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must acknowledge that he did not speak of Wickham as well as he had formerly. He considered him imprudent and extravagant; and since this unhappy business has happened, it is reported that he left Meryton deeply in debt: but I hope it is untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret, if we had told what we knew of him, this might never have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it might have been better,\u201d answered her sister.\\n\\n\u201cBut to reveal any person\u2019s former faults, without knowing what his present feelings might be, seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
