          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"addc6c9171b9cdf4ddf7ca58\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to relieve you a great deal. But what gave you such a start? What did you expect us to have found?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was in a tight spot\u2014the questioning eye was fixed on him. He would have given anything for the makings of a believable answer, but nothing came to him. The eye seemed to bore deeper and deeper. A foolish reply presented itself; there was no time to consider it, so he blurted it out weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too upset to smile, but the old man laughed loudly and happily, shaking every part of himself from head to foot. At last he said that a laugh like that was money in a man\u2019s pocket, because it reduced the doctor\u2019s bill tremendously. Then he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old fellow, you\u2019re pale and worn out\u2014you\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re a little confused and unsteady. But you\u2019ll get over it. Rest and sleep will set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed to think he had been such a fool and shown such suspicious excitement, for he had abandoned the idea that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the talk at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only believed it was not the treasure, though\u2014he had not known it for certain\u2014and so the mention of a captured bundle was too much for his composure. Still, he was glad the little incident had occurred, because now he knew beyond doubt that the bundle was not the bundle, and his mind was peaceful and very comfortable. Indeed, everything now seemed to be moving in exactly the right direction: the treasure must still be in No. 2, the men would be caught and jailed that day, and he and Tom could take the gold that night without trouble or fear of interruption.\\n\\nJust as breakfast ended, there was a knock at the door. Huck sprang toward a hiding place, for he had no wish to be connected, even remotely, with the recent affair. The Welshman let in several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas, and noticed groups of townspeople climbing the hill\u2014to look at the stile. The news had spread, then. The Welshman had to tell his visitors the story of the night. The widow openly expressed her gratitude for having been saved.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t say a word about it, madam. There\u2019s someone else you may owe more to than to me and my boys, but he won\u2019t let me tell his name. We would never have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
        }
