{"request_id":"9d8271f2b49c759b89d3b2d0","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, that is of no consequence! It gives me pleasure. I do not labour for money; I exist wholly for my art.’

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. upon the table, thanked the little German for his courtesy, and, after managing to refuse an invitation to meet certain Anarchists at a meat-tea on the next Saturday, quitted the house and made his way to the Park.

During the following two days he remained in a condition of intense excitement, and on Friday, at twelve o’clock, drove down to the Buckingham to await intelligence. Throughout the afternoon the impassive hall-porter continued to put up telegrams from different parts of the country, announcing the results of horse-races, decisions in divorce cases, the weather, and similar matters, while the tape clicked out tedious particulars of an all-night debate in the House of Commons and a slight panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur withdrew to the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to the great annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who wished to read the accounts of a speech he had made that morning at the Mansion House upon South African Missions and the desirability of black Bishops in every province, and who, for some unexplained reason, had a marked dislike of the _Evening News_. Yet none of the papers contained the faintest reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that the attempt had miscarried. It was a dreadful disappointment, and for a while he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, was profuse in elaborate apologies, and proposed to furnish another clock without charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost price. But Lord Arthur had lost all confidence in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything was so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite was scarcely obtainable in a pure state. The little German, nevertheless, though allowing that something must have gone amiss with the mechanism, still hoped the clock might yet explode, and cited the instance of a barometer he had once dispatched to the military Governor at Odessa which, although arranged to go off in ten days, had delayed for nearly three months. It was certainly true that, when it finally exploded, it had only blown a housemaid to pieces, the Governor having left town six weeks earlier; but it at least proved that dynamite, as a destructive power, was, when directed by machinery, a potent, if somewhat unpunctual, instrument."}

