          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d6e02d66d527b9d382025747\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For that reason, I decided to get down as soon as we reached the town, and place myself beyond his hearing. I carried out this plan successfully. My small portmanteau was in the boot beneath my feet; I had only to turn a hinge to take it out. I threw it down before me, climbed down after it, and found myself left at the first lamp, on the first paving stones of the town. The convicts went on with the coach, and I knew where they would be whisked away to the river. In my imagination, I saw the boat and its convict crew waiting at the slime-stained steps,\u2014heard again the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d as if spoken to dogs,\u2014and saw again the wicked Noah\u2019s Ark lying on the black water.\\n\\nI could not have said what I feared, for my fear was wholly vague and undefined; yet I was greatly afraid. As I walked toward the hotel, I felt a dread that made me tremble, far beyond any mere expectation of a painful or unpleasant recognition. I am sure it assumed no definite form, and was, for a few minutes, the return of childhood terror.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not only ordered my dinner there but had begun eating it before the waiter recognized me. As soon as he apologized for the failure of his memory, he asked whether he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was bound\u2014looked surprised, and at the first opportunity placed a dirty old local newspaper so plainly in my path that I picked it up and read this paragraph:\u2014\\n\\nOur readers will learn, not without some interest, in connection with the recent romantic improvement in the fortunes of a young ironworker from this district (what a subject, incidentally, for the magical pen of our not yet universally recognized fellow 