          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c77f3d597b27e1f954211cf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one; so far from harshly demanding what was owed him, he had voluntarily given it up, cast himself upon a world that offered no favour, made a place for himself in it, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the ruined, debt-burdened estate under written orders to spare the people, to give them whatever little could be given\u2014such fuel as the heavy creditors allowed in winter, and such produce as could be rescued from their grasp in summer\u2014and undoubtedly he had pleaded and proved this fact for his own protection, so that it must now be evident.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate decision Charles Darnay had begun to form: that he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor in the old tale, winds and currents had carried him within reach of the Loadstone Rock; it was drawing him toward itself, and he had to go. Everything that came before his mind drove him onward, faster and faster, more steadily and surely, toward that dreadful attraction. His hidden uneasiness had been that evil purposes were being carried out in his unhappy country by evil agents, while he, who could not help knowing himself better than they, was absent instead of trying to prevent bloodshed and uphold the claims of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness half suppressed and half accusing him, he had been led to compare himself sharply with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so powerful. That comparison, unfair to himself, had immediately been followed by Monseigneur's sneers, which had wounded him deeply, and by Stryver's, which were above all crude and bitter, for old reasons. Then had come Gabelle's letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, in danger of death, to his justice, honour, and good name.\\n\\nHis decision was made. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to sail on until he struck it. He knew of no rock; he saw scarcely any danger. The purpose with which he had done what he had done, though he had left it unfinished, appeared to him as something France would gratefully recognize when he came forward to claim it. Then that glorious vision of doing good, so often the hopeful mirage of good minds, rose before him; and in his illusion he even saw himself possessing some influence to guide this raging Revolution, which was running so terribly wild.\"}"
