          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"00ccaffe8fa02269644c189b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not determine whether the voice was that of a man or a woman. Could not understand what was spoken, though he supposed the language was Spanish. This witness described the condition of the chamber and the bodies as we described it yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor and a silversmith by occupation, states that he belonged to the party which first entered the house. In general, confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s evidence. The instant they had forced their way in, they shut the door again, to exclude the crowd, which had gathered with great rapidity despite the late hour. This witness believed the sharp voice to be Italian. He was certain it was not French. He could not say positively that it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He knew no Italian. Unable to distinguish the words, he was nevertheless persuaded from the intonation that the speaker was an Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter, and had often talked with both. He was certain the sharp voice belonged to neither of the dead women.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his evidence voluntarily. As he did not speak French, he was questioned through an interpreter. He is a native of Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the shrieks began. They continued several minutes\u2014perhaps ten. They were prolonged and loud\u2014terrible and agonizing. He was among those who entered the building. He confirmed the preceding testimony in every particular save one. He was certain the sharp voice was a man\u2019s\u2014a Frenchman\u2019s. He could not make out the words. They were loud, rapid, and irregular\u2014apparently uttered in terror as well as rage. The voice was harsh\u2014not so much shrill as harsh. He could not term it shrill. The rough voice repeated \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu._\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, banker, of the house of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. Is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye possessed some property. She had opened an account at his bank in the spring of the year\u2014(eight years before). She made frequent deposits of small amounts. She had drawn nothing until the third day before her death, when she personally withdrew 4000 francs. The money was paid in gold, and a clerk accompanied it to her home.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk of Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, at about noon, he went with Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence carrying the 4000 francs, contained in two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from his hands, while the old lady took charge of the other.\"}"
