          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4df09a19325d62aa53d611a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Oh, bother my hat! I want Virginia!\u2019 cried the little Duke, laughing, and they galloped on to the railway station. There Mr. Otis asked the station-master whether anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but he could learn nothing. The station-master, however, sent telegrams up and down the line and promised that a careful watch would be kept for her. Then, after buying the little Duke a hat from a linen-draper who was just closing his shop, Mr. Otis rode to Bexley, a village about four miles away. He had been told that gypsies often stayed there, because of the large common beside it.\\n\\nThere they woke the local policeman, but he could give them no information. After riding all over the common, they turned their horses toward home and reached the Chase at about eleven o\u2019clock, exhausted and nearly broken-hearted. Washington and the twins were waiting at the gate-house with lanterns, since the avenue was very dark. No trace at all of Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been caught in Brockley meadows, but she was not with them. They explained their sudden departure by saying they had mistaken the date of Chorton Fair and had hurried away because they feared arriving late. In fact, they were deeply upset to hear that Virginia had vanished, since they were grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of them had remained behind to help search.\\n\\nThe carp-pond had been dragged, and the entire Chase searched thoroughly, but nothing had been found. It was clear that, at least for that night, Virginia was lost to them. Mr. Otis and the boys walked sadly up to the house, while the groom followed with the two horses and the pony. In the hall they found a group of frightened servants. Poor Mrs. Otis lay on a sofa in the library, almost mad with fear and worry, while the old housekeeper bathed her forehead with eau-de-cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted that she eat something and ordered supper for everyone.\\n\\nIt was a sad meal, for hardly anyone spoke. Even the twins were quiet and shaken, because they loved their sister dearly. When they had finished, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s pleas, ordered everyone to bed. He said that nothing more could be done that night, and that in the morning he would telegraph Scotland Yard and ask for detectives to be sent at once.\"}"
