          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"55375c5ea0f3b90a66720e83\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With great exactness, my friend drove a peg into the earth at the very point where the beetle had dropped, and then drew a tape measure from his pocket. Securing one end at the point on the tree trunk nearest the peg, he extended it to the peg, and then continued it onward, in the line already fixed by the tree and peg, for fifty feet\u2014while Jupiter cut away the brambles with the scythe. At the place thus reached, a second peg was set, and around it, as a centre, a rough circle of some four feet in diameter was marked out. He then took a spade, handed one to Jupiter and another to me, and requested that we begin digging with all possible speed.\\n\\nIn truth, I had never felt any particular fondness for such employment, and at that moment I would gladly have excused myself; night was drawing near, and I was already much wearied by the exertion I had undergone. Yet I perceived no means of avoiding it, and feared to unsettle my poor friend's composure by refusing. Had I been able to count upon Jupiter's assistance, I should not have hesitated to try carrying the madman home by force; but I knew the old negro's character too well to imagine that, in any circumstances, he would aid me in a personal struggle against his master. I had no doubt that Legrand had caught one of the countless Southern superstitions concerning buried treasure, and that his fancy had been strengthened by the discovery of the _scarab\u00e6us_, or perhaps by Jupiter's stubborn insistence that it was \u201ca bug of real gold.\u201d A mind inclined to madness might easily be misled by such hints\u2014particularly when they accorded with cherished preconceptions\u2014and I remembered the poor man's remark that the beetle was \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d Altogether, I was sorely annoyed and perplexed; but at last I resolved to make the best of necessity, to dig heartily, and thereby sooner prove to the dreamer, through the evidence of his own eyes, the error of his beliefs.\\n\\nOnce the lanterns were lighted, we all set to work with a zeal deserving a more sensible object; and, as their brightness shone upon our figures and tools, I could not help reflecting what a picturesque party we made, and how odd and suspicious our work must have seemed to any stranger who might accidentally have come upon us.\\n\\nFor two hours we dug without pause.\"}"
