{"request_id":"4ca66e68029aa1093ea9793e","rewritten_text":"Each identifies it—not as the voice of someone from a nation whose language he knows, but the opposite. The Frenchman believes it was a Spaniard’s voice, and says he ‘might have made out some words had he known Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it was French; yet we are told that, ‘not understanding French, this witness was questioned through an interpreter.’ The Englishman takes it for the voice of a German, though he ‘does not understand German.’ The Spaniard is ‘certain’ it was an Englishman, but judges entirely by the intonation, ‘as he has no knowledge of English.’ The Italian thinks it was a Russian, but ‘has never spoken with a native of Russia.’ A second Frenchman also disagrees with the first, and is convinced the voice was Italian; yet, not knowing that language, he too, like the Spaniard, is ‘convinced by the intonation.’ How remarkably strange, then, must that voice truly have been, if testimony like this could be drawn from it! Even in its tones, the inhabitants of Europe’s five great regions could recognize nothing familiar. You may say it could have been the voice of an Asian—an African. Asians and Africans are not common in Paris; but, without rejecting that conclusion, I will merely direct your attention to three points. One witness calls the voice ‘harsh rather than shrill.’ Two others describe it as ‘quick and uneven.’ No witness mentioned any words—or any sounds resembling words—as distinguishable.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what impression I have so far made upon your mind; but I do not hesitate to say that sound deductions from even this part of the testimony—the part concerning the rough and shrill voices—are enough in themselves to create a suspicion that should guide every further step in investigating this mystery. I said ‘sound deductions,’ but that does not fully express my meaning. I meant to suggest that these deductions are the only proper ones, and that the suspicion follows from them inevitably, as their sole result. What that suspicion is, however, I will not yet say. I only want you to remember that, for me, it was strong enough to give my inquiries in the chamber a definite shape—a particular direction.

“Let us now imagine ourselves in this chamber. What should we first look for? The means by which the murderers escaped. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events.”"}

