          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"700ec05bf50a0f8957e0bb22\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is a strange matter, but everyone who vanishes is reported to have been seen in San Francisco. It must be a charming city, with all the attractions of the world to come.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you suppose has become of Basil?\u201d asked Dorian, raising his Burgundy to the light and marvelling that he could speak of it so quietly.\\n\\n\u201cI have not the faintest notion. If Basil has decided to conceal himself, that is no concern of mine. If he is dead, I have no wish to think of him. Death is the one thing that frightens me. I detest it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d said the younger man listlessly.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, holding the gilded lattice of an open vinaigrette case beneath his nostrils, \u201cnowadays one can outlive everything but that. Death and vulgarity are the only two realities of the nineteenth century that cannot be explained away. Let us take our coffee in the music-room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran off with played Chopin superbly. Poor Victoria! I was exceedingly fond of her. The house is somewhat solitary without her. Naturally, married life is only a habit\u2014a bad one. Yet one regrets losing even one\u2019s most disagreeable habits. Perhaps one regrets those most of all. They form so essential a part of one\u2019s personality.\u201d\\n\\nDorian made no reply, but got up from the table and, going into the adjoining room, seated himself at the piano, allowing his fingers to wander over the black and white ivory keys. When the coffee had been served, he ceased playing and, glancing towards Lord Henry, said, \u201cHarry, has it ever crossed your mind that Basil was murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was extremely popular, and he always wore a Waterbury watch. Why should anyone have murdered him? He was not intelligent enough to possess enemies. Certainly, he had a marvellous gift for painting. But a man may paint like Velasquez and still be as dull as can be. Basil was, in truth, rather dull. He interested me only once, when he told me years ago that he adored you wildly and that you were the ruling motive of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was very fond of Basil,\u201d said Dorian, with sadness in his voice. \u201cBut do not people say he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, a few newspapers do. It seems to me wholly improbable. I know Paris has dreadful places, but Basil was not the kind of man who would have visited them. He had no curiosity. That was his principal defect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry, if I told you that I murdered Basil?\u201d said the younger man.\"}"
