{"request_id":"2188748fab90d103df3a45c1","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove took the first chance she had of being alone with Anne to say, “Oh, Miss Anne, I cannot help wishing Mrs Charles had a little of your way with those children. They are completely different creatures with you! But of course, generally speaking, they are so spoiled! It is a pity you cannot show your sister how to manage them. They are as fine, healthy children as ever were seen, poor little things, without any partiality; but Mrs Charles knows no more about how they ought to be treated—! Good heavens, how troublesome they can be sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I believe Mrs Charles is not quite pleased that I do not invite them more often; but you know it is very unpleasant to have children with you whom you must be correcting every moment—‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that’—or whom you can only keep in tolerable order by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

She also received this report from Mary. “Mrs Musgrove thinks all her servants are so respectable that it would be downright treason to question them; but I am sure, without exaggerating, that her upper housemaid and laundry-maid, instead of attending to their work, are wandering about the village all day long. I meet them everywhere I go; and I declare, I never go into my nursery twice without seeing one of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to ruin her; for she tells me they are always tempting her to go walking with them.” And from Mrs Musgrove’s side came this: “I make it a rule never to interfere in any of my daughters-in-law’s affairs, because I know it would not do; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, because you may be able to put things right, that I have no very good opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear strange stories about her; she is always out and about; and from my own knowledge, I can say she is such a dressy young woman that she is enough to ruin any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles swears by her, I know; but I only give you this hint so that you may keep watch, because if you see anything wrong, you need not be afraid to mention it.”

Again, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was very apt not to give her the precedence due to her when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she could see no reason why she should be considered so much at home there that she had to give up her place."}

