          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"144706113969be8025182427\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried to the skies on flowery beds of ease,\\n\\nWhile others fight to win the prize and sail through bloody seas?\\n\\nHe was thought to be a wonderful reader. At church socials, he was always asked to read poetry; and when he finished, the ladies would raise their hands and let them drop helplessly into their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn, the Rev. Mr. Sprague became like a bulletin board and read out notices of meetings, societies, and other things until it seemed the list would last until the end of the world\u2014a strange custom still kept in America, even in cities, in this age of plentiful newspapers. Often, the less reason there is for keeping an old custom, the harder it is to get rid of it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a good, generous prayer, full of details: he prayed for the church and its little children; for the other churches in the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for State officers; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for Government officers; for poor sailors tossed on stormy seas; for the oppressed millions suffering under European monarchies and Eastern despots; for those who had the light and the good news but still had no eyes to see or ears to hear; for the heathen on faraway islands; and he ended by asking that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, like seed planted in rich soil, and in time produce a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustle of dresses, and the congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely put up with it\u2014if he even did that much. He was restless throughout. Without meaning to, he kept count of its details\u2014not because he was listening, but because he knew the ground so well and knew the clergyman\u2019s usual path through it. Whenever a small bit of new material was added, his ear caught it and his whole nature objected; he thought such additions unfair and mean. In the middle of the prayer, a fly landed on the back of the pew in front of him and tormented him by calmly rubbing its hands together, wrapping its arms around its head, and polishing it so hard that its head seemed almost to come off, exposing its thin neck. It scraped its wings with its hind legs and smoothed them against its body as if they were coat tails, going through its entire toilet as peacefully as if it knew it was perfectly safe.\"}"
