          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e8614ace61e92c4ebc14b9c2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted a spirited horse, the one that had carried him toward victory in Greece. He wore himself out with exhausting exercise, drowning the pain of his troubled mind in physical sensation.\\n\\nSlowly he regained control of himself. At last, as if recovering from poison, he raised his head above the mists of fever and passion into the quiet air of calm thought. He considered what he should do. First, he was struck by how much time had passed while madness, rather than reason, had guided his actions. A month had gone by, and during that time he had not seen Evadne. Her hold on him, connected as it was to so few of the lasting feelings of his heart, had greatly weakened. He was no longer her slave, no longer her lover. He would never see her again, and by fully returning to himself, earn Perdita's trust.\\n\\nYet as he made this decision, his imagination called up the miserable home of the Greek girl\u2014a home she had refused, from noble and lofty principle, to exchange for one more luxurious. He remembered the splendour of her position and appearance when he first knew her. He remembered her life in Constantinople, surrounded by every kind of eastern magnificence; then her present poverty, her daily labour, her lonely condition, her faded cheek marked by hunger. Compassion filled his heart. He would see her once more. He would find some way to restore her to society and to the comforts of her rank; then their separation would naturally follow.\\n\\nAgain he remembered how, throughout that long month, he had avoided Perdita, fleeing from her as from the sting of his own conscience. But he was awake now. All this would be repaired, and future devotion would erase the memory of this single stain on the peace of their life. He grew cheerful at the thought, and calmly and firmly planned the course he would take. He remembered that he had promised Perdita to attend, that very evening, a festival held in his honour\u2014the nineteenth of October, the anniversary of his election as Protector. Let this festival be a good sign for the happiness of the years to come. First he would call on Evadne; he would not remain long, but he owed her some explanation, some compensation for his long and unexplained absence. Then he would return to Perdita, to the forgotten world, to the duties of society, the splendour of rank, and the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the scene described in the preceding pages, Perdita had expected a complete change in Raymond's manner and conduct. She looked for open communication and a return to the affectionate habits that had once been the joy of her life.\"}"
