          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0efef610bbef0f31de68063e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Indeed, he was attired for the part of \u2018Jonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u2019 one of his most striking impersonations, and one the Cantervilles had ample cause to recall, since it had been the true beginning of their dispute with their neighbour, Lord Rufford. It was about a quarter past two in the morning, and, so far as he could tell, nobody was awake. But as he wandered towards the library to discover whether any sign of the blood-stain remained, two figures suddenly sprang upon him from a shadowy corner, flung their arms wildly over their heads, and screamed \u2018BOO!\u2019 into his ear.\\n\\nOvercome by a terror that was, in the circumstances, perfectly natural, he fled towards the staircase, only to find Washington Otis stationed there with the large garden-syringe; and so, surrounded by his foes on every side and nearly brought to bay, he disappeared into the great iron stove, which luckily was unlit, and was obliged to reach his home by way of the flues and chimneys, arriving in his own room in a dreadful condition of filth, confusion, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was seen no more upon nocturnal adventures. On several occasions the twins waited in ambush for him, and every night scattered nutshells through the passages, much to the annoyance of their parents and the servants, yet all in vain. It was plain that his feelings had been so deeply hurt that he would not show himself. Mr. Otis therefore returned to his great history of the Democratic Party, upon which he had laboured for several years; Mrs. Otis arranged a magnificent clam-bake, astonishing the entire county; the boys devoted themselves to lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games; and Virginia rode through the lanes on her pony, attended by the young Duke of Cheshire, who had come to Canterville Chase for the final week of his holidays. It was commonly supposed that the ghost had departed, and Mr. Otis did, in fact, write to Lord Canterville to say so; in answer, his lordship declared his great delight at the tidings and sent his warmest congratulations to the Minister\u2019s estimable wife.\\n\\nThe Otises, however, were mistaken, for the ghost remained in the house, and though he was now almost an invalid, he was far from willing to leave matters alone, especially when he learned that the guests included the young Duke of Cheshire, whose grand-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost, and had been discovered next morning on the card-room floor in so helpless a paralytic condition that, although he lived to a very advanced age, he could never afterward say anything except \u2018Double Sixes.\u2019\"}"
