          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bf58e34642b2d108665aaac6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7th. I rose early and, to my great joy, at last saw what could not possibly be mistaken for anything but the North Pole itself. There it was, beyond doubt, directly beneath my feet; but, alas, I had now risen to such an immense height that nothing could be distinguished with precision. Indeed, judging from the progression of the figures marking my various altitudes at different times between six A.M. on April second and twenty minutes before nine A.M. that same day, when the barometer failed, it might reasonably be concluded that, at four o\u2019clock on the morning of April seventh, the balloon had reached an altitude of certainly no less than 7,254 miles above sea level. This height may seem enormous, yet the calculation on which it rests probably produced a result far below the truth. In any case, I unquestionably saw the earth\u2019s entire major diameter; the whole northern hemisphere lay below me like an orthographic map, and the great circle of the equator itself formed the boundary of my horizon. Your Excellencies may readily imagine, however, that the narrow regions still unexplored within the Arctic Circle, though directly beneath me and thus viewed without any apparent foreshortening, were nevertheless comparatively too small and too distant from my point of view to permit any very exact examination. Still, what I could see was strange and stirring. Northward from that vast rim already mentioned\u2014which, with slight qualification, may be called the boundary of human discovery in these regions\u2014there stretched one unbroken, or nearly unbroken, sheet of ice. In the first few degrees of its extent, the surface was noticeably flattened; farther on, it sank into a plane; and finally, becoming distinctly concave, it ended at the Pole itself in a sharply defined circular center. Its apparent diameter subtended an angle of about sixty-five seconds at the balloon, and its dusky color, varying in depth, was always darker than any other spot on the visible hemisphere, sometimes deepening into the most complete and impenetrable blackness. Beyond this, little could be determined. By twelve o\u2019clock the circular center had greatly diminished in circumference, and by seven P.M. I lost sight of it altogether, the balloon having passed over the western edge of the ice and drifted rapidly toward the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8th. I observed a noticeable decrease in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a considerable change in its general color and appearance.\"}"
