{"request_id":"b3f92ed15b8f19976b85ddb1","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Those who committed the crime were physical beings, and they escaped by physical means. But how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason through this question, and it must bring us to a definite conclusion. Let us consider, one by one, every possible means of escape. Clearly, when the party came up the stairs, the murderers were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the adjoining room. Therefore, we need look for exits only from these two rooms. The police had stripped the floors, ceilings, and wall masonry in every direction. No secret passage could have escaped their attention. Yet, not trusting their eyes, I examined them myself. There were no secret exits. Both doors leading from the rooms into the hallway were firmly locked, with the keys on the inside. Let us consider the chimneys. Though they are of ordinary width for eight or ten feet above the hearths, they would not admit the body of a large cat for their entire length. Since escape by the means already mentioned is absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have escaped through those of the front room without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must therefore have gone through the windows of the back room. Having reached this conclusion so unmistakably, we must not reject it merely because it seems impossible. We need only prove that these apparent ‘impossibilities’ are not truly impossible.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One is unobstructed by furniture and fully visible. The lower part of the other is concealed by the head of the massive bedstead, which has been pushed close against it. The first was found securely fastened from within. It resisted the greatest force of those who tried to raise it. A large hole had been bored into the frame on the left, and a very stout nail was fitted into it almost to its head. On examining the other window, they found a similar nail fitted in the same way; and a strong attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were then completely satisfied that no escape had occurred by these routes. Therefore, they considered it unnecessary to remove the nails and open the windows.

“My own examination was more thorough, for the reason I have just stated: I knew that here, above all, every apparent impossibility had to be shown not to be impossible in fact.”"}

