          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0d51d3cf37a7eddcbd61ac1a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were taking place at the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the same one from which the girl had taken Oliver\u2014thinking beside a dull, smoky fire. A pair of bellows lay across his knees, and he seemed to have been trying to make the fire burn more brightly; but he had sunk into deep thought. With his arms folded over the bellows and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared absently at the rusty grate.\\n\\nAt a table behind him sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all busy with a game of whist. The Artful was playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. His face, always unusually sharp and clever-looking, became even more interesting as he watched the game closely and studied Mr. Chitling's hand. From time to time, when the chance arose, he gave the cards earnest looks, wisely guiding his own play by what he observed in his neighbour's hand. As it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat, as he often did indoors. He also held a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he thought it necessary to refresh himself from the quart pot on the table, which stood filled with gin-and-water for the company.\\n\\nMaster Bates also watched the game carefully; but, being more excitable than his skilled friend, he drank the gin-and-water more often, and made many jokes and irrelevant remarks, all quite unsuitable during a serious game. Indeed, the Artful, trusting in their close friendship, more than once solemnly lectured his companion about these faults. Master Bates took every warning very good-naturedly, merely telling his friend to be \\\"blowed,\\\" or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly phrased joke of the same sort. Mr. Chitling greatly admired the clever way these remarks were made. It was noticeable that he and his partner always lost; yet this did not anger Master Bates at all. On the contrary, it seemed to amuse him enormously, for at the end of every deal he laughed loudly and declared he had never seen such a jolly game in all his life.\\n\\n\\\"That's two doubles and the rub,\\\" said Mr. Chitling, looking very gloomy as he took half-a-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \\\"I never saw such a fellow as you, Jack; you win everything.\\\"\"}"
