          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a3ddd65535d8d3b643235cfb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how was I, a poor bewildered country boy, to escape that marvellous inconsistency into which the best and wisest men fall every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be perfectly true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her immensely.\u201d\\n\\nIn brief, when I reached that point, I flung myself face downward, seized the hair at either side of my head, and tugged it hard. Yet all the time I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so wildly mad and so ill-directed, that I was fully aware my face would have deserved it if I had raised it by my hair and dashed it against the pebbles, in punishment for its belonging to such a fool.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and attempted no further argument with me. She laid her hand\u2014comfortable, though made rough by labour\u2014upon mine, first one and then the other, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she lightly stroked my shoulder in a calming manner, while I cried a little with my face on my sleeve,\u2014just as I had cried in the brewery yard,\u2014and felt dimly persuaded that somebody, or everybody, had treated me very badly; which, I cannot say.\\n\\n\u201cI am glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cwhich is that you have felt able to trust me with your confidence, Pip. And I am glad of another, which is that you know, of course, you can rely on my keeping it, and always being as worthy of it as I can. If your first teacher (dear me! so poor a one, and herself so greatly needing instruction!) were your teacher now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a difficult lesson to learn, and you have passed beyond her, and it is useless now.\u201d Then, sighing quietly for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a newly cheerful and agreeable voice, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm about her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI will always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be one, so that means always. Not that I need tell you anything, because you know all that I know,\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy, almost in a whisper, looking away toward the ships. Then, returning to her former pleasant tone, she repeated, \u201cshall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy that we would walk a little farther, and we did; and the summer afternoon softened into the summer evening, and it was very lovely.\"}"
