          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6ce64b53ff71e233bf3e0923\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For that reason, I decided to get down as soon as we reached the town, and put myself beyond his hearing. I managed this successfully. My small portmanteau was in the boot beneath my feet; I only had to turn a hinge to take it out. I threw it down before me, climbed down after it, and found myself left at the first lamp on the first paving stones of the town. The convicts went on with the coach, and I knew where they would be taken away to the river. In my mind, I saw the boat and its convict crew waiting at the slime-covered steps,\u2014heard again the rough cry, \u201cGive way, you!\u201d as if spoken to dogs,\u2014and saw again the wicked Noah\u2019s Ark lying on the black water.\\n\\nI could not have said what I feared, for my fear was entirely vague and shapeless, but it was very strong. As I walked toward the hotel, I felt a dread that made me tremble, far greater than any simple fear of a painful or unpleasant recognition. I am sure it had no clear form, and was, for a few minutes, the return of childhood terror.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had ordered my dinner and begun eating it before the waiter recognised me. As soon as he apologised for his poor memory, he asked whether he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day when I was bound\u2014looked surprised. At the first chance, he placed a dirty old copy of a local newspaper so plainly in my path that I picked it up and read this paragraph:\u2014\\n\\nOur readers will learn, not without some interest, in connection with the recent remarkable rise in fortune of a young ironworker from this neighbourhood (what a subject, by the way, for the magical pen of our not yet universally recognised townsman TOOBY, poet of our columns!), that the young man\u2019s earliest patron, companion, and friend was a highly respected person closely connected with the corn and seed trade, whose notably convenient and spacious business premises stand within a hundred miles of the High Street. It is not without personal feeling that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is pleasing to know that our town produced the founder of the latter\u2019s fortune.\"}"
