          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"608bff96991c491d4f3894a7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief conversation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma great pleasure. It was one of the pleasant memories of the ball that she enjoyed while walking about the lawn the next morning. She was delighted that they had reached such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so alike; and his praise of Harriet, his yielding in her favour, was especially gratifying. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which for a few minutes had threatened to spoil the rest of her evening, had led to some of its greatest satisfactions; and she looked forward to another happy consequence\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From Harriet\u2019s way of speaking about the incident before they left the ballroom, she had strong hopes. It seemed as though her eyes had suddenly been opened, and she could see that Mr. Elton was not the superior being she had believed him to be. The fever was over, and Emma had little reason to fear that her pulse would be quickened again by harmful civility. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 unkind feelings to provide all the instruction of deliberate neglect that might still be needed. Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley not inclined to quarrel with her\u2014what a happy summer lay ahead!\\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not permit himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he had to be home by midday. She did not regret it.\\n\\nHaving settled all these matters, considered them, and put them in order, she was just turning toward the house, her spirits refreshed for the demands of the two little boys and their grandfather, when the great iron carriage gate opened, and two people entered whom she could not have less expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! A moment was enough to convince her that something extraordinary had happened. Harriet looked pale and frightened, and he was trying to comfort her. The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart; all three were soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be revived; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such events are highly interesting, but the suspense cannot last long. Within a few minutes, Emma knew everything.\\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone out walking together and taken the Richmond road, which, though it seemed public enough to be safe, had caused them alarm. About half a mile beyond Highbury, it made a sudden turn and, deeply shaded by elms on either side, became quite secluded for a considerable distance; and when the young ladies had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead of them, a party of gypsies on a wider patch of grass at the roadside.\"}"
        }
      ],
