{"request_id":"f48f12c63c07312ff8ae0b15","rewritten_text":"It seemed to me that this quaint lie was told in the simplest and most beautiful way; but then I had heard it only once, and that makes a difference. No doubt the others enjoyed it when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to wake, and he soon roused the rest with a practical joke of remarkably poor quality. He tied metal mugs to a dog's tail and let him loose. The dog tore wildly around the place in a frenzy of terror, while all the other dogs howled after him, banging and crashing into everything in their path, creating a chaos of confusion and a deafening uproar. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces, and some tumbled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in ecstasy. They were exactly like a pack of children. Sir Dinadan was so proud of his feat that he could not stop explaining, over and over until everyone was weary, how the immortal idea had occurred to him; and, as humorists of his kind always do, he was still laughing after everyone else had finished. He was so pleased with himself that he decided to make a speech—naturally, a humorous speech. I do not think I had ever heard so many stale, worn-out jokes strung together in my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It seemed especially sad to sit there, thirteen hundred years before I was born, listening again to poor, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me dry gripes as a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me that no new joke is possible. Everyone laughed at these antiques—but they always do; I had noticed that centuries afterward. Of course, the scoffer did not laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there was nothing he would not scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan's jokes were rotten, and the rest were petrified. I said “petrified” was good, because I myself believed that the only proper way to classify the majestic ages of some of those jokes was by geologic periods. But that clever thought struck the boy in an empty spot, since geology had not yet been invented. Still, I made a note of the remark and planned to educate the commonwealth up to it if I survived. There is no sense in throwing away a good thing merely because the market is not ready for it.

Now Sir Kay rose and began to crank up his history mill, with me as fuel."}

