          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ea9810904e6619551a56a1b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Otis has also told me\u2014though I should point out that she is no slight authority on Art, having spent several winters in Boston as a girl\u2014that these jewels are worth a great deal of money, and would bring a high price if sold. In these circumstances, Lord Canterville, I am sure you will understand how impossible it would be for me to let them remain in the hands of any member of my family. Indeed, all such frivolous ornaments and trinkets, however appropriate or essential they may be to the dignity of the British aristocracy, would be entirely out of place among people raised on the stern, and, I believe, everlasting principles of republican simplicity. Perhaps I should add that Virginia is very eager for you to let her keep the box as a memento of your unfortunate, though misguided, ancestor. Since it is extremely old and therefore rather damaged, you may perhaps be willing to grant her request. For my own part, I admit I am somewhat astonished to find one of my children showing sympathy for medievalism in any form, and can explain it only by the fact that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs soon after Mrs. Otis returned from a journey to Athens.\u2019\\n\\nLord Canterville listened with great seriousness to the worthy Minister\u2019s speech, occasionally tugging at his grey moustache to conceal an unwilling smile. When Mr. Otis had finished, he warmly shook his hand and said, \u2018My dear sir, your delightful little daughter did my unfortunate ancestor, Sir Simon, a most important service, and my family and I owe her a great debt for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels are plainly hers; and, by Jove, I believe that if I were cruel enough to take them from her, the wicked old man would be out of his grave within two weeks and make my life a perfect misery. As for their being family heirlooms, nothing is an heirloom unless it is named as such in a will or legal document, and these jewels were entirely unknown to us. I assure you, I have no more right to them than your butler; and when Miss Virginia is grown, I dare say she will be glad to have beautiful things to wear. Besides, Mr. Otis, you forget that you bought the furniture and the ghost at an agreed valuation, and everything belonging to the ghost immediately became your property. For, whatever activity Sir Simon may have displayed in the corridor at night, legally speaking he was quite dead, and you acquired his possessions by purchase.\u2019\"}"
