{"request_id":"200098b7870018ae406c7927","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was whom he should kill; for he understood that murder, like the religions of the ancient pagan world, needed both a victim and a priest. Since he was not a genius, he had no enemies, and he felt that this was not the moment to satisfy any personal grudge or dislike. The task he had undertaken was one of great and serious importance. So he made a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of paper and, after careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and since he was himself very rich, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, he could gain no vulgar financial benefit from her death. Indeed, the more he considered it, the more she seemed exactly the right person; and, feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to make his plans at once.

The first thing, naturally, was to settle matters with the palm-reader. He sat at a small Sheraton writing table by the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, put it in an envelope, and told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her. He would keep the secret of his sacrifice hidden in his heart forever.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with lovely white petals and staring pheasant’s eyes. When he reached the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on toxicology. He had firmly decided that poison was the best method for this difficult business. Any kind of personal violence was deeply unpleasant to him, and besides, he was anxious not to kill Lady Clementina in a way that would attract public attention. He hated the thought of being made a celebrity at Lady Windermere’s, or of seeing his name appear in the gossip columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

