          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"104e8f7e31ae27de692a0e4d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don't rush at him suddenly and risk making a clumsy, half-finished job of it. No, I move far off to one side and work toward him slowly, so he never suspects I mean to strike at all. Then, before he knows it, he is flat on his back and cannot, for the life of him, tell how it happened. That was how I handled Brother Dowley. I began talking in an easy, lazy way, as if I were only passing the time; and the oldest man alive could not have guessed from where I started where I meant to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, when you look into it, there are a great many curious things about law, custom, usage, and such matters; and about the course and progress of human opinion and action, too. There are written laws\u2014they perish; but there are unwritten laws\u2014they last forever. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says they must rise, little by little, through the centuries. Notice how it works. We know what wages are now, here and there; we take an average and call that the wages of today. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and two hundred years ago. That is as far back as we can go, but it is enough to show us the law of progress, the amount and rate of the regular increase. So, without a single document to help us, we can come pretty close to finding what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward, turn around, and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people's wages will be on any future date you want to know, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years, wages in your region will have risen to six times what they are now, and farm hands will be allowed 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I might die now and live then!\u201d Smug the wheelwright broke in, with a fine greedy glow in his eye.\\n\\