          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"39cf3b3c267e99b9e5c6c6e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I wish to see you securely connected for life, and for that purpose it will be best to have as few unsuitable acquaintances as possible; and therefore I say, that if you are still in this neighbourhood when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your intimacy with the sisters will not lead you into knowing the wife, who will most likely be merely a farmer\u2019s daughter, without education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure. Yes. Though I do not suppose Mr. Martin would ever marry anybody who had not received some education\u2014and been very properly brought up. Still, I do not mean to place my opinion against yours\u2014and I am certain I shall not desire his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I shall always feel great regard for the Miss Martins, Elizabeth especially, and should be very sorry to give them up, for they are quite as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then certainly I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma observed her through all the variations of this speech, and detected no alarming sign of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but she trusted he had no further influence, and that there would be no serious obstacle, on Harriet\u2019s part, to any friendly scheme of her own.\\n\\nThey encountered Mr. Martin the following day, while walking along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and, after looking at her with great respect, turned to her companion with the most genuine pleasure. Emma was not displeased to have such a chance for inspection; and, walking a few yards ahead while they spoke together, her quick eye soon became sufficiently familiar with Mr. Robert Martin. He was very neatly dressed, and had the look of a sensible young man, but his person possessed no other advantage; and, when compared with gentlemen, she thought he must forfeit all the ground he had won in Harriet\u2019s favour. Harriet was not indifferent to manner; she had of her own accord remarked her father\u2019s gentleness with both admiration and surprise. Mr. Martin appeared not to know what manner meant.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse was not to be kept waiting; and Harriet then ran up to her with a smiling countenance and a flutter of spirits which Miss Woodhouse hoped soon to calm.\\n\\n\u201cOnly imagine our meeting him!\u2014How very strange! It was entirely by chance, he said, that he had not gone round by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked this way. He believed we walked towards Randalls on most days.\u201d\"}"
