          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"96337fdce5382e7f31d9896e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Well, monsieur le mauvais sujet,\u2019 cried the old lady as he came into the room, \u2018why have you not come to see me all this time?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Lady Clem, I never have a moment to myself,\u2019 said Lord Arthur, smiling.\\n\\n\u2018I suppose you mean you spend all day with Miss Sybil Merton, buying chiffons and talking nonsense? I cannot understand why people make such a fuss about marriage. In my day, we never thought of billing and cooing in public\u2014or in private, for that matter.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I assure you, Lady Clem, I have not seen Sybil for twenty-four hours. As far as I can tell, she belongs entirely to her milliners.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Of course; that is the only reason you come to see an ugly old woman like me. I wonder you men do not take warning. On a fait des folies pour moi, and here I am: a poor rheumatic creature, with a false front and a bad temper. Why, if it were not for dear Lady Jansen, who sends me the worst French novels she can find, I do not think I could get through the day. Doctors are completely useless, except for taking one\u2019s fees. They cannot even cure my heartburn.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I have brought you a cure for that, Lady Clem,\u2019 said Lord Arthur seriously. \u2018It is a wonderful thing, invented by an American.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I do not think I like American inventions, Arthur. I am quite sure I do not. I read some American novels lately, and they were utterly nonsensical.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, but there is no nonsense about this, Lady Clem! I assure you, it is a perfect cure. You must promise to try it.\u2019 Lord Arthur took the little box from his pocket and handed it to her.\\n\\n\u2018Well, the box is charming, Arthur. Is it really a present? That is very sweet of you. And is this the wonderful medicine? It looks like a bonbon. I shall take it at once.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Good heavens, Lady Clem!\u2019 cried Lord Arthur, catching her hand. \u2018You must not do anything of the kind. It is a homoeopathic medicine, and if you take it when you do not have heartburn, it might do you a great deal of harm. Wait until you have an attack, and take it then. You will be astonished by the result.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I should like to take it now,\u2019 said Lady Clementina, holding the little transparent capsule up to the light, its bubble of liquid aconitine floating inside. \u2018I am sure it is delicious. The truth is, though I hate doctors, I love medicines. Still, I shall keep it until my next attack.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018And when will that be?\u2019 asked Lord Arthur eagerly.\"}"
