          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5bcae59f2fa3b000155cfdc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew looked at him in vain as he went on toward the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI look forward to the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Show Monsieur my nephew to his room there!\u2014And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you please,\u201d he added to himself, before ringing his little bell again and summoning his valet to his own bedroom.\\n\\nWhen the valet had come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis paced back and forth in his loose dressing gown, gently preparing himself for sleep on that hot, still night. As he rustled about the room, his soft-slippered feet making no sound on the floor, he moved like a polished tiger\u2014looking like some enchanted marquis of the unrepentantly wicked kind in a story, whose regular transformation into a tiger was either just ending or just beginning.\\n\\nHe crossed his luxurious bedroom from end to end, again considering the fragments of the day\u2019s journey that returned unbidden to his mind: the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the setting sun, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the road-mender in his blue cap pointing out the chain beneath the carriage. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain: the small bundle lying on the step, the women bent over it, and the tall man with raised arms crying, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am calm now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis, \u201cand may go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nSo, leaving only one light burning on the great hearth, he drew his thin gauze curtains around him and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he settled himself to sleep.\\n\\nThe stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly into the black night for three heavy hours; for three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled at their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound bearing very little resemblance to the sound conventionally assigned to owls by poetical men. Yet such creatures have the stubborn habit of hardly ever saying what is written down for them.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human alike, stared blindly into the night. Dead darkness covered the whole landscape; dead darkness added its own silence to the silencing dust on every road. The burial ground had reached the point where its small mounds of poor grass could not be distinguished from one another; the figure on the Cross might have come down, for all that could be seen of it. In the village, tax collectors and taxed alike slept deeply. Perhaps dreaming of feasts, as the starving usually do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and yoked ox may, its thin inhabitants slept soundly, fed and free.\"}"
