          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d9eacb58d5c5a74e9b051e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Oh, confound my hat! I want Virginia!\u2019 exclaimed the little Duke, laughing, and they rode at full speed towards the railway station. There Mr. Otis asked the station-master whether anyone resembling Virginia had been noticed upon the platform, but could learn nothing of her. The station-master, however, telegraphed along the line in both directions, and promised that a careful watch should be maintained; and, after purchasing a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper who was in the act of closing his shutters, Mr. Otis set off for Bexley, a village some four miles distant, which he had been told was a favourite resort of the gypsies, owing to the large common beside it. There they woke the country policeman, but obtained no information from him; and, after riding over the whole common, they turned homeward, arriving at the Chase at about eleven, utterly weary and nearly broken-hearted. Washington and the twins were waiting at the gate-house with lanterns, for the avenue was exceedingly dark. No smallest clue to Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been stopped in Brockley meadows, but she was not among them; they accounted for their hasty departure by declaring that they had confused the date of Chorton Fair, and had hurried away lest they should arrive too late. In truth, they had been much troubled on hearing that Virginia was missing, being deeply obliged to Mr. Otis for permitting them to camp in his park, and four of them had remained behind to assist in the search. The carp-pond had been dragged, and every part of the Chase carefully searched, yet with no success. It was plain that, at least for that night, Virginia was lost to them; and Mr. Otis and the boys went slowly up to the house in the profoundest gloom, while the groom followed with the two horses and the pony. In the hall they encountered a cluster of terrified servants, while poor Mrs. Otis lay upon a sofa in the library, almost distracted by fear and anxiety, her forehead being bathed with eau-de-cologne by the old housekeeper. Mr. Otis immediately insisted that she should take some food, and had supper brought up for everyone. It was a sorrowful meal, for scarcely anyone spoke; even the twins were hushed and overawed, since they loved their sister dearly. When it was over, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s entreaties, sent them all to bed, declaring that nothing further could be done that night, and that in the morning he would telegraph Scotland Yard to send detectives down at once.\"}"
