          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"19d958d7f6b91456290af10c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my youth, discontent never entered my mind, and if I was ever overcome by boredom, the beauty of nature or the study of the finest and most sublime works of man could always stir my heart and lift my spirits. But I am a ruined tree; the lightning has struck my soul. I felt then that I would live on only to show what I would soon no longer be\u2014a miserable wreck of humanity, pitied by others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a long time at Oxford, wandering through the surrounding country and trying to find every place connected with the most inspiring period of English history. Our small journeys of discovery were often extended by one interesting sight after another. We visited the tomb of the famous Hampden and the field where that patriot died. For a moment, my soul rose above its degrading and miserable fears as I considered the noble ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice that these places recalled. For an instant I dared to cast off my chains and look about me with a free and elevated spirit; but the iron had sunk into my flesh, and I fell again, trembling and hopeless, back into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford reluctantly and went on to Matlock, our next resting place. The countryside around this village resembled Switzerland more than any we had seen, though everything was smaller, and the green hills lacked the distant white Alps that crown the pine-covered mountains of my native land. We visited the remarkable cave and the small natural-history collections, where curiosities were arranged much as they were in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. When Henry spoke the latter name, I trembled, and I hurried to leave Matlock, since it had become linked with that terrible scene.\\n\\nFrom Derby, continuing north, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine myself again among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow still lying on the northern slopes, the lakes, and the rush of rocky streams were all familiar and dear to me. There, too, we made several acquaintances who nearly succeeded in making me happy. Clerval's pleasure was far greater than mine; his mind grew in the company of talented men, and he discovered in himself greater abilities and resources than he had imagined possible while he kept company with those beneath him.\"}"
