          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e22c5a69790204c961024ca4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI regard it as one of the earliest refinements of civilized society.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, sir; and it also has the advantage of being fashionable among the less civilized societies of the world: every savage can dance.\u201d\\n\\nSir William merely smiled. \u201cYour friend dances delightfully,\u201d he continued after a pause, seeing Bingley join the group; \u201cand I have no doubt that you are skilled in the art yourself, Mr. Darcy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou saw me dance at Meryton, I believe, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, indeed, and I took no small pleasure in the sight. Do you often dance at St. James\u2019s?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you not think it would be a fitting compliment to the place?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is a compliment I never pay to any place if I can help it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have a house in town, I presume?\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy bowed.\\n\\n\u201cI once considered settling in town myself, as I am fond of distinguished society; but I was not quite certain that London air would suit Lady Lucas.\u201d\\n\\nHe paused, hoping for an answer, but his companion was not inclined to offer one. At that moment Elizabeth moved toward them, and he was seized by the idea of doing something very gallant. He called to her,--\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Eliza, why are you not dancing? Mr. Darcy, you must permit me to introduce this young lady to you as a most desirable partner. Surely you cannot refuse to dance when such beauty stands before you.\u201d Taking her hand, he would have placed it in Mr. Darcy\u2019s, who, though greatly surprised, was not unwilling to accept it, when she immediately drew back and said, somewhat flustered, to Sir William,--\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, sir, I have not the slightest intention of dancing. I beg you not to imagine that I came this way to ask for a partner.\u201d\\n\\nWith grave courtesy, Mr. Darcy asked to be allowed the honour of her hand, but without success. Elizabeth was resolute, and Sir William\u2019s efforts to persuade her did nothing to alter her decision.\\n\\n\u201cYou dance so exceptionally well, Miss Eliza, that it is cruel to deny me the pleasure of seeing you; and though this gentleman generally dislikes the amusement, I am sure he can have no objection to obliging us for half an hour.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Darcy is all politeness,\u201d Elizabeth said, smiling.\\n\\n\u201cHe is indeed; but given the inducement, my dear Miss Eliza, we cannot be surprised by his willingness, for who could object to such a partner?\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth gave him an arch look and turned away. Her refusal had not harmed her in the gentleman\u2019s estimation, and he was thinking of her with some satisfaction when Miss Bingley addressed him,--\"}"
