          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"00164d99d6f378906ae695bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed, at Lady Canterville\u2019s urgent request, to retain in her old post. As they stepped down, she gave each of them a deep curtsey and said, in a quaint old-world fashion, \u2018I welcome you to Canterville Chase.\u2019 They followed her through the splendid Tudor hall and into the library, a long, low chamber panelled in dark oak, with a great stained-glass window at one end. Tea had been prepared there, and, when they had removed their wraps, they seated themselves and began to glance about the room, while Mrs. Umney attended them.\\n\\nAll at once Mrs. Otis noticed a faded red mark on the floor near the fireplace and, having no idea what it truly meant, remarked to Mrs. Umney, \u2018I fear something has been spilled there.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, madam,\u2019 said the elderly housekeeper softly, \u2018blood was spilled upon that place.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018How dreadful!\u2019 exclaimed Mrs. Otis. \u2018I have no liking whatever for blood-stains in a sitting-room. It must be taken away immediately.\u2019\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and replied in the same hushed, mysterious tone, \u2018It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, murdered on that very spot by her own husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon outlived her by nine years, then vanished suddenly in circumstances of great mystery. His body has never been found, though his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. The blood-stain has been greatly admired by tourists and others, and it cannot be removed.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018That is utter nonsense,\u2019 cried Washington Otis. \u2018Pinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will have it clean in no time\u2019; and, before the frightened housekeeper could stop him, he was on his knees, briskly rubbing the floor with a little stick resembling a black cosmetic. Within a few moments, every sign of the blood-stain had vanished.\\n\\n\u2018I knew Pinkerton would manage it,\u2019 he declared triumphantly, looking about at his admiring family; but scarcely had he spoken when a dreadful flash of lightning illuminated the gloomy room, a terrible crash of thunder sent them all to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u2018What an abominable climate!\u2019 observed the American Minister calmly, lighting a long cheroot. \u2018I suppose the old country is so overcrowded that there is not enough respectable weather to go round. I have always thought emigration the only remedy for England.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Hiram,\u2019 cried Mrs. Otis, \u2018what are we to do with a woman who faints?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Put it down against her as breakages,\u2019 replied the Minister. \u2018She will not faint again after that\u2019; and, indeed, Mrs. Umney recovered within a few minutes.\"}"
