{"request_id":"add2ccdcfe87e4f433250163","rewritten_text":"I cannot in the least understand why she wished to be intimate with me; yet, were the same circumstances to occur again, I am certain I should be deceived again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday; and in the interval I received neither note nor line. When she came, it was plain enough that she took no pleasure in the visit; she offered a slight and formal excuse for not calling sooner, said nothing of wishing to see me again, and was in every way so changed a person, that when she left me I was fully determined to continue the acquaintance no further. I pity her, though I cannot avoid blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I may safely say that every approach to intimacy came first from her. Yet I pity her, because she must know that she has acted wrongly, and because I am quite convinced that concern for her brother is the cause. I need say no more; and though _we_ know such concern to be entirely unnecessary, still, if she feels it, it readily explains her conduct towards me; and, as he is so justly dear to his sister, whatever uneasiness she may feel for him is both natural and amiable. I cannot help wondering, however, that she should entertain such fears now, for if he had cared for me in the least, we must have met long, long since. He knows that I am in town, I am sure, from something she herself said; and yet, from the way she speaks, it seems as though she wished to convince herself that he is truly attached to Miss Darcy. I cannot make it out. Were I not afraid of judging too severely, I should almost be inclined to say that there is much appearance of duplicity in the whole affair. I will try to dismiss every painful reflection, and think only of what can make me happy—your affection, and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned something of his never returning to Netherfield, and of giving up the house, though she spoke without certainty. We had better say nothing of it. I am exceedingly pleased that you have such agreeable news of our friends at Hunsford. Pray visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits revived when she reflected that Jane would no longer be imposed upon, at least by the sister. Every expectation from the brother was now entirely at an end."}

