          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf6957d0c1cc8c97e6681d89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He wrote that he was wasting his time where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and finish the arrangements they had begun for his Indian venture. He could delay his departure no longer; but since his journey to London might be followed, sooner than he expected, by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could. He therefore asked me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so that we could travel south together. This letter partly brought me back to life, and I decided to leave my island in two days.\\n\\nYet before I left, I had a task to perform that made me shudder even to think of it. I had to pack my chemical instruments, which meant entering the room where I had done my hateful work and touching the tools whose very sight sickened me. The next morning, at dawn, I gathered enough courage and unlocked the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living body of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then entered the room. With shaking hands, I carried the instruments out, but I knew I should not leave the remains of my work behind to arouse the peasants' horror and suspicion. So I placed them in a basket with many stones, and, after weighing it down, decided to throw it into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach, cleaning and arranging my chemical equipment.\\n\\nNothing could have been more complete than the change in my feelings since the night the d\u00e6mon appeared. Before, I had viewed my promise with dark despair, as something that had to be kept whatever the consequences. But now I felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes, and that for the first time I saw clearly. The thought of beginning my work again never entered my mind. The threat I had heard weighed heavily on me, but I did not consider that any willing action of mine could prevent it. I had decided that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be an act of the lowest and most terrible selfishness, and I drove away every thought that might lead me to any other conclusion.\"}"
