          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d89a077825faee3f15108cc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo, no, that is to-morrow; and I especially wanted to see Knightley to-day for that very reason.\u2014Such a dreadful hot morning!\u2014I came across the fields too\u2014(speaking as though she had been badly treated,) which made it all the worse. And then not to find him at home! I assure you, I am not at all pleased. No apology left, no message for me. The housekeeper said she knew nothing of my expecting him.\u2014Very strange!\u2014And no one knew where he had gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, perhaps to Abbey Mill, perhaps into his woods.\u2014Miss Woodhouse, this is not like our friend Knightley!\u2014Can you explain it?\u201d\\n\\nEmma amused herself by agreeing that it was indeed very strange, and that she could not say a word in his defence.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot imagine,\u201d said Mrs. Elton, (feeling the insult as a wife ought to feel it,) \u201cI cannot imagine how he could treat you so, of all people! The very last person one would expect him to forget!\u2014My dear Mr. E., he must have left a message for you; I am sure he must.\u2014Not even Knightley could be so very odd;\u2014his servants must have forgotten it. Depend upon it, that was what happened: and it is very likely with the Donwell servants, who, I have often noticed, are all extremely clumsy and careless.\u2014I am sure I would not have such a creature as his Harry serving at our sideboard for any reason. And as for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks very little of her indeed.\u2014She promised Wright a receipt, and never sent it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI met William Larkins,\u201d Mr. Elton continued, \u201cas I came near the house, and he told me I would not find his master at home, but I did not believe him.\u2014William seemed rather out of sorts. He said he did not know what had come over his master lately, but he could hardly ever get him to speak. I have nothing to do with William\u2019s concerns, but it is truly very important that I should see Knightley to-day; and so it is a very serious inconvenience that I have taken this hot walk for nothing.\u201d\\n\\nEmma felt she could do no better than go home at once. Most likely she was being waited for there at that very moment; and Mr. Knightley might be kept from becoming still more hostile to Mr. Elton, if not to William Larkins.\\n\\nAs she took her leave, she was pleased to find Miss Fairfax determined to see her out of the room, and even to go downstairs with her; it gave Emma an opportunity, which she immediately used, to say,\"}"
