{"request_id":"20ab757edce2af74d3f1dfea","rewritten_text":"All the young people were eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of visiting it again himself; it lay only seventeen miles from Uppercross; November though it was, the weather was not at all bad; and, in brief, Louisa, the keenest among the keen, having determined to go, and, beyond the pleasure of having her own way, now fortified by the notion of merit in persisting in it, overruled every wish of her father and mother to postpone the excursion until summer; and to Lyme they were to go—Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first careless plan had been to leave in the morning and come back at night; but Mr Musgrove would not agree to this, for the sake of his horses; and, when the matter was sensibly weighed, a day in mid-November could allow little time for viewing a new place, after seven hours, required by the nature of the roads, had been taken away for the journey there and back. They were therefore to remain overnight, and were not expected home until dinner the following day. This was considered a great improvement; and though they all assembled at the Great House for breakfast at a rather early hour, and departed with great punctuality, it was so far beyond noon before the two carriages—Mr Musgrove’s coach, carrying the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—came down the long hill into Lyme and entered the town’s yet steeper street, that it was plain they would have scarcely more than time to look around before the day’s light and warmth had vanished.

Having obtained rooms and ordered dinner at one of the inns, their next business was, beyond question, to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any entertainment or bustle that Lyme, as a public resort, could provide. The rooms were closed, nearly all the visitors had departed, and hardly any families remained except the inhabitants; and, since the buildings themselves offer nothing to admire, it is the town’s striking position—the main street seeming almost to rush into the water—the walk to the Cobb, winding round the agreeable little bay, alive in season with bathing machines and company; the Cobb itself, with its ancient marvels and recent alterations; and the lovely range of cliffs extending eastward from the town, that draw the stranger’s eye. And he must be a very peculiar stranger indeed who finds no charm in Lyme’s immediate surroundings, enough to make him desire a better acquaintance with it."}

