          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14190aea8ca70289f84227da\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane owned herself somewhat surprised by the engagement; yet she spoke less of her surprise than of her sincere wishes for their happiness, nor could Elizabeth induce her to regard it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia were by no means jealous of Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was merely a clergyman; and it concerned them only as news to be circulated through Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not help feeling triumphant in being able to return Mrs. Bennet\u2019s consolation of having a daughter advantageously married; and she visited Longbourn more frequently than before to declare how happy she was, though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s gloomy looks and spiteful observations might well have banished all happiness.\\n\\nA reserve existed between Elizabeth and Charlotte which left them both silent upon the matter; and Elizabeth was convinced that true confidence could never again exist between them. Her disappointment in Charlotte led her to look with still greater affection upon her sister, whose integrity and delicacy she knew could never lose her esteem, and for whose happiness she became more anxious every day, since Bingley had now been absent a week and no tidings were received of his return.\\n\\nJane had promptly answered Caroline\u2019s letter, and counted the days until she could reasonably expect another. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks came on Tuesday, directed to their father, and composed with all the grave gratitude that a year\u2019s residence in the family might have inspired. Having satisfied his conscience on that point, he went on to acquaint them, in many ecstatic phrases, with his happiness at gaining the affection of their agreeable neighbour, Miss Lucas; and then stated that it was solely for the pleasure of her company that he had so readily accepted their kind invitation to visit Longbourn again, where he hoped to return on the Monday fortnight. Lady Catherine, he added, approved his marriage so warmly that she desired it to occur as soon as might be; and this, he trusted, would prove an irresistible reason with his amiable Charlotte to appoint an early day for rendering him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer pleasing to Mrs. Bennet. Rather, she was as ready to complain of it as her husband. It was most strange that he should come to Longbourn rather than Lucas Lodge; it was likewise highly inconvenient and excessively troublesome. She detested visitors in the house while her health was so poor, and lovers were, of all persons, the most disagreeable.\"}"
