          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1d789fed2e26b8492965c521\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my youth, discontent never entered my thoughts, and if I was ever overcome by ennui, the sight of beauty in nature or the study of what was excellent and sublime in human creations could always engage my heart and restore energy to my spirits. But I am a ruined tree; the lightning has struck my soul. I felt then that I would live on only to display what I would soon no longer be\u2014a wretched image of shattered humanity, pitiful to others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a considerable time at Oxford, wandering through the surrounding country and trying to identify every place connected with the most stirring period of English history. Our small expeditions of discovery were often extended by the new objects that appeared before us. We visited the tomb of the celebrated Hampden and the field where that patriot fell. For a moment, my soul rose above its degrading and miserable fears to contemplate the sacred ideals of liberty and self-sacrifice for which these places stood as monuments and reminders. For an instant, I dared to cast off my chains and look about me with a free and elevated spirit; but the iron had sunk into my flesh, and I fell again, trembling and hopeless, into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford reluctantly and went on to Matlock, our next resting place. The countryside around this village resembled Swiss scenery more closely than any we had seen; yet everything was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the distant white Alps that always crown the pine-covered mountains of my native land. We visited the remarkable cave and the small natural-history collections, where the curiosities were arranged as they were in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. When Henry spoke the latter name, I trembled, and I hurried to leave Matlock, which had thus become linked in my mind with that dreadful scene.\\n\\nFrom Derby, continuing northward, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could now almost imagine myself among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow still lingering on the northern slopes, the lakes, and the rush of rocky streams were all familiar and beloved sights to me. Here, too, we made several acquaintances who nearly succeeded in deceiving me into happiness. Clerval's pleasure was greater than mine in proportion; his mind broadened in the company of talented men, and he discovered in himself greater abilities and resources than he could have imagined possessing while he kept company with those beneath him.\"}"
