          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ff2539fc79ab766e26ef7dff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The powerful voice\u2014unearthly, we might call it, for we could not possibly connect it with any mechanism of pipe or key\u2014fell silent; and the girl, turning to assist her aged companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nIt was her father, and from childhood she had guided his sightless steps. They were Germans from Saxony and, having emigrated there only a few years earlier, had formed new ties among the neighboring villagers. Around the time the plague began, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to guess. He, a nobleman, loved the beautiful daughter of the poor musician and had followed them in their flight from the persecution of his friends; but soon the great leveller came, scythe still sharp, to cut down the tall flowers of the field along with the grass. The young man was an early victim. She lived on for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to maintain a deception, at first accidental; and now, alone in the land, the only survivors, he remained unaware of the change, never knowing that when he listened to his child\u2019s music, the silent mountains, unfeeling lake, and unconscious trees were, except for himself, her only audience.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, she had been struck by the symptoms of illness. The thought of leaving her old, blind father alone upon the empty earth filled her with horror; yet she lacked the courage to tell him the truth, and the extremity of her despair drove her to extraordinary effort. At the usual vespertide hour, she led him to the chapel; and though she trembled and wept for him, she played without a flaw in rhythm or a wrong note the hymn composed to celebrate the creation of the beautiful earth, which would soon become her tomb.\\n\\nWe seemed to her like visitors from heaven itself; at the sight of relief, her fiercely summoned courage and barely sustained composure gave way. With a cry, she 