pread her wings and flew off to see about it. This did not surprise the boy, for he had long known that this insect was gullible about fires, and he had taken advantage of its simplicity more than once. Next came a tumblebug, pushing sturdily at its ball, and Tom touched it to watch it draw its legs against its body and pretend to be dead. By now the birds were in full uproar. A catbird, the northern mocker, landed in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and poured out imitations of its neighbors in delighted excitement. Then a shrill jay swooped down like a flash of blue flame and stopped on a twig almost within the boy\u2019s reach, tilted its head to one side, and stared at the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large fox squirrel came scurrying along, sitting up now and then to inspect the boys and chatter at them, for the wild creatures had probably never seen human beings before and scarcely knew whether they should be afraid.\"}"
