          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c61cc7185d6a8cd1d3f4368\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have not the nerves for that kind of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you yourself be easy, spending the entire evening away from the poor child?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; his papa can, you know, so why should not I? Jemima is so attentive, and she could send us news every hour of how he was. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we should all come. I am no more uneasy about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly frightened yesterday, but the circumstances are quite different to-day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not consider it too late to send word for yourself, suppose you go too, with your husband. Leave little Charles in my charge. Mr and Mrs Musgrove cannot think it improper while I am staying with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you in earnest?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cDear me! that is a very good idea\u2014very good indeed. Certainly, I may as well go as stay, for I am of no use at home\u2014am I?\u2014and it only worries me. You, who have none of a mother\u2019s feelings, are much the fittest person. You can get little Charles to do anything; he always obeys you at a word. It will be far better than leaving him with Jemima alone. Oh! I shall certainly go; I am sure I ought, if I can, quite as much as Charles, for they are excessively anxious that I should know Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not object to being left alone. An excellent notion of yours, indeed, Anne. I will go and tell Charles, and dress at once. You can send for us, you know, at a moment\u2019s warning, if anything should be wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to frighten you. I would not go, you may be certain, if I did not feel perfectly easy about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe very next instant she was knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door; and Anne, following her upstairs, arrived in time to hear the whole conversation, which opened with Mary saying, in a tone of high triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI intend to go with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. If I shut myself up for ever with the child, I could not make him do anything he did not choose. Anne will remain; Anne has undertaken to stay at home and care for him. It was Anne\u2019s own suggestion, and therefore I shall go with you, which will be much better, for I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind in Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be very happy to have you go; but it seems rather cruel that she should be left at home alone, nursing our sick child.\u201d\"}"
