          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f646c44ceb093ba243551b99\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day passed much as the previous one had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent several hours that morning with the invalid, who continued to improve, though slowly; and in the evening Elizabeth joined the others in the drawing-room. But the loo table did not appear. Mr. Darcy was writing, while Miss Bingley sat near him, watching his letter take shape and repeatedly interrupting him with messages for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were playing piquet, and Mrs. Hurst watched their game.\\n\\nElizabeth picked up some needlework and found enough amusement in listening to Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant praise of his handwriting, the straightness of his lines, and the length of his letter, together with the complete indifference with which he received her compliments, made for a curious conversation and perfectly matched Elizabeth\u2019s opinion of them both.\\n\\n\u201cHow pleased Miss Darcy will be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe did not answer.\\n\\n\u201cYou write remarkably fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must need to write in a year! Business letters too! How hateful I should find them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is fortunate, then, that they are my responsibility rather than yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease tell your sister that I long to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already told her that once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid you do not like your pen. Let me mend it for you. I mend pens extremely well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe was silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am delighted to hear she is improving on the harp, and please let her know that I am quite enchanted by her lovely little design for a table. I think it far superior to Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you allow me to postpone your raptures until I write again? At present I have no room to do them justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it does not matter. I shall see her in January. But do you always write such charming, long letters to her, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are generally long; but whether they are always charming is not for me to decide.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is a rule of mine that anyone who can write a long letter easily cannot write badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat will not serve as a compliment to Darcy, Caroline,\u201d cried her brother, \u201cbecause he does not write easily. He thinks too hard before using words of four syllables. Do you not, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy writing style is very different from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d cried Miss Bingley, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless way imaginable.\u201d\"}"
