          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2105af0af5450adb6b6d7920\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut do not suppose that I did not consider it an evil in itself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBe assured, they were all entirely comfortable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might not, perhaps, esteem them the more for it. So many women and children have no right to be comfortable aboard ship.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Frederick, you are speaking very foolishly. Pray, what would become of us poor sailors\u2019 wives, who so often need to be carried from one port to another after our husbands, if everyone thought as you do?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy opinions, as you see, did not stop me from taking Mrs Harville and her whole family to Plymouth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I cannot bear to hear you speak so much like a fine gentleman, as though women were all fine ladies rather than rational beings. None of us expect smooth water every day of our lives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, my dear,\u201d said the Admiral, \u201conce he has a wife, he will change his tune. When he is married, if we are fortunate enough to live through to another war, we shall see him do what you and I, and many others, have done. He will be very grateful to anyone who brings his wife to him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, indeed he will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow I am finished,\u201d cried Captain Wentworth. \u201cWhenever married people begin upon me with, \u2018Oh! you will think quite differently when you are married,\u2019 I can only answer, \u2018No, I shall not\u2019; and then they repeat, \u2018Yes, you will,\u2019 and that ends the matter.\u201d\\n\\nHe rose and walked away.\\n\\n\u201cWhat a great traveller you must be, ma\u2019am!\u201d said Mrs Musgrove to Mrs Croft.\\n\\n\u201cFairly well, ma\u2019am, during the fifteen years of my marriage, though many women have travelled more. I have crossed the Atlantic four times, and gone once to the East Indies and returned, but only once; besides visiting various places nearer home\u2014Cork, Lisbon, and Gibraltar. But I never passed beyond the Streights, nor was I ever in the West Indies. Bermuda and Bahama, you know, are not called the West Indies.\u201d\\n\\nMrs Musgrove had nothing to offer in disagreement; throughout her life, she could not charge herself with having called them anything at all.\\n\\n\u201cAnd I assure you, ma\u2019am,\u201d Mrs Croft continued, \u201cnothing can surpass the comforts of a man-of-war; I mean, of course, the higher rates. In a frigate you are naturally more restricted, though any sensible woman may be perfectly content in one; and I can truly say that the happiest portion of my life has been passed aboard a ship. While we were together, you know, there was nothing to fear.\"}"
