          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc4face4719735dbc70a3735\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Still, he kept one secret from these beloved friends; a secret cherished from his earliest childhood, which, though he loved his fellow students, he would not confide to the tenderness or compassion of any among them. He was in love. He dreaded that the vehemence of his feeling might provoke their mockery; nor could he endure that they should profane it by deeming slight and fleeting what he felt to be the very life of his life.\\n\\nA gentleman of limited means, living near his family seat, had three beautiful daughters. The eldest was by far the loveliest; yet her beauty merely heightened her other merits\u2014her mind was lucid and vigorous, her temper sweetly angelic. She and my father had played together from infancy: Diana, even as a child, had been a favourite with his mother; and this fondness increased as the beautiful, spirited girl grew older, so that during his holidays from school and college they were continually together. Novels, and all the means through which civilized youth are taught the existence of passions before they truly experience them, had greatly affected one so singularly open to every impression. At eleven, Diana was his dearest companion in play, yet he already spoke the language of love. Though she was nearly two years older, her education had left her more childlike, at least in her understanding and expression of emotion; she accepted his fervent declarations innocently, returning them without knowing their import. She had read no novels and knew only her younger sisters; how should she distinguish love from friendship? When her expanding mind revealed to her the real character of their intercourse, her heart was already given to her friend, and her sole fear was that new attractions or inconstancy might lead him to forsake his childish promises.\\n\\nYet their attachment grew daily more ardent and affectionate. It was a passion that had increased with his own growth, woven itself into every faculty and sentiment, and could end only with his life. No one knew of their love but their two hearts; and though, in every other matter\u2014and even in this\u2014he feared his companions' blame for loving one beneath him in fortune, nothing could for an instant alter his resolve to marry her as soon as he had gathered courage enough to confront the obstacles he meant to overcome.\\n\\nDiana was entirely deserving of his profoundest love.\"}"
