          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32e26301c4d422116ae48fcd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters at the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are rarely very harsh, and during the autumn it is indeed an uncommon occurrence for a fire to be thought needful. Near the middle of October, 18\u2014, there came, however, a day of unusual cold. A little before sundown I made my way through the evergreens to the cottage of my friend, whom I had not called upon for some weeks\u2014my home then being in Charleston, nine miles from the island, while the means of going and returning were greatly inferior to those of the present time. On arriving at the hut, I knocked, according to my habit, and, receiving no answer, looked for the key in the place where I knew it was hidden, opened the door, and entered. A cheerful fire burned on the hearth. It was something novel, and far from unwelcome. I cast aside my overcoat, seated myself in an arm-chair beside the snapping logs, and waited patiently for my hosts to return.\\n\\nNot long after nightfall they came in and welcomed me with great warmth. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried about preparing several marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was seized by one of his fits\u2014what else can I call them?\u2014of excitement. He had discovered an unfamiliar bivalve, constituting a new genus; and, beyond this, he had tracked down and captured, with Jupiter\u2019s help, a _scarab\u00e6us_ which he considered wholly new, though he desired my judgment concerning it on the following day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not this evening?\u201d I asked, warming my hands at the fire, and consigning the entire race of _scarab\u00e6i_ to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, had I but known that you were here!\u201d said Legrand; \u201cbut it has been so long since I last saw you; and how was I to imagine that you would visit me on this particular night, of all nights? While coming home I encountered Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014, from the fort, and, most foolishly, lent him the insect; thus you cannot see it before morning. Remain here to-night, and at sunrise I shall send Jup for it. It is the most beautiful thing in existence!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbsurd! no\u2014the bug. It is a splendid golden color\u2014nearly the size of a large hickory-nut\u2014with two black spots, like jet, near one end of its back, and another, rather longer, at the other. The _antenn\u00e6_ are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey aint no tin in him, Massa Will, I keeps a tellin you,\u201d Jupiter interrupted here; \u201cde bug is a goole bug, solid, ebery piece of him, inside an all, \u2019ceptin he wings\u2014neber felt a bug half so heavy in all my life.\u201d\"}"
