{"request_id":"6e4537c2842c01e2d4ec06a1","rewritten_text":"Many American women, when they leave their own country, take on an air of constant poor health, believing it to be a mark of European elegance. But Mrs. Otis had never made this mistake. She was remarkably healthy and full of energy. In fact, in many ways she was quite English, and proved that America and England now have everything in common except, naturally, the language. Her eldest son, named Washington by his parents in a patriotic moment—a name he never stopped regretting—was a fair-haired, fairly handsome young man. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German dance at the Newport Casino for three seasons in a row, and was known even in London as an excellent dancer. His only weaknesses were gardenias and the peerage. In every other respect, he was very sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was fifteen, slim and lovely as a young deer, with a fine, fearless look in her large blue eyes. She was an excellent rider, and had once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park. She won by a length and a half just in front of the statue of Achilles, to the great delight of the young Duke of Cheshire, who proposed to her at once. His guardians sent him back to Eton that very night, in floods of tears. After Virginia came the twins, usually called “The Stars and Stripes” because they were always being whipped. They were delightful boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the only true republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase was seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had sent a telegram asking for a wagonette to meet them. They began their drive in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was lightly scented by the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood pigeon softly repeating its own sweet call, or saw, deep among the rustling ferns, the shining breast of a pheasant. Small squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and over the mossy hills, their white tails raised in the air. But as they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds. A strange stillness seemed to fill the air, a large flock of rooks flew silently above them, and before they reached the house, several heavy drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to welcome them, neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

