          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"548f5c3ed0886f5dd7a05fc2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The party was split between two adjoining benches. Anne sat with those on the front one, and Mr Elliot, with the help of his friend Colonel Wallis, had arranged matters so skillfully that he was seated beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and the chief object of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attentions, was perfectly satisfied.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s mind was especially well suited to the evening\u2019s entertainment. It gave her just enough to occupy her: she had feeling for the tender pieces, spirits for the lively ones, attention for the scientific, and patience for the tedious; and she had never enjoyed a concert more, at least through the first act. Near its end, during the pause after an Italian song, she explained its words to Mr Elliot. A concert program lay between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis nearly the sense\u2014or rather the meaning\u2014of the words; for of course one must not speak of the sense of an Italian love song. But it is as close to the meaning as I can make it, since I do not claim to understand the language. I am a very poor student of Italian.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I can see that. I see you know nothing about it. You know only enough of the language to translate these inverted, rearranged, abbreviated Italian lines at sight into clear, intelligible, elegant English. You need say no more about your ignorance. Here is perfect proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not argue against such gracious politeness; but I should be sorry to be tested by a true expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not had the pleasure of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d he replied, \u201cwithout learning something of Miss Anne Elliot; and I consider her someone too modest for the world at large to recognize even half her accomplishments, and too accomplished for modesty to seem natural in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! This is far too much flattery. I have forgotten what comes next,\u201d she said, turning to the program.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d said Mr Elliot quietly, \u201cI have known your character longer than you realize.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed? How can that be? You could only have known it since I came to Bath, unless you had heard me spoken of earlier by my own family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew of you long before you came to Bath. I had heard you described by people who knew you intimately. I have known your character for many years. Your appearance, disposition, accomplishments, and manner\u2014they were all familiar to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr Elliot was not disappointed in the interest he had hoped to awaken. No one can resist the charm of such a mystery.\"}"
