          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"af5401f3e58c78a9d45766ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dear me, she could talk. It would not have been proper for me to hint that it was very late, that I was tired, and nearly asleep. I wished I had gone to bed when I had the chance. But now I had to endure it; there was no other way. So she went on and on, in the deep, ghostly silence of the sleeping castle, until at last there came, as though from far beneath us, a distant sound like a muffled scream--with such agony in it that my skin crawled. The queen stopped, her eyes brightening with pleasure; she tilted her graceful head like a bird listening. The sound forced its way up through the silence again.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a truly stubborn soul, and he endureth long. It hath been many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndureth what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come--ye shall see a merry sight. If he yield not his secret now, ye shall see him torn apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a smooth, gentle devil she was; so calm and serene, while every nerve in my legs seemed to ache in sympathy with that man's pain. Escorted by armored guards carrying blazing torches, we marched through echoing corridors and down damp, dripping stone stairs that smelled of mold and the long-imprisoned night--a cold, eerie journey, and a long one. It was made neither shorter nor more pleasant by the sorceress's talk about the sufferer and his crime. An unknown informer had accused him of killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony is not exactly right, your Highness. It would be fairer to let the accused face his accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not thought of that, since it is of little consequence. But even if I wished it, I could not, for the accuser came masked at night, told the forester, and immediately departed again; thus the forester knoweth him not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen this Unknown is the only person who saw the stag killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMarry, no man saw the killing, but this Unknown saw this hardy wretch near the place where the stag lay, and with most loyal zeal betrayed him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag too? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal--while wearing a mask--seems a little suspicious. But what is your Highness's reason for racking the prisoner? What is gained by it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise, and then his soul would be lost. By law, his life is forfeited for his crime--and surely I will see that he pays for it!--but it would endanger my own soul to let him die unconfessed and without absolution.\u201d\"}"
        }
