          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"804f09645b38538006e48e74\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou have not been in Bath long enough,\u201d he said, \u201cto enjoy its evening parties.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, no. Their usual nature has nothing to offer me. I do not play cards.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou did not formerly, I know. You never used to like cards; but time brings many changes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not changed that much yet,\u201d Anne cried, then stopped, afraid of what he might misunderstand. After a few moments, he said, as though prompted by an immediate feeling, \u201cIt is a long time, indeed. Eight and a half years is a long time.\u201d\\n\\nWhether he might have said more was left for Anne to consider in some calmer moment; for while his words still sounded in her ears, Henrietta startled her into other concerns, eager to take advantage of the present opportunity to go out, and urging her companions not to waste time in case someone else came in.\\n\\nThey had to move. Anne said she was quite ready, and tried to appear so; but she felt that if Henrietta had known the regret and reluctance in her heart at leaving that chair, at preparing to leave the room, she would have found, in all her own feelings for her cousin, in the certainty of his affection, enough to pity Anne.\\n\\nTheir preparations, however, were abruptly interrupted. Alarming sounds were heard; more visitors were approaching, and the door was opened for Sir Walter and Miss Elliot, whose arrival seemed to cast a chill over everyone. Anne felt instantly oppressed, and wherever she looked saw signs of the same feeling. The comfort, freedom, and cheerfulness of the room were gone, subdued into cold restraint, deliberate silence, or dull conversation, to suit the heartless elegance of her father and sister. How humiliating it was to feel it so!\\n\\nHer watchful jealousy was satisfied in one respect. Captain Wentworth was acknowledged again by both of them, and by Elizabeth more graciously than before. She spoke to him once, and looked at him more than once. Elizabeth was, in fact, considering an important step. What followed made it clear. After wasting a few minutes on the proper empty remarks, she began to offer the invitation that was to include all the Musgroves still owing one: \u201cTomorrow evening, to meet a few friends; no formal party.\u201d She said it all very gracefully, and the cards she had brought with her\u2014\u201cMiss Elliot at home\u201d\u2014were placed on the table, accompanied by a polite, inclusive smile for everyone, and one smile and one card more distinctly intended for Captain Wentworth. The truth was that Elizabeth had been in Bath long enough to understand the value of a man with his manner and appearance.\"}"
