          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b834aeb317304cd73ec940ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a gentleman, you see. He was a gentleman through and through, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and the Widow Douglas said that counted as much for a man as it did for a horse. Nobody ever denied she belonged to the finest society in our town. Pap said the same thing, too, though he had no more breeding than a mudcat himself. Colonel Grangerford was very tall and thin, with a dark, pale complexion and not a bit of red in it anywhere. Every morning he shaved his whole thin face clean. He had very thin lips and nostrils, a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and the blackest eyes, set so deep they looked as if they were staring out at you from caves. His forehead was high, and his black, straight hair hung down to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin. Every day of his life he wore a clean shirt and a complete linen suit, white enough to hurt your eyes. On Sundays he wore a blue coat with tails and brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver handle. There was nothing foolish about him, not a bit, and he was never loud. He was as kind as could be\u2014you could feel it, and it made you trust him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was a fine thing to see. But when he stood straight as a flagpole and lightning began to flash beneath his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree first and ask what was wrong afterward. He never had to tell anyone to behave; everybody had good manners when he was there. Everybody liked having him around, too. He was sunshine most of the time\u2014I mean, he made it feel like fine weather. When he turned into a storm cloud, it was terribly dark for half a minute, and that was enough. Nothing would go wrong again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came downstairs in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good day, and did not sit again until they had sat down. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, where the decanter stood, mixed a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He held it and waited until Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s drinks were mixed. Then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir and madam.\u201d The colonel and old lady bowed the smallest bit and thanked them. Then all three drank. Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy left in the bottoms of their tumblers, gave it to Buck and me, and we drank to the old people too.\"}"
