          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e6e48828d6742bbac03a882\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far across the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately took on an expression of profound gloom, and he answered, \u201cTo find someone who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you were pursuing travel in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we may have seen him, because the day before we found you, we saw dogs pulling a sledge with a man in it across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked countless questions about the route the demon, as he called him, had taken. Soon afterward, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI have no doubt aroused your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you are too considerate to question me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; it would be both intrusive and inhuman for me to trouble you with my curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and dangerous situation; you kindly restored me to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon after, he asked whether I thought the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I replied that I could not say with any certainty, since the ice had not broken until nearly midnight, and the traveller might have reached a place of safety before then; but I could not judge.\\n\\nFrom that time, a new spirit of life seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed the greatest eagerness to go on deck and watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to remain in the cabin, because he was far too weak to endure the bitter air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and notify him immediately if anything new came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of all that concerns this strange event up to the present day. The stranger has gradually recovered his health, but he is very quiet and seems uneasy whenever anyone but myself enters his cabin. Still, his manner is so gentle and winning that all the sailors have become interested in him, though they have had very little contact with him. As for me, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his constant, profound sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble being in happier days, for even now, in ruin, he is so appealing and amiable.\\n\\nIn one of my letters, my dear Margaret, I said that I would find no friend on the vast ocean; yet I have found a man whom, before misery broke his spirit, I would have been happy to call the brother of my heart.\"}"
