          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"22dfc2d3e3c902d75daad612\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The insect\u2019s weight was exceedingly remarkable, and, all things considered, I could scarcely blame Jupiter for the opinion he held of it; but what to think of Legrand\u2019s agreement with that opinion, I could not for the life of me determine.\\n\\n\u201cI sent for you,\u201d said he, in an elevated manner, when I had finished examining the beetle, \u201cI sent for you that I might obtain your advice and aid in advancing the designs of Fate and of the bug\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Legrand,\u201d I exclaimed, cutting him short, \u201cyou are plainly unwell, and ought to take a few precautions. You shall go to bed, and I will stay with you for several days, until you recover. You are feverish and\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFeel my pulse,\u201d he said.\\n\\nI did so, and, in truth, discovered not the least sign of fever.\\n\\n\u201cBut one may be ill without having fever. Let me prescribe for you this once. First, go to bed. Next\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are wrong,\u201d he interrupted, \u201cI am as well as I can hope to be while suffering the excitement I now endure. If you truly wish me well, you will help to relieve this excitement.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd in what way is that to be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMost easily. Jupiter and I are setting out on an expedition into the hills on the main land, and in this undertaking we shall require the assistance of someone in whom we may place confidence. You are the only person we can trust. Whether we succeed or fail, the excitement you observe in me will be equally quieted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am willing to oblige you in any manner,\u201d I answered; \u201cbut do you mean that this cursed beetle has some connection with your expedition into the hills?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt has.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen, Legrand, I can take no part in such an absurd affair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am sorry\u2014very sorry\u2014for we must attempt it alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAttempt it alone! The man is certainly mad!\u2014but wait!\u2014how long do you intend to be gone?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLikely all night. We shall leave at once, and shall return, in any event, by sunrise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd will you promise me, on your honor, that when this whim of yours is finished, and the business of the bug (good God!) settled to your satisfaction, you will come home and follow my advice without question, as your physician\u2019s?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; I promise; and now let us go, for there is no time to spare.\u201d\\n\\nWith a heavy heart I went along with my friend. We set out at about four o\u2019clock\u2014Legrand, Jupiter, the dog, and I. Jupiter carried the scythe and spades, all of which he insisted on bearing himself\u2014apparently less from unusual industry or complaisance than from fear of allowing either implement to remain within his master\u2019s reach.\"}"
