{"request_id":"fc09cf7028b6d0b61f745f02","rewritten_text":"My father was gone, and his memory became the life of my own. I could feel gratitude toward another, but I could never again love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even my pleasures had to be endured, not enjoyed. I was like a lonely place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep, black cliffs, where no warmth could enter and there was no path out to brighter fields. So, though friendship comforted me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visit; it left, and I scarcely felt its loss. The spirit of life was dead within me. Do not be surprised, then, that when I received the greatest gift of heaven—a friend—I did not welcome it more gladly, nor mourn it more deeply when it departed.

My friend's name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his history, so that you may see how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy. Since he too was deeply unhappy, we were well suited to comfort one another, if I had not been turned to stone by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville's troubles did not strike at the heart's core as mine did. His grief was natural: it would not destroy his heart, but purify it. When its shadow had passed, he might shine brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the very few whom fortune favors from birth, giving every gift of mind and body without limit. Under her special protection, no flaw, however slight, and no disappointment, however brief, was allowed to touch him. She seemed to have made his mind with an excellence no stain could darken, and given him an understanding no error could corrupt. His genius was extraordinary, and when it rose like a bright star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a Poet. That title has been so often lowered that it cannot express all that he was. He was like a poet of ancient times, crowned by the muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly light that set him apart and raised him above them. His remarkable beauty, the blazing fire of his eyes, and words whose rich tones held listeners in silent, ecstatic wonder made him surpass all others, so that beside him they seemed made only to serve his greater excellence."}

