          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9070a31472a8f8d6ab273aab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat is the matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour ordered me to call you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I recollect. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter several heavy attempts to fall asleep once more, which the man skilfully frustrated by poking the fire without pause for five minutes, he rose, flung on his hat, and went out. He made for the Temple, and, refreshing himself by walking twice round the pavements of King\u2019s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, went on to the Stryver chambers.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who was never present at these consultations, had departed for home, and Stryver himself answered the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing-gown, with his throat uncovered for greater comfort. About his eyes was that somewhat fierce, overstrained, scorched expression to be seen in every free liver of his order, from the portrait of Jeffries onward, and discernible, beneath Art\u2019s differing disguises, in the portraits of every Drinking Age.\\n\\n\u201cYou are somewhat late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the customary hour; perhaps a quarter-hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey entered a gloomy room, book-lined and strewn with papers, where a bright fire burned. A kettle was steaming on the hob, and amid the ruin of papers stood a shining table, well supplied with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou have had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo to-night, I believe. I have been dining with the client of the day; or watching him dine\u2014it comes to the same thing!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was an excellent point, Sydney, which you used on the identification. How did you get it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought him a fairly handsome fellow, and thought that I might have been much the same kind of fellow, had 