          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e19c7360140451f83b0bc8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201c\u2018We have called at your house,\u2019 said the first, \u2018and, not having the good fortune to find you there, and being told that you were likely walking this way, we followed in hopes of coming up with you. Will you be so good as to enter the carriage?\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe bearing of both men was commanding, and, as these words were uttered, they moved so as to put me between themselves and the carriage door. They were armed; I was not.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018Gentlemen,\u2019 I said, \u2018forgive me; but I am accustomed to ask who it is that does me the honour of requiring my services, and what may be the nature of the case for which I am called.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe answer came from the one who had spoken second. \u2018Doctor, your patients are persons of rank. As for the nature of the case, our trust in your ability assures us that you will discover it for yourself more readily than we could explain it. Enough. Will you please enter the carriage?\u2019\\n\\n\u201cI had no choice but to obey, and entered without a word. Both followed me\u2014the last leaping in after he had raised the steps. The carriage wheeled round and proceeded at the same pace as before.\\n\\n\u201cI set down this conversation precisely as it passed. I am certain it is the same, word for word. I relate all things exactly as they happened, forcing my thoughts not to stray from the work. Whenever I make the broken marks that occur below, I stop for a while and conceal my paper in its hiding-place.\\n\\n*****\\n\\n\u201cThe carriage quitted the streets, went beyond the North Barrier, and came out upon the country road. About two-thirds of a league from the Barrier\u2014I did not judge the distance then, but later, when I travelled it again\u2014it turned from the principal avenue, and soon halted before a lonely house. The three of us got down and went, along a wet, yielding path through a garden where an