          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7948c9df38813615d0e9d783\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The powerful voice\u2014unnatural, we might call it, since we could not connect it in any way with pipes or keys\u2014fell silent; and the girl, turning to help her elderly companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nIt was her father, and since childhood she had guided his blind steps. They were Germans from Saxony and had emigrated there only a few years earlier, forming new connections among the nearby villagers. Around the time the plague began, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to guess. He, a nobleman, loved the beautiful daughter of the poor musician and had followed them as they fled the persecution of his friends. But soon the great equalizer came, its scythe still sharp, cutting down the tall flowers of the field along with the grass. The young man was an early victim. She lived on for her father's sake. His blindness allowed her to maintain a deception, at first accidental; and now, alone in the land as its only survivors, he knew nothing of the change. When he listened to his child's music, he did not know that, apart from himself, the silent mountains, unfeeling lake, and unconscious trees were her only audience.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, she had been struck by the illness's first signs. She was horrified at the thought of leaving her old blind father alone upon the empty earth; yet she lacked the courage to tell him the truth. The depth of her despair drove her to extraordinary effort. At the usual evening hour, she led him to the chapel; and though she trembled and wept for him, she played without missing a beat or a note the hymn written to celebrate the creation of the beautiful earth, which would soon become her tomb.\\n\\nWe seemed to her like visitors from heaven itself. Her strained courage and barely held firmness vanished when relief appeared. With a cry, she ran toward us, threw herself around Adrian's knees, and, saying only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d opened the long-closed gates of her grief with sobs and hysterical cries.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, which she pointed out to us as she was dying. At last aware of his daughter's danger, and unable to see the changes in her beloved face, her father stubbornly held her hand until it grew cold and stiff in death. Even then he neither moved nor spoke, until twelve hours later kind death carried him to his unbroken rest. They lie beneath the earth, with the tree as their monument. The sacred place remains clear in my memory, enclosed by the rocky Jura and the distant, immeasurable Alps. The spire of the church they attended still rises among the sheltering trees; and though her hand is cold, I still think the divine music they loved drifts there, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
