          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ce2ba58483b3538d0674f186\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they went past desolate brickfields. The fog was thinner there, and he could make out the odd, bottle-shaped kilns, their orange fires spreading like fans. A dog barked as they passed, and somewhere far off in the darkness a stray sea-gull cried. The horse stumbled into a rut, swerved sharply, and then sprang into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they quitted the clay road and once more clattered over roughly paved streets. Nearly all the windows were unlit, though from time to time grotesque shadows showed against a lighted blind. He regarded them with curiosity. They stirred like gigantic puppets and gesticulated like living creatures. He loathed them. A heavy anger filled his heart. As they rounded a corner, a woman shouted something after them from an open doorway, and two men pursued the hansom for some hundred yards. The driver lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nIt is said that passion causes a man to think in circles. With dreadful repetition, certainly, the bitten lips of Dorian Gray formed and re-formed those subtle phrases concerning soul and sense, until he had discovered in them, as it were, the complete utterance of his mood, and had sanctioned by intellectual assent passions which, even without such sanction, would have ruled his nature. From one cell of his brain to another stole the single thought; and the fierce longing to live, most terrible of all human appetites, gave strength to every quivering nerve and fibre. Ugliness, once hateful to him because it rendered things real, grew precious now for that very cause. Ugliness alone was real. The vulgar quarrel, the disgusting den, the brutal violence of disordered existence, even the foulness of thief and outcast, were more vivid in their intense actuality than all the lovely forms of art and the dreamlike shadows of song. They were what he required for oblivion. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nAll at once the man pulled up sharply at the head of a dark lane. Above the low roofs and broken chimney-stacks of the houses rose the black masts of ships. Coils of white mist hung upon the yards like spectral sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere near here, sir, ain\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked about him. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he replied; then, having hurriedly descended and paid the driver the extra fare he had promised, he walked swiftly towards the quay. Here and there a lantern shone at the stern of some great merchant vessel. Its light trembled and broke apart in the puddles. A red glow came from an outward-bound steamer taking on coal. The slimy pavement resembled a wet mackintosh.\"}"
