          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7f0f2f1fa5a59a6f57543f7a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The company was separated and arranged upon two adjoining benches: Anne sat with those on the first, and Mr Elliot, with the aid of his friend Colonel Wallis, had contrived so successfully as to secure a place beside her. Miss Elliot, encircled by her cousins and the chief object of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attentions, was perfectly satisfied.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s spirits were in the happiest condition for the evening\u2019s amusement; it afforded precisely enough occupation: she had feeling for what was tender, animation for what was lively, interest for what was scientific, and endurance for what was tiresome; and she had never enjoyed a concert more, at least through the first act. Near its conclusion, during the pause after an Italian air, she interpreted the song\u2019s words for Mr Elliot. A concert programme lay between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis almost the sense\u2014or rather the meaning\u2014of the words; for the sense of an Italian love-song certainly ought not to be spoken of. But it is as close to the meaning as I can make it, since I do not profess to understand the language. I am a very indifferent Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I perceive that you are. I see that you know nothing at all about it. You possess only sufficient knowledge of the language to render, at sight, these inverted, displaced, abbreviated Italian verses into plain, intelligible, elegant English. Say no more of your ignorance. Here is decisive evidence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not resist such obliging civility; though I should be sorry to undergo examination by a true expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not enjoyed the advantage of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d he replied, \u201cwithout learning something of Miss Anne Elliot; and I consider her one who is too modest for the world at large to discover half her talents, and too accomplished for modesty to seem natural in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! for shame! This is excessive flattery. I forget what comes next,\u201d she said, turning towards the programme.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d said Mr Elliot in a low voice, \u201cI have known your character longer than you suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! In what way? You can only have known it since I came to Bath, unless you had previously heard me mentioned by my own family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew of you by report well before you came to Bath. I had heard you described by people who knew you closely. I have known your character for many years. Your appearance, disposition, accomplishments, and manner\u2014they were all familiar to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr Elliot was not disappointed in the interest he had hoped to awaken. No one can resist the attraction of such a mystery.\"}"
