oys last while they were alive. Many claimed that gloomy honor and offered evidence, more or less altered by the witness. When it was finally decided who really had seen the departed boys last and exchanged the last words with them, the fortunate ones assumed a kind of sacred importance, and everyone else stared at them and envied them. One poor boy, with no other distinction to offer, said with fairly obvious pride at the memory:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer beat me up once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at glory failed. Most of the boys could say the same, and that made the distinction too cheap. The group drifted away, still recalling memories of the lost heroes in awestruck voices.\"}"
