          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb0aebae254d4ba2af2abe55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father, certainly, may excuse you, if your mother is able. Daughters are never of such importance to a father. And if you remain another entire _month_, it will be in my power to carry one of you as far as London, for I shall go there early in June, for a week; and since Dawson has no objection to the barouche-box, there will be ample room for one of you\u2014and indeed, should the weather prove cool, I should not object to taking both, as neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are exceedingly kind, madam; but I think we must adhere to our first plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared to submit. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must provide a servant to accompany them. You know I always say what I think, and I cannot endure the notion of two young women travelling post alone. It is most improper. You must manage to send someone. Nothing in the world displeases me more than that kind of thing. Young women ought always to be suitably protected and attended, according to their rank in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted upon two men-servants attending her. Miss Darcy, daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not properly have appeared in any other fashion. I am extremely particular in all such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am pleased it occurred to me to say so; for it would truly be discreditable to _you_ to allow them to travel alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle intends to send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He has a man-servant, then? I am very glad that someone thinks of such matters. Where are you to change horses? Oh, at Bromley, naturally. If you give my name at the Bell, they will attend to you.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had a great many further questions concerning their journey; and because she did not supply every answer herself, attention was required\u2014which Elizabeth thought fortunate for her; otherwise, with her thoughts so engaged, she might have forgotten where she was. Reflection had to be kept for hours of solitude: whenever alone, she yielded to it as her greatest comfort; and scarcely a day passed without a solitary walk, during which she could indulge in all the pleasure of painful recollections.\\n\\nMr. Darcy\u2019s letter was soon becoming something she knew by heart. She examined every sentence, and her feelings toward its author were sometimes very different. Recalling the manner of his address, she remained full of resentment; but when she reflected on how unjustly she had judged and reproached him, her anger turned upon herself, and his disappointed feelings became an object of pity.\"}"
