          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e664c435cfa968ebfc0012c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that everything? It is no great matter, that! Many lie beneath the Republic\u2019s displeasure, and must peer from the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. Englishman. Which is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Of necessity, as I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has answered every preceding question. It is Jarvis Lorry who has stepped down and stands with one hand upon the coach door, answering a cluster of officials. They stroll around the carriage and slowly climb to the box, to inspect the scanty luggage upon its roof; the country folk lingering near press closer to the coach doors and stare hungrily within; a small child, borne by its mother, has its little arm stretched out, so that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, duly countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay one leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave. On, my postilions! A pleasant journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.--And the first peril is over!\u201d\\n\\nThese are once more Jarvis Lorry\u2019s words, as he folds his hands and raises his eyes. Terror fills the carriage, there is sobbing, there is the laboured breath of the unconscious traveller.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not travelling too slowly? Cannot they be persuaded to drive faster?\u201d asks Lucie, holding fast to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would have the appearance of flight, my darling. I dare not press them too hard; it would awaken suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook behind, look behind, and tell me whether we are followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is empty, my dearest. Thus far, no one follows us.\u201d\\n\\nHouses, two or three together, slip past us; lonely farms, dilapidated structures, dye-houses, tanneries, and such places; open fields, rows of bare trees. Beneath us lies the hard, rough paving, and on either side the soft, deep mire. At times we turn into the mud along the edge, to escape the stones that rattle and jolt us; at times we are caught there in ruts and boggy hollows. Then the torment of our impatience becomes so intense that, in our frantic fear and haste, we would get out and run--hide--do anything rather than remain still.\\n\\nFrom the open country, back again amid ruined buildings, isolated farms, dye-houses, tanneries, and the like; cottages in pairs and threes, avenues of leafless trees. Have these men tricked us, bringing us back by some other route? Are we passing the same spot a second time? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look behind, look behind, and see whether we are pursued! Hush! the posting-house.\\n\\nSlowly, our four horses are unharnessed; slowly, the coach waits in the narrow street, stripped of horses and seeming unlikely ever to move again; slowly, the fresh horses appear, one after another; slowly, the new postilions come after them, sucking and braiding their whip-lashes; slowly, the former postilions reckon their money, make mistaken sums, and reach unsatisfied conclusions.\"}"
