          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"73e2433a8310f49aab26c913\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless; she no longer found pleasure in her usual activities. Any enjoyment seemed like disrespect toward the dead; she believed that endless grief and tears were the proper tribute she owed to innocence so cruelly ruined and destroyed. She was no longer the cheerful girl who, in our younger days, walked with me beside the lake and spoke excitedly of our hopes for the future. The first of those sorrows that draw us away from the world had come to her, and its shadow had extinguished her sweetest smiles.\\n\\n\u201cWhen I think, my dear cousin,\u201d she said, \u201cof the terrible death of Justine Moritz, I can no longer see the world and its people as I once did. Before, the stories of wickedness and injustice that I read in books or heard from others seemed like events from ancient times or imagined evils. At least they were far away, easier to understand with reason than to picture in the mind. But now suffering has come close to home, and people seem to me like monsters thirsty for one another\u2019s blood. Yet perhaps I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl was guilty; and if she had committed the crime for which she died, she would surely have been one of the most corrupt human beings. For a few jewels, to murder the son of her benefactor and friend\u2014a child she had cared for since his birth and seemed to love as her own! I could never agree to the death of any person, but I would certainly have thought such a creature unfit to live among other people. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel that she was innocent. You think so too, and that strengthens my belief. Alas, Victor, when lies can look so much like truth, who can be sure of happiness? I feel as though I am walking along the edge of a cliff, while thousands press around me and try to force me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, while the murderer escapes. He walks freely through the world, perhaps even respected. But even if I were condemned to die on the scaffold for the same crimes, I would not trade places with such a wretch.\u201d\\n\\nI listened to her words in the deepest agony. Though not by my own hand, I was in truth the murderer. Elizabeth saw my suffering in my face and, gently taking my hand, said, \u201cMy dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have affected me deeply, God knows; but I am not as miserable as you are.\u201d\"}"
        }
