ht him.\\n\\n\\\"Truly,\\\" said King Arthur, \\\"that knight of many colors is a good knight.\\\" Therefore the king called Sir Launcelot to him and asked him to meet that knight. \\\"Sir,\\\" said Launcelot, \\\"I can readily bring myself to spare him at this time, for he has labored enough today. When a good knight has done so well in one day, it is not the part of a good knight to take away his honor, especially when he sees that a knight has performed such great deeds. Perhaps,\\\" said Sir Launcelot, \\\"his cause is here today, and perhaps he is best loved by this lady of all who are here. For I can see that he strains and pushes himself to do great deeds. Therefore, as for me, he shall have the honor today. Even if it were in my power to take it from him, I would not.\\\"\"}"
