{"request_id":"63081beac1bf6648d3192e42","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals; few mourners attended them, and onlookers regarded them as signs of the gravest disaster. Some watched the processions with frantic intensity; others ran away in fear; some wept openly.

After providing immediate aid to the sick, Adrian’s chief effort had been to conceal the plague’s symptoms and advance from London’s inhabitants. He knew that fear and gloomy expectation were powerful allies of illness; that despair and anxious brooding made the human body especially vulnerable to infection. Thus no shocking scenes were visible: the shops were generally open, and some flow of pedestrians was maintained. Yet though the outward appearance of an infected city had been prevented, London seemed greatly altered to me, who had not seen it since the visitation began. There were no carriages, and grass had grown high in the streets; the houses looked abandoned; most shutters were closed; and the people I encountered wore a ghastly, frightened expression, utterly unlike Londoners’ usual businesslike manner. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading there looked even more bleak and empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded; it was the hour when he received visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and watched the petitioners enter and leave. They were people of the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had vanished with the halt in trade and in the money-making activity, in all its forms, so characteristic of our country. The arrivals bore an air of anxiety, sometimes terror, sharply contrasting with the resigned, even contented faces of those who had been received. In their brisker movements and cheerful expressions, I could see my friend’s influence. Two o’clock struck, after which no one else was admitted; those turned away departed sullenly or sadly, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was struck by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He was no longer bowed to the ground like an over-tended spring flower which, growing beyond its strength, is weighed down even by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes shone, his face was calm, and an air of focused energy filled his whole person, very unlike his former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting petitions or recording notes from that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners still waited nearby. I admired his fairness and patience. Those able to live outside London, he urged to leave at once, providing them with the means to do so."}

