          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7fefe373f472d1fbd1aa6252\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one. So far from harshly demanding what was owed to him, he had given up his claims willingly, cast himself upon a world that offered him no favour, made a place for himself in it, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the poor and debt-burdened estate under written orders to spare the people, giving them whatever little could be given\u2014such fuel as the heavy creditors allowed in winter, and such produce as could be saved from their grasp in summer. No doubt he had stated these facts in his own defence, so that they must now be clear.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate decision Charles Darnay had begun to form: he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor in the old tale, winds and currents had carried him within reach of the Loadstone Rock; it was pulling him toward itself, and he had to go. Every thought that came before him drove him onward, faster and more steadily, toward that terrible attraction. His hidden uneasiness had been that evil purposes were being carried out in his unhappy country by evil men, while he, who knew he was better than they were, was not there trying to prevent bloodshed and defend mercy and humanity. That uneasiness, half suppressed and half accusing him, had led him to compare himself with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so strong. That comparison, unfavourable to himself, had immediately brought Monseigneur's mocking words to mind; they had wounded him deeply. Then came Stryver's sneers, which were especially crude and painful for old reasons. After them had come Gabelle's letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, in danger of death, to his justice, honour, and good name.\\n\\nHis decision was made. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to sail on until he struck it. He knew of no rock and saw scarcely any danger. The purpose behind what he had done, though he had left it unfinished, made him believe that France would gratefully recognize it when he appeared to claim it. Then that glorious vision of doing good, so often the hopeful mirage of good minds, rose before him. In his illusion, he even saw himself with some power to guide the raging Revolution, which was running so terribly wild.\"}"
