          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"095979c9002ed075c40d6ff1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke that morning, he wondered where he was. He raised himself, rubbed his eyes, and glanced about. Then he understood. It was the cool gray dawn, and a delightful feeling of rest and peace lay in the deep, all-pervading stillness of the woods. Not a leaf moved; no sound disturbed great Nature\u2019s meditation. Dewdrops, like beads, rested on the leaves and grass. A white coating of ashes lay over the fire, and a slender blue wisp of smoke climbed straight upward. Joe and Huck were still asleep.\\n\\nFar off in the forest a bird cried, and another replied; soon the tapping of a woodpecker could be heard. Little by little the cool, shadowy gray of morning turned white, and just as gradually sounds increased and life began to show itself. The wonder of Nature casting aside sleep and setting to work revealed itself before the thoughtful boy. A small green worm crawled across a wet leaf, now and then raising two-thirds of its body into the air and \u201csniffing around,\u201d before moving on again\u2014for it was measuring, Tom declared; and as the worm came toward him of its own accord, he remained as motionless as a stone, his hopes alternately rising and sinking as the creature advanced or appeared disposed to turn elsewhere. At last it paused for a trying instant, its curved body lifted in the air, then came down firmly upon Tom\u2019s leg and began traveling over him. His heart was full of joy, for this signified that he would have a new suit of clothes\u2014beyond all doubt, a showy pirate uniform. Next a line of ants emerged from no particular place and set about their business; one bravely struggled past carrying a dead spider five times its own size, and hauled it directly up a tree trunk. A brown, spotted ladybug climbed the dizzy summit of a blade of grass, and Tom bent close and said, \u201cLady-bug, lady-bug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone,\u201d whereupon she spread her wings and flew away to look into it. This did not surprise the boy, since he had long known the insect to be gullible concerning fires, and had taken advantage of its simplicity more than once. Then came a tumblebug, stoutly pushing at its ball, and Tom touched it to watch it draw its legs against its body and feign death. By now the birds were in full riot. A catbird, the Northern mocker, alighted in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and poured out imitations of its neighbors in delighted ecstasy; then a sharp-voiced jay flashed down like blue fire and perched on a twig nearly within the boy\u2019s reach, tilted its head aside, and studied the strangers with eager curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large fox squirrel came scurrying by, stopping now and then to sit upright, inspect the boys, and chatter at them, for the wild creatures had likely never seen human beings before and scarcely knew whether they ought to fear them.\"}"
