          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e6dbe5e9ac5f12b0ce3c9bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not a syllable, however, escaped him on the subject until their visitor had departed for the evening; but the moment he was gone, he turned to his daughter and said,--\\n\\n\u201cJane, I congratulate you. You are to be a very happy woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he answered, \u201cand it gives me great pleasure to think that you will be so happily established. I do not doubt that you will do very well together. Your dispositions are not at all dissimilar. You are both so yielding, that nothing will ever be decided; so good-natured, that every servant will impose upon you; and so liberal, that you will invariably spend beyond your income.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not. Imprudence or carelessness in pecuniary concerns would be inexcusable in _me_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSpend beyond their income! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d exclaimed his wife, \u201cwhat can you mean? Why, he has four or five thousand a year, and perhaps even more.\u201d Then, turning to her daughter, \u201cOh, my dear, dear Jane, how happy I am! I am certain I shall not sleep a wink all night. I knew it would happen. I always declared it must come about at last. I was sure you could not be so handsome for nothing! I recollect, the very instant I saw him, when he first came into Hertfordshire last year, thinking how probable it was that you should be united. Oh, he is the handsomest young man ever seen!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia were entirely forgotten. Jane was, beyond all rivalry, her favourite child. At that instant she thought of no one else. Her younger sisters soon began to solicit her for those pleasures which she might hereafter have it in her power to grant.\\n\\nMary asked for the use of the library at Netherfield; and Kitty earnestly begged for a few balls there each winter.\\n\\nFrom this time Bingley was, naturally, a daily visitor at Longbourn; often arriving before breakfast, and always staying until after supper; except when some barbarous neighbour, whom it was impossible to detest sufficiently, had invited him to dinner, and he considered himself bound to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now had little opportunity for conversation with her sister; for while he was there, Jane had no attention to spare for anybody else: yet Elizabeth found herself of considerable service to them both during those intervals of separation which must occasionally arise. When Jane was absent, he always attached himself to Elizabeth for the pleasure of speaking of her; and after Bingley had gone, Jane regularly sought the same consolation.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d she said one evening, \u201cby assuring me that he was wholly unaware of my being in town last spring!\u201d\"}"
