          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7202bad1f7827534ec7c0126\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He chose Friday, August 17, for his appearance, and spent most of the day examining his wardrobe. At last he settled on a large slouch hat with a red feather, a shroud frilled at the wrists and neck, and a rusty dagger. Toward evening, a fierce rainstorm began, and the wind blew so hard that every window and door in the old house shook and rattled. It was, in fact, exactly the kind of weather he loved. His plan was as follows. He would quietly make his way to Washington Otis\u2019s room, gibber at the foot of his bed, and stab himself three times in the throat to the sound of slow music. He held a particular grudge against Washington, knowing perfectly well that the boy was the one who regularly removed the famous Canterville bloodstain with Pinkerton\u2019s Paragon Detergent. Once he had reduced the reckless, foolish youth to abject terror, he would go on to the room occupied by the United States Minister and his wife. There he would lay a clammy hand on Mrs. Otis\u2019s forehead while hissing the dreadful secrets of the charnel house into her trembling husband\u2019s ear. As for little Virginia, he had not entirely decided what to do. She had never insulted him, and she was pretty and gentle. A few hollow groans from the wardrobe, he thought, would be more than enough; or, if they failed to wake her, he might clutch at the counterpane with fingers twitching like those of a palsied man. As for the twins, he was firmly resolved to teach them a lesson. First, naturally, he would sit on their chests, producing the suffocating feeling of a nightmare. Then, since their beds stood close together, he would stand between them in the shape of a green, ice-cold corpse until they were paralysed with fear. Finally, he would cast off the shroud and crawl around the room with bleached white bones and one rolling eyeball, playing \u2018Dumb Daniel, or the Suicide\u2019s Skeleton,\u2019 a role in which he had more than once made a great impression and which he considered fully equal to his celebrated part of \u2018Martin the Maniac, or the Masked Mystery.\u2019\\n\\nAt half past ten, he heard the family going to bed. For a while he was disturbed by wild shrieks of laughter from the twins, who, with the carefree high spirits of schoolboys, were clearly amusing themselves before retiring. But by a quarter past eleven everything was quiet, and when midnight struck, he set out. An owl beat against the windowpanes, a raven croaked from the old yew tree, and the wind moaned around the house like a lost soul. Yet the Otis family slept unaware of their fate, and above the rain and storm he could hear the steady snoring of the United States Minister.\"}"
