          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2fd782eb4c3fcb41ffc04bfc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Still, despite all this, the boy grew increasingly melancholy, pale, and downcast. She added hot baths, sitz baths, shower baths, and plunges. The boy remained as gloomy as a hearse. She began to support the water treatment with a thin oatmeal diet and blister plasters. She measured his capacity as if he were a jug, filling him every day with patent remedies.\\n\\nBy this time, Tom had become indifferent to persecution. This stage filled the old lady\u2019s heart with alarm. That indifference had to be broken up at any price. Then she heard of Pain-killer for the first time. She immediately ordered a large supply. She tasted it and was overcome with gratitude. It was nothing but liquid fire. She abandoned the water treatment and everything else, placing all her faith in Pain-killer. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and waited with the deepest anxiety for the effect. Her worries vanished at once, and her soul was at peace again, for the \u201cindifference\u201d was gone. The boy could not have displayed a wilder, more eager interest if she had lit a fire beneath him.\\n\\nTom decided it was time to rouse himself. This kind of life might have been romantic enough in his afflicted state, but it was beginning to contain too little sentiment and too much disturbing variety. So he considered several ways of getting relief, and at last thought of pretending to like Pain-killer. He asked for it so often that he became a nuisance, until his aunt finally told him to help himself and stop bothering her. Had it been Sid, she would have felt no doubt to spoil her pleasure; but because it was Tom, she secretly watched the bottle. She saw that the medicine truly was diminishing, though it never occurred to her that the boy was improving the health of a crack in the sitting-room floor with it.\\n\\nOne day, Tom was dosing the crack when his aunt\u2019s yellow cat came by, purring, staring greedily at the