          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63532dfed4c2fbd09afcdd1f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At noon on Saturday I went to the well and watched for a time. Merlin was still burning smoke-powders, clawing at the air, and muttering gibberish as vigorously as ever, though he looked rather disheartened, since naturally he had not raised so much as a drop of sweat from that well. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow does it look by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am at this moment engaged in testing the mightiest enchantment known to the masters of occult arts in the Eastern lands; if it fail me, nothing can prevail. Peace, till I have done.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that blackened the whole countryside, and it must have troubled the hermits, for the wind blew toward them and carried it down over their dens in a thick, rolling fog. He poured forth a matching flood of words, twisted his body, and slashed the air with his hands in a most remarkable fashion. At the end of twenty minutes he sank down, panting and nearly spent. Then came the abbot with several hundred monks and nuns, followed by a crowd of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all attracted by the tremendous smoke and all in a high state of excitement. The abbot anxiously asked for the outcome. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any exertion of mortal power could break the spell that holds these waters, this which I have but now attempted would have done so. It has failed; and thereby I now know that what I feared is proven true: the token of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name none may speak and live, has cast his spell upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever shall, who can pierce the secret of that spell; and without that secret none can undo it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done all that man can do. Permit me to depart.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally this put the abbot in considerable distress. He turned toward me, his face showing it plainly, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYe have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSome of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then\u2014not all! Which part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has laid his spell upon the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod's wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not surely? Ye mean, not surely?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWherefore, ye mean also that when he says none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he says something not necessarily true. There are circumstances in which an attempt to break it may have some chance\u2014that is, some slight, some trifling chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
