{"request_id":"cd039fd387438c0122cf5bfc","rewritten_text":"When I had done weeping, reason and recollection came back to me, and I began more calmly to consider what had occurred, and what course became me to pursue--Only a few hours had gone by, yet a vast change had taken place in all that concerned me--the work of many years had been accomplished since morning: my father was as one dead to me, and for an instant I seemed to see him, with his white hairs, stretched in his coffin, while I--my youth gone in premature age--wept over his seasonable death. Yet it was not thus; I was still young, Oh! much too young, nor was he dead to others; but I, most unhappy, was never again to see or speak with him. I must flee from him more eagerly than from my bitterest foe: whether in solitude or among cities, I must behold him no more. The thought took away my breath with anguish, and, fastening itself upon my fancy, left me for a while unable to pursue any connected course of reflection. Henceforth, I thought, I would dwell in the dreariest retirement. I would go to the Continent and become a nun; not from religious motives, for I was no Catholic, but that I might be shut out from the world for ever. There I should discover solitude in which to weep, while the voices of life could never come to me.

But my father; my dear and most miserable father? Would he die? Would he never conquer the fierce passion which now ruled him with pitiless sway? Might he not, many, many years hence, when age had extinguished the burning feelings he now endured, might he not then become a father to me once more? This thought smoothed my brow, and I felt--and wept to feel--a half-sorrowful smile steal from my lips their look of pain: I ventured to cherish happier hopes for my future life; years must intervene, but hope would give them wings and make them pass lightly, or, if they moved heavily, yet they would pass, and I had not lost my father for ever. Let him pass another sixteen years in lonely wandering: let him again pour forth his wild lamentations to the immense forests and awful cataracts of another land: let him once more encounter dreadful peril and hardships that subdued the soul: let the fierce southern sun again scorch his passion-wasted cheeks, and the cold rains of night descend upon him and freeze his blood.

To such a life, unhappy father, I consign thee!--Go!--Let thy days be spent among savages, and thy nights beneath heaven's canopy! Let thy limbs be worn away, thy heart grow cold, and every remnant of youth perish within thee!"}

