{"request_id":"947a12f525eaafbf64c2c935","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have while Fate still held this dreadful fortune in the balance? The marriage had to be delayed, whatever the cost. On that point he was completely determined. Deeply as he loved the girl, and though the slightest touch of her fingers as they sat together sent a thrill of perfect joy through every nerve in his body, he saw just as clearly where his duty lay. He knew he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once it was done, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton and place his life in her hands without fear of wrongdoing. Once it was done, he could hold her in his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him or lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner, the better for them both.

Many men in his situation would have chosen the easy path of pleasure instead of the hard climb of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure before principle. His love was more than simple passion, and Sybil represented to him everything good and noble. At first he naturally recoiled from what he had been asked to do, but the feeling soon faded. His heart told him it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that no other choice was open to him. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. Terrible as the task before him was, he knew he must not let selfishness defeat love. Sooner or later, all of us are called on to make the same choice; the same question is put to us all. It came to Lord Arthur early in life, before his nature had been ruined by the cold calculation of middle age, or his heart eaten away by the shallow, fashionable selfishness of the day. He did not hesitate to do his duty. Fortunately for him, he was not merely a dreamer or an idle amateur. If he had been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and allowed indecision to ruin his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him, life meant action rather than thought. He possessed the rarest thing of all: common sense.

By then, the wild and confused feelings of the night before had completely disappeared. He looked back almost ashamed at his mad wandering through the streets and his fierce emotional suffering. The very honesty of his pain now made it seem unreal. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rage and despair over what could not be avoided."}

