          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fafdb8a176716c6721653929\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Farmers, butchers, cattle drivers, hawkers, boys, thieves, loafers, and vagabonds of every low sort were packed together in one mass. The drovers whistled, dogs barked, oxen bellowed and struggled, sheep bleated, pigs grunted and squealed; hawkers cried out, while shouts, curses, and quarrels rose on every side. Bells rang and voices roared from every public house. There was crowding, pushing, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling; a harsh, confused noise came from every corner of the market. Unwashed, unshaven, ragged, dirty figures hurried back and forth, forcing their way into and out of the crowd. It was a deafening, bewildering scene that overwhelmed the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, pulling Oliver along behind him, shoved his way through the densest part of the crowd. He paid little attention to the many sights and sounds that amazed the boy. He nodded two or three times to friends passing by and refused just as many offers of a morning drink. He kept moving until they were free of the uproar and had gone through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, looking up at the clock of St. Andrew\u2019s Church. \u201cNearly seven! You must move faster. Come, don\u2019t start lagging already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nAs he spoke, Mr. Sikes gave his small companion\u2019s wrist a sharp tug. Oliver quickened his pace into a sort of trot, halfway between walking fast and running, and did his best to keep up with the housebreaker\u2019s long strides.\\n\\nThey went on at this speed until they had passed Hyde Park Corner and were heading toward Kensington. Then Sikes slowed down until an empty cart, some distance behind them, caught up. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d written on it, he asked the driver, as politely as he could manage, whether he would give them a ride as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump in,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, he\u2019s my boy,\u201d Sikes replied, staring hard at Oliver and absent-mindedly putting his hand into the pocket where the pistol was.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a little too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, my lad?\u201d the driver asked, seeing that Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, take my hand, Ned. Get in!\u201d\\n\\nCalling Oliver by this name, he helped him into the cart. The driver pointed to a pile of sacks and told him to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed the mile markers, Oliver wondered more and more where his companion meant to take him. They passed Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, yet continued as steadily as though their journey had only just begun.\"}"
