es in the highest terms.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, any commendation of me will be needless. You must permit me to form my own judgment, and do me the honour of believing what I say. I wish you both very happy and very wealthy; and, in declining your hand, I do all that lies in my power to keep you from being otherwise. By making this offer to me, you must have satisfied your delicate feelings concerning my family, and may inherit the Longbourn estate whenever it becomes yours without any reproach to yourself. The affair may therefore be regarded as completely settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins addressed her again\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I next do myself the honour of speaking with you on this matter, I shall hope for a more favourable reply than the one you have now given. Yet I am far from charging you with cruelty at present, for I know it is the settled custom of your sex to refuse a man upon his first application; and perhaps even now you have said as much to encourage my suit as is compatible with the genuine delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
