          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb2af4eac07998e456f4880b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even the mere reading of the dead men's luxury was marvellous.\\n\\nThen he gave himself to embroideries and to the tapestries that served in place of frescoes in the cold chambers of the northern countries of Europe. As he pursued the subject\u2014and he had always the singular power of becoming wholly engrossed, for the time, in whatever he undertook\u2014he was almost made melancholy by the thought of the havoc time wrought upon lovely and wonderful things. He, at least, had escaped it. Summer succeeded summer, and the yellow jonquils flowered and faded again and again, and nights of terror retold the tale of their disgrace, yet he remained unchanged. No winter had spoiled his face or dimmed his flowerlike freshness. How otherwise it was with material things! Whither had they vanished? Where was the great crocus-hued robe, showing the gods in battle with the giants, which brown girls had wrought for Athena's delight? Where was the vast velarium Nero had spread over the Colosseum at Rome, that Titanic purple sail upon which the starry heavens were pictured, with Apollo driving a car drawn by white horses with gilded reins? He desired to see the strange napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, displaying every delicacy and dish that might be required for a banquet; King Chilperic's funeral cloth, with its three hundred golden bees; the fantastic garments that stirred the Bishop of Pontus to indignation, figured with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014all, indeed, that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat once worn by Charles of Orleans, whose sleeves bore the embroidered verses of a song opening \u201c_Madame, je suis tout joyeux_,\u201d while the music to the words was worked in gold thread, each note, square in shape in those days, being made of four pearls. He read of the chamber prepared in the palace at Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, adorned with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrots, made in broidery, and blazoned with the king\u2019s arms, and five hundred and sixty-one butterflies, whose wings were similarly ornamented with the arms of the queen, the whole worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de Medicis had fashioned for herself a mourning-bed of black velvet strewn with crescents and suns. Its curtains were damask, patterned with leafy wreaths and garlands on a ground of gold and silver, their edges fringed with pearl embroideries; it stood in a room hung with rows of the queen's devices, cut in black velvet upon cloth of silver. Louis XIV. possessed gold-embroidered caryatides, fifteen feet high, in his apartment.\"}"
