          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3ec0efbd8071e63b7b379957\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he kept one secret from these dear friends, a secret he had cherished since his earliest years; and though he loved his fellow students, he would not entrust it to the sensitivity or sympathy of any among them. He was in love. He feared the strength of his passion might become a subject for their ridicule, and he could not endure their profaning it by treating as trivial and passing what he felt to be the life of his life.\\n\\nA gentleman of modest means lived near his family estate and had three lovely daughters. The eldest was by far the most beautiful, though her beauty was only an addition to her other gifts\u2014her mind was clear and strong, and her nature angelically gentle. She and my father had been playmates since infancy. Diana, even as a child, had been a favorite of his mother; and this affection increased as the beautiful, lively girl grew older, so that during his school and college holidays they were constantly together. Novels, and all the various ways by which young people in civilized life are brought to know of passions before they truly feel them, had strongly affected one so especially open to every impression. At eleven, Diana was his favorite companion, yet he already spoke the language of love. Though she was nearly two years older than he, the nature of her education had left her more childlike, at least in her understanding and expression of feeling. She received his ardent declarations innocently, and returned them without knowing what they meant. She had read no novels and knew only her younger sisters; what could she understand of the difference between love and friendship? When the growth of her mind revealed the true nature of their relationship, her affections were already bound to her friend, and all she feared was that other attractions or fickleness might lead him to break his childhood vows.\\n\\nBut their feelings grew more ardent and tender each day. It was a passion that had grown as he grew; it had become woven into every faculty and every feeling, and could be lost only with life. No one knew of their love but their own two hearts; yet though in all other matters\u2014and even in this\u2014he feared the judgment of his companions, for loving so truly one beneath him in fortune, nothing could for a moment shake his resolve to unite himself with her as soon as he could gather enough courage to face the difficulties he was determined to overcome.\\n\\nDiana was entirely worthy of his deepest love.\"}"
