{"request_id":"ffbe0c26cff740eb51600920","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock, I had reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It was therefore clear to me that my rate of ascent was not merely increasing, but that its acceleration would have been noticeable, though less so, even if I had not released the ballast I did. The pain in my head and ears returned violently at intervals, and my nose still bled from time to time; yet, overall, I suffered far less than might have been expected. Still, I found it harder and harder to breathe with every passing moment, and each breath brought an unpleasant spasmodic tightening of my chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and prepared it for immediate use.

“The sight of the earth at this stage of my ascent was truly beautiful. To the west, north, and south, as far as I could see, stretched a limitless expanse of seemingly calm ocean, which deepened in blue with every moment and had already begun to show a faint curvature. Far to the east, though perfectly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small part of northern Africa. Not a trace of any individual building could be seen, and humanity’s proudest cities had vanished entirely from the face of the earth. From the Rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a dim speck, the dark Mediterranean, scattered with bright islands as the heavens are scattered with stars, extended eastward as far as my sight could reach, until at last its whole body of water seemed to plunge headlong over the abyss of the horizon; and I found myself listening intently for the echoes of that mighty cataract. Above me, the sky was jet-black, and the stars shone with brilliant clarity.

“The pigeons seemed to be suffering greatly by this time, and I decided to set them free. I first untied one, a beautiful pigeon mottled in gray, and placed him on the edge of the wickerwork. He seemed intensely distressed, looking anxiously about, fluttering his wings, and cooing loudly, but he could not be persuaded to leave the car. At last I picked him up and threw him some six yards from the balloon. Yet instead of trying to descend, as I had expected, he struggled fiercely to return, uttering sharp, piercing cries all the while. At length he managed to regain his former place on the rim, but scarcely had he done so when his head fell upon his breast, and he dropped dead inside the car.”"}

