{"request_id":"1d2b75bf0148e2a71d6c2a1c","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I felt no apprehension on her behalf. She was what the world terms a remarkable woman, quite able to conduct affairs without any help from me. To speak frankly, I think she had always regarded me as an idle lad, a mere encumbrance, fit for nothing save castle-building in the air, and was not sorry to be rid of me. It was a gloomy night when I took leave of her; and, accompanied by the three creditors who had caused me so much annoyance, as my aides-de-camp, I conveyed the balloon, car, and equipment by an indirect route to the station where the remaining articles had been stored. There we discovered everything untouched, and I at once set about my work.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I have already observed, was dark; not a star was visible; and a fine rain, descending now and then, made us exceedingly uncomfortable. My principal concern, however, was for the balloon, which, despite the varnish intended to protect it, was becoming somewhat weighty from the damp; the powder, too, was exposed to injury. I accordingly kept my three duns busily employed, packing ice about the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. Yet they continued to plague me with inquiries concerning my purpose with all this machinery, and showed great displeasure at the dreadful toil to which I subjected them. They could not understand, they declared, what advantage could arise from being soaked to the skin merely to assist in such frightful incantations. I began to feel alarmed, and labored with all my strength, for I truly believe the fools imagined I had made a bargain with the devil, and that, in brief, my present occupation was no better than it ought to be. I was consequently much afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to quiet them by promising to discharge every account in full as soon as I had brought the undertaking to an end. Naturally, they put their own construction upon these assurances; doubtless supposing that I was, in any event, about to acquire immense sums of ready money. So long as I paid all I owed them, with a little extra for their trouble, I dare say they cared scarcely at all what became of my soul or my body.

“In about four hours and a half, I found the balloon sufficiently filled. I therefore fastened on the car and placed all my instruments within it—not omitting the condensing apparatus, an ample store of water, and a considerable supply of provisions, such as pemmican, which contains much nourishment in comparatively small compass."}

