          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3265a688c957d6ff0492e622\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wraps him up in de paper and stuffs a piece ob it in he mouff\u2014dat was de fashion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you believe, then, that your master was actually bitten by the beetle, and that its bite made him ill?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI do n\u2019t believe noffin \u2019bout it\u2014I knows it. What for he dream \u2019bout de goole so much, if it ain\u2019t \u2019cause de goole-bug bit him? Ise heerd ob dem goole-bugs afore now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut how can you tell that he dreams of gold?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow I tell? why, \u2019cause he talks \u2019bout it in he sleep\u2014dat\u2019s how I knows.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jup, maybe you are right; but to what happy circumstance do I owe the honor of your visit to-day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat de matter, massa?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you brought any word from Mr. Legrand?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, massa, I brung dis here pissel;\u201d and Jupiter then gave me a note, which read as follows:\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR \u2014\u2014Why have I not seen you for so considerable a time? I trust you have not been foolish enough to take offence at any slight _brusquerie_ on my part; but no, that is unlikely. Since I last saw you, I have had much cause for anxiety. I have something to communicate, though I scarcely know how to say it, or even whether I ought to say it at all.\\n\\n\u201cI have been somewhat unwell for several days, and poor old Jup vexes me, nearly beyond endurance, with his kindly meant attentions. Would you credit it?\u2014the other day he had made ready a great stick, with which to punish me for slipping away from him and passing the day, _solus_, among the hills of the main land. I truly think my sickly appearance alone preserved me from a flogging.\\n\\n\u201cI have added nothing to my cabinet since we met.\\n\\n\u201cIf you can possibly make it convenient, come across with Jupiter. _Do_ come. I desire to see you to-_night_, on business of importance. I assure you it is of the _highest_ importance.\\n\\n\u201cEver yours, \u201cWILLIAM LEGRAND\u201d.\\n\\nThere was something in the note\u2019s tone that caused me much uneasiness. Its entire manner differed greatly from Legrand\u2019s usual style. What could he be imagining? What fresh whim had seized his excitable mind? What \u201cbusiness of the highest importance\u201d could he possibly have to conduct? Jupiter\u2019s description of him promised nothing good. I feared that the unbroken weight of misfortune had at last completely disturbed my friend\u2019s reason. Without an instant\u2019s delay, therefore, I made ready to go with the negro.\\n\\nWhen we reached the wharf, I observed a scythe and three spades, all seemingly new, lying in the bottom of the boat in which we were to set out.\\n\\n\u201cWhat does all this mean, Jup?\u201d\"}"
