          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b7dfe02cdc887b881b3da1a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI beg leave to remark, Sir Walter,\u201d said Mr Shepherd one morning at Kellynch Hall, putting down the newspaper, \u201cthat the present moment is greatly in our favour. This peace will send all our wealthy naval officers ashore. They will all be seeking homes. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, for a choice of tenants\u2014highly responsible tenants. Many a handsome fortune has been gained during the war. If a rich admiral should happen to come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a most fortunate man, Shepherd,\u201d answered Sir Walter; \u201cthat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would indeed be a prize to him; perhaps the greatest prize of all, though he had captured ever so many before; eh, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he understood he was expected to do, at this pleasantry, and then continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI venture to observe, Sir Walter, that in business matters, naval gentlemen are good people to deal with. I have had some small acquaintance with their manner of conducting business; and I freely confess that their ideas are very liberal, and that they are as likely to prove desirable tenants as any class of people one might encounter. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would presume to suggest is this: if, in consequence of any reports spreading of your intention\u2014which must be regarded as possible, since we know how hard it is to keep the actions and plans of one part of the world from the attention and curiosity of another; consequence has its burden; I, John Shepherd, might hide whatever family concerns I pleased, because no one would consider me worth watching; but Sir Walter Elliot has eyes fixed upon him which may be very difficult to escape\u2014and so I venture thus far, that it would not much surprise me if, despite all our care, some report of the truth were to get abroad. In that case, as I was about to say, since applications will certainly follow, I should consider any from our wealthy naval commanders especially deserving of notice; and I beg leave to add that I can be here within two hours at any time, to spare you the trouble of answering.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter merely nodded. Soon after, however, getting up and walking about the room, he said sarcastically\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are not many among the gentlemen of the navy, I should suppose, who would not be astonished to find themselves in a house of this kind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would certainly look about them and bless their good luck,\u201d said Mrs Clay, who was present; her father had driven her over, since nothing was so beneficial to Mrs Clay\u2019s health as a drive to Kellynch: \u201cbut I entirely agree with my father that a sailor could be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
