          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca6cece9bea5feebe1e4657e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After seeing our friend duly established in his new office, we directed our thoughts toward Windsor. Its proximity to London prevented our leaving Raymond and Perdita from seeming like a grievous separation. We bade them farewell in the Protectoral Palace. It was pleasant enough to watch my sister, as though entering into the spirit of a play, strive to assume her position with suitable dignity. Her inward pride and her modest manner were more than ever in conflict. Her shyness was no affectation, but sprang from a fear of not being rightly valued, from that slight regard for the world's neglect which likewise marked Raymond. Yet Perdita considered others more habitually than he did; and some of her diffidence came from a desire to remove from those about her any feeling of inferiority\u2014a feeling that had never entered her own mind. By birth and education, Idris would have been more suited to the forms of ceremony; but the ease with which habit enabled her to perform them made them wearisome, whereas Perdita, despite every disadvantage, plainly delighted in her new station. Her mind was too crowded with fresh ideas to suffer much at our departure; she took leave of us affectionately and promised soon to visit us, but did not lament the causes of our parting. Raymond's spirits were without bounds; he scarcely knew how to employ his newly acquired power. His mind teemed with projects, though he had settled upon none; but he assured himself, his friends, and the world that the era of his Protectorship would be distinguished by some deed of extraordinary glory. So we spoke of them and reflected, while, fewer in number, we returned to Windsor Castle. We rejoiced intensely in our escape from political disturbance, and sought our retirement with renewed eagerness. We lacked no employment; yet my ardent temper now devoted itself solely to intellectual labour, and I found severe study an admirable remedy for calming a fever of spirit which idleness would, I doubt not, have awakened. Perdita had allowed us to bring Clara back to Windsor; and she, together with my two beautiful children, furnished continual interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe sole thing that troubled our tranquillity was Adrian's health. It visibly failed, though no symptom revealed the nature of his illness, unless his brighter eyes, lively expression, and flushed cheeks gave us cause to fear consumption; yet he knew neither pain nor apprehension. He applied himself eagerly to books, and rested from study in the company he loved best\u2014that of his sister and myself.\"}"
