{"request_id":"9380489718ba6f16ee0da926","rewritten_text":"You see, I had put my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things; but it was the sort of helmet you could not remove by yourself. That had not occurred to me when I put it there; indeed, I did not know it. I thought it would be especially convenient there. And now the thought of its being there, so near and handy, yet impossible to reach, made everything worse and harder to endure. Yes, the thing you cannot get is usually the thing you want most; everyone has noticed that. Well, it took my mind off everything else; took it completely away and fixed it on my helmet; and mile after mile it stayed there, imagining the handkerchief, seeing the handkerchief in my mind; and it was bitterly aggravating to have salty sweat trickling into my eyes when I could not reach it. It may seem a small matter on paper, but it was no small matter at all; it was the most genuine misery. I would not say so if it were not true. I decided that next time I would carry a reticule, no matter how it looked or what people said. Naturally, these iron-clad fellows of the Round Table would consider it scandalous, and perhaps raise hell about it, but for my part, give me comfort first and style afterward. So we plodded on, and now and then crossed a patch of dust that rose in clouds, got into my nose, and made me sneeze and cry; and naturally I said things I should not have said. I do not deny it. I am no better than anyone else.

We could not seem to meet anyone in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in, that was fortunate for the ogre—at least, for an ogre carrying a handkerchief. Most knights would have thought only of taking his armor; but if I got his bandanna, he could keep his hardware, as far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile, it was getting hotter and hotter inside. You see, the sun kept beating down and heating the iron more and more. Well, when you are hot in that fashion, every little thing irritates you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that annoyed me; besides, I could not stand the shield slapping and banging, now against my chest, now around my back. And if I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and squealed with the tiresome sound of a wheelbarrow; and since we made no breeze at that pace, I was likely to be roasted in that stove. Besides, the more quietly you moved, the more heavily the iron settled on you, and the more tons you seemed to weigh with every passing minute."}

