          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e97d17da7877afde9deb066\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Lacking ordinary means, the analyst enters into the spirit of his opponent, identifies himself with it, and often sees at once the only methods\u2014sometimes absurdly simple ones\u2014by which he may lead him into error or rush him into miscalculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been recognized for its effect on what is called the calculating faculty; and men of the highest intellect have been known to take an apparently inexplicable pleasure in it while dismissing chess as frivolous. Without question, nothing of a similar kind places such demands upon the power of analysis. The finest chess player in Christendom may be little more than the finest player of chess; but skill at whist suggests the capacity to succeed in all those more important contests in which mind struggles against mind. By skill, I mean that perfection in the game which includes an understanding of all the sources from which legitimate advantage may be drawn. These are not merely numerous but varied, and often lie in corners of thought wholly beyond the reach of ordinary understanding. To observe closely is to remember clearly; and in this respect the concentrated chess player will do well enough at whist, while Hoyle's rules\u2014being founded on the mere mechanics of the game\u2014are generally easy to understand. Thus, a retentive memory and adherence to \u201cthe book\u201d are commonly considered the whole of good play. But it is in matters beyond the bounds of mere rule that the analyst's skill reveals itself. In silence, he makes a multitude of observations and inferences. Perhaps his companions do the same; yet the difference in the amount of information gained lies less in the soundness of the inference than in the quality of the observation. The essential knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player limits himself in no way; nor, because the game is his object, does he reject conclusions drawn from matters outside it. He studies his partner's face, carefully comparing it with that of each opponent. He considers how the cards are arranged in every hand, often counting trump by trump and honor by honor from the glances their holders cast upon them. He notices every change of expression as play proceeds, gathering material for thought from signs of certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way a trick is gathered up, he judges whether the person who takes it can win another in that suit. He recognizes a feint by the manner in which a card is thrown upon the table. A casual or unintended word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, together with the anxiety or carelessness shown in concealing it; the counting of tricks and the order in which they are arranged; embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or nervousness\u2014all offer his seemingly intuitive perception signs of the true state of affairs.\"}"
