          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e7c38388fc750f3a0f22d88f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray could not escape the influence of this book. Or perhaps it would be truer to say that he never tried to escape it. He ordered from Paris no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition and had them bound in different colours, so they could suit his changing moods and the shifting whims of a nature over which he sometimes seemed to have almost completely lost control. The hero, the remarkable young Parisian in whom romantic and scientific impulses were so oddly combined, became for him a kind of prophetic image of himself. Indeed, the entire book seemed to tell the story of his own life before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect, he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastical hero. He never knew\u2014indeed, never had reason to know\u2014that rather grotesque fear of mirrors, polished metal, and still water that seized the young Parisian so early in life, caused by the sudden decline of a beau who had once apparently been so striking. With an almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps cruelty has a place in nearly every joy, certainly in every pleasure\u2014he would read the latter part of the book, with its truly tragic, if somewhat overstated, account of the grief and despair of a man who had himself lost what he had valued most in others and in the world.\\n\\nFor the extraordinary beauty that had so captivated Basil Hallward, along with many others, seemed never to abandon him. Even those who had heard the worst things about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumours of his way of life spread through London and became club gossip\u2014could not believe anything dishonourable of him when they saw him. He always looked like someone who had kept himself untouched by the world. Men speaking crudely fell silent when Dorian Gray entered the room. There was something in the purity of his face that reproached them. His mere presence seemed to bring back the memory of the innocence they had corrupted. They wondered how someone as charming and graceful as he could have escaped the taint of an age both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, after returning home from one of those mysterious, extended absences that prompted such strange speculation among those who were his friends, or believed themselves to be, he would creep upstairs to the locked room, open it with the key that now never left him, and stand before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him, holding a mirror: looking first at the wicked, aging face on the canvas, then at the beautiful young face laughing back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
