          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f42de4497e17e78222d849c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor confused village boy, escape that strange contradiction which even the best and wisest men fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nAt that, I threw myself face down and seized the hair on both sides of my head, pulling it hard. All the while, I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so foolish and so misplaced that I felt my face deserved it if I had lifted it by the hair and struck it against the pebbles, as punishment for belonging to such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she did not try to reason with me any further. She laid her hand\u2014comfortable though rough from work\u2014on each of mine in turn, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder to comfort me, while I cried a little with my face on my sleeve, just as I had done in the brewery yard. I had a vague feeling that somebody, or everybody, had treated me very badly; I could not say which.\\n\\n\u201cI am glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cand that is that you felt able to trust me, Pip. And I am glad of another: that, of course, you know you can depend on me to keep your trust and always deserve it as far as I can. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor one, and so much in need of teaching herself!\u2014were your teacher now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a hard lesson to learn, and you have gone beyond her now, so it is no use.\u201d Then, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a brighter, pleasanter voice, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you become a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall, so that means always. Besides, I have no need to tell you anything, because you know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy, almost in a whisper, looking away at the ships. Then, returning to her earlier pleasant tone, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon slowly faded into the summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
