          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e8b9c607cf5864b8f2acd04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February, and Anne, after a month in Bath, was becoming very anxious for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wished to know far more than Mary had told her. Three weeks had passed since she had heard anything at all. She knew only that Henrietta was home again, and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still at Lyme. One evening, while thinking very earnestly of them all, she received a letter from Mary, thicker than usual; and, to increase both her pleasure and surprise, it came with Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was enough to interest her. They were people toward whom her heart naturally turned.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have come to Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Such letters are useful passports. They provide an introduction. I should have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could not listen any longer; she could not even have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion had escaped notice. Her letter absorbed her. It had been begun several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI make no apology for my silence, because I know how little anyone thinks of letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to care about Uppercross, which, as you know well, offers little to write of. We have had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not given a single dinner party throughout the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. At last the holidays are over: I do not believe children ever had such long ones. I am sure I never did. The house was emptied yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear that they have never gone home. Mrs Harville must be a strange mother to leave them away so long. I cannot understand it. They are not at all pleasant children, in my opinion; but Mrs Musgrove seems to like them as well as, if not better than, her own grandchildren. What dreadful weather we have had! Perhaps it is not felt in Bath, with your fine pavements; but in the country it matters a great deal. No one has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who had been coming much more often than was welcome. Between ourselves, I think it a great pity Henrietta did not stay at Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her somewhat out of his way.\"}"
