          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5d2b2ea45f213d398fe67e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He chose Friday, the 17th of August, for his manifestation, and passed the greater part of the day examining his wardrobe, finally settling upon a broad slouched hat with a red plume, a winding-sheet frilled at wrist and throat, and a rusty dagger. Towards evening a fierce rainstorm arose, and the wind blew so violently that every window and door in the old house trembled and clattered. Indeed, it was precisely the sort of weather he delighted in. His scheme was as follows. He would steal quietly to Washington Otis\u2019s room, gibber at him from the foot of the bed, and stab himself three times in the throat to the accompaniment of slow music. He cherished a particular resentment against Washington, knowing well that it was he who regularly removed the celebrated Canterville blood-stain with Pinkerton\u2019s Paragon Detergent. When he had brought the rash and heedless youth into a state of utter terror, he intended to go on to the room occupied by the United States Minister and his wife, lay a clammy hand upon Mrs. Otis\u2019s forehead, and hiss the dreadful secrets of the charnel-house into the ear of her quaking husband. As for little Virginia, he had not entirely decided what to do. She had never offended him at all, and was both pretty and sweet. A few sepulchral groans from the wardrobe, he thought, would be quite enough; or, should these fail to rouse her, he might clutch at the counterpane with fingers twitching like those of palsy. Concerning the twins, he was firmly resolved to give them a lesson. The first step, naturally, was to sit upon their chests, thus producing the suffocating feeling of nightmare. Then, since their beds stood very near one another, he would stand between them in the guise of a green, ice-cold corpse until fear paralysed them, and at last cast off the winding-sheet and crawl about the room with bleached white bones and one rolling eyeball, as \u2018Dumb Daniel, or the Suicide\u2019s Skeleton,\u2019 a r\u00f4le in which he had more than once created a great sensation, and which he judged fully the equal of his renowned performance as \u2018Martin the Maniac, or the Masked Mystery.\u2019\\n\\nAt half-past ten he heard the family retire to bed. For a while he was troubled by the twins\u2019 wild bursts of laughter, for with the carefree merriment of schoolboys they were plainly entertaining themselves before going to sleep; but by a quarter past eleven everything was quiet, and when midnight struck he set out. The owl fluttered against the window-panes, the raven croaked from the ancient yew-tree, and the wind moaned about the house like a wandering soul; yet the Otis family slept unaware of their fate, and above the rain and tempest he could hear the regular snoring of the Minister for the United States.\"}"
