{"request_id":"3eb994d015774bd22ee71be1","rewritten_text":"I cannot describe the joyful relief with which I turned from political quarrels at home and the bodily suffering of foreign lands to my own dear house: the chosen home of goodness and love, of peace, and of every sacred sympathy shared between us. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings would not have been so strong; but in Greece I had been exposed to fear and terrible change. There, after a time of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two people depart whose very names stood for greatness and virtue. Yet such misery could never enter the family circle that remained to me, while we lived quietly, hidden away in our beloved forest. The passing years did bring small changes even there, and time, as always, marked our pleasures and hopes with signs of mortality. Idris, the most loving wife, sister, and friend, was also a tender and devoted mother. Motherhood was not, as it is with many, merely an amusement to her; it was a passion. We had had three children, but one—the second in age—died while I was in Greece. This had mixed the proud, joyful feelings of motherhood with grief and fear. Before that loss, the little creatures born from her, the young heirs of her brief life, had seemed certain to live; now she feared that the merciless destroyer might take her remaining darlings, as it had taken their brother. The slightest illness filled her with terror; she was unhappy whenever she was away from them. She had stored all her happiness in their fragile lives and watched constantly, afraid that the hidden thief would again steal these precious jewels. Fortunately, she had little reason to fear. Alfred, now nine, was an honest, manly little boy, with a bright brow, soft eyes, and a gentle yet independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheek glowed with the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed the age which, because of its silent helplessness, had caused Idris such fear. Clara was dear to Idris, and to us all. She joined intelligence with innocence, sensitivity with patience, and seriousness with perfect good humour. Her extraordinary beauty was matched by such lovable simplicity that she seemed like a pearl among our treasures, a wonder of excellence.

At the beginning of winter, Alfred, now nine years old, first went to school at Eton. He saw this as his first step toward manhood and was pleased accordingly. Sharing study and play with others brought out the best in him: his steady perseverance, generosity, and calm firmness."}

