          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3fffc597048eeb7b1b17fb14\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But my father was beside me, comforting me and driving away bitter memories with hopeful ones. I thought it sweet to grieve when he could dry my tears.\\n\\nHe also turned my mind from sorrow by comparing it to his own despair when he lost my mother. Even then, I shuddered at the image he gave of his emotions. He had a poet\u2019s imagination, and when he described the storm that had torn through his feelings, he made his words so vividly alive that I believed him even as I trembled. I wondered how he could ever have returned to the duties of life after his wild thoughts had seemed to make him kin to the supernatural. As he spoke, the ideas he conveyed were so immense that the human heart seemed far too limited to contain them. His feelings appeared better suited to a spirit dwelling among earthquakes and volcanoes than to one confined within a mortal body and human form. But these were only memories; he had changed since then. He was now entirely loving, entirely gentle; and when I looked up at him in wonder as he spoke, the smile on his lips showed that his heart was filled with the mildest emotions.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death, we moved to London, where my father guided me toward more serious studies than those that had occupied me before. My progress delighted him; he stayed with me throughout my studies and helped me, or joined me, in every lesson. We saw a great deal of society, and not a day passed without my father trying to enrich it with some new pleasure. The tender affection he felt for me, and the love and reverence with which I returned it, cast a charm over every moment. The hours passed slowly because every minute was occupied; we lived more in one week than many people do over several months, and the variety and novelty of our pleasures gave each one its flavor.\\n\\nWe were constantly taking excursions together. Whether we went to see beautiful landscapes, fine paintings, or sometimes with no purpose except to find whatever amusement might arise, I was always happy when I was near my father. I regretted it whenever a third person joined us; yet if I turned an uneasy look toward my father, his eyes, fixed on me and shining with tenderness, immediately restored joy to my heart. Oh, hours of intense delight! Brief as you were, you have become as long to me as an entire life when I look back through the mist of grief that rose so soon afterward, as though to hide you from my sight.\"}"
