{"request_id":"eb5dc5d3f82601f0129326d8","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove took the first chance to be alone with Anne and said, “Oh, Miss Anne, I cannot help wishing Mrs Charles had a little of your way with those children. They are completely different with you! But of course, in general they are so spoiled. It is a pity you cannot show your sister how to manage them. They are as fine, healthy children as ever were seen, poor little dears, and I say that without prejudice; but Mrs Charles has no idea how they ought to be treated—bless me, how troublesome they can be sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I believe Mrs Charles is not quite pleased that I do not invite them more often; but you know how unpleasant it is to have children with you whom you must correct every moment—‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that’—or whom you can only keep reasonably quiet by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

Mary had also told Anne, “Mrs Musgrove thinks all her servants are so dependable that it would be almost treason to question them; but I am sure, without exaggerating, that her upper housemaid and laundry-maid, instead of doing their work, wander about the village all day. I meet them everywhere I go, and I declare, I never enter my nursery twice without seeing one of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to ruin her, because she tells me they are always tempting her to go walking with them.” And Mrs Musgrove, for her part, said, “I make a rule never to interfere in any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because I know it would not do; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, because you may be able to put things right, that I do not have a very good opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear strange things about her. She is always out and about, and from what I have seen myself, I can say she dresses so finely that she is enough to spoil any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles trusts her completely, I know; but I only give you this warning, so that you may keep watch. If you see anything wrong, you need not be afraid to mention it.”

Mary also complained that Mrs Musgrove was very likely not to give her the proper precedence when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she could see no reason why she should be treated as so much at home that she lost her place."}

