{"request_id":"d7072fb018682078c839fd79","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a light breeze soon fanned it into flame. I noticed this and made a fan from branches, which stirred the embers whenever they were close to dying out. When night came again, I discovered with delight that the fire gave light as well as warmth, and that this element was useful in preparing food. Some of the scraps the travellers had left behind had been roasted, and they tasted far more savory than the berries I gathered from the trees. I therefore tried to prepare my food in the same way, placing it on the glowing embers. The berries were ruined by this process, but the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, however, grew scarce, and I often spent an entire day searching unsuccessfully for a few acorns to ease the pains of hunger. When I realized this, I decided to leave the place where I had lived until then and seek another, where the few needs I had might be more easily met. In setting out, I deeply regretted losing the fire I had acquired by chance and did not know how to make again. I spent several hours seriously considering this problem, but was forced to abandon every effort to replace it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I crossed the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days and at last came upon open country. A heavy snowfall had fallen the night before, and the fields lay in an unbroken sheet of white. The sight was bleak, and my feet were chilled by the cold, wet substance covering the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on rising ground, no doubt built for the use of some shepherd. It was a new sight to me, and I examined it with great curiosity. Finding the door open, I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, preparing his breakfast over it. He turned at the sound, and when he saw me, he gave a loud cry and fled the hut, running across the fields with a speed his weakened body scarcely seemed capable of. His appearance, unlike any I had ever seen, and his flight surprised me somewhat. But I was enchanted by the hut itself: snow and rain could not enter there; the ground was dry; and at that moment it seemed to me as exquisite and divine a refuge as Pandæmonium had seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

