          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c69e7b1cb5cfef2a83de155d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to make her observations were sure to arise. Anne had soon been in company with all four of them together often enough to form an opinion, though she was too sensible to admit it at home, where she knew it would have pleased neither husband nor wife. Though she thought Louisa rather the favourite, she could not help believing, so far as memory and experience allowed her to judge, that Captain Wentworth was in love with neither sister. They were more in love with him; yet even there it was not love. It was a slight fever of admiration, but it might\u2014probably must\u2014end in love for one of them. Charles Hayter seemed conscious of being neglected, while Henrietta sometimes appeared divided between the two men. Anne wished she had the power to show them all what they were doing, and to point out some of the dangers to which they were exposing themselves. She suspected no one of deceit. It gave her the greatest comfort to believe that Captain Wentworth was entirely unaware of the pain he caused. There was no triumph in his manner, no cruel triumph. He had probably never heard of, or considered, any claims Charles Hayter might have. His only fault was in accepting\u2014for accepting was the word\u2014the attentions of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to withdraw from the contest. Three days passed without his coming even once to Uppercross, a most marked change. He had even declined one of his usual dinner invitations; and when Mr. Musgrove found him on that occasion sitting before some large books, Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove were certain that something was wrong, and spoke gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had given him a definite dismissal, while her husband constantly expected to see him tomorrow. Anne could only feel that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth having gone shooting together, the sisters at the Cottage were sitting quietly at their work when the sisters from the Mansion-house appeared at the window.\\n\\nIt was a beautiful November day, and the Miss Musgroves came through the small grounds, stopping only to say that they were going for a long walk and had therefore assumed Mary would not wish to join them. When Mary immediately replied, somewhat jealous at not being thought a good walker, \u201cOh yes, I should like to come with you very much. I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne was convinced by the looks exchanged by the two girls that this was exactly what they did not want. Once again, she admired the kind of necessity produced by the family\u2019s habits: everything had to be shared, and everything had to be done together, however unwanted or inconvenient.\"}"
