          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8fbb0f0be04c7d0b4394fb7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must leave, uncertain of my fate; but I will return here, or follow your party, as soon as I can. A word, a look, will be enough to determine whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening\u2014or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter could not be quickly recovered from. Half an hour alone to think might have calmed her; but the mere ten minutes that passed before she was interrupted, with all the constraints of her situation, could do nothing to restore her peace. Each moment only brought fresh agitation. It was overwhelming happiness. And before she had moved beyond that first intensity of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all came in.\\n\\nThe absolute need to appear like herself caused an immediate struggle; but after a time she could do no more. She began not to understand a word they were saying, and had to claim illness and excuse herself. They then saw how unwell she looked, were alarmed and concerned, and would not leave her for anything. This was dreadful. If only they would go away and leave her in the peaceful possession of that room, it would have cured her; but to have them all standing or waiting around her was unbearable, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs. Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once, and take care of yourself, so that you may be well enough for this evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair would never do. Worse than anything! To lose the chance of speaking two words to Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up through the town\u2014and she felt almost certain she would meet him\u2014was more than she could bear. She strongly protested against the chair, and Mrs. Musgrove, who thought only of one kind of illness, had anxiously assured herself that there had been no fall: that Anne had not recently slipped and struck her head; that she was entirely certain she had not fallen. She could therefore let her go cheerfully, trusting that she would find her better that night.\\n\\nAnxious not to neglect any possible precaution, Anne made an effort and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid, ma\u2019am, that this has not been fully understood. Please be so kind as to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your entire party this evening. I am afraid there has been some misunderstanding; and I particularly wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear, it is perfectly understood, I give you my word.\u201d\"}"
