          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a6799572b7f75109f5b26c7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was occupied with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note in the post. The sight of the envelope alone made me tremble with excitement; for although I had never seen the handwriting before, I guessed at once whose hand had written it. It had no formal opening\u2014no Dear Mr. Pip, Dear Pip, Dear Sir, or Dear Anything\u2014but simply said:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am coming to London by the midday coach the day after to-morrow. I believe it was arranged that you should meet me? In any case, Miss Havisham thinks so, and I write because she wishes it. She sends you her regards.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nIf there had been time, I would probably have ordered several new suits for the occasion; but there was not, so I had to be satisfied with what I owned. My appetite disappeared at once, and I had no peace or rest until the day came. Its arrival did not bring either, for I was worse than before. I began hovering around the coach office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. Though I knew this perfectly well, I still felt it was unsafe to let the coach office out of my sight for more than five minutes at a time. In this unreasonable state, I had completed the first half-hour of a watch that was to last four or five hours when Wemmick came upon me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said. \u201cHow are you? I should hardly have thought this was your usual ground.\u201d\\n\\nI explained that I was waiting to meet someone arriving by coach, and asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth doing well, thankye,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cespecially the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful spirits. He\u2019ll be eighty-two on his next birthday. I\u2019m thinking of firing eighty-two shots, if the neighbours don\u2019t object and my cannon can stand the strain. But this isn\u2019t London talk. Where do you think I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, since he was walking that way.\\n\\n\u201cThe next thing to it,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We have a banker\u2019s-parcel case at present, and I\u2019ve been down the road to take a look at the scene of action. Now I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d Wemmick answered very dryly. \u201cBut he is accused of it. So might you or I be. Either of us could be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
