          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc40f4bde5d5b7d4df456379\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, was a man who, for his own entertainment, never opened any book but the Baronetage. In it he found something to occupy an idle hour and comfort in a troubled one; there his mind was stirred to admiration and respect by considering the few surviving patents of the earliest creation; there any unpleasant feelings caused by family matters naturally turned into pity and contempt as he turned through the nearly endless creations of the last century; and there, if every other page failed him, he could read his own history with an interest that never diminished. This was the page at which his favourite volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly how the paragraph had originally come from the printer; but Sir Walter had improved it by adding, for the benefit of himself and his family, these words after the date of Mary\u2019s birth\u2014\u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset\u201d\u2014and by inserting, with great precision, the day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nThen came the history and advancement of the ancient and respectable family, in the customary language: how it had first settled in Cheshire; how it was mentioned in Dugdale; its service as high sheriff, its representation of a borough in three successive parliaments, its acts of loyalty, and its baronetcy in the first year of Charles II, along with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. It filled two handsome duodecimo pages and ended with the arms and motto:\u2014\u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed once more by Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity about his appearance and his position. He had been remarkably handsome when young and, at fifty-four, was still a very fine-looking man. Few women thought more about their appearance than he did, nor could the valet of any newly made lord take greater pleasure in his place in society. He regarded the gift of beauty as second only to the gift of a baronetcy; and Sir Walter Elliot, possessing both, was the constant object of his deepest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis looks and rank had one legitimate claim on his affection, since he must have owed to them a wife of far superior character to anything he himself deserved.\"}"
