          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c9b8d481c5b4654155564c95\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General was obliged to tell the jury that the prisoner at the bar, though youthful in age, was seasoned in those treasonable pursuits which demanded the sacrifice of his life. That this intercourse with the public enemy was no affair of to-day, yesterday, last year, or the year preceding. That it was beyond doubt the prisoner had, for a longer period, been accustomed to travel to and fro between France and England, upon secret errands for which he could render no honest explanation. That, had it been the character of traitorous dealings to prosper\u2014which, happily, it never was\u2014the true depravity and criminality of his occupation might have escaped detection. That Providence, nevertheless, had inspired a person above fear and above reproach to search out the prisoner\u2019s designs, and, horror-stricken, reveal them to his Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. That this patriot would appear before them. That his situation and bearing were, altogether, sublime. That he had been the prisoner\u2019s friend, yet, discovering his infamy at once in a fortunate and unhappy hour, had determined to offer up the traitor whom he could no longer hold in his heart upon the sacred altar of his country. That, if Britain bestowed statues upon public benefactors, as ancient Greece and Rome had done, this illustrious citizen would certainly have received one. That, since they were not bestowed, he would probably receive none. That Virtue, as the poets had remarked\u2014in numerous passages which he was sure the jury knew word for word and had ready upon their tongues, at which the jury\u2019s faces betrayed a guilty awareness that they knew nothing whatever of the passages\u2014was, in some degree, contagious; especially that radiant virtue called patriotism, or love of country. That the exalted example of this spotless and unassailable witness for the Crown, to mention whom, however inadequately, was an honour, had extended itself to the prisoner\u2019s servant, and had produced in him a pious resolve to inspect his master\u2019s table-drawers and pockets, and conceal his papers. That he, Mr. Attorney-General, was prepared to hear some detraction directed against this excellent servant; but that, generally speaking, he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters, and esteemed him more highly than his own father and mother. That he confidently called upon the jury to do the same. That the testimony of these two witnesses, together with the papers they had discovered and which would be produced, would prove that the prisoner had been supplied with lists of his Majesty\u2019s forces, their arrangement and readiness, by land and sea alike, and would admit of no doubt that he had regularly carried such intelligence to a hostile power.\"}"
