          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9df8a79abd35b79b27e7332e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was filled with the powerful feelings of recovered health. As general of the Athenians, he believed he held a position worthy of his ambition; and in his hope of conquering Constantinople, he looked forward to an event that would stand as a landmark through the empty ages, a deed unmatched in human history. A city rich in great memories, whose beautiful setting was the wonder of the world, and which for centuries had been the stronghold of the Moslems, would be freed from slavery and barbarism and restored to a people famous for genius, civilization, and love of liberty. Perdita rested in his renewed companionship, his love, his hopes, and his fame, as a Sybarite rests on a luxurious couch; every thought brought delight, and every feeling seemed bathed in a gentle, welcoming air.\\n\\nWe arrived at Kishan on the seventh of July. The weather throughout our journey had been calm and clear. Every day, before dawn, we left our camp for the night and watched the shadows withdraw from hill and valley before the golden glory of the rising sun. The soldiers with us, in their national liveliness, took eager pleasure in the beauty of nature. They greeted the rising sun with triumphant music, while birdsong, heard in brief bursts, filled the pauses between the notes. At noon, we set up our tents in a shaded valley or a leafy mountain wood, where a stream murmuring over pebbles invited grateful sleep. Our evening march, quieter though it was, delighted us even more than the restless spirit of the morning. If the band played, they unconsciously chose tunes of restrained feeling: a lover's farewell or a song of sorrow at absence, followed and ended by a solemn hymn that suited the peaceful beauty of evening and lifted the mind toward lofty religious thoughts. Often every sound fell silent so that we could listen to the nightingale, while fireflies danced in bright patterns and the soft call of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travellers. When we passed through a valley, gentle shadows surrounded us, with rocks coloured in lovely shades. When we crossed a mountain, Greece lay below like a living map, its famous peaks cutting the sky and its rivers winding in silver lines through fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travellers looked in ecstasy on this splendid scene, so unlike the quiet colours and melancholy beauty of our native landscape. After we left Macedonia, the fertile but flat plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties; yet the journey remained interesting. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people quickly began to move about, preparing to honour Lord Raymond.\"}"
