          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3eb0df110bcde590d6fecde4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No later discovery required Emma to withdraw her poor opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her judgment had been quite accurate. Mrs. Elton was, on this second meeting and every meeting afterward, exactly what she had seemed at first\u2014self-important, presumptuous, overfamiliar, ignorant, and ill-mannered. She possessed a little beauty and a little talent, but so little sense that she believed she had arrived with superior worldly knowledge to enliven and improve a country neighbourhood; and imagined that Miss Hawkins had occupied a social position surpassed only by Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton viewed his wife differently. He seemed not only happy with her, but proud of her. He looked as though he congratulated himself on bringing to Highbury a woman whom not even Miss Woodhouse could equal; and most of her new acquaintances, inclined to praise, or unused to judging, guided by Miss Bates\u2019s kindness, or assuming that the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she claimed, were perfectly satisfied. Thus Mrs. Elton\u2019s praises passed from mouth to mouth as they should, unhindered by Miss Woodhouse, who readily maintained her original contribution and said graciously that she was \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one respect Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had first appeared. Her feelings toward Emma changed. Probably offended by the little encouragement her offers of intimacy received, she withdrew in return and gradually grew much colder and more distant. Though this result was agreeable, the ill-will that caused it necessarily increased Emma\u2019s dislike. Her manner toward Harriet\u2014and Mr. Elton\u2019s as well\u2014was unpleasant. They were sneering and neglectful. Emma hoped it would quickly complete Harriet\u2019s cure; but the feelings capable of prompting such conduct lowered them both greatly in her estimation. There could be no doubt that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been offered up to marital confidence, and that her own part in the story, presented in the light least favorable to her and most comforting to him, had most likely been told as well. Naturally, she was the object of their shared dislike. Whenever they had nothing else to discuss, it must have been easy to begin criticizing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not show through open disrespect toward Emma found freer expression in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a great liking to Jane Fairfax, and did so from the beginning. It was not merely that being at odds with one young woman might make another more appealing, but from the very first; and she was not content merely to express a natural and reasonable admiration. Without invitation, excuse, or right, she had to be eager to help and befriend her. Before Emma had lost her confidence, around their third meeting, she heard all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s chivalrous schemes on the subject.\"}"
