{"request_id":"a6a0e5037dc94c435e8fc211","rewritten_text":"“It was some time before I recovered enough to attend to the usual concerns of the balloon. When I did, I examined it carefully and found, to my great relief, that it was undamaged. All my instruments were safe, and fortunately I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. In fact, I had secured them so firmly in place that such an accident was impossible. Looking at my watch, I saw that it was six o’clock. I was still rising rapidly, and my barometer showed that I was then three and three-quarter miles high. Directly below me on the ocean lay a small black object, somewhat oblong, apparently about the size and shape of one of those children’s toys called a domino. Using my telescope, I clearly saw that it was a British ninety-four-gun ship, sailing close to the wind and pitching heavily in the sea, with her head toward the W.S.W. Apart from this ship, I saw only ocean, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now time that I explained to your Excellencies the purpose of my dangerous voyage. Your Excellencies will remember that my desperate circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to consider suicide. It was not that I felt any real hatred of life itself, but that I was troubled beyond endurance by the miseries connected with my situation. In this state of mind, wishing to live but tired of life, the treatise at the bookseller’s stall offered my imagination a way out. I then made my final decision. I resolved to depart, yet live—to leave the world, yet continue to exist. In short, to stop speaking in riddles, I decided, whatever might happen, to force a passage to the moon if I could. Now, so that I may not seem more mad than I truly am, I will explain as well as I can the reasons that led me to believe such an undertaking, though certainly difficult and undeniably dangerous, was not entirely beyond the reach of a bold spirit.

“The moon’s true distance from the earth was the first matter to consider. The mean, or average, distance between the centers of the two planets is 59.9643 times the earth’s equatorial radius, or only about 237,000 miles. I say the mean distance, but it must be remembered that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse, with an eccentricity of no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse’s major semi-axis, and that the earth’s center lies at one focus. Thus, if I could somehow arrange to meet the moon at its perigee, the distance just mentioned would be greatly reduced."}

