 own. They were indeed\u2014weeping is fitting, then; for surely, lonely being, you will be gentle by nature. Shed tears of pity, but also give your attention to this tale, and learn the actions and sufferings of those who came before you.\\n\\nYet the final events of our passage through France were so filled with strange horror and dark misery that I dare not linger too long over them. If I examined every incident, each small part of a second would hold a dreadful story, whose slightest word would chill the blood in your young veins. It is right that I should raise this memorial to instruct you about the race that has passed away; but it is not right to drag you through the wards of a hospital or the hidden rooms of a charnel house.\"}"
