          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0df67386dbd7526c13a99976\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a gentleman, you see. He was a gentleman through and through, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and that counts as much for a man as it does for a horse, according to Widow Douglas; and nobody ever denied she belonged to the highest aristocracy in our town. Pap said it too, though he had no more breeding than a mudcat himself. Colonel Grangerford was very tall and very slender, with a dark, pale complexion, without a trace of red anywhere. Every morning he shaved his whole thin face clean. He had extremely thin lips and nostrils, a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and the blackest eyes, set so deep they seemed to be looking out at you from caves, you might say. His forehead was high, and his black, straight hair hung to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin, and every day of his life he wore a fresh shirt and a complete linen suit, white enough to hurt your eyes. On Sundays he wore a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver handle. There was no foolishness about him, not a bit, and he was never loud. He was as kind as could be\u2014you could feel it, and it made you trust him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was a fine thing to see; but when he drew himself up straight as a flagpole and lightning began to flash beneath his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree first and ask what was wrong afterward. He never had to tell anyone to behave\u2014everybody had good manners when he was present. Everybody liked having him around, too. He was sunshine nearly all the time\u2014I mean, he made it feel like fair weather. When he turned into a cloudbank, it was terribly dark for half a minute, and that was enough; nothing would go wrong again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came downstairs in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good day, and did not sit again until they had sat down. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, where the decanter stood, mixed him a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He held it and waited until Tom's and Bob's drinks were mixed; then they bowed and said, \u201cOur respects to you, sir and madam.\u201d They bowed the slightest bit and thanked them, and then all three drank. Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy left in the bottoms of their tumblers, gave it to Buck and me, and we drank to the old people too.\"}"
