{"request_id":"98b6492a870885c568309bed","rewritten_text":"Among the other public edifices of a certain town—which, for a variety of reasons, it will be wise not to name, and to which I shall give no invented designation—stands one institution formerly usual in towns both large and small: namely, a workhouse; and in that workhouse was born, upon a day and date which I need not take the trouble to state again, since it can be of no possible importance to the reader at this point in the affair, the mortal creature whose name appears at the head of this chapter.

For some considerable time after he had been introduced into this world of grief and difficulty by the parish surgeon, it was highly uncertain whether the child would live long enough to receive any name whatever; in which event it is more than likely that these recollections would never have been written; or, had they been, being contained in two pages or so, they would have enjoyed the priceless distinction of forming the briefest and most accurate example of biography to be found in the literature of any age or nation.

Though I have no wish to assert that birth in a workhouse is, of itself, the happiest or most desirable fate that can attend a human being, I do intend to say that, in this particular case, it was the very best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth was that there was no small difficulty in persuading Oliver to undertake the duty of breathing—a troublesome habit, though custom has made it essential to our comfortable existence; and for a while he lay panting upon a small flock mattress, rather unevenly balanced between this world and the next, with the advantage plainly on the side of the latter. Had Oliver, during that short interval, been attended by watchful grandmothers, concerned aunts, practised nurses, and physicians of deep learning, he would, most certainly and beyond dispute, have been dispatched in very little time. But as there was no one present except an old pauper woman, somewhat clouded by an unusual allowance of beer, and a parish surgeon who attended to such business by contract, Oliver and Nature settled the matter between themselves. The consequence was that, after a few efforts, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inmates that a fresh burden had been laid upon the parish, by raising as loud a cry as might reasonably be expected from a male infant who had lacked that highly useful appendage, a voice, for a period considerably longer than three minutes and a quarter."}

