          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c75884bda9380abe17fc78a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOn that subject, therefore, I shall remain entirely silent; and you may be assured that no unkind reproach will ever pass my lips once we are married.\u201d\\n\\nIt was absolutely necessary to interrupt him now.\\n\\n\u201cYou are too hasty, sir,\u201d she cried. \u201cYou forget that I have given you no answer. Let me give it without further delay. Accept my thanks for the compliment you have paid me. I am very aware of the honour of your proposal, but it is impossible for me to do anything but refuse it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not unfamiliar,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, with a formal gesture of his hand, \u201cwith the custom of young ladies rejecting the attentions of the man they secretly intend to accept when he first seeks their favour; and sometimes the refusal is repeated a second or even a third time. I am therefore not at all discouraged by what you have just said, and I hope to lead you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUpon my word, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cyour hope is rather extraordinary after what I have declared. I assure you, I am not one of those young ladies\u2014if such young ladies exist\u2014who are bold enough to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked a second time. I am entirely serious in refusing you. You could not make me happy, and I am convinced that I am the last woman in the world who could make you happy. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am certain she would find me in every way unfit for the position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins very solemnly\u2014\u201cbut I cannot imagine that her ladyship would disapprove of you in the least. And you may be sure that, when I have the honour of seeing her again, I shall speak in the highest terms of your modesty, thrift, and other agreeable qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, any praise of me will be unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the courtesy of believing what I say. I wish you very happy and very wealthy, and in refusing your hand I do all I can to prevent your being otherwise. By making me this offer, you must have satisfied your delicate feelings concerning my family, and may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it comes to you without any self-reproach. This matter may therefore be considered finally settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had Mr. Collins not addressed her again\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I next do myself the honour of speaking to you on this subject, I shall hope for a more favourable answer than the one you have now given me. Yet I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it is the established custom of your sex to reject a man at his first proposal; and perhaps even now you have said as much to encourage my suit as is consistent with the true delicacy of female character.\u201d\"}"
