          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6cd740e9412483cc446a88e8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently placing his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm again after a brief silence between them, \u201cwhat do you think caused this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d Doctor Manette replied, \u201cthat the thoughts and memories which first caused his illness returned with unusual force. I think some powerful associations of a deeply painful kind came back to him vividly. It is likely that he had long feared those memories would return\u2014perhaps in certain circumstances\u2014perhaps on a particular occasion. He tried, unsuccessfully, to prepare himself; perhaps trying to prepare made him less able to endure it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked, naturally hesitant.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked sadly around the room, shook his head, and answered in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd what of the future?\u201d Mr. Lorry suggested.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, becoming firm again, \u201cI have great hope. Since Heaven, in its mercy, restored him so quickly, I have great hope. He gave way under the pressure of something complicated, long feared, dimly expected, and struggled against; and now that the storm has broken and passed, I hope the worst is over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is comforting. I am grateful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am grateful,\u201d repeated the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cabout which I am anxious to learn your opinion. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou cannot do your friend a better service.\u201d The Doctor held out his hand.\\n\\n\u201cThen the first. He is studious and unusually energetic; he works with great eagerness to gain professional knowledge, carry out experiments, and do many other things. Does he do too much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI do not think so. His mind may be one that always has an unusual need for activity. That may partly be natural to him, and partly the result of suffering. The less his mind was occupied with healthy things, the greater the danger that it would turn in an unhealthy direction. He may have noticed this in himself and understood it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are sure he is not under too much strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I am quite sure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt that could easily happen. There has been a violent strain in one direction, and it needs a counterbalance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForgive me for being persistent, as a man of business. Suppose, for a moment, that he was overworked; would it show itself in some return of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
