          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b31a646655a498da7380892d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret the condescension of having gone to the Coles. The visit supplied her with many agreeable reflections on the following day; and whatever she might be thought to have sacrificed in dignified retirement, was surely more than compensated by the glory of popularity. She must have charmed the Coles\u2014excellent people, who deserved to be pleased!\u2014And had left behind a name that would not quickly be forgotten.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in recollection, is unusual; and there were two matters on which she was not entirely at ease. She questioned whether she had not failed in the duty of woman towards woman, by revealing her suspicions of Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill. It was scarcely proper; but the notion had been so powerful that it escaped her, and his ready assent to all she said was such a tribute to her discernment, that she found it hard to be wholly convinced she ought to have been silent.\\n\\nThe second cause for regret concerned Jane Fairfax as well; and on that point she had no uncertainty. She sincerely and decidedly lamented the inferiority of her own music and singing. She most earnestly mourned the idleness of her early years\u2014and seated herself to practise diligently for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet\u2019s entrance; and had Harriet\u2019s admiration been enough for her, she might soon have found consolation.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not put us together, Harriet. My playing resembles hers no more than a lamp resembles the sun.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! dear\u2014I think you are the better player of the two. I think you play every bit as well as she does. I am sure I would much rather listen to you. Every body said last night how beautifully you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThose who understood any thing of it must have perceived the difference. The truth, Harriet, is that my playing is merely good enough to receive praise, while Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far superior.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always believe you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is a difference, nobody could ever discover it. Mr. Cole spoke of the great taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill said a great deal about your taste, and that he valued taste far more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax possesses both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you certain? I saw that she had execution, but I did not know she had taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I dislike Italian singing.\u2014One cannot understand a word of it. Besides, if she plays so exceedingly well, you know, it is only what she is required to do, because she will need to teach.\u201d\"}"
