age always rises with every attempt to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not say you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly believe that I intended to alarm you; and I have known you long enough to understand that you take great pleasure in sometimes declaring opinions that are not, in fact, your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this portrait of herself and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very flattering idea of me, and teach you not to believe a word I say. I am especially unfortunate to meet someone so capable of exposing my true character in a part of the world where I had hoped to present myself with some degree of credit.\u201d\"}"
