          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0867e691332391c59fbf864c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her position was, truly, one of unusual trial and perplexity. Though she felt the keenest and most ardent wish to pierce the mystery that shrouded Oliver\u2019s history, she could not fail to regard as sacred the confidence which the unhappy woman she had just spoken with had placed in her, a young and innocent girl. Her words and bearing had moved Rose Maylie\u2019s heart; and, blended with her affection for her youthful charge, and scarcely less strong in sincerity and warmth, was her earnest desire to reclaim the outcast to penitence and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to remain in London for only three days before leaving for several weeks at some remote part of the coast. It was now midnight of the first day. What step could she resolve upon that might be taken within eight-and-forty hours? Or by what means could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was staying with them, and would remain for the next two days; but Rose knew too well the impetuosity of that excellent gentleman, and too plainly anticipated the anger with which, in the first burst of his indignation, he would view the means of Oliver\u2019s recapture, to entrust him with the secret when her pleas for the girl could receive support from no person of experience. These were all grounds for the utmost caution and reserve in revealing it to Mrs. Maylie, whose first impulse would certainly be to confer with the worthy doctor upon the matter. To seek the aid of any legal adviser, even had she known how, was scarcely to be considered, for the same reason. Once she thought of asking Harry\u2019s assistance; but this recalled their last farewell, and it appeared unworthy in her to summon him back when\u2014the tears filled her eyes as she followed the thought\u2014he might by this time have learned to forget her, and be happier at a distance.\\n\\nHarassed by these various reflections; turning first toward one plan and then another, and shrinking again from all as each new consideration rose before her; Rose spent a wakeful and troubled night. After further reflection with herself on the following day, she came to the desperate determination of consulting Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it is painful for him,\u201d she thought, \u201cto return here, how painful must it be for me! Yet perhaps he will not return; he may write, or he may come himself and carefully avoid seeing me\u2014he did so when he left. I scarcely expected that he would; but it was best for us both.\u201d Here Rose laid down the pen and turned aside, as if the very paper that was to carry her message must not behold her tears.\"}"
