          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"57d206b6703286e374d320fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft, he counted up and found he had gathered eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also brought along a three-gallon jug of whisky that he found under a wagon while he was heading home through the woods. The king said that, all things considered, it beat any day he had ever spent in the missionary business. He said there was no use denying it: heathens were nothing compared with pirates when it came to working a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke had thought he had done pretty well until the king showed what he had made, but afterward he did not think so much of it. At the printing office, he had set and printed two small jobs for farmers\u2014horse notices\u2014and collected four dollars. He had also gotten ten dollars\u2019 worth of ads for the paper, telling the people he would print them for four dollars if they paid ahead of time\u2014so they did. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he got three subscriptions at fifty cents each by making them pay in advance. They were going to pay in firewood and onions, as usual, but he said he had just bought the business, cut the price as low as he could afford, and meant to run it for cash. He set up a short poem he had made up himself\u2014three verses, sort of sweet and sad\u2014called \u201cYes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart.\u201d He left it all set and ready to print in the paper, and charged nothing for it. Altogether, he took in nine dollars and a half, and said he had earned it honestly enough.\\n\\nThen he showed us another small job he had printed without charging for it, because it was for us. It had a picture of a runaway Black man with a bundle on a stick over his shoulder, with \u201c$200 reward\u201d underneath. The writing was all about Jim and described him exactly. It said he had run away from St. Jacques\u2019 plantation, forty miles below New Orleans, the winter before, and had probably gone north. Whoever caught him and sent him back could have the reward and expenses.\\n\\n\u201cNow,\u201d said the duke, \u201cafter tonight we can travel in daylight if we want to. Whenever we see somebody coming, we can tie Jim hand and foot with a rope, lay him in the wigwam, show this handbill, and say we captured him upriver. Then we can say we were too poor to travel by steamboat, so our friends let us have this little raft on credit, and we are going down to collect the reward.\u201d\"}"
