          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"60f737c2267ed413703e183d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She had left everything behind, except the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nAnne had not been inside Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need for it, and on the few occasions when she might have gone to the Hall, she had managed to avoid it. Her first return was to take her place again in the modern, elegant rooms of the Lodge, and to delight its mistress.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s joy at seeing her was mixed with some anxiety. She knew who had been often at Uppercross. But fortunately, either Anne had become fuller and better-looking, or Lady Russell imagined that she had; and, as Anne accepted her compliments, she amused herself by linking them to her cousin\u2019s quiet admiration, and hoping she might be granted a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nOnce they began talking, Anne quickly noticed a change in her own mind. The matters that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, which had hurt her by being treated lightly and which she had been forced to hide among the Musgroves, had now become only secondary concerns. Lately she had almost lost sight of her father, her sister, and Bath. Their affairs had been overshadowed by those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell returned to their old hopes and fears, expressed pleasure in the house they had taken in Camden Place, and regretted that Mrs Clay was still with them, Anne would have been ashamed if anyone knew how much more she was thinking of Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her friends there. The Harvilles\u2019 home and friendship, and Captain Benwick, interested her far more than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s friendship with Mrs Clay. She had to make a real effort to seem as concerned as Lady Russell about subjects that naturally had the strongest claim on her attention.\\n\\nAt first, there was some awkwardness when they spoke of another matter. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had been in town less than five minutes the day before when she heard the full story; yet it still had to be discussed. She had to ask questions, regret the carelessness, mourn the result, and both of them had to mention Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne knew she did not manage it as well as Lady Russell. She could not say his name and look directly into Lady Russell\u2019s eyes until she found a way around it: she briefly told her what she thought of the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
