          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"739fc8dbe53c20917fab1182\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had, in truth, been entirely unaware of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery with no intention of testing it. In his anxiety to see how she endured Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, he had come with no selfish purpose\u2014no purpose at all except to comfort or advise her, if she gave him the chance. The rest had been the work of the moment, the immediate effect on his feelings of what he heard. The wonderful certainty that she was completely indifferent to Frank Churchill, that her heart was entirely free of him, had awakened the hope that, in time, he might win her affection himself; but it was not an immediate hope. In the brief triumph of eagerness over judgment, he had only dared to ask whether she would not forbid him from trying to win her. The greater hopes that gradually revealed themselves were all the more enchanting. The affection he had been asking permission to create, if he could, was already his. Within half an hour, he had moved from a state of complete distress to something so close to perfect happiness that it could be called nothing else.\\n\\nHer change was just as great. This single half-hour had given each of them the same precious certainty of being loved, and had removed from each the same measure of ignorance, jealousy, and distrust. On his side, there had been a long-held jealousy, as old as Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival, or even the expectation of it. He had loved Emma and been jealous of Frank Churchill from nearly the same time; one feeling had probably made him aware of the other. It was jealousy of Frank Churchill that had driven him from the country. The party at Box Hill had decided him to leave. He would spare himself from again seeing attentions so openly permitted and encouraged. He had gone away to learn indifference. But he had gone to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother\u2019s house; woman appeared there in too lovable a form; Isabella was too much like Emma\u2014differing only in those obvious lesser qualities that always made the other shine more brilliantly in his mind\u2014for much to be accomplished, even if he had stayed longer. Still, he had remained resolutely, day after day, until that morning\u2019s post brought the story of Jane Fairfax. Then, along with the joy he must feel\u2014indeed, which he did not hesitate to feel, since he had never thought Frank Churchill at all worthy of Emma\u2014came such tender concern and sharp anxiety for her that he could remain no longer. He rode home through the rain and, directly after dinner, walked over to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless despite all her faults, bore the discovery.\"}"
