          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1b7d4a5791f74e1f4fa2277\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was finally arranged that we would live together during my stay in the city; and since my financial circumstances were somewhat less troubled than his, I was allowed to bear the cost of renting and furnishing, in a manner suited to the fantastical gloom of our shared disposition, an ancient, grotesque house. It had long been abandoned because of superstitions we did not investigate, and stood nearly ready to collapse in a secluded, desolate part of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad the world known the pattern of our lives there, we would have been considered madmen\u2014though perhaps harmless ones. Our isolation was complete. We received no visitors. Indeed, the location of our retreat had been carefully concealed even from my former acquaintances; and Dupin had for many years ceased either to know anyone in Paris or to be known by them. We lived entirely within ourselves.\\n\\nMy friend had a whim\u2014what else can I call it?\u2014of being in love with night for its own sake; and I quietly entered into this eccentricity, as I did all his others, surrendering myself completely to his wild fancies. The dark goddess could not remain with us always, but we could imitate her presence. At the first light of morning, we shut every grimy shutter in our old house and lit two heavily perfumed candles, which gave off only the faintest, most ghostly light. By their aid, we occupied our minds with dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock warned us that true Darkness had arrived. Then we went out into the streets arm in arm, continuing the subjects of the day or wandering far and wide until late, seeking amid the wild lights and shadows of the crowded city that boundless mental excitement which quiet observation can provide.\\n\\nAt such times, I could not help noticing and admiring\u2014though his fertile imagination had led me to expect it\u2014Dupin's unusual power of analysis. He seemed to take eager pleasure in using it, if not precisely in displaying it, and readily admitted the enjoyment it gave him. With a low, chuckling laugh, he would boast that most men wore windows in their chests for him, and he often supported such claims with direct, deeply startling proofs of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. At these moments his manner was cold and detached; his eyes had a vacant look; and his voice, ordinarily a rich tenor, rose to a treble that would have sounded peevish if not for the measured care and perfect clarity of his speech.\"}"
