{"request_id":"5b3a813d6d68b652198b832b","rewritten_text":"Once this was done, several more loops were loosened from the rim, another section of the cloth was inserted, and the freed loops were fastened again to their proper buttons. In this manner, the entire upper portion of the bag could be placed between the netting and the hoop. Clearly, the hoop would now hang down inside the car, while the full weight of the car and everything in it would be supported solely by the buttons. At first glance, this might seem an insufficient support; but it was not, for the buttons were not only very strong themselves, but set so closely together that each bore only a small share of the total weight. Indeed, even if the car and its contents had been three times as heavy, I would have felt no concern. I then raised the hoop again inside the gum-elastic covering and supported it at nearly its former height with three light poles prepared for that purpose. This was done, naturally, to keep the bag expanded at the top and to maintain the lower part of the netting in its proper position. Nothing remained except to close the mouth of the enclosure; this was easily done by gathering the folds of material together and twisting them very tightly on the inside with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, now arranged around the car, were set three round panes of thick but clear glass, through which I could see easily around me in every horizontal direction. In the section of cloth forming the bottom there was also a fourth window of the same kind, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This allowed me to look straight down; but because of the particular way the opening above had to be closed, and the resulting wrinkles in the cloth, I had found it impossible to install any similar device overhead. I could therefore expect to see no objects directly above me at the zenith. This was, of course, of little importance, since even if I had been able to place a window at the top, the balloon itself would have prevented me from using it.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a circular opening eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded for a screw. Into this rim the large tube of the condenser was screwed, while the body of the machine, naturally, remained inside the gum-elastic chamber.”"}

