          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2d3c864c7cea7487123faa36\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I met the strange man I am going to describe. Three things drew me to him: his open simplicity, his remarkable knowledge of ancient armor, and the peacefulness of his company\u2014for he did all the talking. Like modest people often do, we ended up at the back of the group being led through the castle, and he immediately began saying things that interested me. As he spoke, gently, pleasantly, and smoothly, he seemed to slip without notice out of this world and time into some distant age and forgotten land. Little by little he cast such a spell over me that I felt myself moving among the ghosts, shadows, dust, and decay of a gray old past, talking with one of its surviving relics. He spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table exactly as I might speak of close friends, enemies, or familiar neighbors. And as he continued, he himself came to look old\u2014very old, beyond words old\u2014faded, dry, dusty, and ancient. At last he turned to me and said, as casually as if discussing the weather or any ordinary subject\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know about the transmigration of souls; do you know about the transposition of ages\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI said I had never heard of it. He cared so little\u2014just as people care little when talking about the weather\u2014that he did not even notice whether I answered him or not. There was a brief silence, broken at once by the droning voice of the paid guide:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, from the sixth century, the time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; notice the round hole in the chain mail over the left breast; cannot be explained; believed to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell's soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy companion smiled\u2014not a modern smile, but one that must have disappeared from common use many centuries ago\u2014and muttered, apparently to himself:\\n\\n\u201cKnow this well, I saw it done.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBy the time I had recovered from the shock of those words, he had vanished.\\n\\nThat whole evening I sat by my fire at the Warwick Arms, lost in dreams of old times, while rain struck the windows and the wind howled around the eaves and corners. Now and then I opened old Sir Thomas Malory's wonderful book and feasted on its marvels and adventures, breathed in the scent of its outdated names, and dreamed again.\"}"
