          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da2d10b45cfc1396dc7d2555\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed, and it was deep winter by then. I had gone to school most of the time and could spell, read, and write a little, and could say the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t reckon I could ever get beyond that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t care much for mathematics anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got so I could bear it. Whenever I got especially tired of it, I played hookey, and the whipping I got the next day did me good and cheered me up. So the longer I went to school, the easier it became. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they didn\u2019t rub me the wrong way so much. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed was hard on me most of the time, but before the cold weather I used to slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that rested me. I liked the old ways best, but I was beginning to like the new ones a little too. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely, and doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast, I happened to knock over the salt-cellar. I reached for some salt as quick as I could, to throw it over my left shoulder and keep away bad luck, but Miss Watson got there first and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you are always making!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but that wasn\u2019t going to keep off the bad luck. I knew that well enough. After breakfast, I started out feeling worried and shaky, wondering where it would strike me and what it would be. There are ways to prevent some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those kinds. So I didn\u2019t try to do anything; I just wandered along, low-spirited and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I saw somebody\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry and stood around the stile for a while, then gone on around the garden fence. It was strange that they hadn\u2019t come in after standing there so long. I couldn\u2019t understand it. Somehow, it seemed very curious. I was going to follow the tracks around, but first I bent down to look at them.\"}"
