          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b35b77a7423a5a44d14f83c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival had kindled a sort of fire in the heart of Saint Antoine, spreading quickly as they passed, and glowing in faces at nearly every door and window. Yet no one followed them, and no man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, though every eye within was fixed on them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nIt might have been the signal that loosened every tongue. A chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d answered him.\\n\\n\u201cIt is bad weather, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt that, each man looked at his neighbour; then all lowered their eyes and sat in silence. Except for one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d Defarge said aloud, speaking to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have travelled several leagues with this good road-mender, called Jacques. I met him\u2014by chance\u2014a day and a half\u2019s journey from Paris. He is a good fellow, this road-mender called Jacques. Give him something to drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender called Jacques, who removed his blue cap to the company and drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some rough dark bread; he ate it from time to time, sitting and chewing and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge refreshed himself with a drink of wine\u2014but took less than the stranger had been given, since wine was no rarity to him\u2014and waited until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He looked at no one there, and no one looked at him now; not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting and was working.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked at the proper moment.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome, then! You shall see the room I told you that you could use. It will suit you perfectly.\u201d\\n\\nOut of the wine-shop into the street, out of the street into a courtyard, out of the courtyard up a steep staircase, out of the staircase into a garret\u2014once the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, bent forward and busily making shoes.\\n\\nThe white-haired man was not there now; but the three men who had left the wine-shop one by one were there. And between them and that white-haired man far away lay the one small connection: they had once looked in at him through cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge carefully shut the door and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness I met by arrangement: myself, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
