{"request_id":"804729a132f49dc9049e9661","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was drawn by eight black horses, each with a great tuft of nodding ostrich plumes on its head, and the lead coffin was covered with a rich purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lit torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, and the entire procession was remarkably solemn. Lord Canterville, who had come especially from Wales for the funeral, was the chief mourner and rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. Next came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney rode in the last carriage. It was generally agreed that, having been frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she had a right to see the last of him. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following an old Canterville family custom, put out their torches; and as the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed upon it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. As she did so, the moon emerged from behind a cloud and poured its silent silver light over the little churchyard, while a nightingale began to sing in a distant copse. She remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death, and her eyes filled with tears; during the drive home, she scarcely spoke at all.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were truly magnificent, especially a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship; their value was so great that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about letting his daughter keep them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country mortmain applies to personal ornaments as well as land, and it seems perfectly clear to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms of your family. I must therefore ask you to take them to London with you and regard them simply as part of your property, restored to you under certain unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is only a child and, I am happy to say, has so far shown little interest in such accessories of empty luxury.’"}

