          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"804729a132f49dc9049e9661\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o\u2019clock at night. The hearse was drawn by eight black horses, each with a great tuft of nodding ostrich plumes on its head, and the lead coffin was covered with a rich purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lit torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, and the entire procession was remarkably solemn. Lord Canterville, who had come especially from Wales for the funeral, was the chief mourner and rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. Next came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney rode in the last carriage. It was generally agreed that, having been frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she had a right to see the last of him. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following an old Canterville family custom, put out their torches; and as the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed upon it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. As she did so, the moon emerged from behind a cloud and poured its silent silver light over the little churchyard, while a nightingale began to sing in a distant copse. She remembered the ghost\u2019s description of the Garden of Death, and her eyes filled with tears; during the drive home, she scarcely spoke at all.\\n\\nThe following morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were truly magnificent, especially a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship; their value was so great that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about lett