          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed0efe37760f91b258f4a94c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my time, in my own land, this revenue was raised by imposts, and the citizen supposed the foreign importer bore it, which made him easy in his mind; though in truth it was paid by the American people, and so evenly and precisely apportioned among them that the yearly charge upon the 100-millionaire and upon the day-laborer\u2019s nursing infant was exactly identical--each paid $6. Nothing could have been more equal, I suppose. Now Scotland and Ireland paid tribute to Arthur, and the combined population of the British Islands was somewhat under 1,000,000. A mechanic\u2019s ordinary pay was 3 cents a day, when he furnished his own board. On that basis the expenses of the national government were $90,000 yearly, or roughly $250 daily. So, by replacing gold with nickels on a king\u2019s-evil day, I not only harmed nobody and offended nobody, but gratified everybody concerned and, besides, saved four-fifths of that day\u2019s national outlay--a saving equal to $800,000 in the America of my day. In making this exchange I had resorted to wisdom from a very distant quarter--the wisdom of my childhood--for the genuine statesman scorns no wisdom, however humble its source: as a boy I had always kept my pennies and given buttons to the foreign missionary fund. The buttons served the ignorant savage as well as money did, while the money served me better than buttons; everybody was satisfied and no one suffered.\\n\\nMarinel received the patients in their turn. He inspected the applicant; if he failed to qualify, he was sent away with warning; if he qualified, he was forwarded to the king. A priest uttered the words, \u201cThey shall lay their hands on the sick, and they shall recover.\u201d Then, as the reading went on, the king touched the sores; at last the patient completed the process, received his nickel--which the king himself suspended from his neck--and departed. Would you imagine that could effect a cure? It surely did. Any sort of mummery will cure when the patient\u2019s faith in it is sufficiently strong. Near Astolat stood a chapel where the Virgin had once appeared to a girl who herded geese in that neighborhood--the girl herself said it--and they erected the chapel on the place and placed in it a picture depicting the event--a picture which one would think unsafe for an invalid even to come near; yet, quite the reverse, thousands of lame and sick people came each year, prayed before it, and departed cured and sound; and even healthy persons could gaze upon it and survive.\"}"
