          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8ed2c32e646f96f184c115ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They know what a sharp lad he is; he\u2019ll be a lifer. They\u2019ll make the Artful a lifer, sure enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by lagging and a lifer?\u201d asked Mr. Bolter. \u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking that way to me; why don\u2019t yer speak so I can understand yer?\u201d\\n\\nFagin was on the point of rendering these obscure terms into plain speech; and, once explained, Mr. Bolter would have learned that they meant \u201ctransportation for life,\u201d when the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of Master Bates, his hands in his breeches-pockets and his face drawn into an expression of half-comic misery.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s all over, Fagin,\u201d said Charley, after he and his new companion had been introduced.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ve found the gentleman that owns the box; two or three more are coming to \u2019dentify him; and the Artful\u2019s booked for a voyage out,\u201d answered Master Bates. \u201cI must have a complete suit of mourning, Fagin, and a hatband, to wisit him in before he starts on his travels. To think of Jack Dawkins\u2014lummy Jack\u2014the Dodger\u2014the Artful Dodger\u2014going abroad for a common twopenny-halfpenny sneeze-box! I never thought he\u2019d have done it for less than a gold watch, chain, and seals. Oh, why didn\u2019t he rob some rich old gentleman of all his walables, and go out like a gentleman, instead of a common prig, with no honour nor glory!\u201d\\n\\nHaving thus expressed his feelings for his unlucky friend, Master Bates seated himself in the nearest chair, wearing an air of vexation and despair.\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you talking about, saying he has neither honour nor glory!\u201d cried Fagin, shooting an angry glance at his pupil. \u201cWasn\u2019t he always the top-sawyer among you all! Is there one of you who could touch him, or come near him on any scent! Eh?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot one,\u201d returned Master Bates, his voice made hoarse with sorrow; \u201cnot one.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen what are you talking about?\u201d Fagin replied angrily; \u201cwhat are you blubbering for?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2019Cause it isn\u2019t on the rec-ord, is it?\u201d said Charley, driven into complete defiance of his venerable friend by the force of his regrets; \u201c\u2019cause it can\u2019t come out in the \u2019dictment; \u2019cause nobody will ever know half of what he was. How will he stand in the Newgate Calendar? P\u2019raps he won\u2019t be there at all. Oh, my eye, my eye, what a blow it is!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d cried Fagin, holding out his right hand and turning to Mr. Bolter in a fit of laughter that shook him as if he had the palsy; \u201csee what pride they take in their profession, my dear.\u201d\"}"
