          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb7864ccb85df786925502b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must leave, uncertain what my fate may be; but I will return here, or join your party, as quickly as I can. One word, one glance, will suffice to determine whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening, or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter could not soon be recovered from. Half an hour alone, given to reflection, might have calmed her; but the mere ten minutes that elapsed before she was interrupted, amid all the restraints of her circumstances, could do nothing to restore tranquillity. Each instant only brought new agitation. It was happiness too powerful to bear. And before she had passed beyond the first rush of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all entered.\\n\\nThe urgent need to appear like herself at once created a struggle; but before long she could manage no more. She ceased to comprehend a word they were saying, and was forced to plead illness and beg to be excused. They then perceived how unwell she looked, were alarmed and distressed, and would not leave her on any account. This was terrible. Had they only gone away and left her to the peaceful possession of that room, it would have cured her; but to have them all standing or lingering about her was distracting, and, in desperation, she declared that she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once, and take care of yourself, so that you may be well for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to prescribe for you, but I am no physician. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut a chair would never answer. Worse than anything! To forfeit the chance of exchanging two words with Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town\u2014and she felt nearly certain she would meet him\u2014was not to be endured. She protested earnestly against the chair; and Mrs Musgrove, who thought only of one kind of illness, having anxiously satisfied herself that there had been no fall\u2014that Anne had not lately slipped and struck her head, and that she was entirely certain she had suffered no fall\u2014could let her go cheerfully, trusting to find her better at night.\\n\\nDetermined to neglect no possible precaution, Anne made an effort and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI fear, ma\u2019am, that it has not been quite understood. Pray have the goodness to tell the other gentlemen that we expect to see your entire party this evening. I am afraid some misunderstanding has occurred; and I particularly wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear, it is perfectly understood, I assure you.\u201d\"}"
