{"request_id":"e0706c6d9b58d45d2bdbcfc1","rewritten_text":"Put down your weapons, fellow men! brothers! Forgiveness, aid, and fraternal affection await your repentance. You are precious to us, since you bear the fragile form of humanity; every one of you shall find a friend and a host among these ranks. Shall man remain man's enemy, while plague, the enemy of all, hangs over us even now, rejoicing in our slaughter, more pitiless than its own?

Both armies stood still. On our side, the soldiers held their weapons tightly and fixed severe looks upon the enemy. The others had not cast down their arms, less from any desire to fight than from fear; they glanced from one to another, each longing to follow an example,—yet no leader appeared. Adrian sprang from his horse and went to one who had lately been killed. “He was a man,” he exclaimed, “and now he is dead. O, swiftly bind the wounds of the fallen—let none perish; let no further soul pass through your ruthless cuts, to tell before God's throne the story of brother killing brother; bind their wounds—return them to their friends. Cast from your breasts the tiger hearts that rage within them; fling away those instruments of hatred and cruelty; in this interval granted by destroying fate, let every man be brother, protector, and support to another. Away with those bloodied weapons, and let some of you hasten to dress these wounds.”

While he spoke, he knelt upon the earth and lifted a man in his arms, from whose side the warm current of life poured forth—the miserable creature gasped—so motionless had both hosts grown that his groans were plainly audible, and every heart, so lately intent upon general slaughter, now throbbed with anxious hope and dread for this single man's fate. Adrian ripped away his military scarf and tied it around the wounded man—it came too late—the man drew one long sigh, his head dropped backward, and his limbs could no longer sustain him.—“He is dead!” Adrian said, as the body slipped from his arms to the ground, while he bent his head in grief and reverence. The destiny of the world appeared joined to the death of that one man. On both sides the troops dropped their arms; even the old soldiers wept, and our party extended their hands to their enemies, as a flood of love and profound friendship filled every breast. The two armies joined together, weaponless and hand in hand, speaking only of how each could help the other; the former enemies united, each regretting—the one side its earlier cruelties, the other its recent violence—and followed the General's command to move toward London."}

