          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c426eba86b50a93a277ebdd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I had no fear for her. She was what people call a capable woman, fully able to manage worldly affairs without my help. To tell the truth, I believe she had always regarded me as an idle boy, a mere burden, fit for nothing but daydreaming, and was rather pleased to be rid of me. It was a dark night when I said good-bye to her. Taking with me, as my assistants, the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, we carried the balloon, its car, and its equipment by an indirect route to the station where the other supplies had been stored. We found everything there untouched, and I immediately set to work.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the first of April. The night, as I have said, was dark, without a single star visible, and a light rain that fell now and then made us very uncomfortable. But my greatest concern was for the balloon. Despite the varnish protecting it, it was beginning to grow heavy with dampness, and the powder might also be spoiled. So I kept my three creditors hard at work, packing ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. Yet they kept bothering me with questions about what I meant to do with all this equipment, and complained bitterly about the dreadful labor I made them perform. They could not see, they said, what good could come from being soaked to the skin merely to take part in such terrible spells. I began to feel uneasy and worked with all my strength, for I truly believe the fools thought I had made a bargain with the devil, and that what I was doing was, in short, no proper business. I was therefore very afraid they would leave me altogether. Still, I managed to calm them by promising to pay every debt in full as soon as I had finished the present undertaking. Naturally, they understood these words in their own way, no doubt imagining that I would somehow gain a great deal of ready money. As long as I paid all I owed them, with a little extra for their services, I dare say they cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.\\n\\n\u201cIn about four and a half hours, I found the balloon sufficiently inflated. I then attached the car and placed all my equipment inside, not forgetting the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a large store of provisions, including pemmican, which contains much nourishment in very little space.\"}"
