          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9cfbcf6b228d6561aee4ed71\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those aged and infirm persons were invariably observed by the public in the posture of bowing, and it was commonly supposed that, after bowing one customer out, they continued bowing in the vacant office until they bowed another customer in.\\n\\nThe barrister was shrewd enough to perceive that the banker would not have carried his opinion so far without grounds amounting to moral certainty. Though unready for the substantial pill he was required to swallow, he swallowed it. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, when it was down, wagging his legal forefinger at the Temple at large, \u201cmy way of getting out of this is to put you all in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a stroke of Old Bailey strategy, from which he derived considerable comfort. \u201cYou shall not put me in the wrong, young lady,\u201d said Mr. Stryver; \u201cI will do that for you.\u201d\\n\\nThus, when Mr. Lorry called that evening, as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver, amid a heap of books and papers spread about for the purpose, appeared to have anything rather than the morning\u2019s matter in his thoughts. He even expressed surprise at seeing Mr. Lorry, and was wholly absent-minded and preoccupied.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said that kind-hearted messenger, after a full half-hour of fruitless efforts to bring him to the point. \u201cI have been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d Mr. Stryver repeated coolly. \u201cOh, certainly! What can I have been thinking of!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am convinced,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat I was right in the conversation we held. My opinion is confirmed, and I repeat my advice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d Mr. Stryver replied in the most friendly manner, \u201cthat I am sorry for it on your behalf, and sorry for it on the poor father\u2019s behalf. I know it must always be a painful subject in the family; let us speak no more of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI do not understand you,\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI dare say you do not,\u201d returned Stryver, nodding in a soothing, conclusive fashion; \u201cnever mind, never mind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d insisted Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cNo, it does not; I assure you, it does not. Having imagined sense where there is no sense, and a praiseworthy ambition where there is no praiseworthy ambition, I am happily free of my error, and no injury has been done. Young women have fallen into such follies many times before, and have regretted them in poverty and obscurity many times before. From an unselfish point of view, I am sorry the matter has fallen through, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly sense; from a selfish point of view, I am glad it has fallen through, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly sense\u2014it is scarcely necessary to add that I could have gained nothing by it.\"}"
