{"request_id":"47fc9dd283632529592b9cfb","rewritten_text":"We spent several miserable hours waiting until eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. My father and the rest of the family had to attend as witnesses, and I went with them to the court. Throughout this dreadful imitation of justice, I suffered unbearable torment. It was to be decided whether the result of my curiosity and unlawful actions would bring about the deaths of two fellow creatures: one, a cheerful little child full of innocence and happiness; the other, murdered even more terribly, under every shameful circumstance that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine, too, was a worthy girl, with qualities that seemed certain to make her life happy; now all of that was to be buried in a disgraceful grave, and I was the cause. A thousand times I would rather have confessed to the crime charged against Justine, but I had been away when it happened, and such a confession would have been taken for the madness of a lunatic. It would not have cleared her, though she suffered because of me.

Justine looked calm. She wore mourning clothes, and her face, always pleasing, was made wonderfully beautiful by the seriousness of her feelings. Yet she seemed sure of her innocence and did not shake, though thousands stared at her and cursed her. Any kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired in the spectators had been erased by their thoughts of the terrible crime she was believed to have committed. She was composed, but her calm was clearly forced; and because her earlier confusion had been used as evidence of guilt, she gathered her strength and tried to appear brave. When she entered the court, she looked around and quickly saw where we sat. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she saw us, but she soon recovered, and her expression of sad affection seemed to prove her complete innocence.

The trial began, and after the lawyer against her had stated the accusation, several witnesses were called. Many strange facts seemed to point against her, enough to shake anyone who did not possess the proof of her innocence that I did. She had been out all night on the night of the murder, and near morning a market woman had seen her not far from the place where the murdered child’s body was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strange and gave only a confused, unclear answer. She returned to the house at about eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether anything had been heard about him."}

