          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7f78db794cdff1adef37d6e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHow long I remained in this condition, it is impossible to say. It must have been quite some time, for when I partly regained my sense of being alive, I found dawn breaking, the balloon at a tremendous height above a vast waste of ocean, with no sign of land anywhere within the wide horizon. Yet my feelings on recovering were not nearly as full of agony as one might have expected. Indeed, there was something of beginning madness in the calm way I started to examine my situation. I raised each hand to my eyes in turn and wondered what could have caused the swollen veins and the dreadful blackness beneath my fingernails. Then I carefully examined my head, shaking it again and again and feeling it closely, until I convinced myself that it was not, as I had half suspected, larger than my balloon. Next, with an air of understanding, I felt in both my trouser pockets. Finding that a set of tablets and a toothpick case were missing, I tried to explain their disappearance; and when I could not, I felt deeply annoyed. It then occurred to me that my left ankle joint hurt badly, and a faint awareness of my situation began to dawn on my mind. But, strangely enough, I was neither surprised nor terrified. If I felt anything, it was a sort of amused satisfaction at the cleverness I was about to show in getting myself out of this difficulty; and I never for a moment doubted that I would ultimately be safe. For several minutes I sat absorbed in the deepest thought. I clearly remember often pressing my lips together, placing my forefinger against the side of my nose, and making other gestures and faces common to men who, comfortably seated in armchairs, think over difficult or important matters. Having, as I believed, gathered my thoughts sufficiently, I carefully and deliberately put my hands behind my back and unfastened the large iron buckle on the waistband of my trousers. The buckle had three teeth, which, being somewhat rusty, turned on their pivots only with great difficulty. After some effort, however, I brought them to right angles with the body of the buckle, and was pleased to find that they stayed firmly in place. Holding this tool between my teeth, I then began to untie the knot of my cravat. I had to rest several times before I could manage it, but at last it was done.\"}"
