          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5916cf054194b6f86ac88805\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter. They had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and a desolate countryside, nursing fierce hatred for one another; and now they argued their separate cases with unchecked party feeling.\\n\\nBy questioning the deputies separately, and after much inquiry, we learned the true condition of Paris. Since parliament had chosen him as Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had accepted Adrian\u2019s authority. He was our captain, to lead us from our native land to unknown shores; our lawgiver and our protector. When we first planned our emigration, we had not expected our people to remain divided for long, and the command of the whole body, rising step by step in power, ended with the Earl of Windsor. But unexpected events changed our plans and caused most of our numbers to be separated from their supreme leader for nearly two months. They had crossed over in two separate groups, and when they reached Paris, disagreement arose between them.\\n\\nThey found Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, returning travellers and merchants, along with letters, regularly told us of the disease\u2019s destruction on the continent. But as deaths increased, this contact grew less frequent and finally stopped. Even within England, communication between one part of the island and another became slow and rare. No ship crossed the waters between Calais and Dover; or if some sorrowful traveller, wishing to learn whether his relatives lived or died, set out from the French coast to return to us, the hungry sea often swallowed his small vessel. Or, after a day or two, he caught the disease and died before he could tell of France\u2019s desolation. We therefore knew little of the continent\u2019s condition, and still held some vague hope of finding many companions across its broad lands. But the same causes that