          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ea55aae8178773af8654be74\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something new happened. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armour, and as Adonis in a hunter\u2019s cloak, holding a gleaming boar-spear. Crowned with heavy lotus flowers, you had sat on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking out across the green, muddy Nile. You had bent over the still pool in some Greek wood and seen, in the water\u2019s silent silver, the wonder of your own face. And it had all been what art ought to be\u2014unconscious, ideal, and distant. Then one day\u2014a fatal day, I sometimes think\u2014I decided to paint a magnificent portrait of you as you truly are, not in the costume of vanished ages, but in your own clothes and your own time. Whether it was the realism of the method, or simply the wonder of your personality, presented so directly to me without mist or veil, I cannot say. But I know that, as I worked, every touch and layer of colour seemed to expose my secret. I became afraid that others would discover my idolatry. I felt, Dorian, that I had revealed too much, that I had put too much of myself into it. That was when I decided never to let the picture be exhibited. You were somewhat annoyed, but you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, whom I spoke to about it, laughed at me. But I did not care. When the portrait was finished and I sat alone with it, I felt I had been right.... Well, after a few days it left my studio, and once I was free of the unbearable fascination of its presence, I began to think I had been foolish to imagine I saw anything in it beyond the fact that you were remarkably handsome and that I could paint. Even now, I cannot help feeling that it is mistaken to believe the passion one feels while creating is ever truly visible in the work itself. Art is always more abstract than we suppose. Form and colour speak only of form and colour\u2014that is all. It often seems to me that art hides the artist far more completely than it reveals him. So, when I received this offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the central work in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have told you.\"}"
