ts of him. Because of all this, I am inclined to deal gently with you now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford, even now\u2014and I blush for the unworthiness of one who bears that name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat does the name have to do with it?\u201d the other asked, after watching, partly in silence and partly in stubborn amazement, his companion\u2019s agitation. \u201cWhat is that name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d Mr. Brownlow answered, \u201cnothing to you. But it was hers, and even after all this time it brings back to me, an old man, the warmth and thrill I once felt merely on hearing it spoken again by a stranger. I am very glad you changed it\u2014very, very glad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very fine,\u201d said Monks\u2014keeping his assumed name\u2014after a long silence, during which he had shifted back and forth in sullen defiance while Mr. Brownlow sat with his hand shading his face.\"}"
