          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"61c0b9f4f056f2928056155f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been during that very period of Mr Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cwhich has always especially interested me. It must have been around the time he became known to my father and sister. I never knew him myself; I only heard about him. But there was something in his behaviour then, toward my father and sister, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that I could never quite reconcile with the present. It seemed to suggest a different kind of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know all about it, I know all about it,\u201d cried Mrs Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I knew him, but I heard him speak of them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. Perhaps I can satisfy you on matters you would hardly expect; and as for his marriage, I knew everything about it at the time. I knew all the arguments for and against it; I was the friend in whom he confided his hopes and plans. Though I did not know his wife beforehand\u2014her lower position in society made that impossible\u2014I knew her throughout the rest of her life, or at least until the last two years of it, and can answer any question you might wish to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular question about her. I have always understood that they were not a happy couple. But I should like to know why, at that point in his life, he should have rejected my father\u2019s acquaintance as he did. My father was certainly inclined to show him every proper kindness and attention. Why did Mr Elliot withdraw?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs Smith, \u201chad one aim at that time in his life: to make his fortune, and by a quicker means than the law. He was determined to make it through marriage. At the very least, he was determined not to ruin it by an imprudent marriage; and I know he believed\u2014whether rightly or wrongly, I cannot say\u2014that your father and sister, in their attentions and invitations, intended a match between the heir and the young lady. Such a match could not possibly have suited his ideas of wealth and independence. That was why he withdrew, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He kept nothing from me. It was strange that, having just left you behind in Bath, my first and closest acquaintance after marrying should be your cousin, and that through him I should continually hear of your father and sister.\"}"
