          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"88fffb2c467a8342fd015240\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities described as analytical are, in themselves, scarcely open to analysis. We recognize them only through their effects. Among other things, we know that, when possessed in excess, they are always a source of the keenest pleasure to their owner. Just as a strong man takes pride in his physical power, delighting in exercises that put his muscles to work, so the analyst rejoices in that mental activity which disentangles. He finds pleasure even in the most trivial pursuits that call his talent into use. He enjoys riddles, puzzles, and hieroglyphics, displaying in his solutions to each a sharpness that seems supernatural to ordinary understanding. His conclusions, produced by the very spirit and essence of method, truly have all the appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe power of resolving things again may perhaps be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, especially by its highest branch, which has unjustly been called analysis above all others merely because of its backward operations. Yet calculation is not, in itself, analysis. A chess player, for instance, performs the former without making any effort at the latter. It follows that chess is greatly misunderstood in its effects on the mind. I am not writing a treatise now, but merely introducing a rather unusual narrative with observations made somewhat at random; I will therefore take this opportunity to assert that the higher powers of reflective thought are more clearly and more usefully exercised by the modest game of draughts than by all the elaborate frivolity of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different and bizarre ways and have various, changing values, mere complexity is mistaken\u2014an error not uncommon\u2014for depth. Attention is strongly demanded. If it lapses for a moment, an oversight occurs, bringing loss or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but intricate, the chances of such oversights multiply; and in nine cases out of ten, it is the more concentrated player, rather than the more acute one, who wins. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are uniform and vary little, the likelihood of carelessness is reduced; and, with mere attention comparatively unoccupied, whatever advantages either player gains come through superior acumen. To make this less abstract, imagine a game of draughts in which only four kings remain, and in which, naturally, no oversight can be expected. It is clear that victory can then be decided, assuming the players are otherwise equal, only by some subtle move resulting from a powerful effort of intellect.\"}"
