          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cfd64f38722f1a89d95353b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I could not stay away from the places where they lay, but felt that they were somehow more hopeful and less forlorn when I was near. In this irrational uneasiness and distress of mind, I would pace the streets in the evening, passing those offices and houses where I had deposited the petitions. Even now, the dreary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring evening, with their lines of severe, closed mansions and their long ranks of lamps, are sad to me through this association.\\n\\nThe daily visits I was allowed to make him were now curtailed, and he was kept under closer watch. Perceiving, or imagining, that I was suspected of meaning to bring him poison, I requested to be searched before taking my place beside his bed, and told the officer who was constantly present that I would submit to anything which might convince him of the honesty of my purpose. No one dealt harshly with him or with me. A duty had to be performed, and it was performed, though without severity. The officer invariably assured me that he was worse; and certain other sick prisoners in the room, together with other prisoners who served them as sick nurses, (wrongdoers, yet not beyond kindness, thank God!) always confirmed the same account.\\n\\nAs the days passed, I observed increasingly that he would lie quietly gazing at the white ceiling, his face without brightness until some word from me lit it for a moment, after which it faded again. At times he was nearly, or altogether, unable to speak; then he answered by faint pressures of my hand, and I came to comprehend his meaning very well.\\n\\nThe days had reached ten when I perceived a greater alteration in him than any I had yet seen. His eyes were fixed upon the door, and brightened when I came in.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said, as I seated myself beside his bed, \u201cI thought you was late. But I knowed you couldn\u2019t be that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is exactly the time,\u201d said I. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always waits at the gate; don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. So as not to lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank\u2019ee, dear boy, thank\u2019ee. God bless you! You\u2019ve never forsaken me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand silently, for I could not forget that I had once intended to forsake him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd what\u2019s best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun was shining.\u201d\"}"
