          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"650636d459426bcce6084353\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one; so far had he been from sternly demanding the payment of his dues, that he had voluntarily surrendered them, cast himself upon a world that showed him no favour, made his own modest place in it, and earned his own livelihood. Monsieur Gabelle had administered the ruined and encumbered estate under written directions to spare the people, and to allow them whatever little could be allowed--such firewood as the pressing creditors permitted in winter, and such produce as might be rescued from that same grasp in summer--and doubtless, for his own protection, he had pleaded and proved the fact, so that it must now be evident.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate determination Charles Darnay had begun to form: that he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor in the ancient tale, winds and currents had borne him into the power of the Loadstone Rock; it drew him towards itself, and he had to go. Every thought that came before him carried him onward, swifter and swifter, with ever firmer certainty, towards that dreadful attraction. His hidden disquiet had been that evil purposes were being accomplished in his unhappy country by evil agents, while he, who could not but know himself better than they, was absent instead of striving to check bloodshed and uphold the claims of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness partly smothered and partly accusing him, he had been led to the sharp comparison between himself and the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so powerful; upon that comparison, damaging to himself, had immediately come Monseigneur's sneers, which had wounded him deeply, and Stryver's, which were above all vulgar and irritating, for old causes. Then had come Gabelle's letter: the appeal of an innocent captive, threatened with death, to his justice, his honour, and his good name.\\n\\nHis decision was fixed. He must go to Paris