          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a29433a0a84400dc8732486\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always pretended Peter wrote those things for him; but that was a lie: he got every single one from that young fool whose canoe we took up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane brought the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud and cried over it. It gave the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls; and it gave the tannery, which was doing good business, along with some other houses and land worth about seven thousand dollars, and three thousand dollars in gold, to Harvey and William, and said where the six thousand in cash was hidden in the cellar. So the two frauds said they would go get it and have everything fair and aboveboard, and told me to bring a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag, they poured it out on the floor. It was a beautiful sight, all those gold coins. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said:\\n\\n\u201cOh, this isn\u2019t fine or anything! Oh no, I suppose not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke agreed it did. They handled the gold coins, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle onto the floor; and the king said:\\n\\n\u201cThere\u2019s no use denying it: being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs who have been left an inheritance is the line of work for you and me, Bilge. This comes from trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there isn\u2019t any better way.\u201d\\n\\nMost people would have been satisfied with the pile and taken it on trust; but no, they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king said:\\n\\n\u201cDamn him, I wonder what he did with that four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d\\n\\nThey fretted over it for a while and searched everywhere for it. Then the duke said:\\n\\n\u201cWell, he was a very sick man, and he probably made a mistake\u2014I suppose that\u2019s what happened. The best thing is to let it go and keep quiet about it. We can spare it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, sure, we can spare it. I don\u2019t care anything about that\u2014it\u2019s the count I\u2019m thinking about. We want to be terribly fair and open and aboveboard here, you know. We want to carry this money upstairs and count it before everybody\u2014then there won\u2019t be anything suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
