          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b1d97a93c0bca87c8f17bd1b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you talking about\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I am. I could never have meant anyone else\u2014and I thought you understood. When we spoke of him, it seemed perfectly clear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot entirely,\u201d Emma replied, trying to remain calm. \u201cEverything you said then seemed to me to concern someone else. I could almost swear that you named Mr. Frank Churchill. I am sure you spoke of the service Mr. Frank Churchill did you, when he protected you from the gypsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember very well the substance of what I said then. I told you I was not surprised by your attachment; that, considering the service he had done you, it was very natural. And you agreed, speaking very warmly of how grateful you felt, and even describing your feelings when you saw him come to rescue you. I remember it strongly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I understand what you mean; but I was thinking of something quite different at the time. It was not the gypsies\u2014it was not Mr. Frank Churchill I meant. No!\u201d she added, with some dignity. \u201cI was thinking of something far more precious\u2014of Mr. Knightley coming to ask me to dance when Mr. Elton would not dance with me, and there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind act; that was the noble generosity and goodness; that was what first made me feel how much he was above every other person on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d cried Emma. \u201cThis has been a most unfortunate\u2014most dreadful mistake! What can be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood? Still, I cannot be worse off than I would have been if the other gentleman had been the one; and now\u2014it is possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe stopped for a few moments. Emma could not speak.\\n\\n\u201cI do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d Harriet continued, \u201cthat you should see a great difference between the two, whether for me or for anyone. You must think one of them five hundred million times more above me than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, supposing\u2014if\u2014strange as it may seem\u2014 But you know those were your own words: that more surprising things had happened, and matches with greater differences had been made than one between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. So it seems that even such a thing as this may have happened before. And if I were fortunate enough, beyond words, if Mr. Knightley really should\u2014if he does not mind the difference between us, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not oppose it or try to place difficulties in the way.\u201d\"}"
