red and sixty-one butterflies, whose wings were similarly ornamented with the arms of the queen, the whole worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de Medicis had a mourning bed made of black velvet scattered with crescents and suns. Its curtains were damask, patterned with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold-and-silver background, edged with pearl embroidery; it stood in a room hung with rows of the queen\u2019s emblems, cut from black velvet and set on cloth of silver. Louis XIV had gold-embroidered caryatids fifteen feet high in his apartment.\"}"
