          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b8b2487c7aba1446cf28863\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams to vanish like this? Our name had been written \u201ca little lower than the angels\u201d; yet behold, we were no better than ephemera. We had called ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and now we were a \u201cquintessence of dust.\u201d We complained that the pyramids had survived the embalmed body of their builder. Alas! the simple straw shepherd\u2019s hut we passed on the road held within its structure the principle of greater endurance than the entire human race. How could we reconcile this dreadful transformation with our former hopes, our seeming powers?\\n\\nThen an inward voice, distinct and clear, seemed suddenly to say:\u2014So it was decreed from eternity: the steeds that carry Time onward had this hour and its fulfillment bound to them from the moment the void brought forth its burden. Would you read backward the immutable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Omnipotent! eternal, unchanging Necessity, who sits forever with busy fingers weaving the unbreakable chain of events!\u2014I will not complain of your acts. Though my human mind cannot admit that all that exists is right, still, since what is must be, I will sit among the ruins and smile. Truly, we were not born to enjoy, but to submit and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow weary if I describe in detail our long journey from Paris to Geneva? If, day by day, I recorded in the form of a journal the crowded miseries of our condition, could my hand write, or language provide words to express, the variety of our suffering\u2014the jostling and pressing of one miserable event upon another? Be patient, reader! Whoever you are, wherever you live, whether of spiritual race or descended from some surviving pair, your nature will be human and your home the earth. Here you will read of the deeds of an extinct race, and wonder whether those who endured what you find recorded were made of frail flesh and delicate organization like your own. Indeed they were\u2014weeping is therefore fitting; for surely, solitary being, you will be gentle by nature. Shed tears of compassion; but meanwhile give your attention to this tale, and learn the deeds and sufferings of your predecessors.\\n\\nYet the final events that marked our passage through France were so filled with strange horror and dark misery that I dare not linger too long over the account. If I dissected every incident, each smallest fragment of a second would contain a harrowing story, whose slightest word would chill the blood in your young veins. It is right that I raise, for your instruction, this monument to the vanished race; but not that I should drag you through the wards of a hospital or the hidden chambers of the charnel house.\"}"
