          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a460419bf6be530798807a33\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If she had known that her sister meant to deprive her of prospects and realities such as these, what would she have felt? Her mother alone could have understood it, and might almost have shared the feeling. Lydia\u2019s journey to Brighton was the only comfort she had against the sorrowful certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nYet they knew nothing of what had occurred; and their delight went on, scarcely interrupted, until the very day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to meet Mr. Wickham for the last time. Having often been in his company since her return, her agitation had nearly subsided; that arising from her former partiality had vanished altogether. She had even learned to perceive, in the gentleness which had first charmed her, an affectation and uniformity that tired and disgusted her. His present conduct toward herself also gave her new cause for displeasure; for the readiness he soon showed to renew the attentions of their early acquaintance could, after all that had happened, only offend her. She ceased to care for him when she found herself chosen for such vain and trifling gallantry; and though she firmly discouraged it, she could not help feeling the rebuke implied by his supposing that, however long and for whatever reason his attentions had been withheld, their return would at any time flatter her vanity and secure her preference.\\n\\nOn the regiment\u2019s final day in Meryton, he dined at Longbourn with several other officers; and Elizabeth was so little inclined to take leave of him pleasantly that, when he asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she told him that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both stayed three weeks at Rosings, and inquired whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe appeared surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s reflection, with his smile returning, he answered that he had once seen him frequently; and, having remarked that he was a very gentlemanlike man, asked how she had liked him. Her reply was warmly favourable. Soon afterward, with an indifferent air, he added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners differ greatly from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, greatly; but I think Mr. Darcy improves upon acquaintance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look she did not fail to notice. \u201cAnd may I ask--\u201d But stopping himself, he continued in a livelier tone, \u201cIs it in his address that he improves?\u201d\"}"
