st eventually learn was taught to me in a way few are forced to receive it. I mourn now, and must always mourn, those few brief months of paradise; I broke no command, I ate no apple, yet I was ruthlessly driven from it. Alas! my companion did, and I was cast down in his fall. But I stray from my account\u2014let sorrow come at its appointed time; at this point in my story, I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship when my aunt became ill. I spent an entire month in her room nursing her, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for some time. Death is so terrible to the living; the bonds of habit are so strong, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must suffer agony when they are broken.\"}"
