          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fee451a4dab40e820e07f5c0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI haven\u2019t the nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really be comfortable, spending the entire evening away from the poor child?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see, his papa can, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he is. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I am no more anxious about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly alarmed yesterday, but things are very different today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it is too late to send word for yourself, suppose you go too, along with your husband. Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I am staying with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cDear me, that is a very good idea\u2014very good indeed. Of course, I may as well go as stay, since I am no use at home, am I? And it only worries me. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s feelings, are much more suited to it. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at a word. It will be far better than leaving him with Jemima alone. Oh, I shall certainly go. I am sure I ought to, if I can, quite as much as Charles, because they are so eager for me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. An excellent idea of yours, Anne. I will go tell Charles and get ready at once. You can send for us, you know, at a moment\u2019s notice if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to alarm you. You may be sure I would not go if I did not feel perfectly easy about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment, she was tapping at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and Anne, following her upstairs, arrived in time to hear the entire conversation, which began with Mary saying in a tone of great triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I am no more use at home than you are. If I shut myself away forever with the child, I could not persuade him to do anything he did not want to do. Anne will stay; Anne has agreed to remain at home and care for him. It was Anne\u2019s own suggestion, so I shall go with you, which will be much better, because I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I would be very glad to have you go, but it seems rather hard that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
