{"request_id":"8e9300d9aa87fd2f7d59866c","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each with a large tuft of waving ostrich feathers on its head. The lead coffin was covered with a rich purple cloth embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lighted torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, and the whole procession was deeply impressive. Lord Canterville, who had come especially from Wales for the funeral, was the chief mourner. He rode in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney sat in the last carriage. Everyone felt that, since the ghost had frightened her for more than fifty years, she had the right to see the last of him. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. When it ended, the servants put out their torches, following an old Canterville family custom. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed upon it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud and filled the little churchyard with silent silver light, while a nightingale began to sing in a distant grove. Virginia remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death. Her eyes filled with tears, and she scarcely spoke during the drive home.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were truly magnificent, especially a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century workmanship. They were so valuable that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about letting his daughter keep them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country the law of mortmain applies to ornaments as well as land, and it seems clear to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, family heirlooms. I must therefore ask you to take them to London and consider them simply part of your property, returned to you under unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is only a child and, I am happy to say, has little interest yet in such useless luxuries.’"}

