{"request_id":"0955244d30e55f6fbcf05589","rewritten_text":"When I stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to think more calmly about what had happened and what I ought to do. Only a few hours had passed, yet a great change had taken place in my life. Since morning, I had lived through what would naturally take years: my father seemed dead to me, and for a moment I imagined him, his hair white with age, lying in his coffin, while I—my youth gone, nearing old age—wept over his fitting death. But it was not so. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to others. Yet I, most miserable of all, must never see or speak to him again. I must flee from him more eagerly than from my worst enemy; whether alone or among crowds, I must never look upon him again. The thought took my breath away with grief, and so filled my mind that for a time I could follow no other thought. From then on, I believed, I would live in the deepest isolation. I would go abroad and become a nun—not for religion, since I was not Catholic, but so that I might be shut away from the world forever. There I would find solitude in which to weep, and never hear the voices of life.

But my father—my beloved, most unhappy father? Would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now ruled him without mercy? Might he not, many years from then, when age had cooled the burning feelings he now suffered, become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow, and I felt—and wept to feel it—a sad half-smile soften the suffering on my lips. I dared to hope for a better future. Years would have to pass, but hope would give them wings; or, if they passed slowly, they would still pass, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering alone. Let him once more speak his wild complaints to vast forests and terrible waterfalls in another land. Let him again face dreadful dangers and hardships that crush the soul. Let the hot southern sun burn his passion-worn cheeks again, and cold night rain fall upon him and chill his blood.

To such a life, miserable father, I give you! Go! Let your days be spent among savages, and your nights beneath the open sky. Let your limbs grow weary, your heart grow cold, and all youth die within you!"}

