          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9a85d5ced25be8c57bf8b5ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Otis was greatly troubled by Lord Canterville\u2019s refusal, and implored him to think again; but the kindly peer remained quite resolute, and at last persuaded the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift the ghost had bestowed upon her. Thus, when the young Duchess of Cheshire was presented at the Queen\u2019s first drawing-room in the spring of 1890, upon her marriage, her jewels were admired by every one. For Virginia had received the coronet, the reward of all good little American girls, and married her childhood sweetheart as soon as he reached his majority. They were both so delightful, and loved one another so dearly, that everybody rejoiced in the union, save the old Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had hoped to secure the Duke for one of her seven unmarried daughters, and had given no fewer than three costly dinner-parties to that end, and, oddly enough, Mr. Otis himself. Mr. Otis was exceedingly fond of the young Duke in person, but in theory he disapproved of titles and, as he put it, \u2018was not without apprehension lest, amid the enervating influences of a pleasure-loving aristocracy, the true principles of republican simplicity should be forgotten.\u2019 Yet his objections were entirely set aside, and I believe that, as he walked up the aisle of St. George\u2019s, Hanover Square, his daughter resting on his arm, no man in all England was prouder than he.\\n\\nWhen their honeymoon had ended, the Duke and Duchess went down to Canterville Chase, and on the afternoon following their arrival they walked across to the solitary churchyard beside the pine-woods. At first there had been much dispute over the inscription for Sir Simon\u2019s tombstone, but it was eventually resolved that it should bear only the old gentleman\u2019s initials and the verse from the library window. The Duchess had brought some beautiful roses with her, and scattered them over the grave; and, after standing there for a while, they wandered into the ruined chancel of the ancient abbey. There the Duchess seated herself upon a fallen pillar, while her husband lay at her feet, smoking a cigarette and gazing into her lovely eyes. All at once he flung away his cigarette, took her hand, and said, \u2018Virginia, a wife ought to have no secrets from her husband.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Cecil! I have no secrets from you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, you do,\u2019 he replied with a smile. \u2018You have never told me what befell you when you were shut up with the ghost.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I have told no one, Cecil,\u2019 Virginia said gravely.\"}"
