          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5f092b787b1d0c58c1383ce8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no work to occupy him, he placed the knuckles of his right hand in the palm of his left, then the knuckles of his left hand in the palm of his right, then drew one hand across his bearded chin, and continued these movements in regular succession without a moment\u2019s pause. To call him back from the wandering state into which he always fell after speaking was like reviving a very weak person from a faint, or trying, in hopes of some revelation, to hold back the spirit of a man dying quickly.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a weary sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he bent over his work again, until the silence was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by profession?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, watching him steadily.\\n\\nHis hollow eyes turned toward Defarge, as though he wished to pass the question on to him; but receiving no help from that direction, they searched the floor and then returned to the man who had asked it.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by profession? No, I was not a shoemaker by profession. I-I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to--\u201d\\n\\nHe drifted away again, for several minutes, making those measured changes with his hands all the while. At last his eyes slowly returned to the face from which they had strayed; when they settled there, he started and continued, like a sleeper just awakened and returning to a subject from the night before.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time I obtained it with great difficulty, and I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still looking steadily into his face:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing about me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the floor, and he sat staring fixedly at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry put his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time come back to your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nAs the captive of so many years stared, first at Mr. Lorry and then at Defarge, faint traces of an alert, intent intelligence long erased began gradually to show through the dark mist that had settled over him, in the middle of his forehead. They were clouded over again, grew fainter, and vanished; but they had been there.\"}"
