          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"675fbb8b98b4c664555b2595\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Throughout dinner, Mr. Bennet said scarcely a word; but after the servants had left the room, he considered it proper to converse with his guest, and accordingly introduced a topic on which he expected him to excel, remarking that he appeared singularly fortunate in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s regard for his wishes, and her concern for his comfort, seemed highly remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have selected a better subject. Mr. Collins became eloquent in her commendation. The theme raised him to an unusual solemnity of deportment; and, wearing an air of great importance, he declared that never in his life had he observed such conduct in a person of rank--such affability and condescension as he himself had received from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both the sermons which he had already enjoyed the honour of preaching before her. She had likewise invited him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the preceding Saturday, to complete her quadrille party for the evening. Lady Catherine was thought proud by many, he knew, but _he_ had seen in her nothing except affability. She had invariably addressed him as she might any other gentleman; she had raised not the least objection either to his mixing with the neighbourhood\u2019s society or to his quitting his parish now and then for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even condescended to recommend that he marry as soon as possible, if he chose prudently; and had once visited him at his humble parsonage, where she had entirely approved the improvements he was making, and had even deigned to propose a few herself,--some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is certainly very proper and civil, I am sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a most agreeable woman. It is a pity great ladies generally are not more like her. Does she reside near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden containing my humble abode is divided from Rosings Park, her Ladyship\u2019s residence, by only a lane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you mentioned that she was a widow, sir? Has she any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has but one daughter, the heiress of Rosings and of a very extensive property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better situated than many girls. And what kind of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is indeed a most charming young lady. Lady Catherine herself says that, in the matter of real beauty, Miss de Bourgh is greatly superior to the handsomest of her sex; for there is something in her features that distinguishes the young woman of high birth.\u201d\"}"
