          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"394448a4372158ce84550d4d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had rented a very fine house in Camden Place, in a high, impressive position suited to a man of importance; and he and Elizabeth were both settled there, greatly pleased with it.\\n\\nAnne entered with a heavy heart, expecting to be confined there for many months, and anxiously asking herself, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Yet the unexpected warmth of her welcome did her some good. Her father and sister were glad to see her, because they could show her the house and its furnishings, and they greeted her kindly. Having her as a fourth person at dinner was considered an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was very agreeable and full of smiles, but her politeness and smiles were more habitual. Anne had always known she would act as propriety required when she arrived, but the kindness of the others had not been expected. They were plainly in very high spirits, and Anne was soon to hear why. They had no wish to hear about her. After inviting a few compliments about how much she had been missed in their old neighbourhood, which Anne could not give, they asked only a few weak questions before taking over the conversation. Uppercross interested them not at all, and Kellynch very little: everything was Bath.\\n\\nThey delighted in assuring her that Bath had exceeded their expectations in every way. Their house was certainly the best in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms had clear advantages over every other they had seen or heard of, and were equally superior in their decoration and the taste of their furniture. Their acquaintance was eagerly sought. Everyone wished to visit them. They had refused many introductions, yet cards were constantly being left by people they did not know.\\n\\nThere was plenty here to please them. Could Anne be surprised that her father and sister were happy? She need not be surprised, but she had to sigh that her father felt no loss of dignity in the change, saw nothing to regret in the responsibilities and importance of a resident landowner, and could find so much vanity in the small concerns of a town. She had to sigh, smile, and wonder as Elizabeth opened the folding doors and walked proudly from one drawing-room to the other, boasting of their size; wondering how a woman who had been mistress of Kellynch Hall could take pride in a space between two walls perhaps thirty feet apart.\\n\\nBut that was not all that made them happy. They had Mr Elliot too.\"}"
