          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2e4afb085ca24be3a4298bb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the sight of it, the Ourang-Outang immediately bounded through the chamber door, down the staircase, and then, by way of a window unhappily left open, into the street.\\n\\nThe Frenchman pursued in despair; the ape, with the razor yet in its hand, now and then pausing to glance behind and make gestures at its pursuer, until the latter had almost overtaken it. Then it fled anew. Thus the pursuit went on for a considerable time. The streets were utterly still, it being close upon three o\u2019clock in the morning. As it passed through an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the fugitive\u2019s eye was caught by a light shining from the open window of Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s chamber, on the fourth floor of her house. Dashing to the building, it noticed the lightning rod, climbed it with unimaginable nimbleness, caught hold of the shutter, which stood thrown entirely back against the wall, and by this contrivance swung itself straight onto the bed\u2019s headboard. The entire exploit consumed less than a minute. As the Ourang-Outang entered the room, it kicked the shutter open once more.\\n\\nMeanwhile, the sailor was at once delighted and bewildered. He had high hopes of recovering the beast, since it could scarcely get out of the snare into which it had rushed except by the rod, where he might intercept it on its descent. Yet there was ample reason to fear what it might do within the house. This thought, however, impelled the man to continue after the fugitive. A lightning rod may be climbed readily enough, particularly by a sailor; but when he had risen as far as the window, lying far to his left, his progress was arrested; all he could manage was to lean over far enough to catch a view of the room\u2019s interior. At that sight, the extremity of his horror nearly caused him to lose his grasp. It was then that those dreadful cries broke upon the night, rousing the sleepers of the Rue Morgue. Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, dressed in their nightclothes, seemed to have been engaged in sorting papers in the iron chest already referred to, which had been rolled into the center of the room. It stood open, its contents scattered beside it on the floor. The victims must have been seated with their backs to the window; and from the interval between the beast\u2019s entrance and the screams, it appears likely that they did not notice it at once. The closing flap of the shutter would naturally have been ascribed to the wind.\\n\\nAs the sailor peered in, the enormous creature had caught Madame L\u2019Espanaye by her hair, loose because she had been combing it, and was waving the razor about her face in imitation of a barber\u2019s movements.\"}"
