          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ad1347e181978276e298e4aa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General told the jury that the prisoner before them, though young in age, was already experienced in the treasonable acts that demanded his life. His correspondence with the public enemy, he said, had not begun today, yesterday, last year, or even the year before. It was certain that, for longer than that, the prisoner had been travelling back and forth between France and England on secret business for which he could give no honest explanation. If treason could ever prosper\u2014which, happily, it never could\u2014the true wickedness and guilt of his dealings might have remained hidden. Providence, however, had moved a person beyond fear and beyond reproach to uncover the prisoner\u2019s plans and, horrified by them, reveal them to his Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. This patriot, he said, would appear before them. His position and conduct were, altogether, sublime. He had been the prisoner\u2019s friend, but, in an hour both fortunate and terrible, had discovered his infamy and resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer keep in his heart upon the sacred altar of his country. If Britain awarded statues to public benefactors, as ancient Greece and Rome had done, this brilliant citizen would surely have received one. Since it did not, he probably would not.\\n\\nVirtue, he continued, as the poets had observed in many passages\u2014which he knew the jury had word for word on the tips of their tongues, though their faces showed a guilty awareness that they knew none of them\u2014was contagious in a way. This was especially true of the bright virtue called patriotism, or love of country. The noble example of this spotless and unquestionable witness for the Crown\u2014whom it was an honour even to mention, however unworthily\u2014had spread to the prisoner\u2019s servant. It had inspired in him the holy resolve to search his master\u2019s desk drawers and pockets and hide away his papers. Mr. Attorney-General was prepared, he said, to hear attempts to speak badly of this admirable servant; but generally, he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters, and respected him more than his own father and mother. He confidently called upon the jury to do the same. The evidence of these two witnesses, together with the documents they had discovered and which would be produced, would show that the prisoner had been supplied with lists of his Majesty\u2019s forces and their positions and readiness, by sea and by land. It would leave no doubt that he had regularly carried such information to a hostile power.\"}"
