          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"76f4298ebee09c72aa605b30\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We declared so loudly and earnestly that we were free men that we caught the attention of that liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic crowd. They gathered around us and took a very firm stand. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf ye truly are freemen, ye have nothing to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround ye as shield and shelter! (Applause.) Ye shall soon see. Bring forth your proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proof?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that ye are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered! I came to my senses and said nothing. But the king burst out:\\n\\n\u201cThou art mad, man. It would be better, and more reasonable, for this thief and scoundrel to prove that we are not freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he knew his own laws as people so often know laws: by their words, not their results. Laws take on a meaning, and become very clear, when they are applied to yourself.\\n\\nEveryone shook their heads and looked disappointed; some turned away, no longer interested. The orator said\u2014and this time his voice was businesslike, not sentimental:\\n\\n\u201cIf ye do not know your country's laws, it is time ye learned them. Ye are strangers here; ye will not deny that. Ye may be freemen\u2014we do not deny it\u2014but ye may also be slaves. The law is plain: it does not require the claimant to prove ye are slaves; it requires ye to prove ye are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cDear sir, give us only time to send to Astolat; or only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and ye cannot expect them to be granted. They would take much time and would unfairly inconvenience your master\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaster, idiot!\u201d the king thundered. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God's sake!\u201d\\n\\nI got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in enough trouble; it could not help to make these people think we were lunatics.\\n\\nThere is no point in dragging out the details. The earl put us up for sale and sold us at auction. This same cursed law had existed in our own South in my own time, more than thirteen hundred years later. Under it, hundreds of free men who could not prove they were free had been sold into slavery for life, without the fact making much impression on me. But the moment the law and the auction block became part of my own experience, something that had seemed merely wrong suddenly became hellish. Well, that is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like pigs. In a large town with a busy market, we would have brought a good price; but this place was completely dead, and we sold for an amount that still makes me ashamed whenever I think of it.\"}"
