          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"366225cf53be24b964d2eb31\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and horses in heavy armor could not travel very quickly. They would make the best speed they could, and now that the ground was firm, with no snow or mud, they would probably manage seven miles an hour; they would have to change horses a couple of times; they would arrive around six, or a little later; there would still be plenty of daylight; they would see the white cloth I would tie around my right arm, and I would take command. We would surround that prison and have the king out in no time. Considering everything, it would be showy and picturesque enough, though I would have preferred noon, because the affair would have had a more theatrical appearance.\\n\\nThen, to add another string to my bow, I thought I would seek out some of those people I had recognized before and reveal myself to them. That might help get us out of our trouble without the knights. But I had to move carefully, because it was risky business. I needed to get into splendid clothing, and it would not do to rush straight into it. No, I had to work my way up gradually, buying one suit after another in shops far apart, each outfit a little finer than the last, until at last I reached silk and velvet and was ready for my plan. So I set out.\\n\\nBut the scheme collapsed instantly! At the first corner I turned, I ran straight into one of our slaves, prowling about with a watchman. I coughed just then, and he gave me a sharp look that went straight to my marrow. I suppose he thought he had heard that cough before. I immediately turned into a shop and moved along the counter, asking prices and watching from the corner of my eye. Those two had stopped and were talking together, looking in through the doorway. I decided to escape through the back, if there was a back exit, and asked the shopwoman whether I might step out there to look for the escaped slave, believed to be hiding somewhere behind the shop. I told her I was an officer in disguise, that my partner was over by the door with one of the murderers in custody, and asked whether she would kindly go tell him not to wait, but to hurry to the far end of the back alley and be ready to cut the man off when I drove him out.\\n\\nShe was aflame with eagerness to see one of those already famous murderers, and she set off on the errand at once.\"}"
