          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1ed5d5326a6ae680d40177a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma never had to take back her poor opinion of Mrs. Elton because of anything she later learned. Her judgment had been quite accurate. Mrs. Elton was, on this second meeting and on every meeting after it, exactly as Emma had first found her: self-important, presumptuous, overfamiliar, ignorant, and badly mannered. She had some beauty and some accomplishments, but so little sense that she believed she had come, with superior knowledge of the world, to brighten and improve a country neighbourhood. She imagined that Miss Hawkins had held a position in society which only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance could exceed.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton saw his wife differently. He seemed not only happy with her, but proud of her. He looked as if he congratulated himself on bringing to Highbury a woman whom not even Miss Woodhouse could equal. Most of her new acquaintances were ready to praise her, or were not used to judging people closely. Following Miss Bates\u2019s kindly lead, or assuming that the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she said she was, they were quite satisfied. Thus Mrs. Elton\u2019s praises passed easily from person to person, as such praise should, with no obstacle from Miss Woodhouse, who willingly added her own first approval and said pleasantly that Mrs. Elton was \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one way, Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings toward Emma changed. Probably offended by the little encouragement Emma gave to her offers of friendship, she withdrew in return and gradually became much colder and more distant. Though this was agreeable in itself, the ill-will behind it naturally increased Emma\u2019s dislike. Her manner toward Harriet, and Mr. Elton\u2019s as well, was unpleasant. They treated her with sneering neglect. Emma hoped it would quickly help cure Harriet; but the feelings that led them to behave so made both of them seem much worse to her. There could be little doubt that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been shared freely between husband and wife, and that Harriet\u2019s part in the story had probably been told too, in the light least favourable to her and most comforting to him. Naturally, she was the object of their shared dislike. Whenever they had nothing else to discuss, it must have been easy to begin criticizing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they did not dare show Emma openly found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a great liking to Jane Fairfax, and did so from the beginning. It was not merely that being at odds with one young woman might make another seem more appealing, but that she liked Jane from the first. Nor was she content with expressing a natural and reasonable admiration. Without being asked, without any excuse or right, she had to offer Jane help and friendship. Before Emma had lost Mrs. Elton\u2019s confidence, around their third meeting, she heard all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s eager plans on the subject.\"}"
