          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2b366cb6fbec5f6036c17db1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, midnight rang out from the great tower at Westminster, and with every stroke of the deep bell the night itself seemed to shudder. Then the railway lights were extinguished, leaving one lone lamp shining like a great ruby atop a gigantic mast, and the city\u2019s roar grew quieter.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and walked towards Blackfriars. Everything looked so unreal\u2014like some bizarre dream. The houses across the river appeared to be made of darkness. It was as though silver and shadow had remade the world. The vast dome of St. Paul\u2019s rose through the dim air like a bubble.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man bent over the parapet. When he came closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight fell directly on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the palmist. No one could have mistaken that fat, slack face, those gold-rimmed spectacles, that weak, unhealthy smile, or that sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A dazzling idea came to him, and he crept quietly up behind the man. In an instant, he seized Mr. Podgers by the legs and hurled him into the Thames. There was a rough curse, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur leaned over anxiously, but he could see nothing of the palmist except a tall hat spinning in an eddy of moonlit water. After a while, that too sank, and no sign of Mr. Podgers remained. Once he thought he saw the bulky, misshapen figure swimming towards the steps by the bridge, and a dreadful sense of failure seized him; but it proved to be only a reflection, which vanished when the moon emerged from behind a cloud. At last, it seemed that he had fulfilled destiny\u2019s decree. He let out a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u2018Have you dropped something, sir?\u2019 a voice suddenly asked behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and saw a policeman carrying a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u2018Nothing important, sergeant,\u2019 he replied with a smile. Then, hailing a passing hansom, he climbed in and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next several days, he swung between hope and fear. At times he almost expected Mr. Podgers to enter the room; at others, he felt that Fate could not be so unjust to him. Twice he went to the palmist\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but could not make himself ring the bell. He craved certainty and feared it.\\n\\nAt last it came. He was sitting in the club smoking-room, drinking tea and listening rather wearily to Surbiton\u2019s account of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
