{"request_id":"342fea4d45eab3a3029cb9d8","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which sustained me at the beginning of my career, now only drives me deeper into the dust. All my plans and hopes are nothing, and, like the archangel who sought omnipotence, I am bound in an eternal hell. My imagination was vivid, and my powers of analysis and application were strong; through the union of these qualities, I conceived the idea and carried out the creation of a man. Even now I cannot remember without passion the dreams I had while the work remained unfinished. In my thoughts I walked in heaven, sometimes rejoicing in my powers, sometimes consumed by the thought of their consequences. From childhood I had been filled with great hopes and lofty ambition; but how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this degraded state. Despair seldom entered my heart; a great destiny seemed to carry me onward, until I fell, never, never to rise again.”

Must I then lose this remarkable being? I have longed for a friend; I have searched for someone who would understand and love me. Here, upon these desolate seas, I have found such a person, yet I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I would persuade him to return to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind intentions toward so miserable a wretch; but when you speak of new bonds and fresh affections, do you think any can replace those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even when our affections are not deeply stirred by exceptional virtue, the companions of our childhood always hold a certain power over our minds that scarcely any later friend can gain. They know our childhood dispositions, which, though they may later be altered, are never entirely erased; and they can judge our actions with greater certainty regarding the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never—unless such signs appeared early—suspect the other of deceit or falsehood, while another friend, however deeply attached, may still, despite himself, be viewed with suspicion. But I had friends dear to me not merely through habit and shared history, but because of their own worth; and wherever I may be, the comforting voice of my Elizabeth and Clerval’s conversation will always whisper in my ear. They are dead, and in such solitude only one feeling could persuade me to preserve my life. If I were engaged in some great undertaking or purpose, bringing broad benefit to my fellow creatures, then I could live to accomplish it."}

