          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"44ca6db4384a9b4c9332d785\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut, in truth, once an ascent has been made to any particular altitude, the weight of air passed through in any subsequent ascent is not at all proportional to the added height attained (as may plainly be inferred from what has already been said), but bears a ratio continually diminishing. It is therefore clear that, however high we ascend, we cannot, in the literal sense, reach a boundary beyond which atmosphere ceases to exist. It must be there, I reasoned, though it may be there in a condition of infinite tenuity.\\n\\n\u201cOn the other hand, I knew that arguments had not been lacking to establish the existence of an actual and fixed boundary to the atmosphere, beyond which no air whatever is present. Yet one circumstance, overlooked by those who maintain such a boundary, seemed to me\u2014though not a direct disproof of their doctrine\u2014nevertheless a matter deserving the gravest inquiry. In comparing the intervals between the successive returns of Encke\u2019s comet to its perihelion, after allowing, with the utmost precision, for every disturbance caused by planetary attraction, it is found that these periods are gradually shortening; in other words, the major axis of the comet\u2019s ellipse is becoming less, by a slow yet wholly regular diminution. This, then, is exactly what should occur if we assume that the comet encounters resistance from an exceedingly rare ethereal medium diffused throughout the regions of its orbit. For such a medium, by checking the comet\u2019s speed, must increase its centripetal force through the weakening of its centrifugal force. Put differently, the attraction of the sun would continually gain strength, and the comet would be drawn closer at each revolution. Indeed, no other explanation can be given for the variation in question. But again:\u2014The actual diameter of this same comet\u2019s nebulosity is seen to shrink rapidly as it nears the sun, and to expand with equal rapidity as it recedes toward its aphelion. Was I not warranted in supposing, with M. Valz, that this seeming condensation of bulk arises from the compression of the same ethereal medium of which I have spoken, and which grows denser only in proportion to its nearness to the sun? The lenticular appearance known also as the zodiacal light was likewise deserving of notice. This radiance, so conspicuous in the tropics and not to be confused with any meteoric gleam, stretches obliquely upward from the horizon and generally follows the direction of the sun\u2019s equator. It seemed to me plainly a rare atmosphere extending outward from the sun, at least beyond the orbit of Venus, and, I believed, indefinitely farther still. Indeed, I could not suppose this medium limited either to the course of the comet\u2019s ellipse or to the sun\u2019s immediate vicinity.\"}"
