          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f8601cb1f0c43214a42f287\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The cramped streets and courts finally opened into a broad vacant space, dotted with pens for animals and other signs of a cattle-market. Sikes eased his pace upon reaching it, for the girl could no longer endure the swift rate at which they had been walking. He turned to Oliver and harshly ordered him to take Nancy\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cD\u2019ye hear?\u201d snarled Sikes, as Oliver paused and glanced about him.\\n\\nThey stood in a gloomy corner, wholly apart from the path of passers-by.\\n\\nOliver saw only too clearly that resistance would be useless. He extended his hand, and Nancy gripped it firmly in her own.\\n\\n\u201cGive me the other,\u201d said Sikes, catching Oliver\u2019s free hand. \u201cHere, Bull\u2019s-Eye!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog raised his head and growled.\\n\\n\u201cLook here, boy!\u201d said Sikes, laying his other hand upon Oliver\u2019s throat; \u201cif he says the faintest word, hold him! D\u2019ye mind!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog growled once more, and, licking his lips, watched Oliver as though eager to fasten himself upon his windpipe at once.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s as ready as a Christian\u2014strike me blind if he ain\u2019t!\u201d said Sikes, looking at the creature with a sort of savage, grim approval. \u201cNow you know what\u2019s in store for you, master; so cry out as soon as you please: the dog\u2019ll soon put a stop to that sport. Move on, young \u2019un!\u201d\\n\\nBull\u2019s-Eye wagged his tail in answer to this unusually affectionate address; then, uttering another warning growl for Oliver\u2019s benefit, went ahead.\\n\\nThey were crossing Smithfield, though it might as well have been Grosvenor Square, so far as Oliver knew. The night was black and foggy. The shop lights could scarcely force their way through the dense mist, which grew thicker each moment, veiling streets and houses in darkness; making the unfamiliar place seem stranger still to Oliver, and his uncertainty more miserable and oppressive.\\n\\nThey had hastened only a few steps when a deep church bell sounded the hour. At its first stroke, his two guides halted and turned their heads toward the quarter from which the sound came.\\n\\n\u201cEight o\u2019clock, Bill,\u201d said Nancy, when the bell had stopped.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of telling me that? I can hear it, can\u2019t I?\u201d Sikes replied.\\n\\n\u201cI wonder if _they_ can hear it,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, of course they can,\u201d answered Sikes. \u201cIt was Bartlemy time when I was shopped, and there wasn\u2019t a penny trumpet in the fair whose squeaking I couldn\u2019t hear. After they\u2019d locked me up for the night, the noise and racket outside made the thundering old jail so quiet that I could nearly have dashed my brains out against the iron plates of the door.\u201d\"}"
