          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"69b9262f5743a749f9dec151\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd think, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, though tears came into his eyes despite all his efforts. \u201cOh, think how young and good she is, and how much happiness and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014certain\u2014quite certain\u2014that for your sake, because you are so good; and for her own sake; and for the sake of all those she makes happy, she will not die. Heaven will never allow her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou reason like a child, poor boy. Yet you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, though I hope I may be forgiven, because I am old and have seen enough sickness and death to understand the pain of losing those we love. I have also seen enough to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared for those who love them. But that should comfort us in our grief, because Heaven is just; and such things teach us clearly that there is a brighter world than this, and that the way to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke, she stopped her grieving as if by a single effort. As she drew herself upright, she became calm and resolute. He was even more amazed to find that this strength remained. Through all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie was always prepared and composed, carrying out every duty that had fallen to her steadily and, to outward appearance, even cheerfully. But he was young and did not know what strong minds can endure in difficult times. How could he, when even those who possess such strength so rarely know it themselves?\\n\\nA troubled night followed. By morning, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears had proved only too true. Rose was in the first stage of a severe and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not surrender to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, putting a finger to her lips as she looked steadily into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne as quickly as possible. It must be taken to the market town, which is no more than four miles away by the footpath across the fields, and from there sent by a rider straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will arrange it, and I know I can trust you to make sure it is done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his face showed how eager he was to leave at once.\"}"
