          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14220e4efb9b2f58d51622f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and horses burdened with heavy armor could not move very swiftly. They would make the best speed possible, and now that the ground was firm, with neither snow nor mud, they would likely manage a seven-mile pace; they would need to change horses once or twice; they would get there around six, or shortly afterward; there would still be ample daylight; they would notice the white cloth I was to fasten about my right arm, and I would assume command. We would hem in that prison and have the king out almost immediately. It would be sufficiently showy and picturesque, considering everything, though I should have preferred noon, because the affair would have had a more theatrical appearance.\\n\\nThen, to add another string to my bow, I decided to seek out some of those persons I had recognized before and reveal myself to them. That might get us out of our difficulty without the knights. But I had to move carefully, since it was dangerous work. I must acquire splendid clothing, and it would never do simply to rush into it. No; I must advance by stages, purchasing one suit after another in shops far apart, each outfit a little finer than the last, until at length I arrived at silk and velvet and was prepared for my undertaking. So off I went.\\n\\nBut the plan collapsed like scat! At the first corner I rounded, I ran straight into one of our slaves, prowling about with a watchman. I happened to cough, and he shot me a sharp look that went to my very marrow. I supposed he believed he had heard that cough before. At once I turned into a shop and edged my way down the counter, asking prices and keeping watch from the corner of my eye. Those two had halted, were speaking together, and peering in through the doorway. I resolved to leave by the rear, if there was a rear exit, and asked the shopwoman whether I might step out there to search for the escaped slave, who was thought to be concealed somewhere behind the place. I told her I was an officer in disguise, that my pard was over at the door holding one of the murderers, and asked whether she would kindly go tell him not to wait, but to proceed at once to the far end of the back alley and stand ready to head the fellow off when I drove him out.\\n\\nShe was aflame with curiosity to see one of those now celebrated murderers, and immediately set out on the errand.\"}"
