          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"414db23e5d0b9f99b1afcbcf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you would have continued, if you had been left alone. I wonder when you would have spoken if I had not asked you! My decision to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had a great effect. Too great, I am afraid; for what happens to the moral, if our happiness comes from a broken promise, since I ought not to have mentioned the matter? This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou need not trouble yourself. The moral will be entirely sound. Lady Catherine\u2019s unjustifiable attempts to separate us removed all my doubts. I do not owe my present happiness to your eagerness to express your gratitude. I was not inclined to wait for an opportunity from you. My aunt\u2019s information had given me hope, and I was determined to learn everything at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been of immense use, which ought to please her, since she loves being useful. But tell me, what did you come down to Netherfield for? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and be embarrassed? Or had you intended more serious consequences?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true purpose was to see you, and to decide, if I could, whether I might ever hope to make you love me. My stated purpose\u2014or what I told myself it was\u2014was to discover whether your sister still cared for Bingley, and, if she did, to make the confession to him that I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the courage to tell Lady Catherine what is going to happen to her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more likely to need time than courage, Elizabeth. But it ought to be done; and if you will give me a sheet of paper, it shall be done immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I did not have a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire the steadiness of your handwriting, as another young lady once did. But I too have an aunt, who must not be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she had been unwilling to admit how greatly her intimacy with Mr. Darcy had been exaggerated, Elizabeth had not yet answered Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter. But now, having something to tell her that she knew would be most welcome, she was almost ashamed to realize that her uncle and aunt had already lost three days of happiness, and she immediately wrote as follows:--\\n\\n\u201cI should have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I ought to have done, for your long, kind, satisfying account of every detail; but, to tell the truth, I was too upset to write. You imagined more than truly existed. But now imagine as much as you please; let your fancy run free, indulge your imagination in every possible flight the subject allows, and unless you believe me actually married, you cannot be very far wrong.\u201d\"}"
