          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fdb89445d3c8b2ea325c7f25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All this, and much more, I would gladly explain. But to be brief, I must have my reward. I long to return to my family and my home; and as the price of any further communication from me\u2014in return for the light I can shed on many important areas of physical and metaphysical science\u2014I must ask, through the influence of your honorable body, for a pardon for the crime I committed in causing the deaths of the creditors when I left Rotterdam. This, then, is the purpose of the present paper. Its bearer, a resident of the moon, whom I have persuaded and carefully instructed to act as my messenger to the earth, will wait for your Excellencies\u2019 decision and return to me with the pardon in question, if it can possibly be obtained.\\n\\n\u201cI have the honor to be, etc., your Excellencies\u2019 most humble servant,\\n\\n\u201cHANS PFAALL.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he had finished reading this most extraordinary document, Professor Rub-a-dub, it is said, dropped his pipe to the ground in utter surprise. Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk removed his spectacles, wiped them, and put them in his pocket; then, forgetting both himself and his dignity, he turned three times on his heel in the height of astonishment and admiration. There could be no doubt\u2014the pardon must be obtained. Professor Rub-a-dub swore this with a hearty oath, and the famous Von Underduk finally agreed. Taking the arm of his fellow scientist, and saying nothing, he started home as quickly as possible to consider what should be done. But when they reached the burgomaster\u2019s door, the professor dared to point out that, since the messenger had chosen to vanish\u2014no doubt frightened to death by the fierce appearance of Rotterdam\u2019s citizens\u2014the pardon would be of little use, because no one but a man from the moon would attempt a journey over such an enormous distance. The burgomaster admitted the truth of this point, and so the matter ended.\\n\\nThe rumors and guesses did not end, however. Once the letter was published, it produced all kinds of gossip and opinion. Some of the too-clever people even made fools of themselves by dismissing the whole affair as nothing more than a hoax. But with people of that sort, I believe, \u201choax\u201d is a general word for anything beyond their understanding. For my own part, I cannot imagine what evidence they have used to support such a charge. Let us see what they say:\"}"
