          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"20d297f54e626b5b39eec741\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be car-ri-ed to the skies, on flow\u2019ry _beds_ of ease,\\n\\nWhile others struggle for the prize, and sail through _blood_-y seas?\\n\\nHe was thought a marvelous reader. At church \u201csociables\u201d he was invariably asked to recite poetry; and when he had finished, the ladies would raise their hands and drop them helplessly into their laps, and \u201cwall\u201d their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords cannot tell it; it is too lovely, TOO lovely for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nWhen the hymn was over, the Rev. Mr. Sprague made himself into a bulletin-board, and read aloud \u201cnotices\u201d of meetings and societies and such matters until the catalogue appeared likely to run on till the crack of doom\u2014a strange practice still maintained in America, even in cities, here in this era of plentiful newspapers. Frequently, the less reason there is for an old custom, the more difficult it is to abolish it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a fine, liberal prayer, full of particulars: it petitioned for the church and the church\u2019s little children; for the other village churches; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for State officials; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for Government officers; for poor sailors tossed upon tempestuous seas; for the oppressed millions groaning beneath the heel of European monarchies and Oriental despotisms; for those who possessed the light and the glad tidings, yet had no eyes to see or ears to hear withal; for the heathen on the distant islands of the sea; and it ended with a plea that the words he was about to utter might receive grace and favor, and prove like seed cast into fruitful soil, bringing forth in due season a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere came a rustle of dresses, and the congregation, which had been standing, sat down. The boy whose story this book tells took no pleasure in the prayer; he merely bore it\u2014if indeed he bore it even that well. He was uneasy throughout; without knowing it, he counted the prayer\u2019s particulars\u2014for he was not attending, though he knew the ground by heart and the clergyman\u2019s customary course across it\u2014and whenever some small new item was slipped in, his ear caught it and his entire being rebelled; he judged such additions unjust and scoundrelly. During the prayer, a fly settled on the back of the pew before him and tormented his spirit by coolly rubbing its hands together, clasping its head in its arms, and polishing it so fiercely that it seemed nearly to separate from its body, leaving the thin thread of its neck exposed; by scraping its wings with its hind legs and laying them smooth against its body as though they were coat-tails; by performing its entire toilet as serenely as if it knew itself to be wholly secure.\"}"
