          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"207462b6810ebca7c07221e2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No imagination could conceive the sublimity that might have been displayed by such a phenomenon occurring in the darkness of night. Hell itself might well have served as its image. Even as it was, my hair rose upon my head while I looked far down into the gaping abysses, allowing fancy, as it were, to descend and wander through the strange vaulted chambers, the ruddy gulfs, and the red, ghastly fissures of that hideous, unfathomable fire. I had truly escaped by a narrow chance. Had the balloon remained but a little longer in the cloud\u2014that is, had not the discomfort of being wet induced me to cast out the ballast\u2014certain destruction must have followed. Such dangers, though seldom thought of, are perhaps the greatest to be met with in balloons. By this time, however, I had reached too lofty an elevation to feel further anxiety on that account.\\n\\n\u201cI was now ascending swiftly, and at seven o\u2019clock the barometer showed an altitude of no less than nine miles and a half. I began to experience much difficulty in breathing. My head, likewise, pained me severely; and, having for some time noticed a dampness upon my cheeks, I finally found that it was blood, flowing rather rapidly from the drums of my ears. My eyes also caused me considerable distress. When I passed my hand over them, they seemed to have started from their sockets to no slight extent; and every object in the car, even the balloon itself, appeared misshapen to my sight. These symptoms exceeded my expectations and gave me some alarm. At this moment, most rashly and without reflection, I threw from the car three five-pound portions of ballast. The increased speed of ascent thus gained bore me too quickly, and without proper gradation, into a highly rarefied layer of atmosphere; and the consequence nearly proved fatal both to my expedition and myself. I was suddenly attacked by a spasm that endured more than five minutes; and even after it had partly subsided, I could draw breath only at long intervals and with gasping\u2014while blood flowed copiously from my nose and ears, and slightly even from my eyes. The pigeons seemed distressed beyond measure and struggled to get free; while the cat mewed mournfully and, her tongue hanging from her mouth, reeled back and forth in the car as though poisoned. I now, too late, perceived the great imprudence I had committed in casting away the ballast, and my agitation was extreme. I expected nothing short of death\u2014and death within a few minutes.\"}"
