          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"79c402db7be8297f62e13f04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself enough to go to the Coles'. The visit gave her many pleasant memories the next day; and whatever she might be thought to have lost in dignified reserve was surely repaid by the glory of being so popular. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014good people, who deserved to be made happy!\u2014and left behind a name that would not soon be forgotten.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in recollection, is uncommon; and there were two matters on which she was not entirely comfortable. She wondered whether she had not failed in the duty one woman owes another by revealing her suspicions of Jane Fairfax's feelings to Frank Churchill. It was scarcely right; but the idea had been so strong that it slipped out, and his ready acceptance of everything she said was such a compliment to her insight that she found it hard to be fully convinced she ought to have kept silent.\\n\\nThe other cause for regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and on that point she had no doubt. She sincerely and unmistakably regretted the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She deeply mourned the idleness of her childhood\u2014and sat down to practise diligently for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet's arrival; and if Harriet's praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not put us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunlight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear\u2014I think you play better than either of you. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am sure I would much rather listen to you. Everyone last night said how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything about it must have felt the difference. The truth is, Harriet, my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax's is far beyond it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is any difference, no one could ever discover it. Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill spoke a great deal about your taste, and said he valued taste much more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I could see that she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing.\u2014You cannot understand a word of it. Besides, if she does play so very well, you know, it is only what she is obliged to do, because she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
