{"request_id":"62fc86c3c373616615ed0b2e","rewritten_text":"It struck me that this quaint falsehood was told in the simplest and prettiest fashion; but I had heard it only once, and that counts for something; doubtless it had pleased the others when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first, and presently stirred up the rest by means of a practical joke of distinctly inferior sort. He fastened some tin cups to a dog's tail and let him go, and the creature rushed round and round the place in a panic of terror, while all the other dogs howled after him, knocking into and smashing against everything in their path, until the whole scene became a chaos of disorder and a deafening uproar; whereupon every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces, and some tumbled from their chairs and rolled upon the floor in delight. They were exactly like a pack of children. Sir Dinadan was so pleased with his performance that he could not refrain from recounting again and again, to the point of exhaustion, how the deathless notion had come to him; and, as humorists of his kind commonly do, he was still laughing when everybody else had finished. He was so puffed up that he decided to deliver a speech--a humorous speech, naturally. I do not think I ever heard so many stale, worn-out jokes linked together in all my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It was especially melancholy to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my birth, and hear once more the miserable, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me the dry gripes as a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly persuaded me that no new joke can possibly exist. Everyone laughed at those relics--but people always do; I had observed that in later centuries. Naturally, the scoffer did not laugh--the boy, I mean. No, he scoffed; there was nothing at which he would not scoff. He declared that most of Sir Dinadan's jokes were rotten, and the remainder petrified. I said “petrified” was good; for I myself believed that the proper way to classify the venerable ages of certain of those jokes was by geological eras. But that clever thought struck the boy on a vacant spot, since geology had not yet been invented. Still, I wrote the remark down, intending to educate the commonwealth up to it if I survived. There is no sense in wasting a good thing merely because the market is not ready for it.

Then Sir Kay got up and began to set his history-mill going, with me as its fuel."}

