ht listen to the nightingale, while fireflies danced in bright rhythm and the gentle cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travellers. When we passed through a valley, soft shadows enclosed us, with rocks tinted in beautiful colours. When we crossed a mountain, Greece lay spread below us like a living map, her famous peaks cleaving the sky and her rivers threading silver lines through the fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travellers gazed in ecstasy upon this splendid landscape, so unlike the sober colours and melancholy graces of our native scenery. After we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties; yet our journey remained interesting. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people quickly stirred themselves to honour Lord Raymond.\"}"
