          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e6a1bfef9862f8fcd2161436\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked why I wanted it, and why I believed I had any right to it, I would tell him\u2014though he cared little for such humble dreams\u2014that I had loved Estella deeply and for a long time, and that though I had lost her and must live a life of bereavement, anything connected with her was still closer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. Seeing that Mr. Jaggers remained perfectly still and silent, apparently unmoved by this appeal, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you are a man with a kind heart. I have seen your pleasant home, your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful habits by which you brighten your business life. I beg you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to make him understand that, considering all the circumstances, he ought to be more frank with me!\u201d\\n\\nI had never seen two men look at each other more strangely than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this speech. At first I feared that Wemmick would be dismissed from his employment at once; but that fear faded when I saw Mr. Jaggers soften into something like a smile, and Wemmick grow more confident.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is all this?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou with an old father, and you with pleasant, playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what does it matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, putting his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis man must be the cleverest impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Wemmick replied, becoming bolder still. \u201cI think you are another.\u201d\\n\\nAgain they gave each other those same strange looks, each seeming still suspicious that the other was deceiving him.\\n\\n\u201cYou with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cSo long as it doesn\u2019t interfere with business,\u201d Wemmick replied, \u201clet it be so. Now that I look at you, sir, I should not be surprised if you were planning and arranging to have a pleasant home of your own someday, when you are tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded reflectively two or three times, and actually sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe will not speak of \u2018poor dreams\u2019; you know more about such things than I do, having much more recent experience of them. But now, as to this other matter. I will put a case to you. Remember: I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited for me to say that I fully understood he had expressly stated that he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201csuppose this case. Suppose that a woman, in circumstances such as those you have mentioned, kept her child hidden, and was obliged to reveal the fact to her legal adviser because he told her that, in order to understand the full scope of his defence, he had to know the truth concerning that child.\u201d\"}"
