          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a12e3450e915ec3577b3c25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox!\\n\\nI rushed to the king and spoke in his ear:\\n\\n\u201cOut the door at once, sire! The woman is dying of the disease that ravaged the outskirts of Camelot two years ago.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not move.\\n\\n\u201cTruly, I shall stay\u2014and help as well.\u201d\\n\\nI whispered again:\\n\\n\u201cKing, you must not. You have to leave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mean well, and you do not speak foolishly. But it would shame a king to show fear, and shame a belted knight to hold back his hand where people need aid. Peace, I will not go. You are the one who must leave. The Church\u2019s ban is not upon me, but it forbids you to be here, and she will deal harshly with you if word reaches her of your trespass.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a desperate place for him to be, and it might cost him his life, but there was no use arguing. If he believed his knightly honor was involved, that ended the matter; he would remain, and nothing could stop him. I knew that. So I let the subject drop. The woman spoke:\\n\\n\u201cKind sir, will you climb that ladder and bring me word of what you find? Do not fear to tell me, for there are times when even a mother\u2019s heart cannot break any further\u2014having already broken.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWait,\u201d said the king, \u201cand give the woman something to eat. I will go.\u201d He set down the knapsack.\\n\\nI turned to begin, but the king had already gone ahead. He stopped and looked down at a man lying in the dim light, who had not noticed us or spoken so far.\\n\\n\u201cIs that your husband?\u201d the king asked.\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank God for that one mercy, yes\u2014these past three hours. How can I ever repay my gratitude? My heart is bursting with it for the sleep he sleeps now.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cWe will be careful. We will not wake him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, no, you will not, for he is dead.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDead?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014what a triumph it is to know it! No one can hurt him now, no one can insult him again. He is in heaven now, and happy; or if he is not there, he waits in hell and is content, for there he will find neither abbot nor bishop. We were boy and girl together; we were husband and wife for five-and-twenty years, never parted until this day. Think how long that is to love and suffer together. This morning he was out of his mind, and in his fancy we were boy and girl again, wandering through the happy fields. In that innocent, joyful talk he wandered farther and farther, still chatting lightly, and entered those other fields we know nothing of, and was shut away from mortal sight.\"}"
