          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1216c38f330e449d33fedbf6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJoseph!! Joseph!!!\u201d At that, he shook his head and tapped it, showing what little opinion he had of Joseph.\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear young friend,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, \u201cyou must be hungry, you must be worn out. Sit down. Here is a chicken sent round from the Boar, here is a tongue sent round from the Boar, and here are one or two small things sent round from the Boar, which I hope you will not look down on. But do I,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again as soon as he had sat down, \u201csee before me the one I used to play with in his happy childhood? And may I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nThis \u201cMay I\u201d meant, might he shake hands? I agreed, and he did so warmly, then sat down again.\\n\\n\u201cHere is wine,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook. \u201cLet us drink, Thanks to Fortune, and may she always choose her favourites with equal good judgment! And yet I cannot,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again, \u201csee before me One\u2014and drink to One as well\u2014without once more saying\u2014May I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nI said he could, and he shook hands with me again, drained his glass, and turned it upside down. I did the same; and even if I had turned myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more straight to my head.\\n\\nMr. Pumblechook gave me the liver wing and the best slice of tongue\u2014none of those remote No Thoroughfares of Pork now\u2014and, by comparison, took no care of himself at all. \u201cAh! poultry, poultry!\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, speaking to the bird on the dish, \u201cwhen you were a young fledgling, you never thought what was waiting for you. You never thought you would become refreshment beneath this humble roof for one as\u2014Call it a weakness, if you like,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again, \u201cbut may I? may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nIt was becoming unnecessary to repeat that he could, so he did it at once. How he managed it so often without cutting himself on my knife, I cannot imagine.\\n\\n\u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he went on, after eating steadily for a while, \u201cwho had the honour of bringing you up by hand! It is a sad picture to think that she is no longer able fully to understand the honour. May\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI saw that he was about to come at me again, and stopped him.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll drink to her health,\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s how you know them, sir!\u201d (I do not know who Sir was, but it certainly was not me, and there was no third person there); \u201cthat\u2019s how you know the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
