          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cd545bfbc519759352917a0d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I says, \u201cI reckon you might tell a body. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have one. All noblemen do.\u201d\\n\\nThat was his fashion. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something, he just wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t change a thing.\\n\\nHe had the whole coat-of-arms matter settled, and now he went on to complete the rest of that job, which was to devise a sorrowful inscription\u2014said Jim had to have one, same as all of them did. He thought up a good many and wrote them on paper, then read them aloud, this way:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart was burst._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, deserted by the world and friends, wore out his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart was broken, and a weary spirit passed to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of cruel captivity, died a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook as he read them, and he nearly gave way. When he finished, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim ought to scratch onto the wall, they were all so fine; but finally he said he would have him scratch every one of them on. Jim said it would take a year to scratch so much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and besides he didn\u2019t know how to make letters; but Tom said he would mark them out for him, and then all Jim had to do was follow the marks. By and by he says:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, logs won\u2019t answer; dungeon walls ain\u2019t made of logs: we\u2019ve got to cut the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll bring in a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than logs; he said it would take such a pison long while to cut them into rock that he would never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he looked to see how Jim and me were coming along with the pens. It was mighty tedious, difficult, slow work, and gave my hands no chance to heal from the sores, and we scarcely seemed to get ahead at all; so Tom says:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to arrange it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with the same rock.\u201d\"}"
