          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"601fa705602115a9210b92a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day the ghost was exceedingly feeble and worn out. The dreadful agitation of the past four weeks was beginning to tell upon him. His nerves were utterly unstrung, and he jumped at the faintest sound. For five days he remained in his room, and finally resolved to abandon the matter of the blood-stain upon the library floor. If the Otis family did not desire it, they plainly did not merit it. They were obviously persons upon a low and material level of existence, wholly unable to value the symbolic significance of sensuous phenomena. The matter of phantasmic appearances and the evolution of astral bodies was, naturally, quite another affair, and not really within his power. It was his solemn obligation to show himself in the corridor once a week, and to gibber from the great oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of every month, and he could not see how he might honourably evade these duties. It was certainly true that his life had been very wicked, yet, on the other hand, he was extremely conscientious in everything relating to the supernatural. During the next three Saturdays, therefore, he paced the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every precaution possible not to be heard or seen. He took off his boots, stepped as softly as he could upon the old worm-eaten boards, wore a large cloak of black velvet, and made sure to employ the Rising Sun Lubricator in oiling his chains. I must admit that he found it rather difficult to bring himself to adopt this final safeguard. Yet one evening, while the family were dining, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and made away with the bottle. At first he felt somewhat degraded, but later he had sufficient sense to perceive that much could be said in favour of the invention, and that, to some extent, it answered his purpose. Even so, despite all his care, he was not allowed to pass unmolested. Strings were constantly stretched across the corridor, over which he stumbled in the darkness; and once, when attired for the role of \u2018Black Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u2019 he suffered a bad fall by stepping upon a butter-slide which the twins had made from the entrance to the Tapestry Chamber down to the head of the oak staircase. This final affront so infuriated him that he decided upon one last attempt to maintain his dignity and social standing, and resolved to visit the insolent young Etonians on the following night in his famous guise of \u2018Reckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u2019\\n\\nHe had not worn this disguise for over seventy years; indeed, not since he had frightened lovely Lady Barbara Modish so thoroughly with it that she abruptly ended her engagement to the grandfather of the present Lord Canterville, and fled to Gretna Green with handsome Jack Castleton, declaring that nothing on earth could persuade her to marry into a family which permitted so dreadful a phantom to pace the terrace at twilight.\"}"
