          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3f40ca0b1f30da384bf02cbf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I shall be late for luncheon if I remain any longer. Thank you for giving me the information I wished for. I always enjoy knowing everything about my new friends, and nothing at all about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you lunching, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I have invited myself and Mr. Gray. He is her newest _prot\u00e9g\u00e9_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to plague me any further with her charitable appeals. I am tired of them. Why, the good woman imagines I have nothing else to do than write cheques for her foolish fads.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cVery well, Uncle George, I will tell her, though it will produce no result. Philanthropic people lose every sense of humanity. That is their distinguishing trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman grunted his approval and rang for his servant. Lord Henry went up the low arcade into Burlington Street, and made his way towards Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo this was the tale of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Though it had been related to him crudely, it had nevertheless moved him with its hint of a strange, almost modern romance. A lovely woman staking all upon a mad passion. A handful of wild weeks of happiness ended by a horrible, treacherous crime. Months of silent anguish, followed by a child born in pain. The mother carried off by death, the boy abandoned to loneliness and the rule of an old man without love. Yes; it was an interesting setting. It gave the lad a pose, rendered him more perfect, as it were. Behind every exquisite thing in existence lay something tragic. Worlds must be in travail before even the humblest flower could bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the previous night, sitting opposite him at the club with startled eyes and lips parted in fearful delight, while the red candle-shades deepened the awakening wonder of his face to a richer rose. To talk with him was like playing an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and tremor of the bow.... There was something terribly fascinating in the exercise of influence. No other pursuit resembled it. To cast one\u2019s soul into some gracious shape and let it linger there awhile; to hear one\u2019s own intellectual opinions returned with all the added music of youth and passion; to communicate one\u2019s temperament to another as if it were a subtle liquid or an unfamiliar perfume: there was true pleasure in it\u2014perhaps the most satisfying pleasure still left to us in an age as narrow and vulgar as ours, an age grossly carnal in its enjoyments and grossly common in its purposes.... He was a marvellous type, too, this lad whom such a curious chance had brought him to meet in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or, at least, who might be made into a marvellous type.\"}"
