{"request_id":"e000c270f58334e411b5d2e3","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s father and mother. They were rather old-fashioned people and might object to the marriage if there were any hint of scandal. Still, he was sure that if he explained the whole situation, they would be the first to understand the motives behind his actions. He had every reason, then, to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and quiet, and avoided the need for painful scenes, which, like most Englishmen, he deeply disliked.

He knew absolutely nothing about poisons, however. The waiter could find nothing in the library except _Ruff’s Guide_ and _Bailey’s Magazine_, so he searched the shelves himself. At last he found a handsome edition of the _Pharmacopoeia_ and a copy of Erskine’s _Toxicology_, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the oldest members of the Buckingham. He had been elected by mistake for someone else, an error that angered the Committee so much that, when the real man appeared, they black-balled him unanimously. Lord Arthur was greatly confused by the technical language in both books and began to regret not paying more attention to his classics at Oxford. But in the second volume of Erskine, he found a very interesting and thorough account of aconitine, written in reasonably clear English. It seemed exactly the poison he needed. Its effect was swift—almost immediate—completely painless, and, when taken in a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, not at all unpleasant. He wrote the amount needed for a fatal dose on his shirt cuff, returned the books to their places, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the famous chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always served aristocratic customers himself, was rather surprised by the order and respectfully murmured that a medical certificate was required. But when Lord Arthur explained that it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to dispose of, since it showed early signs of rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf, he said he was entirely satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a pretty little silver _bonbonnière_ he saw in a Bond Street shop window, threw away Pestle and Humbey’s ugly pill-box, and immediately drove to Lady Clementina’s."}

