          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ea32d9d173cd7ab14ae6b34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We were reared together, with scarcely a year between our ages. It is needless to say that we knew nothing of discord or quarrel. Harmony was the spirit of our society, while the difference and opposition in our dispositions bound us still more closely. Elizabeth had a gentler, more reflective nature; yet, despite all my ardour, I could apply myself more intensely and was more profoundly possessed by a desire for knowledge. She occupied herself in pursuing the airy visions of poets; and amid the grand and marvellous prospects around our Swiss dwelling\u2014the lofty forms of the mountains, the shifting seasons, storm and serenity, winter's stillness, and the animation and tumult of our Alpine summers\u2014she discovered abundant matter for wonder and pleasure. While my companion regarded these splendid aspects of nature with grave contentment, I took delight in seeking out their causes. The world seemed to me a mystery I longed to penetrate. Curiosity, eager inquiry into nature's concealed laws, and a joy almost ecstatic when they were revealed to me, rank among my earliest recollections.\\n\\nWhen a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents wholly abandoned their wandering existence and settled in their native land. We had a house at Geneva and a _campagne_ at Belrive, upon the eastern bank of the lake, a little more than a league from the town. We lived chiefly at the latter place, and my parents passed their days in considerable retirement. By nature I shunned crowds and attached myself warmly to a small number. I therefore cared little for my schoolfellows generally; but with one of them I formed the closest ties of friendship. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant. He was a lad of uncommon talent and imagination. He delighted in enterprise, difficulty, and even peril for their own sakes. He had read deeply in tales of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic ballads and began many stories of magic and knightly exploits. He sought to have us perform plays and join in masquerades, whose parts were taken from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur's Round Table, and that chivalrous band who poured out their blood to rescue the holy sepulchre from the infidels.\\n\\nNo one could have enjoyed a more fortunate childhood than I did. My parents were animated by the purest kindness and indulgence. We knew they were not tyrants who governed our fate by whim, but the authors and instruments of the countless pleasures we possessed.\"}"
