          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42e2da366e1d0ba5f65aaec1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, quite ready to assume that this hope was meant as a special compliment to herself, smiled very graciously.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,\u201d he continued, \u201cand know that he is my son, though he does not bear my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, and I shall be very pleased to make his acquaintance. I am sure Mr. Elton will waste no time in calling on him, and we shall both be delighted to see him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind. Frank will be extremely happy, I am sure. He is to be in town next week, if not sooner. We had news of it in a letter today. I met the post on my way this morning, and, seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, took the liberty of opening it\u2014though it was not addressed to me; it was for Mrs. Weston. She is his chief correspondent, I assure you. I scarcely ever receive a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you actually opened a letter addressed to her! Oh, Mr. Weston\u201d\u2014laughing affectedly\u2014\u201cI must object to that. A most dangerous precedent indeed! I beg you will not allow your neighbours to follow your example. Upon my word, if this is what I am to expect, we married women must begin to stand up for ourselves! Oh, Mr. Weston, I could never have believed it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, we men are dreadful creatures. You must watch yourself, Mrs. Elton. This letter tells us\u2014it is a short letter, written in haste, merely to inform us\u2014that they are all coming to town immediately, on Mrs. Churchill\u2019s account. She has been unwell all winter, and finds Enscombe too cold for her, so they are all moving south without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed? From Yorkshire, I think. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they are about one hundred and ninety miles from London\u2014a considerable journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, upon my word, very considerable. Sixty-five miles farther than Maple Grove is from London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people with large fortunes? You would be astonished to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, sometimes rushes about. You will scarcely believe me, but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand it, has not been able to leave the sofa for an entire week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter, he said she complained of being too weak to reach her conservatory without both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d\"}"
