          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aedfe5982c7e06fbd6ef37f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If she had known that her sister meant to pull her away from hopes and realities like these, what would she have felt? Only her mother, who might have felt almost the same, could have understood. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was all that comforted her for the sad certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they knew nothing of what had happened, and their delight continued, with scarcely a pause, until the very day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Having often been in his company since her return, she had largely overcome her agitation; every feeling stirred by her former preference was entirely gone. She had even learned to recognize, in the gentleness that had first charmed her, an affectation and monotony that disgusted and tired her. His present conduct toward her gave her fresh cause for displeasure. The eagerness he soon showed to renew the attentions that had marked the beginning of their acquaintance could only provoke her, after all that had happened since. She lost all concern for him when she found herself chosen as the object of such empty, frivolous flirtation; and though she firmly discouraged it, she could not help feeling the insult implied by his belief that, however long and for whatever reason he had withdrawn his attentions, her vanity would be pleased and her preference secured whenever he chose to renew them.\\n\\nOn the regiment\u2019s final day in Meryton, he dined at Longbourn with several other officers. Elizabeth was so little inclined to part from him pleasantly that, when he asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings, and asked whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s thought, and with a returning smile, replied that he had often seen him before. After remarking that he was a very gentlemanly man, he asked how she had liked him. Her answer was warmly favorable. Soon afterward, with an air of indifference, he added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves as one gets to know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d Wickham cried, with a look she did not miss. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d But checking himself, he continued in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in his manner that he improves?\u201d\"}"
