{"request_id":"e7e195ede2ee90f607ce6b42","rewritten_text":"“It was some time before I recovered enough to attend to the ordinary concerns of the balloon. When I did, however, I examined it carefully and found, to my great relief, that it was undamaged. All my instruments were safe, and fortunately I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. Indeed, I had secured them so firmly in their places that such an accident was impossible. Looking at my watch, I saw that it was six o’clock. I was still ascending rapidly, and my barometer indicated a current altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly beneath me on the ocean lay a small black object, slightly oblong, apparently about the size of, and in every respect strongly resembling, one of those children’s toys called a domino. Training my telescope upon it, I clearly saw that it was a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled and pitching heavily in the sea, her head toward the W.S.W. Apart from this ship, I saw nothing but ocean, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now time that I explained to your Excellencies the purpose of my dangerous voyage. Your Excellencies will remember that my desperate circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to consider suicide. It was not that I felt any direct hatred of life itself, but that I was tormented beyond endurance by the incidental miseries of my condition. In this state of mind—wishing to live, yet exhausted by life—the treatise at the bookseller’s stall offered my imagination a means of escape. I then made my final decision. I resolved to depart, yet live; to leave the world, yet continue to exist. In short, to abandon riddles, I determined, whatever might follow, to force a passage, if possible, to the moon. Now, lest I be thought more mad than I truly am, I will describe as clearly as I can the considerations that led me to believe such an undertaking, though undoubtedly difficult and undeniably dangerous, was not entirely beyond the reach of a daring spirit.

“The moon’s actual distance from the earth was the first matter requiring attention. The mean, or average, distance between the centers of the two planets is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii, or only about 237,000 miles. I say the mean or average distance, but it must be remembered that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse, with an eccentricity of no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse’s major semi-axis, and that the earth’s center lies at its focus. Thus, if I could somehow arrange to meet the moon, as it were, at its perigee, the distance mentioned above would be considerably reduced."}

