{"request_id":"0fd92a15e0fa2b43e8b1e232","rewritten_text":"About ten minutes later, the tea bell rang, and when Virginia did not come downstairs, Mrs. Otis sent a footman up to tell her. After a while he returned, saying he could not find Miss Virginia anywhere. Since she often went into the garden each evening to pick flowers for the dinner table, Mrs. Otis was not alarmed at first. But when six o’clock struck and Virginia still had not appeared, she became truly anxious. She sent the boys out to search for her, while she and Mr. Otis looked through every room in the house. At half-past six the boys returned, saying they could find no sign of their sister. Everyone was now in a state of great excitement and did not know what to do, when Mr. Otis suddenly remembered that a few days earlier he had allowed a group of gypsies to camp in the park. He immediately set out for Blackfell Hollow, where he knew they were staying, accompanied by his eldest son and two farm servants. The little Duke of Cheshire, frantic with worry, pleaded to be allowed to come as well, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing there might be a fight. When he reached the place, however, he found that the gypsies had gone. Their departure had plainly been hurried: the fire was still burning, and several plates lay on the grass. After sending Washington and the two men to search the surrounding area, he hurried home and sent telegrams to every police inspector in the county, telling them to watch for a little girl kidnapped by tramps or gypsies. He then ordered his horse brought around and, after insisting that his wife and the three boys sit down to dinner, rode off along the Ascot Road with a groom. He had gone scarcely two miles when he heard someone galloping behind him. Looking back, he saw the little Duke riding up on his pony, bareheaded and with his face flushed.

‘I’m terribly sorry, Mr. Otis,’ the boy gasped, ‘but I can’t eat dinner while Virginia is missing. Please don’t be angry with me. If you had let us get engaged last year, none of this trouble would have happened. You won’t send me back, will you? I can’t go! I won’t go!’

The Minister could not help smiling at the handsome young rascal, and he was deeply moved by his devotion to Virginia. Leaning down from his horse, he patted the boy kindly on the shoulder and said, ‘Well, Cecil, if you refuse to go back, I suppose you must come with me. But I must get you a hat in Ascot.’"}

