          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0271f87a11606e83dad654e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you hear me? I have learned the language of despair: I know it all by heart, for I am Despair; and I am a strange creature, joyful, triumphant Despair. Yet those words are false, for the wave may be dark, but it is not bitter. We lie down and close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. Come then, delay no longer, you tardy one! See the pleasant potion! Look, it is a spirit of good, not a human girl, who invites you and, in persuasive tones\u2014oh, that they might persuade you!\u2014says, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I fixed my eyes on his face; and his exquisite beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, his gentle yet earnest expression of reproach and wonder, even before he spoke, changed my intensely strained feelings, taking from me all the harshness of despair and filling me only with the softest grief. I saw that his eyes too were moist as he took both my hands in his; then, sitting beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad act to which you would lead me, dearest friend, and your sorrow must indeed be profound to have filled you with such unhappy thoughts. You long for death, yet fear it, and wish me to be your companion. But I have less courage than you, and even with you beside me I do not dare to die. Listen to me, then consider whether you ought to draw me into your plan, even if, with the overpowering eloquence of despair, you could make black death seem so inviting that fair heaven appeared darkness. Listen, I beg you, to one who has himself cherished desperate thoughts and longed impatiently for death, but who has at last trampled the phantom beneath his feet and crushed its sting. Come: as you have played Despair to me, I will play Una to you, and lead you unharmed from his dark cavern. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words in which no selfish passion remains.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know what this vast world means, with its strange mixture of good and evil. But we have been placed here and commanded to live and hope. I do not know what we are to hope for; but there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that is our task on earth. If misfortune comes against us, we must struggle with her; we must cast her aside and continue onward, to discover what it is in our nature to desire. Whether this prospect of future good prepares us for another existence, I do not know; or whether it is simply that we, as laborers in God\u2019s vineyard, must lend a hand in smoothing the way for those who come after us.\"}"
