          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dbaef55c3ef75d1372f41030\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You suggested it yourself two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember\u2014the time the Church first tested its strength against us, then decided it was wiser to wait for a more favorable season. Well, how have you set up the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI run twelve extremely strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014from a large dynamo in the cave. The dynamo has no brushes except a positive and a negative one\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a circular piece of level ground a hundred yards across. They form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014that is, twelve circles inside one another\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight; go on.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fences are attached to heavy oak posts, only three feet apart, and the posts are sunk five feet into the ground.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is good and strong.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. The wires have no ground connection outside the cave. They leave the positive brush of the dynamo; there is a ground connection through the negative brush; the other ends return to the cave, and each is grounded separately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt costs too much\u2014wastes power for nothing. You need no ground connection except the one through the negative brush. The other end of every wire must come back into the cave and be fastened separately, without any ground connection. Now see how economical that is. A cavalry charge rushes against the fence; you use no power and spend no money, because there is only one ground connection until those horses strike the wire. The instant they touch it, they make a connection with the negative brush through the ground, and fall dead. Don\u2019t you see? You use no energy until it is needed. Your lightning is there and ready, like a loaded gun, but it costs you nothing until you fire it. Oh, yes, the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I don\u2019t know how I missed that. It is not only cheaper, but more effective than the other plan, because if wires break or become tangled, no harm is done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, especially if we have an indicator in the cave and disconnect the broken wire. Well, go on. The gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014that is arranged. In the center of the inner circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I have placed a battery of thirteen Gatling guns, with plenty of ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it. They cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights arrive, there will be music. The edge of the precipice above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve put a wire fence there, and a gatling.\u201d\"}"
