{"request_id":"c15a3b616e0bc362cce4abef","rewritten_text":"Each person compares it—not to the voice of someone from a nation whose language he knows—but to the opposite. The Frenchman thinks it was a Spaniard’s voice, and says he ‘might have made out some words had he known Spanish.’ The Dutchman insists it was a Frenchman’s; yet we are told that, ‘not understanding French, this witness was examined through an interpreter.’ The Englishman believes it was a German’s voice, and ‘does not understand German.’ The Spaniard ‘is sure’ it was an Englishman’s, but judges entirely by its intonation, ‘as he has no knowledge of English.’ The Italian thinks it was a Russian’s voice, but ‘has never spoken with a native of Russia.’ A second Frenchman also disagrees with the first, and is certain the voice was Italian; but, not knowing that language, he too, like the Spaniard, is ‘convinced by the intonation.’ How remarkably strange, then, must that voice have been, if such testimony could be given about it! Even in its tones, people from the five great regions of Europe could recognize nothing familiar. You may say it was the voice of an Asian or an African. Asians and Africans are not common in Paris; but without denying that possibility, I will only draw your attention to three facts. One witness calls the voice ‘harsh rather than shrill.’ Two others describe it as ‘quick and unequal.’ No witness mentioned any words, or even sounds like words, that could be understood.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what impression I have made on your mind so far; but I do not hesitate to say that sound deductions from even this part of the testimony—the part concerning the rough and shrill voices—are enough to create a suspicion that should guide every further step in investigating the mystery. I said ‘sound deductions,’ but that does not fully express my meaning. I mean that these deductions are the only proper ones, and that the suspicion follows from them inevitably, as their single result. I will not yet say what that suspicion is. I only want you to remember that, for me, it was strong enough to give my inquiries in the chamber a definite direction.

“Let us now imagine ourselves in this chamber. What should we look for first? The way the murderers escaped. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events."}

