          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"31126b755a8ff62bf61a7e0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m going, Massa Will\u2014indeed I am,\u201d the negro replied at once. \u201cI\u2019m almost at the end now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_At the end!_\u201d Legrand nearly screamed. \u201cDo you mean you are at the end of that branch?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSoon be at de end, massa\u2014o-o-o-o-oh! Lord have mercy! What is dis here on de tree?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d cried Legrand, greatly excited. \u201cWhat is it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, it ain\u2019t nothing but a skull\u2014somebody done left his head up de tree, and de crows done picked every bit of meat off it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA skull, you say? Very well\u2014how is it attached to the branch? What holds it there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSure enough, massa; I got to look. Why, dis is a very curious thing, upon my word\u2014there\u2019s a great big nail in de skull, fastening it to de tree.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, Jupiter, do exactly as I tell you. Do you hear?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, massa.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen pay attention\u2014find the skull\u2019s left eye.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHum! hoo! Dat\u2019s a good one! Why, there ain\u2019t no eye left at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCurse your stupidity! Do you know your right hand from your left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I knows dat\u2014I knows all about dat. It\u2019s my left hand I chops wood with.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! You are left-handed, and your left eye is on the same side as your left hand. Now, I suppose you can find the skull\u2019s left eye, or the place where its left eye used to be. Have you found it?\u201d\\n\\nThere was a long pause. At last the negro asked,\\n\\n\u201cIs de skull\u2019s left eye on de same side as de skull\u2019s left hand too? \u2019Cause de skull ain\u2019t got a hand at all\u2014but never mind! I got de left eye now\u2014here de left eye! What must I do with it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet the beetle drop through it, as far as the string will reach\u2014but be careful not to let go of the string.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll done, Massa Will; mighty easy to put de bug through de hole\u2014look out for him down there!\u201d\\n\\nDuring this exchange, no part of Jupiter could be seen; but the beetle he had lowered was now visible at the end of the string. It shone like a globe of polished gold in the last rays of the setting sun, which still faintly lit the hill where we stood. The scarab hung clear of every branch and, if released, would have fallen at our feet. Legrand immediately took the scythe and cleared a circular space, three or four yards across, directly beneath the insect. When he had finished, he told Jupiter to release the string and come down from the tree.\"}"
