          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dea0557ad79cb3127a8e2d8c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often reflected on the ancient philosophy of the Twofold Soul, and entertained myself with the notion of a double Dupin\u2014the inventive and the analytical.\\n\\nLet no one suppose, from what I have just said, that I am recounting some mystery or writing a romance. What I described in the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited, perhaps even disordered, mind. But an example of the nature of his remarks at such times will best explain what I mean.\\n\\nOne night we were walking along a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Both of us seemed absorbed in thought, and neither had spoken a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin said:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small man, certainly, and would be better suited to the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of it,\u201d I replied without thinking, not at first noticing\u2014so deeply had I been lost in reflection\u2014the extraordinary way in which he had joined my thoughts. A moment later I came to myself, and my astonishment was immense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said solemnly, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I do not hesitate to say that I am astonished, and can scarcely trust my senses. How could you possibly know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d Here I stopped, to make certain that he truly knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were saying to yourself that his small stature made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly the subject of my thoughts. Chantilly had once been a cobbler in the Rue St. Denis and, becoming obsessed with the stage, had attempted the role of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name, and had been famously ridiculed for it.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is one\u2014by which you have managed to read my soul in this matter.\u201d Indeed, I was even more alarmed than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruit seller,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of soles was not tall enough for Xerxes et id genus omne.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruit seller! You astonish me\u2014I know no fruit seller at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you as we entered the street\u2014perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI then remembered that a fruit seller, carrying a large basket of apples on his head, had nearly knocked me down by accident as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the street where we now stood; but I could not imagine what this had to do with Chantilly.\"}"
