          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ba88243d455a19eb91b80881\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made her understand that Frank admired her greatly\u2014thought her very beautiful and very charming; and with so much else to recommend him, she felt she ought not to judge him too severely. As Mrs. Weston said, \u201call young people have their little fancies.\u201d\\n\\nThere was one person among his new acquaintances in Surrey who was not so forgiving. Generally, throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury, he was judged with great fairness; generous allowances were made for the minor excesses of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so readily and bowed so gracefully. But there was one spirit among them whose power of criticism could not be softened by bows or smiles\u2014Mr. Knightley. The incident was reported to him at Hartfield; for a moment he said nothing, but Emma heard him soon afterward mutter to himself over the newspaper in his hand, \u201cHum! Exactly the frivolous, foolish fellow I took him for.\u201d She was half inclined to take offence; but a moment\u2019s observation convinced her that he had said it merely to ease his own feelings, not to provoke her, and so she let it pass.\\n\\nThough Mr. and Mrs. Weston\u2019s visit that morning brought unwelcome news in one respect, it was particularly timely in another. Something happened while they were at Hartfield that made Emma want their advice; and, more fortunately still, she wanted precisely the advice they gave her.\\n\\nThis was what happened:\u2014The Coles had been established in Highbury for some years, and were very worthy people\u2014friendly, generous, and unassuming; but, on the other hand, they came from humble origins, were in trade, and only moderately genteel. When they first arrived in the neighbourhood, they had lived according to their income, quietly, seeing little company, and entertaining that little inexpensively. But the past year or two had brought them a considerable increase in wealth\u2014the business in town had produced greater profits, and fortune generally had favoured them. As their wealth grew, so did their ambitions: they wanted a larger house and more company. They enlarged their house, increased their servants, and raised every kind of expense; and by now, in fortune and style of living, they ranked second only to the family at Hartfield. Their fondness for society, along with their new dining room, led everyone to expect that they would begin giving dinner parties; and a few gatherings, chiefly of unmarried men, had already taken place. Emma could scarcely suppose they would dare invite the regular and best families\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing would induce her to go if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal seem less significant than she wished.\"}"
