          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f90f3137a830178a7903d4ce\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, quite ready to believe that this hope was meant as a special compliment to herself, smiled very graciously.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I suppose,\u201d he continued, \u201cand know that he is my son, though he does not have my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, and I shall be very happy to know him. I am sure Mr. Elton will waste no time in calling on him, and we shall both be delighted to see him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind. Frank will be extremely happy, I am sure. He is to be in town next week, if not sooner. We had news of it in a letter today. I met the letters on my way this morning, and, seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, I took the liberty of opening it\u2014though it was not addressed to me. It was for Mrs. Weston. She is his chief correspondent, I assure you. I hardly ever receive a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you actually opened a letter addressed to her! Oh, Mr. Weston\u201d\u2014laughing in an affected way\u2014\u201cI must object to that. A very dangerous example indeed! I beg you not to let your neighbours copy you. Upon my word, if this is what I am to expect, we married women must begin to defend ourselves! Oh, Mr. Weston, I could never have believed it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, we men are terrible fellows. You must be careful, Mrs. Elton. This letter tells us\u2014it is a short letter, written in haste, only to give us notice\u2014it tells us that they are all coming to town at once, because of Mrs. Churchill. She has not been well all winter, and thinks Enscombe is too cold for her, so they are all moving south without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! From Yorkshire, I think. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, it is about one hundred and ninety miles from London\u2014a considerable journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, upon my word, very considerable. Sixty-five miles farther than Maple Grove is from London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people with large fortunes? You would be amazed to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, sometimes rushes about. You will hardly believe me, but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand it, has not been able to leave the sofa for a whole week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter, he said she complained of being too weak even to get into her conservatory without both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d\"}"
