          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f3600d58b0109fa9c0a2fe0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat is it?\u201d cried Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cQuiet!\u201d answered the Dodger. \u201cDo you notice that old cove by the book-stall?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old gentleman across the street?\u201d said Oliver. \u201cYes, I see him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019ll suit,\u201d said the Dodger.\\n\\n\u201cA capital plant,\u201d remarked Master Charley Bates.\\n\\nOliver glanced from one to the other in the utmost astonishment; but he was allowed no time to ask questions, for the two boys stole across the road, and crept close behind the old gentleman to whom his attention had been drawn. Oliver followed them a few steps; then, uncertain whether to go forward or draw back, remained standing and watching in mute wonder.\\n\\nThe old gentleman was a most respectable-looking person, with a powdered head and gold-rimmed spectacles. He wore a bottle-green coat with a black velvet collar, white trousers, and carried a neat bamboo cane beneath his arm. He had picked up a book from the stall, and stood there reading it as earnestly as though seated in his elbow-chair in his own study. Indeed, he may well have imagined himself there; for his absorption plainly showed that he saw neither the book-stall, nor the street, nor the boys\u2014nothing, in brief, except the book: which he read straight through, turning the leaf upon reaching the foot of each page, beginning with the first line of the next, and proceeding steadily with the keenest interest and eagerness.\\n\\nWhat were Oliver\u2019s terror and dismay, as he stood a few paces away, staring with his eyes opened as wide as possible, when he saw the Dodger thrust his hand into the old gentleman\u2019s pocket and pull out a handkerchief! To see him pass it to Charley Bates; and then to watch them both dash round the corner at full speed!\\n\\nIn a moment, the entire mystery of the handkerchiefs, the watches, the jewels, and the Jew came rushing into the boy\u2019s mind.\\n\\nFor an instant he stood with the blood coursing so fiercely through his veins in terror that he felt as though he were in a blazing fire; then, bewildered and afraid, he fled; and, scarcely knowing what he did, ran as fast as his feet could carry him.\\n\\nAll this happened within a minute. At the very moment Oliver started to run, the old gentleman put his hand into his pocket and, finding his handkerchief gone, wheeled sharply round. Catching sight of the boy speeding away so rapidly, he naturally took him for the thief; and, crying \u201cStop thief!\u201d with all his strength, set off after him, book still in hand.\"}"
