          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"01fc1d77cb3a322d16e3b19f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, never read any book for pleasure except the Baronetage. In it he found something to do when bored and comfort when troubled. It stirred him to admiration and respect as he considered the few surviving titles from the earliest grants. Any unpleasant feelings caused by family matters naturally became pity and contempt as he turned through the nearly endless new titles of the last century. And if no other page could interest him, he could always read his own history with unfailing pleasure. His favourite book always opened at this page:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThis was exactly how the paragraph had first come from the printer. But Sir Walter had improved it, for the benefit of himself and his family, by adding after Mary\u2019s birth date: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset.\u201d He had also inserted, with great accuracy, the day of the month on which his wife had died.\\n\\nNext came the history and rise of the ancient, respectable family, in the usual style: how it had first settled in Cheshire; how it was mentioned in Dugdale; how its members had served as high sheriff, represented a borough in three successive parliaments, shown loyalty, and received the dignity of baronet in the first year of Charles II; along with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. It filled two handsome small pages and ended with the family arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed once more by Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity about his appearance and his position. He had been remarkably handsome when young and, at fifty-four, was still a very fine-looking man. Few women cared more about their appearance than he did, and no valet to a newly made lord could be more pleased with his place in society. He thought beauty was second only to a baronetcy as a blessing, and Sir Walter Elliot, possessing both, was the constant object of his deepest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis looks and rank had one fair claim on his affection, since he owed them a wife whose character was far superior to anything he himself deserved.\"}"
