          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37df8b3c441fd44ae6451367\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings had proved stronger than her self-interest, and for the young man\u2019s sake she had given up the chance of continuing her plans for Sir Walter. Yet she had cleverness as well as feeling; and it remained uncertain whether his skill or hers would win in the end\u2014whether, after stopping her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he might not eventually be charmed and persuaded into making her Sir William\u2019s wife.\\n\\nSir Walter and Elizabeth were certainly shocked and humiliated by losing their companion and discovering how she had deceived them. They had their distinguished cousins, of course, to turn to for comfort; but they must long feel that flattering and following others, without receiving flattery and attention in return, was only half a pleasure.\\n\\nAnne, having soon become sure that Lady Russell meant to love Captain Wentworth as she should, had only one shadow over her happiness: she had no family to offer him that a sensible man could value. In this she felt her own lack very deeply. The difference in their fortunes meant nothing to her and caused her no regret; but having no family to welcome and appreciate him properly, no respectable, harmonious, kind relations to offer in return for all his worth and for the eager welcome she received from his brothers and sisters, caused as much pain as she could feel amid such great happiness. She had only two friends in the world to add to his circle: Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. Yet he was very willing to become attached to both. Despite all Lady Russell\u2019s former mistakes, he could now value her sincerely. Though he was not required to say that she had been right to separate them at first, he was ready to say nearly everything else in her favour; and Mrs Smith had many reasons to recommend her quickly and permanently.\\n\\nHer recent help to Anne would have been enough by itself, and their marriage, instead of taking one friend from her, gave her two. She was their first visitor after they had settled into married life; and Captain Wentworth, by helping her recover her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014writing and acting for her, and guiding her through every small difficulty of the case with the energy of a fearless man and a determined friend\u2014fully repaid the services she had given, or had ever intended to give, to his wife.\"}"
