          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf1d0d6d6f4e0a9a81e47e37\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd think, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, while tears rose in his eyes in spite of every effort to restrain them. \u201cOh! think how young and good she is, and how much happiness and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014certain\u2014quite certain\u2014that, for your sake, who are so good yourself; and for her own; and for the sake of all whom she makes so happy; she cannot die. Heaven will never suffer her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand upon Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou reason like a child, poor boy. Yet you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver; but I trust I may be forgiven, for I am old, and have seen enough of sickness and death to understand the anguish of being parted from those we love. I have seen enough, too, to know that it is not always the youngest and the best who are left to those who love them; but this ought to comfort us in our grief; for Heaven is just; and such events teach us solemnly that there is a brighter world than this, and that the way to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her; and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was astonished to observe that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke, she restrained her grief as if by a single effort; and, raising herself as she continued, grew calm and resolute. He was even more surprised to discover that this resolution endured; and that, through all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie remained prepared and self-possessed: fulfilling every duty that had fallen to her, steadily, and, to outward appearance, even with cheerfulness. But he was young, and knew nothing of what strong minds can do in seasons of trial. How could he, when those who possess them so rarely know it themselves?\\n\\nA troubled night followed. With the morning, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears were only too fully confirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a violent and perilous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not surrender ourselves to fruitless sorrow,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, putting a finger to her lip as she gazed steadily into his face; \u201cthis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne with the utmost speed. It must go to the market-town, which lies no more than four miles away by the footpath over the fields; and from there be forwarded by a mounted express directly to Chertsey. The people at the inn will see to this; and I know I may rely on you to make certain it is done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his look showed how eager he was to leave at once.\"}"
