          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ceb4078c93d3b1bf569f11b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Col. Grangerford was a gentleman, you understand. He was a gentleman through and through, and his family was the same. He was well born, as folks say, and that counts as much in a man as it does in a horse, according to the Widow Douglas; and nobody ever disputed that she belonged to the very first aristocracy in our town. Pap said so too, though he had no more quality than a mudcat himself. Col. Grangerford was mighty tall and slender, with a dark, pale complexion, without a speck of red anywhere in it. Every morning he shaved his whole thin face clean, and he had the thinnest lips and nostrils, a high nose, heavy brows, and the blackest eyes, set so far back they looked as if they were peering at you from caves, as you might say. His forehead was high, his hair black and straight, hanging down to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin, and every day he wore a fresh shirt and a complete suit, from head to foot, of linen so white it pained your eyes to look upon it; Sundays he put on a blue swallowtail coat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany walking-stick with a silver head. There was no foolishness in him, not the least bit, and he was never noisy. He was as kind as could be\u2014you could sense it, and it made you trust him. Now and then he smiled, and it was pleasant to see; but when he drew himself up straight as a liberty-pole, and lightning began to flash beneath his eyebrows, you felt like climbing a tree first and asking what was wrong afterward. He never needed to tell anybody to behave\u2014everyone minded their manners whenever he was there. Folks liked having him about, too; he was sunshine nearly all the time\u2014I mean, he made it feel like fair weather. When he changed into a cloudbank, it was terribly dark for half a minute, but that was enough; nothing would go amiss again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came downstairs in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good-day, and did not sit again until they had taken their seats. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, where the decanter stood, mixed him a glass of bitters, and handed it over. He held it until Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s drinks had been mixed too; then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam;\u201d and they gave the slightest bow in the world and thanked them. Then all three drank, and Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy left in the bottoms of their tumblers, gave it to Buck and me, and we drank to the old people as well.\"}"
