{"request_id":"0e349c7829dacf1ea166c3c3","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became acquainted with a Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a succession of misfortunes had brought him to poverty. To escape the humiliation that followed his losses, he left New Orleans, the city of his ancestors, and settled on Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina.

This island is a very unusual place. It is made up of little but sea sand and is about three miles long. Nowhere is it more than a quarter mile wide. It is divided from the mainland by a barely visible creek that winds through a wilderness of reeds and mud, a favorite haunt of the marsh hen. As one might expect, the vegetation is sparse, or at least stunted. There are no trees of any size. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands, and where a few wretched frame houses are occupied in summer by those fleeing Charleston’s dust and fever, one may indeed find the rough palmetto. But the entire island, except for this western point and a strip of firm white beach along the sea, is covered by a dense growth of sweet myrtle, so highly valued by English gardeners. Here the shrub often reaches fifteen or twenty feet in height, forming an almost impenetrable thicket and filling the air with its fragrance.

In the deepest part of this thicket, not far from the island’s eastern, more distant end, Legrand had built a small hut, which he occupied when I first met him by pure chance. Our acquaintance soon became friendship, for there was much in the recluse to inspire both interest and respect. I found him well educated and possessed of remarkable mental powers, though afflicted by misanthropy and prone to strange shifts between enthusiasm and melancholy. He kept many books, but seldom used them. His chief pleasures were hunting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells or insect specimens; his collection of the latter might have been envied by a Swammerdamm. On these excursions he was usually accompanied by an old Black man named Jupiter, who had been freed before the family’s reverses but could not be persuaded, by either threats or promises, to abandon what he regarded as his right to follow his young “Massa Will.” It is quite possible that Legrand’s relatives, believing his mind somewhat unsettled, had deliberately encouraged Jupiter’s stubbornness so that he might watch over and protect the wanderer."}

