          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5221faa6f2d7b67b51ecdfe1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity, ever productive of misery, must have been severed, and the malignant fate that rules it would not suffer such a violation of her eternal laws. Yet why should I complain of this? Misery was my native element, and nothing save what was wretched could come near me; had Woodville been happy, I should never have known him. And can I, who for so many years was sustained by tears and reared beneath the dew of grief, pause to recount a history of sorrow and death?\\n\\nWoodville was compelled to undertake a journey into the country, and day after day was detained in wearisome absence from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her saying that she was a little ill, yet bidding him hasten to her, for from his eyes she would draw health, and his presence would be her surest medicine. He was delayed three days more, and then hurried to her. His heart, though he knew not why, foretold calamity; he had heard from her no more; he feared that she had grown worse, and this apprehension made him restless and impatient for the instant when he might again behold her standing before him in health and beauty. For a dark voice seemed continually to murmur, \u201cYou will never again behold her as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her dwelling, all within was silent. He passed through several rooms; in one he saw a servant weeping bitterly. Faint with terror, he could scarcely ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d and only listened for the dreadful reply, \u201cNot yet.\u201d These startling words struck him as less terrible than those he had expected; and to know that she still existed, and that hope remained, brought him relief. He recalled the words of her letter and gave way to the wild thought that his kisses, warm with love and life, would breathe fresh spirit into her; that while he was beside her she could not die; that his presence was the talisman of her life.\\n\\nHe hastened to her chamber; she lay with cheeks flushed by fever, her eyes closed, and seemingly without sense. He folded her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses upon her burning lips; he called to her, in a voice subdued by anguish, by the tenderest names: \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am with you; your life, your love. Return; dearest one, you promised me this gift, that I should bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? To see you no more? To be parted from that which is part of myself; without whom I have neither memory nor futurity?\u201d\"}"
