{"request_id":"db02213e2c48459bb8ced46d","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was wasting his time uselessly where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and complete the arrangements they had begun for his Indian venture. He could delay his departure no longer; but since his journey to London might be followed, sooner even than he expected, by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore urged me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so that we could travel south together. This letter partly restored me to life, and I decided to leave my island after two days.

Yet before I left, I had a task to perform that I shuddered to consider: I had to pack my chemical instruments, and to do so I had to enter the room that had been the scene of my hateful work and handle the tools whose very sight made me sick. The next morning, at dawn, I gathered enough courage and unlocked the door to my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living flesh of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then entered the room. With trembling hands I carried the instruments out, but I realized that I must not leave the remnants of my work to arouse the horror and suspicion of the peasants. I put them in a basket with a large quantity of stones and, having packed them up, resolved to throw them into the sea that very night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach, cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could have been more complete than the change in my feelings since the night the demon appeared. Before, I had regarded my promise with grim despair, as something that had to be fulfilled whatever the consequences; but now I felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes, and that for the first time I saw clearly. The thought of resuming my work never entered my mind. The threat I had heard weighed heavily on my thoughts, but I did not consider that any voluntary action of mine might prevent it. I had decided that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be an act of the lowest and most monstrous selfishness, and I drove from my mind every thought that might lead me to any other conclusion."}

