          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5f631dbef33ae389a08ffae3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is a strange thing, but everyone who vanishes is said to have been seen in San Francisco. It must be a charming city, with all the attractions of the afterlife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you suppose has become of Basil?\u201d Dorian asked, raising his Burgundy to the light and wondering how he could speak of it so calmly.\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t the faintest idea. If Basil has chosen to conceal himself, that is no concern of mine. If he is dead, I do not wish to think of him. Death is the only thing that has ever frightened me. I hate it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d the younger man asked wearily.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, holding the gilded lattice of an open vinaigrette box beneath his nostrils, \u201cthese days one can survive anything except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts of the nineteenth century that cannot be explained away. Let us take our coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran away with played Chopin beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was very fond of her. The house is rather lonely without her. Of course, marriage is only a habit\u2014a bad habit. Yet one regrets losing even one\u2019s worst habits. Perhaps one regrets them most of all. They are such an essential part of one\u2019s personality.\u201d\\n\\nDorian said nothing. He rose from the table, went into the next room, sat at the piano, and let his fingers wander over the black and white ivory keys. When the coffee had been brought in, he stopped and, looking across at Lord Henry, said, \u201cHarry, has it ever occurred to you that Basil may have been murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was very popular, and he always wore a Waterbury watch. Why would anyone murder him? He was not clever enough to make enemies. Certainly, he had an extraordinary gift for painting. But a man may paint like Velasquez and still be as dull as possible. Basil was really rather dull. He interested me only once, when he told me, years ago, that he had a wild adoration for you and that you were the ruling inspiration of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was very fond of Basil,\u201d Dorian said, with sadness in his voice. \u201cBut do people not say that he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, some newspapers do. It does not seem at all likely to me. I know there are terrible places in Paris, but Basil was not the kind of man who would have gone to them. He had no curiosity. That was his chief flaw.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry, if I told you that I murdered Basil?\u201d the younger man said.\"}"
