{"request_id":"9e6dedda359e569ff43ed55e","rewritten_text":"The stars still shone brightly when I woke, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, showed that it was midnight. I had risen from troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s final feast; I arrived ravenous, the covers were lifted, and hot water gave off its unsatisfying steam, while I fled from the host’s anger, for he had taken Raymond’s form. To my fevered imagination, the vessels he threw after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s figure, warped by a thousand distortions, swelled into a gigantic phantom bearing the mark of pestilence on its brow. The growing shadow rose and rose, filling, then seeming to struggle beyond, the adamantine vault that arched above, supporting and enclosing the world. The nightmare became torment; with a powerful effort, I shook off sleep and restored reason to its usual office. My first thought was Perdita; I had to return to her. I had to support her, finding in despair whatever nourishment might sustain her wounded heart; calling her back from grief’s wild excesses through duty’s stern laws and regret’s gentle tenderness.

The stars’ positions were my only guide. I turned away from the dreadful ruin of the Golden City and, after great effort, managed to free myself from its enclosure. Outside the walls I encountered a group of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one and hurried to my sister. In that brief interval, the plain’s appearance had changed: the camp had broken up; remnants of the disbanded army gathered in scattered little groups; every face was darkened, every gesture expressed astonishment and alarm.

With a heavy heart, I entered the palace and stood afraid to move forward, to speak, or to look. Perdita was in the middle of the hall. She sat on the marble floor, her head bowed upon her breast, her hair disordered, her fingers restlessly twisting around one another. She was pale as marble, and agony had drawn tight every feature. She saw me and looked up in question; that faint look of hope was itself misery. The words died before I could say them; I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture; her head fell again, and her fingers resumed their restless work. At last I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my expression, and for all the world she would not have had the story of her terrible misery formed and confirmed by harsh, irreversible words. Indeed, she seemed to want to turn my thoughts from it. She rose from the floor. “Hush,” she whispered. “After much weeping, Clara is asleep; we must not wake her.”"}

