          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d9047f4740d1eb25b27cfdf2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"_PS._\u2014Do tell me about the bows. Jennings declares they are all the fashion.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so grave and miserable over the letter that the Duchess burst into peals of laughter.\\n\\n\u2018My dear Arthur,\u2019 she exclaimed, \u2018I shall never let you see a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what am I to say about the clock? I think it a splendid invention, and should like one myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I don\u2019t think very highly of them,\u2019 said Lord Arthur, with a melancholy smile; and, after kissing his mother, he quitted the room.\\n\\nOn reaching his room upstairs, he threw himself upon a sofa, his eyes brimming with tears. He had done all he could to commit this murder, yet on both attempts he had failed, through no fault of his own. He had endeavoured to perform his duty, but it seemed that Destiny herself had betrayed him. He was weighed down by the sterility of good intentions, by the uselessness of striving to be noble. Perhaps it would be wiser to end the engagement altogether. Sybil would suffer, certainly, but suffering could not truly spoil a nature as noble as hers. And for himself, what did it signify? There was always some war in which a man might die, some cause for which he might surrender his life; and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny fulfil his doom. He would do nothing to assist her.\\n\\nAt half-past seven he dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and he was compelled to dine with them. Their frivolous talk and pointless jokes failed to interest him, and the moment coffee was served he left, pleading some engagement in order to escape. As he was leaving the club, the hall-porter gave him a letter. It came from Herr Winckelkopf, requesting that he call the following evening to inspect an explosive umbrella, which discharged itself the instant it was opened. It was the newest invention, and had only just come from Geneva. He tore the letter into pieces. He had resolved to make no further experiments. Then he strolled down to the Thames Embankment and sat beside the river for hours. The moon looked through a mane of tawny cloud like the eye of a lion, while countless stars glittered in the hollow sky like gold dust scattered over a purple dome. From time to time a barge swung into the muddy current and drifted away upon the tide, while the railway signals shifted from green to scarlet as trains shrieked across the bridge.\"}"
