          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f817d66042560e1845082952\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Good heavens!\u2019 the Duchess said to herself. \u2018So he is some kind of palm-reader after all. How dreadful. I hope, at least, that he is a foreigner. That would not be quite so bad.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I really must introduce him to you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Introduce him!\u2019 cried the Duchess. \u2018You don\u2019t mean he is here?\u2019 She began searching for a small tortoiseshell fan and a very worn lace shawl, so that she could leave at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u2018Of course he is here. I would never think of giving a party without him. He says I have a perfectly psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been even a tiny bit shorter, I would have become a complete pessimist and entered a convent.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I understand!\u2019 said the Duchess, greatly relieved. \u2018He tells fortunes, I suppose?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018And bad fortunes too,\u2019 Lady Windermere replied, \u2018as many as you like. Next year, for example, I am in terrible danger both on land and at sea, so I intend to live in a balloon and have my dinner sent up in a basket every evening. It is all written on my little finger, or in the palm of my hand\u2014I forget which.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018But surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Duchess, surely Providence has learned to resist temptation by now. I think everyone ought to have their hands read once a month, just to know what not to do. Naturally, one does it anyway, but it is so pleasant to have been warned. Now, unless someone fetches Mr. Podgers at once, I shall have to go myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Let me go, Lady Windermere,\u2019 said a tall, handsome young man standing nearby, who had been listening with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u2018Thank you, Lord Arthur, but I am afraid you would not recognise him.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018If he is as remarkable as you say, Lady Windermere, I can hardly fail to find him. Tell me what he looks like, and I will bring him to you immediately.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, he does not look at all like a palm-reader. I mean, he is not mysterious, strange, or romantic-looking. He is a short, stout man, with an odd bald head and large gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014somewhere between a family doctor and a country lawyer. I am truly sorry, but it is not my fault. People are so irritating. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets exactly like pianists. Last season, I remember inviting a most dreadful conspirator to dinner\u2014a man who had blown up a great many people, always wore a coat of mail, and carried a dagger up his sleeve. And do you know, when he arrived, he looked exactly like a pleasant old clergyman and told jokes all evening?\u2019\"}"
