          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e367498da4921fb580e5898\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On Saturday nights in our village, there was a pleasant feeling of tidying up and taking a quiet break before beginning life again, and this encouraged Joe to risk staying out half an hour later than usual. When the half-hour and the rum and water ran out together, Joe rose to leave and took my hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I may have a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe found it among a handful of loose coins, wrapped it in crumpled paper, and gave it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cRemember! Your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him much longer than good manners allowed, and gripping Joe tightly. He said good-night to Joe, and to Mr. Wopsle, who left with us, but gave me only a look from his aiming eye\u2014or not even a look, because he closed it; yet an eye can do wonders by being hidden.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I had felt like talking, I would have had to do all the talking myself. Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe walked home with his mouth wide open, trying to wash the rum away with as much air as possible. But I was almost stunned by the return of my old wrongdoing and my old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in too bad a temper when we came into the kitchen, and Joe was encouraged by this unusual fact to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d Mrs. Joe said triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be a good one. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d Mrs. Joe said, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were nothing less than two thick, sweaty one-pound notes, which seemed to have been very familiar with every cattle market in the county. Joe grabbed his hat again and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, quite sure the man would not be there.\\n\\nSoon Joe returned. He said the man had gone, but that he had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
