          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d1776a939964e8000ee3c890\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Almost a year had elapsed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had nearly completed their course--It was then the close of May; the woods were arrayed in their newest green, and the pleasant scent of freshly cut grass filled the fields. I imagined that the soft air and the beautiful aspect of Nature might help to awaken in him gentler emotions, and dispose him to feelings of peace and affection before the confidence I resolved to obtain from him.\\n\\nI therefore selected the evening of one of these days for my endeavour. I asked him to walk with me, and conducted him to a neighbouring beech wood, where the thin shade protected us from the oblique and brilliant rays of the setting sun--After we had walked some time without speaking, I sat down beside him on a moss-covered knoll--It is strange, yet even now I seem to behold the place--many of the slender, smooth trunks were entwined with ivy, whose glossy leaves of deepest green stood in contrast with the pale bark and the light foliage of the young beech shoots springing from their parent stems--the close grass was mixed with moss, and partly strewn with the dead leaves of the preceding autumn, which the winds had driven together here and there into small heaps--a few stumps, overgrown with moss, stood around--The leaves stirred softly in the breeze, and through their green roof the clear blue sky was visible--As evening advanced, the distant trunks were coloured red by the sun, and the wind wholly ceased, while a few birds passed us on their way to evening repose.\\n\\nIt was here, then, that we sat together; and when you learn all that occurred--all that so terribly rent our souls even in this tranquil place, which, but for strange passions, might have been a paradise to us, you will not be surprised that I remember it, as I gazed upon it, hoping its serenity might impart serenity to me, and inspire not courage alone but persuasive language. I observed all these things, and marked them vaguely in my mind, while I tried to put my thoughts into suitable order for my purpose. My heart beat quickly as I summoned myself to address him, for I was resolved not to be turned aside; yet I trembled to think what impression my words might make upon him. At last, after much hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness towards me, my dearest father, and the affection--the excessive affection--which you showed me when you first returned, will, I trust, excuse in your eyes my daring to speak to you, though with a daughter\u2019s tender love, yet likewise with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
